ne': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'David Botti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Botti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/e9e9cdfe-1a6f-5690-9c76-493599b992c0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/e9e9cdfe-1a6f-5690-9c76-493599b992c0'}, {'abstract': 'David Daniels sings the title role in a Metropolitan Opera revival of the Mark Morris production of Gluck’s “Orfeo ed Euridice.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/orfeo-ed-euridice-at-the-metropolitan-opera-review.html', 'snippet': 'David Daniels sings the title role in a Metropolitan Opera revival of the Mark Morris production of Gluck’s “Orfeo ed Euridice.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A renewed sense of loss is inevitable when the Metropolitan Opera revives the Mark Morris production of Gluck’s “Orfeo ed Euridice,” which was originally conceived for the superb mezzo-soprano Lorraine Hunt Lieberson, who died in 2006, 10 months before it was first performed. That sense became even more acute when the production returned on Friday evening, just a week after the death of Hunt Lieberson’s husband, the composer Peter Lieberson, also from cancer. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/orfeo/orfeo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/orfeo/orfeo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/orfeo/orfeo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Underworld Rescue With Dancers and Chorus', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Underworld Rescue With Dancers and Chorus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:12:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/612dea0b-1fbe-5bc9-8259-f1ddb099ed5f', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/612dea0b-1fbe-5bc9-8259-f1ddb099ed5f'}, {'abstract': 'Yuri Bashmet and Evgeny Kissin offered Schubert, Brahms and Shostakovich at Carnegie Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/yuri-bashmet-and-evgeny-kissin-at-carnegie-review.html', 'snippet': 'Yuri Bashmet and Evgeny Kissin offered Schubert, Brahms and Shostakovich at Carnegie Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One may be the loneliest number, but two can be the most revealing. More than other ensembles, musical duos suggest narratives: rival siblings, say, or a blissful romance. But at a duet recital on Thursday evening at Carnegie Hall, the image that came to mind was of a couple out to dinner, each partner talking intently on his cellphone.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Viola and a Piano in Search of Common Ground', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Viola and a Piano in Search of Common Ground', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bashmet, Yuri', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kissin, Evgeny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:13:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32928383-5d16-5b46-9be9-d41b40c5083c', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32928383-5d16-5b46-9be9-d41b40c5083c'}, {'abstract': 'Ivo Dimchev, the Bulgarian-born artist, uses blood (his own) as a means of expression in “Som Faves” in the Performance Mix Festival at Dixon Place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/dance/ivo-dimchev-at-performance-mix-festival-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ivo Dimchev, the Bulgarian-born artist, uses blood (his own) as a means of expression in “Som Faves” in the Performance Mix Festival at Dixon Place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Friday night a recording of Ivo Dimchev’s voice preceded his appearance in “Som Faves” with some words about the performance to follow: “My body will lose energy”; “We are all going to lose time”; and “Somebody will even lose his own blood.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/festival/festival-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/festival/festival-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/festival/festival-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stage Where the Blood Is Real and Freshly Drawn', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stage Where the Blood Is Real and Freshly Drawn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eagly, Ursula', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:15:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/257275cf-58a9-56bf-82a7-cd0fc1274b14', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/257275cf-58a9-56bf-82a7-cd0fc1274b14'}, {'abstract': '“The Crowds at El Morocco,” a retrospective of songs reminiscent of cafe society and Manhattan nightlife, was performed by Billy Stritch, James Naughton, Karen Ziemba and La Tanya Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/the-crowds-at-el-morocco-at-92nd-street-y-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Crowds at El Morocco,” a retrospective of songs reminiscent of cafe society and Manhattan nightlife, was performed by Billy Stritch, James Naughton, Karen Ziemba and La Tanya Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Copacabana, the Latin Quarter, El Morocco, the Stork Club, the Blue Angel and on and on: nowadays these fabled names tend to dissolve into a hazy, undifferentiated fantasy of glamour, cafe society and Manhattan nightlife before television arrived to change everything. And on Saturday evening the mists shrouding a world that is remembered largely by word of mouth and vintage live recordings were dissipated at the 92nd St. Y’s new Lyrics & Lyricists show, “The Crowd’s at El Morocco: The Heyday of the New York Nightclubs.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/lyrics/lyrics-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 489, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunes for the Swank Who’d Rather Cha-Cha', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunes for the Swank Who’d Rather Cha-Cha', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stritch, Billy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Naughton, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ziemba, Karen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:16:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dadd95ec-1b87-5165-b671-4d2c91c761e6', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dadd95ec-1b87-5165-b671-4d2c91c761e6'}, {'abstract': 'The baritone Thomas Hampson sang selections from George Crumb’s “Six American Songbooks” in a concert presented by the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/george-crumb-selections-from-chamber-music-society-review.html', 'snippet': 'The baritone Thomas Hampson sang selections from George Crumb’s “Six American Songbooks” in a concert presented by the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sarcasm is the prevailing mode in contemporary music about the American experience. Since many talented composers are dubious about the country and its history, their well-meaning attempts to grapple with them musically can easily slide into self-righteous critique. It may not be wrong, but it’s not interesting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/chamber/chamber-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/chamber/chamber-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/chamber/chamber-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Songs of America, With Beauty and Gusto', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Songs of America, With Beauty and Gusto', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hampson, Thomas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crumb, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Li