revived by the Metropolitan Playhouse. “It’s unconstitutional!” yells another, the president of a mortgage company.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Depression-Era Protest Ripped From the Headlines', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'One-Third of a Nation'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Depression-Era Protest Ripped From the Headlines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roe, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:19:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbdb9a17-6303-5d4d-8a3c-7d7be0be9eec', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbdb9a17-6303-5d4d-8a3c-7d7be0be9eec'}, {'abstract': 'Albert Brooks, actor, screenwriter and director, tries his hand at writing a novel, one that looks ahead to what awaits Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/books/albert-brookss-2030-his-first-novel-review.html', 'snippet': 'Albert Brooks, actor, screenwriter and director, tries his hand at writing a novel, one that looks ahead to what awaits Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his most recent film, “Looking for Comedy in the Muslim World,” Albert Brooks can be seen as a stand-up comic trying to entertain an audience in India. He tells a few jokes. Nobody laughs. He wonders why. Then he has the bright idea that maybe the crowd simply can’t follow him. How many people in the auditorium understand English, he asks? Every person in the audience raises a hand.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/book-jump/book-jump-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/book-jump/book-jump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/book-jump/book-jump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Wry Eye on Problems of the Future', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Comfort In Worrywart’s Crystal Ball', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Albert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:26:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/41569f3a-1c50-56c7-b370-c82fae041444', 'word_count': 1063, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41569f3a-1c50-56c7-b370-c82fae041444'}, {'abstract': 'Geir Helgemo’s six-diamond bid threw his partner for a loop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/crosswords/bridge/02card.html', 'snippet': 'Geir Helgemo’s six-diamond bid threw his partner for a loop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Cavendish Invitational tournament starts on Wednesday in Henderson, Nev., on the outskirts of Las Vegas. The first two days feature the John Roberts Teams, won last year by Pierre Zimmermann, Franck Multon, Michel and Thomas Bessis (father and son), Geir Helgemo and Tor Helness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting a Brash 2010 Slam', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting a Brash 2010 Slam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:29:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2be20ee-b93d-5833-894c-28e1b00bbfdd', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2be20ee-b93d-5833-894c-28e1b00bbfdd'}, {'abstract': 'But will the Tea Party agree?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/01/faminedeath-2012/', 'snippet': 'But will the Tea Party agree?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Johann Hari on Donald Trump and the GOP:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Famine/Death 2012!', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c80160b-cb3b-5783-b720-5bf36232f96e', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c80160b-cb3b-5783-b720-5bf36232f96e'}, {'abstract': 'The Confederacy declares war on the United States, Maryland calls a secession convention, and Lincoln and Scott lay down the law.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/01/maryland-my-maryland/', 'snippet': 'The Confederacy declares war on the United States, Maryland calls a secession convention, and Lincoln and Scott lay down the law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'April 27 – May 4, 1861', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maryland, My Maryland', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie Malanowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malanowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ac2bd01-13c0-5832-adef-9db41321ed07', 'word_count': 1790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ac2bd01-13c0-5832-adef-9db41321ed07'}, {'abstract': 'Known for his Beethoven, the English pianist Paul Lewis is now in the midst of an ambitious consideration of Schubert, whose work he played on Friday night at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/paul-lewis-plays-schubert-at-metropolitan-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'Known for his Beethoven, the English pianist Paul Lewis is now in the midst of an ambitious consideration of Schubert, whose work he played on Friday night at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is nothing flashy or even very charismatic about the shy-looking, bushy-haired English pianist Paul Lewis. Still Mr. Lewis, 38, has steadily gained a well-deserved reputation as an accomplished and exceptionally thoughtful artist. So a sizable audience turned out for his recital on Friday night at the Grace Rainey Rogers Auditorium of the Metropolitan Museum of Art. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/lewis/lewis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 812, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/lewis/lewis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/02/arts/lewis/lewis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beethoven Devotee Makes Some Keyboard Time for Schubert', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beethoven Devotee Makes Some Keyboard Time for Schubert', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schubert, Franz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:35:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7ae3195-c29f-51e5-a9e7-099974f48619', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7ae3195-c29f-51e5-a9e7-099974f48619'}, {'abstract': 'This year City Center’s Encores! series and Jazz at Lincoln Center will present a tribute to Ellington’s performances at the Cotton Club.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/music/new-series-to-honor-duke-ellington.html', 'snippet': 'This year City Center’s Encores! series and Jazz at Lincoln Center will present a tribute to Ellington’s performances at the Cotton Club.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Duke Ellington, above, brought dance, music and variety shows to the Cotton Club in Harlem in the 1920s and ’30s. This year City Center’s Encores! series and Jazz at Lincoln Center will present “Cotton Club Parade,” a tribute to Ellington’s performances there, and the first in a new series of musical theater and jazz collaborations from the organizations. “Cotton Club Parade” will have six performances at City Center, Nov. 18 to 22, directed by Warren Carlyle (“Finian’s Rainbow”), with musical direction by Wynton Marsalis and featuring the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra. The series will run every two years, with the next production scheduled at the Rose Theater at Jazz at Lincoln Center during its 2013-14 season. Tickets for “Cotton Club Parade” can be purchased through City Center beginning Aug. 15.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Series to Honor Duke Ellington', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Series to Honor Duke Ellington', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellington, Duke', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:37:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4a53b31-6103-5cd0-ae28-a189d85a97d5', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4a53b31-6103-5cd0-ae28-a189d85a97d5'}, {'abstract': '“Mike Birbiglia’s My Girlfriend’s Boyfriend” extends, and Branden Jacobs-Jenkins has been named the winner of the Paula Vogel playwriting award.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/theater/footnotes.html', 'snippet': '“Mike Birbiglia’s My Girlfriend’s Boyfriend” extends, and Branden Jacobs-Jenkins has been named the winner of the Paula Vogel playwriting award.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Mike Birbiglia’s My Girlfriend’s Boyfriend” has extended its run at the Barrow Street Theater. The sad but humorous love life of Mr. Birbiglia, below, a “This American Life” and “Moth” favorite, will play through June 25. Mr. Birbiglia’s 2008 show, “Sleepwalk With Me,” ran for eight months at the Bleecker Street Theater, was turned into a book and CD, and is being made into a movie. ... Branden Jacobs-Jenkins has been named the winner of the Paula Vogel playwriting award, the Vineyard Theater said. Ms. Vogel will present the award, which includes a residency at the Vineyard as well as a prize of $3,000, to Mr. Jacobs-Jenkins at a luncheon on June 9. Mr. Jacobs-Jenkins is the author of “Neighbors,” which played at the Public Theater last spring, and “The Octoroon: An Adaptation of The Octoroon Based on The Octoroon,” which was produced at Performance Space 122 last summer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnotes', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnotes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Birbiglia, Mike', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs-Jenkins, Branden', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:38:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/05cd8cbf-439d-5629-b0b4-5022f8628aea', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05cd8cbf-439d-5629-b0b4-5022f8628aea'}, {'abstract': 'The North American box office came roaring back to life over the weekend as “Fast Five” opened in first place to an estimated $83.6 million in ticket sales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/movies/fast-five-vrooms-to-no-1-at-box-office.html', 'snippet': 'The North American box office came roaring back to life over the weekend as “Fast Five” opened in first place to an estimated $83.6 million in ticket sales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The North American box office came roaring back to life over the weekend as “Fast Five” (Universal Pictures), with Vin Diesel and Dwayne Johnson, from left above, opened in first place to an estimated $83.6 million in ticket sales. An animated holdover, 20th Century Fox’s “Rio,” was second with $14.4 million for a three-week total of $103.6 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles ticketing statistics. “Tyler Perry’s Madea’s Big Happy Family” (Lionsgate) was third with $10.5 million, for a two-week total of $41.1 million. “Water for Elephants” (Fox) was fourth, selling $9.1 million for a two-week total of $32.3 million. “Prom,” a low-budget comedy from Walt Disney Studios, failed to gain traction in its opening weekend, placing fifth with about $5 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Fast Five’ Vrooms to No. 1 at Box Office', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Fast Five’ Vrooms to No. 1 at Box Office', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:39:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/04454f60-05b6-58e9-9030-f20f45c2e57e', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04454f60-05b6-58e9-9030-f20f45c2e57e'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Ferguson has taken on his first nondocumentary project with HBO Films, directing a movie about Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, according to The Hollywood Reporter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/movies/ferguson-to-direct-film-about-wikileaks-founder.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Ferguson has taken on his first nondocumentary project with HBO Films, directing a movie about Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, according to The Hollywood Reporter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Ferguson has taken on his first nondocumentary project with HBO Films, directing a movie about Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, according to The Hollywood Reporter. Mr. Ferguson won an Oscar this year for his documentary “Inside Job,” about the origins of the 2008 financial crisis, and received a nomination for his 2007 film “No End In Sight,” about the American occupation in Iraq. Mr. Assange has been embroiled in controversy since November, when WikiLeaks made public confidential diplomatic cables from the State Department. Shortly afterward, he was arrested in Britain on charges of sexual abuse in Sweden; he has appealed a decision allowing his extradition to Sweden. He is also under investigation by the United States Justice Department. Other films about Mr. Assange, below, and WikiLeaks are in the works, The Los Angeles Times reported. DreamWorks has optioned several memoirs about Mr. Assange and WikiLeaks; Universal has a documentary by Alex Gibney (“Enron: The Smartest Guys in the Room”) in production; and Annapurna Productions has said it is financing a film to be produced, and possibly written by, Mark Boal, whose “Hurt Locker” won him two Oscars in 2009, for original screenplay and best film.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ferguson to Direct Film About WikiLeaks Founder', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ferguson to Direct Film About WikiLeaks Founder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferguson, Charles H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assange, Julian P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:40:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d0beda2-2c2f-5788-82e0-52dde519782b', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d0beda2-2c2f-5788-82e0-52dde519782b'}, {'abstract': 'In the long run, some of us aren’t getting it.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/01/on-not-reading-keynes/', 'snippet': 'In the long run, some of us aren’t getting it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been staying out of the truly stupid debate about whether Keynes advocated central planning; folks, this is not an issue to be settled by trawling his works for gotcha quotes. Keynes had a model, laid out at length in his General Theory; it was a model of how an otherwise workable market economy can suffer from inadequate demand, and how government can play a useful role by pumping up demand to achieve full employment. This model is, as Keynes himself said, moderately conservative in its implications.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Not Reading Keynes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:41:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f64da76f-9595-5ab8-8a9c-5f1e40c171d0', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f64da76f-9595-5ab8-8a9c-5f1e40c171d0'}, {'abstract': 'The wedding of Prince William and Kate Middleton on Friday was a snooze for most Americans, according to television audience estimates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/television/mixed-television-ratings-for-the-royal-wedding.html', 'snippet': 'The wedding of Prince William and Kate Middleton on Friday was a snooze for most Americans, according to television audience estimates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wedding of Prince William and Kate Middleton on Friday was a snooze for most Americans, according to television audience estimates. The Nielsen Company said that an average of 22.7 million viewers at home in the United States watched the wedding live (between 6 a.m. and 7:15 a.m. Eastern daylight time). That figure, encompassing viewers of 11 channels, reaffirmed pre-wedding polls that found only lukewarm interest in the nuptials among most Americans. Still, millions did tune in, leading to a record ratings day for some television channels. Between 4 a.m. and 10 a.m., when the wedding coverage was at its peak, CNN had an average of 1.66 million viewers, its biggest audience since the day after the presidential election in 2008. Between 7 a.m. and 9 a.m. NBC’s “Today” had an average of more than 9.6 million viewers, its biggest audience since the day after the presidential election in 2000. “Good Morning America” on ABC had an average of 8.65 million viewers, its biggest audience in at least 20 years. CBS’s “Early Show” averaged 3.91 million viewers. The Nielsen ratings count only viewers at home, so it is unknown how many wedding aficionados gathered for viewing parties.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mixed Television Ratings for the Royal Wedding', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mixed Television Ratings For the Royal Wedding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'William, Duke of Cambridge', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Catherine, Duchess of Cambridge', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:41:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c45bc4cc-4dfe-5ac9-b0b8-fb87fec37ff9', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c45bc4cc-4dfe-5ac9-b0b8-fb87fec37ff9'}, {'abstract': 'The latest book from R. L. Stine, the author of the “Goosebumps” children’s books, will be turned into a family-adventure feature film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/movies/goosebumps-book-to-be-a-feature-film.html', 'snippet': 'The latest book from R. L. Stine, the author of the “Goosebumps” children’s books, will be turned into a family-adventure feature film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest book from R. L. Stine, the author of the “Goosebumps” children’s books, will be turned into a family-adventure feature film. According to The Hollywood Reporter, Gotham Group, a management and production company, has optioned the rights to the book, “It’s the First Day of School ... Forever,” about a 12-year-old boy whose first, horrible day of school keeps repeating itself. The book won’t be released until July, and a writer and studio have not been determined for the film. Mr. Stine’s “Goosebumps” and “Haunting Hour” series have already been adapted for television; in an interview with moviefone.com Mr. Stine said that his adult novel “The Sitter” was also being developed as a feature film under the title “The Au Pair,” to be produced by Sam Raimi’s Ghost House Pictures.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'R.L. Stine’s Lastest Book to Be a Feature Film', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Goosebumps’ Book To Be a Feature Film', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stine, R L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gotham Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:42:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/234f3315-d6a3-59ac-a9de-3409460c3e92', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/234f3315-d6a3-59ac-a9de-3409460c3e92'}, {'abstract': 'Moviegoers at the Tribeca Film Festival chose “Give Up Tomorrow” as the winner of the Heineken Audience Award for best feature film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/movies/tribeca-audience-chooses-philippine-documentary.html', 'snippet': 'Moviegoers at the Tribeca Film Festival chose “Give Up Tomorrow” as the winner of the Heineken Audience Award for best feature film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Moviegoers at the Tribeca Film Festival chose “Give Up Tomorrow” as the winner of the Heineken Audience Award for best feature film at a ceremony in New York on Saturday night. The film’s director, Michael Collins, an alumnus of the Tribeca Film Institute’s All Access program, which supports filmmakers from underrepresented groups, will receive $25,000 as part of the prize. “Give Up Tomorrow” is a documentary that follows the case of Paco Larranaga, who was arrested in 1997 and accused of killing two teenage sisters on the island of Cebu in the Philippines, despite overwhelming evidence of his innocence.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tribeca Audience Chooses Philippine Documentary', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tribeca Audience Chooses Philippine Documentary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tribeca Film Festival (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:43:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e94807c-201a-5e87-847f-c331a2469cd9', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e94807c-201a-5e87-847f-c331a2469cd9'}, {'abstract': 'When Dave Duerson committed suicide, he shot himself in the chest rather than the head, so his brain could be examined for a degenerative disease caused by repetitive brain trauma.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/sports/football/02duerson.html', 'snippet': 'When Dave Duerson committed suicide, he shot himself in the chest rather than the head, so his brain could be examined for a degenerative disease caused by repetitive brain trauma.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question of whether the former Chicago Bear Dave Duerson had football-related brain damage when he killed himself in February will be answered Monday morning, when Boston University researchers announce autopsy results.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/01/sports/football/0duersonpix/0duersonpix-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/01/sports/football/0duersonpix/0duersonpix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/01/sports/football/0duersonpix/0duersonpix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Duerson Findings Could Shape Debate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Duerson Findings Could Shape Debate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duerson, Dave', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-01T21:47:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Schwarz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwarz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e35d7f8-e79a-59c8-a00e-fc2e32ee6714', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e35d7f8-e79a-59c8-a00e-fc2e32ee6714'}, {'abstract': 'Gabriel Mvumvure of Louisiana State and Ngonidzashe Makusha of Florida State, from an unlikely home for sprinters, have run to the forefront of college track and field.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/sports/02track.html', 'snippet': 'Gabriel Mvumvure of Louisiana State and Ngonidzashe Makusha of Florida State, from an unlikely home for sprinters, have run to the forefront of college track and field.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Philadelphia', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/02/sports/YTRACK/YTRACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/02/sports/YTRACK/YTRACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '