sed news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/monday-reading-budget-airlines-in-small-cities/', 'snippet': 'Budget airlines that link small cities with vacation spots, sites like Jewelmint that offer personalized online shopping, financing a vacation home and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Reading: Budget Airlines in Small Cities', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T13:43:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/64ad4f84-8f33-58b3-8883-17c3846f3bb6', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64ad4f84-8f33-58b3-8883-17c3846f3bb6'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers ask for reader input in the development of an online tool intended to provide realistic medical prognoses.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/figuring-the-odds/', 'snippet': 'Researchers ask for reader input in the development of an online tool intended to provide realistic medical prognoses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What if your doctor, making use of a Web site that collected a number of tested geriatric scales, could enter information about your history and your health, and then predict with reasonable accuracy your odds of living another year, or four, or nine?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Figuring the Odds', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T13:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbc9d737-5a79-57bf-9ae0-13416d6a2f47', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbc9d737-5a79-57bf-9ae0-13416d6a2f47'}, {'abstract': 'For someone trying to tap into the sublimity of a true gimlet, you’ve got to make your own lime cordial.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/case-study-building-a-better-mixer/', 'snippet': 'For someone trying to tap into the sublimity of a true gimlet, you’ve got to make your own lime cordial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you entreat a conscientious bartender to make you a gimlet these days, he frequently does something that is at once resolutely incorrect and completely forgivable: he makes you a drink containing gin, simple syrup and fresh lime juice. I’ve done it many times myself. Depending on whom you talk to, that might be a type of sour or it might be a type of fix, but it almost certainly is not a gimlet. A proper gimlet is made with lime cordial. And what most of the world knows as lime cordial is Rose’s, that dusty, fluorescent yellow-green bottle in the drinks section of your supermarket. I am willing to bet that at some point in its very long history Mr. Lauchlin Rose’s Cordial Mixer Lime Juice may have been a bracing product, redolent of the fruit that it promises. Sadly, its current incarnation is to limes as Spam is to steak. It tastes of the high-fructose corn syrup, stabilizers and various chemicals that give it its cesium-like shelf life, which is all fine for Cadbury, which has owned the brand since 1981, but is bitter gruel for someone trying to tap into the sublimity of a true gimlet. For that, as for many such rich rewards, you’ve got to make your own.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Case Study | Building a Better Mixer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toby Cecchini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cecchini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4633f7a9-70ad-5ec9-b7de-545d7a704b7f', 'word_count': 1415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4633f7a9-70ad-5ec9-b7de-545d7a704b7f'}, {'abstract': 'The feelings released by the news of a firefight thousands of miles away proved as complicated as these times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/us/03mood.html', 'snippet': 'The feelings released by the news of a firefight thousands of miles away proved as complicated as these times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, the sworn enemy of this American generation had been cornered and killed by our determined special forces over there, ending a decade-long hunt for the villain who had altered our way of life. Here, it seemed, was our moment to plant a celebratory kiss on a nurse or soldier in Times Square, to chant “U.S.A., U.S.A., U.S.A.” until dawn — to see the faint outline of a better tomorrow.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mix of Emotion Stored for a Decade', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Singular Moment, And a Mix of Emotion Stored for a Decade', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:04:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e86ad744-472d-510f-97e0-acc87df6f0ea', 'word_count': 1550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e86ad744-472d-510f-97e0-acc87df6f0ea'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/what-were-reading-173/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:08:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/89ef5921-a96c-50dd-9596-47cfd87be902', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89ef5921-a96c-50dd-9596-47cfd87be902'}, {'abstract': 'N.F.L. owners and players remained in a holding pattern, as a decision by a federal appeals court in St. Louis expected early this week will largely determine what the off-season looks like.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/sports/football/03nfl.html', 'snippet': 'N.F.L. owners and players remained in a holding pattern, as a decision by a federal appeals court in St. Louis expected early this week will largely determine what the off-season looks like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'N.F.L. owners and players remained in a holding pattern Monday, as a federal appeals court considered the league’s request for a full stay of the injunction that lifted the seven-week-old lockout last week. The lockout went back in place Friday night, when the appeals court granted the league a temporary stay, buying the three-judge panel more time to consider arguments by both sides. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Mulls N.F.L. Bid for a Full Stay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Court Mulls N.F.L. Bid for a Full Stay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35a9d9b8-82d5-54c7-aceb-268dafae7703', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35a9d9b8-82d5-54c7-aceb-268dafae7703'}, {'abstract': 'When President Obama last week chided critics for the “silliness” over his birth certificate, he was two days away from ordering the raid on Bin Laden’s compound.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/obamas-remarks-on-birth-certificate-controversy-take-on-new-meaning/', 'snippet': 'When President Obama last week chided critics for the “silliness” over his birth certificate, he was two days away from ordering the raid on Bin Laden’s compound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Wednesday, President Obama concluded his remarks about the release of his birth certificate by chiding his critics — and the press — with words that now seem far more ominous after the United States raid that killed Osama bin Laden.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama’s Remarks on Birth Certificate Take on New Meaning', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:17:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0093c18-0ed6-5e9e-8d54-b3f3f75f1e3f', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0093c18-0ed6-5e9e-8d54-b3f3f75f1e3f'}, {'abstract': 'One commonly overlooked element of sustainability is repurposing as much food waste as possible.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/the-school-of-every-last-scrap/', 'snippet': 'One commonly overlooked element of sustainability is repurposing as much food waste as possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Monday’s paper, I write about efforts to make tilapia farming more sustainable. Americans consume 475 million pounds of that fish a year, and careless aquaculture can damage the environment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The School of Every Last Scrap', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honduras', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:36:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/366c04da-8563-5c55-a809-4cbcc356636d', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366c04da-8563-5c55-a809-4cbcc356636d'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/morning-take-out-234/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOP STORIES', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:46:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6da86e39-3b73-56c3-a9af-e8be074f9c42', 'word_count': 2124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6da86e39-3b73-56c3-a9af-e8be074f9c42'}, {'abstract': 'A recent road trip in Florida provided the perfect excuse to explore roadside motels.', 'web_url': 'https://frugaltraveler.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/in-florida-do-tell-motels/', 'snippet': 'A recent road trip in Florida provided the perfect excuse to explore roadside motels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American roadside motels – those metastasized ranch houses with parking spaces right outside each room entrance, are the opposite of chic. But staying at them was just about the only way I could hope to keep lodging down to my $60-a-night budget during my jaunt down the Atlantic coast of north Florida. In five nights, I stayed in four motels, three of which turned out to be comfortable and clean enough to satisfy any budget traveler. A dose of lost-in-time real America was just a bonus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Florida, Do-Tell Motels', 'kicker': 'Frugal Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:49:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Kugel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kugel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0143622a-1a89-56ef-b3df-483704739fdc', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0143622a-1a89-56ef-b3df-483704739fdc'}, {'abstract': 'Owners complained that the low-beam headlights on their vehicles stopped working, but no accidents were reported to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/288000-honda-cr-vs-under-investigation-for-headlight-failure/', 'snippet': 'Owners complained that the low-beam headlights on their vehicles stopped working, but no accidents were reported to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:34 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '288,000 Honda CR-Vs Under Investigation for Headlight Failure', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:57:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b877e0e5-a2e9-59be-9018-b2463da980d2', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b877e0e5-a2e9-59be-9018-b2463da980d2'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Sheindlin, who has headed up “Judge Judy” for the last 15 years, has signed a new contract that will keep her on the show through 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/more-time-in-court-for-judge-judy/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Sheindlin, who has headed up “Judge Judy” for the last 15 years, has signed a new contract that will keep her on the show through 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nation’s No. 1 television judge, Judy Sheindlin, has decided to continue settling disputes for at least the next four years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Time in Court for Judge Judy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheindlin, Judy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T14:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/adf14d07-99a3-598e-a823-41e7c49c67c0', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adf14d07-99a3-598e-a823-41e7c49c67c0'}, {'abstract': 'If Toby’s Corner knows he’s headed to the most important thoroughbred race in North America, he doesn’t show it.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/diary-of-a-contender-tobys-corner/', 'snippet': 'If Toby’s Corner knows he’s headed to the most important thoroughbred race in North America, he doesn’t show it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe and Sean Clancy, the publishers of Steeplechase Times and the Saratoga Special, have chronicled the daily life and routine of the Kentucky Derby contender Toby’s Corner, winner of the Wood Memorial. He had his legs soaked in a cold saltwater spa on Sunday at the Fair Hill training center in Elkton, Md., which offers as close to European-styled training as there is in America and was the home to 2006 