doomed the Japanese nuclear plant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trait that doomed the Fukushima reactors might not be so obvious, the chairman of the Nuclear Regulatory Commission told a mostly skeptical audience at a forum organized by an anti-nuclear group on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Will the N.R.C. Learn From Fukushima?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:12:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcae8288-0767-5a0d-98a8-569875083f95', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcae8288-0767-5a0d-98a8-569875083f95'}, {'abstract': 'The East Village shop will be under new ownership.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/bluebird-coffees-owners-move-on/', 'snippet': 'The East Village shop will be under new ownership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bluebird Coffee Shop’s owners have sold the store. I first set foot in the tiny coffee shop in the East Village early last year, when I was researching an article on where you could (finally) get a cappuccino or brewed-to-order coffee in New York on par with what you find in San Francisco or Portland, Ore. The coffee at Bluebird Coffee Shop was great, and when the head barista, Katie Duris, was on her game, it could be exquisite. I named it as one of the 10 “most notable” establishments in the city. For diehard coffee nuts, it was a place of pilgrimage. For the neighborhood, it was the next-door beauty who just happened to be a star.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headli