9fa78', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b5aa343-3b19-5e69-aaec-859e7be9fa78'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine exhaustively covered 9/11 and the wars that followed — the wars, especially, with in-depth reports from Iraq and Afghanistan as well as coverage of policy debates in Washington and leading trends in South Asia and what the State Department calls MENA (Middle East/North Africa). But Bin Laden only occasionally wafted into view.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/the-magazine-on-osama-bin-laden/', 'snippet': 'The magazine exhaustively covered 9/11 and the wars that followed — the wars, especially, with in-depth reports from Iraq and Afghanistan as well as coverage of policy debates in Washington and leading trends in South Asia and what the State Depar...', 'lead_paragraph': 'What did we say about Osama bin Laden? The magazine exhaustively covered 9/11 and the wars that followed — the wars, especially, with in-depth reports from Iraq and Afghanistan as well as coverage of policy debates in Washington and leading trends in South Asia and what the State Department calls MENA (Middle East/North Africa). But Bin Laden only occasionally wafted into view. In the first few months after 9/11, he was omnipresent — Andrew Sullivan, for example, filed an article late that month that talked about how it was a mistake to deny that the conflict we were in was “about religion.” His main evidence was the statements of Osama bin laden, which were then forthcoming in abundance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Magazine on Osama bin Laden', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:35:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Malcomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malcomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/55420077-efa9-502c-bda6-cf7e50b85477', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55420077-efa9-502c-bda6-cf7e50b85477'}, {'abstract': 'The Morning Line starts off with questions about the quality of the field for Saturday’s Kentucky Derby.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/the-morning-line-slow-slower-slowest/', 'snippet': 'The Morning Line starts off with questions about the quality of the field for Saturday’s Kentucky Derby.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks for rejoining us at the Morning Line on The Rail where we try to summarize each day’s news coverage of the Triple Crown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: Slow, Slower, Slowest', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:35:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/59a092bd-d3f7-57c6-ac40-6c926fde5d7d', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59a092bd-d3f7-57c6-ac40-6c926fde5d7d'}, {'abstract': 'The English-language site was dominated by Bin Laden news but made space for one of its own missing journalists.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/media-react-al-jazeera-on-bin-ladens-death/', 'snippet': 'The English-language site was dominated by Bin Laden news but made space for one of its own missing journalists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By midday the headline on Al Jazeera’s English-language Web site read “Osama bin Laden killed by US forces” and all 10 of the most-viewed headlines on the Web site were about the killing of Bin Laden. The network live-streamed its broadcast on the Web site, including interviews with heads of state from around the world and a live press conference from the White House.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Media React: Al Jazeera on Bin Laden’s Death', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL JAZEERA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:40:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3623c9a0-3a82-533f-a468-79ac865033a9', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3623c9a0-3a82-533f-a468-79ac865033a9'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time on record, most Americans say they do not believe today’s youth will have better lives than their parents, according to new survey data from Gallup.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/dimming-optimism-for-todays-youth/', 'snippet': 'For the first time on record, most Americans say they do not believe today’s youth will have better lives than their parents, according to new survey data from Gallup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe it has to do with sky-high levels of youth unemployment. Maybe it’s because student loan levels are climbing. Maybe it’s because today’s young’uns will someday be stuck supporting so many of their elders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dimming Optimism for Today’s Youth', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gallup Poll', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:43:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/937de1fa-bcd8-516a-a045-5a10e3c5c065', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/937de1fa-bcd8-516a-a045-5a10e3c5c065'}, {'abstract': 'Nevada’s Secretary of State rules that a special election to replace Representative Dean Heller, about to be appointed to the Senate, should be open to all candidates.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/nevada-official-rules-special-election-must-be-open/', 'snippet': 'Nevada’s Secretary of State rules that a special election to replace Representative Dean Heller, about to be appointed to the Senate, should be open to all candidates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A special election to replace Representative Dean Heller, a Nevada Republican about to be appointed to the United States Senate, should be open to all candidates and not just those selected by the two political parties, Nevada’s Secretary of State ruled on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nevada Official Rules Special Election Must be Open', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Angle, Sharron E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heller, Dean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:47:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f779221c-1944-52e9-b6b3-448ed8fe32e8', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f779221c-1944-52e9-b6b3-448ed8fe32e8'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists working in the western Antarctic have found the densest populations of whale and krill ever recorded.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/science/03obwhales.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists working in the western Antarctic have found the densest populations of whale and krill ever recorded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists doing observations in the Wilhelmina Bay in the western Antarctic have found an estimated two million tons of krill, and more than 300 humpback whales feeding on them. It is the densest population of these whales ever recorded, more than 15 per square mile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Antarctic Buffet Where Whales Gather to Feast', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Antarctic Buffet Where Whales Gather to Feast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Krill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-02T21:49:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4fa441a-998e-5b29-8f88-18b79a3ad809', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4fa441a-998e-5b29-8f88-18b79a3ad809'}, {'abstract': 'The Festival of Ideas for the New City, which begins on Wednesday at the New Museum, includes an art installation rendered upon security gates along the Bowery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/02/arts/design/20110503-festival-ss.html', 'snippet': 'The Festival of Ideas for the New City, which begins on Wednesday at the New Museum, includes an art installation rendered upon security gates along the Bowery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Festival of Ideas for the New City, which begins on Wednesday at the New Museum, includes an art installation rendered upon security gates along the Bowery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,