d training this summer in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/us/politics/03brown.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Scott P. Brown, Republican of Massachusetts, asked to conduct his annual National Guard training this summer in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — Senator Scott P. Brown of Massachusetts announced Monday that he had asked to conduct his annual training as a member of the state’s Army National Guard this summer in Afghanistan. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/us/BROWN/BROWN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/us/BROWN/BROWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/03/us/BROWN/BROWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Senator, a Guardsman, Seeks Afghanistan Stint', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senator, a Guardsman, Seeks Afghanistan Stint', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reserves (Military)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Guard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T00:19:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b318aa91-c2b2-5dc4-85df-8b570922bb0b', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b318aa91-c2b2-5dc4-85df-8b570922bb0b'}, {'abstract': 'Is the world a safer place with Osama bin Laden dead?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/opinion/03nocera.html', 'snippet': 'Is the world a safer place with Osama bin Laden dead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To give the devil his awful due, Osama bin Laden was the greatest terrorist of the modern age. He took what had been disparate, disorganized terrorist groups and reshaped them into a disciplined and immensely ambitious organization, Al Qaeda, with the singular goal of waging jihad on the West in general and the United States in particular. Its terrorist prowess was never more evident than on that horrible day of Sept. 11, 2001.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '4 Questions He Leaves Behind', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '4 Questions He Leaves Behind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T00:20:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d0edd29-19bc-536c-917e-5d435285e32c', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d0edd29-19bc-536c-917e-5d435285e32c'}, {'abstract': 'The team’s owners said the city must approve, finance and build a new arena if it wants the team to stay past the 2011-12 season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/sports/basketball/03nba.html', 'snippet': 'The team’s owners said the city must approve, finance and build a new arena if it wants the team to stay past the 2011-12 season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sacramento will hold onto the Kings for one more season, but its chances of keeping them long term rest solely on the city’s ability to approve, finance and build a new arena — a goal the city has failed to meet many times in the last decade.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/sports/sub-nba/sub-nba-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 853, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kings Staying Put for Now, but Arena Is Key to Future', 'kicker': 'Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kings Staying Put for Now, But Arena Is Key to Future', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sacramento Kings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T00:22:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/588750f1-72d7-5a7e-b5d8-e28e78f09545', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/588750f1-72d7-5a7e-b5d8-e28e78f09545'}, {'abstract': 'A followup to a column on epistemology, including reader discussion.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/ideas-and-theory-the-political-difference/', 'snippet': 'A followup to a column on epistemology, including reader discussion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last of the 200 comments to my previous column declaring the political impotence of the everything-is-socially-constructed thesis accuses me of assuming that “ideas have no consequences.” Nothing could be further from the truth. “Women have long been marginalized and suppressed by a dominant male culture” is an idea. “Gays have been denied the rights that are theirs as citizens” is an idea. Each of these ideas has been enormously consequential; and each has been the basis of concerted action that altered our political and cultural landscape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ideas and Theory: The Political Difference', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T00:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa8ce69c-6713-54a3-be43-e711a15d1a9e', 'word_count': 1437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa8ce69c-6713-54a3-be43-e711a15d1a9e'}, {'abstract': 'The lawsuit claims that EDMC defrauded the government by illegally paying recruiters based on the number of students they enrolled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/education/03edmc.html', 'snippet': 'The lawsuit claims that EDMC defrauded the government by illegally paying recruiters based on the number of students they enrolled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Justice Department plans to intervene in a whistle-blower lawsuit charging that one of the nation’s largest for-profit college companies, the Education Management Corporation, defrauded the government by illegally paying recruiters based on the number of students they enrolled, according to a Securities and Exchange Commission filing on Monday. ', 'print_