{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/opinion/03osamaoped-art/03osamaoped-art-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/opinion/03osamaoped-art/03osamaoped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/03/opinion/03osamaoped-art/03osamaoped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of the Jihadist Dream', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The End of the Jihadist Dream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zawahri, Ayman Al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Awlaki, Anwar al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T01:23:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By ALI H. SOUFAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ali', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'SOUFAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a594f78-26f7-5468-8832-52cdb156876b', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a594f78-26f7-5468-8832-52cdb156876b'}, {'abstract': 'The death of Osama bin Laden reflects strong leadership and painstaking intelligence work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/opinion/03tue1.html', 'snippet': 'The death of Osama bin Laden reflects strong leadership and painstaking intelligence work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news that Osama bin Laden had been tracked and killed by American forces filled us, and all Americans, with a great sense of relief. But our reaction was strongly tinged with sadness. Nearly a decade after the Sept. 11, 2001, attacks, the horror has not faded, nor has the knowledge of how profoundly our lives were changed. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Long-Awaited News', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Long-Awaited News: The death of Osama bin Laden reflects strong leadership and painstaking intelligence work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T01:28:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5d56db4-8fca-5dbc-9914-c2231392076f', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5d56db4-8fca-5dbc-9914-c2231392076f'}, {'abstract': 'As Bostonians mourned the first Union dead, some heard echoes of an earlier battle.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/shots-heard-round-the-world/', 'snippet': 'As Bostonians mourned the first Union dead, some heard echoes of an earlier battle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three hearses, flag-draped and drawn by horses, rolled through the colonial streets of Boston on May 1. The men of the Sixth Massachusetts — the “noble sons who have given their life’s blood in defense of the laws and maintenance of their country’s honor,” in the words of the next morning’s newspaper — had come home. The cortège, accompanied by Gov. John A. Andrew and by militia cadets marching with their muskets reversed, drew up at last before the staid 18th-century portico of King’s Chapel on Tremont Street. Slowly, the chapel’s great bell, cast long ago in bronze by Paul Revere himself, tolled a dirge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shots Heard ‘Round the World', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Revolutionary War, American (1775-83)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Concord (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T01:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maya Jasanoff and Adam Goodheart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jasanoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodheart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/25fc74a0-1682-500e-a591-b9995ae83417', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25fc74a0-1682-500e-a591-b9995ae83417'}, {'abstract': 'A poll by The New York