d_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a1381a6e-2541-5842-80d2-5e9fc8e4e28d'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the 2011 nominations for Best Play and Best Revival of a Play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/03/theater/theaterspecial/20110503-tonyplays.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the 2011 nominations for Best Play and Best Revival of a Play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at the 2011 nominations for Best Play and Best Revival of a Play.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/theater/theaterspecial/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/03/theater/theaterspecial/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/03/theater/theaterspecial/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7/20110503-tonyplays-slide-SMO7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Nominations: Plays', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T12:57:11+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Tony Awards', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9b87ee2d-c575-59ac-a4f3-40f5ca0eed61', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9b87ee2d-c575-59ac-a4f3-40f5ca0eed61'}, {'abstract': 'I do not think that word “track” means what you think it means.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/headline-inflation-and-wages-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'I do not think that word “track” means what you think it means.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I suspect that this article by Heleen Mees will get a lot of attention from the inflationistas, who will see in it another reason to worry. So I guess I’d better weigh in to say that the, um, core argument doesn’t seem to make any sense.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Headline Inflation and Wages (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T12:57:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/43608cd3-6e1a-5518-b822-bb61967fe61b', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43608cd3-6e1a-5518-b822-bb61967fe61b'}, {'abstract': 'My son, now on a “gap year” in China between high school and college, weighs in on the idea.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/on-the-ground-with-a-gap-year/', 'snippet': 'My son, now on a “gap year” in China between high school and college, weighs in on the idea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve periodically recommended that high school students take a “gap year” after graduation, by deferring college entrance for one year. Most colleges encourage students to take a gap year, partly because students then arrive a little more mature, a little more ready to study, a little more worldly. It’s becoming a bit more common, and Princeton now pays for some kids to travel during their gap year, but most people are still wary of the idea. In fact, almost every kid I’ve talked to who has taken a gap year raves about the idea, so I thought I’d commission a guest column from my eldest son, Gregory, who is currently taking a gap year in China. He spent most of the year studying Chinese at Tsinghua University and then at a language school in Dalian, and soon he’s going to finish up the year studying Spanish in Peru. On the side, he worked and volunteered. Here’s his take:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Ground with a ‘Gap Year’', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc8ec84b-740a-5cb1-b586-549b9e2535fa', 'word_count': 1430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc8ec84b-740a-5cb1-b586-549b9e2535fa'}, {'abstract': 'Why travel when the perfect cup of organic fair-trade Sumatran coffee can come to you?', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/now-renting-traveling-coffee-bar/', 'snippet': 'Why travel when the perfect cup of organic fair-trade Sumatran coffee can come to you?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The quest for the perfect cup of organic fair-trade Sumatran coffee can take you far afield (or at least off the island of Manhattan). But why travel when Melissa Knott of Grounded Coffee Catering will bring her coffee bar to you? Knott will journey anywhere in New York, New Jersey, Connecticut and Pennsylvania where there’s a dedicated electrical outlet and “four clear feet of space” for her folding table and portable Pasquini espresso machine. Along with filter coffee and espresso drinks — including a fantastic decaf — Knott offers loose-leaf black and herbal teas, hot chocolate (made with dairy, soy or coconut milk) and Italian sodas, all organic and fair-trade. She works with a local roaster to create custom blends and has them specially roasted for each event she caters. Since founding her business three and a half years ago, Knott has pulled shots and poured cups everywhere from suburban pool parties to the set of a slasher film on Governors Island. Her dedication to coffee — she wrote her thesis on it — is such that she even checks the water quality of an area before heading out. “I actually prefer to bring my own,” she says. “If the water’s too hard or too soft it really affects the taste of the coffee.” For more on Grounded Coffee Catering, including information on the farmers’ markets where Knott sells her custom roasted beans, go to groundedcatering.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Renting | Traveling Coffee Bar', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b505975e-bcd9-5967-9b48-f66ed5f90a7c', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b505975e-bcd9-5967-9b48-f66ed5f90a7c'}, {'abstract': 'Police in Britain said Tuesday that they had arrested five men under anti-terrorism laws near the Sellafield nuclear power plant in the north of England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/world/europe/04britain.html', 'snippet': 'Police in Britain said Tuesday that they had arrested five men under anti-terrorism laws near the Sellafield nuclear power plant in the north of England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Five men have been arrested near the Sellafield nuclear fuel reprocessing plant in the north of England under antiterrorism laws, the police said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Men Arrested Near Plant In Britain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Five Men Arrested Near Plant In Britain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/330687a9-36dd-59b8-986c-7bad8f0bb2ab', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/330687a9-36dd-59b8-986c-7bad8f0bb2ab'}, {'abstract': 'A year after the suppression of anti-government protests in Bangkok, no official has been charged with a crime related to the deaths of about 90 people, Human Rights Watch said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/world/asia/04thailand.html', 'snippet': 'A year after the suppression of anti-government protests in Bangkok, no official has been charged with a crime related to the deaths of about 90 people, Human Rights Watch said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — A year after the bloody suppression of antigovernment protests in Bangkok, no government official has yet been charged with a crime related to the deaths of about 90 people, including medical workers and others who were apparently the deliberate targets of sniper fire, Human Rights Watch said in a report released Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/world/THAILAND_span/THAILAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/04/world/THAILAND_span/THAILAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/world/THAILAND_span/THAILAND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rights Group Urges Prosecutions in Thai Violence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rights Group Urges Prosecutions in Thai Violence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:14:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Mydans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mydans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d79bfb7-2e99-5564-b0e5-08f51ea38aed', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d79bfb7-2e99-5564-b0e5-08f51ea38aed'}, {'abstract': 'Stability for me, deflation for thee.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/more-reasons-to-say-eeh-when-you-learn-about-the-ecb/', 'snippet': 'Stability for me, deflation for thee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kevin O’Rourke directs us to a very good post that refers to evidence from the gold standard era on the advantages of a little inflation for a currency union — and the problems with tight money. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Reasons to Say “Eeh” When You Learn About the ECB', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:17:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/73c8c5c7-3d91-5528-8d93-b63991d30fab', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73c8c5c7-3d91-5528-8d93-b63991d30fab'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel Radcliffe couldn’t conjure up enough magic to secure a Tony nomination for “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying,” making him one of the most glaring oversights of the morning.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/the-tony-nominations-who-got-snubbed-2/', 'snippet': 'Daniel Radcliffe couldn’t conjure up enough magic to secure a Tony nomination for “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying,” making him one of the most glaring oversights of the morning.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Somewhere, Lord Voldemort is surely smiling while “Harry Potter” fans are heartbroken: Daniel Radcliffe leveraged his years as the star of that boy-wizard franchise to play J. Pierrepont Finch in the current Broadway revival of “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying,” and spent months in vocal and dance training to get up to speed for the role. Yet he couldn’t conjure up enough magic to secure a Tony nomination on Tuesday for a performance that excited many theatergoers and critics, making him one of the most glaring snubs of the morning. Did Tony nominators agree with Ben Brantley, who wrote in The New York Times that Mr. Radcliffe “is clearly not to the musical manner born.” Or was Mr. Radcliffe simply the victim of an evil spell cast by Severus Snape? We may never know.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tony Nominations: Who Got Snubbed This Year?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Radcliffe, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T13:20:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e8cc689-1507-5ce5-81dc-17e5accb6705', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e8cc689-1507-5ce5-81dc-17e5accb6705'}, {'abstract': 'A bestselling author writes an intimate caregiving memoir.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/a-marriage-reshaped-by-stroke/', 'snippet': 'A bestselling author writes an intimate caregiving memoir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another caregiver’s memoir?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caregiver’s Bookshelf: A Marriage Reshaped by Stroke', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major':