ocial)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/77d7463e-2071-5c46-a060-180d51e02833', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/77d7463e-2071-5c46-a060-180d51e02833'}, {'abstract': 'Scott Pelley, the “60 Minutes” correspondent, will succeed Katie Couric as the anchor of the “CBS Evening News” next month — and he will keep working almost full-time on “60 Minutes,” the popular newsmagazine.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/pelley-to-replace-couric-on-evening-news-and-stay-on-60-minutes/', 'snippet': 'Scott Pelley, the “60 Minutes” correspondent, will succeed Katie Couric as the anchor of the “CBS Evening News” next month — and he will keep working almost full-time on “60 Minutes,” the popular newsmagazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scott Pelley, the “60 Minutes” correspondent, will succeed\xa0Katie Couric as the anchor of the “CBS Evening News” next month — and he plans to stay almost full time at his current job.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pelley Talks About New Job on ‘Evening News’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Couric, Katie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelley, Scott', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:11:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3d90176-1c01-5fa0-99cc-f3047098463c', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3d90176-1c01-5fa0-99cc-f3047098463c'}, {'abstract': 'Darren Robbins, the screen printing company’s co-founder, responds to reader comments.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/was-big-d-wise-to-pursue-small-customers/', 'snippet': 'Darren Robbins, the screen printing company’s co-founder, responds to reader comments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, we published a case study that explored whether Big D Custom Screen Printing could become profitable by specializing in small T-shirt orders. After a year in business, the company had sales of $325,000 on which it suffered a nominal loss. Big D’s co-founder, Darren Robbins, and his partner disagreed as to the best way to turn things around. His partner wanted to focus on large corporate accounts. Mr. Robbins believed Big D could thrive by targeting customers with small orders — as small as one T-shirt. The partners split (amicably) over the issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Big D Wise to Pursue Small Customers?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7ee64b4-31bf-5768-8fd5-e3dc760eedb9', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7ee64b4-31bf-5768-8fd5-e3dc760eedb9'}, {'abstract': 'As the Tony Awards nominations on Tuesday show, legitimate dramas are still being written, and they’re being written well.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/this-time-the-tonys-grow-up-and-get-it-right/', 'snippet': 'As the Tony Awards nominations on Tuesday show, legitimate dramas are still being written, and they’re being written well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s this? A full slate of real, original plays – and I mean plays, like mom and dad used to dress up for and would talk about over martinis afterward. Though the Tony nominations announced Tuesday morning didn’t offer many surprises (the hearty, 12-nomination celebration of “Scottsboro Boys” was the only mild shocker), it did confirm some good news. Legitimate dramas are still being written, and they’re being written well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Time, The Tonys Grow Up and Get It Right', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:22:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a348bf4-17cb-529a-901f-78a6afb16b18', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a348bf4-17cb-529a-901f-78a6afb16b18'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Garfinkel, whose theories espoused the mind-sets and behaviorial patterns of jurors, was affiliated with the sociology department at U.C.L.A. for more than 50 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/us/04garfinkel.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Garfinkel, whose theories espoused the mind-sets and behaviorial patterns of jurors, was affiliated with the sociology department at U.C.L.A. for more than 50 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harold Garfinkel, an innovative sociologist who turned the study of common sense into a dense and arcane discipline, creating one of his field’s most challenging and fruitful branches of inquiry, died April 21 at his home in Pacific Palisades, Calif. He was 93.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/nyregion/Garfinkel/Garfinkel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/nyregion/Garfinkel/Garfinkel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/04/nyregion/Garfinkel/Garfinkel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harold Garfinkel, a Common-Sense Sociologist, Dies at 93', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harold Garfinkel, a Common-Sense Sociologist, Dies at 93', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garfinkel, Harold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Los Angeles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jury System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:24:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5c07922-ea3c-51e9-b62f-592b72b69250', 'word_count': 955, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5c07922-ea3c-51e9-b62f-592b72b69250'}, {'abstract': 'Consumers migrated again last month toward fuel-efficient small cars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/business/04auto.html', 'snippet': 'Consumers migrated again last month toward fuel-efficient small cars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Vehicle sales rose 18 percent in April, automakers said Tuesday, as consumers flocked to smaller cars in response to higher fuel prices and improved models from surging American companies.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Demand for Small Cars Pushed April Sales Up 18%', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Demand for Small Cars Pushed April Sales Up 18%', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:30:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb92a238-6731-591c-b67c-536eb180e418', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb92a238-6731-591c-b67c-536eb180e418'}, {'abstract': 'We started with a graphic approach, experimenting with every font in our arsenal.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/the-making-of-a-cover/', 'snippet': 'We started with a graphic approach, experimenting with every font in our arsenal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our cover story on Sunday was about the American soldiers accused of murdering Afghan civilians. Some reports have depicted them as a rogue unit, but our story goes deeper into the psyche of the American soldier and questions whether the killings are symptoms of a much larger issue. Being a weekly, time was a factor, so we executed some staff-produced, alternate versions of the magazine’s cover, two days before it had to ship. We started with a graphic approach, experimenting with every font in our arsenal. We did more than can be featured in this blog post, but the following is a curated depiction of our process for developing a cover. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Making of a Cover', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Typography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T15:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arem Duplessis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duplessis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt