stract': 'The Nook Color electronic reader from Barnes & noble may not have set out to be a tablet, but it seems to be turning into one.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/the-nook-enters-tablet-territory/', 'snippet': 'The Nook Color electronic reader from Barnes & noble may not have set out to be a tablet, but it seems to be turning into one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new system upgrade for the Nook Color e-reader from Barnes & Noble does more than improve its usefulness as an electronic book — it adds new features that you’d normally associate with a tablet. That includes the addition of apps, new e-mail capabilities and improved the social features.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Nook Enters Tablet Territory', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T16:55:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/48d4e9c5-b199-598e-b268-20e5db7ad4a4', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48d4e9c5-b199-598e-b268-20e5db7ad4a4'}, {'abstract': 'Château Trotanoy, overshadowed by its illustrious neighbors and overlooked by its distributor, needed a boost.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/how-to-call-attention-to-a-great-wine/', 'snippet': 'Château Trotanoy, overshadowed by its illustrious neighbors and overlooked by its distributor, needed a boost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My column this week is on a tasting of nine vintages of Château Trotanoy, the excellent Pomerol that is sometimes overlooked with all the attention paid to its superstar stablemate, Château Pétrus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Call Attention to a Great Wine', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T16:59:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc818a35-1a4f-58c6-be25-cfca847b8590', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc818a35-1a4f-58c6-be25-cfca847b8590'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott discusses Corneliu Porumboiu’s film about revolution and memory.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/critics-picks-video-1208-east-of-bucharest/', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott discusses Corneliu Porumboiu’s film about revolution and memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, A. O. Scott looks at a film from Corneliu Porumboiu, one of the directors who has emerged out of the new wave of Romanian cinema. His film, “12:08 East of Bucharest,” looks back at the Romanian revolution of 1989 and asks what the revolution meant to the country and how such a dynamic moment could quickly fade into history. In the film, three characters gather in a drab television studio to commemorate the revolution and to also question whether or not it really happened. Mr. Scott says that one of the elements that makes the film stand out is its “sly, deadpan, understated wit.” Have you seen “12:08 East of Bucharest” or other films from the Romanian new wave? Please share your thoughts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ’12:08 East of Bucharest’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Porumboiu, Corneliu', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Romania', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T17:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d117665-6b24-586e-af5c-87d6a6498f56', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d117665-6b24-586e-af5c-87d6a6498f56'}, {'abstract': 'The golden age of water — unlimited, cheap, safe water — is over, an author argues.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/the-big-thirst-the-future-of-water/', 'snippet': 'The golden age of water — unlimited, cheap, safe water — is over, an author argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Fishman, a longtime writer for Fast Company magazine, is the author of “The Big Thirst,” a new book on water. He previously wrote “The Wal-Mart Effect,” which was an Economist “book of the year” in 2006 and a finalist in The Financial Times’s awards for best business book. Our conversation follows.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "‘The Big Thirst': The Future of Water", 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fishman, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T17:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f73cb904-3761-5f2e-b5a3-565f924c014d', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f73cb904-3761-5f2e-b5a3-565f924c014d'}, {'abstract': 'Demographers called the report a reminder that the population explosion, which helped define global politics in the 20th century, is far from solved in the 21st.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/world/04population.html', 'snippet': 'Demographers called the report a reminder that the population explosion, which helped define global politics in the 20th century, is far from solved in the 21st.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The population of the world, long expected to stabilize just above 9 billion in the middle of the century, will instead keep growing and may hit 10.1 billion by the year 2100, the United Nations projected in a report released Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/world/POPULATION_span/POPULATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/04/world/POPULATION_span/POPULATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/world/POPULATION_span/POPULATION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/