ester Square.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Centuries ago, Leicester Square was a genteel patch, but now it is brash and loud, bordered by an all-night Chinatown and ringed by West End theaters, carousels and street stalls. On weekend evenings, gaggles of young women soused on Magners or caffeinated tonic wine don dangerously short skirts (even in frigid temperatures) and stumble around the hucksters, buskers and neophyte tourists who’ve been sucked in, mothlike, by all that neon. There are good movie theaters here, great soup dumplings, and the square is convenient to just about everything in London. But it is not a traditionally desirable neighborhood for a hotel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Checking In | W Hotel, London', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T18:54:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By STEPHEN HEYMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HEYMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5231ab7f-25c8-5c38-b6c4-977db79eb301', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5231ab7f-25c8-5c38-b6c4-977db79eb301'}, {'abstract': 'Michigan replaces one of its two allotted statues in the Rotunda of the United States Capitol with one of Gerald R. Ford.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/congress-dedicates-statue-of-president-ford-in-rotunda/', 'snippet': 'Michigan replaces one of its two allotted statues in the Rotunda of the United States Capitol with one of Gerald R. Ford.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Presidents Andrew Jackson and Ronald Reagan have new company: On Tuesday, a bronze statue of President Gerald R. Ford was dedicated in the Rotunda of the United States Capitol by Congressional leaders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress Dedicates Statue of President Ford in Rotunda', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T18:59:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a438ade-3a69-50cc-97a5-36955e1bf29f', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a438ade-3a69-50cc-97a5-36955e1bf29f'}, {'abstract': 'It’s very easy to catch “founders disease” and end up relegated to being the chief creative officer with no actual power or influence.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/six-tips-on-taking-outside-investors/', 'snippet': 'It’s very easy to catch “founders disease” and end up relegated to being the chief creative officer with no actual power or influence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having a great idea for a company is one thing. Getting the money to get it moving and growing is quite another — especially when you’ve got an unconventional product or business model that your average investor isn’t likely to recognize as an obvious winner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Six Tips on Taking Outside Investors', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TerraCycle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Trenton (NJ)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-03T19:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name':