settled her case, her lawyer said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lana Lawless, the transgender woman who sued the L.P.G.A. and the Long Drivers of America last October challenging their rule that competitors be “female at birth,” has settled her case, her lawyer said. Lawless, 57, won the world long-drive title in 2008 but was later ruled ineligible after Long Drivers of America, which oversees the competition, changed its rules to bar transgender competitors. She was prevented from competing in the L.P.G.A. because of a similar rule.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Transgender Woman Settles Suit Against Two Organizations', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Golf', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Transgender Woman Settles Suit Against Two Organizations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ladies Professional Golf Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexual', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T00:16:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/86919029-b4dc-57ba-9c9a-bba4d0efba36', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86919029-b4dc-57ba-9c9a-bba4d0efba36'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Campbell, an actor with many film and television credits, was also the first husband of Judith Campbell Exner, a mistress of President John F. Kennedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/arts/television/william-campbell-played-star-trek-klingon-dies-at-87.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Campbell, an actor with many film and television credits, was also the first husband of Judith Campbell Exner, a mistress of President John F. Kennedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Campbell, an actor who was widely familiar to film and television audiences and who was also known as the first husband of Judith Campbell Exner, a mistress of President John F. Kennedy, died on April 28 in Woodland Hills, Calif. He was 87.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/sports/04campbell-1/04campbell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/04/sports/04campbell-1/04campbell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/sports/04campbell-1/04campbell-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/sports/04campbell-1/04campbell-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/04/sports/04campbell-1/04campbell-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'William Campbell, Who Played ‘Star Trek’ Klingon, Dies at 87', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Exner, Judith Campbell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T00:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margalit Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margalit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/7439823b-b46d-57f6-b4ed-025487be5db5', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7439823b-b46d-57f6-b4ed-025487be5db5'}, {'abstract': 'Leaders of the State House of Representatives introduced legislation to allow civil unions for gay couples after conceding there was not enough support in the chamber for a same-sex marriage bill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/us/politics/04brfs-CIVILUNIONSP_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Leaders of the State House of Representatives introduced legislation to allow civil unions for gay couples after conceding there was not enough support in the chamber for a same-sex marriage bill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leaders of the State House of Representatives introduced legislation Tuesday to allow civil unions for gay couples after conceding there was not enough support in the chamber for a same-sex marriage bill. Gay rights advocates criticized the move, saying civil unions were an insufficient compromise and protesting outside the State House. Same-sex marriage is legal in Connecticut, Iowa, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Vermont and the District of Columbia. Lawmakers in Maryland also failed to muster enough votes to pass a same-sex marriage bill this year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhode Island: Civil Unions Proposed', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | NEW ENGLAND', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rhode Island: Civil Unions Proposed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T00:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4804ebd7-37dc-5cd6-8450-549caef8c8f1', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4804ebd7-37dc-5cd6-8450-549caef8c8f1'}, {'abstract': 'Jon M. Huntsman Jr. took the first steps toward joining the Republican presidential race by opening a political action committee allowing him to raise and spend money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/us/politics/04brfs-HUNTSMANTAKE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Jon M. Huntsman Jr. took the first steps toward joining the Republican presidential race by opening a political action committee allowing him to raise and spend money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three days after resigning as ambassador to China, Jon M. Huntsman Jr. took the first steps on Tuesday toward joining the Republican presidential race by opening a political action committee allowing him to raise and spend money as he considers a candidacy. Mr. Huntsman, a former governor of Utah, has been ensconced in a Washington hotel for the last two days with advisers who are preparing him for the nominating contest. Politico first reported the opening of the committee. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Huntsman Takes a Step Toward Race', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Huntsman Takes a Step Toward Race', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T00:29:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/abea5dc8-5c34-5978-9cec-db4b3c0a4322', 'word_count': 81, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abea5dc8-5c34-5978-9cec-db4b3c0a4322'}, {'abstract': 'The government’s new food-marketing guidelines are too weak to help our children.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/03/junk-food-guidelines-wont-help/', 'snippet': 'The government’s new food-marketing guidelines are too weak to help our children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine your child’s teacher was distributing twice daily snacks, before and after lunch — maybe Snickers and PopTarts in the morning, Mountain Dew and fries in the afternoon. Now let’s pretend you complain to the principal, who tells the teacher, “Could you please stop doing that? You have until … five years from Tuesday.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Junk Food ‘Guidelines’ Won’t Help', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T00:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccb1a7dc-ff16-5e0d-914f-299b470efa2e', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccb1a7dc-ff16-5e0d-914f-299b470efa2e'}, {'abstract': 'The decision means a ruling could be made nearly two months before the regular season begins, time for the sides to work out a deal without delaying the season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/sports/football/04nfl.html', 'snippet': 'The decision means a ruling could be made nearly two months before the regular season begins, time for the sides to work out a deal without delaying the season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal court said Tuesday that it would expedite the appeal that will probably determine which side in the labor dispute between N.F.L. owners and players will have the most leverage. The good news for fans is that the hearing on the N.F.L.’s appeal of the injunction that stopped the league’s lockout was set for June 3. Although it is impossible to determine when a decision by a three-judge panel would come, it is logical to assume the decision will be expedited as well, said Michael Gans, the clerk of the Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit in St. Louis. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Agrees To Expedite N.F.L.’s Appeal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Court Agrees To Expedite N.F.L.’s Appeal', 'name': None, 