ue': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Abbottabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T02:41:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maureen Dowd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maureen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dowd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c13a6717-a823-5f08-ad8a-ad0f970668cc', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c13a6717-a823-5f08-ad8a-ad0f970668cc'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans will have a hard time charging that the president can’t make up his mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/opinion/04wed2.html', 'snippet': 'Republicans will have a hard time charging that the president can’t make up his mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s display of leadership in directing the killing of Osama bin Laden raises the prospect that American politics can move away from mindless debates over the president’s loyalties and fortitude. Perhaps the 2012 campaign might even shift to real issues, like the economy and the major parties’ competing visions of government’s role.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Myth of Mr. Obama’s Weakness', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Myth of Mr. Obama’s Weakness: Republicans will have a hard time charging that the president can’t make up his mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T02:46:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d4df411-3930-5c22-8a56-0d1cd5983b39', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d4df411-3930-5c22-8a56-0d1cd5983b39'}, {'abstract': 'The idea that a seasoned jurist was unfit to preside over Proposition 8, and that his decision should be set aside, is preposterous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/opinion/04wed3.html', 'snippet': 'The idea that a seasoned jurist was unfit to preside over Proposition 8, and that his decision should be set aside, is preposterous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given ample chance during a 13-day trial to offer an argument apart from prejudice, proponents of Proposition 8, the prohibition against same-sex marriage in California, found no evidence. Now they are trying to disqualify Vaughn Walker, the now-retired Federal District Court judge who ruled that the measure was unconstitutional.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fit to Rule on Same-Sex Marriage', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fit to Rule on Same-Sex Marriage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Judges', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Vaughn R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'r