le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f49799d0-27cd-5a9b-8e4c-a66c40aaa4bb', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f49799d0-27cd-5a9b-8e4c-a66c40aaa4bb'}, {'abstract': 'How long is a year? The year, of course, is the time it takes the earth to orbit around the sun, a rate that is slowing fractionally each century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/opinion/04wed4.html', 'snippet': 'How long is a year? The year, of course, is the time it takes the earth to orbit around the sun, a rate that is slowing fractionally each century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How long is a year? Ask most people, and they’d say 365 days and not nearly long enough. The year, of course, is the time it takes the Earth to orbit around the Sun, a rate that is slowing fractionally each century. For many reasons, scientists need a more precise definition of the year than its length in days, yet the only unit of time defined in the International System of Units is the second, which is measured in oscillations of cesium atoms. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Past, Present, and the Quantity of the Year', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Past, Present, and the Quantity of the Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sun', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Time', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weights and Measures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T02:53:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'