eahawks-what-the-film-revealed/', 'snippet': 'The Seahawks never seemed to establish a true identity offensively.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While we wonder whether there will be a 2011 season, this is part of a series looking back at the 2010 season, with the benefit of film analysis. This week, we look at the N.F.C. West:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2010 Seahawks: What the Film Revealed', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T10:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/27d3564c-def6-505a-be70-72088f268594', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27d3564c-def6-505a-be70-72088f268594'}, {'abstract': 'As automakers begin to bundle free Internet-radio apps into their infotainment systems, the satellite-radio service tries to become more user-friendly.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/siriusxm-tunes-in-a-new-program-guide/', 'snippet': 'As automakers begin to bundle free Internet-radio apps into their infotainment systems, the satellite-radio service tries to become more user-friendly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The corporate merger of the satellite radio giants Sirius and XM formally took place in 2008. On Wednesday, a remapped channel lineup should make the two networks finally feel more cohesive for listeners.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SiriusXM Tunes In a New Program Guide', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sirius XM Radio Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T10:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae77d1d3-9916-5184-9b4a-c2c2fabd21ca', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae77d1d3-9916-5184-9b4a-c2c2fabd21ca'}, {'abstract': 'The Keynesian assumption that if employers cut prices, demand will increase and jobs will thus be created does not hold up in the current landscape, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/new-keynesian-economics-misses-the-point-for-now/', 'snippet': 'The Keynesian assumption that if employers cut prices, demand will increase and jobs will thus be created does not hold up in the current landscape, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Keynesian Economics Misses the Point, for Now', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keynes, John Maynard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T10:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey B. Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/93b835cc-7b25-538f-8e64-70e305ba0932', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93b835cc-7b25-538f-8e64-70e305ba0932'}, {'abstract': 'To combat consumers increasingly turning to supermarkets and their industrial products, Rome’s farmer’s markets are offering extended weekend hours and an opportunity to purchase local food directly from producers.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/in-rome-farmers-markets-are-in-season/', 'snippet': 'To combat consumers increasingly turning to supermarkets and their industrial products, Rome’s farmer’s markets are offering extended weekend hours and an opportunity to purchase local food directly from producers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly every neighborhood in Rome has a local market — a mercato rionale. Market hours — generally Monday to Saturday until around 1:30 p.m. — aren’t convenient for many consumers, who turn increasingly to supermarkets and their industrial products. Rome’s farmers’ markets are seeking to combat this by offering extended weekend hours and an opportunity to purchase local food directly from producers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Rome, Farmers’ Markets Are in Season', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Farmers' Markets", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T10:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Parla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ff12b25-ecfe-5e02-afab-d77d465f64da', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ff12b25-ecfe-5e02-afab-d77d465f64da'}, {'abstract': 'The Beatles on the immigration issue of their day.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/the-beatles-discuss-arizonas-immigration-bill/', 'snippet': 'The Beatles on the immigration issue of their day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last weekend, when I wasn’t delivering meals to the homebound, I was “researching” Beatles bootlegs. And I discovered the so-called “Commonwealth Song.” It’s not so much a song as it is an extended improvisation during the interminable “Get Back” studio sessions in 1969 (in fact, some theorize that “The Commonwealth Song” is a prototype for “Get Back”). “Commonwealth” name-checks Enoch Powell (the Tom Tancredo of his day, or Thilo Sarazin, if you prefer a German reference), who had delivered his anti-immigrant “Rivers of Blood” speech the previous year. “Commonwealth” was Paul McCartney’s mocking response. All of which shows that the sun never sets on some issues. It’s also nice to know that as late as 1969, Lennon and McCartney could still crack each other up, especially when John interjects his high-pitched “Yes!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Beatles Discuss Arizona’s Immigration Bill', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T10:05:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', '