f94c45d0'}, {'abstract': "The Op-Ed columnist Thomas L. Friedman on the Arab spring's rejection of the culture of violent extremism taught by Bin Laden and his followers.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/opinion/100000000802992/opinion--thomas-l-friedman.html', 'snippet': "The Op-Ed columnist Thomas L. Friedman on the Arab spring's rejection of the culture of violent extremism taught by Bin Laden and his followers.", 'lead_paragraph': "The Op-Ed columnist Thomas L. Friedman on the Arab spring's rejection of the culture of violent extremism taught by Bin Laden and his followers.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/opinion/video-tc-110504-friedman/video-tc-110504-friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/04/opinion/video-tc-110504-friedman/video-tc-110504-friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/04/opinion/video-tc-110504-friedman/video-tc-110504-friedman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/04/opinion/video-tc-110504-friedman/video-tc-110504-friedman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happens to Bin Ladenism Now?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/93f797f8-8a97-5294-b175-82c661c224f9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/93f797f8-8a97-5294-b175-82c661c224f9'}, {'abstract': 'After losing her husband to the Rwandan genocide, Languida Nyirababeruka started a funeral home. She came to the United States as part of a mentoring program.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/business-life-after-death-in-rwanda/', 'snippet': 'After losing her husband to the Rwandan genocide, Languida Nyirababeruka started a funeral home. She came to the United States as part of a mentoring program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a previous post, I wrote about the Business Council for Peace, a nonprofit network of professionals who provide pro bono business services to mostly female entrepreneurs in countries affected by conflict, including Rwanda. Last month, three Rwandan owners, graduates of the three-year BPeace program, visited the United States during a trip that paired them with American businesses in their industries. The participants included Languida Nyirababeruka, who founded Pompe Funebre Twifatanye, a funeral home, after the 1994 genocide.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Business Life After Death in Rwanda', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rwanda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/44bec920-34c5-542e-8685-31c068af591f', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44bec920-34c5-542e-8685-31c068af591f'}, {'abstract': 'The latest New York Times/CBS News poll is based on telephone interviews conducted May 2 and 3 with 532 adults throughout the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/us/politics/05pollbox.html', 'snippet': 'The latest New York Times/CBS News poll is based on telephone interviews conducted May 2 and 3 with 532 adults throughout the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest New York Times/CBS News poll is based on telephone interviews conducted May 2 and 3 with 532 adults throughout the United States. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Poll Was Conducted', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Poll Was Conducted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a39e1d6-0eee-5978-888d-c5874c9beae1', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a39e1d6-0eee-5978-888d-c5874c9beae1'}, {'abstract': 'A majority now approves of the president’s overall job performance, as well as his handling of foreign policy, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/us/politics/05poll.html', 'snippet': 'A majority now approves of the president’s overall job performance, as well as his handling of foreign policy, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Support for President Obama rose sharply after the killing of Osama bin Laden, with a majority now approving of his overall job performance, as well as his handling of foreign policy, the war in Afghanistan and the threat of terrorism, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Obama, Big Rise in Poll Numbers After Bin Laden Raid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bin Laden Raid Gives President Big Lift in Poll', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:01:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao and Dalia Sussman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dalia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sussman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5e9f8e6-bc65-59ae-892a-7b9aa447c7b2', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5e9f8e6-bc65-59ae-892a-7b9aa447c7b2'}, {'abstract': 'We all know that hemlines rise during tough economic times. Do they also rise (or disappear altogether) during revolutionary ones?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/a-different-kind-of-arab-spring/', 'snippet': 'We all know that hemlines rise during tough economic times. Do they also rise (or disappear altogether) during revolutionary ones?', 'lead_paragraph': 'At The Daily Beast’s The Dish, Andrew Sullivan links to an article on the Beast about the first Muslim playmate. It’s hard to embrace this as a form of liberation, as Asra Q. Nomani reluctantly does. (“I’m not a fan of porn as a symbol of empowerment, but as a Muslim woman, hearing divinely sanctioned mandates all my life about what a good Muslim girl looks like, I can understand why Sahin has responded to our community sanctions about honor, shame and modesty by stripping — and why members of her family have ostracized her for it.”) But it did make me flash back to a recent post on 3quarksdaily, showing a graffito of a burqa-clad woman having a Marilyn moment. We all know that hemlines rise during tough economic times. Do they also rise (or disappear altogether) during revolutionary ones?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Different Kind of Arab Spring', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:35:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf30ea8d-fd95-52cb-af3e-d52051c8fd9b', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf30ea8d-fd95-52cb-af3e-d52051c8fd9b'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of Osama bin Laden’s death, Bush supporters have praised President Obama, but also defended the legacy of the former president.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/bush-aides-walk-delicate-line-on-bin-laden/', 'snippet': 'In the wake of Osama bin Laden’s death, Bush supporters have praised President Obama, but also defended the legacy of the former president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the better part of a decade, those at the helm of George W. Bush‘s political and national security teams argued that their approach to terrorism was a necessary means to a desired end: the takedown of Al Qaeda and its leadership.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bush Aides Walk Delicate Line on Bin Laden', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:37:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9ee532a-cc12-5a96-90f2-0681c667d6f6', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9ee532a-cc12-5a96-90f2-0681c667d6f6'}, {'abstract': 'In a city where parking is at a premium, drivers find creative ways to try to avoid an unwelcome note from a ticket agent.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/dont-ticket-me-im-one-of-you/', 'snippet': 'In a city where parking is at a premium, drivers find creative ways to try to avoid an unwelcome note from a ticket agent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was something official-looking about the gleaming GMC Sierra pickup truck that sat one recent weekday morning in a no-parking zone on Washington Square South in Greenwich Village.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Ticket Me. I’m One of You.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T11:49:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a499f20-9ecd-58af-bbeb-45f16acf8138', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a499f20-9ecd-58af-bbeb-45f16acf8138'}, {'abstract': 'Every week at New Vocations Racehorse Adoption Program we receive more than 15 requests from thoroughbred owners or trainers in need of retiring one or more of their horses.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/z-fortune-awaits-life-after-racing/', 'snippet': 'Every week at New Vocations Racehorse Adoption Program we receive more than 15 requests from thoroughbred owners or trainers in need of retiring one or more of their horses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every week at New Vocations Racehorse Adoption Program we receive more than 15 requests from thoroughbred owners or trainers in need of retiring one or more of their horses. The correspondence is normally the same and consists of me asking questions about the retiree’s reason for retirement, race history and current situation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Z Fortune Awaits Life After Racing', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T12:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Ford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2a9646e-7f84-52b4-aedc-b60413482624', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2a9646e-7f84-52b4-aedc-b60413482624'}, {'abstract': 'Electronic health records, according to a new study, can yield an environmental payoff. But the potential to reduce pollution depends on using the technology to change how work is done and care is delivered.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/electronic-health-records-green-or-polluter/', 'snippet': 'Electronic health records, according to a new study, can yield an environmental payoff. But the potential to reduce pollution depends on using the technology to change how work is done and care is delivered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the year that billions of dollars in government incentive payments are set to begin flowing to the nation’s doctors and hospitals to encourage them to adopt computerized patient records. The goal, of course, is to help improve the quality of care and curb costs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Electronic Health Records: Green or Polluter?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Health Records', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kaiser Permanente', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T12:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/77e71535-a317-53c4-83b7-00a3c8e67257', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77e71535-a317-53c4-83b7-00a3c8e67257'}, {'abstract': 'After getting rebuffed earlier, ConAgra Foods on Wednesday announced an all-cash offer of $86 a share for Ralcorp, in a push to expand its presence in generic products.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/conagra-bids-4-9-billion-for-ralcorp/', 'snippet': 'After getting rebuffed earlier, ConAgra Foods on Wednesday announced an all-cash offer of $86 a share for Ralcorp, in a push to expand its presence in generic products.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:11 p.m. | Updated After getting rebuffed earlier, ConAgra Foods announced on Wednesday that it had upped its offer for Ralcorp Holdings to $4.9 billion, in a push to expand its presence in generic products.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ConAgra Bids $4.9 Billion for Ralcorp', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T12:05:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook