r handbag. To her, a bag was not a fashion statement but a symbol of independence at a time when women were not allowed to enter into a contract or even open a bank account.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/fashion/Z-ROW-B/Z-ROW-B-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 969, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/fashion/Z-ROW-B/Z-ROW-B-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/05/fashion/Z-ROW-B/Z-ROW-B-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Her Arm, a Symbol of Freedom', 'kicker': 'Front Row', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Her Arm, A Symbol Of Freedom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handbags and Purses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, SUSAN B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T17:59:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdf0df08-4a16-5570-9c48-bdf41cc910fb', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdf0df08-4a16-5570-9c48-bdf41cc910fb'}, {'abstract': 'American Based Confused Desi – an updating of the term “American Born Confused Desi,” used to describe Indians in America debating whether to return to their homeland.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/abcd-2/', 'snippet': 'American Based Confused Desi – an updating of the term “American Born Confused Desi,” used to describe Indians in America debating whether to return to their homeland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American Based Confused Desi – an updating of the term “American Born Confused Desi,” used to describe Indians in America debating whether to return to their homeland.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ABCD', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T18:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/65ee9b08-8ce2-5d33-a42e-c45fcd4a5b76', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65ee9b08-8ce2-5d33-a42e-c45fcd4a5b76'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans who are going to officially join the race for the presidency will have to get on the track and start driving, and that track is the early primary and caucus states: Iowa, New Hampshire and South Carolina.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/who-will-run-for-president-early-voting-states-may-hold-a-clue/', 'snippet': 'Republicans who are going to officially join the race for the presidency will have to get on the track and start driving, and that track is the early primary and caucus states: Iowa, New Hampshire and South Carolina.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re still in the “Who Will Run?” phase of the 2012 presidential race. Only a couple of major names — Tim Pawlenty and Mitt Romney — have registered with the Federal Election Commission to become 2012 candidates. And the words “prospective candidate” are bandied about before someone’s name almost as often as Mr. and Ms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Will Run for President? Early Voting States May Hold a Clue', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T18:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9b22590-9cf3-59a3-9008-7c7793fe55bf', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9b22590-9cf3-59a3-9008-7c7793fe55bf'}, {'abstract': 'As a nurse on the oncology floor, I’m usually responsible for at least four patients each shift. But sometimes, one patient needs me more than most, writes nurse Theresa Brown.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/one-nurse-one-patient/', 'snippet': 'As a nurse on the oncology floor, I’m usually responsible for at least four patients each shift. But sometimes, one patient needs me more than most, writes nurse Theresa Brown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a nurse on the oncology floor, I’m usually responsible for at least four patients each shift. But sometimes, one patient needs me more than most.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Nurse, One Patient', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Theresa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T18:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theresa Brown, R.N', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'R.', 'middlename': 'N', 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/89d98d17-4cbf-5b36-a60b-8f3287f313c2', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89d98d17-4cbf-5b36-a60b-8f3287f313c2'}, {'abstract': 'In college quads in the south, camouflage is replacing Kelly green as the prepster’s color of choice.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/campus-camo/', 'snippet': 'In college quads in the south, camouflage is replacing Kelly green as the prepster’s color of choice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Looks like the lax bros these days are laying down their lacrosse sticks in favor of something with a little more firepower. In college quads in the south, camouflage is replacing Kelly green as the prepster’s color of choice. K. Cooper Ray, the Charleston, S.C.-based blogger of all things gentlemanly, saw local college kids “polished and groomed and bow-tied and then they turn around and they’ve got their camo on and shotguns over their shoulder, heading off to the marshes of the low country to go duck hunting.” For his third Social Primer for Brooks Brothers accessory collection, Cooper tips his hunting cap to these rustic dandies who proudly refer to themselves as “Prep-necks.” Intrigued by these seemingly opposing ideas of masculinity, Cooper combined a green camouflage print with a “Palm Beach pink” repp stripe for his reversible bow tie, just one of a collection of ties, key fobs and pocket squares available at Brooks Brothers stores nationwide. Those poor ducks don’t stand a chance against these dapper dudes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Campus Camo', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-04T18:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b9f26c1-0061-5de8-913e-6f8f081f73bd', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b9f26c1-0061-5de8-913e-6f8f081f73bd'}, {'abstract': 'Her album “21” is No. 1 for a sixth week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/04/adele-tops-chart-again-in-a-slow-week/', 'snippet': 'Her album “21” is No. 1 for a sixth week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adele tops the Billboard album chart for a sixth time this week, easily taking the top slot in a down week for music sales.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Slow Week, Adele Tops Chart Again', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Susan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Norah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spears, Britney', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', '