imedia', 'news_desk': 'ContinuousNews', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Zena Barakat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barakat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/67eff3a1-e677-5900-a338-687d428c0a24', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/67eff3a1-e677-5900-a338-687d428c0a24'}, {'abstract': 'Tomatoes fight acne; Jennifer Aniston’s perfume (with a nod to St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital); powder purchases benefit Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/fashion/05SPOTS.html', 'snippet': 'Tomatoes fight acne; Jennifer Aniston’s perfume (with a nod to St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital); powder purchases benefit Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VEGETABLE LOVE Yes To, a hair and skin care collection made from organic produce and minerals from the Dead Sea, has some new products. Tomato, rich in the antioxidant lycopene, is the featured ingredient in a new acne spot stick that also contains tea tree oil, and in a pore scrub with mango seeds (both $9.99.) And carrots — rich in beta-carotene, a natural form of vitamin A — appear in a new body lotion and hand cream whose other ingredients include soybeans and allantoin ($8.99 to $12.99). Available at Target, Walgreens, Duane Reade and yestocarrots.com.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/fashion/05spots-F/Z-SKIN-F-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/fashion/05spots-F/05spots-F-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'t