n', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba9a85c4-0df3-5c30-8f73-2475993bd811', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba9a85c4-0df3-5c30-8f73-2475993bd811'}, {'abstract': 'Short-term measures may let the government keep paying its bills as late as Aug. 2, but the eventual cost could be vast and the damage difficult to reverse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/business/economy/05debt.html', 'snippet': 'Short-term measures may let the government keep paying its bills as late as Aug. 2, but the eventual cost could be vast and the damage difficult to reverse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The federal government will not run short of money to pay its bills on May 16, when the federal debt reaches the legal maximum of $14.3 trillion. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/business/Debt2/Debt2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/business/Debt2/Debt2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/05/business/Debt2/Debt2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Ceiling Has Some Give, Until Roof Falls In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debt Ceiling Has Some Give, Until Roof Falls In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T01:07:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daf347c6-0292-59f4-8457-c219ee5e3a9b', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daf347c6-0292-59f4-8457-c219ee5e3a9b'}, {'abstract': 'Avery Leigh Cox, 24, landed a job as a junior designer at Amanda Nisbet Design after aggressively pursuing the firm twice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/nyregion/from-floor-plans-to-room-decor-a-focus-on-interior-design.html', 'snippet': 'Avery Leigh Cox, 24, landed a job as a junior designer at Amanda Nisbet Design after aggressively pursuing the firm twice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Avery Leigh Cox, 24, has been a junior designer for the past year at Amanda Nisbet Design and helped install the frisky bedroom the firm designed for the Kips Bay Decorator Show House, on display through May 26. Ms. Cox graduated with a B.F.A. in interior design from the Lamar Dodd School of Art at the University of Georgia and moved to New York from Dallas, her hometown, in 2009. She scraped along through a couple of unpaid internships before joining Nisbet and lives in Greenwich Village, where she customized her apartment by painting its brick fireplace hot pink. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/05/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'ima