 'Legislator Apologizes for Calling Neil Gaiman a ‘Weasel’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T15:50:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff938cda-5874-589d-82bb-8e72063a6f00', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff938cda-5874-589d-82bb-8e72063a6f00'}, {'abstract': '“Thor,” directed by Kenneth Branagh, is a programmed triumph of commercial calculation over imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/movies/thor-with-chris-hemsworth-review.html', 'snippet': '“Thor,” directed by Kenneth Branagh, is a programmed triumph of commercial calculation over imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I stumbled out of the Imax multiplex all-media advance screening of “Thor,” depositing my 3-D glasses in the appropriate bin, I thought of seeking shelter: in a nearby bar; under a passing bus; in the velvet shadows of an art house playing the longest, slowest, most obscure movie imaginable. But when something like “Thor” comes to town, there is really no refuge to be found in drink, death or subtitles, and so I tried to reason myself out of the leaden gloom that lay upon my soul as heavy as Mjolnir. (That’s Thor’s magic hammer, for those not versed in Marvelized Norse mythology.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/THOR/THOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'c