l gaze divided between the front of the room and the laptop screen. So I know about the frustrations of teachers in trying to engage students in the real world even as they’re buffeted with digital information.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Birds, Twitter and Teaching', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Secondary)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Connecticut', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T16:49:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/01cda5a5-a0fc-5448-a901-f3f9a117cdcc', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01cda5a5-a0fc-5448-a901-f3f9a117cdcc'}, {'abstract': 'Like most veteran horse racing handicappers privileged to inside information, I have been loath to disclose the secrets of my selection process, honed over 20 years of betting on the Kentucky Derby and that could prove highly valuable to anyone planning to bet  this coming Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/use-at-own-risk-a-derby-winning-breeder-handicaps-the-derby/', 'snippet': 'Like most veteran horse racing handicappers privileged to inside information, I have been loath to disclose the secrets of my selection process, honed over 20 years of betting on the Kentucky Derby and that could prove highly valuable to anyone pl...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like most veteran horse racing handicappers privileged to inside information, I have been loath to disclose the secrets of my selection process, honed over 20 years of betting on the Kentucky Derby, and that could prove highly valuable to anyone planning to bet this coming Saturday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Use at Own Risk: a Derby-Winning Breeder Handicaps the Derby', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T17:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Squires', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Squires', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff60177e-3617-5266-9d08-77f17419f530', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff60177e-3617-5266-9d08-77f17419f530'}, {'abstract': 'In his show at Hauser & Wirth, Gupta turns his attention to everyday objects and rituals, making them symbolic and elegant.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/cuisine-art-subodh-gupta-at-hauser-wirth/', 'snippet': 'In his show at Hauser & Wirth, Gupta turns his attention to everyday objects and rituals, making them symbolic and elegant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every morning, the the gallery staff at Hauser & Wirth on the Upper East Side must top off a glass of water that sits on a bare wooden table under a glass display box. “A Glass of Water,” as the work is appropriately titled, is by the artist Subodh Gupta, whose show, by the same name, opens today. “If guests drinks a glass of water offered at another person’s home, it means they’ve taken the reception,” said Gupta, explaining its significance in his native India. And yet, there is something inhospitable here. The found stainless steel vessel is filled to that delicate point where the liquid threatens to spill over on to the table. Gupta — who has been called the Damien Hirst of India — is thrilled by its dynamics: the beauty in that delicate curvature of the water and the precipice of something almost, but not quite, being pushed over the edge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuisine Art | Subodh Gupta at Hauser & Wirth', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T17:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marina Cashdan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cashdan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/19051dc7-200d-5eb1-b1ad-b0d376ef48f9', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19051dc7-200d-5eb1-b1ad-b0d376ef48f9'}, {'abstract': 'LastPass, a service for managing passwords, said that out of concern about a possible hacking attack, it would force its more than 1 million users to change their master passwords.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/password-service-warns-of-possible-hacking-attack/', 'snippet': 'LastPass, a service for managing passwords, said that out of concern about a possible hacking attack, it would force its more than 1 million users to change their master passwords.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:28 p.m. | Updated  Adding more background and information from interview with the company’s chief executive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Password Service Warns of Possible Hacking Attack', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T17:01:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Riva Richmond', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Riva', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richmond', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca2420f0-cffd-5c20-a926-f1b100b03d1e', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca2420f0-cffd-5c20-a926-f1b100b03d1e'}, {'abstract': 'Scotty McCreery has gone from “American Idol” front-runner to behaving like a Nashville star on his third album.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/american-idol-top-5-competition-triumph-or-career-success/', 'snippet': 'Scotty McCreery has gone from “American Idol” front-runner to behaving like a Nashville star on his third album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scotty McCreery > Lauren Alaina > Haley Reinhart > Jacob Lusk > James Durbin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘American Idol’ Top 5: Competition Triumph or Career Success?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iovine, Jimmy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T17:03:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/82943070-7c8c-53c5-b45b-c529cc6e474a', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82943070-7c8c-53c5-b45b-c529cc6e474a'}, {'abstract': 'There are moral and practical considerations to whatever targeted killings our government undertakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/opinion/rasenberger.html', 'snippet': 'There are moral and practical considerations to whatever targeted killings our government undertakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the jubilation surrounding Osama bin Laden’s death on Monday, which followed closely on the heels of an apparent attempt to take out Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi in an air strike, it’s difficult to remember a time when “assassination” was a dirty word in this country. But it was, and not so long ago. Before the practice becomes a bad habit, we might want to heed the counsel of the past.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thinking Through Assassination', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thinking Through Assassination', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T17:14:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rasenberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rasenberger', 'qualifier'