leted by the son of Ettore Bugatti, will soon receive a body.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1939, shortly before his death in a track accident, Jean Bugatti, the son of Ettore Bugatti, who founded the car-manufacturing company that bears his name, designed and built a chassis for the Type 64, which was intended to succeed the legendary Type 57. But World War II and a father’s grief intervened.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Naked for 72 Years, a Bugatti Gets Fitted for a Suit', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bugatti, Jean', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bugatti Motors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oxnard (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T18:02:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motavalli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/23937fd8-ad69-5b6c-8101-b56f95095c35', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23937fd8-ad69-5b6c-8101-b56f95095c35'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty took a swipe at his potential rivals for the Republican presidential nomination on Thursday, calling them out for skipping the first debate of the 2012 campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/republican-debate-notable-for-no-shows/', 'snippet': 'Tim Pawlenty took a swipe at his potential rivals for the Republican presidential nomination on Thursday, calling them out for skipping the first debate of the 2012 campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former governor of Minnesota, took a swipe at his potential rivals for the Republican presidential nomination on Thursday, calling them out for skipping the first debate of the 2012 campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republican Debate Notable for No-Shows', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Mitchell E Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T18:04:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c44d4c3-6726-5db9-a8f2-f556ecfbfff6', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c44d4c3-6726-5db9-a8f2-f556ecfbfff6'}, {'abstract': 'A sampling of Op-Eds about Osama bin Laden shows how perceptions of the man evolved as he transformed from an obscure fundamentalist to the embodiment of global terrorism and hatred for the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/05/opinion/20110505_Oped_BinLaden.html', 'snippet': 'A sampling of Op-Eds about Osama bin Laden shows how perceptions of the man evolved as he transformed from an obscure fundamentalist to the embodiment of global terrorism and hatred for the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sampling of Op-Eds about Osama bin Laden shows how perceptions of the man evolved as he transformed from an obscure fundamentalist to the embodiment of global terrorism and hatred for the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Op-Eds About Osama bin Laden', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T18:06:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ca124b33-dcb0-5367-b1c1-4d4c01724a16', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ca124b33-dcb0-5367-b1c1-4d4c01724a16'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08replyall-t-WHYSHEWENT_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janny Scott’s article (“The Young Mother Abroad”) about Ann Dunham resonated with me as an American parent who years ago took a small child to live in Southeast Asia. It resonated even more with me as a high-school principal. I have observed that parents who teach their children compassion often forget to teach them resilience, and those who teach their children to “stop being wimpy” often do so with an admonition that the world is a mean place in which they will have to be tough to compete. Ann Dunham’s insistence on both idealism and strength is a model for creating useful adults and perhaps even presidents.EMILY H. JONESPutney, Vt., e-mail', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why She Went', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'WHY SHE WENT', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunham, Stanley Ann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T18:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/010b4cd0-12eb-5128-b0be-37208bc45cfd', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/010b4cd0-12eb-5128-b0be-37208bc45cfd'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08replyall-t-THELMAANDLOU_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where do these two movies, the subject of Carina Chocano’s Riff column, put men? Either our value is reduced to how much is in our bank accounts, so that we can buy love and a beautiful woman, or we are threatened by a woman who thinks for herself and has her own opinions and desires. What about a guy that doesn’t make a lot of money and loves strong, independent women and is loved back? Where is our cultural trope?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '“Thelma and Louise” vs. “Pretty Woman”', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘THELMA AND LOUISE’ VS. ‘PRETTY WOMAN’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T18:14:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_ma