}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0d4f28f-d666-5fb3-a970-c32597205af8', 'word_count': 4150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0d4f28f-d666-5fb3-a970-c32597205af8'}, {'abstract': 'The creators of the go-go ’80s club Area are reuniting for a spark of madness and creativity by hosting tonight’s Creative Time Benefit Gala.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/asked-answered-eric-goode/', 'snippet': 'The creators of the go-go ’80s club Area are reuniting for a spark of madness and creativity by hosting tonight’s Creative Time Benefit Gala.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A handful of nightclubs have achieved legendary status and are symbolic of the excesses and revelry of the decade. The 1970s had Studio 54, of course, and the ’90s are synonymous with the club-kid shenanigans of the Limelight. Nothing exemplified the art boom of the go-go ’80s and the cross-breeding of uptown and downtown like Area. Located on a quiet Hudson Street factory block, when TriBeCa was an out-of-the way ghost town, Area was equal parts conceptual installation, fun house and disco. Every six weeks or so, the outlandish theme would change — and we’re not talking about using balloons instead of streamers. The indoor pool would morph from, say, “Sports” to “Bikers,” featuring actual bikers and beyond. What nightclub in today’s atmosphere of bottle service and a Lady Gaga soundtrack would actually have an art department?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked & Answered | Eric Goode', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T21:45:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Van Meter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Meter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'o