Defend Their Industry', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shipping Executives Defend Their Industry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T23:38:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/99d1f390-e8c5-5751-82d8-b608acab3404', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99d1f390-e8c5-5751-82d8-b608acab3404'}, {'abstract': '“There Be Dragons,” Roland Joffé’s new film, is the story of a lifelong rivalry as well as a Spanish Civil War saga.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/movies/there-be-dragons-roland-joffes-film-review.html', 'snippet': '“There Be Dragons,” Roland Joffé’s new film, is the story of a lifelong rivalry as well as a Spanish Civil War saga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Clunk, clunk, squish. That is the sound of the dead language in Roland Joffé’s screenplay for “There Be Dragons” as it tramples his would-be epic of the Spanish Civil War into an indigestible pulp. This calamitous film, written and directed by Mr. Joffé, the creator of “The Killing Fields” and “The Mission,” consists of two stories mashed into an interminable two-hour Sunday school sermon punctuated with battlefield carnage. Beyond the lugubrious pageantry, there is no sign of emotional or spiritual life in the film, only windy posturing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06THERE-span/THERE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/