Family Foundation has contributed $800 million to the Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Walton Family Foundation has contributed $800 million to the Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art, The Arkansas Times reported. The amount is thought to be one of the largest donations to an American museum. Founded by Alice L. Walton, the Wal-Mart heiress whose father, Sam Walton, opened his first store in Bentonville, Ark., in 1951, Crystal Bridges will be a showcase for American art and is expected to be a cultural magnet for the Ozarks region. It is set to open in Bentonville on Nov. 11. Of the $800 million, $350 million will go to an endowment for operating expenses, which are expected to be $16 million to $20 million a year. Another $325 million is to go to the general endowment, and $125 million for future capital use. Named for a nearby spring, the museum has been designed by the architect Moshe Safdie as a series of pavilions nestled around two creek-fed ponds. It will house a collection of paintings, drawings and sculptures by American artists, including Winslow Homer, Asher B. Durand, John Singer Sargent, Marsden Hartley and Norman Rockwell. The works have been bought by Mrs. Walton and the foundation over about six years.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crystal Bridges Museum Receives $800 Million From Walton Family', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crystal Bridges Museum Receives $800 Million From Walton Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walton, Alice L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T23:41:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5927e40a-0d8e-56e1-8d91-7bac34288547', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5927e40a-0d8e-56e1-8d91-7bac34288547'}, {'abstract': 'Two playwrights, Daniel Goldfarb and Jonathan Marc Sherman, talk about the experience of becoming fathers and the influence that has had on their work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/theater/daniel-goldfarb-and-jonathan-marc-sherman-on-kids.html', 'snippet': 'Two playwrights, Daniel Goldfarb and Jonathan Marc Sherman, talk about the experience of becoming fathers and the influence that has had on their work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT doesn’t take long for the playwright Daniel Goldfarb, 37, to whip out his phone to show off an adorable video he shot of his older daughter dancing while his baby plays the piano. “Look at the Dogma cinematography,” he says to Jonathan Marc Sherman, 42-year-old fellow playwright and father of two similarly aged children (15 months and 4 years). Mr. Sherman moons over the cuteness, wistfully recalling how his kids once played on a similar piano before it was hurled across the room. These are not tiger dads. Judging by their comic new plays about anxious New York fatherhood, they’re more like kittens. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/arts/DAD/DAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kids: Life-Changing and Play-Inspiring', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kids: Life-Changing and Play-Inspiring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Jonathan Marc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldfarb, Daniel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-05T23:43:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f9e3bb5-89d4-57f6-af63-99c0eabd38b2', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f9e3bb5-89d4-57f6-af63-99c0eabd38b2'}, {'abstract': 'The water level along San Francisco Bay has fallen since the late 1970s, but oceanographers say it will be rising soon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/us/06bcshort.html', 'snippet': 'The water level along San Francisco Bay has fallen since the late 1970s, but oceanographers say it will be rising soon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nation’s oldest tide gauge, housed in a wooden shack on a pier at Fort Point in San Francisco, recorded something unusual over the past 30 years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/us/06BCRISE/06BCRISE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/us/06BCRISE/06BCRISE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlar