, 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/43980b39-0192-5a7a-8a48-f82cc0ad3902', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43980b39-0192-5a7a-8a48-f82cc0ad3902'}, {'abstract': 'The trainer Jinks Fires and his son-in-law, jockey Jon Court, have realized their Derby dream with Archarcharch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/sports/06racing.html', 'snippet': 'The trainer Jinks Fires and his son-in-law, jockey Jon Court, have realized their Derby dream with Archarcharch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOUISVILLE, Ky. — Late Saturday afternoon, Jinks Fires is going to walk a horse, a pretty good one by the name of Archarcharch, over to run in the Kentucky Derby. It will be his first time, and waiting for him and his colt will be his rider, another rookie, Jon Court, who also happens to be his son-in-law.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/sports/2-sub-racing-1/2-sub-racing-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/sports/2-sub-racing-1/2-sub-racing-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 347}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/sports/2-sub-racing-1/2-sub-racing-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/sports/2-sub-racing-1/2-sub-racing-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/sports/2-sub-racing-1/2-sub-racing-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Derby Rookies, a Lifetime of Experience', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Derby Rookies, A Lifetime of Experience', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sellers, Johnny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Churchill Downs Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:01:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43dd6188-4dcc-511b-b6dc-6245e6233e83', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43dd6188-4dcc-511b-b6dc-6245e6233e83'}, {'abstract': '“Carson McCullers Talks About Love,” Suzanne Vega’s mixture of nightclub act and theater piece at the Rattlestick Playwrights Theater, is a funky ramble through McCullers’s life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/theater/reviews/suzanne-vegas-carson-mccullers-talks-about-love-review.html', 'snippet': '“Carson McCullers Talks About Love,” Suzanne Vega’s mixture of nightclub act and theater piece at the Rattlestick Playwrights Theater, is a funky ramble through McCullers’s life.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The face drew me in,” Suzanne Vega says in the opening moments of “Carson McCullers Talks About Love,” her funky ramble through the life of that Southern writer at the Rattlestick Playwrights Theater. Speaking of her ardent affection for McCullers, the novelist with “the face of a wise old child, but also of a film noir antihero,” Ms. Vega recalls the moment when her 17-year-old imagination was sparked by the searching appeal in McCullers’s dark eyes and tough, set mouth. “Somehow at that moment I felt she picked me out, tapped me on the shoulder and had things to say to me,” she says.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06CARSON-span/CARSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06CARSON-span/CARSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06CARSON-span/CARSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06CARSON-span/CARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06CARSON-span/CARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Alienated Souls Whisperer', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Carson McCullers Talks About Love'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Alienated Souls Whisperer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCullers, Carson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vega, Suzanne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01668ec8-9be5-5559-a9c4-a2221d0e4ce7', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01668ec8-9be5-5559-a9c4-a2221d0e4ce7'}, {'abstract': 'The long-awaited new play by Tony Kushner is a densely textured portrait of a Brooklyn family losing its (strictly secular) religion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/theater/reviews/the-intelligent-homosexuals-guide-by-tony-kushner-review.html', 'snippet': 'The long-awaited new play by Tony Kushner is a densely textured portrait of a Brooklyn family losing its (strictly secular) religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the young hustler and the older man whose furtive phone conversation begins the long-awaited new play by Tony Kushner, certain words are more effective than Viagra. I don’t have to spell them out, do I? Well, if you insist. I mean words like “gradualism,” “commodity of fetishism,” “self-alienation” and “sentimental pseudosocialism.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06INTELL-span/INTELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06INTELL-span/INTELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06INTELL-span/INTELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06INTELL-span/INTELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/06INTELL-span/INTELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Debating Dialectics and Dad’s Suicide Plan', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Intelligent Homosexual’s Guide to Capitalism and Socialism With a Key to the Scriptures'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debating Dialectics and Dad’s Suicide Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emond, Linda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cristofer, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Esper, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spinella, Stephen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Tony', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greif, Michael', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pasquale, Steven', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c49c8f69-b09f-5961-b31b-c0244bc7c233', 'word_count': 1413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c49c8f69-b09f-5961-b31b-c0244bc7c233'}, {'abstract': 'Derek Jacobi helps make Michael Grandage’s production of “King Lear” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music a true entertainment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/theater/reviews/king-lear-with-derek-jacobi-at-bam-review.html', 'snippet': 'Derek Jacobi helps make Michael Grandage’s production of “King Lear” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music a true entertainment.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Entertaining” is hardly the first word that springs to mind when thoughts turn to “King Lear,” Shakespeare’s profound and merciless tragedy of old age. Yet Michael Grandage’s crackling production at the Brooklyn Academy of Music — which stars Derek Jacobi at the peak of his powers — is, among other (and more strictly virtuous) things, terrifically entertaining.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/arts/07king-fri/07king-fri-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/07/arts/07king-fri/07king-fri-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/arts/07king-fri/07king-fri-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/arts/07king-fri/07king-fri-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/07/arts/07king-fri/07king-fri-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasies Aside, Life’s Tough at the Top', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'King Lear'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fantasies Aside, Life’s Tough At the Top', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobi, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandage, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4f5671c-dfc6-5294-92ea-9ca3d667b958', 'word_count': 1316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4f5671c-dfc6-5294-92ea-9ca3d667b958'}, {'abstract': 'Several executives said that Judy McGrath had been put into a position to resign after she received an offer that would have changed her duties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/business/media/06mtv.html', 'snippet': 'Several executives said that Judy McGrath had been put into a position to resign after she received an offer that would have changed her duties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MTV Networks, the powerhouse cable television operation behind hits like “Jersey Shore,” “SpongeBob SquarePants” and “The Daily Show With Jon Stewart,” announced on Thursday that Judy McGrath, its chairwoman and chief executive, was resigning effective immediately. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/business/media/06mtvPicA/06mtvPicA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/business/media/06mtvPicA/06mtvPicA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Head of MTV Networks Resigns Abruptly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'MTV Chief Resigns Abruptly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dauman, Philippe P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Judy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:02:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5b981cb-26ec-5289-8b7f-8f47a4625ed1', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5b981cb-26ec-5289-8b7f-8f47a4625ed1'}, {'abstract': 'Mike Pelfrey priases the work of Ronny Paulino amid Josh Thole’s struggles. Will the Mets let Thole be a part-time catcher in the majors?', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/05/pelfrey-lauds-paulino-and-what-that-means-for-thole/', 'snippet': 'Mike Pelfrey priases the work of Ronny Paulino amid Josh Thole’s struggles. Will the Mets let Thole be a part-time catcher in the majors?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ronny Paulino had no hits in the Mets’ 5-2 victory over the Giants on Thursday, but he did draw a walk to settle his on-base percentage at an even .500. Most important, he added to his brief legend by coaxing a fine performance out of Mike Pelfrey, one night after Chris Capuano pitched very well, and that could have an impact on Josh Thole’s immediate future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pelfrey Lauds Paulino, And What That Means For Thole', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelfrey, Mike', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thole, Josh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:03:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3ea0159-20ec-54dc-bdbe-f59f7c498a51', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3ea0159-20ec-54dc-bdbe-f59f7c498a51'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Soboroff, the Dodgers’ vice chairman, questioned baseball’s investigation into the team’s finances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/sports/baseball/06bats.html', 'snippet': 'Steve Soboroff, the Dodgers’ vice chairman, questioned baseball’s investigation into the team’s finances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Los Angeles Dodgers’ series against the Mets at Citi Field starting Friday is a meeting of teams with financial troubles. The Dodgers are resisting Major League Baseball’s decision last month to take control of the team, and the Mets needed Commissioner Bud Selig’s approval of a $25 million loan last year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image