10506-a-stealthier-helicopter-for-the-bin-laden-raid.html', 'snippet': 'The tail section of one of the helicopters used in the Osama bin Laden raid hints at secret developments in stealth technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tail section of one of the helicopters used in the Osama bin Laden raid hints at secret developments in stealth technology.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Stealthier Helicopter for the Bin Laden Raid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:40:05+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b085b753-f132-5100-a35d-0504f63afb29', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b085b753-f132-5100-a35d-0504f63afb29'}, {'abstract': 'The shifting narrative of the raid of Osama bin Laden’s may have distracted from the accomplishments of the Seal team and raised suspicions of the operation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/asia/06memo.html', 'snippet': 'The shifting narrative of the raid of Osama bin Laden’s may have distracted from the accomplishments of the Seal team and raised suspicions of the operation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — On Monday, the Obama administration said that Osama bin Laden had been killed after a firefight with Navy Seal commandos, and that he had used his wife as a human shield. On Tuesday, the administration said that Bin Laden was not armed at all, and that his wife had not been a shield, but had rushed her husband’s assaulter and was shot in the leg.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Raid Account, Hastily Told, Proves Fluid', 'kicker': 'Washington Memo', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raid Account, Hastily Told, Proves Fluid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brennan, John O', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Navy Seals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Abbottabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:52:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Bumiller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/734de064-f507-506c-9302-a166dd8e46fe', 'word_count': 915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/734de064-f507-506c-9302-a166dd8e46fe'}, {'abstract': 'The four corpses on the floor of a clinic were migrants from Nigeria, trapped in another country’s war. When they died, they were minutes from escape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/africa/06misurata.html', 'snippet': 'The four corpses on the floor of a clinic were migrants from Nigeria, trapped in another country’s war. When they died, they were minutes from escape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MISURATA, Libya — The four fresh corpses, the remains of people recently killed in the shelling of Misurata, rested on the floor of an office in a small clinic. Each was wrapped in a dirty blanket. No one knew what to do with them, just as no one present had any idea of their names. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/world/06misurata1/06misurata1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/world/06misurata1/06misurata1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/world/06misurata1/06misurata1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/world/06misurata1/06misurata1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/world/06misurata1/06misurata1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lives at the End of the Rockets’ Arc', 'kicker': 'Misurata Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lives at the End of the Rockets’ Arc', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Misurata (Libya)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T02:59:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f4df44a-82ba-5bdc-be59-d4ba07ef3384', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f4df44a-82ba-5bdc-be59-d4ba07ef3384'}, {'abstract': 'The government has banned all spy dramas and any related programs from being broadcast on major television networks from May to July, according to reports on Thursday in the Chinese news media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/asia/06briefs-China.html', 'snippet': 'The government has banned all spy dramas and any related programs from being broadcast on major television networks from May to July, according to reports on Thursday in the Chinese news media.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The government has banned all spy dramas and any related programs from being broadcast on major television networks from May to July, according to reports on Thursday in the Chinese news media. The government agency in charge of regulating entertainment programming has not announced a reason for the ban, but observers speculate that it may be an effort to clear the way for official programming in the run-up to the 90th anniversary of the founding of the Chinese Communist Party on July 1. Spy dramas scheduled for May, June or July must be rescheduled for later in the year. In April, the agency issued a guideline discouraging the production of time travel dramas for television. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: TV Spy Dramas Banned', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: TV Spy Dramas Banned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T03:33:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a00ebff1-891b-5496-9ae1-590172d32cab', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a00ebff1-891b-5496-9ae1-590172d32cab'}, {'abstract': 'Sheryl Cwele, the wife of State Security Minister Siyabonga Cwele, was convicted Thursday on drug trafficking charges, the South African Press Association reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/africa/06webbrfs-Southafrica.html', 'snippet': 'Sheryl Cwele, the wife of State Security Minister Siyabonga Cwele, was convicted Thursday on drug trafficking charges, the South African Press Association reported.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sheryl Cwele, the wife of State Security Minister Siyabonga Cwele, was convicted Thursday on drug trafficking charges, the South African Press Association reported. Mr. Cwele heads the country’s intelligence services, and the case against his wife has embarrassed the administration of President Jacob Zuma. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Africa: Minister’s Wife Guilty of Drug Trafficking', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Africa: Minister’s Wife Guilty of Drug Trafficking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuma, Jacob G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T03:35:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia W. Dugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d56d99cc-0659-5a54-8041-ac8c4322a218', 'word_count': 45, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d56d99cc-0659-5a54-8041-ac8c4322a218'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Ignatieff, the Liberal Party leader who resigned Tuesday after the party’s poor performance in this week’s federal election, said Thursday he would take a position at the University of Toronto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/americas/06webbrfs-Canada.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Ignatieff, the Liberal Party leader who resigned Tuesday after the party’s poor performance in this week’s federal election, said Thursday he would take a position at the University of Toronto.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Michael Ignatieff, the Liberal Party leader who resigned Tuesday after the party’s poor performance in this week’s federal election, said Thursday he would take a position at the University of Toronto. Mr. Ignatieff, a journalist and academic before entering politics about five years ago, will become the senior resident at the university’s Massey College, a graduate school. He previously ran a human rights center at Harvard University was a senior research fellow in King’s College at the University of Cambridge. The Liberals have not yet named an interim leader. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada: Liberal Leader Returns to Academia', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canada: Liberal Leader Returns to Academia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Toronto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ignatieff, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T03:36:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4bd610e-9cec-55f0-9a0a-797da7271a23', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4bd610e-9cec-55f0-9a0a-797da7271a23'}, {'abstract': 'The Africa Progress Panel, an international group of prominent individuals led by the diplomat Kofi Annan, said Thursday that the continent was experiencing strong but low-quality economic growth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/africa/06webbrfs-africaprogress.html', 'snippet': 'The Africa Progress Panel, an international group of prominent individuals led by the diplomat Kofi Annan, said Thursday that the continent was experiencing strong but low-quality economic growth.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Africa Progress Panel, an international group of prominent individuals led by the diplomat Kofi Annan, said Thursday that the continent was experiencing strong but low-quality economic growth. In a report released in Cape Town, the panel said that Africa depended too heavily on exports of raw materials, including oil, platinum, diamonds and gold, sectors with a limited capacity to generated much-needed jobs. The panel advocated more collaborative efforts of government, business and nonprofit groups. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Panel Says Africa Is Too Reliant on Raw Exports', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel Says Africa Is Too Reliant on Raw Exports', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T03:38:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia W. Dugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddb7f261-b183-587f-a0b0-70b51456ca78', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddb7f261-b183-587f-a0b0-70b51456ca78'}, {'abstract': 'Kurdish separatists claimed responsibility on Thursday for the attack on the prime minister’s campaign convoy the day before, the semi-official Anatolian News Agency reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/world/europe/06webbrfs-Turkey.html', 'snippet': 'Kurdish separatists claimed responsibility on Thursday for the attack on the prime minister’s campaign convoy the day before, the semi-official Anatolian News Agency reported.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Kurdish separatists claimed responsibility on Thursday for the attack on the prime minister’s campaign convoy the day before, the semi-official Anatolian News Agency reported. Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan was not travelling with the convoy when it was attacked by a group of five or six gunmen, reported to be members of the rebel Kurdistan Workers Party, near Kastamonu, a Black Sea port. One policeman was killed. The governor of Kastamonu, Erdogan Bektas, said the attack was not an attempt on the prime minister’s life but simply aimed at stirring chaos. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: Kurdish Rebels Claim Attack', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: Kurdish Rebels Claim Attack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T03:41:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d63976e-a2f7-509f-848c-fd8a282b48a5', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d63976e-a2f7-509f-848c-fd8a282b48a5'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Thompson portrayed archetypal mothers, from the loving family caretaker to the brutalizing harridan and mythical adulteress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/arts/sada-thompson-actress-known-for-maternal-roles-dies-at-83.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Thompson portrayed archetypal mothers, from the loving family caretaker to the brutalizing harridan and mythical adulteress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sada Thompson, a Tony- and Emmy-winning actress known for her portrayals of archetypal mothers, from the loving family caretaker and the world-weary, had-it-with-the-kids older woman to the brutalizing harridan and mythical adulteress and murderess, died Wednesday in Danbury, Conn. She was 83.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/THOMPSON2-obit/THOMPSON2-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/arts/THOMPSON2-obit/THOMPSON2-obit-thumbStand