: '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/474321bc-5790-58d8-a2c8-d14c1a26693f', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/474321bc-5790-58d8-a2c8-d14c1a26693f'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | If you feel your education has been lacking, what do you wish you understood better about how your government works, and about your rights and duties as a citizen? Why?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/how-would-you-do-on-a-civics-test/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | If you feel your education has been lacking, what do you wish you understood better about how your government works, and about your rights and duties as a citizen? Why?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you know the purpose of the Bill of Rights? The differences between the legislative, executive and judicial branches of the United States government? Recent results of the National Assessment of Educational Progress indicate that if you’re an American student you probably don’t. Do you think it is important to understand how your government operates and what the rights and duties of citizens are? What topics in civics do you wish you understood better? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Would You Do on a Civics Test?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T08:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a10995b-11b4-55fc-9510-5ef01172e2e9', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a10995b-11b4-55fc-9510-5ef01172e2e9'}, {'abstract': 'A wayfarer, esp. an itinerant merchant or trader. Chiefly in pl., in  Court of Piepowders n. (also in sing.) a summary court formerly held at fairs and markets to administer justice among itinerant dealers and others temporarily resident.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/daily-lexeme-piepowder/', 'snippet': 'A wayfarer, esp. an itinerant merchant or trader. Chiefly in pl., in  Court of Piepowders n. (also in sing.) a summary court formerly held at fairs and markets to administer justice among itinerant dealers and others temporarily resident.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s word, in association with the comprehensive Oxford English Dictionary, is', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Lexeme: Piepowder', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T08:34:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/361b8e6a-ce3c-5c11-a379-270fce5cafa2', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/361b8e6a-ce3c-5c11-a379-270fce5cafa2'}, {'abstract': 'In a humiliating defeat for the Liberal Democrats, voters rejected a proposal to reform the electoral system — a flagship policy for the party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/world/europe/07britain.html', 'snippet': 'In a humiliating defeat for the Liberal Democrats, voters rejected a proposal to reform the electoral system — a flagship policy for the party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'British voters redrew areas of the country’s political landscape on Friday with a series of local, regional and national votes that punished the Liberal Democratic junior coalition partner for the g