ake of a solution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Backtracking', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/89df296f-399a-570d-bfe5-eece91028b9a', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89df296f-399a-570d-bfe5-eece91028b9a'}, {'abstract': 'Manufacturers of alternative-energy vehicles will compete for a trophy not seen at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway oval since 1996.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/100th-indy-500-marks-brickyard-return-of-innovation-challenge/', 'snippet': 'Manufacturers of alternative-energy vehicles will compete for a trophy not seen at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway oval since 1996.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six years before the Indianapolis Motor Speedway hosted its first 500-mile race in 1911, Carl G. Fisher, a local businessman, envisioned a flat track ideal for testing horseless carriages made around the city. The 2.5-mile oval was also ideal for racing, of course.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 100th Anniversary of Indy 500 Marks Brickyard Return of Innovation Challenge', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indianapolis 500 (Auto Race)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indianapolis (Ind)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Caldwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caldwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ce6f7dd-9173-5387-a7c5-f7d16aee58e5', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ce6f7dd-9173-5387-a7c5-f7d16aee58e5'}, {'abstract': 'The Lakers have to find a way to impose their style of play on the Mavericks, or adapt their style of play to what the Mavericks are doing.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/have-mavericks-passed-the-lakers-by/', 'snippet': 'The Lakers have to find a way to impose their style of play on the Mavericks, or adapt their style of play to what the Mavericks are doing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the N.B.A. has gotten more and more obsessed with fast guards, pick-and-roll play, 3-point shots and “stretch” fours, the Lakers have simply played the way they have always played — and kept on winning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have the Mavericks Passed the Lakers By?', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Lakers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Krolik', 'person': [{'firstna