cle/be2768db-bad1-5b69-824a-1db11c95ef2c', 'word_count': 1312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be2768db-bad1-5b69-824a-1db11c95ef2c'}, {'abstract': 'Mucho Macho Man has been declared by the media as The Story of this Kentucky Derby. And why not, it has an amazing cast of characters.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/mucho-macho-mans-supporting-cast-steals-show/', 'snippet': 'Mucho Macho Man has been declared by the media as The Story of this Kentucky Derby. And why not, it has an amazing cast of characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a bucket-list moment, Julie June Stewart woke up at 4 a.m. and bought a ticket to the 2008 Belmont. She hasn’t stopped going to the races since. That is when she isn’t isn’t taking on a wildfire, hurricane, volcano or oil spill as the nation’s leading expert in disaster airspace coordination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mucho Macho Man’s Supporting Cast Steals Show', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie June Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': 'June', 'lastname': 'Stewart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/391a24ed-a093-5d0b-a6d7-647092fd383e', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/391a24ed-a093-5d0b-a6d7-647092fd383e'}, {'abstract': 'The verdict of “unlawful killing” was never in doubt in the inquest in the 2007 bombings on London’s transit system, which killed 52 people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/world/europe/07london.html', 'snippet': 'The verdict of “unlawful killing” was never in doubt in the inquest in the 2007 bombings on London’s transit system, which killed 52 people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — For five months the stories poured out into the courtroom, describing the worst and best of human behavior.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/world/07london-web1/07london-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 890, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/world/07london-web1/07london-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/07/world/07london-web1/07london-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Six Years and Hundreds of Witnesses Later, Coroner Rules in London Bombings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Six Years and Hundreds of Witnesses Later, Coroner Rules in London Bombings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Menezes, Jean Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:03:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9e1c18b-f3ed-5fe7-8009-0d6e4f3ef9cb', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9e1c18b-f3ed-5fe7-8009-0d6e4f3ef9cb'}, {'abstract': 'Since the time I took my very first cuts with a baseball bat, my father has been telling me about Stan Musial.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/the-story-of-stan-the-man/', 'snippet': 'Since the time I took my very first cuts with a baseball bat, my father has been telling me about Stan Musial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the time I took my very first cuts with a baseball bat, my father has been telling me about Stan Musial. Not just about the corkscrew batting stance but also about the gentleman. You may not be able to imitate the stance, I think was the lesson, but you could always emulate the man. Somehow Musial slipped from the pantheon of baseball’s immortals among the general public; more fathers chose to tell their sons about DiMaggio and Mantle and Mays, and as their legends grew, Musial’s kind of faded into some kind of runner-up, a tragic slight that culminated with him being left off the all-century team voted on by fans in 1999.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story of Stan the Man', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Musial, Stan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vecsey, George', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:28:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bf002a0-621a-52aa-b407-0210702a6765', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bf002a0-621a-52aa-b407-0210702a6765'}, {'abstract': 'Shortcuts to find the recipes you want on the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/tip-of-the-week-a-new-way-to-search-for-recipes/', 'snippet': 'Shortcuts to find the recipes you want on the Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Searching for recipes on the Web is not new, but Google has a search feature catered for cooks. When you use the search engine to look for a specific dish or ingredients, Google returns your first batch results as usual, but also presents a Recipes option in a list of categories in a column on the left side of the page; click the “More” link at the bottom of the column if you do not see it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: A New Way to Search for Recipes', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food Network', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T10:42:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0781f58a-6b29-5ef9-94b2-a9fe2db1f47b', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0781f58a-6b29-5ef9-94b2-a9fe2db1f47b'}, {'abstract': 'Here are the issues I’ve been pondering this week: sales training, employee reviews, and finding someone to review me.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/my-three-problems-this-week/', 'snippet': 'Here are the issues I’ve been pondering this week: sales training, employee reviews, and finding someone to review me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hi everyone. Here are the problems I’ve been pondering this week:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Three Problems This Week', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T11:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a98f350-b5b7-5521-ab9b-4d4cf0a33646', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a98f350-b5b7-5521-ab9b-4d4cf0a33646'}, {'abstract': 'An aversion to birds — even a mere feather — makes it hard to read many children’s stories. And City Room’s Bird Week is out straightaway.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/complaint-box-feathered-fiends/', 'snippet': 'An aversion to birds — even a mere feather — makes it hard to read many children’s stories. And City Room’s Bird Week is out straightaway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One recent click on City Room and I knew I wouldn’t be back till Bird Week was over. I cannot stand the sight or thought of the flying varmints.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Feathered Fiends', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T11:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Lynne Werlwas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Lynne', 'lastname': 'Werlwas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/eeb638b0-3e73-5861-bc01-fb4b7f70d07e', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eeb638b0-3e73-5861-bc01-fb4b7f70d07e'}, {'abstract': 'The Kentucky Derby has created numerous unforgettable moments over the years, but it is a real testament to its power that fans will remember the weather conditions of the 2010 post parade for years to come.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/churchill-downs-during-derby-week-a-magical-destination/', 'snippet': 'The Kentucky Derby has created numerous unforgettable moments over the years, but it is a real testament to its power that fans will remember the weather conditions of the 2010 post parade for years to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Todd Schrupp is an American television personality who works for TVG Network.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Churchill Downs During Derby Week: A Magical Destination', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T11:07:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Todd Schrupp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Todd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schrupp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ba4a16a-4235-5cc0-a648-390c43878d89', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ba4a16a-4235-5cc0-a648-390c43878d89'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former governor of Minnesota, is hoping to eliminate one of his biggest political liabilities by admitting he made a “mistake.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/pawlenty-hopes-im-sorry-is-enough/', 'snippet': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former governor of Minnesota, is hoping to eliminate one of his biggest political liabilities by admitting he made a “mistake.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a question every candidate faces: how to deal with political liabilities. Do you face them head-on and try to get them out of the way, or try to work around them in the hope that voters will lose interest?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pawlenty Hopes ‘I’m Sorry’ Is Enough', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T11:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c0eca0f-e27f-5f38-8ed1-1150364255ed', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c0eca0f-e27f-5f38-8ed1-1150364255ed'}, {'abstract': 'Appreciating a natural gift for its own sake.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08lives-t.html', 'snippet': 'Appreciating a natural gift for its own sake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One June afternoon in 2004, my family and I hiked up a hill to a playground, taking along our newest member, Fisseha, a 10-year-old boy who arrived in Atlanta from Ethiopia the previous day. He seemed like a quiet, polite, regular child — with curly hair and a shy smile, trying bravely to absorb the change in his life — and so he was, until he took off.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Flying Son', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Flying Son', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Fay Greene', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': 'Fay', 'lastname': 'Greene', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69c75db2-2c37-5843-b8d2-0a43352b8f89', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69c75db2-2c37-5843-b8d2-0a43352b8f89'}, {'abstract': 'The Cars have finally returned, but their frontman isn’t having much fun.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Talk-t.html', 'snippet': 'The Cars have finally returned, but their frontman isn’t having much fun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I saw that the Cars were touring and had a new album, “Move Like This,” I thought of what Frank Black said when he was asked why the Pixies were reuniting: “I did the arty-farty part, and now it’s time to talk about the money.” Is that true for the Cars?  I don’t think so. Money is great if it comes along, but when we first talked about it, we were going to just do a record for the Internet. We really didn’t want to do the fanfare thing or the “Hey, we’re back and we’re going to show you what the ’80s were like” thing. I wrote all these songs and then just out of the blue one night thought that, Gee, maybe I’ll call the guys and see if they want to do this.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08talk/mag-08Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 367, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Return of Ric Ocasek', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE RETURN OF RIC OCASEK', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ocasek, Ric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b1f44aa-713b-5848-b3d1-38136c13a611', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b1f44aa-713b-5848-b3d1-38136c13a611'}, {'abstract': '“I’m a photograph junkie, but I don’t want to take them.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Riffsidebar-t.html', 'snippet': '“I’m a photograph junkie, but I don’t want to take them.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Boston Review: Why did you decide to write about photography?Susan Sontag: Because I’ve had the experience of being obsessed by photographs. And because virtually all the important aesthetic, moral and political problems — the question of “modernity” itself and of “modernist” taste — are played out in photography’s relatively brief history. William K. Ivins has called the camera the most important invention since the printing press. For the evolution of sensibility, the invention of the camera is perhaps even more important. It is, of course, the uses to which photography is put in our culture, in the consumer society, that make photography so interesting and so potent. In the People’s Republic of China, people don’t see “photographically.” The Chinese take pictures of each other and of famous sites and monuments, as we do. But they’re baffled by the foreigner who will rush to take a picture of an old, battered, peeling farmhouse door. They don’t have our idea of the “picturesque.” They don’t understand photography as a method of appropriating and transforming reality — in pieces — which denies the very existence of inappropriate or unworthy subject matter. As a current ad for the Polaroid SX-70 puts it: “It won’t let you stop. Suddenly you see a picture everywhere you look.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Block Quote: Susan Sontag Makes the Case for Not Owning a Camera', 'kicker': 'Bonus Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Block Quote: Susan Sontag Makes the Case for Not Owning a Camera', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sontag, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81fd7634-7826-5645-ab82-e2b4828d2981', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81fd7634-7826-5645-ab82-e2b4828d2981'}, {'abstract': 'Amid a blizzard of images, what is the worth of one photograph?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Riff-t.html', 'snippet': 'Amid a blizzard of images, what is the worth of one photograph?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A while ago, a botched laptop sync wiped out all the pictures on my iPhone, consigning to oblivion 18 months of poorly framed vacation snapshots, blurred portraits of my children and candid, accidental glimpses of my feet. I should have downloaded it all earlier, winnowing as I went, but somehow I have never acquired that habit. Until I got that phone, I was never much of a photographer, so I’ve always depended on others — grandfathers, my mother, my father-in-law and especially my intermittently camera-obsessed wife — to keep me surrounded by images. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08riff/08riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 92, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08riff/08riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 92}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08riff/08riff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 156, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08riff/08riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08riff/08riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On (Digital) Photography: Sontag, 34 Years Later', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Capturing a Single Moment Of Perfect Stillness’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sontag, Susan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01bc3704-4168-519e-a89b-719db90e189d', 'word_count': 1750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01bc3704-4168-519e-a89b-719db90e189d'}, {'abstract': 'Sex crime; casino time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Ethicist-t.html', 'snippet': 'Sex crime; casino time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband is a registered sex offender. His sex crime was more than two decades ago and involved an adult woman. He is not a pedophile, and he is not on parole or probation. When people find out, however, we are often harshly condemned. If our children have play dates in our home, I believe we should tell the parents about my husband’s past. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s All in the Telling', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'IT’S ALL IN THE TELLING', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/474e6504-4a9b-5308-95ab-354585bfcd8a', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/474e6504-4a9b-5308-95ab-354585bfcd8a'}, {'abstract': 'Inside Father School, a program that teaches authoritarian fathers how to loosen up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Here-t.html', 'snippet': 'Inside Father School, a program that teaches authoritarian fathers how to loosen up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A soft-spoken electrical engineer named Edmond Rhim sat in a packed gymnasium with his wife, Hanna, gripping her tiny hand in his. It was the last of four five-hour-long sessions of Father School, and by the end of the night, 70 men — all of them Korean, and almost all of them Christian — would be declared more emotionally adjusted dads. They would even get a certificate, a group photo and a polo shirt to prove it. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08yourhere_span/08yourhere_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08yourhere_span/08yourhere_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08yourhere_span/08yourhere_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08yourhere_span/08yourhere_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/magazine/08yourhere_span/08yourhere_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Korean Dads’ 12-Step Program', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE DADDY FACTORY', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seoul (South Korea)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Korean-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T12:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole LaPorte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaPorte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb0d4110-ad82-5a23-9bda-1aae444c1e93', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb0d4110-ad82-5a23-9bda-1aae444c1e93'}, {'abstract': 'After a mother’s death, a daughter’s swift decline.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/magazine/mag-08Diagnosis-t.html', 'snippet': 'After a mother’s death, a daughter’s swift decline.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYMPTOMSA fiercely independent and active 76-year-old woman spent the past decade caring for her aged mother, who died at 99. Weeks after her mother’s death, the woman collapsed at home. She was found to have bleeding from a collection of abnormal blood vessels (known as AVMs, or arteriovenous malformations) in her colon. In the months after, the patient’s red-blood-cell count returned to normal, but she never regained her old energy and strength. She told her daughters that she was weaker and more tired than she had ever been in her life.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagnosis-well-promo/diagnosis-well-promo-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 190, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagnosis-well-promo/diagnosis-well-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagno