ific and environmental worthies to study the rapidly growing method of natural gas extraction known as hydraulic fracturing and to make recommendations about how it can be done more cleanly and more safely. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fracture on Fracking', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burr, Richard M', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chu, Steven', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deutch, John M', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horn, Miriam', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krupp, Fred', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tierney, Susan', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Upton, Fred', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yergin, Daniel', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:22:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f336c63f-4fdd-5f42-9e2a-f3bf63189045', 'word_count': 1487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f336c63f-4fdd-5f42-9e2a-f3bf63189045'}, {'abstract': 'The latest images after Western intervention in Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/world/africa/2011-may-libya-slide-show.html', 'snippet': 'The latest images after Western intervention in Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest images after Western intervention in Libya.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'May 2011', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:22:28+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/57b063b5-a1bb-526e-bb40-1db0b2783555', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/57b063b5-a1bb-526e-bb40-1db0b2783555'}, {'abstract': '“Raising my children has less to do with getting it right and more to do with loving them well.”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/lessons-from-a-sleeping-baby/', 'snippet': '“Raising my children has less to do with getting it right and more to do with loving them well.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'My older son slept through the night at three weeks. I thought I was a remarkable mother. My younger son, we joke, still hasn’t slept a normal night. He’s 17. So much for sleep as a measure of my parenting skills.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons From a Sleeping Baby', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/33e434c9-8a47-5f35-a386-822dd0a38a71', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33e434c9-8a47-5f35-a386-822dd0a38a71'}, {'abstract': 'Aldona Daukantas wanted to live in a more urban setting. She liked the look of Jersey City, and went apartment-hunting there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/realestate/08hunt.html', 'snippet': 'Aldona Daukantas wanted to live in a more urban setting. She liked the look of Jersey City, and went apartment-hunting there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALDONA DAUKANTAS grew up in the 1960s as one of five siblings in an apartment beneath the elevated J line near the Woodhaven Boulevard Station in Queens. She was a subway student at City College, where she studied art. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/hunt4/hunt4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/hunt4/hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/hunt4/hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Little Less Suburban, Please', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Little Less Suburban, Please', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jersey City (NJ)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:24:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffeb94e5-f95f-573c-b03d-e2e8b405d0c4', 'word_count': 1122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffeb94e5-f95f-573c-b03d-e2e8b405d0c4'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Zucker, is the vice chairwoman of the Zucker Organization, a development company that owns and operates apartments in the New York area', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/realestate/08sqft.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Zucker, is the vice chairwoman of the Zucker Organization, a development company that owns and operates apartments in the New York area', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ms. Zucker, 55, is the vice chairwoman of the Zucker Organization, a development company, which with its management firm, Manhattan Skyline, owns and operates about 3,000 apartments in the New York area. The latest developments include Love Lane Mews in Brooklyn Heights and the Story House in the Flatiron district. The company was started by Ms. Zucker’s father, Donald.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/SUB-sq-feet/SUB-sq-feet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/SUB-sq-feet/SUB-sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/SUB-sq-feet/SUB-sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Laurie Zucker', 'kicker': 'Square Feet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 30-Minute Interview/Laurie Zucker', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:27:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivian Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/056c8e3f-facf-57c5-b346-b8c2a496c552', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/056c8e3f-facf-57c5-b346-b8c2a496c552'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to Candelaria, authentic Mexican food can now be found in the Marais.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/paris-with-hot-sauce/', 'snippet': 'Thanks to Candelaria, authentic Mexican food can now be found in the Marais.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a city dominated by coq au vin and rillettes, authentic Mexican food — let alone a good margarita — has been hard to come by. Now there is Candelaria, Paris’s first real taqueria, which recently opened in the Marais. The galley-size kitchen, helmed by the Mexican chef Luis Rendon, is buffered by a five-seat counter and a communal table. The seasonal menu, listed on a jagged blackboard, currently includes dishes like rajas con queso (tacos with sautéed green peppers, onions and melted cheese), totopos (chips) with zesty guacamole and longaniza (spicy pork) or pollo pibil (stewed chicken) tostados dressed with shredded red cabbage. The crisp tortillas are made in-house with meseca imported from Spain. Spicy sauces are on hand — and thankfully so are Mexican beers, bubbly fluorescent Jarritos sodas and hibiscus juice. There’s no wine or coffee — positively heresy in Paris — but here “nothing has been compromised for a French palate,” insists Carina Soto Velasquez, who founded the restaurant with Adam Tsou and Joshua Fontaine. And about those margaritas? Hidden behind a mural-covered door in the back is a cocktail bar whose alcoholic arsenal includes well-aged tequilas, organic mezcal, rare rums and a curated drink selection with contributions from international bartender aficionados. (One is from the provenance of the East Village’s Death & Co.) Assorted young artists helped supply the décor, including Adrian Rubi-Dentzel, who repurposed the space’s original floor into the bar, and Confetti System, whose shimmering pendant adds a nice flourish.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris, With Hot Sauce', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/91c33ad7-ae1c-583b-a940-f14e2c256951', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91c33ad7-ae1c-583b-a940-f14e2c256951'}, {'abstract': 'Having trouble handicapping this wide-open Kentucky Derby? Let the little jockeys and trainers at Louisville Collegiate School lend you a helping hand.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/nurse-i-think-i-have-derby-fever/', 'snippet': 'Having trouble handicapping this wide-open Kentucky Derby? Let the little jockeys and trainers at Louisville Collegiate School lend you a helping hand.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nurse, I Think I Have Derby Fever', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T14:31:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Hoppert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoppert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7399b8d2-9aab-5724-8de6-c09abb6a811b', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7399b8d2-9aab-5724-8de6-c09abb6a811b'}, {'abstract': 'The jobs report offers reason for relief.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/5-answers-from-the-jobs-report/', 'snippet': 'The jobs report offers reason for relief.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Yesterday, I posed five questions about this morning’s jobs report. Here are some answers:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '5 Answers from the Jobs Report', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05