 the South of France for the Hyères Fashion Festival and in San Francisco to meet students at the Academy of Art University, with a stop in New York for the opening of the Alexander McQueen exhibition.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/the-evolution-of-design/', 'snippet': 'In the last week I’ve been in the South of France for the Hyères Fashion Festival and in San Francisco to meet students at the Academy of Art University, with a stop in New York for the opening of the Alexander McQueen exhibition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the last week I’ve been in the South of France for the Hyères Fashion Festival and in San Francisco to meet students at the Academy of Art University, with a stop in New York for the opening of the Alexander McQueen exhibition. What links these places is not just fashion, but the design process itself and how the ideas of designers evolve over time. That evolution is one of the pleasures of Andrew Bolton’s selections for the McQueen show, especially in the tailoring choices. It was inspiring to see how his cutting methods changed over his career, yet how certain assumptions remained fixed. One of the most rewarding and maddening of these assumptions is that design is a learning process. And McQueen always wanted to learn. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Jury Is Still Out', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:37:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/209d45aa-9ef2-5917-912b-a1082a4df075', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/209d45aa-9ef2-5917-912b-a1082a4df075'}, {'abstract': 'When the Chrysler 300 swaggered into the full-size sedan market in 2004, it helped the company stay in business. Now the car has been redesigned to look less Chrysler, more upscale and less menacing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/automobiles/autoreviews/08WHEEL.html', 'snippet': 'When the Chrysler 300 swaggered into the full-size sedan market in 2004, it helped the company stay in business. Now the car has been redesigned to look less Chrysler, more upscale and less menacing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE is no questioning the sales success of the Chrysler 300 that swaggered into the full-size sedan market in 2004 like a mob underboss; some 650,000 were sold in the 2005-10 model years. The 300 was instrumental in keeping Chrysler in business through its dark days of drift, bankruptcy and reorganization.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 267, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/08/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 267}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gangster Gets Rehabilitated', 'kicker': 'Behind the Wheel | 2011 Chrysler 300', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gangster Gets Rehabilitated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:37:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5a1a973-45e7-5cfc-b388-0f474a21e749', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5a1a973-45e7-5cfc-b388-0f474a21e749'}, {'abstract': 'The focus of this week may be on  boys running in the 137th Kentucky Derby, but the Kentucky Oaks has just as rich a history on display through 136 previous runnings.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/before-the-roses-the-lilies-for-the-fillies/', 'snippet': 'The focus of this week may be on  boys running in the 137th Kentucky Derby, but the Kentucky Oaks has just as rich a history on display through 136 previous runnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Todd Schrupp is an American television personality who works for TVG Network.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Before the Roses, the Lilies for the Fillies', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:38:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Todd Schrupp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Todd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schrupp', 'qualifier': None,