go before it fully recovers from the Great Recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/comparing-recoveries-job-changes-6/', 'snippet': 'A look at how far the U.S. economy has to go before it fully recovers from the Great Recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Source: Bureau of Labor Statistics. Chart by Amanda Cox. Horizontal axis shows months. Vertical axis shows the ratio of that month’s nonfarm payrolls to the nonfarm payrolls at the start of recession. Note: Because employment is a lagging indicator, the dates for these employment trends are not exactly synchronized with National Bureau of Economic Research’s official business cycle dates.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing Recoveries: Job Changes', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:41:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed6c3d4a-abf7-5ac0-8fff-861a3d535521', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed6c3d4a-abf7-5ac0-8fff-861a3d535521'}, {'abstract': 'From George Orwell to Osama Bin Laden.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/appointment-in-abbottabad/', 'snippet': 'From George Orwell to Osama Bin Laden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reflecting on her own reactions to Osama Bin Laden’s death, Megan McArdle excerpts a long quote from George Orwell, on the hollowness of revenge. The scene is post-war Germany, and Orwell is watching a German Jew abuse an imprisoned S.S. man:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Appointment in Abbottabad', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:41:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bb0d593-d33b-5e5d-8028-d1691939cb70', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bb0d593-d33b-5e5d-8028-d1691939cb70'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Cunningam, who has lived for 25 years with a psychoanalyst, on how that profession is portrayed in fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/up-front-michael-cunningham.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Cunningam, who has lived for 25 years with a psychoanalyst, on how that profession is portrayed in fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this issue, Michael Cunningham reviews Rikki Ducornet’s novel “Netsuke,” about a psychotherapist who compulsively has sex with his clients.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 638, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Michael Cunningham', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:42:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d002e00-8763-5ea8-bc26-213c651e08ce', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d002e00-8763-5ea8-bc26-213c651e08ce'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Adrian’s novel is a loose retelling of “A Midsummer Night’s Dream,” set in contemporary San Francisco.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/book-review-the-great-night-by-chris-adrian.html', 'snippet': 'Chris Adrian’s novel is a loose retelling of “A Midsummer Night’s Dream,” set in contemporary San Francisco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nature does not stop to grieve, an observation that since time began has either appalled or inspired the human beings who have made it. Poppies sprout in bloody battlefields, and birds sing outside death-room windows. On one hand, the big thing that just happened to you is insignificant to the rest of creation, and on the other, you are still part of a creation that’s so much bigger than your loss.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Miller/Miller-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Miller/Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Miller/Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Midsummer Night’s Dream,’ Retold in San Francisco', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wondrous Strange', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haight-Ashbury (San Francisco)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADRIAN, CHRIS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:49:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bc3340e-c2e2-5d37-a412-271790272e2e', 'word_count': 1351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bc3340e-c2e2-5d37-a412-271790272e2e'}, {'abstract': 'Once a bookseller, always a bookseller, say some published authors who haven’t quit their day jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/selling-books-by-day-writing-them-by-night.html', 'snippet': 'Once a bookseller, always a bookseller, say some published authors who haven’t quit their day jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like all good independent bookstores, BookCourt in Brooklyn has a robust section of staff recommendations. There, nestled in with titles by Jennifer Egan, Haruki Murakami and David Foster Wallace, is “Other People We Married,” a collection of short stories by Emma Straub. A handwritten note taped to the wall below reads: “I wrote this book. Please buy it. I love you.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Sullivan/Sullivan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Sullivan/Sullivan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Sullivan/Sullivan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Selling Books by Day, Writing Them by Night', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t I Know You From the Dust Jacket?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:51:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Courtney Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Courtney', 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a56183e9-1a92-54d2-884b-1b31f518751f', 'word_count': 1260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a56183e9-1a92-54d2-884b-1b31f518751f'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Thomas Perry, Belinda Bauer, Chris Knopf and the late Robert B. Parker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/crime-novels-by-thomas-perry-belinda-bauer-chris-knopf-and-robert-b-parker.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Thomas Perry, Belinda Bauer, Chris Knopf and the late Robert B. Parker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe you’ve heard of him. Named after the foster father (Eddie the Butcher) who taught him his trade, and introduced almost 30 years ago by Thomas Perry in “The Butcher’s Boy,” this cold-blooded professional killer is one of the immortals of the genre. Michael Schaeffer, to give this \xadantihero his current alias, seemed a bit mechanical when he briefly came out of retirement two decades ago in “Sleeping Dogs,” but he makes a great comeback in THE INFORMANT (Otto Penzler/Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $27) — older, wiser and deadlier.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 915, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Departed', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Departed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Robert B', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knopf, Chris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bauer, Belinda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:51:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/87a0e52d-16da-533b-b0bc-33d52dc0546f', 'word_count': 945, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87a0e52d-16da-533b-b0bc-33d52dc0546f'}, {'abstract': 'Stuart Woods has had 10 books on the hardcover fiction list over the past three years. His “Bel-Air Dead” debuts at No. 3 this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/15/books/review/bestsellers-weekly-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'Stuart Woods has had 10 books on the hardcover fiction list over the past three years. His “Bel-Air Dead” debuts at No. 3 this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stuart Woods has had 10 books on the hardcover fiction list over the past three years. His “Bel-Air Dead” debuts at No. 3 this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers Weekly Graphic: Frequent Flier', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:51:54+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/93aac62d-3934-5bc3-986f-17db32c1230c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/93aac62d-3934-5bc3-986f-17db32c1230c'}, {'abstract': 'Bernie Maddoff files his review of Diana Henriques’s “Wizard of Lies,” disputes charges that he is a closet Danielle Steel fan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Bernie Maddoff files his review of Diana Henriques’s “Wizard of Lies,” disputes charges that he is a closet Danielle Steel fan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERNIE MADOFF, BOOK REVIEWER: “The Wizard of Lies: Bernie Madoff and the Death of Trust,” by the New York Times financial reporter Diana B. Henriques, enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10 this week. Liaquat Ahamed, who reviews the book in our May 15 issue, calls it “riveting reading,” a vivid portrait of “a sad, hapless man who, lacking the character to tell the truth at the critical moment, stumbled foolishly and blindly into one of the crimes of the century.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/15insidelist/15insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/15insidelist/15insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:52:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aab6ddd-94f3-5d3c-aa0b-15a168ba0fd7', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aab6ddd-94f3-5d3c-aa0b-15a168ba0fd7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE TRAGEDY OF ARTHUR,  by Arthur Phillips (Random House, $26.) Phillips’s splendidly devious novel consists of a Shakespearean play of his own virtuosic creation and an “introduction” that devastatingly reveals the psychological life of its author.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice | Recent books of particular interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:52:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/8462f105-7cdf-5e66-9df5-0fdb641fd733', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8462f105-7cdf-5e66-9df5-0fdb641fd733'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OUR KIND OF TRAITOR,  by John le Carré (Penguin, $15.) Though set in the present, le Carré’s latest thriller is steeped in cold war nostalgia. During a Caribbean vacation, an English couple find themselves caught up in a high-stakes espionage game with a Russian money-launderer looking to strike a deal with British intelligence in exchange for safe harbor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41cc42d6-6ad6-5a65-9295-f666428af09e', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41cc42d6-6ad6-5a65-9295-f666428af09e'}, {'abstract': 'The concert production of “Camelot,” which also stars James Barbour as Lancelot and Melissa Errico as Guenevere, will be presented at the Shubert Theater on June 6 as a benefit for the Irish Repertory Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/fie-on-badness-jeremy-irons-to-play-king-arthur-for-one-night/', 'snippet': 'The concert production of “Camelot,” which also stars James Barbour as Lancelot and Melissa Errico as Guenevere, will be presented at the Shubert Theater on June 6 as a benefit for the Irish Repertory Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'His sinister voice and unnerving gaze have guaranteed Jeremy Irons a career playing nefarious characters, but does he dream of playing a hero – even a once-great medieval king who is ditched by his wife for a newer and better model of knight? If so, Mr. Irons, a star of the Showtime series “The Borgias” (not to mention films like “Reversal of Fortune” and “The Lion King”) will get to fulfill that goal in a one-night concert production of “Camelot” for the Irish Repertory Theater, in which he will play King Arthur. Press representatives for the production, which will be presented on June 6 at the Shubert Theater, said on Friday that Mr. Irons will play Arthur, the Excalibur-wielding monarch, in the Lerner and Loewe musical. James Barbour and Melissa Errico will play Lancelot and Guenevere, reprising the roles they played in a 2005 production at the Hollywood Bowl. The Irish Rep concert production, which will be directed by Charlotte Moore, with musical direction by Mark Hartman, will be presented as a benefit for the theater, so be prepared to dig up a little more coin of the realm for tickets. Prices for individual seats are $100 to $300.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fie on Badness! Jeremy Irons to Play King Arthur for One Night', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irons, Jeremy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Irish Repertory Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:52:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e66f49b3-f51a-5663-a892-5c9852216b02', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e66f49b3-f51a-5663-a892-5c9852216b02'}, {'abstract': 'Introducing Border collies to Prospect Park as a way to prevent having to kill geese.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/bird-week-whats-good-for-the-goose-collies/', 'snippet': 'Introducing Border collies to Prospect Park as a way to prevent having to kill geese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dogs of every breed come to Prospect Park and bother the geese, but only Cleo, a black and white Border collie, is paid to do it. Her job is to save the geese by tormenting them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bird Week | What’s Good for the Goose: Collies?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geese', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:53:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Dolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7075b6ee-b6db-56ad-a0d6-7f3675f674aa', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7075b6ee-b6db-56ad-a0d6-7f3675f674aa'}, {'abstract': 'In this weeks’ Recipes for Health, Martha Rose Shulman explains why even the simple cracker can be improved in the home kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/going-crackers-for-homemade-crackers/', 'snippet': 'In this weeks’ Recipes for Health, Martha Rose Shulman explains why even the simple cracker can be improved in the home kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Recipes for Health, Martha Rose Shulman explains why even the simple cracker can be improved in the home kitchen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Crackers for Homemade Crackers', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 