/krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/what-do-you-mean-we-white-man-deficit-edition/', 'snippet': 'Top-down budget busters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whenever I read pieces like David Brooks’s column this morning — pieces that attribute our budget deficits to the public’s irresponsibility and lack of realism — I find myself wondering how so much recent history went down the memory hole.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do You Mean We, White Man? Deficit Edition', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:56:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/85392352-ea52-50d4-8d7d-3a86fde87cd9', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85392352-ea52-50d4-8d7d-3a86fde87cd9'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events in New York this weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/weekend-fare-42/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events in New York this weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starting Friday and open daily through June 9 from 11 a.m. to 9 p.m. is Mad. Sq. Eats at Worth Square in Madison Square Park, near Fifth Avenue and 24th Street. The list of eateries participating include Goat Town, Asia Dog, Momofuku Milk Bar, Hong Kong Street Cart, Roberta’s Pizza, the P&H Soda Company, Cookie Panache and Eataly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:56:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/275a7d44-81e0-56c2-b793-e4092635fbef', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/275a7d44-81e0-56c2-b793-e4092635fbef'}, {'abstract': 'A camera trap video of fishers interacting with a plastic flamingo doused with orange scent. The difference in the behavior of different fishers may reflect bold or shy personalities of individuals. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/science/100000000807480/fisher-behavior.html', 'snippet': 'A camera trap video of fishers interacting with a plastic flamingo doused with orange scent. The difference in the behavior of different fishers may reflect bold or shy personalities of individuals. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'A camera trap video of fishers interacting with a plastic flamingo doused with orange scent. The difference in the behavior of different fishers may reflect bold or shy personalities of individuals. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/science/video-06fishervid/video-06fishervid-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/06/science/video-06fishervid/video-06fishervid-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/06/science/video-06fishervid/video-06fishervid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/06/science/video-06fishervid/video-06fishervid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fisher Behavior', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:58:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/31837332-299c-5e3b-adff-d0c86bb72664', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/31837332-299c-5e3b-adff-d0c86bb72664'}, {'abstract': 'Lofts predominate in TriBeCa, with co-op conversions from the late 1970s or early 1980s, and condos developed during a wave of conversions that began in the 1990s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/realestate/08living.html', 'snippet': 'Lofts predominate in TriBeCa, with co-op conversions from the late 1970s or early 1980s, and condos developed during a wave of conversions that began in the 1990s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON Sept. 11, 2001, TriBeCa, the Lower Manhattan loft district two blocks north of the World Trade Center, shook. Much of the neighborhood saw one if not both hijacked airliners fly into the twin towers. Residents watched aghast as bodies and then the buildings themselves crashed to earth. Clouds of ash descended on the streets as if in some macabre snowstorm.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/living/living-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/living/living-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/living/living-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/living/living-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/realestate/living/living-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Land of the $800 Stroller', 'kicker': 'Living In | TriBeCa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Land of the $800 Stroller', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'TriBeCa (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T15:59:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Freeman Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Freeman', 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c450fa99-9a52-5dca-9f33-2bed7bc0a6b9', 'word_count': 1547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c450fa99-9a52-5dca-9f33-2bed7bc0a6b9'}, {'abstract': 'International designers, tastemakers and fashion devotees gathered in the South of France last weekend for three days of competition, conferences, concerts in the grass, and midday rosé.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/dispatch-the-hyeres-festival-of-fashion-and-photography/', 'snippet': 'International designers, tastemakers and fashion devotees gathered in the South of France last weekend for three days of competition, conferences, concerts in the grass, and midday rosé.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While most style watchers spent last weekend glued to the nearest television awaiting Kate Middleton’s wedding dress, a crowd of fashion devotees gathered in the South of France for a different sort of unveiling: the winners of the 26th Hyères Festival of Fashion and Photography.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dispatch | The Hyères Festival of Fashion and Photography', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T16:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Pfeiffer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfeiffer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/83c8fe10-fd3c-5594-ae01-f3a625dc5585', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83c8fe10-fd3c-5594-ae01-f3a625dc5585'}, {'abstract': 'Defining good news down.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/jobs-report-the-age-of-diminished-expectations/', 'snippet': 'Defining good news down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Better than most people, myself included, expected. So good news, definitely.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobs Report: The Age of Diminished Expectations', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T16:03:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/aca0bd8a-42f7-5b4a-adda-8972a607d064', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aca0bd8a-42f7-5b4a-adda-8972a607d064'}, {'abstract': 'John Gray, a philosopher, explores a century or so of investigations into immortality by mystically inclined intellectuals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/books/review/book-review-the-immortalization-commission-by-john-gray.html', 'snippet': 'John Gray, a philosopher, explores a century or so of investigations into immortality by mystically inclined intellectuals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British philosopher and freewheeling intellectual John Gray is in serious danger of making philosophy exciting and fun to read. “The Immortalization Commission” opens with an 1874 séance attended by Charles Darwin, Francis Galton and George Eliot; a few pages later we are introduced to the Society for Psychical Research, which counted William James, Henri Bergson, John Ruskin, Alfred, Lord Tennyson and W. E. Gladstone among its members. The central concern driving the many supernatural investigations of these luminaries was the not inconsequential question of whether life exists after death. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Scientific Revolt Against Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heaven Can Wait', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Occult Sciences', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T16:03:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clancy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9da2965-0193-5af1-adc0-977d0bb99f2c', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9da2965-0193-5af1-adc0-977d0bb99f2c'}, {'abstract': 'Call it finding inspiration, but since the royal vows were exchanged on April 29, wedding planners have reported requests from many clients to make their ceremonies a little more regal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/fashion/weddings/08FIELD.html', 'snippet': 'Call it finding inspiration, but since the royal vows were exchanged on April 29, wedding planners have reported requests from many clients to make their ceremonies a little more regal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUDDENLY trees are sprouting — and not only as proof that spring has finally arrived. Credit the recent royal wedding and the couple’s decision to line the aisle in Westminster Abbey with English field maples and hornbeams.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/fashion/FIELDNOTES/FIELDNOTES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub