ork', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bf16bf6-1916-58d1-84bd-72aecf10f97e', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bf16bf6-1916-58d1-84bd-72aecf10f97e'}, {'abstract': 'At a rehearsal for “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” on Friday, the actor Christopher Tierney returned to the scene of an accident that nearly killed him during a performance on Dec. 20.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/lots-of-drama-but-no-fall-during-spider-man-rehearsal/', 'snippet': 'At a rehearsal for “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” on Friday, the actor Christopher Tierney returned to the scene of an accident that nearly killed him during a performance on Dec. 20.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rehearsal for Broadway’s “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” on Friday was no normal slice of scene work for Christopher Tierney, the actor who fell 30 feet from a platform and nearly died during a performance on Dec. 20. Mr. Tierney, who plays the masked super-hero in stunt sequences, flew Thursday for the first time since rejoining the cast last week; Friday’s work call had him back at the moment in the musical when his accident occurred.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lots of Drama, but No Fall, During Spider-Man Rehearsal', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohl, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKinley, Philip William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taymor, Julie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tierney, Christopher', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foxwoods Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T22:59:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c75a5a87-4a9a-5fe1-83cf-56c290dd63fa', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c75a5a87-4a9a-5fe1-83cf-56c290dd63fa'}, {'abstract': 'A plate of glass can look like clear skies to a bird, especially if it is near greenery. Turns out the Javits center isn’t much of a problem.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/bird-week-the-lure-and-danger-of-the-high-line/', 'snippet': 'A plate of glass can look like clear skies to a bird, especially if it is near greenery. Turns out the Javits center isn’t much of a problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bright lights, big city: That combination draws birds to the same kinds of places the characters in a Jay McInerney novel would go if all that ’80s dissipation did not do them in first. There is, for example, the High Line elevated park. Except that at least one spot along the High Line is not for the birds — for them, it can be deadly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bird Week| The Lure and Danger of the High Line', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Glass', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Line (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postal Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windows', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': '