multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/494a466b-0022-58c6-99b9-1fc51fe6c26e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/494a466b-0022-58c6-99b9-1fc51fe6c26e'}, {'abstract': 'In the first episode of “The Office” without its star Steve Carell, ratings for the NBC sitcom were down two million viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/arts/television/nbcs-office-takes-a-hit.html', 'snippet': 'In the first episode of “The Office” without its star Steve Carell, ratings for the NBC sitcom were down two million viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first episode of “The Office” without its star Steve Carell, ratings for the NBC sitcom were down two million viewers and some 15 percent in the demographic of viewers 18 to 49 compared with a week ago. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 6.8 million people tuned in to the episode at 9 p.m., down from 8.4 million previously. For the night, NBC ranked a distant fourth, earning its second- and third-highest ratings at 9:30 and 10 for “Parks and Recreation” (5.1 million) and “30 Rock” (4.2 million). Fox dominated prime time as usual thanks to an elimination episode of “American Idol” at 8 (20.8 million) in which Jacob Lusk failed to make the cut for the Top 4. CBS was second with “CSI” at 9 (10.5 million) and “The Mentalist” at 10 (13.6 million). ABC finished in third place, attracting its best ratings at 9 for “Grey’s Anatomy” (9.9 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NBC’s ‘Office’ Takes a Hit', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'NBC’s ‘Office’ Takes a Hit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-06T23:42:35+0000', 'document_type': 'ar