c Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T00:58:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e9c38e1-9990-58ff-b6aa-4f5221e0181b', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e9c38e1-9990-58ff-b6aa-4f5221e0181b'}, {'abstract': 'The correlation between a presidential candidate’s standing in the earliest polls and the candidate’s eventual share of the popular vote is far from perfect, but it is also far above zero.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/06/a-brief-history-of-primary-polling-part-iii/', 'snippet': 'The correlation between a presidential candidate’s standing in the earliest polls and the candidate’s eventual share of the popular vote is far from perfect, but it is also far above zero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a month ago, I published a pair of articles (see Part I and Part II) that focused on the predictive value of polls conducted in the early stage of the presidential primary process. Specifically, it looked at national polls conducted in January through June of the year before the primaries — for example, polls taken in January through June 1987 for the 1988 nomination races.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brief History of Primary Polling, Part III', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4336bec2-13f4-58fa-8186-000a180bf8a2', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4336bec2-13f4-58fa-8186-000a180bf8a2'}, {'abstract': 'Word of Seve Ballesteros’s rapidly worsening health swept across golf courses around the world, and its impact was felt by many of the players who had competed against him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/sports/golf/07seve.html', 'snippet': 'Word of Seve Ballesteros’s rapidly worsening health swept across golf courses around the world, and its impact was felt by many of the players who had competed against him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLOTTE, N.C. — An ocean apart, at a PGA Tour event in North Carolina and a PGA European Tour event in Madrid, professional golfers had Seve Ballesteros on their minds Friday. Word of his rapidly worsening health had swept across the golf courses, and its impact was felt by many of the players who had competed against him.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/sports/seve-2/seve-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 670, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/07/sports/seve-2/seve-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/07/sports/seve-2/seve-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Golfers Reflect as Ballesteros’s Condition Deteriorates', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Golfers Reflect as Ballesteros’s Condition Deteriorates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Vijay', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Love, Davis III', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ballesteros, Seve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Paul', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jimenez, Miguel Angel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PGA Tour Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Sergio', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mickelson, Phil', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Dorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28dd5cb4-f1e0-5729-8a95-ece6ffd42974', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28dd5cb4-f1e0-5729-8a95-ece6ffd42974'}, {'abstract': 'Bouchon Wine Bar Café, a block from the Pleasantville train station, has a speakeasy feel and a substantial wine cellar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/nyregion/a-hideaway-for-wine.html', 'snippet': 'Bouchon Wine Bar Café, a block from the Pleasantville train station, has a speakeasy feel and a substantial wine cellar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE dimly lighted storefront gives Bouchon Wine Bar Café a speakeasy feel, like an exciting hideaway, although it is located only a block from the homey Pleasantville train station. But there’s more to Bouchon than its narrow facade implies: its long, deep space has been divided smartly into cozy areas fitted with couches, a few high and low tables and a full bar. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Hideaway for Wine', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Pleasantville', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Hideaway for Wine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pleasantville (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:13:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By M. H. Reed', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d065f5e-5690-57dc-89ed-7b71dc2c1d54', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d065f5e-5690-57dc-89ed-7b71dc2c1d54'}, {'abstract': 'Jorge Adriazola, the chef at Cienega, takes the familiar food of Spanish-speaking countries and makes it new again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/nyregion/cienega-latin-restaurant-in-new-rochelle-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jorge Adriazola, the chef at Cienega, takes the familiar food of Spanish-speaking countries and makes it new again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE chupe at Cienega, a saffron-infused yellow, is lovely just to look at. But sample a spoonful of this Peruvian chowder and the pleasure really begins. Filled with firm shrimp, sweet corn kernels, bits of green and red peppers — even a poached quail egg — the soup is somehow both strong and subtle, rich and light: an interplay of flavors and textures. It is a fitting introduction to Cienega’s complex Latin cuisine and a metaphor for the overall dining experience there.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, '