ine': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:42:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3b679e6-6319-551d-9461-2934babf4d12', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3b679e6-6319-551d-9461-2934babf4d12'}, {'abstract': 'The party’s first debate demonstrated why Republicans are having a hard time delivering an appealing message ahead of the 2012 campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/opinion/07sat3.html', 'snippet': 'The party’s first debate demonstrated why Republicans are having a hard time delivering an appealing message ahead of the 2012 campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even John Boehner, the speaker of the House and Republican chieftain, didn’t bother to watch his party’s presidential debate on Thursday, and it’s hard to blame him. Only five candidates showed up, none with impressive followings. And what they said demonstrated why Republicans are having such a hard time delivering a coherent and appealing message ahead of the 2012 campaign.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Search of a Republican Presidential Field', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Search of a Republican Presidential Field', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e102050-1dec-566a-9968-0070b0d6ce7a', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e102050-1dec-566a-9968-0070b0d6ce7a'}, {'abstract': 'CUNY’s trustees should respect free speech and reverse their decision to block an honorary degree for Tony Kushner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/opinion/07sat4.html', 'snippet': 'CUNY’s trustees should respect free speech and reverse their decision to block an honorary degree for Tony Kushner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trustees of the City University of New York got it exactly backward this week. They supported the political agenda of an intolerant board member and shunned one of America’s most important playwrights. They should have embraced the artist and tossed out the board member.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CUNY Shamed Itself', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CUNY Shamed Itself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Tony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City University of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiesenfeld, Jeffrey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T01:44:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mate