I might take a minute to explain something I sometimes say. Once in a while I mention, in passing, that the Bush administration saw torturing people as a plus, not a cost. And whenever I do, some readers clutch their breasts and reach for the smelling salts: how dare I say such a thing?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shadow of the Torturers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T12:10:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e35ab7c7-11f5-5a10-8e08-95e9710eb30b', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e35ab7c7-11f5-5a10-8e08-95e9710eb30b'}, {'abstract': 'Bob Baffert’s Derby horse Midnight Interlude bit the trainer Phillip Thomas twice on the arm. In the world of racetrack superstitions, Baffert views this as a very good sign.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/a-healers-horse-repays-the-favor/', 'snippet': 'Bob Baffert’s Derby horse Midnight Interlude bit the trainer Phillip Thomas twice on the arm. In the world of racetrack superstitions, Baffert views this as a very good sign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Midnight Interlude wins the Kentucky Derby, Bob Baffert should place his friend Philip Thomas on the payroll through the Belmont. In 2002, Baffert’s Derby horse War Emblem bit his groom before winning the Derby. He bit the same groom before winning the Preakness. Word is that the groom disappeared before the Belmont which War Emblem lost. This week, Baffert’s Derby horse Midnight Interlude bit Phillip Thomas twice on the arm. In the world of racetrack superstitions, Baffert vie