'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/weekinreview/FLOOD3/FLOOD3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting the Mississippi', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi River', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T20:06:48+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Week in Review', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/034003da-d459-5d8e-b6df-eee1a5937a1a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/034003da-d459-5d8e-b6df-eee1a5937a1a'}, {'abstract': 'There have been floods before. A 1993 report and a selection of photographs recall some of the worst.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/weekinreview/08mississippi.html', 'snippet': 'There have been floods before. A 1993 report and a selection of photographs recall some of the worst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rain-swollen Mississippi River crested to an all-time high last week as it surged past Cairo, Ill., exceeding the 1937 record by 2 feet. Government engineers were left with two choices: Let Cairo flood or explode a levee to save the town. They blew up the levee. But while Cairo was saved, other communities may not be so lucky.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Untamable Mississippi River', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The River Untamable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilkerson, Isabel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T20:06:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Wilkerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilkerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0184f3ce-ab68-51f8-afc5-a2c8708e17f7', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0184f3ce-ab68-51f8-afc5-a2c8708e17f7'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to our coverage of the 137th Kentucky Derby.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/welcome-to-the-137th-kentucky-derby/', 'snippet': 'Welcome to our coverage of the 137th Kentucky Derby.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Animal Kingdom, who had never run a race on dirt, beat Nehro by two and three-quarter lengths to win the 137th running of the Kentucky Derby on Saturday in his fifth career start. Trained by Graham Motion and owned by Team Valor International, Animal Kingdom had won twice and finished second twice in his previous four starts. Mucho Macho Man finished third. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Animal Kingdom Wins Kentucky Derby', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albarado, Robby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Motion, Graham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Velazquez, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T20:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Hoppert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoppert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/71d0bee2-499b-5ee4-8146-31fa0a4325bc', 'word_count': 1488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71d0bee2-499b-5ee4-8146-31fa0a4325bc'}, {'abstract': 'We have all the money, but why should we pay all the taxes?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/federal-tax-chutzpah/', 'snippet': 'We have all the money, but why should we pay all the taxes?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chutzpah, according to the traditional definition, is when you murder your mother and father, then plead for mercy from the court on the grounds that you’re an orphan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Federal Tax Chutzpah', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T20:15:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cf37a9d-6e69-5136-b2a8-f2817415daf5', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cf37a9d-6e69-5136-b2a8-f2817415daf5'}, {'abstract': 'Juan Marichal, 73, never won a World Series with the Giants, but he was the winning pitcher is a classic 1963 game, winning a 16-inning duel with Warren Spahn.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/30-seconds-with-juan-marichal/', 'snippet': 'Juan Marichal, 73, never won a World Series with the Giants, but he was the winning pitcher is a classic 1963 game, winning a 16-inning duel with Warren Spahn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1963, Juan Marichal was making a name for himself, fresh off a no-hitter and on the way to a 25-win season for the San Francisco Giants. On July 2, his Milwaukee Braves opponent was Warren Spahn, 42 and nearing the end of his career. Marichal and Spahn pitched scoreless inning after scoreless inning until the 16th, when Willie Mays homered to win it for the Giants. The game is celebrated in Jim Kaplan’s book “The Greatest Game Ever Pitched.” Marichal, 73, now has a farm in the Dominican Republic and still remembers the game vividly. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '30 Seconds With Juan Marichal', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marichal, Juan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T20:17:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1facef4-cef7-5384-a2dc-4d87e87ac430', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1facef4-cef7-5384-a2dc-4d87e87ac430'}, {'abstract': 'The Castro is becoming less gay, and the huge success of the restaurant and club Lime is evidence of that, as well as being the cause of more than a few complaints from neighbors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/us/08bcjames.html', 'snippet': 'The Castro is becoming less gay, and the huge success of the restaurant and club Lime is evidence of that, as well as being the cause of more than a few complaints from neighbors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s an old saying in San Francisco: Market Street is straight, until it reaches the Castro.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source'