, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T21:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c622c23-f239-5574-b92a-c4688772b737', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c622c23-f239-5574-b92a-c4688772b737'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel A. Finan opposes us.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/opposition/', 'snippet': 'Daniel A. Finan opposes us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUNDAY’S PUZZLE — Some people just like to be difficult. You say day, I say night. Tomato, tomahto. You get the idea. In today’s puzzle, however, Daniel Finan has decided to play both sides of the coin, and I think it works very well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday: Working in Opposition', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/049191d8-bf6e-5496-bc2f-28523af9f85d', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/049191d8-bf6e-5496-bc2f-28523af9f85d'}, {'abstract': 'Exactly 50 years after he made his famous “vast wasteland” speech, Newton Minow may have had more influence in other areas of television — and the Internet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/us/08cncwarren.html', 'snippet': 'Exactly 50 years after he made his famous “vast wasteland” speech, Newton Minow may have had more influence in other areas of television — and the Internet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Warren writes a column for the Chicago News Cooperative.\xa0', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Never Mind the ‘Vast Wasteland.’ Minow Has More to Say', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Never Mind the ‘Vast Wasteland.’ Minow Has More to Say.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:12:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7212025e-c314-5403-8638-99060cc070cc', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7212025e-c314-5403-8638-99060cc070cc'}, {'abstract': 'The story of Mr. Celery is too amusing not to share. As the team broadcaster John Sadak wrote in an e-mail, “More casual fans know the Blue Rocks for Mr. Celery over anything else.”', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/pure-logo-wackiness-in-wilmington/', 'snippet': 'The story of Mr. Celery is too amusing not to share. As the team broadcaster John Sadak wrote in an e-mail, “More casual fans know the Blue Rocks for Mr. Celery over anything else.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Fort Wayne, Ind., fans root for the snarling apple logo of the Tin Caps, in honor of Johnny Appleseed. In Las Vegas, home of the 51s, folks cheer for an alien with a baseball-shaped head. A bear with a suitcase once promoted the Asheville (N.C.) Tourists, who now use a smiling moon wearing sunglasses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pure Logo Wackiness in Wilmington', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Logos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wilmington (Del)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:14:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/eeb49773-1d0e-54cb-9b4f-778bd832c5e3', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eeb49773-1d0e-54cb-9b4f-778bd832c5e3'}, {'abstract': 'A Roman Catholic priest is leading the plan to build five group homes in the Back of the Yards neighborhood for at-risk children who expect to reunite with their parents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/us/08cncyards.html', 'snippet': 'A Roman Catholic priest is leading the plan to build five group homes in the Back of the Yards neighborhood for at-risk children who expect to reunite with their parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than 20 years, the Rev. Bruce Wellems has been a cheerleader, a confidant, a voice of reason and, at times, the only advocate for troubled children in Back of the Yards. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCYARDS/08CNCYARDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCYARDS/08CNCYARDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCYARDS/08CNCYARDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Group Homes to Nurture At-Risk Youths', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Group Homes To Nurture At-Risk Youths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boys Town', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daley, Richard M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:18:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meribah Knight', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meribah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knight', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/628e0dc9-801a-5217-bd1e-56a42d71c06b', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/628e0dc9-801a-5217-bd1e-56a42d71c06b'}, {'abstract': 'A highly praised schools bill that sailed through the Senate has hit snags as it heads for the House. For one thing, Chicago Teachers Union is raising a red flag.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/us/08cnclightford.html', 'snippet': 'A highly praised schools bill that sailed through the Senate has hit snags as it heads for the House. For one thing, Chicago Teachers Union is raising a red flag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sweeping schools bill that advocacy groups lauded as a landmark and that the federal secretary of education, Arne Duncan, described as a national model is hitting snags in the Illinois House.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCLIGHTFORD/08CNCLIGHTFORD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCLIGHTFORD/08CNCLIGHTFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/us/08CNCLIGHTFORD/08CNCLIGHTFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Highly Praised Schools Bill Hits Some Snags', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Highly Praised Schools Bill Hits Some Snags', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Teachers Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duncan, Arne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Rahm', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:21:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristen McQueary', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McQueary', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d34b948-c0f0-538b-9f6c-dc5f03626606', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d34b948-c0f0-538b-9f6c-dc5f03626606'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my Mother’s Day column saying that family planning is a cheap and effective way to save women’s lives worldwide.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/reader-comments-on-my-family-planning-column/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my Mother’s Day column saying that family planning is a cheap and effective way to save women’s lives worldwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column is from Somaliland but makes an argument closer to home: that Republican cuts in family planning assistance are short-sighted and will lead to more maternal deaths. And on Mother’s Day (or, as I like to style it, Mothers’ Day), shouldn’t saving mothers’ lives be a top priority?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Comments on my Family Planning Column', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:23:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b21a7ebf-2f98-5ed8-9f4d-5b629751f6fe', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b21a7ebf-2f98-5ed8-9f4d-5b629751f6fe'}, {'abstract': 'What will the Democratic Party stand for if it follows Republicans in covering up campaign donors?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/opinion/08sun1.html', 'snippet': 'What will the Democratic Party stand for if it follows Republicans in covering up campaign donors?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year several pro-Republican advocacy groups degraded the Congressional elections by spending at least $138 million in secret donations on advertisements. The public did not know which lobbying interests gave money, or how much, or what they would demand in return. But the donations became a significant factor in the Republican gains in the House and the Senate.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Democrats, Seduced by Secret Dollars', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Democrats, Seduced by Secret Dollars: What will the party stand for if it follows Republicans in covering up campaign donors?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Priorities USA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Priorities USA Action', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-07T22:25:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 's