ghterhouse and a new inspection system will make it easier to get poultry locally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAROLE PECK can’t forget the horseradish incident: 15 quarts of locally grown and preserved horseradish in the walk-in cooler of her well-known Woodbury restaurant, Good News Cafe, confiscated by the local health inspector.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/nyregion/08DINECT2/08DINECT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/nyregion/08DINECT2/08DINECT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/nyregion/08DINECT2/08DINECT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rules and Reality Test Chefs Who Think Locally', 'kicker': 'Connecticut Dining | Farm to Table', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules and Reality Test Chefs Who Think Locally', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farmers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T00:31:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Ellen Spiegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': 'Ellen', 'lastname': 'Spiegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organiz