at did not please the crowd.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing.html', 'snippet': 'Manny Pacquiao retained his World Boxing Association welterweight title in a bout against Shane Mosley that did not please the crowd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — For much of his welterweight title defense on Saturday, Manny Pacquiao heard from the MGM Grand crowd a unified sound rarely belted at his boxing matches. As Pacquiao and Shane Mosley tapped gloves, made small talk and generally engaged in a glorified sparring session, the crowd, once rowdy, once standing, once into it, simply booed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing1_span/08boxing1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing1_span/08boxing1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing1_span/08boxing1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing1_span/08boxing1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/08/sports/08boxing1_span/08boxing1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pacquiao Takes Decision, but Disappoints Fans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pacquiao Takes Decision, but Doesn’t Win Over Fans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pacquiao, Manny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boxing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mosley, Shane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T05:51:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ede70ebf-db53-54e8-bd1f-fa218fdb387c', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ede70ebf-db53-54e8-bd1f-fa218fdb387c'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the next generation of NASA space vehicles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2008/12/29/science/123008-Nasa_index.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the next generation of NASA space vehicles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at the next generation of NASA space vehicles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/12/29/science/30space_75a.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2008/12/29/science/30space_75a.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Images of NASA’s Future', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SPACE SHUTTLE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T06:01:34+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ba7d414c-e9f0-5c25-bb6b-8ec39d67f0fb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ba7d414c-e9f0-5c25-bb6b-8ec39d67f0fb'}, {'abstract': 'An insurgent leader led an hours-long revolt inside a Baghdad jail, security authorities said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/09/world/middleeast/09iraqprison.html', 'snippet': 'An insurgent leader led an hours-long revolt inside a Baghdad jail, security authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — An insurgent leader accused of plotting the deadly siege against an Iraqi church last autumn led a bloody revolt for several hours early on Sunday morning inside a Baghdad prison, the security authorities said. The prison holds some of the people suspected of being the country’s most violent terrorists.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/world/middleeast/09iraq/09iraq-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/world/middleeast/09iraq/09iraq-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/09/world/middleeast/09iraq/09iraq-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Officers and Inmates Are Killed in Iraq Jail Revolt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Officers and Inmates Killed in Iraqi Prison Revolt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T09:53:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66f5dd05-5c1f-55c0-a5a6-8b45c6e03449', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66f5dd05-5c1f-55c0-a5a6-8b45c6e03449'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition at the Capitoline Museums in Rome, titled “Portraits: The Many Faces of Power,” charts the development of Roman portraiture from the Republic through the Empire.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/ancient-roman-portraiture-comes-to-life/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition at the Capitoline Museums in Rome, titled “Portraits: The Many Faces of Power,” charts the development of Roman portraiture from the Republic through the Empire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Romans expanded their territory and conquered the Mediterranean, they, in turn, were captivated by the portraiture of their subjugated peoples. Images of private citizens, politicians and philosophers in Etruria (what is now central Italy) and Greece influenced the way the Romans portrayed themselves in their own artwork. These external factors were blended with local traditions and gave birth to a portraiture style that was uniquely Roman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ancient Roman Portraiture Comes to Life', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T10:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Parla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cac081d-8df0-57bb-b64c-83836cf04ad1', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cac081d-8df0-57bb-b64c-83836cf04ad1'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of readers’ photos of bird habitats in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/bird-week-urban-bird-habitats/', 'snippet': 'A slide show of readers’ photos of bird habitats in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bird Week | Urban Bird Habitats', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T11:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e0dbf32-20ab-59f2-8282-6ec7d3f7e7ac', 'word_count': 39, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e0dbf32-20ab-59f2-8282-6ec7d3f7e7ac'}, {'abstract': 'A Dot Earth reader makes the case that humans need a work-around for deeply rooted traits that hinder responses to some kinds of risks.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/is-a-human-here-and-now-bias-clouding-climate-reasoning/', 'snippet': 'A Dot Earth reader makes the case that humans need a work-around for deeply rooted traits that hinder responses to some kinds of risks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a “Your Dot” contribution from Jacob Tanenbaum, a computer technology teacher from Tappan, N.Y., who sent the following thoughts after reading “On Birth Certificates, Climate Risk and an Inconvenient Mind“:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is a Human “Here and Now” Bias Clouding Climate Reasoning?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Edge Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T11:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c39e4be-f0ea-5d74-ba2a-c82313368696', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c39e4be-f0ea-5d74-ba2a-c82313368696'}, {'abstract': 'The more we rely on our phones as our primary communication devices, the more the chances of spectacularly hilarious misfires increases.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/when-technology-attacks/', 'snippet': 'The more we rely on our phones as our primary communication devices, the more the chances of spectacularly hilarious misfires increases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently received a panicked text from a friend who had bumped into his handsome crush on the street, an unrequited love interest who had stood him up after the two made plans for a casual date. He wanted to know what he should do. I hastily tapped out a reply. I tried to send “RUNNNNNN!” which my phone mistook for “SEEEEEEEEEEEEEE” and I accidentally sent that instead. My poor friend sent back a frantic “HUH!?” and I had to send a handful more to clarify and calm him down.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Technology Attacks', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T11:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3555e5a2-f5f9-5e6f-bac9-0ead2ca613b9', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3555e5a2-f5f9-5e6f-bac9-0ead2ca613b9'}, {'abstract': 'Alanis Morisette shares her Mother’s Day wish list.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/mother-knows-best-alanis-morissette/', 'snippet': 'Alanis Morisette shares her Mother’s Day wish list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ironic or not, the singer-songwriter Alanis Morissette does not welcome rain or clouds this Mother’s Day. The Los Angeles-based mother is looking forward to repeating her family’s one-year-old Mother’s Day tradition of picnicking with snacks on the beach and nestling in a hammock with her husband, Mario Treadway, and son, Ever Imre, also known as “lil’ man.” She may be “new to the momma gang,” as she calls it, but Morissette isn’t opposed to enjoying every minute of her son and the holiday’s perks. On her wish list? A Gracious Home picnic basket, Lainey blanket and beach bag from Heather Heron for the family’s beach excursion and a Loup Charmant slip dress. Older traditions die hard. “I always sing ‘Happy Mother’s Day’ to my mom in the tune of ‘Happy birthday,’ ” Morissette says. “It’s a sweet little offering—a thank you for having me.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mother Knows Best | Alanis Morissette', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T12:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1856a13d-b257-5afe-be0d-aa7041d515d2', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1856a13d-b257-5afe-be0d-aa7041d515d2'}, {'abstract': 'Alien Skin’s Exposure 3 let you see how your photo might look if shot on a variety of 35mm-film stocks.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/mama-didnt-take-your-kodachrome-she-just-moved-it/', 'snippet': 'Alien Skin’s Exposure 3 let you see how your photo might look if shot on a variety of 35mm-film stocks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the olden days of photography — maybe 10 years ago — you carefully picked the film you used because it contributed as much to the look of your images as lighting or a lens filter. Each film had it own distinctive grain, color and contrast. If you wanted eye-popping greens for scenery, Fuji Velvia 50 was the stuff. Flattering skin tones? Kodak Portra. The list goes on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mama Didn’t Take Your Kodachrome, She Just Moved It', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Agfa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eastman Kodak Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T12:04:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/203067bf-a878-557d-ac9a-3957814cb697', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/203067bf-a878-557d-ac9a-3957814cb697'}, {'abstract': 'The crackdown seemed to mark a decisive turn in an uprising that has posed a grave challenge to President Bashar al-Assad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/09/world/middleeast/09syria.html', 'snippet': 'The crackdown seemed to mark a decisive turn in an uprising that has posed a grave challenge to President Bashar al-Assad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — A military crackdown on Syria’s seven-week uprising escalated Sunday, with reinforcements sent to two cities, more forces deployed in a southern town and nearly all communications severed to besieged locales, activists and human rights groups said. Fourteen people were killed in the city of Homs, they said, and hundreds were arrested.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/global-home/09syria-map/09syria-map-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 260, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria Broadens Deadly Crackdown on Protesters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crackdown Is Escalating Across Syria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T13:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Shadid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shadid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ef8e387-9981-536e-9351-2f0930fe78a9', 'word_count': 1112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ef8e387-9981-536e-9351-2f0930fe78a9'}, {'abstract': 'Can’t find it with a flashlight.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/the-inflation-monster-under-the-bed/', 'snippet': 'Can’t find it with a flashlight.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m glad to see Greg Mankiw agreeing with me on the absence of any inflation risk in the current environment. Maybe he should have a word with everyone else in his party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Inflation Monster Under the Bed', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T13:46:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b03b8672-06d0-5ab5-b6b9-bdfd5e25d2f7', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b03b8672-06d0-5ab5-b6b9-bdfd5e25d2f7'}, {'abstract': 'Japan remains committed to nuclear power despite the nuclear plant crisis, Prime Minister Naoto Kan said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/09/world/asia/09japan.html', 'snippet': 'Japan remains committed to nuclear power despite the nuclear plant crisis, Prime Minister Naoto Kan said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Japan remains committed to nuclear power despite the crisis at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Station, Prime Minister Naoto Kan indicated Sunday, as workers moved closer to repairing the crippled plant by opening the doors of a damaged reactor building.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/world/JAPAN/JAPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Reaffirms Nuclear Energy Use', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Remains Committed to Nuclear Energy, Prime Minister Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T15:21:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c40dd6a1-c7ec-5e10-9890-a5df4dd72295', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c40dd6a1-c7ec-5e10-9890-a5df4dd72295'}, {'abstract': 'The commission overseeing nuclear plant safety needs an overhaul.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/overhaul-time-for-u-s-nuclear-overseers/', 'snippet': 'The commission overseeing nuclear plant safety needs an overhaul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m convinced that the United States will be better off keeping existing nuclear power stations running, where their management can be demonstrated to be reliable, rather than initiating a decades-long decommissioning process that would not resolve community concerns about spent fuel and many other sources of risk. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Overhaul Time for U.S. Nuclear Overseers', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Regulatory Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-08T15:46:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d8dc1e8-24e1-539e-80ed-251c7ccdbbd7', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d8dc1e8-24e1-539e-80ed-251c7ccdbbd7'}, {'abstract': 'Long-suppressed sectarian animo