-f2c427d6fc35', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff79b4e3-1671-5639-9485-f2c427d6fc35'}, {'abstract': 'Denying the playwright an honorary degree may have been a dumb idea, but it is not a violation of  academic freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/the-kushner-flap-much-ado-about-nothing/', 'snippet': 'Denying the playwright an honorary degree may have been a dumb idea, but it is not a violation of  academic freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On May 5, the historian Ellen Schrecker of Yeshiva University gave back an honorary degree she had received from John Jay College because the playwright Tony Kushner had been denied the same honor by the CUNY Board of Trustees.\xa0 At the urging of a trustee who objected to Kushner’s\xa0 views on Israel, the nomination, which had been forwarded by the faculty and administration of John Jay, was tabled.\xa0 (Kushner had been informed of the impending honor.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Kushner Flap: Much Ado About Nothing', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACADEMIC FREEDOM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Tony', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T01:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b64709ca-1c0c-5f29-8328-af8a5eaf2c0d', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b64709ca-1c0c-5f29-8328-af8a5eaf2c0d'}, {'abstract': 'In the Appalachians, the Civil War was often literally brother against brother.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/08/family-feuds/', 'snippet': 'In the Appalachians, the Civil War was often literally brother against brother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The narrative of the Civil War revolves around the metaphor of neighbor against neighbor, brother against brother. But in the mountains of the Upper South, this was no metaphor: counties, towns and even families split between the two sides, and then set upon each other with gruesome fury in one of the bloodiest — and least understood — theaters of the war. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Feuds', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnston, Joseph E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'APPALACHIAN REGION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T01:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian D. McKnight', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e8c7e26-56c1-5de2-baec-13a383e36147', 'word_count': 1