, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T10:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1669ae6b-dcc5-5ec5-a20a-1ddc723b7d4d', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1669ae6b-dcc5-5ec5-a20a-1ddc723b7d4d'}, {'abstract': 'Art new and old collide at a show, through June 20 at the Museo Lázaro-Galdiano in Madrid, aptly titled “What’s That Doing Here?”', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/09/collections-old-and-new-collide-at-madrid-museum/', 'snippet': 'Art new and old collide at a show, through June 20 at the Museo Lázaro-Galdiano in Madrid, aptly titled “What’s That Doing Here?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It can be a bit of a buzz kill to walk into one of those cozy little “time-capsule” museums that feature the private collection of an enlightened prince or recreate life in that particular place during an earlier century, only to discover that the museum has brought in contemporary art to “dialogue” with their historical holdings. Such presentations can easily break the spell of time travel that so-called house museums typically offer, without helping the visitor appreciate or understand the art of either epoch. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Madrid, Disparate Art Collections Collide', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T10:00:59+0000', 'document_ty