et': 'Jon M. Huntsman Jr. says that if he runs for president, he will not use his family’s fortune to pay for the campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLUMBIA, S.C. – If Jon M. Huntsman Jr. decides to join the field of Republican presidential candidates, he would be among the wealthiest contenders. But he vowed in an interview here that he would not use his family’s fortune to pay for a campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Huntsman Says No ‘Self-Financing’ if He Runs', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T14:51:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny and Jim Rutenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd8eba13-7ee3-507e-aaf0-69a13693c564', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd8eba13-7ee3-507e-aaf0-69a13693c564'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times and CBS News began the first newspaper-television polling partnership between two major national news organizations in 1975.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/09/us/pollpage-intro.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Times and CBS News began the first newspaper-television polling partnership between two major national news organizations in 1975.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times and CBS News began the first newspaper-television polling partnership between two major national news organizations in 1975. A "CBS Evening News" report on American attitudes toward President Ford\'s response to the possible default of New York City was introduced by Walter Cronkite on Nov. 4, 1975. The next morning, readers of The Times saw a front-page article by Robert Reinhold titled, "Poll Finds Public Thinks Ford Minimizes City Peril." ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New York Times Polling Practices: An Introduction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T14:54:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Connelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Connelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a989384-dc4e-5da5-9633-ca1a3e8c2622', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a989384-dc4e-5da5-9633-ca1a3e8c2622'}, {'abstract': 'A Texas lawyer helped get the state to increase payouts to those wrongfully convicted. But his clients got the tab.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/us/10exonerate.html', 'snippet': 'A Texas lawyer helped get the state to increase payouts to those wrongfully convicted. But his clients got the tab.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALLAS — If you are a wrongly convicted inmate in Texas and are exonerated, you stand to receive a very rewarding apology from the state.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/us/SUB-Exonerate-1/SUB-Exonerate-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 6