, 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dac866e4-fdaa-5ec1-a27c-6e941df99c24', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dac866e4-fdaa-5ec1-a27c-6e941df99c24'}, {'abstract': 'The Miller Theater’s Composer Portraits series ended on Thursday with work by Joan Tower performed by Curtis 20/21.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/arts/music/composer-portraits-joan-tower-by-curtis-2021-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Miller Theater’s Composer Portraits series ended on Thursday with work by Joan Tower performed by Curtis 20/21.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Miller Theater’s Composer Portraits series covered a lot of ground this year, as it moved from Pierre Boulez’s elaborate serialist explorations to Julia Wolfe’s post-Minimalist string works and Chaya Czernowin’s arcane experiments, with other stylistic detours along the way. The series ended on Thursday evening with chamber music by Joan Tower, a composer whose directness and eclecticism make her music instantly accessible, and whose imaginative sense of development — often by way of insistent but evolving rhythms and surprising juxtapositions — gives it an original, distinctive personality. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/curtis/curtis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/curtis/curtis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/curt