, 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-09T21:12:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/071ec3ca-fdcc-5a90-ad6e-cd770f13797b', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/071ec3ca-fdcc-5a90-ad6e-cd770f13797b'}, {'abstract': 'The new Manton Memorial Organ at the Church of the Ascension in Greenwich Village, built in France with 6,183 pipes, was inaugurated on Thursday with a concert by Jon Gillock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/arts/music/jon-gillock-inaugurates-organ-at-church-of-ascension-review.html', 'snippet': 'The new Manton Memorial Organ at the Church of the Ascension in Greenwich Village, built in France with 6,183 pipes, was inaugurated on Thursday with a concert by Jon Gillock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pipe organ is a mysterious and complex instrument, whose flight deck of keys, knobs, tabs and pedals require the performer to be pianist, pilot and tap dancer simultaneously. Organists often work diligently in the shadows, their consoles hidden from view. But the handsome new Manton Memorial Organ at the Church of the Ascension in Greenwich Village was on full display for its inauguration on Thursday evening, with a concert by Jon Gillock. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/MANTON/MANTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/MANTON/MANTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'heig