ly like themes like this one. At first glance, it may not seem too difficult to come up with an idea like this, but look again. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday: Assists and Steals', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/183ac9f0-6340-569b-93c6-e459dd7c3fb5', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/183ac9f0-6340-569b-93c6-e459dd7c3fb5'}, {'abstract': '“By the Way, Meet Vera Stark” imagines the back story of one of those talented black actresses seen on 1930s movie screens almost exclusively in the roles of maids, slaves or mammies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/theater/reviews/by-the-way-meet-vera-stark-at-second-stage-review.html', 'snippet': '“By the Way, Meet Vera Stark” imagines the back story of one of those talented black actresses seen on 1930s movie screens almost exclusively in the roles of maids, slaves or mammies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A biting wind of realism, blowing both hot and cold, sweeps into the second act of “By the Way, Meet Vera Stark,” the fitful comedy by Lynn Nottage that opened on Monday night at Second Stage Theater. Reality, in this instance, assumes the fantastical form of an aging, teetering woman in a psychedelic-print dress and Technicolor makeup, applied with a trowel. She slurs when she speaks or sings, and you just know that her vision is blurry. But as embodied by Sanaa Lathan, she is so sharply focused she stings.', 'pri