person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e8807f7-5f91-5f81-9da3-114142fb8c70', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e8807f7-5f91-5f81-9da3-114142fb8c70'}, {'abstract': 'It is outrageous that Syria is even being discussed for membership to the United Nations Human Rights Council considering its bloody crackdown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/opinion/10tue3.html', 'snippet': 'It is outrageous that Syria is even being discussed for membership to the United Nations Human Rights Council considering its bloody crackdown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Syrians have shown extraordinary courage, defying a bloody government crackdown to demand greater political rights and freedom. Their courage, and their blood, should shame the many governments that are cynically supporting Syria’s election later this month to the United Nations Human Rights Council. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'They Should Be Condemning Syria', 'kicker': 'Editorial ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They Should Be Condemning Syria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Human Rights Council', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:12:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbdc57fa-96f9-5c2e-bad7-4c35b944235d', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbdc57fa-96f9-5c2e-bad7-4c35b944235d'}, {'abstract': 'Motion-sensing cameras on the island of Sumatra captured video of two families of endangered tigers in a lowland forest scheduled for destruction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/world/asia/10briefs-ART-Indonesia.html', 'snippet': 'Motion-sensing cameras on the island of Sumatra captured video of two families of endangered tigers in a lowland forest scheduled for destruction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Motion-sensing cameras on the island of Sumatra captured video of two families of endangered tigers in a lowland forest scheduled for destruction, the World Wildlife Fund said Monday. Twelve tigers, including two adult females with cubs, passed in front of the cameras over two months between the Bukit Tigapuluh National Park and nearby mountains. Asia Pulp and Paper has plans to clear large areas of the forest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indonesia: Tigers Spotted in Forest Where Paper Mill Saws Are Coming', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indonesia: Tigers Spotted in Forest Where Paper Mill Saws Are Coming', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Pulp and Paper', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:14:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/25321d6a-a3d5-533d-89fc-8414e0ef0091', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25321d6a-a3d5-533d-89fc-8414e0ef0091'}, {'abstract': 'More vigorous research is needed to study the long-term effects of the worst nuclear power plant accident in history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/opinion/10tue4.html', 'snippet': 'More vigorous research is needed to study the long-term effects of the worst nuclear power plant accident in history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been 25 years since the worst nuclear power accident in history at the Chernobyl plant in Ukraine, and we still aren’t certain what health damage it may ultimately cause. That gap needs to be filled by a vigorous research program — both to improve readiness to cope with another bad nuclear accident and to enhance understanding of the long-term effects of low doses of radiation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Continuing Questions About Chernobyl', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Continuing Questions About Chernobyl', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chernobyl (Ukraine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radiation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba6a8bde-aad5-5b8e-bd28-3932dc80358a', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba6a8bde-aad5-5b8e-bd28-3932dc80358a'}, {'abstract': 'When “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,” returns to previews, several of Julie Taymor’s ideas will be gone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/theater/spider-man-musical-rewrite-hews-to-comic-book.html', 'snippet': 'When “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,” returns to previews, several of Julie Taymor’s ideas will be gone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bathed in green light, Patrick Page took center stage at the Foxwoods Theater on Broadway and hissed his lines with the sinister glee of a villain back from the dead.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/spiderman-3/spiderman-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/spiderman-3/spiderman-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/spiderman-3/spiderman-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/spiderman-3/spiderman-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/10/arts/spiderman-3/spiderman-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'More Familiar ‘Spider-Man’ Hopes to Bury the Dark', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A More Familiar ‘Spider-Man’ Is Back, Hoping to Bury the Dark', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKinley, Philip William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taymor, Julie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohl, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:22:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f7a32f2-7d43-5519-8c64-4ede2ee9d3a9', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f7a32f2-7d43-5519-8c64-4ede2ee9d3a9'}, {'abstract': 'The Palestinian prime minister said that the Palestinian Authority could not pay its employees on time because of Israel’s decision to withhold the transfer of tax revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/world/middleeast/10briefs-ART-Westbank.html', 'snippet': 'The Palestinian prime minister said that the Palestinian Authority could not pay its employees on time because of Israel’s decision to withhold the transfer of tax revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Palestinian prime minister, Salam Fayyad, left, said Monday that the Palestinian Authority could not pay the salaries of its employees on time this month because of Israel’s decision to withhold the transfer of tax revenue it collects on behalf of the Palestinians. Israel delayed the transfer of almost $90 million after the reconciliation last week between Fatah, the party that dominates the West Bank-based Palestinian Authority, and Hamas, the Islamic militant group that controls Gaza. Israel said it was suspending all such transfers until there were guarantees that none of the money would end up in the treasury of Hamas, which is sworn to Israel’s destruction. The Palestinian Authority has had financial difficulties since late last year and is running a monthly deficit of $30 million. The French government provided it with a special grant of $14.3 million on Monday. On Friday, the European Union said it would advance $121.9 million to help it meet its needs. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'West Bank: Tax Withholding by Israel Will Delay Paychecks for Palestinians', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'West Bank: Tax Withholding by Israel Will Delay Paychecks for Palestinians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fayyad, Salam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palestinian Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:24:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9460b53a-1d1f-5418-9288-b27d801be900', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9460b53a-1d1f-5418-9288-b27d801be900'}, {'abstract': 'India’s Supreme Court on Monday overturned a lower court ruling that called for the country’s most disputed religious site to be divided between Muslims and Hindus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/world/asia/10briefs-ART-India.html', 'snippet': 'India’s Supreme Court on Monday overturned a lower court ruling that called for the country’s most disputed religious site to be divided between Muslims and Hindus.', 'lead_paragraph': ' India’s Supreme Court on Monday overturned a lower court ruling that called for the country’s most disputed religious site to be divided between Muslims and Hindus. The Mughal emperor Babur built a mosque at the site, in Ayodhya, in the 16th century. Some Hindus believe that the same spot is the birthplace of the god Ram, and that Babur had destroyed a Hindu shrine to build his mosque. In 1992, Hindu activists tore down the mosque, unleashing sectarian violence in which about 2,000 people were killed, mostly Muslims. In October a court ruled that the site should be split between the two religions, but the Supreme Court rejected that solution, calling it “strange.” The Supreme Court is expected to rule later on the question of how to resolve control of the site. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India: Supreme Court Rejects Order That Religious Site Should be Shared', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India: Supreme Court Rejects Order That Religious Site Should be Shared', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hinduism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mosques', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:25:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1622106f-46f2-589a-8d95-2329eb2f99a4', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1622106f-46f2-589a-8d95-2329eb2f99a4'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Swift and her partner discovered an implicit bias against women in everyday English when they edited a sexual education manual for junior high school students.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/education/10swift.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Swift and her partner discovered an implicit bias against women in everyday English when they edited a sexual education manual for junior high school students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kate Swift, a writer and editor who in two groundbreaking books — “Words and Women” and “The Handbook of Nonsexist Writing” — brought attention to the sexual discrimination embedded in ordinary English usage, died on Saturday in Middletown, Conn. She was 87. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/us/swift/swift-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/09/us/swift/swift-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/09/us/swift/swift-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kate Swift, Writer Who Rooted Out Sexism in Language, Dies at 87', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kate Swift Dies at 87; Detailed Sexism in Language', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Education', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T02:26:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c3e530b-6a31-57c0-935f-d57e03ddcb09', 'word_count': 742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c3e530b-6a31-57c0-935f-d57e03ddcb09'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Bosh, LeBron James and Dwyane Wade scored a combined 83 points to give the Heat a 3-1 lead over the Celtics in the East Conference semifinals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/sports/basketball/10celtics.html', 'snippet': 'Chris Bosh, LeBron James and Dwyane Wade scored a combined 83 points to give the Heat a 3-1 lead over the Celtics in the East Conference semifinals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The emotions overcame Chris Bosh again Monday night, in a barely controlled burst, but the timing was right, the purpose pure, and there was no sense in restraining himself.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/sports/10celtics-1/10celtics-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/10/sports/10celtics-1/10celtics-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'