one, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T13:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Traina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Traina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/688aea64-4b21-57d0-9e40-60d443d7eae9', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/688aea64-4b21-57d0-9e40-60d443d7eae9'}, {'abstract': 'When a multiethnic French soccer team won the World Cup in 1998, the nation celebrated as one, elevated above race and politics. So how did things suddenly turn so ugly?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/sports/soccer/11iht-SOCCER11.html', 'snippet': 'When a multiethnic French soccer team won the World Cup in 1998, the nation celebrated as one, elevated above race and politics. So how did things suddenly turn so ugly?', 'lead_paragraph': 'London', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Racial Politics Reared Its Head', 'kicker': 'On Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Racial Politics Rear Their Head in France', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T13:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50db3577-223d-5799-a6c1-8909d0cd9dcf', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50db3577-223d-5799-a6c1-8909d0cd9dcf'}, {'abstract': 'DuPont confirmed that its $6.4 billion bid for the Danish food ingredient company would not be raised again.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/confronting-elliott-dupont-calls-danisco-bid-final/', 'snippet': 'DuPont confirmed that its $6.4 billion bid for the Danish food ingredient company would not be raised again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DuPont confirmed on Tuesday that its $6.4 billion bid for the Danish food ingredient company Danisco would not be raised again, a day after it emerged that Elliott Advisors had increased its stake to just above 10 percent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confronting Elliott, DuPont Calls Danisco Bid ‘Final’', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danisco A/S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T13:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e5aea4c-3ba2-5c81-89e6-3e3e3554353a', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e5aea4c-3ba2-5c81-89e6-3e3e3554353a'}, {'abstract': 'As an employee of Walt Disney, Kermit now enjoys corporate perks like interviews with Johnny Depp, Penélope Cruz and Keith Richards.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/kermit-the-frogs-ribbiting-report-from-the-pirates-of-the-caribbean-premiere/', 'snippet': 'As an employee of Walt Disney, Kermit now enjoys corporate perks like interviews with Johnny Depp, Penélope Cruz and Keith Richards.', 'lead_paragraph': ' As an employee of the Walt Disney Company, Kermit the Frog may have lost his role in vintage Muppet specials like “Emmet Otter’s Jug-Band Christmas,” but his gig affords him other perks, like getting his own celebrity-interview station at the red carpet — make that the black carpet — for the premiere of the new “Pirates of the Caribbean” movie at Disneyland in Anaheim, Calif. In a winking style more reminiscent of “The Muppet Show” than “Sesame Street,” Kermit is introduced to “Pirates” stars like Geoffrey Rush, Penélope Cruz and Johnny Depp (who appears baffled by the concept of a talking frog), mingles with stray guests like Steven Tyler and Bruno Tonioli, and steals kisses from Vanessa Hudgens and Kirstie Alley. (Miss Piggy might want to skip those scenes.) Yes, it’s all a promotion for a coming Disney-produced Muppets movie, and while we would have preferred to see Gonzo introduced to Keith Richards, Kermit’s brief encounter with that randy Rolling Stone is still worth the price of admission. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kermit the Frog’s Ribbiting Report from the ‘Pirates of the Caribbean’ Premiere', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puppets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEY, KIRSTIE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Penelope', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Depp, Johnny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hudgens, Vanessa Anne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyler, Steven', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T13:18:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a102e34f-d862-59aa-8c67-ff71bc003ea7', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a102e34f-d862-59aa-8c67-ff71bc003ea7'}, {'abstract': 'The deal for the entertainment company CKX, which also owns rights to Elvis Presley’s name and image, follows a long effort to sell itself.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/apollo-to-buy-out-american-idol-owner/', 'snippet': 'The deal for the entertainment company CKX, which also owns rights to Elvis Presley’s name and image, follows a long effort to sell itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The owner of “American Idol” and “So You Think You Can Dance” agreed on Tuesday to be acquired by Apollo Global Management for more than $509 million. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apollo to Buy Out ‘American Idol’ Owner', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T13:39:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4b3fc17-a043-5f8c-a4e7-19c964f2eb88', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4b3fc17-a043-5f8c-a4e7-19c964f2eb88'}, {'abstract': 'Another episode of The Simpson.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/adult-conversation/', 'snippet': 'Another episode of The Simpson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Erskine Bowles, co-chairman of President Obama’s debt commission: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline':