for takeout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Keller’s latest, an edition of Bouchon Bakery on a highly trafficked corner of Rockefeller Center, is opening on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bouchon Bakery Outpost in Rockefeller Center Is Set to Open', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T14:23:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/28b8be69-ee66-5f12-a39c-aaa34823c118', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28b8be69-ee66-5f12-a39c-aaa34823c118'}, {'abstract': 'Not yet, maybe never — but not unthinkable.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/greek-out/', 'snippet': 'Not yet, maybe never — but not unthinkable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kudos to Mark Weisbrot for saying the unsayable, and making a case for Greek exit from the euro.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greek Out?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T14:28:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/106dff3c-a759-5af8-bc47-d4c84c6107e0', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/106dff3c-a759-5af8-bc47-d4c84c6107e0'}, {'abstract': 'The iron gates at the Strawberry Field Salvation Army Children’s Home, a popular destination for Beatles fans, have been taken down to prevent deterioration and will be replaced with replicas.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/strawberry-fields-not-quite-forever-gates-at-beatles-landmark-are-removed/', 'snippet': 'The iron gates at the Strawberry Field Salvation Army Children’s Home, a popular destination for Beatles fans, have been taken down to prevent deterioration and will be replaced with replicas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When John Lennon took us down to Strawberry Fields, he asked our permission first. But the Salvation Army, the owner of the site of the former children’s home that figured prominently in Lennon’s youth and inspired the hit Beatles song “Strawberry Fields Forever,” has decided to take down the iron gates that remained at the site and replace them with replicas, The Guardian reported. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strawberry Fields, Not Quite Forever: Gates at Beatles Landmark Are Removed', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lennon, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Salvation Army', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liverpool (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T14:36:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c50235-d423-58ad-a39c-3c6dcc84b3a0', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c50235-d423-58ad-a39c-3c6dcc84b3a0'}, {'abstract': 'At the James Beard Foundation’s annual restaurant and chef awards Monday night, the founder of Prune won Best Chef New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/eleven-madison-park-abc-kitchen-andgabrielle-hamilton-win-james-beard-awards/', 'snippet': 'At the James Beard Foundation’s annual restaurant and chef awards Monday night, the founder of Prune won Best Chef New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the all heartfelt Oscar-style thank-you speeches Monday evening at the annual James Beard Foundation Awards, Gabrielle Hamilton’s remarks came as a cold shower. The chef and owner of Prune in the East Village, who was named Best Chef for New York City, looked sleek in creamy satin, apologized for not having prepared a speech, and said: “All you have to do is open a can of sardines and a box of Triscuits, call it a signature dish, and you get Best Chef New York City.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eleven Madison Park, ABC Kitchen and Gabrielle Hamilton Win James Beard Awards', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Gabrielle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zraly, Kevin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beard, James, Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T14:36:45+0000', 'document_type': 'art