sia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlotta Gall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlotta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1624e9d-f94e-5bbb-b670-4320cea5aec6', 'word_count': 1238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1624e9d-f94e-5bbb-b670-4320cea5aec6'}, {'abstract': 'While not a recall, the engineering analysis by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration represents a step in that direction.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/investigation-of-fuel-leaks-expands-to-2-7-million-ford-f-150-truck/', 'snippet': 'While not a recall, the engineering analysis by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration represents a step in that direction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite Ford’s earlier statements that gas tanks breaking loose on F-150 pickups do not represent “an unreasonable risk to motor vehicle safety,” the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration is intensifying an investigation of the problem covering 2.7 million pickups from the 1997–2001 model years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Investigation of Fuel Leaks Expands to 2.7 Million Ford F-150s', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T15:25:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/61d23f9b-e59d-5b73-bcea-24c4aa91dc39', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61d23f9b-e59d-5b73-bcea-24c4aa91dc39'}, {'abstract': 'The Parks Department releases the highly anticipated results of its third annual Freshkills Park haiku contest.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/singing-of-freshkills-park-in-haiku-form/', 'snippet': 'The Parks Department releases the highly anticipated results of its third annual Freshkills Park haiku contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Staten Island’s famously putrid Fresh Kills landfill is being transformed into the 2,200-acre Freshkills Park, an urban oasis with wildlife habitats, horseback riding, mountain biking and meandering nature trails. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Singing of Freshkills Park, in Haiku Form', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fresh Kills Landfill (Staten Island)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Staten Island (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T15:31:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/41df747b-b2fc-57ea-a05b-5b130ff2b942', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41df747b-b2fc-57ea-a05b-5b130ff2b942'}, {'abstract': 'The salaries of Major League Soccer players might make you feel a little bit better about pro sports in America.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/major-league-soccer-little-fish-little-pond/', 'snippet': 'The salaries of Major League Soccer players might make you feel a little bit better about pro sports in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year, Major League Soccer Players Union releases the salaries of all players under contract in M.L.S. Those figures are just out, and they’re worth a look. (Other people’s salaries always are.) Depending on how you feel about lockouts and LOOGYs who get $8 million for two years, they might make you feel a little bit better about pro sports in America. Yes, there have been some ugly leg-breaking tackles of late. But at least our soccer players aren’t a lot of spoiled rich guys, you know? By my quick count, only six players make more than the $1.2 million Bobby Bonilla will be getting from the Mets every year for the next 25 years (he retired from baseball in 2001, in case you’re wondering why you haven’t heard that name in a while). And if you pool the salaries of the two biggest names in M.L.S., David Beckham and Thierry Henry, you still won’t have enough to cover Eddy Curry’s $11 million-plus salary this past season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Major League Soccer: Little Fish, Little Pond', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T15:35:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0394c2fe-0572-5008-a62b-8e3da9e0a677', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0394c2fe-0572-5008-a62b-8e3da9e0a677'}, {'abstract': 'Though the Grizzlies lost on a night when most thoughts were on the Mississippi, their fans applauded both teams when it was over.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/sports/11leading.html', 'snippet': 'Though the Grizzlies lost on a night when most thoughts were on the Mississippi, their fans applauded both teams when it was over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was fitting that something amazing would happen at the FedEx Forum in Memphis Tuesday night. The arena is not much more than a long golf shot from the Mississippi River, which had risen so high Tuesday, people were flocking downtown just to ogle it, perhaps the oddest of all tourist attractions. If some of them filed into the Grizzlies’ playoff game against Oklahoma City, they found themselves watching another force of nature: a 133-123 triple-overtime victory by the Thunder with 256 points, 206 field goal attempts, 90 free throw attempts and 67 personal fouls.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11leading1/11leading1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11leading1/11leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11leading1/11leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Epic Game 4 in Memphis', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Epic Game 4 Leaves Fans 