ce-for-alternative-energy-sources/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Stempel was among the company’s first and most insistent supporters of an electric-vehicle program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert C. Stempel, the former General Motors chairman and chief executive who died on Saturday at 77, spent a turbulent two years atop the country’s largest automaker, during which he cut jobs and closed plants to minimize company losses. However, for every automaker that deepens its experimentation in alternative-energy sources, Mr. Stempel’s legacy as an auto-industry seer is bolstered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Stempel, a Voice for Alternative-Energy Sources, Dies at 77', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ENERGY CONVERSION DEVICES INCORPORATED', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/06b9c533-5e5e-55bd-b22a-fd2b2439a8fe', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06b9c533-5e5e-55bd-b22a-fd2b2439a8fe'}, {'abstract': 'An attention-grabbing carry-on from Longchamp: a Kate Moss travel bag.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/bag-a-winner/', 'snippet': 'An attention-grabbing carry-on from Longchamp: a Kate Moss travel bag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kate Moss may have a lot of baggage, but when she marries Jamie Hince this summer, she’ll no doubt leave it all behind for the new Kate Moss for Longchamp travel bag ($1,320; longchamp.com). The attention-grabbing carry-on is designed to meet airline restrictions and keep clothes crease-free. If only it could fend off paparazzi.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bag a Winner', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:36:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/727995c3-5aea-51e7-a953-f58981cf5ea8', 'word_count': 54, 'uri': 'nyt://article/727995c3-5aea-51e7-a953-f58981cf5ea8'}, {'abstract': 'The collective modern uneasiness with the idea of hired household help.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/never-mention-the-housekeeper/', 'snippet': 'The collective modern uneasiness with the idea of hired household help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are certain words you learn to use carefully when you write about parenting. Breast-feeding is one. Co-sleeping is another. Home schooling is on the list, too. These are words that tend to divide parents by world views that they did not necessarily even know they had.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Never Mention the Housekeeper', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:38:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/aab8aad0-c997-5e00-9b83-96c8adb19272', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aab8aad0-c997-5e00-9b83-96c8adb19272'}, {'abstract': 'Adobe released three new applications for the Apple iPad that will let digital artists take advantage of the device’s multitouch screen to enhance the use of Adobe Photoshop on a desktop computer.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/adobe-makes-apps-to-enhance-photoshop/', 'snippet': 'Adobe released three new applications for the Apple iPad that will let digital artists take advantage of the device’s multitouch screen to enhance the use of Adobe Photoshop on a desktop computer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Apple iPad is quickly moving from a consumption device to a creation device.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adobe Makes Apps to Enhance Photoshop', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ADOBE SYSTEMS INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:40:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/00b74159-eba1-54d0-a550-fd0c58658f76', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00b74159-eba1-54d0-a550-fd0c58658f76'}, {'abstract': 'American Crossroads is beginning a $350,000 advertising campaign to help Jane L. Corwin, the Republican running for the vacant House seat in western New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/nyregion/powerful-republican-group-enters-special-house-race.html', 'snippet': 'American Crossroads is beginning a $350,000 advertising campaign to help Jane L. Corwin, the Republican running for the vacant House seat in western New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A conservative group formed with help from the Republican strategist Karl Rove has jumped into a special Congressional election in upstate New York that has become important to the two major parties.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Crossroads Backs Republican Financially in Upstate House Race', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Conservative Group Buying Ads In Special House Race Upstate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Crossroads', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochul, Kathy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corwin, Jane L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:53:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Hernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec7cfb2f-0865-548e-a217-9a8b93cb3ea7', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec7cfb2f-0865-548e-a217-9a8b93cb3ea7'}, {'abstract': 'Racial attitudes are deep, often below awareness and certainly without regard to political affiliation.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/race-and-response/', 'snippet': 'Racial attitudes are deep, often below awareness and certainly without regard to political affiliation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Butler and David Broockman of Yale had a very bright idea. They mailed a series of letters to legislators in which a fictional constituent asked for help in registering to vote. Some of the letters were signed with putatively black names (like DeShawn Jackson) and some were signed with putatively white names (like Jake Mueller).\xa0 The letters from the putatively black constituents received fewer responses from white legislators and received more responses from minority legislators.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Race and Response', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T17:55:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/160b6aac-994f-5d1c-b088-6c84a7bcbcb7', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/160b6aac-994f-5d1c-b088-6c84a7bcbcb7'}, {'abstract': 'In a new “personality” test, scientists examine whether suburban fishers are more likely than the large weasels in wilderness areas to approach novel objects and potential food sources.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/fishers-with-personality/', 'snippet': 'In a new “personality” test, scientists examine whether suburban fishers are more likely than the large weasels in wilderness areas to approach novel objects and potential food sources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roland Kays, curator of mammals at the New York State Museum, writes from Albany, where he is comparing the behavior of fishers in urban and wild settings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fishers With Personality?', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Positioning System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALBANY (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roland Kays', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kays', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7eb835e-378e-5884-8f3d-4101069e7f44', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7eb835e-378e-5884-8f3d-4101069e7f44'}, {'abstract': 'Text of the statement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/world/asia/binladen-statement.html', 'snippet': 'Text of the statement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Statement from the family of Sheikh Osama bin Laden', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Statement From the Family of Osama bin Laden', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Statement From the Family of Osama bin Laden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d9ab823-cced-5c63-9d6e-16f08c5ed715', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d9ab823-cced-5c63-9d6e-16f08c5ed715'}, {'abstract': 'How will the wireless industry respond to the news that Microsoft is throwing its weight behind a service that allows people to make free calls over the Internet?', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/what-does-the-skype-deal-mean-for-windows-phones/', 'snippet': 'How will the wireless industry respond to the news that Microsoft is throwing its weight behind a service that allows people to make free calls over the Internet?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Microsoft’s deal to buy Skype has raised many eyebrows in the technology industry, both for the price tag and the partnership, which many have said is puzzling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Does the Skype Deal Mean for Windows Phones?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skype Technologies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:07:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a18b0ba-8386-5ebb-a8fc-a12fe5e77b9d', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a18b0ba-8386-5ebb-a8fc-a12fe5e77b9d'}, {'abstract': 'A cartoonist finally makes it to the top; his art is to be exhibited on Mount Everest.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/high-art-which-is-to-say-29029-feet/', 'snippet': 'A cartoonist finally makes it to the top; his art is to be exhibited on Mount Everest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cartoonist Ranan R. Lurie has had his downs and ups: He has gone skydiving with Israeli soldiers and the 101st Airborne Division, and he has climbed Mount Fuji in Japan. But only the art, not the artist, is going to the top of Mount Everest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'High Art, Which Is to Say 29,029 Feet', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lurie, Ranan R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mount Everest', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:07:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/24923347-f1d5-5979-9c63-ef41b699d0a9', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24923347-f1d5-5979-9c63-ef41b699d0a9'}, {'abstract': '“Let’s focus on thanking our soldiers for risking their lives for us instead of focusing on the extreme minority of those who commit crimes like this.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/magazine/the-fighting-mans-beast.html', 'snippet': '“Let’s focus on thanking our soldiers for risking their lives for us instead of focusing on the extreme minority of those who commit crimes like this.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Luke Mogelson questions the “bad apples” theory of war crimes but doesn’t question the enterprise that makes such crimes possible, perhaps inevitable. What’s the difference between soldiers’ taking advantage of a dangerous and confusing situation to commit murder and countries using a dangerous and confusing situation as an excuse for war? If the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan had caused the surrender of the militant fundamentalists who seek our destruction, if they had denied these enemies the ability to attack, if the wars hadn’t devastated the economies and the innocent citizens of the countries we attacked, they might be justified.JAMIE BALDWIN,Redding, Conn., e-mail', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fighting Man’s Beast', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "THE FIGHTING MAN'S BEAST", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:09:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/26687506-4a08-5e99-a2ba-1f34019efaa3', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26687506-4a08-5e99-a2ba-1f34019efaa3'}, {'abstract': 'In a letter to The New York Review of Books, Joyce Carol Oates defends her decision not to include the fact that she remarried 13 months after her husband died in her memoir, “A Widow’s Story.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/joyce-carol-oates-updates-her-widows-story/', 'snippet': 'In a letter to The New York Review of Books, Joyce Carol Oates defends her decision not to include the fact that she remarried 13 months after her husband died in her memoir, “A Widow’s Story.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' When Joyce Carol Oates’s memoir, “A Widow’s Story,” came out in February, some reviewers took her to task for not mentioning that 13 months after the death of her husband, Raymond Smith, in February 2008, she remarried. Writing in The New York Review of Books, Julian Barnes said “some readers will feel they have a good case for breach of narrative promise.” In a letter in the current issue of The New York Review Ms. Oates defended her choice , writing that the book was meant to be about the immediate experience of widowhood, not her subsequent life. But she also wrote that “in retrospect I can see that I should have added something like an appendix, to bring my personal history up to date,” and said that she hopes to add such an appendix to later editions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joyce Carol Oates Updates Her ‘Widow’s Story’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:17:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e610d903-3449-56be-8843-a57e55154981', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e610d903-3449-56be-8843-a57e55154981'}, {'abstract': 'The firm said in a regulatory filing that it had received more subpoenas related to Abacus and other collateralized debt obligations that it made during the mortgage boom.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/goldman-faces-new-legal-woes/', 'snippet': 'The firm said in a regulatory filing that it had received more subpoenas related to Abacus and other collateralized debt obligations that it made during the mortgage boom.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:02 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Discloses More Subpoenas', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collateralized Debt Obligations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d720f89b-7652-58b3-9f22-e7810200a651', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d720f89b-7652-58b3-9f22-e7810200a651'}, {'abstract': 'House Speaker John A. Boehner on Tuesday offered praise for Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey and Gov. Mitch Daniels of Indiana for the 2012 presidential race.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/boehner-likes-christie-and-daniels-for-2012/', 'snippet': 'House Speaker John A. Boehner on Tuesday offered praise for Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey and Gov. Mitch Daniels of Indiana for the 2012 presidential race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Visit msnbc.com for breaking news, world news, and news about the economy', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boehner Likes Christie and Daniels for 2012', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:31:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bba5fed-ead4-504c-a699-978f63da6bf0', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bba5fed-ead4-504c-a699-978f63da6bf0'}, {'abstract': 'Embarking on a statewide tour, the governor was combative while calling on the Legislature to focus on his legislative priorities, including ethics reform.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/cuomo-says-legislature-rife-with-corruption/', 'snippet': 'Embarking on a statewide tour, the governor was combative while calling on the Legislature to focus on his legislative priorities, including ethics reform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONONDAGA, N.Y. — Decrying the State Legislature as infiltrated with corruption and unable to police itself, Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo began a statewide tour on Tuesday aiming to shame lawmakers into revamping ethics rules and passing the rest of his legislative agenda.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo Says Legislature Is Rife With Corruption', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption in Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:34:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a95868fb-f832-54a7-a8db-1c01674fc720', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a95868fb-f832-54a7-a8db-1c01674fc720'}, {'abstract': 'The hearing, called by Senator Al Franken, follows a series of embarrassing reports about how much information is collected and stored by mobile phones.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/congress-hears-from-apple-and-google-on-privacy/', 'snippet': 'The hearing, called by Senator Al Franken, follows a series of embarrassing reports about how much information is collected and stored by mobile phones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saying there has been a fundamental shift for cellphone users in determining “who has their information and what they’re doing with it,” Senator Al Franken, a Minnesota Democrat, called a Congressional hearing today to question executives from both Google and Apple on data location and mobile devices. The hearing was the first for the newly formed Senate Judiciary subcommittee on privacy, technology and the law, led by Senator Franken.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress Hears From Apple and Google on Privacy', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:36:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffce0dfc-0676-5f9a-8b9d-8c7d2da0c23b', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffce0dfc-0676-5f9a-8b9d-8c7d2da0c23b'}, {'abstract': 'A brief history of the tulip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/15/magazine/look-tulips.html', 'snippet': 'A brief history of the tulip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brief history of the tulip.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bloom and Bust', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:36:27+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/e91305a3-b89f-51b9-92fa-e6fefa99ddc6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/e91305a3-b89f-51b9-92fa-e6fefa99ddc6'}, {'abstract': 'The backstory behind our latest T, designed for the upcoming Summer Travel issue.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/postcard-from-the-edge-clifford-ross-for-t/', 'snippet': 'The backstory behind our latest T, designed for the upcoming Summer Travel issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pretty much every T we commission for the magazine has a back story. But few go as far back—or have as many plot twists—as the story behind this particular T, which was created by the artist Clifford Ross and will appear in the upcoming Summer Travel issue. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Postcard from the Edge | Clifford Ross for T', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b6814ff-28a1-5530-be03-a8abf4d09005', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b6814ff-28a1-5530-be03-a8abf4d09005'}, {'abstract': 'A newspaper catering to ultra-Orthodox Jews in New York apologized on Monday for publishing a photograph of the White House Situation Room from which Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton had been erased.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/newspaper-regrets-erasing-hillary-clinton/', 'snippet': 'A newspaper catering to ultra-Orthodox Jews in New York apologized on Monday for publishing a photograph of the White House Situation Room from which Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton had been erased.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 5:06 p.m. A newspaper catering to ultra-Orthodox Jews in New York apologized on Monday for publishing a photograph of the White House Situation Room during the raid on Osama bin Laden’s compound that was digitally altered to make it seem as if Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and another female official had not been present.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newspaper ‘Regrets’ Erasing Hillary Clinton', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:47:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bd6434d-115d-5574-b10f-4f32186d7c5c', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bd6434d-115d-5574-b10f-4f32186d7c5c'}, {'abstract': 'Last night we won two National Magazine Awards, or “Ellies.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/two-new-national-magazine-awards/', 'snippet': 'Last night we won two National Magazine Awards, or “Ellies.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two new Alexander Calder stabiles in our possession. Last night, the magazine won two National Magazine Awards, or Ellies (so called for the sculpture’s resemblance to an elephant). One was for News and Documentary Photography for “The Shrine Down the Hall,” photographs by Ashley Gilbertson; essay by Dexter Filkins. The photo essay was a portrait of the preserved bedrooms of the young people killed in the wars of Iraq and Afghanistan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two New National Magazine Awards', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibovich, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Magazine Awards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bbc4d3e-fb5e-5741-b25f-8198e599654d', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bbc4d3e-fb5e-5741-b25f-8198e599654d'}, {'abstract': 'The decline in the use of nicknames is most easily gauged in sports, where they have long played a role in distinguishing athletes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/sports/great-sports-nicknames-like-magic-are-disappearing.html', 'snippet': 'The decline in the use of nicknames is most easily gauged in sports, where they have long played a role in distinguishing athletes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s baseball rosters are filled with names, not nicknames, not like the ones that used to be. The N.B.A. playoffs are equally devoid of onomastic pleasures, just cheap echoes of Magic and the Mailman, Tiny and Tree, Chief and Cornbread. The N.F.L. cannot match the treasured nicknames that evoke folk heroes like Night Train, Hacksaw and the Refrigerator.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11nicknamesillo/11nicknamesillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11nicknamesillo/11nicknamesillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11nicknamesillo/11nicknamesillo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11nicknamesillo/11nicknamesillo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/11nicknamesillo/11nicknamesillo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Like Magic, Great Sports Nicknames Are Disappearing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vanishing Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Personal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T18:56:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6bc5d11-e13b-5e60-add6-b90b51f184e7', 'word_count': 1568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6bc5d11-e13b-5e60-add6-b90b51f184e7'}, {'abstract': 'The Landmarks Preservation Commission designated the former East 54th Street Bath and Gymnasium and the former headquarters of the Brooklyn Union Gas Company as landmarks.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/bathhouse-and-gas-company-building-are-named-landmarks/', 'snippet': 'The Landmarks Preservation Commission designated the former East 54th Street Bath and Gymnasium and the former headquarters of the Brooklyn Union Gas Company as landmarks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A former public bathhouse in Midtown Manhattan and the former headquarters of the Brooklyn Union Gas Company in Downtown Brooklyn have been designated as landmarks, the New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission announced on Tuesday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bathhouse and Gas Company Building Are Named Landmarks', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Landmarks Preservation Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Downtown Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T19:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elissa Gootman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gootman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2599b110-0e5e-55d6-ae3f-e04f0fb6728b', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2599b110-0e5e-55d6-ae3f-e04f0fb6728b'}, {'abstract': 'The hearing on mobile privacy was the first for the newly formed Senate Judiciary Subcommittee on Privacy, Technology and the Law.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/on-capitol-hill-apple-google-executives-testify-over-privacy/', 'snippet': 'The hearing on mobile privacy was the first for the newly formed Senate Judiciary Subcommittee on Privacy, Technology and the Law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saying there has been a fundamental shift for cellphone users in determining “who has their information and what they’re doing with it,” Senator Al Franken, Democrat of Minnesota, held a hearing Tuesday to question executives from Google and Apple on data location and mobile devices. The hearing was the first for the newly formed Senate Judiciary Subcommittee on Privacy, Technology and the Law, led by Mr. Franken. Read more about the hearing on Media Decoder. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple and Google Executives Testify on Mobile Privacy', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T19:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcc8bfff-767a-58db-bb31-a9203d9acaa7', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcc8bfff-767a-58db-bb31-a9203d9acaa7'}, {'abstract': 'Finding out what students know about government and citizenship, and creating proposals for ways to improve the school’s approach to civics education.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/good-citizenship-assessing-civics-knowledge-and-education/', 'snippet': 'Finding out what students know about government and citizenship, and creating proposals for ways to improve the school’s approach to civics education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overview | Why is civics education important? What are the most effective and engaging ways to engage teenagers in learning civics? In this lesson, students will conduct a research project about civic education and students’ knowledge at their school. They will then prepare a presentation of their findings and recommendations to a panel of their school;s history faculty and administrators.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Citizenship: Assessing Civics Knowledge and Education', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T19:02:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kavanagh and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kavanagh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ee8dcb0-9d43-5563-8192-3a95cdd7c179', 'word_count': 1733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ee8dcb0-9d43-5563-8192-3a95cdd7c179'}, {'abstract': '“Dancing With the Stars” leaves the competition in the dust.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/dancing-to-the-top/', 'snippet': '“Dancing With the Stars” leaves the competition in the dust.', 'lead_paragraph': ' “Dancing With the Stars” attracted 20.2 million viewers on Monday, lifting ABC far head of its network competitors in the night’s ratings, according to Nielsen’s estimates. ABC’s “Castle” won the 10 p.m. time slot after the two-hour installment of “Dancing With the Stars,” garnering 12.6 million viewers and outperforming CBS’s “Hawaii Five-0” (9.2 million) and NBC’s “Law & Order: LA” (5 million) in the hour. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'h