eased by the White House:", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama’s Remarks on Immigration', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama’s Remarks on Immigration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T19:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4058d82-55f1-5c5f-bbc4-77b3a37f7dc9', 'word_count': 4299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4058d82-55f1-5c5f-bbc4-77b3a37f7dc9'}, {'abstract': 'With a smart, accessible and not terribly expensive menu by the chef Geoffrey Zakarian, the National delivers more than its Midtown location would promise, and handsomely.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/dining/reviews/the-national-in-midtown-nyc-restaurant-review.html', 'snippet': 'With a smart, accessible and not terribly expensive menu by the chef Geoffrey Zakarian, the National delivers more than its Midtown location would promise, and handsomely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIFTIETH Street and Lexington Avenue is the pulsing heart of no particular neighborhood, a lively intersection near the back door to the Waldorf-Astoria, just south of a DoubleTree. Office workers stream through on the way to the subway stop at the corner. Hotel guests pilot wheelie bags deep into yellow-cab traffic, and bark at one another in French. If you were looking for a neighborhood bistro of the sort where you might linger over dinner for a while, happily talking with friends while picking at fries and finishing a bottle of wine, the corner would rank low on a list of possible locations.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images