The American nuclear industry insists it is still going strong —  although low demand for electricity and low prices for natural gas may get in the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “renaissance” that the American nuclear industry has been talking about for almost 20 years looks a bit slow and small at the moment, with two of the four leading reactor candidates chosen for aid by the Department of Energy apparently having fizzled.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Staying the Course, Post-Fukushima', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy Institute', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Regulatory Commission', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:13:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cddb9e9-2dbb-549a-a612-88f1224a36fc', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cddb9e9-2dbb-549a-a612-88f1224a36fc'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Libya, Japan, Uganda and Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/pictures-of-the-day-libya-and-elsewhere-24/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Libya, Japan, Uganda and Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Libya, Japan, Uganda and Afghanistan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Libya and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uganda', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:13:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8854d723-75e1-5310-89f0-ed0067e36670', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8854d723-75e1-5310-89f0-ed0067e36670'}, {'abstract': 'The boat balanced precariously in the background of a photo featured in Sunday’s Automobiles section.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/in-japan-a-boat-stranded-by-tsunami-is-lifted-from-rooftop/', 'snippet': 'The boat balanced precariously in the background of a photo featured in Sunday’s Automobiles section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photos of compelling cars are nothing new to the Automobiles section. But the shot of a Mitsubishi i-MiEV in a tsunami-struck town in Japan, which ran in Sunday’s Automobiles section, was arresting because, about one block away from the electric vehicle, a two-story building was topped by a boat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Japan, a Boat Stranded by Tsunami Is Lifted From Rooftop', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:16:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa53bdb9-b852-5bdd-a288-9c297bfdb8d0', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa53bdb9-b852-5bdd-a288-9c297bfdb8d0'}, {'abstract': 'ChargeSmart acts as a middleman, accepting your credit or debit card to pay the mortgage or electricity bill. But is the convenience worth the fee ChargeSmart charges?', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/bill-paying-made-easier-but-for-a-fee/', 'snippet': 'ChargeSmart acts as a middleman, accepting your credit or debit card to pay the mortgage or electricity bill. But is the convenience worth the fee ChargeSmart charges?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lots of people like to pay big bills with their credit or debit cards so they can rack up reward points or manage their cash flow when money is tight. But it’s sometimes hard to do that with certain big bills, like those from your mortgage servicer or your electricity provider. Many of those companies still refuse to accept plastic — or accept only certain brands.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Paying Made Easier, but for a Fee', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern California Gas Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1dd893a-4fbc-544e-bc4c-22c7c5f86ecf', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1dd893a-4fbc-544e-bc4c-22c7c5f86ecf'}, {'abstract': 'The Gaylord hotel company has teamed up with DreamWorks to feature animated characters at its four resorts, in Nashville, Tenn., Kissimme, Fla., on the Potomac in National Harbor, Md., and Grapevine, Tex.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/bed-and-shrekfast-gaylord-hotels-meet-dreamworks/', 'snippet': 'The Gaylord hotel company has teamed up with DreamWorks to feature animated characters at its four resorts, in Nashville, Tenn., Kissimme, Fla., on the Potomac in National Harbor, Md., and Grapevine, Tex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are theme parks tied to movie studios and there are theme cruises, and now, the themed hotel stay. The Gaylord hotel company has teamed up with DreamWorks to feature animated characters at its four resorts, in Nashville, Tenn., Kissimme, Fla., on the Potomac in National Harbor, Md., and Grapevine, Tex., Gaylord announced recently.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bed and Shrekfast: Gaylord Hotels Meet DreamWorks', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DreamWorks Animation SKG Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gaylord Entertainment Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:18:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Drake', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drake', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1abc8e3c-827e-5222-bb2a-fdb6362915fd', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1abc8e3c-827e-5222-bb2a-fdb6362915fd'}, {'abstract': 'The Twitter revolution still works between covers.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/the-book-of-tweets/', 'snippet': 'The Twitter revolution still works between covers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Did we always think in search terms and we just didn’t know it? Thoughts melded into words, and the words, if you squinted, began to yield Dominant Nouns. The dominant nouns then formed their own relationships. Early Internet searching was an art. The available results were mostly useless, so you had to “refine your search” — you had to try to think like the algorithm just as the algorithm was trying to think like you. An unexpected partnership. I always thought this was why search took off: you had the vague feeling that you were working with someone else, on the path to refinement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Book of Tweets', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T20:22:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byli