e, Andrew Sullivan runs down what he sees as the five fundamental discontinuities between the two administration’s approaches:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debating the Bush-Obama Era', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T21:14:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/65e60005-57b4-512b-9aed-ed41bbf699db', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65e60005-57b4-512b-9aed-ed41bbf699db'}, {'abstract': 'K T Sullivan covers a wide range of musical and emotional territory in her new show, “Rhyme, Women and Song,” at the Oak Room of the Algonquin Hotel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/arts/music/k-t-sullivan-at-algonquin-oak-room-review.html', 'snippet': 'K T Sullivan covers a wide range of musical and emotional territory in her new show, “Rhyme, Women and Song,” at the Oak Room of the Algonquin Hotel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the pleasures of cabaret reviewing over the long haul has been to observe the evolution of K T Sullivan from an effervescent musical comedian into the increasingly fearless and complex singing character actor she is today. Her new show, which opened at the Oak Room of the Algonquin Hotel last week, is called “Rhyme, Women and Song,” but it might just as well be titled “The Seven Ages of Woman,” for all the musical and emotional territory she covers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/KT-Sullivan/KT-Sullivan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/KT-Sullivan/KT-Sullivan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/KT-Sullivan/KT-Sullivan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Woman for All Seasons, Ballads and Emotions', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Woman for All Seasons, Ballads and Emotions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oak Room', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, K T', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T21:16:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/673a9292-8fed-51dc-8020-a89c6e1eb6b0', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/673a9292-8fed-51dc-8020-a89c6e1eb6b0'}, {'abstract': '“City of Life and Death” portrays the hundreds of thousands of soldiers and civilians tortured and killed during the mass butchery known as the Rape of Nanjing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/movies/city-of-life-and-death-from-lu-chuan-review.html', 'snippet': '“City of Life and Death” portrays the hundreds of thousands of soldiers and civilians tortured and killed during the mass butchery known as the Rape of Nanjing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two terrible faces stare out from “City of Life and Death,” a fictionalized telling of the Rape of Nanjing, a pair of indelible bookends for this anguished film. The first belongs to Lu (Liu Ye), who, with hundreds of other soldiers, has been rounded up by invading Japanese troops amid a frenzy of violence. The close-up of Lu’s impassive face locked in unspoken emotion floods the screen. Much later, after innumerable deaths and acts of barbarism and heroism, the face of a woman will similarly fill the screen in close-up, her frantic eyes stretched wide, as if they had been permanently shocked open by what they have seen.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 253, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 253}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/LIFE/LIFE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tale of Nanjing Atrocities That Spares No Brutal Detail', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'City of Life and Death'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tale of Nanjing Atrocities That Spares No Brutal Detail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lu Chuan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nanjing (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T21:17:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/913bccef-0788-5a1c-b925-193a3675de07', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/913bccef-0788-5a1c-b925-193a3675de07'}, {'abstract': 'The president sought to reassure frustrated voters that he remained committed to changing immigration laws.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/us/politics/11obama.html', 'snippet': 'The president sought to reassure frustrated voters that he remained committed to changing immigration laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EL PASO — President Obama came to this border city on Tuesday to argue that he is doing his part to crack down on illegal immigration, and that Republicans must now join him in overhauling the nation’s immigration laws for the millions of workers already here illegally. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/us/10obama1_span/10obama1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/10/us/10obama1_span/10obama1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/10/us/10obama1_span/10obama1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None