ns', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T22:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/61588f8f-aa4b-543b-832a-b96d82250564', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61588f8f-aa4b-543b-832a-b96d82250564'}, {'abstract': 'Ray LaHood responds to a Joe Sharkey column about the regulation limiting airline tarmac delays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/opinion/l11tarmac.html', 'snippet': 'Ray LaHood responds to a Joe Sharkey column about the regulation limiting airline tarmac delays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stranded on the Tarmac', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stranded on the Tarmac', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delays (Transportation)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T22:53:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/e66b12cd-869f-5980-89c4-a73714b3a2f1', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e66b12cd-869f-5980-89c4-a73714b3a2f1'}, {'abstract': 'The New York chief executive got positive marks from 72 percent of the state’s voters, much higher than many of his peers across the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/nyregion/cuomo-among-most-popular-governors-polls-show.html', 'snippet': 'The New York chief executive got positive marks from 72 percent of the state’s voters, much higher than many of his peers across the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s effort to rally the public to support his agenda is based on a simple political calculation: He is popular, and the Legislature is not.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Popularity Polls of Governors, Cuomo Is High on the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Popularity Polls of Governors, Cuomo Is High on the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Governors (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T22:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f15754d6-8902-5bd2-a2dc-c059e37996ca', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f15754d6-8902-5bd2-a2dc-c059e37996ca'}, {'abstract': 'Google introduced tools for connecting Android phones with things like exercise machines, light bulbs and someday dishwashers and even robots.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/turn-your-android-phone-into-a-remote-control-for-your-home/', 'snippet': 'Google introduced tools for connecting Android phones with things like exercise machines, light bulbs and someday dishwashers and even robots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google wants people to use its Android software to operate a lot more than just phones and tablets. And at Google I/O, the company’s conference for developers in San Francisco this week, the company showed off a future where someone could use an Android phone to turn on and off the lights, the dishwasher, a lamp or a sprinkler system — or anything else they want to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turn Your Android Phone Into a Remote Control for Your Home', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T22:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6d030f4-9baf-5d5b-9214-a636db05ac9b', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6d030f4-9baf-5d5b-9214-a636db05ac9b'}, {'abstract': 'Supporters and opponents of legalizing same-sex marriage in New York are making moves to try to bolster their position.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/new-bill-supporting-same-sex-marriage-is-introduced-in-assembly/', 'snippet': 'Supporters and opponents of legalizing same-sex marriage in New York are making moves to try to bolster their position.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With only weeks to go before the end of the legislative session in June, a senior Democratic lawmaker in the New York State Assembly introduced legislation on Tuesday to legalize same-sex marriages, signaling that Assembly lawmakers were unwilling to wait for efforts by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo and gay rights groups to pull together enough votes to move the measure through the Republican-controlled State Senate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Bill Supporting Same-Sex Marriage Is Introduced in Assembly', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T23:09:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Confessore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5326067c-be11-56d5-b6fd-f0cb52f2ab96', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5326067c-be11-56d5-b6fd-f0cb52f2ab96'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Sarah Shourd, who was imprisoned along with Shane Bauer and Josh Fattal in Tehran, reflects on what life must be life for her two friends, who continue to be held at Evin Prison.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/hikers-on-trial/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Sarah Shourd, who was imprisoned along with Shane Bauer and Josh Fattal in Tehran, reflects on what life must be life for her two friends, who continue to be held at Evin Prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On May 11, Shane Bauer and Josh Fattal, two of the three American hikers who were detained by Iranian officials after crossing a border between Iraqi Kurdistan and Iran in 2009, will go on trial in Tehran for espionage. They have been held in Evin Prison for almost two years. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hikers on Trial', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T23:11:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Shourd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shourd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/20a7e6d0-903c-58b0-8387-68a68ea4b7d9', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20a7e6d0-903c-58b0-8387-68a68ea4b7d9'}, {'abstract': 'The Food and Drug Administration ordered all producers of a popular category of artificial hip to study the implants, which have been linked to severe health effects in some patients.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/business/11hip.html', 'snippet': 'The Food and Drug Administration ordered all producers of a popular category of artificial hip to study the implants, which have been linked to severe health effects in some patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an unusual move, the Food and Drug Administration has ordered all producers of a popular category of artificial hip to undertake studies of the implants, which have been linked to high early failure rates and severe health effects in some patients.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hip Makers Told to Study More Data', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hip Makers Told to Study More Data', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Implants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Devices', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-10T23:27:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Meier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eedbd05a-a294-5eac-8b92-44c75aa75c26', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eedbd05a-a294-5eac-8b92-44c75aa75c26'}, {'abstract': 'Allied forces say they killed the right man — a shadow governor affiliated with Al Qaeda — but a human rights report paints a murkier picture involving acquaintances and false names.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/world/asia/11afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Allied forces say they killed the right man — a shadow governor affiliated with Al Qaeda — but a human rights report paints a murkier picture involving acquaintances and false names.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Two new reports addressing human rights issues in Afghanistan have raised questions about how NATO troops are conducting the war. Both reports center on a question that has vexed international forces since the war began: How can the military be sure it has killed, detained or made deals with the right people?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/world/AFGHANISTAN/AFGHANISTAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/world/AFGHANISTAN/AFGHANISTAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnai