still remember the name of the food service company that ran the cafeteria where I went to college, and I would still revile it, except it deserves partial credit for forcing me to learn how to cook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Future of Cafeteria Food', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T00:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3487adea-f57d-5c46-96eb-d7305fb0fe16', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3487adea-f57d-5c46-96eb-d7305fb0fe16'}, {'abstract': 'Iran may be close to wrapping up work that has dragged on for more than three decades at the site of the Bushehr power plant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/world/middleeast/11briefs-Iranbrf.html', 'snippet': 'Iran may be close to wrapping up work that has dragged on for more than three decades at the site of the Bushehr power plant.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Iran has started the nuclear reaction at its Bushehr power plant, the Russian company that built the reactor said in a statement on Tuesday that indicated Iran is close to wrapping up work on the site that has dragged on for more than three decades. The German company Siemens abandoned the partly completed plant after the 1979 Islamic revolution in Iran, and more delays followed even after Russia resumed construction in 1994. The United States once opposed the plant. But the International Atomic Energy Agency monitors it, and the United States dropped its objections after Russia provided assurances of controls on the fuel supply and the disposal of spent fuel rods. The Russian state company that builds reactors abroad said that operators at the plant would test safety and control equipment before connecting the reactor to electricity generating turbines, a process that could take about two months. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Nuclear Reaction Is Begun', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Nuclear Reaction Is Begun', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Atomic Energy Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T00:32:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c3e8c96-c70b-5e90-912e-ec9a8bce188d', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c3e8c96-c70b-5e90-912e-ec9a8bce188d'}, {'abstract': 'As Republicans inch away from their plan to reshape Medicare, their equally transformative ideas for Medicaid, now largely in the shadows of the budget debate, are moving front and center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/health/policy/11medicaid.html', 'snippet': 'As Republicans inch away from their plan to reshape Medicare, their equally transformative ideas for Medicaid, now largely in the shadows of the budget debate, are moving front and center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — As Republicans inch away from their plan to resha