Creation” is regarded as the definitive account of the breakthroughs that transformed molecular biology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/science/11judson.html', 'snippet': 'A writer whose 1979 book “The Eighth Day of Creation” is regarded as the definitive account of the breakthroughs that transformed molecular biology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Horace Freeland Judson, a science writer whose 1979 book “The Eighth Day of Creation” is regarded as the definitive account of the breakthroughs that transformed molecular biology in the mid-20th century, died on Friday at his home in Baltimore. He was 80.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/us/JUDSON-obit/JUDSON-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 620, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/us/JUDSON-obit/JUDSON-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/us/JUDSON-obit/JUDSON-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Horace Freeland Judson, Science Historian, Dies at 80', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Horace F. Judson, 80, Historian of Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Judson, Horace Freeland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T00:49:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/32be5a93-ae89-5e02-bfbf-158898b7f671', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32be5a93-ae89-5e02-bfbf-158898b7f671'}, {'abstract': 'Sean Foley, who has worked with several Tour players, including Tiger Woods, is confident in his theoretical methods, even if his critics are not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/sports/golf/sean-foley-tiger-woodss-coach-follows-his-own-path.html', 'snippet': 'Sean Foley, who has worked with several Tour players, including Tiger Woods, is confident in his theoretical methods, even if his critics are not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WINDERMERE, FLA. — When Sean Foley was a college golfer, he sometimes exasperated his coach by giving tips to opposing players. He was precociously interested in swing theory and swing mechanics, more concerned with how many greens he hit in regulation than with his final score. When he was 14, an obsessive reader of golf instruction books, he was offering unsolicited — and possibly incorrect — advice to hapless duffers. At 16, he could imitate Nick Faldo frame by frame on video. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/sub-y-jp-foley/sub-y-jp-foley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/sub-y-jp-foley/sub-y-jp-foley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/sports/sub-y-jp-foley/sub-y-jp-foley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Focused on the Swing, Not the Score', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Focused on the Swing, Not the Score', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foley, Sean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T00:56:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5e2dbd7-7d56-58e4-b21f-506f88461be0', 'word_count': 1463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5e2dbd7-7d56-58e4-b21f-506f88461be0'}, {'abstract': 'In the conversations about music, politics and culture, why not hear from the composers themselves?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/10/the-composers-other-voice/', 'snippet': 'In the conversations about music, politics and culture, why not hear from the composers themselves?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have been a composer for my entire adult life. And for the most part, despite the exhaustion and relative poverty it has entailed, this self-styled “job” has been a source of satisfaction, even great joy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Composer’s Other Voice', 'kicker': 'The Score', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T01:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Felsenfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Felsenfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca810d06-6f94-53fe-98e1-a1cb69a6d952', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca810d06-6f94-53fe-98e1-a1cb69a6d952'}, {'abstract': 'Jimmy McElroy, one of Hell’s Kitchen’s infamous Westies, was eulogized in a very different place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/nyregion/a-farewell-to-jimmy-mcelroy-gangster-of-a-lost-era.html', 'snippet': 'Jimmy McElroy, one of Hell’s Kitchen’s infamous Westies, was eulogized in a very different place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The man and woman unfolded themselves from a cab at the corner of Ninth Avenue and 42nd Street, then stood blinking in the sunlight for a few seconds, searching for a familiar piece of ground, a bearing. So there: the bus terminal across the street. But their gazes lingered on tall buildings that had risen in the last two decades or so. It was nearly 10:30 on Tuesday morning. They shuffled down the street and melted into a small crowd on the sidewalk outside the Church of the Holy Cross. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/nyregion/ABOUT1/ABOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/11/nyregion/ABOUT1/ABOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/nyregion/ABOUT1/ABOUT1-jumbo.