ether.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Since the start of the year, one of the big mysteries here has been what the state’s most famous couple — Arnold Schwarzenegger, the former governor, and Maria Shriver, a former television news correspondent and a daughter of the Kennedy dynasty— would do after Mr. Schwarzenegger returned to private life.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/us/schwarzenegger/schwarzenegger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 644, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/us/schwarzenegger/schwarzenegger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/us/schwarzenegger/schwarzenegger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Schwarzenegger and Shriver, a Transition and a Separation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Couple Who Spoke Of Transition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwarzenegger, Arnold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shriver, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T02:57:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/917fa6b4-216b-53e9-bc3f-9ce4ba24f723', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/917fa6b4-216b-53e9-bc3f-9ce4ba24f723'}, {'abstract': 'A federal judge in Utah on Tuesday temporarily barred a state law to curb illegal immigration from taking effect, saying he wanted to give both sides in a lawsuit more time to prepare their arguments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/us/11utah.html', 'snippet': 'A federal judge in Utah on Tuesday temporarily barred a state law to curb illegal immigration from taking effect, saying he wanted to give both sides in a lawsuit more time to prepare their arguments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge in Utah on Tuesday temporarily barred a state law to curb illegal immigration from taking effect, saying he wanted to give both sides in a lawsuit more time to prepare their arguments.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Utah Immigration Law Is Blocked', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Utah Immigration Law Is Blocked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T02:58:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aacc3957-2e77-5598-95d7-7283f01b79e6', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aacc3957-2e77-5598-95d7-7283f01b79e6'}, {'abstract': 'A doctor who the federal authorities said was one of the largest national dispensers of Oxycodone, a powerful prescription painkiller, was convicted on Tuesday of illegal distribution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/us/11brfs-DOCTORFOUNDG_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A doctor who the federal authorities said was one of the largest national dispensers of Oxycodone, a powerful prescription painkiller, was convicted on Tuesday of illegal distribution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A doctor who the federal authorities said was one of the largest national dispensers of Oxycodone, a powerful prescription painkiller, was convicted on Tuesday of illegal distribution. As a result of the verdict, in federal court in Cincinnati, Dr. Paul H. Volkman of Chicago could face life in prison for illegal distribution of a controlled substance that resulted in the death of four people in southern Ohio, where he practiced. Such a sentence would be the first of its kind in Ohio, where the authorities are trying to strengthen laws to combat the drug scourge, one of the worst in the nation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ohio: Doctor Found Guilty on Drug Counts', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ohio: Doctor Found Guilty on Drug Counts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T03:04:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b46887f9-c5fb-5755-b4b2-02a004ba4997', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b46887f9-c5fb-5755-b4b2-02a004ba4997'}, {'abstract': 'California should rethink its plan to build the first leg of the nation’s only true high-speed rail project because it would attract too few riders, a report issued Tuesday by the State Legislative Analyst’s Office said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/us/11brfs-HIGHSPEEDRAI_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'California should rethink its plan to build the first leg of the nation’s only true high-speed rail project because it would attract too few riders, a report issued Tuesday by the State Legislative Analyst’s Office said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'California should rethink its plan to build the first leg of the nation’s only true high-speed rail project in a stretch of the Central Valley because it would attract too few riders, a report issued Tuesday by the State Legislative Analyst’s Office said. The report, which warns that the full route from San Francisco to Los Angeles may never be completed and is likely to cost more than the estimated $43 billion, calls for starting construction in a more densely populated area and for giving control of the project to the State Transportation Department. The independent authority currently overseeing the work said it would review the recommendations. The federal government has awarded the project $3.6 billion. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: High-Speed Rail Plan Is Questioned', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: High-Speed Rail Plan Is Questioned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High-Speed Rail Projects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T03:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/05a1a8c5-e6a4-5ad7-a116-520a875854b1', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05a1a8c5-e6a4-5ad7-a116-520a875854b1'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court has reversed two charges against a former health executive in a closely watched corruption case.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/us/11brfs-ART-ANOTHERTWIST_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court has reversed two charges against a former health executive in a closely watched corruption case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court has reversed two charges against a former health executive in a closely watched corruption case. The United States Court of Appeals for the 11th Circuit, based in Atlanta, upheld most of the convictions stemming from $500,000 in contributions by Richard M. Scrushy, a former HealthSouth chief executive, to retire the debt of a state lottery campaign backed by former Gov. Don E. Siegelman, and sent both men’s cases back to Federal District Court for resentencing. A Department of Justice spokeswoman, Laura Sweeney, said the department was “pleased” with the court’s decision. But Sam Heldman, an attorney for Mr. Siegelman, said the court’s definition of bribery was open to interpretation by prosecutors, and many politicians and contributors could run afoul of it. “It places everyone’s liberty at the mercy of mind readers,” he said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alabama: Another Twist in Corruption Case', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alabama: Another Twist in Corruption Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegelman, Donald E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption in Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HealthSouth Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scrushy, Richard M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T03:09:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/60881bc9-d6c1-51b4-afa8-eca59a0cf331', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60881bc9-d6c1-51b4-afa8-eca59a0cf331'}, {'abstract': 'In a salesroom overflowing with big-money collectors, the audience did more watching than shopping, with thin bidding on the most expensive works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/nyregion/sixteen-jackies-by-warhol-sells-for-20-million.html', 'snippet': 'In a salesroom overflowing with big-money collectors, the audience did more watching than shopping, with thin bidding on the most expensive works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A mix of overly optimistic sellers and price-conscious buyers made for a bumpy evening at Sotheby’s sale of contemporary art on Tuesday night. In an overflowing salesroom filled with big-money collectors like the former Hollywood agent Michael Ovitz, the fashion designer Valentino and the Los Angeles financier Eli Broad, the audience did more watching than shopping, with thin bidding on the most expensive works. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/nyregion/AUCTION/AUCTION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}