_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/928ba428-d611-55c8-8829-a0c075008790'}, {'abstract': 'Black bears have killed only 63 people in the United States and Canada over the last 109 years. Most attacks involved a bear on the prowl, likely hunting for food, not mothers protecting cubs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/science/11bears.html', 'snippet': 'Black bears have killed only 63 people in the United States and Canada over the last 109 years. Most attacks involved a bear on the prowl, likely hunting for food, not mothers protecting cubs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you believe Stephen Colbert — and really, who doesn’t? — bears are deadly. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/science/11bears2/11bears2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/science/11bears2/11bears2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/11/science/11bears2/11bears2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Study of Black Bears Finds It’s Not the Mamas That Should Be Feared the Most', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study of Black Bears Finds It’s Not the Mamas That Should Be Feared the Most', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bears', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T04:01:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pam Belluck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belluck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c4af40b-cad7-5549-bfbd-f6852051b826', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c4af40b-cad7-5549-bfbd-f6852051b826'}, {'abstract': 'If you can’t squeeze a workout into the day’s busy schedule, bobbing your foot, walking to the window or chopping onions for dinner may help to maintain or augment your fitness.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/fidgeting-your-way-to-fitness/', 'snippet': 'If you can’t squeeze a workout into the day’s busy schedule, bobbing your foot, walking to the window or chopping onions for dinner may help to maintain or augment your fitness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If work is interfering with your plans for a workout today, perhaps you should drum your fingers against your desktop in frustration. Doing so may help, in some small way, to maintain or augment your fitness, at least according to a study published last month in the journal Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fidgeting Your Way to Fitness', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T04:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'n