kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T06:39:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d16a2bc2-53e8-5d25-b72c-e0d96da714ef', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d16a2bc2-53e8-5d25-b72c-e0d96da714ef'}, {'abstract': 'Charges of corruption by the former president of England’s Football Association have added a new chapter in the continuing story of corruption inside FIFA’s World Cup bidding process.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/f-a-official-names-names-but-only-after-playing-along/', 'snippet': 'Charges of corruption by the former president of England’s Football Association have added a new chapter in the continuing story of corruption inside FIFA’s World Cup bidding process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a strong stench of foul play when FIFA awarded the hosting rights of the 2018 and 2022 World Cups to Russia and Qatar six months ago. Several reports of corruption and bribery within the sport’s world governing body preceded the final voting process, calling into question the integrity of the organization and way in which it operates. And the final decision only served to heighten suspicions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.A. Official Names Names, but Only After Playing Along', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2018 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blatter, Sepp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Triesman, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T06:55:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Ruthven', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruthven', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc427917-9dcf-53f7-afd6-1bffe36657f0', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc427917-9dcf-53f7-afd6-1bffe36657f0'}, {'abstract': 'The carmaker, whose operations have been severely disrupted since the earthquake and tsunami in Japan, said that it expects production to begin recovering sooner than forecast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/business/global/12toyota.html', 'snippet': 'The carmaker, whose operations have been severely disrupted since the earthquake and tsunami in Japan, said that it expects production to begin recovering sooner than forecast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toyota, whose operations have been severely disrupted since the March 11 earthquake and tsunami in Japan, said Wednesday that its quarterly profit fell 77 percent but that it expected production to begin recovering up to two months sooner than previously forecast.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Quake, Toyota’s Profit Plummets 77%', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Quake, Toyota’s Profit Plummets 77%', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T07:09:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bunkley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bunkley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4206976c-658f-580c-b159-4d80a57bd8d5', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4206976c-658f-580c-b159-4d80a57bd8d5'}, {'abstract': 'One of the biggest European banks, HSBC said it would focus on commercial banking activities and wealth management, while scaling back its retail banking operations.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/hsbc-aims-for-3-5-billion-in-savings-trimming-retail/', 'snippet': 'One of the biggest European banks, HSBC said it would focus on commercial banking activities and wealth management, while scaling back its retail banking operations.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:49 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'HSBC Aiming to Cut $3.5 Billion in Costs', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diamond, Robert E Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gulliver, Stuart', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Bank AG', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC.', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T07:33:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f65a622-3286-5b06-a638-b0c8189d394d', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f65a622-3286-5b06-a638-b0c8189d394d'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Simon’s show at the Beacon Theater on Tuesday night was all about his pursuit of ecstasy through rhythm, and it was marvelous: brisk and unsinkable, smartly balanced, deftly paced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/arts/music/paul-simon-beacon-theater-music-review.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Simon’s show at the Beacon Theater on Tuesday night was all about his pursuit of ecstasy through rhythm, and it was marvelous: brisk and unsinkable, smartly balanced, deftly paced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Simon assumed the voice of God at the Beacon Theater on Tuesday night. I mean that literally: during an interlude in “Love Is Eternal Sacred Light” from his new album, “So Beautiful or So What,” Mr. Simon threw his arms wide, deepened his voice and ventured his best deity. Somehow the Supreme Being, confiding and complaining as if bellied up to the bar, sounded a lot like a guy in a Paul Simon song. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/SIMON/SIMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/SIMON/SIMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/SIMON/SIMON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju