 Denver walks readers through an enviable conundrum: trying to choose between two of the nation’s finest colleges.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/cherry-creek-uyanga-tamir-5/', 'snippet': 'Uyanga Tamir of Cherry Creek High in Denver walks readers through an enviable conundrum: trying to choose between two of the nation’s finest colleges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, at a school-sponsored event called “After Prom,” a fortuneteller predicted that I was going to make a tough choice between two great colleges. Despite my disbelief in such prophecies, I walked off wondering how likely that would be. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fortuneteller’s Prophecy: Dartmouth vs. Princeton', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:16:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uyanga Tamir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uyanga', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tamir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f4be0d1-ffc1-5ad2-80f7-579866be00eb', 'word_count': 662, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f4be0d1-ffc1-5ad2-80f7-579866be00eb'}, {'abstract': 'Dissecting the sounds of the city’s often-heard taxi horns, while a few New Yorkers with recognizable voices offer their own renditions.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/off-the-rails-taxi-horns/', 'snippet': 'Dissecting the sounds of the city’s often-heard taxi horns, while a few New Yorkers with recognizable voices offer their own renditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Out-of-control horns are one of the biggest complaints about taxis in New York. So thousands of ringing ears perked up last week when the city announced that its high-tech new taxicab, the Nissan NV200, would include a so-called “low-annoyance” horn, which promised to reduce ear-splitting honks. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Imagining the Sound of a Taxi Horn Not Honking', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Edward I', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mason, Jackie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:20:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0a892e2-7baa-515a-9e2a-75119a72c237', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0a892e2-7baa-515a-9e2a-75119a72c237'}, {'abstract': 'The theft forces a shutdown of part of the ancient palace.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/rare-works-stolen-from-exhibition-at-forbidden-city-in-beijing/', 'snippet': 'The theft forces a shutdown of part of the ancient palace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Rare handicrafts from a private museum in Hong Kong that were on display at the Forbidden City in Beijing were stolen on Sunday, forcing a shutdown of one part of the ancient palace, according to an article on Wednesday in the English-language edition of Global Times, a government-run newspaper in Beijing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rare Works Stolen From Exhibition at Forbidden City in Beijing', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:24:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e3344ee-e4f6-5850-b88f-5d3041762fb9', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e3344ee-e4f6-5850-b88f-5d3041762fb9'}, {'abstract': 'It hardly qualifies as news these days when another athlete gets in Twitter trouble. But there was something a little deeper going on in the case of the tennis player Donald Young a few weeks ago.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/double-faults/', 'snippet': 'It hardly qualifies as news these days when another athlete gets in Twitter trouble. But there was something a little deeper going on in the case of the tennis player Donald Young a few weeks ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It hardly qualifies as news these days when another athlete gets in Twitter trouble. But there was something a little deeper going on in the case of the tennis player Donald Young a few weeks ago. Young, upset at how the U.S.T.A. was allotting the wild-card entry it had to the French Open this month (the association used a playoff that Young lost), leveled a tweet at the organization that we can’t print. Patrick McEnroe, the head of player of development, blasted back and demanded an apology from Young before his organization would back Young any further. (Young closed out his Twitter account and apologized.) Beneath it all there seemed to simmer tensions over coaching, player development and support and maybe even race.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Double Faults', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wachter, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Donald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:24:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9effeb7-448f-5b7b-8274-10fadf4f9535', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9effeb7-448f-5b7b-8274-10fadf4f9535'}, {'abstract': 'Term for House seats which may or may not exist, depending on how district lines are drawn.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/schrodingers-seat/', 'snippet': 'Term for House seats which may or may not exist, depending on how district lines are drawn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Term for House seats which may or may not exist, depending on how district lines are drawn.Writing for National Journal, Charlie Cook commented:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schrödinger’s Seat', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/078ea1c8-5335-5705-9219-6483c7862cfb', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/078ea1c8-5335-5705-9219-6483c7862cfb'}, {'abstract': 'A year after testifying in relation to the gulf oil spill, the oil industry’s Big Five executives will appear before a Senate panel to explain why they need more than $4 billion a year in tax breaks even as they rake in near-record profits.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/round-up-the-usual-c-e-o-s/', 'snippet': 'A year after testifying in relation to the gulf oil spill, the oil industry’s Big Five executives will appear before a Senate panel to explain why they need more than $4 billion a year in tax breaks even as they rake in near-record profits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been nearly a year since the chief executives of the five biggest multinational oil companies were hauled before a committee of Congress for a ritual flaying. Last June, the heads of Exxon Mobil, Chevron, Shell, BP and ConocoPhillips were called by the House Energy and Commerce Committee to testify on the Deepwater Horizon disaster, which at the time was still pouring 50,000 barrels of oil a day into the Gulf of Mexico.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Round Up the Usual C.E.O.’s', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevron Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ConocoPhillips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exxon Mobil Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Dutch Shell Plc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:28:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dc6e92c-868c-5537-af75-ee4de16ec4e2', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dc6e92c-868c-5537-af75-ee4de16ec4e2'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists measure the amount of oxygen in the water between sediment particles to calculate the rate at which carbon reaches the bottom of the ocean.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/collecting-sediments-on-the-ocean-floor/', 'snippet': 'Scientists measure the amount of oxygen in the water between sediment particles to calculate the rate at which carbon reaches the bottom of the ocean.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alyson Santoro, a postdoctoral researcher at Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, writes from off the coast of Chile, where she is studying microbes in the nitrogen cycle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Collecting Sediments on the Ocean Floor', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microbiology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T15:44:36+0000', 'document_type