,\xa0customized to your situation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tool to Weigh Debt Payment Options', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Experian Group Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T16:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1bbda88-4796-58be-8b10-e971286bf00a', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1bbda88-4796-58be-8b10-e971286bf00a'}, {'abstract': 'Is there ever a deadline so urgent you can’t eat with your hands?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/extreme-work-habits/', 'snippet': 'Is there ever a deadline so urgent you can’t eat with your hands?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I used to consider myself a pretty hard worker. But then I read Burkhard Bilger’s terrific profile in The New Yorker of David Eagleman, a neuroscientist at Baylor College of Medicine, and it turns out I’m a slacker. I’m never going to pass the potato standard established by Eagleman when he was a grad student:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Extreme Work Habits', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eagleman, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T16:55:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Titunik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Titunik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d03c10b-6305-5992-a0ad-d7c179d8d077', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d03c10b-6305-5992-a0ad-d7c179d8d077'}, {'abstract': 'Manohla Dargis, a chief film critic of The New York Times, is in France for the Cannes Film Festival.  She’ll answer readers’ questions in her regular Q. and A. session with her colleague, A.O. Scott. Submit your questions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/ask-the-film-critics-cannes-edition/', 'snippet': 'Manohla Dargis, a chief film critic of The New York Times, is in France for the Cannes Film Festival.  She’ll answer readers’ questions in her regular Q. and A. session with her colleague, A.O. Scott. Submit your questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Manohla Dargis, a chief film critic of The New York Times, is in France for the Cannes Film Festival where Woody Allen’s “Midnight in Paris” will open the festival tonight. Ms. Dargis and her colleague A.O. Scott are taking questions by e-mail as part of their regular monthly exchange with Times readers – “Ask the Film Critics” – where they respond to queries about individual films, industry trends and historical trivia. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ask the Film Critics,’ Cannes Edition', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEN, WOODY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dargis, Manohla', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cannes (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T17:02:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a062b8d7-3f14-59be-920e-dbc4363ed4b4', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a062b8d7-3f14-59be-920e-dbc4363ed4b4'}, {'abstract': 'Danza Contemporánea de Cuba, in existence for more than five decades, is only now enjoying its first American tour, starting at the Joyce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/arts/dance/danza-contemporanea-de-cuba-review.html', 'snippet': 'Danza Contemporánea de Cuba, in existence for more than five decades, is only now enjoying its first American tour, starting at the Joyce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though Danza Contemporánea de Cuba has been in existence for more than five decades, it is only now enjoying its first American tour. Given the dearth of exchange between the United States and Cuba, anticipation had been understandably high before Tuesday night, when the troupe made its Joyce Theater debut as part of the interdisciplinary festival ¡Si Cuba! How would these performances compare to those of more familiar companies?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/danza1/danza1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/danza1/danza1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/11/arts/danza1/danza1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}