: [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dirty Secret’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T21:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessie Sholl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sholl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fcedaa0-aefb-55c8-b316-a755eadb8c10', 'word_count': 5268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fcedaa0-aefb-55c8-b316-a755eadb8c10'}, {'abstract': 'Top executives from AT&T and T-Mobile USA went to Capitol Hill to argue that a combined company would produce better mobile service for consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/technology/12phone.html', 'snippet': 'Top executives from AT&T and T-Mobile USA went to Capitol Hill to argue that a combined company would produce better mobile service for consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A few weeks ago, AT&T and T-Mobile were robust competitors. T-Mobile ads made merciless fun of AT&T’s reputation for dropped calls and sluggish wireless data connections while celebrating T-Mobile’s fast network and low rates. AT&T promoted superior nationwide service and its exclusive array of the sleekest, fastest smartphones.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/business/Phone/Phone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'AT&T and T-Mobile Chiefs Field Skeptical Questions on Capitol Hill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AT&T and T-Mobile Chiefs Field Skeptical Questions on Capitol Hill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T21:17:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cc2ceeb-a488-5452-bbdb-4456af6ea406', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cc2ceeb-a488-5452-bbdb-4456af6ea406'}, {'abstract': 'Sutton Foster says she wants to be known “as an actress who sings and dances.” But this is far from the way she started off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/theater/theaterspecial/sutton-foster-stars-in-anything-goes.html', 'snippet': 'Sutton Foster says she wants to be known “as an actress who sings and dances.” But this is far from the way she started off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS a little girl growing up in Georgia, Sutton Foster used to make up her own opera arias and sing them in the bathtub. Her family had no background in music, acting or theater — her father worked for General Motors. So as a child her instinctive desire to sound operatic was curious.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/SUTTON-1/SUTTON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/SUTTON-1/SUTTON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/SUTTON-1/SUTTON-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/SUTTON-1/SUTTON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/SUTTON-1/SUTTON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Big Belter Who Found a True Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Big Belter Who Found A True Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Sutton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T21:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Tony Awards', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dbfc388-20bb-5ec7-8c29-653c933cbe27', 'word_count': 1595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dbfc388-20bb-5ec7-8c29-653c933cbe27'}, {'abstract': 'At the MATA Festival at Le Poisson Rouge on Tuesday night, the composers were individualists, and their works tilted toward the experimental.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/arts/music/at-mata-festival-christopher-adler-acme-and-others-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the MATA Festival at Le Poisson Rouge on Tuesday night, the composers were individualists, and their works tilted toward the experimental.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The MATA Festival, now in its 13th year, has always been nondogmatic, even antidogmatic. Its focus is young composers, never a monolithic bunch even when they share nationalities and musical backgrounds, and even harder to pigeonhole once you bring them in from around the globe, as MATA’s directors have always done. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/sub-Mata-1/sub-Mata-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/sub-Mata-1/sub-Mata-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/12/arts/sub-Mata-1/sub-Mata-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seven Composers, Seven Countries', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seven Composers, Seven Countries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MATA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-11T21:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank':