: 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge F. Kovaleski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Kovaleski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad1a0dc7-4557-58e3-90e0-bad5c77f1d6b', 'word_count': 1501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad1a0dc7-4557-58e3-90e0-bad5c77f1d6b'}, {'abstract': 'If Wall Street is not going to be held more accountable, we need to know why.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/dont-let-go-of-the-anger/', 'snippet': 'If Wall Street is not going to be held more accountable, we need to know why.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most frustrating facts of the recently abated financial crisis is that those who might have been partly responsible for it have got off scot-free. The only two people prosecuted criminally — the Bear Stearns hedge fund managers Ralph Cioffi and Matthew Tannin — were found not guilty by a jury in Brooklyn. Other potential culprits — Angelo Mozilo, chief executive of Countrywide Financial, Joseph Cassano, chief executive of AIG Financial Products, and Dick Fuld, the chief executive of Lehman Brothers — were either slapped with a small civil penalty, in the case of Mozilo, or the Justice Department made the decision not to prosecute after months of investigation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Let Go of the Anger', 'kicker': 'William D. Cohan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T00:30:25+0000', 'document_type': '