com/2011/05/11/next-up-a-crackdown-on-outside-expert-firms/', 'snippet': 'Authorities suspect insider trading at some expert network firms, which match investors like hedge funds with outside experts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the government securing a conviction against Raj Rajaratnam of the Galleon Group on Wednesday, federal prosecutors will shift their focus to expert networks — the intricate web of money managers, corporate executives and consultants at the center of another wave of insider trading cases.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Next Up, a Crackdown on Outside-Expert Firms', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consultants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Primary Global Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:11:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2e148e1-610e-5e33-90b8-4cefe10e1da4', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2e148e1-610e-5e33-90b8-4cefe10e1da4'}, {'abstract': 'Telling tall tales may be a matter of pride in Texas. But it may soon be against state law to tell one about a fish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/us/12fish.html', 'snippet': 'Telling tall tales may be a matter of pride in Texas. But it may soon be against state law to tell one about a fish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Telling tall tales may be a matter of pride in Texas. But it may soon be against state law to tell one about a fish.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/us/FISH/FISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/us/FISH/FISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/12/us/FISH/FISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Texas Passes Bill to Make Some Fish Tales a Crime', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Texas Bill Adds A Grain of Salt To Fish Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Sport', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:12:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erica Goode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c62703e-3eaf-5a7a-8044-67ed0db5b7db', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c62703e-3eaf-5a7a-8044-67ed0db5b7db'}, {'abstract': 'India’s Supreme Court declined to reduce the charges against seven men convicted of negligence for their roles in the 1984 leak at the Union Carbide chemical plant in Bhopal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/world/asia/12briefs-India.html', 'snippet': 'India’s Supreme Court declined to reduce the charges against seven men convicted of negligence for their roles in the 1984 leak at the Union Carbide chemical plant in Bhopal.', 'lead_paragraph': ' India’s Supreme Court dismissed a petition on Wednesday to reconsider a decision to reduce the charges against seven men convicted for their roles in the 1984 leak at the Union Carbide chemical plant in Bhopal. The ruling was a setback to the victims of the gas leak who sought tougher penalties against the executives serving at the time of the accident, which killed 3,000 people and sickened thousands more. India’s Central Bureau of Investigation had filed a petition requesting that the men be charged with the Indian equivalent of manslaughter instead of negligence. The executives, all Indian citizens, were sentenced last year to two years in prison. Union Carbide paid a $470 million settlement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India: Court Stands by Charges in Bhopal Leak', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India: Court Stands By Charges in Bhopal Leak', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bhopal (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union Carbide Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:16:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4676298-546e-55f6-a91b-8f94a51c2a90', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4676298-546e-55f6-a91b-8f94a51c2a90'}, {'abstract': 'During Mr. Reinhardt’s tenure at the network, he oversaw the opening of overseas news bureaus and the creation of shows like “Crossfire” and “Larry King Live.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/business/media/12reinhardt.html', 'snippet': 'During Mr. Reinhardt’s tenure at the network, he oversaw the opening of overseas news bureaus and the creation of shows like “Crossfire” and “Larry King Live.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Burt Reinhardt, who as the detail-minded, taciturn television executive behind his more extroverted boss, Ted Turner, played a crucial role in the formative years of CNN and the 24-hour cable news cycle, died on Tuesday at his home in Marietta, Ga. He was 91.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/business/media/12reinhardtPicA/12reinhardtPicA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/12/business/media/12reinhardtPicA/12reinhardtPicA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Burt Reinhardt, Ex-CNN President, Is Dead at 91', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Burt Reinhardt, 91, Former President of CNN', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cable News Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:17:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Hevesi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hevesi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/edf70612-02c8-5b9c-b2bd-026b0a62f181', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edf70612-02c8-5b9c-b2bd-026b0a62f181'}, {'abstract': 'Kizza Besigye, Uganda’s main opposition politician and recent protest leader, was barred Wednesday from flying home after undergoing medical treatment in Kenya, Kenya Airways said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/world/africa/12briefs-uganda.html', 'snippet': 'Kizza Besigye, Uganda’s main opposition politician and recent protest leader, was barred Wednesday from flying home after undergoing medical treatment in Kenya, Kenya Airways said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Uganda’s main opposition politician and recent protest leader was barred Wednesday from flying home after undergoing medical treatment in Kenya, Kenya Airways said. Kizza Besigye, left, who was being treated for injuries sustained during a recent arrest, was blocked from boarding a flight to Uganda after the airline said it was told by “internal intelligence sources” that the flight would not be allowed to land in Uganda with Mr. Besigye onboard. Uganda’s minister of information, Kabakumba Matsiko, told Parliament that the government did not block Mr. Besigye’s return, but that the former Kenyan President Daniel arap Moi was on the plane and did not want him on it, The Associated Press reported. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uganda: Opposition Leader Kept Off Flight', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uganda: Opposition Leader Kept Off Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Besigye, Kizza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kenya Airways', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:18:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/438378b6-85a4-5d54-bdcf-dbadf7004700', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/438378b6-85a4-5d54-bdcf-dbadf7004700'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'May 11, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ede2c0a-b2b6-5cc1-9d54-16dc1108ab22', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ede2c0a-b2b6-5cc1-9d54-16dc1108ab22'}, {'abstract': 'The Nocturnalist: Off to a party for a 23-foot bronze teddy bear, a Parsons fashion benefit and a hospital fund-raiser.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/nyregion/a-party-whose-honoree-slumped-on-the-pavement-nocturnalist.html', 'snippet': 'The Nocturnalist: Off to a party for a 23-foot bronze teddy bear, a Parsons fashion benefit and a hospital fund-raiser.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a teddy bear, its weight measured in tons, sitting along Park Avenue, and we spent Saturday night at a curbside party for said bear. But that was not the strangest part of our outing. The lumpy, 23-foot-tall lemon-yellow bear — its forlorn button eyes and akimbo ears appear nearly hacked off by, of all things, a giant black gas lamp that bisects its head — looks as squishy as the Velveteen Rabbit, despite being made of rumpled bronze.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/nyregion/NOCTURNALIST-WEB/NOCTURNALIST-WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/12/nyregion/NOCTURNALIST-WEB/NOCTURNALIST-WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/nyregion/NOCTURNALIST-WEB/NOCTURNALIST-WEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/nyregion/NOCTURNALIST-WEB/NOCTURNALIST-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/12/nyregion/NOCTURNALIST-WEB/NOCTURNALIST-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Party Whose Honoree Was So Very Touchable', 'kicker': 'Nocturnalist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Party Whose Honoree Was So Very Touchable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:28:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5e5a6c8-e29e-5405-835c-79191eba6351', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5e5a6c8-e29e-5405-835c-79191eba6351'}, {'abstract': 'In the early days of the Civil War, Missouri’s largest city nearly fell apart before settling on an uneasy compromise.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/how-st-louis-was-won/', 'snippet': 'In the early days of the Civil War, Missouri’s largest city nearly fell apart before settling on an uneasy compromise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Missouri began the Civil War early: from the rancorous debates over the Compromise of 1850 to the ideologically driven violence spilling over the border in Kansas, its residents had long seen how disagreements over the future of slavery and westward expansion could easily spark armed conflict. But in the second week of May 1861, St. Louisans could imagine what the end of the Civil War might bring as well: after a bloody skirmish between Confederate sympathizers and federal troops in St. Louis, city leaders sought to instill a new order, one that would maintain the property rights of Confederate sympathizers while guaranteeing Union control of the city. Some called it victory; some called it occupation. With the perspective of 150 years, we might call it the beginnings of Reconstruction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How St. Louis Was Won', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Arenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9180fa6a-444c-5d08-81c2-3494dbc72029', 'word_count': 1369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9180fa6a-444c-5d08-81c2-3494dbc72029'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on May 12, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/pageoneplus/corrections.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on May 12, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NATIONAL', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:34:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3ea2b7e-6477-5b26-b1ed-74a9a54719e6', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3ea2b7e-6477-5b26-b1ed-74a9a54719e6'}, {'abstract': 'After years of frittering away Cisco’s cash on acquisitions and overpriced stock buybacks, John Chambers has gotten religion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/business/12views.html', 'snippet': 'After years of frittering away Cisco’s cash on acquisitions and overpriced stock buybacks, John Chambers has gotten religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Chambers and Steve Ballmer have much in common. For more than a decade, both have run dominant, rapidly maturing tech juggernauts — Mr. Chambers at Cisco Systems and Mr. Ballmer at Microsoft. And both have let down their shareholders. The difference is that one seems to have recognized the error of his ways and is repenting.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/12/business/Views/Views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Head of Cisco Mends His Ways', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'con