Bid As Opening a Door to Past Pain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Mitchell E Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:39:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca05c6d0-ceb3-5a2a-bef2-92459a46729e', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca05c6d0-ceb3-5a2a-bef2-92459a46729e'}, {'abstract': 'The conviction of a major hedge fund manager comes at a moment of distrust in the markets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/opinion/12thu1.html', 'snippet': 'The conviction of a major hedge fund manager comes at a moment of distrust in the markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The jury in the illegal insider trading case against Raj Rajaratnam, the billionaire founder of the Galleon Group hedge fund, did what the government has largely failed to do: hold a major Wall Street figure accountable for his reckless behavior. It is an important step toward restoring trust as a prerequisite of America’s financial markets.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street, Held Accountable', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wall Street, Held Accountable: The conviction of a major hedge fund manager comes at a moment of distrust in the markets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Galleon Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:40:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0ec61a3-ae7e-587f-8954-097011b1c6bd', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0ec61a3-ae7e-587f-8954-097011b1c6bd'}, {'abstract': 'Lawsuits brought by whistle-blowers make a strong case for why more regulation of higher-education companies is needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/opinion/12thu3.html', 'snippet': 'Lawsuits brought by whistle-blowers make a strong case for why more regulation of higher-education companies is needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The for-profit education industry complained of excessive regulation last fall when the Obama administration issued new rules intended to curb abuses at profit-making colleges and trade schools. But lawsuits brought by whistle-blowers with firsthand knowledge of the industry make a strong case for why tough rules are needed. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Education Is the Last Thing on Their Minds', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Education Is the Last Thing on Their Minds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Management Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whistle-Blowers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:43:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f854af4-ad0e-5c12-bf81-c86a50da38cd', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f854af4-ad0e-5c12-bf81-c86a50da38cd'}, {'abstract': 'Nassau County announced the referendum on a $400 million bond issue, which would be used for a new hockey arena and a minor league baseball stadium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/sports/hockey/vote-scheduled-on-bonds-for-new-islanders-arena.html', 'snippet': 'Nassau County announced the referendum on a $400 million bond issue, which would be used for a new hockey arena and a minor league baseball stadium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fate of the Islanders’ long quest for another home may be decided Aug. 1, when Nassau County residents will vote in a special referendum to approve or reject a $400 million bond issue to finance a new arena for the team. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vote Scheduled on Bonds for New Islanders Arena', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vote Scheduled on Bonds for New Islanders Arena', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Islanders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nassau County (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nassau Coliseum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T01:43:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63469db3-6f9e-5d79-a3c5-7eda4a39555d', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63469db3-6f9e-5d79-a3c5-7eda4a39555d'}, {'abstract': 'The government won its biggest victory yet in its widening investigation of insider trading when Raj Rajaratnam was found guilty of fraud and conspiracy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/11/galleon-conviction-is-expected-to-embolden-prosecutors/', 'snippet': 'The government won its biggest victory yet in its widening investigation of insider trading when Raj Rajaratnam was found guilty of fraud and conspiracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Raj Rajaratnam, the billionaire investor who once ran one of the world’s largest hedge funds, was found guilty on Wednesday of fraud and conspiracy by a federal jury in Manhattan, giving the government its biggest victory yet in a widening investigation of insider trading.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Galleon Conviction Likely to Embolden Prosecutors', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Galleon Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05