ead_paragraph': '7:52 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2nd G.M. Stock Sale May Come Later Than Expected', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN INTERNATIONAL GROUP INC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T13:53:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/17ab7203-4540-5bea-87e7-65a0e3eca4d1', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17ab7203-4540-5bea-87e7-65a0e3eca4d1'}, {'abstract': 'The French have made no secret of their love for Woody Allen, and they flocked to the Palais des Festivals for the premiere of “Midnight in Paris” at this year’s typically surreal Cannes Film Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/midnight-in-cannes-and-a-surreal-opening-ceremony/', 'snippet': 'The French have made no secret of their love for Woody Allen, and they flocked to the Palais des Festivals for the premiere of “Midnight in Paris” at this year’s typically surreal Cannes Film Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France — Lady Gaga was performing on the beach a few hundred feet away, but you’d never have known it at the start of the Cannes Film Festival on Wednesday, where everything was Woody-mania. The French have made no secret of their adoration for Monsieur Allen, whose “Midnight in Paris” opened the festivities, and drew legions of fans to the Palais des Festivals. “We will be on a balcony overlooking it, saying, ‘Woody, Woody!’ ” said one local, who couldn’t manage to get in. Around the red carpet, among the throngs of tuxedo-clad men looking for extra tickets to the premiere, was one who held a sign which read simply: “Woody … Merci.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Midnight’ in Cannes and a Surreal Opening Ceremony', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T14:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9e7e33e-7a45-501e-bba0-9a0fe419d2a7', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9e7e33e-7a45-501e-bba0-9a0fe419d2a7'}, {'abstract': 'Here is a look at 13 unlucky (or under-performing) Yanks who will probably not look back on their 2010-11 seasons with pride.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/americans-abroad-report-card-part-ii-the-rest-of-the-class/', 'snippet': 'Here is a look at 13 unlucky (or under-performing) Yanks who will probably not look back on their 2010-11 seasons with pride.', 'lead_paragraph': ' On Wednesday we posted our grades for the top-performing American soccer players competing overseas — the honor roll, if you will.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans Abroad, Part II: The Rest of the Class', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'English Premier League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'German Bundesliga', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T14:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Godfrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Godfrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/13f14c8d-3c54-50b7-960b-03f5e2ab7162', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13f14c8d-3c54-50b7-960b-03f5e2ab7162'}, {'abstract': 'What words tell us.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/use-your-words/', 'snippet': 'What words tell us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s notoriously difficult to figure out which top collegiate quarterbacks will succeed in the NFL. Pro teams give prospects an SAT-type test called the Wonderlic test, which doesn’t predict all that accurately.\xa0 A company called Achievement Metrics, on the other hand, studies the words college athletes use in media interviews. Some use words that signal high degrees of confidence, conceptual complexity and team orientation. Others use words that signal self-centeredness and distrust.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Use Your Words', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco 49ers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T14:06:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastna