cer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/13ed7447-bc1e-5e24-bcff-04d8142fd6b6', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13ed7447-bc1e-5e24-bcff-04d8142fd6b6'}, {'abstract': 'A bungled art heist this week at the Palace Museum in the Forbidden City in Beijing has prompted questions and criticisms about security at Chinese museums.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/world/asia/13beijing.html', 'snippet': 'A bungled art heist this week at the Palace Museum in the Forbidden City in Beijing has prompted questions and criticisms about security at Chinese museums.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — A bungled art theft this week at the Palace Museum in the Forbidden City in Beijing may not have been worthy of a master thief, with the police quickly claiming to have found the suspect, but the episode has nonetheless produced questions and criticism about security at Chinese museums. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theft Reveals Lapses in Chinese Museum’s Security', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theft Reveals Lapses in Chinese Museum’s Security', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palace Museum (Beijing)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T14:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', '