 Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shortz, Will', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T14:59:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2a6dc6-fd1c-504c-a23b-8248a5e89ccc', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2a6dc6-fd1c-504c-a23b-8248a5e89ccc'}, {'abstract': 'There is a proud mother who lives in Texas. Her son, Todd Pletcher, is the trainer of the winner of the 136th Kentucky Derby winner, Super Saver.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/the-mother-behind-a-derby-winning-trainer/', 'snippet': 'There is a proud mother who lives in Texas. Her son, Todd Pletcher, is the trainer of the winner of the 136th Kentucky Derby winner, Super Saver.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a bucket-list moment, Julie June Stewart woke up at 4 a.m. and bought a ticket to the 2008 Belmont. She hasn’t stopped going to the races since. That is when she isn’t isn’t taking on a wildfire, hurricane, volcano or oil spill as the nation’s leading expert in disaster airspace coordination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mother Behind a Derby-Winning Trainer', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T15:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie June Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': 'June', 'lastname': 'Stewart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0f588fd-0527-5c30-aa21-b74c4ca57b07', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0f588fd-0527-5c30-aa21-b74c4ca57b07'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Museum has filed suit against  Robert Wylde, a British collector who bought a painting from the Gagosian Gallery that belongs partly to the museum, and that wasn’t for sale.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/met-museum-sues-man-who-bought-a-painting-it-wasnt-selling/', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Museum has filed suit against  Robert Wylde, a British collector who bought a painting from the Gagosian Gallery that belongs partly to the museum, and that wasn’t for sale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Chapter 2 of a long story that is growing longer, the Metropolitan Museum of Art filed suit Tuesday in United States District Court in Manhattan seeking possession of a painting now in the hands of Robert Wylde, a British collector. The 1981 painting, “The Innocent Eye Test” by Mark Tansey, was bought by Mr. Wylde for $2.5 million from the Gagosian Gallery in 2009. Last year, the gallery told Mr. Wylde it had learned that the Met already owned 31 percent of the painting — which depicts a cow gazing at a portrait of other cows — and that the other owner had promised that the museum would eventually own the whole thing. Mr. Wylde filed suit in federal court in Manhattan against the gallery in March of this year, contending it had misled him, and held on to the painting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Met Museum Sues Man Who Bought a Painting It Wasn’t Selling', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowles, Charles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Golub, Aaron Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tansey, Mark', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gagosian Gallery', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T15:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d339e92-7a57-511a-bd5d-2eba5614325c', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d339e92-7a57-511a-bd5d-2eba5614325c'}, {'abstract': 'The influence of Friends, Fans and Followers on purchasing decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/f-factor/', 'snippet': 'The influence of Friends, Fans and Followers on purchasing decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The influence of Friends, Fans and Followers on purchasing decisions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F-Factor', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T15:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/73a8bcf1-814d-5d43-b234-463e80119ad9', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73a8bcf1-814d-5d43-b234-463e80119ad9'}, {'abstract': 'The Butcher’s Guild opens its ranks to the public.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/butchers-of-the-world-unite/', 'snippet': 'The Butcher’s Guild opens its ranks to the public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On June 1, people who love meat will have a new way to express their feelings: by joining the Butcher’s Guild, a new trade organization for butchers. The guild was started last month, complete with a mission statement, a crest (a hand, a heart, a steer, a book) and an oath. The founders, Tia Harrison (a chef and butcher) and Marissa Guggiano (a writer), say the goal is to build an active national community of butchers and meat-centric chefs. Charter members include the nose-to-tail celebrities Chris Cosentino of Boccalone in San Francisco, Guy Arnone of Eataly in Manhattan and the women behind the hip new butcher shop Lindy & Grundy  in Los Angeles, Erika Nakamura and Amelia Posada. (Tattoos are not required for membership, though they are prevalent.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Butchers of the World, Unite!', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T15:03:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/36f28be0-48db-5d26-a7b7-afec3ac085c3', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36f28be0-48db-5d26-a7b7-afec3ac085c3'}, {'abstract': 'After Japan’s earthquake, General Motors identified 118 products that it needed to monitor for shortages; it has resolved issues with all but five.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto.html', 'snippet': 'After Japan’s earthquake, General Motors identified 118 products that it needed to monitor for shortages; it has resolved issues with all but five.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Two months after Japan’s devastating earthquake and tsunami, Japanese automakers in the United States continue to struggle with significant supply disruptions. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto-span/13auto-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto-span/13auto-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto-span/13auto-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto-span/13auto-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/business/global/13auto-span/13auto-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Piecing Together a Supply Chain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Piecing Together a Supply Chain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles',