'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/77a27381-93b4-5343-a1d5-4dde21729d4d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/77a27381-93b4-5343-a1d5-4dde21729d4d'}, {'abstract': 'Along with the well-known heights and sights, a weekend in San Francisco can take in the city’s maritime heart — its waterfront, much of which has been refurbished.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/travel/36-hours-in-san-francisco.html', 'snippet': 'Along with the well-known heights and sights, a weekend in San Francisco can take in the city’s maritime heart — its waterfront, much of which has been refurbished.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO typically wows visitors with its heights or its sights. The hills sear themselves into memory after a few up-and-down-and-up-again cable car rides or punishing walks. Then there are the vistas: the sweeping views of the city, the postcard-ready Victorians below Alamo Square Park. But surrounding all of that is the waterfront: miles of Pacific Ocean coastline and the expansive natural harbor of San Francisco Bay. Once a working industrial area with pockets of outright blight, much of the city’s waterfront has been polished into another of its pleasures. Start exploring in the east, south of the Bay Bridge, and loop your way west to the Golden Gate and then south to Ocean Beach. In one weekend romp, you’ll join San Franciscans in many of the places they love best — and see what remains of their city’s maritime heart.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15hoursspan/15hoursspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15hoursspan/15hoursspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15hoursspan/15hoursspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15hoursspan/15hoursspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in San Francisco', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'San Francisco', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T19:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26b4cd07-86ba-57d9-885b-bd5e4dc5ac98', 'word_count': 1505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26b4cd07-86ba-57d9-885b-bd5e4dc5ac98'}, {'abstract': 'A study explores improving classroom instruction, using research-backed methods for testing students’ understanding as well as how science is taught.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/science/13teach.html', 'snippet': 'A study explores improving classroom instruction, using research-backed methods for testing students’ understanding as well as how science is taught.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past few years, scientists have been working to transform education from the inside out, by applying findings from learning and memory research where they could do the most good, in the classroom. A study published in the journal Science on Thursday illustrates how promising this work can be — and how treacherous.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia'