at a space becomes virtually unlivable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Children of Hoarders', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hoarding', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T19:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fd5fdc1-c467-5311-bd40-e5de7846624a', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fd5fdc1-c467-5311-bd40-e5de7846624a'}, {'abstract': 'Typography and tagging come together in an exhibition, “Getting Upper,” at the Pasadena Museum of California Art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/seeing-things-getting-upper/', 'snippet': 'Typography and tagging come together in an exhibition, “Getting Upper,” at the Pasadena Museum of California Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tagging and typography come together in “Getting Upper: Graphic Designers and Artists Reconsider the Alphabet,” an exhibition that opens this weekend at the Pasadena Museum of California Art. While typographers build letters and alphabets, graffiti artists take them apart, reshaping language with their tags.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | ‘Getting Upper’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T19:09:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 