nded eaters — not to mention radiation alarmists — fish is a quandary these days. Much of the seafood served in American restaurants is imported, caught using methods that endanger other animals or farmed unsustainably. But a few chefs around the country are trying to turn the tide with local, eco-conscious menus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Safer Sushi', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sushi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T20:22:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hannah Wallace', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wallace', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/537a7876-6882-52f7-9b72-d17f5d7a054d', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/537a7876-6882-52f7-9b72-d17f5d7a054d'}, {'abstract': 'A new approach to continuing education is gaining traction: collaborative learning. The idea? We’ve all got something to learn and something to teach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/brooklyn-brainery-unclassesorg-and-brooklyn-skillshare.html', 'snippet': 'A new approach to continuing education is gaining traction: collaborative learning. The idea? We’ve all got something to learn and something to teach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LEARNING should be a lifelong pursuit. But between the cost of tuition and dark memories of failure at the blackboard, formally committing to edification can be daunting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype