ridge, please; billing a tenant for legal expenses; no guarantees on code compliance; who can file a mechanic’s lien.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/realestate/real-estate-question-and-answer.html', 'snippet': 'Keep the pictures on the fridge, please; billing a tenant for legal expenses; no guarantees on code compliance; who can file a mechanic’s lien.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keep the Pictures on the Fridge, Please', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q & A', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Q & A', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landlords', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:39:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Romano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06da539f-13b3-5a75-9b12-e86c69fdccb7', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06da539f-13b3-5a75-9b12-e86c69fdccb7'}, {'abstract': 'Hungarian silver at Christie’s; Ethiopian spells on vellum scrolls; ship models and maritime paintings for sale; and new respect for plaster-cast copies of ancient Mediterranean artifacts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/design/hungarian-silver-ethiopian-scrolls-and-maritime-art.html', 'snippet': 'Hungarian silver at Christie’s; Ethiopian spells on vellum scrolls; ship models and maritime paintings for sale; and new respect for plaster-cast copies of ancient Mediterranean artifacts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hungarian gilded-silver vessels and jewelry that narrowly escaped World War II looters will go on display on Friday for the first time in seven decades at a Christie’s auction preview in New York.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13ANTIQUES-1/ANTIQUES-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silver That Survived War and Centuries', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silver That Survived War and Centuries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:41:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45bca0aa-2355-5e33-b66c-bd87b5bfe265', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45bca0aa-2355-5e33-b66c-bd87b5bfe265'}, {'abstract': 'Caricatures and satirical art at the Metropolitan Museum; new visual and performing art at the High Line park; and a David Smith steel sculpture purchased by Colby College.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/design/infinite-jest-caricatures-at-met-and-art-at-high-line.html', 'snippet': 'Caricatures and satirical art at the Metropolitan Museum; new visual and performing art at the High Line park; and a David Smith steel sculpture purchased by Colby College.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Funny” is a rare adjective for an exhibition at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. It just might apply, however, to “Infinite Jest: Caricature and Satire From Leonardo to Levine,” which opens there on Sept. 13.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13VOGEL/VOGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13VOGEL/13VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13VOGEL/13VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Metropolitan, in All Its Hilarity', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Metropolitan, In All Its Hilarity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Colby College', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Line (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:45:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b42b8ec-4eb8-5694-a02c-715574be0bda', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b42b8ec-4eb8-5694-a02c-715574be0bda'}, {'abstract': '“The 21st century will see a fight for resources, and Russia should not be defeated in this fight,” the Russian ambassador to NATO is quoted as saying in a leaked cable.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/on-our-radar-nations-race-to-carve-up-arctic/', 'snippet': '“The 21st century will see a fight for resources, and Russia should not be defeated in this fight,” the Russian ambassador to NATO is quoted as saying in a leaked cable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A stream of leaked diplomatic cables reveal a race by Arctic nations to “carve up” the region, as melting ice, driven by climate change, opens up potentially vast natural resource reserves, including billions of barrels of oil. The cables cite warlike language from Russian officials, who have aggressively asserted claims over large swaths of the Arctic seafloor. [BBC]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Nations Race to ‘Carve Up’ Arctic', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:49:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Collins Rudolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Rudolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab9a20b5-88c0-57d7-a174-d4f2145fd363', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab9a20b5-88c0-57d7-a174-d4f2145fd363'}, {'abstract': 'A wide assortment of outdoor film series cater to a variety of audiences, from Francophiles to children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/movies/outdoor-film-series-in-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'A wide assortment of outdoor film series cater to a variety of audiences, from Francophiles to children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN New York this summer the drive-in movie is thriving. Minus the cars.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13OUTDOORS-span/OUTDOORS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13OUTDOORS-span/OUTDOORS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13OUTDOORS-span/OUTDOORS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13OUTDOORS-span/OUTDOORS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13OUTDOORS-span/OUTDOORS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Starry, Starry Cinema (The Breezes Are Free)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Starry, Starry Cinema (The Breezes Are Free)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bryant Park (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CONEY ISLAND (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Riverside Park (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island City (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:51:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b974ab30-397d-537f-a4aa-4a6b79c30f8e', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b974ab30-397d-537f-a4aa-4a6b79c30f8e'}, {'abstract': 'Robinson Cano was cleared by a neurologist to play Thursday night, the day after he was beaned by a 93-mile-per-hour fastball.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/cano-cleared-to-play-thursday-night-for-yankees/', 'snippet': 'Robinson Cano was cleared by a neurologist to play Thursday night, the day after he was beaned by a 93-mile-per-hour fastball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robinson Cano was cleared by a neurologist to play Thursday night, the day after he was beaned by a 93-mile-per-hour fastball. A CT scan taken Wednesday came back negative, and Yankees Manager Joe Girardi said all other testing, including Impact, a 20-minute computerized evaluation that assesses memory, reaction time and processing speed, came back normal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cano Cleared to Play Thursday Night for Yankees', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cano, Robinson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:52:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1423b92a-f91c-5079-8ff2-b03fbadcec58', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1423b92a-f91c-5079-8ff2-b03fbadcec58'}, {'abstract': 'The New York City Ballet opened its Spring Gala on Wednesday night with “The Seven Deadly Sins,” then closed with “Vienna Waltzes.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-dances-the-seven-deadly-sins-review.html', 'snippet': 'The New York City Ballet opened its Spring Gala on Wednesday night with “The Seven Deadly Sins,” then closed with “Vienna Waltzes.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New York City Ballet’s new production of “The Seven Deadly Sins,” the sins seem far from deadly, and not even strikingly sinful. Nothing is irritatingly wrongheaded about Lynne Taylor-Corbett’s direction or choreography — cynical ambivalence about these sins is built into this 1933 ballet by Bertolt Brecht and Kurt Weill — but nothing is remotely memorable either. Call it “A Few Minor, Dull Peccadillos” instead. After its Wednesday’s gala premiere, the only feature of the staging that stayed in my head was the mannered enunciation employed by Patti LuPone as Anna 1, as she rhymes “An-na” with “Louisian-na” and distorts her prolonged final syllables. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13GALA-span/SUB-JP-GALA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13GALA-span/SUB-JP-GALA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13GALA-span/SUB-JP-GALA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 583, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13GALA-span/SUB-JP-GALA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13GALA-span/SUB-JP-GALA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Brecht and Weill Danced, Revisited', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Brecht and Weill Danced, Revisited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whelan, Wendy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balanchine, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brecht, Bertolt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weill, Kurt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LuPone, Patti', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor-Corbett, Lynne', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2120d74-f8c7-5011-9a9c-f30317245784', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2120d74-f8c7-5011-9a9c-f30317245784'}, {'abstract': 'At Helly Nahmad Gallery, “Soutine/Bacon” the affinities between these two artists, both drawn to meaty depictions of bodies and carcasses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/design/soutinebacon-at-helly-nahmad-gallery-review.html', 'snippet': 'At Helly Nahmad Gallery, “Soutine/Bacon” the affinities between these two artists, both drawn to meaty depictions of bodies and carcasses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1952, Francis Bacon posed for photographs with two sides of beef attached, like wings, to his naked torso. The gesture was provocative, as Lady Gaga’s meat dress would be a half-century later, but it was also deferential, a nod to Chaim Soutine, whose paintings of carcasses dangling in butchers’ stalls had been haunting the art world since a 1950 retrospective at the Museum of Modern Art.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SOUTINE-span/SOUTINE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 252, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SOUTINE-span/SOUTINE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 252}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SOUTINE-span/SOUTINE-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SOUTINE-span/SOUTINE-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SOUTINE-span/SOUTINE-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Meaty Visions of Flesh and Blood', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Meaty Visions Of Flesh and Blood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soutine, Chaim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bacon, Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T21:55:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3075f744-dc77-5180-9837-eb64975d7c88', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3075f744-dc77-5180-9837-eb64975d7c88'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Photo Festival 2011 surveys the ever-widening field of photojournalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/arts/design/new-york-photo-festival-2011-brooklyn-review.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Photo Festival 2011 surveys the ever-widening field of photojournalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a witness to human history, photography has been much questioned over the past three decades. Ever more sophisticated digital techniques of manipulation have only further eroded faith in its trustworthiness as a truth teller. Yet intrepid photojournalists continue to fan out all over the world, uploading images of people, places and events of almost every conceivable kind, from scenes of mundane domesticity in remote locations to recordings of terrifying natural and manmade disaster. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13PHOTO-span/PHOTO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 