ted a vivid New York premiere of “August 4, 1964” at Carnegie Hall, as part of the Spring for Music festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In September 2008, during the second week of his first season as music director of the Dallas Symphony Orchestra, the Dutch conductor Jaap van Zweden led the premiere of “August 4, 1964,” a 70-minute oratorio by the American composer Steven Stucky, with text by the librettist Gene Scheer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SPRING-span/SPRING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SPRING-span/SPRING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SPRING-span/SPRING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SPRING-span/SPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13SPRING-span/SPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strife of ’64, in Somber Tones', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strife of ’64, in Somber Tones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Zweden, Jaap', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T22:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7fdc30d-e08f-59e9-b67a-0a016e3b058b', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7fdc30d-e08f-59e9-b67a-0a016e3b058b'}, {'abstract': 'The head of Bahrain’s military claimed that the country’s brutal crackdown on dissent was entirely justified because the kingdom’s security forces had been confronted by young protesters under the influence of mind-altering drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/bahrains-protesters-were-drugged-official-claims/', 'snippet': 'The head of Bahrain’s military claimed that the country’s brutal crackdown on dissent was entirely justified because the kingdom’s security forces had been confronted by young protesters under the influence of mind-altering drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adopting what might be called the Qaddafi defense, the head of Bahrain’s military claimed that the country’s brutal crackdown on dissent was entirely justified because the kingdom’s security forces had been confronted by young protesters under the influence of mind-altering drugs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bahrain’s Protesters Were Drugged, Official Claims', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-12T22:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported'