al', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scholastic Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Coal Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Campaign for a Commercial-Free Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:32:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcf5a33e-2555-5a7a-a5d5-1ad06dd833ed', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcf5a33e-2555-5a7a-a5d5-1ad06dd833ed'}, {'abstract': 'In “Skateland,” Shiloh Fernández is a teenager whose world of El Caminos, vinyl records and Jordache jeans breaks apart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/movies/skateland-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Skateland,” Shiloh Fernández is a teenager whose world of El Caminos, vinyl records and Jordache jeans breaks apart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blondie and Foreigner rule the soundtrack, and the roller-skating rink is front and center in “Skateland,” Anthony Burns’s sweetly nostalgic re-creation of small-town Texas in the early 1980s.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13skate-span/13skate-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13skate-span/13skate-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13skate-span/13skate-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},