 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan Army Chief Balks at U.S. Demands to Cooperate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Pakistan, General Balks At U.S. Push', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kayani, Ashfaq Parvez', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:36:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7d5239a-922a-5e7b-be85-980ef7b708dd', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7d5239a-922a-5e7b-be85-980ef7b708dd'}, {'abstract': 'A Victorian orphan goes in search of her missing sister in Mary Hooper’s historical novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/young-adult-books-fallen-grace-by-mary-hooper.html', 'snippet': 'A Victorian orphan goes in search of her missing sister in Mary Hooper’s historical novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once upon a time, Henry Treece, Roger Lancelyn Green, Rosemary Sutcliff and Elizabeth George Speare — all authors of historical novels for children — were household names. The genre was so vibrant that writers like Joan Aiken and Susan Cooper could veer into “alternate history” sagas, confident their young readers would have sufficient knowledge to appreciate the subtle interplay of historical “fact” and historical \xad“fiction.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Victorian Novel for the 21st Century', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hard-Knock Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hooper, Mary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Orphans and Orphanages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Foreman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foreman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c687114-d7d6-5dfd-8409-d4ff4e1dfd52', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c687114-d7d6-5dfd-8409-d4ff4e1dfd52'}, {'abstract': 'Gary D. Schmidt tells a tale of an eighth grader’s healing and discovery through art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/childrens-books-okay-for-now-by-gary-d-schmidt.html', 'snippet': 'Gary D. Schmidt tells a tale of an eighth grader’s healing and discovery through art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We slip conventionally enough into “Okay for Now” when a city kid behind a whole rack of metaphorical eight balls heads to a new school in a Catskill backwater. He’s Douglas Swie\xadteck, an eighth grader last seen in Gary D. Schmidt’s much praised “Wednesday Wars,” to which this book plays sequel, though it very much stands on its own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Outsider’s Comeback', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Comeback Kid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Gary D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Peck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6591ead3-669c-5ada-a5a1-12abff59c421', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6591ead3-669c-5ada-a5a1-12abff59c421'}, {'abstract': 'In these two picture books, an appreciation of folk music is passed from generation to generation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/childrens-books-about-folk-music.html', 'snippet': 'In these two picture books, an appreciation of folk music is passed from generation to generation.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The folk process,” as Pete Seeger has called it, easily lends itself to a populist pastoral myth, with supposedly pure rural tunes handed anonymously from generation to generation. “Passing the Music Down,” written by Sarah Sullivan and illustrated by Barry Root, and “When Bob Met Woody,” written by Gary Golio and illustrated by Marc Burckhardt, break through the anonymity and illuminate the process, even as they remain at least partly beholden to fabled styling.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wilentz1/Wilentz1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wilentz1/Wilentz1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wilentz1/Wilentz1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wilentz1/Wilentz1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wilentz1/Wilentz1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Folk Music, Passed Generation to Generation', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oldies for Newbies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guthrie, Woody', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean Wilentz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilentz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f97651d-a8b6-5833-a076-126cc199e199', 'word_count': 58