: 'A science project in the “Babymouse” graphic novel series takes on a life of its own in “Squish: Super Amoeba.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/childrens-books-babymouse-graphic-novels-by-jennifer-l-holm-and-matthew-holm.html', 'snippet': 'A science project in the “Babymouse” graphic novel series takes on a life of its own in “Squish: Super Amoeba.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Remember the episode of “The Cosby Show” in which the Huxtables visit their daughter Denise at Hillman College? And the next season, “A Different World” was born? In television, that’s known as a “backdoor pilot”: an episode of an established hit that’s meant to introduce a lucrative spinoff. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Kois/Kois-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Kois/Kois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Kois/Kois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Science Fair Comics', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Alive!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Kois', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kois', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/25eb2d8b-21e4-5464-b33f-4c3a578aee1e', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25eb2d8b-21e4-5464-b33f-4c3a578aee1e'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of biographies connect a pre-eminent primatologist’s lifelong work to her childhood fascinations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/childrens-books-about-jane-goodall.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of biographies connect a pre-eminent primatologist’s lifelong work to her childhood fascinations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you consider Jane Goodall’s happy British childhood, largely spent outdoors and filled with horses, dogs, chickens and pet turtles — earthworms tucked beneath her pillow at night — and her adult life as the world’s pre-eminent authority on chimpanzees and an influential conservationist, you can’t help wondering: why haven’t there been more biographies of Goodall for young readers?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/jenkins2/jenkins2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/jenkins2/jenkins2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/jenkins2/jenkins2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/jenkins2/jenkins2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/jenkins2/jenkins2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jane Goodall’s Childhood Fascinations', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curious Jane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodall, Jane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonnell, Patrick (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winter, Jeanette', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Jenkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jenkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd0799ca-d741-5a7f-8710-5d2188fbeaf0', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd0799ca-d741-5a7f-8710-5d2188fbeaf0'}, {'abstract': 'Beauty pageant contestants crash on an island, and defy expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/young-adult-books-beauty-queens-by-libba-bray.html', 'snippet': 'Beauty pageant contestants crash on an island, and defy expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If bookstore browsers unfamiliar with the best-selling Y.A. author Libba Bray assume that her fifth novel, “Beauty Queens,” is an updated, female version of “Lord of the Flies,” they can be forgiven. Considering the premise — a plane full of pageant contestants crashes on a small tropical island — and the cover image of a bikini-clad girl’s midriff draped with a bandoleer of lipstick cases instead of cartridges, it could seem like another excuse to pit girls against one another, a log on the mean-girl fire. Gorgeous teenage girls used to tanning, teeth whitening and tearing apart the competition fend for themselves while awaiting rescue! Who will go down first?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shipwrecked Beauty Queens', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Imperfect 10s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bray, Libba', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty Contests', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Joiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c72b8bb-0815-5acf-90fe-94ce730c165a', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c72b8bb-0815-5acf-90fe-94ce730c165a'}, {'abstract': 'Two young adult novels explore abusive dating relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/young-adult-novels-about-abusive-relationships.html', 'snippet': 'Two young adult novels explore abusive dating relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The purpose of young adult literature is often twofold: to tell a story, and to send a message, usually in the form of a much-needed lesson.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Novels About Abusive Relationships', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Possessed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77ccdcc1-5bab-5ae8-9918-b61dc6a5c42c', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77ccdcc1-5bab-5ae8-9918-b61dc6a5c42c'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a London schoolgirl inherits a time-travel gene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/young-adult-books-ruby-red-by-kerstin-gier.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a London schoolgirl inherits a time-travel gene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Already a phenomenon in Germany, where it was published in 2009, “Ruby Red,” the first installment in a time travel trilogy by Kerstin Gier, is now making its American debut. “Ruby Red” has the kind of fans who upload videos of themselves performing scenes from the book on YouTube (as part of a casting contest for a film in preproduction). One amateur translation attempt posted online, while less expert than that of the book’s award-winning translator, Anthea Bell, was somehow more stirring: I pictured a teenage girl sitting at a desk in her bedroom, holding her copy of “Rubinrot” open in one hand, mouthing a few words, then turning to her keyboard to share it with the world. What is it about this book that inspires such enthusiasm?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Time Travel Gene', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Anthea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Burton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e94e46d-549e-5e5c-87c4-f50847487e52', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e94e46d-549e-5e5c-87c4-f50847487e52'}, {'abstract': 'In Veronica Roth’s first novel, an urban dystopia is divided into five factions, each guided by a particular virtue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/young-adult-books-divergent-by-veronica-roth.html', 'snippet': 'In Veronica Roth’s first novel, an urban dystopia is divided into five factions, each guided by a particular virtue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine the publishing world as it might look in a dystopian universe in the distant future. In this world, college English majors — call them “Englies” — aspire to write only one kind of book: the dystopian young adult novel set in the distant future. (Englies of a certain status are permitted to write about dystopias populated by vampires.) Another subset of the population — “the Fans” — provides a kind of slave labor, posting endlessly to dedicated blogs and recording podcasts, providing free marketing for an unceasing succession of aspiring best-seller trilogies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In This Dystopia, Teens Must Choose Wisely', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Choose Wisely', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dominus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8663395-85b4-5aee-a550-98084690639e', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8663395-85b4-5aee-a550-98084690639e'}, {'abstract': 'Why Britain and France refused to choose sides at the outbreak of the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/12/a-dangerous-neutrality/', 'snippet': 'Why Britain and France refused to choose sides at the outbreak of the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Francis Adams, distinguished scion of Boston and son of John Quincy Adams, had heard the stories since childhood about his father’s and grandfather’s long, frightening trip to France in 1778. The surging, implacable Atlantic had tried to drown the emissaries even before their mission began. Now, in May 1861, it took little more than a week to cross the pond, and it was Charles’s turn to duel England as his country’s representative in London.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Dangerous Neutrality', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, CHARLES FRANCIS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Cobbs Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'Cobbs', 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/619750ed-7a0d-5d05-8a4d-7fd6e60dbfba', 'word_count': 1713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/619750ed-7a0d-5d05-8a4d-7fd6e60dbfba'}, {'abstract': 'In the aftermath of Osama bin Laden’s death, flawed or little-known candidates are on the list of possible replacements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/world/asia/13qaeda.html', 'snippet': 'In the aftermath of Osama bin Laden’s death, flawed or little-known candidates are on the list of possible replacements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — When Al Qaeda’s online propaganda arm sought to rally supporters this week after the killing of Osama bin Laden, it did not hint that the network had a major terrorist plot in the works. Instead, it proposed do-it-yourself terrorism, urging militants around the world to come up with their own attacks, however modest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/world/13qaeda1/13qaeda1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/world/13qaeda1/13qaeda1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/world/13qaeda1/13qaeda1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Signs of Leadership Void as Al Qaeda Pushes On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Signs of Leadership Void As Al Qaeda Pushes On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Al Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18af174d-a0a6-5f63-9526-59270ebff964', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18af174d-a0a6-5f63-9526-59270ebff964'}, {'abstract': 'Ending proprietary trading in order to stop banks from behaving like hedge funds is proving devilish, but there may be a partial solution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/business/13views.html', 'snippet': 'Ending proprietary trading in order to stop banks from behaving like hedge funds is proving devilish, but there may be a partial solution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The central aim of the financial overhaul proposed by the former Federal Reserve chief Paul Volcker was quite simple: Let’s stop banks from behaving like hedge funds.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treating Traders Like Hedge Funds', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treating Traders Like Hedge Funds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:51:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROB COX and ANTONY CURRIE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'COX', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CURRIE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0060859d-bfb6-599d-9ed9-200b42a85c8d', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0060859d-bfb6-599d-9ed9-200b42a85c8d'}, {'abstract': 'Mary McDonagh Murphy’s documentary tells how “To Kill a Mockingbird” was written and how it influenced America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/movies/hey-boo-harper-lee-and-to-kill-a-mockingbird.html', 'snippet': 'Mary McDonagh Murphy’s documentary tells how “To Kill a Mockingbird” was written and how it influenced America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are affecting moments amid the excessive hagiography of “Hey, Boo: Harper Lee and ‘To Kill a Mockingbird,’\xa0” Mary McDonagh Murphy’s documentary about one of the most influential novels of the 20th century.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13heyboo-span/ROUNDUP-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13heyboo-span/ROUNDUP-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13heyboo-span/ROUNDUP-4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 856, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13heyboo-span/ROUNDUP-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/13heyboo-span/ROUNDUP-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside an Influential Novel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Movie Review | 'Hey, Boo: Harper Lee and 'To Kill a Mockingbird''", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Mary McDonagh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:54:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e62c10a-df4a-5408-87e3-7c30de5e76a8', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e62c10a-df4a-5408-87e3-7c30de5e76a8'}, {'abstract': 'The publisher of Penthouse magazine and the United States Treasury find themselves in similar stock-sale situations this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/business/13norris.html', 'snippet': 'The publisher of Penthouse magazine and the United States Treasury find themselves in similar stock-sale situations this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In real estate, there is such a thing as a motivated seller who wants out even if he must accept a very low price. That can produce bargains for buyers. Or perhaps the determination to sell means the seller knows something the buyer does not and wants out before others catch on.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75/norris-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75/norris-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mighty Determined Sellers', 'kicker': 'High & Low Finance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mighty Determined Sellers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Penthouse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FriendFinder', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN INTERNATIONAL GROUP INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T01:55:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73368369-200f-586e-8ddc-577112227080', 'word_count': 1241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73368369-200f-586e-8ddc-577112227080'}, {'abstract': 'The administration said it had elected not to seek authority for stringent, top-down regulations that would require companies to erect specific barriers to computer intrusions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/us/politics/13obama.html', 'snippet': 'The administration said it had elected not to seek authority for stringent, top-down regulations that would require companies to erect specific barriers to computer intrusions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Almost two years after outlining a broad strategy intended to strengthen the security of the nation’s computers and networks, the Obama administration said Thursday that it was sending proposed legislation to Congress that would strengthen penalties for any invasion of private computer systems.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/us/OBAMA/OBAMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam