ogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dcbf3d4-a4d9-5cb8-bdc5-dcbc6fe8dba7', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dcbf3d4-a4d9-5cb8-bdc5-dcbc6fe8dba7'}, {'abstract': 'A colony of Bauhaus-style bungalows on Cape Cod evokes the summer idylls of a lost bohemia.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/the-well-fleet-ten/', 'snippet': 'A colony of Bauhaus-style bungalows on Cape Cod evokes the summer idylls of a lost bohemia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, it was a club,” says Eleanor Stefani, the longtime owner of the Colony of Wellfleet. “It was a private club. You had to have your name in the Social Register — literally!” The 10 Bauhaus-style cottages, which have housed devoted guests for decades, were first known as the Mayo Hill Colony Club. They were built on the bay side of Wellfleet, the little Cape Cod town, in 1949 by Nathaniel Saltonstall, an architect from a prominent Boston family who was a founder of the Institute of Contemporary Art in Boston. Along with his business partner Oliver Morton, he designed it as a midcentury Modern retreat for high-society art collectors, with views of the sparkling bay and grassy fairways of the Chequessett Yacht and Country Club through the pines.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wellfleet Ten', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T13:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Lustig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lustig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization':