ok a big step forward on Thursday, as the country’s savings banks undergo a transformation by consolidating and raising funds.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/la-caixa-moves-to-take-banking-arm-public/', 'snippet': 'The restructuring of La Caixa, Spain’s third-largest lender after Santander and BBVA, took a big step forward on Thursday, as the country’s savings banks undergo a transformation by consolidating and raising funds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The restructuring of La Caixa, Spain’s third-largest lender after Santander and BBVA, took a big step forward on Thursday, as the country’s savings banks undergo a transformation by consolidating and raising money. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'La Caixa Moves to Take Banking Arm Public', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T13:55:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fbbe486-416c-587f-aa45-a846e3ac8f57', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fbbe486-416c-587f-aa45-a846e3ac8f57'}, {'abstract': 'The major indexes fell Friday after some disappointing earnings reports and concerns about Europe’s debt crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/business/14markets.html', 'snippet': 'The major indexes fell Friday after some disappointing earnings reports and concerns about Europe’s debt crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stocks were mostly lower on Friday, erasing a weekly gain, as CA Inc. led technology shares lower after its earnings missed estimates. Bank stocks slid over concerns about Europe’s debt crisis and closer government scrutiny.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tumble for Shares in Technology and Finance', 'kicker': 'Stocks & Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tumble for Shares in Technology and Finance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Price Index', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T13:58:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6211f6f4-ce15-5374-9e63-9482e7f0473a', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6211f6f4-ce15-5374-9e63-9482e7f0473a'}, {'abstract': 'Springing up across New York are markets where people can find an old camera or LP, some gourmet food and maybe even some insight into the meaning of life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/nyregion/the-flea-marketing-of-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'Springing up across New York are markets where people can find an old camera or LP, some gourmet food and maybe even some insight into the meaning of life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THEY came for freshly shucked oysters and straight-off-the-leg prosciutto. They came for clacking vintage typewriters and old LPs repurposed as dog tags and bracelets. And they came, to the Brooklyn Flea in Fort Greene last Saturday, searching for meaning and connection in their rudderless lives.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/nyregion/15FLEA5-Span/15FLEA5-Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/nyregion/15FLEA5-Span/15FLEA5-Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/nyregion/15FLEA5-Span/15FLEA5-Span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/nyregion/15FLEA5-Span/15FLEA5-Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/nyregion/15FLEA5-Span/15FLEA5-Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Flea Marketing of New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Flea Marketing of New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flea Markets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Flea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a18f02e-05ab-519a-923c-4317009c0be9', 'word_count': 1758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a18f02e-05ab-519a-923c-4317009c0be9'}, {'abstract': 'What went up need not go down.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/commodities-and-inflation/', 'snippet': 'What went up need not go down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing I’ve been meaning to post about for a while is a common misunderstanding of what monetary doves — not just me, but also Ben Bernanke, Bill Dudley at the NY Fed, and others — believe about commodity prices and broader inflation. Specifically, I often find people assuming or asserting that for the dovish view to be proved right, commodity prices have to go back down. But that’s not at all right; the dovish view relies only on the assertion that the rapid rate of rise in prices since last summer will not continue indefinitely.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commodities and Inflation', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/78910ec7-65f2-5787-b1ac-a31e83fa94ce', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78910ec7-65f2-5787-b1ac-a31e83fa94ce'}, {'abstract': 'Agencies across New York that have provided a last-ditch legal lifeline to distressed homeowners are imperiled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/nyregion/budget-cuts-imperil-legal-aid-in-foreclosure-cases.html', 'snippet': 'Agencies across New York that have provided a last-ditch legal lifeline to distressed homeowners are imperiled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the large immigrant community of Jamaica, Queens, which is ground zero of New York City’s foreclosure crisis, a small squad of young lawyers fans out to local courts every day to do battle with lenders, negotiate hard-fought changes to onerous loans and provide free legal representation to low-income homeowners about to lose their homes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/FORECLOSE/FORECLOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/FORECLOSE/FORECLOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/FORECLOSE/FORECLOSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/FORECLOSE/FORECLOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/FORECLOSE/FORECLOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Budget Cuts Imperil Aid in Foreclosure Cases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Budget Cuts Imperil Aid In Foreclosure Cases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Real Estate and Urban Policy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:07:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Bilefsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/724c3aee-3c45-5bac-a0c5-44d5056a6f27', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/724c3aee-3c45-5bac-a0c5-44d5056a6f27'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/what-were-reading-182/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:21:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4809e7d-72b0-5b1d-9586-b30378882448', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4809e7d-72b0-5b1d-9586-b30378882448'}, {'abstract': 'Household debt is now the core issue.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/its-not-a-banking-problem/', 'snippet': 'Household debt is now the core issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ll need to write more about this, but I thought I should put this up for now: one theme you still find running through many policy discussions, especially about things like taking action on foreclosures, is the constant warning that you mustn’t be mean to the banks — because things are fragile, you know, and we don’t want another financial crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Not A Banking Problem', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:24:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/97acd7be-2bfc-5653-a6c3-663b3fff7296', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97acd7be-2bfc-5653-a6c3-663b3fff7296'}, {'abstract': 'White House wants tougher penalties for security breaches, building wealth through renting, early H.I.V. treatment and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/friday-reading-tougher-penalties-for-computer-breaches-proposed/', 'snippet': 'White House wants tougher penalties for security breaches, building wealth through renting, early H.I.V. treatment and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Tougher Penalties for Computer Breaches Proposed', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:24:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/77ca46ef-0598-51ac-ab09-9743b943b02e', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77ca46ef-0598-51ac-ab09-9743b943b02e'}, {'abstract': 'How he could have threaded the needle on health care.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/what-mitt-romney-should-have-said/', 'snippet': 'How he could have threaded the needle on health care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Mitt Romney’s big speech yesterday defending his Massachusetts health care bill:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Mitt Romney Should Have Said', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:27:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a85ae5c2-7a1c-5023-a8dd-5099c3c9842c', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a85ae5c2-7a1c-5023-a8dd-5099c3c9842c'}, {'abstract': 'Léa Seydoux was a vision in blue, posing for the paparazzi in Cannes in a Prada dress and Christian Louboutins.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/look-of-the-moment-lea-seydoux/', 'snippet': 'Léa Seydoux was a vision in blue, posing for the paparazzi in Cannes in a Prada dress and Christian Louboutins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Look: Blue Belle – a dainty cap-sleeve, knee-length dress in a Mexican mosaic print.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look of the Moment | Léa Seydoux', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4645b637-0070-5d16-9dd4-a6ebb2d31d7a', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4645b637-0070-5d16-9dd4-a6ebb2d31d7a'}, {'abstract': 'It was up again last month, roughly in line with economists’ expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/inflation-in-perspective/', 'snippet': 'It was up again last month, roughly in line with economists’ expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inflation rose again last month, roughly in line with economists’ expectations, to 3.1 percent over the last year. Core inflation — a measure that removes volatile and food and energy prices — also rose, with airfares, car prices and toy prices up. Core inflation is not a particularly good measure of how prices are affecting people’s lives, because food and energy prices obviously matter, but it does tend to be a better guide to where overall inflation is headed. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inflation, in Perspective', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inflation (Economics)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:41:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7bb5567-5e8f-5f04-9068-7c4164162fd2', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7bb5567-5e8f-5f04-9068-7c4164162fd2'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from the past week in New York City and the region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/13/nyregion/20110513WIP.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from the past week in New York City and the region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from the past week in New York City and the region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/nyregion/20110513WIP-slide-653D/20110513WIP-slide-653D-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/nyregion/20110513WIP-slide-653D/20110513WIP-slide-653D-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/nyregion/20110513WIP-slide-653D/20110513WIP-slide-653D-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Pictures for May 13', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T14:44:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/e20bfcb8-78f0-53e1-8dbe-a4b9e4a8a1fb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/e20bfcb8-78f0-53e1-8dbe-a4b9e4a8a1fb'}, {'abstract': 'Apple and Moses use “Lalo.” Ryder calls Goldie “Glam-Ma.” How about yours?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/a-grandma-by-any-other-name/', 'snippet': 'Apple and Moses use “Lalo.” Ryder calls Goldie “Glam-Ma.” How about yours?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alexandra Zissu is wondering what your children call their grandparents. In an article in yesterday’s Styles section, she notes that Gwyneth Paltrow’s kids, Apple and Moses, call Blythe Danner “Lalo” (even though Ms. Danner tried at first to get them to call her “Woof”). And Kate Hudson’s son, Ryder, calls Goldie Hawn “Glam-Ma,” and all around New York, grandparents with less well-known given names are being called things like Mamo, Buya Buya, Nonna and Mimi. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Grandma by Any Other Name', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grandparents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cad0b802-57a5-587b-a608-b3376d40d096', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cad0b802-57a5-587b-a608-b3376d40d096'}, {'abstract': 'Courthouse Confidential: In a legal twist, two men accused of a terror-related plot will be tried in a state court, not a federal one.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/terror-trials-in-state-court-pluses-and-minuses/', 'snippet': 'Courthouse Confidential: In a legal twist, two men accused of a terror-related plot will be tried in a state court, not a federal one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps the most surprising thing about Thursday’s announcement that two Queens men had been arrested on charges of planning a terrorist attack was that the case was being handled by local prosecutors — the Manhattan district attorney’s office — rather than federal officials.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Terror Trials in State Court: Pluses and Minuses', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:04:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/60c27fd9-089c-535b-a86c-701a6cacf594', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60c27fd9-089c-535b-a86c-701a6cacf594'}, {'abstract': 'Netflix’s streaming app is now available for some Android devices, but the fact that only some Android phones get the latest features and others do not is a continuing problem for Google.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/netflix-is-now-on-android-but-not-all-of-android-and-thats-the-problem/', 'snippet': 'Netflix’s streaming app is now available for some Android devices, but the fact that only some Android phones get the latest features and others do not is a continuing problem for Google.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Movie-rental and streaming company Netflix announced today that it had a streaming app for Android. The iOS app for Apple devices was released in 2010, but now users of Google’s mobile operating system can watch the thousands of movies and TV shows that are available for instant online viewing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Netflix Is Available for Some Android Devices, but Not All', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:10:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a04bb8a-85a7-511d-b0dc-e729072aa324', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a04bb8a-85a7-511d-b0dc-e729072aa324'}, {'abstract': 'Mike Huckabee’s comments suggest that he might be ready to make his second bid for the Republican nomination — or not.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/huckabee-teases-decision-on-presidential-run/', 'snippet': 'Mike Huckabee’s comments suggest that he might be ready to make his second bid for the Republican nomination — or not.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:52 p.m. | Updated  Mike Huckabee, the former governor of Arkansas, will announce on Saturday whether he will run for president, the producer of his show on the Fox News Channel said Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Huckabee to Announce Decision on Presidential Run Saturday', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huckabee, Mike', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:13:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'new