Libya', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Misurata (Libya)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:15:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d46dec9c-0c46-5f86-983a-9229dd5219e7', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d46dec9c-0c46-5f86-983a-9229dd5219e7'}, {'abstract': 'Three of Osama bin Laden’s wives, who survived the American raid on his compound in Abbottabad, Pakistan, and are now in Pakistan’s custody, were reportedly “hostile” when they spoke to United States intelligence officials.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/u-s-interrogated-hostile-bin-laden-wives/', 'snippet': 'Three of Osama bin Laden’s wives, who survived the American raid on his compound in Abbottabad, Pakistan, and are now in Pakistan’s custody, were reportedly “hostile” when they spoke to United States intelligence officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 11:33 a.m. Three of Osama bin Laden’s wives, who survived the American raid on his compound in Abbottabad, Pakistan, and are now in Pakistan’s custody, were “hostile” when they spoke to United States intelligence officials this week, American and Pakistani officials told CNN.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Interrogated ‘Hostile’ Bin Laden Wives', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Abbottabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:17:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/37e2a1d6-1a31-5459-9417-b640b846d826', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37e2a1d6-1a31-5459-9417-b640b846d826'}, {'abstract': 'For now, the Bay Area airport’s 15 charge points, which will be formally inaugurated next week, do not suffer from excess demand.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/oakland-airport-builds-e-v-chargers-and-awaits-the-e-v-s/', 'snippet': 'For now, the Bay Area airport’s 15 charge points, which will be formally inaugurated next week, do not suffer from excess demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If every electric car in the United States suddenly descended on Oakland International Airport, only 15 of them would be able to charge their batteries. But for E.V. drivers in the Bay Area, that is plenty of capacity, for now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oakland Airport Builds E.V. Chargers and Awaits the E.V.’s', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oakland (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Negroni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Negroni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2070dffb-c7c9-5020-b468-221973535f2d', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2070dffb-c7c9-5020-b468-221973535f2d'}, {'abstract': 'Got a cheatin’ heart? Be careful what you put on Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/fashion/weddings/divorce-lawyers-new-friend-social-networks.html', 'snippet': 'Got a cheatin’ heart? Be careful what you put on Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DISCRETION and privacy have become antiquated notions on social networks, and the generous revelation of secrets make some people cringe — though not divorce lawyers searching for evidence of misbehavior by their clients’ spouses. What once could be uncovered only through laborious and often unsavory sleuthing can now sometimes be found with the click of a mouse.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/fashion/fieldnotes/fieldnotes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/fashion/fieldnotes/fieldnotes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/fashion/fieldnotes/fieldnotes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Divorce Lawyers’ New Friend: Social Networks', 'kicker': 'Field Notes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Divorce Lawyers’ New Friend: Social Networks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:19:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nadine Brozan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brozan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ea9aa72-a3b8-5b2f-9c67-7b9ed6f69cf4', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ea9aa72-a3b8-5b2f-9c67-7b9ed6f69cf4'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Hochschild’s stirring account of World War I concentrates on appalling losses in the ranks and the courage of dissenters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-to-end-all-wars-by-adam-hochschild.html', 'snippet': 'Adam Hochschild’s stirring account of World War I concentrates on appalling losses in the ranks and the courage of dissenters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Woodrow Wilson’s fatuous claim about the European war of 1914-18 — sarcastically annexed by Adam Hochschild for the title of this moving and important book — was an object of satire and contempt even as it was being uttered. “A peace to end peace,” commented Sir Alfred Milner, that powerhouse of the British war cabinet, as he surveyed the terms of the Versailles treaty that supposedly brought the combat to a close. Increasingly, modern historians have come to regard that bleak November “armistice” as a mere truce in a long, terrible conflict that almost sent civilization into total eclipse and that did not really terminate until the peaceful and democratic reunification of Germany after November 1989. Even that might be an optimistic reading: the post-1918 frontiers of the former Ottoman Empire (one of the four great thrones that did not outlast the “First” World War) are still a suppurating source of violence and embitterment.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Hitchens-COVER/Hitchens-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Hitchens-COVER/Hitchens-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Hitchens-COVER/Hitchens-COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Hitchens-COVER/Hitchens-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Hitchens-COVER/Hitchens-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pacifists and the Trenches', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mortal Debate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochschild, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:25:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Hitchens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hitchens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/653d6043-6f32-5056-8abb-2e967389c659', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/653d6043-6f32-5056-8abb-2e967389c659'}, {'abstract': 'Christopher Hitchens sees similarities between those who protested World War I and his generation’s antiwar movement in the ’60s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/up-front-christopher-hitchens.html', 'snippet': 'Christopher Hitchens sees similarities between those who protested World War I and his generation’s antiwar movement in the ’60s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this week’s cover, Christopher Hitchens reviews “To End All Wars,” Adam Hochschild’s book about World War I.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 758, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Christopher Hitchens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitchens, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb530305-a3ff-5e44-9c64-c8a8a0ed12b3', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb530305-a3ff-5e44-9c64-c8a8a0ed12b3'}, {'abstract': 'New Yorkers tend to move as their needs and their lives change. Unless, that is, they are of the breed that prefers staying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/realestate/the-joys-of-staying-put.html', 'snippet': 'New Yorkers tend to move as their needs and their lives change. Unless, that is, they are of the breed that prefers staying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1973, a few years out of college, Esther Cohen and a friend named Emily rented a little two-bedroom apartment on West 77th Street overlooking the American Museum of Natural History. Ms. Cohen, who had grown up near New Haven, fell in love with the crosshatch shadows made by the fire escape and the view of the museum’s turrets. She painted a rainbow atop the archway that led to the Creamsicle-orange hallway.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/cover/cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/cover/cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/cover/cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 544, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/cover/cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/cover/cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joys of Staying Put', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Joys Of Staying Put', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:28:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Constance Rosenblum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Constance', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenblum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83f5c127-1eae-5966-8e1f-e730111db5d1', 'word_count': 1822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83f5c127-1eae-5966-8e1f-e730111db5d1'}, {'abstract': 'Egyptian corruption investigators have questioned the wife of the ousted president Hosni Mubarak over allegations that she profited illegally from her position.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/middleeast/14egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Egyptian corruption investigators have questioned the wife of the ousted president Hosni Mubarak over allegations that she profited illegally from her position.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Egyptian corruption investigators detained Suzanne Mubarak, the wife of the ousted president, Hosni Mubarak, for questioning over allegations that she profited illegally from her position, government officials said on Friday. Hours later, state news agencies said Mrs. Mubarak had been hospitalized for a heart attack, an echo of reports several weeks ago that Mr. Mubarak, 82, had suffered a heart attack under questioning about official corruption.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-First Lady of Egypt Is Detained in Inquiry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex-First Lady Of Egypt Is Detained In Inquiry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Suzanne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:29:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d5ecdb7-506e-5064-b0ce-bd05d36fcd0e', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d5ecdb7-506e-5064-b0ce-bd05d36fcd0e'}, {'abstract': 'China and America are mutually dependent economic giants, Henry Kissinger argues, but they need a design of partnership.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-on-china-by-henry-kissinger.html', 'snippet': 'China and America are mutually dependent economic giants, Henry Kissinger argues, but they need a design of partnership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Henry Kissinger was not only the first official American emissary to Communist China, he persisted in his brokerage with more than 50 trips over four decades, spanning the careers of seven leaders on each side. Diplomatically speaking, he owns the franchise; and with “On China,” as he approaches 88, he reflects on his remarkable run.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Frankel/Frankel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Frankel/Frankel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Frankel/Frankel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Frankel/Frankel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Frankel/Frankel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Henry Kissinger on China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Toward a Pacific Community', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kissinger, Henry A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T15:34:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Frankel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frankel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'ny