'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/77d8c9ee-63a1-5572-bc79-4d766a976d4e', 'word_count': 1075, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77d8c9ee-63a1-5572-bc79-4d766a976d4e'}, {'abstract': 'Snedens Landing, a neighborhood in the Palisades, has charming and historic homes plus outgoing, sometimes eccentric residents, artists and celebrities among them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/realestate/history-and-scenery-with-a-whiff-of-celebrity-living-inpalisades-ny.html', 'snippet': 'Snedens Landing, a neighborhood in the Palisades, has charming and historic homes plus outgoing, sometimes eccentric residents, artists and celebrities among them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT first, Loren Plotkin, an entertainment lawyer living in Greenwich Village, was of two minds about whether to pack up his family and leave the conveniences of city life for a remote, wooded area in Rockland County on the Hudson River. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/liv/liv-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/liv/liv-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/liv/liv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/liv/liv-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/realestate/liv/liv-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'History and Scenery, With a Whiff of Celebrity', 'kicker': 'Living In | Palisades, N.Y.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'History and Scenery, With a Whiff of Celebrity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:01:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Brenner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brenner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/867f96ae-80a3-59ed-8601-70cf1b9b55b2', 'word_count': 1526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/867f96ae-80a3-59ed-8601-70cf1b9b55b2'}, {'abstract': 'In experiments with a harbor seal named Henry, German researchers found that he could distinguish the size and shape of objects moving underwater, using only his whiskers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/17/science/17obseals.html', 'snippet': 'In experiments with a harbor seal named Henry, German researchers found that he could distinguish the size and shape of objects moving underwater, using only his whiskers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is well known that harbor seals use their whiskers to detect water velocities and the wakes of moving objects, and that these abilities help in capturing prey. But now German researchers have shown that a seal can also discriminate size and shape using only its whiskers.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hard-Working Whiskers Are a Yardstick for Seals', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hard-Working Whiskers Are a Yardstick for Seals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seals (Animals) and Sealing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c74a46d7-4f7d-5b4c-9999-543b35edf363', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c74a46d7-4f7d-5b4c-9999-543b35edf363'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of an 18th-century widow who, in her 70s, invented a new way to depict flowers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-the-paper-garden-by-molly-peacock.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of an 18th-century widow who, in her 70s, invented a new way to depict flowers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 18th century, the twice-\xadwidowed Mary Delany created nearly a thousand breathtakingly beautiful and intricate paper “mosaicks” of flowers. Glued onto black backgrounds, they were not only stunning but also botanically precise. Mrs. D., as her latest biographer, the poet Molly Peacock, sometimes calls her, began this project in her early 70s and only stopped roughly 10 years later, in 1783, when her eyesight declined. Today the collages reside in the British Museum.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wulff/Wulff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wulff/Wulff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Wulff/Wulff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artist of the Botanical World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Late Roses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delany, Mary', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peacock, Molly', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:03:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Wulf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wulf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0d1449b-add1-5262-a6ad-599dc5acf8f6', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0d1449b-add1-5262-a6ad-599dc5acf8f6'}, {'abstract': 'In Geraldine Brooks’s historical novel, a missionary’s daughter forms a bond with a scholarly Indian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-calebs-crossing-by-geraldine-brooks.html', 'snippet': 'In Geraldine Brooks’s historical novel, a missionary’s daughter forms a bond with a scholarly Indian.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Geraldine Brooks’s new novel, “Caleb’s Crossing,” her fourth in a decade, is a short and seemingly modest historical work — no kings, no famous events — told by an equally modest narrator who does not go on to become acquainted with, say, the infant Benjamin Franklin. Bethia Mayfield’s given name means “servant of Jehovah,” and Bethia means to abide by the rules of her family and her Puritan religious affiliation. But even as she begins writing her confession on precious scraps of scavenged paper, she is transgressing the boundaries her father and older brother consider appropriate for a woman — and they have God’s word on this.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Smiley/Smiley-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 353, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Smiley/Smiley-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 353}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Smiley/Smiley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Smiley/Smiley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Smiley/Smiley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Geraldine Brooks’s Pilgrims and Indians', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kindred Spirits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Geraldine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:10:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Smiley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smiley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cff1601c-b0b0-5d98-a273-687d27fb3f29', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cff1601c-b0b0-5d98-a273-687d27fb3f29'}, {'abstract': 'Green garlic cloves are enrobed in several layers of soft, moist, pinkish skin — and their flavor is less pungent than that of regular garlic, writes Martha Rose Shulman.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/cooking-with-green-garlic/', 'snippet': 'Green garlic cloves are enrobed in several layers of soft, moist, pinkish skin — and their flavor is less pungent than that of regular garlic, writes Martha Rose Shulman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Garlic lovers have much to celebrate in the early days of summer, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking With Green Garlic', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:14:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad433d25-e5fd-5dff-a4c2-9b2935b83db7', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad433d25-e5fd-5dff-a4c2-9b2935b83db7'}, {'abstract': '… though perhaps not for the same reasons people do. Photos from our readers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/cats-also-like-to-watch-the-hawk-cam/', 'snippet': '… though perhaps not for the same reasons people do. Photos from our readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Update, May 17 | We added more pix! See below. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cats Also Like to Watch the Hawk Cam', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:17:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a05e58e8-6a27-5b43-a0f0-b4cfb9a4a2ab', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a05e58e8-6a27-5b43-a0f0-b4cfb9a4a2ab'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events around New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/weekend-fare-43/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events around New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Manhattan Cocktail Classic kicks off Friday with a tasting party from 9 p.m. to 1 a.m. at the New York Public Library that includes art and live music. Other events throughout the weekend include seminars on the history of distilling in New York, cocktail competitions and an 1980s-themed party with rum-based drinks. Many events are sold out, but check for available tickets online. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:18:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b677cb2d-52b5-5411-907b-3e8eda880a8f', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b677cb2d-52b5-5411-907b-3e8eda880a8f'}, {'abstract': 'Undiagnosed, the disorder can wreak havoc on relationships and one’s self-esteem. But getting a proper diagnosis can be costly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/health/14consumer.html', 'snippet': 'Undiagnosed, the disorder can wreak havoc on relationships and one’s self-esteem. But getting a proper diagnosis can be costly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Liz Goldberg, 53, was growing up, she always felt “a little off.” She received good grades and even completed a master’s degree in health administration, but it was always a struggle.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/business/CONSUMER/CONSUMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/business/CONSUMER/CONSUMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/business/CONSUMER/CONSUMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Speed Bumps on the Way to an A.D.H.D. Diagnosis', 'kicker': 'The Health Consumer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Speed Bumps on the Way To an A.D.H.D. Diagnosis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:18:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lesley Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lesley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b5f86c4-d522-5b05-ae87-8a887a1370fe', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b5f86c4-d522-5b05-ae87-8a887a1370fe'}, {'abstract': 'Warner Brothers and CBS are expected to announce Friday afternoon that Ashton Kutcher has been selected to replace Charlie Sheen on the hit comedy, “Two and a Half Men.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/kutcher-hints-of-a-role-on-two-and-a-half-men/', 'snippet': 'Warner Brothers and CBS are expected to announce Friday afternoon that Ashton Kutcher has been selected to replace Charlie Sheen on the hit comedy, “Two and a Half Men.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ashton Kutcher, one of the former stars of the sitcom “That 70’s Show,” has been selected to replace Charlie Sheen on the hit comedy “Two and a Half Men,” Warner Brothers and CBS announced on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kutcher to Replace Sheen on ‘Two and a Half Men’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kutcher, Ashton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Charlie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Warner Brothers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T16:19:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f34b1eb6-f685-5bf6-a46b-e116913dcc72', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f34b1eb6-f685-5bf6-a46b-e116913dcc72'}, {'abstract': 'The narrator of this novel journeys into Guyana’s interior to seek answers about the country’s past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-the-sly-company-of-people-who-care-by-rahul-bhattacharya.html', 'snippet': 'The narrator of this novel journeys into Guyana’s interior to seek answers about the country’s past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the opening paragraph of Rahul Bhattacharya’s first novel, “The Sly Company of People Who Care,” the unnamed narrator, a former cricket journalist from India, declares his intentions for his life, and thus his story — to be a wanderer, or in his words, “a slow ramblin’ stranger.” That rambling, through the forests of Guyana; the ruined streets of its capital, Georgetown; and out to the borders of Brazil and Venezuela, constitutes the novel’s central action. But its heart lies in the exuberant and often arresting observations of a man plunging himself into a world full of beauty, violence and cultural strife.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Mengestu/Mengestu-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 