 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc02a1ba-d59a-549f-83e0-47bdc15b14a1', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc02a1ba-d59a-549f-83e0-47bdc15b14a1'}, {'abstract': 'The sometimes controversial comedian Gilbert Gottfried looks back on the performances that brought him attention.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/book-review-rubber-balls-and-liquor-by-gilbert-gottfried.html', 'snippet': 'The sometimes controversial comedian Gilbert Gottfried looks back on the performances that brought him attention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In comedy, the saying goes, timing is everything. The publication of Gilbert Gottfried’s first book, which is kind of a memoir but not really, comes little more than a month after Gottfried made news by tweeting jokes about the Japanese earthquake and tsunami so tasteless that they cost him the longest-lasting and perhaps most lucrative job of his career: providing the duck’s voice in those omni\xadpresent Aflac commercials. He quickly apologized, but the damage was done.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Keepnews/Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/books/review/Keepnews/Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gilbert Gottfried’s Laughs and Gaffes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laughs and Gaffes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gottfried, Gilbert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T17:18:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a301730b-c774-577e-baf5-5c33629522f8', 'word_count': 701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a301730b-c774-577e-baf5-5c33629522f8'}, {'abstract': 'George J. Mitchell Jr. is resigning the same day President Obama is to meet with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/middleeast/14mitchell.html', 'snippet': 'George J. Mitchell Jr. is resigning the same day President Obama is to meet with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — George J. Mitchell Jr., the former senator who brokered peace in Northern Ireland and investigated the use of steroids in Major League Baseball, is resigning as the chief United States envoy to the Israelis and Palestinians amid growing frustration over the impasse in peace talks, the White House announced Friday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/world/14mitchell/14mitchell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/14/world/14mitchell/14mitchell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/w