type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c0714c5-1ac3-5244-90d2-a054b82f841a', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c0714c5-1ac3-5244-90d2-a054b82f841a'}, {'abstract': 'For religious groups in this country, there is tight link between education and income. Hindus and Jews are the most educated and most affluent. Pentecostals and Jehovah’s Witnesses are the least educated and least affluent.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/faith-education-and-income/', 'snippet': 'For religious groups in this country, there is tight link between education and income. Hindus and Jews are the most educated and most affluent. Pentecostals and Jehovah’s Witnesses are the least educated and least affluent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this weekend’s Times Magazine, I have a column explaining the tight link between education and income for religious groups in this country. The most educated groups, like Hindus and Jews, are the most affluent, while the least educated are the least affluent. The chart with the column has more details.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Faith, Education and Income', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:04:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/148a7450-25fc-5631-a239-c00a3f986ded', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/148a7450-25fc-5631-a239-c00a3f986ded'}, {'abstract': 'Herb Kohl’s Senate seat was fairly safe, but his decision not to seek re-election in 2012 throws it open, and may nationalize the contest.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/wisconsin-retirement-adds-to-democrats-burden-in-senate-races/', 'snippet': 'Herb Kohl’s Senate seat was fairly safe, but his decision not to seek re-election in 2012 throws it open, and may nationalize the contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, Herb Kohl of Wisconsin will become the sixth Democratic senator to retire rather than seek another term in 2012. Of those six retirement announcements, this is surely among the more problematic for Democrats.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wisconsin Retirement Adds to Democrats’ Burden in Senate Races', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:04:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd6988e-f16b-5cb0-8524-a99118188ef4', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd6988e-f16b-5cb0-8524-a99118188ef4'}, {'abstract': 'Charlaine Harris is back atop the hardcover fiction list with “Dead Reckoning.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/22/books/review/bestsellers-weekly-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'Charlaine Harris is back atop the hardcover fiction list with “Dead Reckoning.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charlaine Harris is back atop the hardcover fiction list with “Dead Reckoning.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers Weekly Graphic: Dead First', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/8f403e57-e263-542d-b6b9-33b13cdac4fc', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/8f403e57-e263-542d-b6b9-33b13cdac4fc'}, {'abstract': 'With a two-year window that makes it cheaper to transfer wealth to offspring, the richest Americans are struggling with how much money to give.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth.html', 'snippet': 'With a two-year window that makes it cheaper to transfer wealth to offspring, the richest Americans are struggling with how much money to give.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRESIDENT OBAMA and the Republican Congress gave the wealthiest Americans an enormous gift at the end of 2010. They increased the exemption on a series of estate-related taxes and lowered the tax rate on any amount over those limits. The catch is it lasts only two years.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth-span/14wealth-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth-span/14wealth-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth-span/14wealth-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth-span/14wealth-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/your-money/taxes/14wealth-span/14wealth-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wealthy Hesitate to Take a Break on Estate Taxes', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wealthy Hesitate to Take a Break on Estate Taxes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wills and Estates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inheritance and Estate Taxes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Taxes', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37742f91-36cd-59a2-a0db-b5d3f396554b', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37742f91-36cd-59a2-a0db-b5d3f396554b'}, {'abstract': 'Graham Motion walked out of the barn to the parking lot, extended his right hand and asked a question. “What I want to know is how do you get any sleep during the playoffs?” The man at the other end of the handshake -– Philadelphia Eagles head coach Andy Reid -– laughed and knew exactly what Motion meant.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/andy-reid-talks-horses-with-graham-motion/', 'snippet': 'Graham Motion walked out of the barn to the parking lot, extended his right hand and asked a question. “What I want to know is how do you get any sleep during the playoffs?” The man at the other end of the handshake -– Philadelphia Eagles head coa...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steeplechase Times has been with Animal Kingdom, winner of the Kentucky Derby, at the Fair Hill Training Center in Maryland. Joe Clancy was there when Andy Reid, head coach of the Philadelphia Eagles, and two others — the offensive coordinator Marty Mornhinweg and the scout Brett Veach — talked with Animal Kingdom’s trainer, Graham Motion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Andy Reid Talks Horses With Graham Motion', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc9cce75-bc7d-5991-8194-0534495351a1', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc9cce75-bc7d-5991-8194-0534495351a1'}, {'abstract': 'The new Darkspore game from Electronic Arts is a large leap forward in the action-role-playing Spore series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/arts/video-games/darkspore-from-electronic-arts-review.html', 'snippet': 'The new Darkspore game from Electronic Arts is a large leap forward in the action-role-playing Spore series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spore, released in 2008, was the most over-hyped game in recent memory. Through years of prerelease publicitymongering, Spore’s publisher, Electronic Arts, and its famed designer, Will Wright, managed to convince much of the general public and the gaming world that they had created an “everything simulator.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/darkspore/darkspore-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/darkspore/darkspore-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/darkspore/darkspore-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/darkspore/darkspore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/arts/darkspore/darkspore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Galaxy, Less Hype and Finally Real Action', 'kicker': 'Video Game Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Galaxy, Less Hype And Finally Real Action', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Electronic Arts Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:31:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Video Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aff45539-fa39-5486-b131-4236176938bd', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aff45539-fa39-5486-b131-4236176938bd'}, {'abstract': 'CNBC looks at how investors are reacting to Citigroup after it resumed paying a dividend. The bank, which required a huge bailout during the financial crisis, cut the payout in 2009.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/citigroups-symbolic-dividend/', 'snippet': 'CNBC looks at how investors are reacting to Citigroup after it resumed paying a dividend. The bank, which required a huge bailout during the financial crisis, cut the payout in 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CNBC looks at how investors are reacting to Citigroup after it resumed paying a dividend. The bank, which required a huge bailout during the financial crisis, cut the payout in 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citigroup’s Symbolic Dividend', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:32:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f2a9126-9baa-5177-8bd0-5b6726925efc', 'word_count': 31, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f2a9126-9baa-5177-8bd0-5b6726925efc'}, {'abstract': 'Leading the rest of the pack, CBS finishes in second place.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/fox-rides-idol-to-another-ratings-victory/', 'snippet': 'Leading the rest of the pack, CBS finishes in second place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fox finished atop the Thursday-night ratings as 21.6 million viewers tuned in for the “American Idol” elimination episode at 8 p.m., according to Nielsen’s estimates. The “Idol” finalist James Durbin failed to advance to next week’s round, earning the lowest vote tally of nearly 72 million cast, leaving Lauren Alaina, Scotty McCreery and Haley Reinhart in the running. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fox Rides ‘Idol’ to Another Ratings Victory', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dca3bcac-9de2-5664-84f1-716dc193195f', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dca3bcac-9de2-5664-84f1-716dc193195f'}, {'abstract': 'A look at some show-stopping moments from “Catch Me if You Can,” “Anything Goes” and “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/the-tonys-stop-the-show/', 'snippet': 'A look at some show-stopping moments from “Catch Me if You Can,” “Anything Goes” and “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Catch Me if You Can” might have gotten mixed reviews, but pretty much everyone agrees that Norbert Leo Butz’s rendition of “Don’t Break the Rules” in the first act is the kind of showstopper you don’t see on Broadway all that often. Except this season, which is notable for numbers that, through the alchemy of song, sound, legs, lights and star power — not to mention tap dancing — build into something more joyful than the sum of their parts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tonys: Stop the Show!', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Anything Goes (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Catch Me If You Can (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:33:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d8c839a-2b3b-5e3e-af48-bf19f315de9d', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d8c839a-2b3b-5e3e-af48-bf19f315de9d'}, {'abstract': 'Arthur Kulkov, the model currently towering over Port Authority in an ad over five stories tall, is known around town for his eccentric and super-stylish way.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/sunny-delight/', 'snippet': 'Arthur Kulkov, the model currently towering over Port Authority in an ad over five stories tall, is known around town for his eccentric and super-stylish way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arthur Kulkov, the Siberian born, Coney Island raised in-demand model currently towering over Port Authority in an ad over five stories tall, is known around town for his eccentric and super-stylish way. His ease with fashion and sense of humor about his style adds to his appeal as a professional model, certainly. But his eye-catching outfits and lack of self-consciousness are what really sets him apart. “I hate to say that this business has influenced my style, but it certainly did. All these years you have no choice but to pick up on things here and there, but obviously with my own spin on things. Ha.” While on the set for a campaign shoot in Sarasota, Fla., he caught stares sporting these Day-Glo orange pieces (athletic sandals and shorts by Adidas, hat by American Apparel). He ordered the flip-flops from a soccer magazine, got the shorts at Y-3 and snagged the hat from another shoot. “I thought it was brightly cool … I wouldn’t rock all together, but I think it looks cool in parts.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunny Delight', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:34:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fc81565-7ffa-5126-982d-df9776c9dc04', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fc81565-7ffa-5126-982d-df9776c9dc04'}, {'abstract': 'The measure, which sought to impose the death penalty for the “offense of homosexuality,” drew widespread criticism and threatened the country’s international standing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/africa/14uganda.html', 'snippet': 'The measure, which sought to impose the death penalty for the “offense of homosexuality,” drew widespread criticism and threatened the country’s international standing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KAMPALA, Uganda — After receiving overwhelming criticism from across the globe, Uganda’s Parliament on Friday let the time expire on a contentious anti-homosexuality bill that had threatened this East African country’s international standing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Antigay Bill in Uganda Is Shelved in Parliament', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Antigay Bill In Uganda Is Shelved In Parliament', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Museveni, Yoweri', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uganda', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:35:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86f374c2-e09f-5376-b873-910912f46961', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86f374c2-e09f-5376-b873-910912f46961'}, {'abstract': 'Index mutual funds are not required to be offered in 401(k) plans, but they should be, because of their many advantages over actively managed funds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/your-money/401ks-and-similar-plans/14money.html', 'snippet': 'Index mutual funds are not required to be offered in 401(k) plans, but they should be, because of their many advantages over actively managed funds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The people who help you with your investments ought to act in your best interest.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/lieber-75/lieber-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/lieber-75/lieber-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why 401(k)’s Should Offer Index Funds', 'kicker': 'Your Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why 401(k)’s Should Offer Index Funds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': '401(k), 403(b) and 457 Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asset Allocation (Personal Finances)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mutual Funds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:36:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': "401(k)'s and Similar Plans", 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc8148eb-e12e-5498-888e-7c8ab827ec3a', 'word_count': 1539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc8148eb-e12e-5498-888e-7c8ab827ec3a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers share their stories of attempts to change their 401(k) or other workplace defined contribution retirement plans.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/lobbying-for-a-better-401k-plan/', 'snippet': 'Readers share their stories of attempts to change their 401(k) or other workplace defined contribution retirement plans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this weekend’s Your Money column, I encouraged readers to storm the barricades (but nicely!) to suggest improvements to their 401(k) or other retirement plans. Namely, I suggest that the majority of plans could use more index mutual funds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lobbying for a Better 401(k) Plan', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': '401(k), 403(b) and 457 Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Your Money', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:39:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/111b114c-f881-5323-a5e2-def87a3c9fb8', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/111b114c-f881-5323-a5e2-def87a3c9fb8'}, {'abstract': 'Getting to backcountry couldn’t be easier when it’s the Trans-Catalina Trail, a 90-minute ferry ride from Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/travel/los-angeles-backcountry-traversing-catalina.html', 'snippet': 'Getting to backcountry couldn’t be easier when it’s the Trans-Catalina Trail, a 90-minute ferry ride from Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE problem with wilderness is that it is, by definition, too darn far from where most people live. Yes, many cities have a few nice day hikes, but it’s a lucky accident of geography and conservation that creates serious, “out there” backcountry hiking opportunities close to a major urban area.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15overnighterspan/15overnighterspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15overnighterspan/15overnighterspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15overnighterspan/15overnighterspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15overnighterspan/15overnighterspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/15overnighterspan/15overnighterspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '22 Miles and a World Away From Los Angeles', 'kicker': 'The U.S. Issue: Overnighter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Backcountry Los Angeles: Traversing Catalina', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Catalina Island (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:47:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Todras-Whitehill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Todras-Whitehill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c5fc02f-ee7a-54cc-88e5-32fef66d73b0', 'word_count': 1626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c5fc02f-ee7a-54cc-88e5-32fef66d73b0'}, {'abstract': 'A 37-mile trail takes hikers on a four-day trek.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/15/travel/5152011CATALINA.html', 'snippet': 'A 37-mile trail takes hikers on a four-day trek.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 37-mile trail takes hikers on a four-day trek.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/travel/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM/5152011CATALINA-slide-YVKM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trans-Catalina Trail', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Catalina Island (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:48:09+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ad9bf554-a1c8-5f61-a18c-ca16838d7a81', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ad9bf554-a1c8-5f61-a18c-ca16838d7a81'}, {'abstract': 'Given the attention paid to every other aspect of the royal marriage, it seems appropriate if not necessary to contemplate these activities. And how better to gain insight into them, as well as into William and Kate’s general compatibility, than with the aid of astrology?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/regarding-the-royal-consummation/', 'snippet': 'Given the attention paid to every other aspect of the royal marriage, it seems appropriate if not necessary to contemplate these activities. And how better to gain insight into them, as well as into William and Kate’s general compatibility, than w...', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now we can assume that Prince William and the new Duchess of Cambridge have consummated their marriage, or anyway we can hope. These things can be problematic. It’s said, for example, to have taken seven years for the Dauphin Louis-Auguste of France, later Louis XVI, and the Hapsburg Archduchess Maria Antonia, known as Marie Antoinette, to consummate their union. (They were just teenagers, 15 and 14, at the time of their wedding in 1770, though on the face of it that would hardly explain the delay.) But at least William and Kate can spend time alone. In the old days, servants and witnesses would have been spending the night in the royal bedchamber with them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Regarding the Royal Consummation', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Astrology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Family', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marie Antoinette', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'William, Duke of Cambridge', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Glassie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glassie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ea4a658-5070-507b-af8e-6c02f8a0d800', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ea4a658-5070-507b-af8e-6c02f8a0d800'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of cultural events this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/may-15-21.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of cultural events this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Film', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/GUIDE-3/GUIDE-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/GUIDE-3/GUIDE-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/GUIDE-3/GUIDE-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/GUIDE-3/GUIDE-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/GUIDE-3/GUIDE-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'May 15 — 21', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'May 15 — 21', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:51:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f261063d-f7c1-5e02-b1f3-309e5d0904e7', 'word_count': 1717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f261063d-f7c1-5e02-b1f3-309e5d0904e7'}, {'abstract': 'Letter to the Editors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/travel/letters-short-hop-airlines.html', 'snippet': 'Letter to the Editors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: Regarding “Small Airlines for Shorter Hops” (Practical Traveler, May 1): As a frequent purchaser of direct air, my only complaint thus far is trying to book a flight when seats are limited and not always available unless one pays full price. Other than that the convenience of going direct far outweighs the limitations. One has to be flexible with an itinerary.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Short-Hop Airlines', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'SHORT-HOP AIRLINES', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:55:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a65694a1-63d5-50c0-9b96-ee4c14f4c0ff', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a65694a1-63d5-50c0-9b96-ee4c14f4c0ff'}, {'abstract': 'The search for a new host of “A Prairie Home Companion” has begun, leading some to wonder if the radio show can succeed without Garrison Keillor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/television/garrison-keillor-and-prairie-home-companion.html', 'snippet': 'The search for a new host of “A Prairie Home Companion” has begun, leading some to wonder if the radio show can succeed without Garrison Keillor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wanted: Host for a two-hour live public radio show Saturday nights, from the Fitzgerald Theater in St. Paul. Qualifications: Brilliant writer; eclectic musical tastes with a fondness for traditional Protestant hymns and patriotic songs; knack for poking fun at Midwesterners without offending them. Ability to charm four million listeners unhappy to see the present occupant go a plus. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/KEILLOR-4/KEILLOR-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/KEILLOR-4/KEILLOR-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/KEILLOR-4/KEILLOR-4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/KEILLOR-4/KEILLOR-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/KEILLOR-4/KEILLOR-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Host Needed: Be Prepared to Fill Big Shoes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Host Needed: Be Prepared To Fill Big Shoes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keillor, Garrison', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:57:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/124f5357-4c71-5a3a-ae39-8260669b4215', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/124f5357-4c71-5a3a-ae39-8260669b4215'}, {'abstract': '“Freedom Riders,” a new “American Experience” documentary by Stanley Nelson, is the latest installment in his exploration of the byways of black history and culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/television/for-stanley-nelson-the-prize-is-documentary-filmmaking.html', 'snippet': '“Freedom Riders,” a new “American Experience” documentary by Stanley Nelson, is the latest installment in his exploration of the byways of black history and culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR some people, filmmaking is a lifelong dream. For Stanley Nelson, it began as more of a situational thing, a response to time and place. The time was the late 1960s and early ’70s, the place to be avoided was Vietnam, the refuge was film school at the City University of New York. “It was kind of my motive to stay in school no matter what, you know what I’m saying?” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/NELSON-1/NELSON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/NELSON-1/NELSON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/NELSON-1/NELSON-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/NELSON-1/NELSON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/NELSON-1/NELSON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Explorer of Black History’s Uncharted Terrain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Explorer Of Black History’s Uncharted Terrain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:57:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2eea29c8-211d-511b-88d3-b5a1e375eb0f', 'word_count': 1686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2eea29c8-211d-511b-88d3-b5a1e375eb0f'}, {'abstract': 'What Alex Turner, frontman of Artic Monkeys, was listening to when he wrote the album “Suck It and See.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/music/alex-turner-on-arctic-monkeys-suck-it-and-see.html', 'snippet': 'What Alex Turner, frontman of Artic Monkeys, was listening to when he wrote the album “Suck It and See.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE 2006, when Arctic Monkeys appeared on the British music scene, Alex Turner, the frontman, has been acclaimed as a songwriter wise beyond his years. (He was 20 when the group hit it big, thanks in part to the tendency of the British press to trumpet and acclaim.) Through lineup changes and side projects, and a recent foray in New York with his girlfriend, Alexa Chung, the MTV personality and Madewell designer, Mr. Turner has gravitated toward classic musicians, like Gene Clark of the Byrds, and films like the 1959 western “Rio Bravo.” His retro sensibility is evident on the band’s fourth album, “Suck It and See” (Domino), to be released June 6. The title seems cheeky but it’s not, Mr. Turner said. “It’s an old Britishism, like a bit Dick van Dyke-y, like ‘give it a try’ almost — it’d be a slogan for some candy,” he said. “That’s not really traveled very well.” In a conversation peppered with other Britishisms (“nice one, love” he said, often, alongside saucier phrases) he recently spoke with Melena Ryzik from Stockholm, where the band members were starting their tour, performing together for the first time in over a year. “I’m a bit nervous, to be honest with you,” Mr. Turner said. “I have just opened a beer, actually. We’ll see if that’s going to help it out. I don’t want to do too much of that. I’m worried I might become forgetful, and there’s a lot of words in our songs.” Here are excerpts from their conversation. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/PLAYLIST-1/PLAYLIST-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 677, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rock Star Tunes in to the Elders of the Tribe', 'kicker': 'Playlist | Arctic Monkeys', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rock Star Tunes In to the Elders of the Tribe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Arctic Monkeys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T18:57:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a78547b-5d26-5d0a-ab6a-1ccc4b2be0e8', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a78547b-5d26-5d0a-ab6a-1ccc4b2be0e8'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Demme’s 1986 movie “Something Wild,” a screwball comedy that turns into a noir, now looks like a template for the coming auteur-as-curator era of independent film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/movies/jonathan-demmes-something-wild-from-criterion-collection.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Demme’s 1986 movie “Something Wild,” a screwball comedy that turns into a noir, now looks like a template for the coming auteur-as-curator era of independent film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRET EASTON ELLIS, the author of “Less Than Zero” and a man who knows a thing or two about the 1980s, recently tweeted, “The two key American films of the 1980s: ‘Blue Velvet,’ ‘Something Wild’...” The first choice is no surprise, but the second confused some of his followers. “When I tweeted it, people responded to ‘Blue Velvet’ but asked, ‘What’s the other one?,’\xa0” Mr. Ellis said in a telephone interview. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/WILD-1/WILD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/WILD-1/WILD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/WILD-1/WILD-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/WILD-1/WILD-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': 