dline': 'Something About a Witch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T19:25:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mendelsohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mendelsohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f711cb25-778d-507b-95dc-e6cd23231afa', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f711cb25-778d-507b-95dc-e6cd23231afa'}, {'abstract': 'General Motors introduced high-end audio to cars almost 30 years ago, and now most cars have premium sound systems, either as standard equipment or an option.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/automobiles/15SOUND.html', 'snippet': 'General Motors introduced high-end audio to cars almost 30 years ago, and now most cars have premium sound systems, either as standard equipment or an option.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1982, General Motors began selling three models that for the first time offered high-end designer sound systems, made by Bose, starting a trend in which branded audio systems are now available in almost every vehicle. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Name-Dropping in Designer Audio', 'kicker': 'Technology', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Name-Dropping in Designer Audio', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bose Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speakers (Audio)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T19:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/135639da-ccea-5564-9ca9-a2b30d5bd260', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/135639da-ccea-5564-9ca9-a2b30d5bd260'}, {'abstract': 'Only math homework matters.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/homework-follies/', 'snippet': 'Only math homework matters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many parents dread homework time, and the struggle they will have to go through to get their kids to do it. So, is all this effort worthwhile? Over the years a disturbing amount of evidence has emerged suggesting that it is not.\xa0 For a forthcoming study in the journal Economics of Education Review, Ozkan Eren and Daniel Henderson measured the effect of homework on math, science, English and history tests for 8th grade students in the United States. They used a method that controlled student and teacher traits.\xa0 They found that doing additional math homework had large and statistically significant effects on math test scores. They found that additional homework in science, English and history had little or no impact.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Homework Follies', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homework', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_da