an and one woman “is hardwired into our human reason.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dolan Reasserts Traditional Definition of Marriage', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolan, Timothy M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T19:52:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Vitello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vitello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6bfd784-3842-51b9-bf1e-6fe6df34af97', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6bfd784-3842-51b9-bf1e-6fe6df34af97'}, {'abstract': 'For those needing some background on the civil unrest sweeping the Arab world, a Harvard historian provides his book list.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/desert-prose/', 'snippet': 'For those needing some background on the civil unrest sweeping the Arab world, a Harvard historian provides his book list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Harvard historian Roger Owen is one of the pre-eminent minds on the modern Middle East, but even he was dumbstruck by the civil unrest sweeping the Arab world. The books that will shed light on it all, he says, have yet to be written. In the meantime, one could turn to the classics: “The literature you need now is almost Shakespearean. We’re dealing with family rule with fatal flaws.” Or to the following volumes, selected by Owen, which give some background on this complicated revolutionary moment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Desert Prose', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T19:59:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/954274c9-0496-5426-b62c-e823c4dd63e7', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/954274c9-0496-5426-b62c-e823c4dd63e7'}, {'abstract': 'A picture in Fortune magazine features the directors of Procter & Gamble, including Rajat K. Gupta — weeks before regulators accused him of leaking insider information to Raj Rajaratnam.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/the-photo-pg-probably-wanted-to-retouch/', 'snippet': 'A picture in Fortune magazine features the directors of Procter & Gamble, including Rajat K. Gupta — weeks before regulators accused him of leaking insider information to Raj Rajaratnam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Fortune 500 issue, which hit newsstands this week, has the business magazine’s annual look of seven of “the most photogenic” boards in the United States. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Photo P.&G. Probably Wanted to Retouch', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gupta, Rajat K', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonald, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortune (Magazine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Procter & Gamble Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bc2c2b5-2dcf-5fc1-8995-722585416ef7', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bc2c2b5-2dcf-5fc1-8995-722585416ef7'}, {'abstract': 'Vincent L. Leibell III will serve a prison term after being convicted of tax evasion and obstruction of justice.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/21-months-in-prison-for-former-state-senator/', 'snippet': 'Vincent L. Leibell III will serve a prison term after being convicted of tax evasion and obstruction of justice.', 'lead_paragraph': ' WHITE PLAINS — A former state senator was sentenced in federal district court here on Friday to 21 months in prison and three years of supervised release after pleading guilty last year to obstruction of justice and tax evasion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '21 Months in Prison for Former State Senator', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eginton, Warren W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibell, Vincent L III', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Putnam County (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'White Plains (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:02:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f3f6e21-1220-5090-bee8-bf8ce955b0d8', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f3f6e21-1220-5090-bee8-bf8ce955b0d8'}, {'abstract': 'Ashley Parker explains how shopping for vintage clothing, old typewriters and artisanal cheese became the hippest way to spend your weekend', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/metropolitan-preview-the-fleas/', 'snippet': 'Ashley Parker explains how shopping for vintage clothing, old typewriters and artisanal cheese became the hippest way to spend your weekend', 'lead_paragraph': ' “In a city that thrums with opportunity and a veritable buffet of wonderful things to do — theaters! museums! parks! — flea markets have somehow emerged as many people’s first choice of a way to spend the weekend,” writes Ashley Parker in this week’s Metropolitan cover article, “The Flea Marketing of New York.” Over the last few years, the markets, with their vintage clothing, handmade jewelry and profusion of handcrafted foods have proliferated, as have the crowds drawn to them. On Brooklyn Flea’s opening weekend, about 25,000 people filed through the Fort Greene and Williamsburg markets, according to the organizers, and SuChin Pak, a founder of the Hester Street Fair, estimated that 11,000 people came to her market’s opening day this year. Read Ms. Parker’s explanation of how shopping for vintage clothing, old typewriters and artisanal cheese became the hippest way to spend your weekend here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Metropolitan Preview | The Fleas', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flea Markets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pak, SuChin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Flea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Greene (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Virshup', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Virshup', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/206d386e-dde4-5d11-bac7-1d80f89e5c8a', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/206d386e-dde4-5d11-bac7-1d80f89e5c8a'}, {'abstract': 'It may be time to get over our unease with the biological mashup around us.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/british-parakeets-and-the-tumbleweed-blues/', 'snippet': 'It may be time to get over our unease with the biological mashup around us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life on Earth is getting stranger by the minute. Parakeets are flocking in Britain, as described in Elisabeth Rosenthal’s news story today. But don’t forget the monk parrots in Brooklyn, the American bullfrogs in Japan and France, the Burmese pythons in the Everglades, the West Nile virus spanning the Americas. (Oh, and the American squirrels that are also displacing their kin in Britain.) You get the idea. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Parakeets and the Tumbleweed Blues', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 's