rk, I Love You, but You’re Bringing Me Down,” is the final track on LCD Soundsystem‘s 2007 album, “Sound of Silver.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is New York Bringing You Down?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marist College', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:43:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfe138e5-9bef-553b-941e-22f97e9fde06', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfe138e5-9bef-553b-941e-22f97e9fde06'}, {'abstract': 'A new stamp, Save Vanishing Species, will raise money for the Multinational Species Conservation Fund.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/how-sending-a-letter-can-help-save-wildlife/', 'snippet': 'A new stamp, Save Vanishing Species, will raise money for the Multinational Species Conservation Fund.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Looking for little ways to help endangered species?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Sending a Letter Can Help Save Wildlife', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postal Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stamps (Postal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Postal Service (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a464beb-3965-52d6-acb0-e9d723abbd52', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a464beb-3965-52d6-acb0-e9d723abbd52'}, {'abstract': 'Mari Kimura has found notes that other violinists thought not possible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/music/the-violinist-mari-kimura-looks-for-low-notes.html', 'snippet': 'Mari Kimura has found notes that other violinists thought not possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE Pythagoras, musicians and scientists have known (or thought they knew) that the lowest pitch a string stretched taut can produce — the fundamental — is the pitch it produces when vibrating freely. “Stop” the string anywhere between the end points, and the vibrating section sounds at a higher pitch. Theoretically, there is no ceiling. As with dancing, the sky’s the limit, but the floor is always the floor.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/MARI-1/MARI-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/MARI-1/MARI-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/arts/MARI-1/MARI-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Violinist, Success Means a New Low Point', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Violinist, Success Means A New Low Point', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spectrum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Gurewitsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gurewitsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7866731e-87dd-52ef-af10-bada40aa141c', 'word_count': 1304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7866731e-87dd-52ef-af10-bada40aa141c'}, {'abstract': 'After about seven years of collaborating with various retailers, the men behind Salvor Projects will open their first shop this Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/now-opening-salvor-projects/', 'snippet': 'After about seven years of collaborating with various retailers, the men behind Salvor Projects will open their first shop this Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After about seven years of collaborating with various retailers, the men behind Salvor Projects will open their first shop this Saturday. “The store is like a lab,” the designer Ross Menuez says, having sought a space where he could continue to experiment with his designs as well as bring together everything Salvor has done in the past. Specializing in screen prints, they hope to cater to a range of customers from the Madison Avenue woman interested in a graphic tote bag to your average skateboarder looking for a printed shirt. At the shop, these mostly one-of-a-kind pieces are displayed against an austere, monotone interior that allows the clothes to be the focal point — an architectural decision that should attract many a design enthusiast.Prices range from $140 – $300. Salvor Projects, 172 Forsyth Street. Go to www.salvorprojects.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Opening | Salvor Projects', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:45:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Tudela', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tudela', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/855f9c52-cf6c-552c-8d16-6869339bb59e', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/855f9c52-cf6c-552c-8d16-6869339bb59e'}, {'abstract': 'Women television writers are having a great pilot season this year. Last year’s was called one of the worst.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/pilot-paternalism/', 'snippet': 'Women television writers are having a great pilot season this year. Last year’s was called one of the worst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dearth of women television writers is well documented. But it reached a sort of negative milestone last year when Neely Swanson, a former development executive and adjunct professor at the U.S.C. School of Cinematic Arts, published her essay “Women Can’t Create and White Men Can’t Jump” at the behest of the Deadline Hollywood blogger Nikki Fink, who called 2010 the “worst network pilot season for women.” Melissa Silverstein, of the blog Women and Hollywood, was one of many to pick up the story. She compared the numbers and showed, for example, that there was an 11 percent decline in comedy pilots by women writers between 2009 and 2010.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pilot Paternalism', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:48:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lia Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/343b491e-2f09-5ebd-bba5-4186c17e0aed', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/343b491e-2f09-5ebd-bba5-4186c17e0aed'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Van Sant discussed his new film “Restless” in an interview at the Carlton Hotel here on Friday', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/gus-van-sant-on-young-love-and-young-death/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Van Sant discussed his new film “Restless” in an interview at the Carlton Hotel here on Friday', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France — For the last decade the director Gus Van Sant, a regular presence at the Cannes Film Festival, has undergone an artistic rebirth with movies about death. In 2003 he won the festival’s top prize, the Palme d’Or, for “Elephant,” his minimalist evocation of a Columbine-like school shooting. He returned to the competition here with “Last Days” (2005), an opaque antibiopic about the final hours of a suicidal rock star modeled on Kurt Cobain, and “Paranoid Park” (2007), a coming-of-age tale whose teenage hero wrestles with his culpability in a man’s gruesome trainyard demise. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gus Van Sant on Young Love and Young Death', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Sant, Gus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cannes (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:55:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Lim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/90b58a7d-f25c-507c-b066-fb3c65277958', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90b58a7d-f25c-507c-b066-fb3c65277958'}, {'abstract': 'The Appalachian Mountain Club is offering youth lodging discounts, kids’ meals and more family-focused activities.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/appalachian-mountain-club-rolls-out-specials-for-children/', 'snippet': 'The Appalachian Mountain Club is offering youth lodging discounts, kids’ meals and more family-focused activities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best known for its backcountry hut-to-hut hiking system in the White Mountains, the Appalachian Mountain Club is seeking to encourage the next generation of trekkers with new youth-oriented program called “Kid Spoken Here.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Appalachian Mountain Club Rolls Out Specials for Children', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APPALACHIAN MOUNTAIN CLUB', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:56:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0701f7c-501e-5348-8dc6-6e12f0c9dfae', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0701f7c-501e-5348-8dc6-6e12f0c9dfae'}, {'abstract': 'The killing of Nasrullah Afridi, a journalist working in Peshawar, is the latest in a series of killings in Pakistan that have singled out reporters.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/pakistan-loses-another-journalist/', 'snippet': 'The killing of Nasrullah Afridi, a journalist working in Peshawar, is the latest in a series of killings in Pakistan that have singled out reporters.', 'lead_paragraph': ' “You better come to your senses.” This was the last threat received by the Pakistani journalist Nasrullah Afridi days before he was killed in a car bomb blast in Peshawar on May 10, his friend and colleague Sher Khan said in a telephone interview.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan Loses Another Journalist', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mangal Bagh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Committee to Protect Journalists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peshawar (Pakistan)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rabia Mehmood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rabia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mehmood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/438e20c7-3f35-5f4b-a020-a4f2cadcf80f', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/438e20c7-3f35-5f4b-a020-a4f2cadcf80f'}, {'abstract': 'It has been nearly a year since Amazon announced a new Kindle product, but Jeff Bezos, the company’s chief executive, dropped a hint that a  “multipurpose tablet device” could be on the way.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/on-the-lookout-for-an-amazon-tablet/', 'snippet': 'It has been nearly a year since Amazon announced a new Kindle product, but Jeff Bezos, the company’s chief executive, dropped a hint that a  “multipurpose tablet device” could be on the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest version of the Kindle went on sale over 10 months ago, so it’s been some time since we’ve heard a major new hardware product announcement from Amazon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Lookout for an Amazon Tablet', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handheld Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezos, Jeffrey P', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Reports', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:58:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5650044-1675-568b-976b-d283dfaaed55', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5650044-1675-568b-976b-d283dfaaed55'}, {'abstract': 'Brooks Stevens designed a broad range of products, like the common clothes iron, but the cars he created are still recognized as fresh and distinctive departures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/automobiles/15BROOKS.html', 'snippet': 'Brooks Stevens designed a broad range of products, like the common clothes iron, but the cars he created are still recognized as fresh and distinctive departures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Brooks Stevens automotive designs of the mid-20th century, including the one-off Paxton Phoenix he created for Robert P. McCulloch, have long been recognized as fresh and distinctive departures from Detroit creations of the era. His singular perspective had many roots, but it grew at least in part from his fertile background as an innovator of a broad range of products. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/BROO/BROO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/BROO/BROO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/BROO/BROO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Pen of a Giant of Industrial Design', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From the Pen of a Giant of Industrial Design', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf78b463-a415-5c2b-b781-a8b8f527ec29', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf78b463-a415-5c2b-b781-a8b8f527ec29'}, {'abstract': 'This round of technical service bulletins show Ford telematics systems that have gone haywire and Chryslers and Dodges that can’t get out of Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/automobiles/15TSB.html', 'snippet': 'This round of technical service bulletins show Ford telematics systems that have gone haywire and Chryslers and Dodges that can’t get out of Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORD telematics systems that have gone haywire and Chryslers and Dodges that can’t get out of Park are among the problems covered in the latest technical service bulletins.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Touchy Ford Telematics', 'kicker': 'Motoring', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Touchy Ford Telematics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sturgis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sturgis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e44d62e5-955e-5363-9104-e83722b5bf38', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e44d62e5-955e-5363-9104-e83722b5bf38'}, {'abstract': 'The Paxton Phoenix was conceived to run on steam, but it didn’t turn out that way. No matter, the car was designed by Brooks Stevens and is still one of the more notable cars of the 1950s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/automobiles/15PAXTON.html', 'snippet': 'The Paxton Phoenix was conceived to run on steam, but it didn’t turn out that way. No matter, the car was designed by Brooks Stevens and is still one of the more notable cars of the 1950s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO Robert Paxton McCulloch, the idea of transplanting London Bridge to an Arizona desert probably seemed no more far-fetched in the 1960s than his dream, nearly two decades earlier, of mass-producing a steam-powered automobile.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/PAXT-2/PAXT-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/PAXT-2/PAXT-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/PAXT-2/PAXT-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/PAXT-2/PAXT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/automobiles/PAXT-2/PAXT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dreaming of Steam, but Running on Gas', 'kicker': 'Collecting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dreaming of Steam, but Running on Gas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pebble Beach (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:59:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2abb23e-c13b-578b-bb9a-da063eb7fb7c', 'word_count': 1282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2abb23e-c13b-578b-bb9a-da063eb7fb7c'}, {'abstract': 'The estimates, in the annual report by the Medicare trustees, were immediately swept up into the already inflamed political battle over federal spending, debt and the future of entitlement programs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/health/policy/14medicare.html', 'snippet': 'The estimates, in the annual report by the Medicare trustees, were immediately swept up into the already inflamed political battle over federal spending, debt and the future of entitlement programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The financial outlook for Medicare deteriorated in the last year because of the sluggish economic recovery, and the program’s hospital insurance trust fund will be exhausted in 2024, five years earlier than projected last year, the government said Friday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/us/14medicaremapNEW/14medicaremapNEW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Slow Recovery Worsens Financial State of Medicare', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trustees Say Medicare Finances Worsened Because of Sluggish Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T20:59:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Money & Policy', 'byline': {'origina