t_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '"Priest"', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vampires', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bettany, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Scott', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:21:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d7bdd2d-faa7-57a7-8e1a-c99b83d5a735', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d7bdd2d-faa7-57a7-8e1a-c99b83d5a735'}, {'abstract': 'Oprah Winfrey is powerful, but she did not reschedule the Eastern Conference finals.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/chicago-is-winfreys-town-but-there-is-room-for-the-bulls/', 'snippet': 'Oprah Winfrey is powerful, but she did not reschedule the Eastern Conference finals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oprah Winfrey is powerful, but she did not single-handedly reschedule the Eastern Conference finals. However, she will be borrowing the Chicago Bulls’ home court for 48 hours next week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chicago Is Winfrey’s Town, but There Is Room for the Bulls', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bulls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:25:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c91c0e5-4f40-51b3-af03-f21db1b59395', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c91c0e5-4f40-51b3-af03-f21db1b59395'}, {'abstract': 'Should school schedules accommodate themselves to students’ sleep preferences?', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/teenage-dreams/', 'snippet': 'Should school schedules accommodate themselves to students’ sleep preferences?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly every teenager I’ve ever known likes to go to bed late and wake up late. So why does high school often start at 8 a.m.? Robert Daniel Vorona and a team of researchers decided to take advantage of a natural experiment. High schools in Virginia Beach, Virginia start 75-80 minutes earlier than high schools in the neighboring town of Chesapeake, which has similar demographics. Vorona and his team investigated whether students in Virginia Beach also had more automobile crashes. They did. Virginia Beach crash rates were significantly higher and their peak crash times were one hour earlier than peak Chesapeake crash times.\xa0 The study was published in the Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teenage Dreams', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia Beach (Va)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:27:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/52c4668a-5f6b-5e5c-83bf-9fbac82c34f8', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52c4668a-5f6b-5e5c-83bf-9fbac82c34f8'}, {'abstract': 'Carmen Marron’s “Go for It!,” starring Aimee Garcia, is a briskly edited dance-out-of-the-slums story that speaks to working-class young women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/movies/go-for-it-review.html', 'snippet': 'Carmen Marron’s “Go for It!,” starring Aimee Garcia, is a briskly edited dance-out-of-the-slums story that speaks to working-class young women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opened on Friday nationwide. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/movies/goforit-span/goforit-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/13/movies/goforit-span/goforit-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/movies/goforit-span/goforit-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/13/movies/goforit-span/goforit-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/13/movies/goforit-span/goforit-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hip-Hop for Life', 'kicker': 'Film in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '"Go for It!\'', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:28:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44a39978-d138-56ba-92d7-ce54687595fc', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44a39978-d138-56ba-92d7-ce54687595fc'}, {'abstract': 'Video posted online by Syrian activists appear to show that protests continued on Friday, despite a bloody crackdown that sowed fear in some cities.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/video-of-protests-and-clashes-in-syria/', 'snippet': 'Video posted online by Syrian activists appear to show that protests continued on Friday, despite a bloody crackdown that sowed fear in some cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague Anthony Shadid reports, despite a brutal crackdown in Syria, protesters took to the streets in at least five neighborhoods in Homs, Syria’s third-largest city and a center of the two-month uprising. Activists said protests ranged in numbers from hundreds to thousands, and at least two people were killed when security forces opened fire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video of Protests and Clashes in Syria', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:29:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/197fa394-d1eb-5624-8b7a-5448d29511d2', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/197fa394-d1eb-5624-8b7a-5448d29511d2'}, {'abstract': '“An early autumn bite is in the air as a gold-tinged late afternoon falls over the rolling countryside of northern France.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/books/review/excerpt-to-end-all-wars-by-adam-hochschild.html', 'snippet': '“An early autumn bite is in the air as a gold-tinged late afternoon falls over the rolling countryside of northern France.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduction: Clash of Dreams', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘To End All Wars’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:31:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ec4156c-d660-58a0-b433-32afa8e13d5f', 'word_count': 3014, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ec4156c-d660-58a0-b433-32afa8e13d5f'}, {'abstract': 'Carlos Kalmar brings Ives’s “Unanswered Question” (1906) to a program on war, and proposed that for the evening, Ives’s question could be “Why do we go to war?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/arts/music/oregon-symphony-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'Carlos Kalmar brings Ives’s “Unanswered Question” (1906) to a program on war, and proposed that for the evening, Ives’s question could be “Why do we go to war?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is hard to believe that the Oregon Symphony had never performed in Carnegie Hall until Thursday evening, when it played a vivid, often wrenching program, “Music for a Time of War,” as part of the Spring for Music festival. The orchestra was formed in 1896, and its international reputation has grown since 1987, when it began recording big, opulent works and sonic spectacular CDs for the Delos label. Many of these discs, conducted by James DePreist, the orchestra’s music director at the time (and now emeritus), remain in print and show the ensemble to be a highly polished precision instrument. Since 2003 it has been directed by Carlos Kalmar, a Uruguayan conductor who has maintained it admirably. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/arts/SPRING/SPRING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/arts/SPRING/SPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/arts/SPRING/SPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Themes of War, Both the Existential and the Concrete', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exploring Themes of War, Both the Existential and the Concrete', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oregon Symphony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:32:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/561984c7-b09e-5fb7-b62f-af436af31f3e', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/561984c7-b09e-5fb7-b62f-af436af31f3e'}, {'abstract': 'RoseAnne Spradlin’s “beginning of something” is performed with fervor in intimate quarters at the Chocolate Factory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/arts/dance/roseanne-spradlins-beginning-of-something-review.html', 'snippet': 'RoseAnne Spradlin’s “beginning of something” is performed with fervor in intimate quarters at the Chocolate Factory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RoseAnne Spradlin has a knack for placing dancers and audience members in close quarters. For a while in downtown dance, this sort of intimacy was the new black. Choreographers moved the audience closer to the stage, limited the seating and favored a deadpan display of body parts. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/arts/something/something-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking the Audience Up Close and Personal, and Beyond Skin Deep', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking the Audience Up Close and Personal, and Beyond Skin Deep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spradlin, RoseAnne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:33:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dde9ef04-4ff0-5495-9b9c-2af7579271fa', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dde9ef04-4ff0-5495-9b9c-2af7579271fa'}, {'abstract': 'This Sunday in the magazine: The Money Issue.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/this-sunday-the-lure-of-a-dumb-rock/', 'snippet': 'This Sunday in the magazine: The Money Issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The unofficial sub-theme of our Money Issue is, What now? The worldwide recession is over (fingers crossed), and the developing world has seemingly forgotten that anything untoward ever happened. Meanwhile, in the U.S. and Europe, doubt, confusion and recriminations reign. While the recovery lurches ahead, dangers loom everywhere — the deficit battle in Washington, dissension in the European Union, oil-price shocks. For this year’s Money Issue, we went out in search of narratives that reflect this mixed-up state of affairs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hot Rocks', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:35:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac06353c-d5fa-53c3-971e-dc809f3eebc9', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac06353c-d5fa-53c3-971e-dc809f3eebc9'}, {'abstract': 'Bruce Levingston and Colin Jacobsen joined forces at Zankel Hall in the kind of recital for which each has become known: driven by ideas and filled with music old, new and well worth hearing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/arts/music/bruce-levingston-and-colin-jacobsen-review.html', 'snippet': 'Bruce Levingston and Colin Jacobsen joined forces at Zankel Hall in the kind of recital for which each has become known: driven by ideas and filled with music old, new and well worth hearing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Attending a concert by the pianist Bruce Levingston can feel a bit like showing up at a party at which you don’t know most of your fellow guests. Don’t hold it against him. Evidence indicates that Mr. Levingston mingles with ease in an exalted social stratum. Its members presumably help to support his nonprofit organization, Premiere Commission, which has financed the creation of some 40 new works; admirably, they also attend his concerts, greeting his efforts with exuberant response.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/arts/bruce/bruce-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Little Offbeat Humor in Cyclical Explorations', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Little Offbeat Humor In Cyclical Explorations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobsen, Colin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levingston, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:35:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e72a2f-01f0-57b1-8aac-4a5882eb9581', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e72a2f-01f0-57b1-8aac-4a5882eb9581'}, {'abstract': 'Wine lovers who want to keep track of bottles they’ve encountered have apps to record their oenophilliac journeys.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/app-smart-extra-keeping-track-of-great-wines/', 'snippet': 'Wine lovers who want to keep track of bottles they’ve encountered have apps to record their oenophilliac journeys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve probably collected more than 50 corks from bottles of wine that I tasted at restaurants and wanted to remember later. The corks were invariably chewed up by my dog or tossed in the garbage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'App Smart Extra: Keeping Track of Great Wines', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Google Phone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-13T22:38:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Tedeschi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tedeschi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a33aead-5999-5248-b051-7efc165af60e', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a33aead-5999-5248