 '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/sports/y-matt-2/y-matt-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 838, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/sports/y-matt-2/y-matt-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/sports/y-matt-2/y-matt-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote in Majors, Sensation in Japan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote In Majors, Sensation In Japan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Red Sox', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Records and Achievements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suzuki, Ichiro', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T01:56:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Lefton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lefton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca432989-467c-5446-9492-7493b652e5b4', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca432989-467c-5446-9492-7493b652e5b4'}, {'abstract': 'A leading philosophy journal has caused an uproar by issuing a quasi-apology for the tone of an issue on intelligent design that went online two years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/us/14beliefs.html', 'snippet': 'A leading philosophy journal has caused an uproar by issuing a quasi-apology for the tone of an issue on intelligent design that went online two years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to one cynical view, academic disputes are so vicious only because the stakes are so low. Yet as the editors of Synthese, a leading philosophy journal, can tell you, what they publish matters: in debates over Christianity, the teaching of evolution, and American politics.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/BELIEFS1/BELIEFS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 687, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/BELIEFS1/BELIEFS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/nyregion/BELIEFS1/BELIEFS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Debate Over Intelligent Design Ensnares a Journal', 'kicker': 'Beliefs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debate Over Intelligent Design Ensnares a Journal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forrest, Barbara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creationism and Intelligent Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T01:56:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/074471c9-6920-5704-a7e9-7c2939d16ab3', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/074471c9-6920-5704-a7e9-7c2939d16ab3'}, {'abstract': 'I’ll get you next time, Mike Nothnagel.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/home/', 'snippet': 'I’ll get you next time, Mike Nothnagel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SATURDAY’S PUZZLE — It’s tough to pinpoint exactly where I crashed and burned on this one, because normally I like to fancy myself on Mike Nothnagel’s wavelength. His puzzles usually fall fairly readily for me, and I always enjoy them. This one was highly enjoyable once I got a toehold in the puzzle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saturday: Home to One and a Half Billion People', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T02:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5afdca46-db6b-550f-bdc5-b98676c6d9f6', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5afdca46-db6b-550f-bdc5-b98676c6d9f6'}, {'abstract': 'A fight among the descendants of the United Farm Workers’ founder Cesar Chavez seems certain to accelerate the decline of a once-powerful movement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/us/14chavez.html', 'snippet': 'A fight among the descendants of the United Farm Workers’ founder Cesar Chavez seems certain to accelerate the decline of a once-powerful movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KEENE, Calif. — A series of dirt roads in this tiny Central Valley town leads to a warren of homes and offices that has been, for decades, the headquarters of the United Farm Workers union. This is where Cesar Chavez, the labor and civil rights leader, carved out a retreat and raised his family. In a former tuberculosis sanatorium, the Chavez children learned the lessons of the union and listened to endless conversations about the backbreaking work in the nearby fields. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/us/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/14/us/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/us/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/14/us/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/14/us/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899/JP-CHAVEZ-1-1305326413899-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Quarrel Imperils a Labor Hero’s Legacy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Quarrel Imperils a Labor Hero’s Legacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Cesar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Farm Workers of America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T02:03:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2a12a35-157f-5f1a-9f05-208d4a11cb7a', 'word_count': 1099, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2a12a35-157f-5f1a-9f05-208d4a11cb7a'}, {'abstract': 'Fresh off his first three-homer game, the Mets’ Carlos Beltran arrived in the city where he once had a career three months, including one of the most productive Octobers in history.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/13/victory-lap-for-beltran-in-houston/', 'snippet': 'Fresh off his first three-homer game, the Mets’ Carlos Beltran arrived in the city where he once had a career three months, including one of the most productive Octobers in history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — In the mind of the typical Houston Astros fan, it must seem fitting that Carlos Beltran came back to play here a day after having a career game.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Victory Lap for Beltran in Houston', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beltran, Carlos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Astros', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T02:07:08+0000