'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Memphis Grizzlies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T04:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/bf875db5-424b-57de-8376-a5abed962e64', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/bf875db5-424b-57de-8376-a5abed962e64'}, {'abstract': 'A former worker at a rural credit cooperative in China lighted a bottle full of gasoline and threw it into a meeting of his former colleagues on Friday morning, injuring 49 people, officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/asia/14briefs-Blast.html', 'snippet': 'A former worker at a rural credit cooperative in China lighted a bottle full of gasoline and threw it into a meeting of his former colleagues on Friday morning, injuring 49 people, officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A former worker at a rural credit cooperative in northwest China lighted a bottle full of gasoline and threw it into a meeting of his former colleagues on Friday morning, injuring 49 people, 19 of them seriously, the Chinese government said in a statement. The suspect was identified as Yang Xianwen, who fled after starting the fire about 8:30 a.m., the statement said. He was captured by the police at 5 p.m. Mr. Yang had been fired from the cooperative for embezzling public funds, the statement said, without explaining whether the money had belonged to the cooperative or another public entity. The incident occurred in Wuwei City in Gansu Province. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Credit Cooperative is Firebombed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Credit Cooperative is Firebombed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gansu Province (China)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T04:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/94481e90-4d09-5d9e-ac97-f85518e5d1bf', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94481e90-4d09-5d9e-ac97-f85518e5d1bf'}, {'abstract': 'The boys bond on the road, and Julie Taylor finds herself in a mess of trouble on “Friday Night Lights.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/friday-night-lights-watch-forging-and-breaking-ties/', 'snippet': 'The boys bond on the road, and Julie Taylor finds herself in a mess of trouble on “Friday Night Lights.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before we get into breaking down the most recent episode of “Friday Night Lights,” I want to address a question that came up in the comments section last week about what was going on with T.M.U.’s back-channel method of aiming to recruit Vince.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Friday Night Lights’ Watch: Forging and Breaking Ties', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T04:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d07ec8c-8737-5c59-bfdd-7c7dc010e1fc', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d07ec8c-8737-5c59-bfdd-7c7dc010e1fc'}, {'abstract': 'France’s European affairs minister scolded the British prime minister after he backed a move to phase out the French city of Strasbourg as the seat of the European Parliament.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/europe/14briefs-France.html', 'snippet': 'France’s European affairs minister scolded the British prime minister after he backed a move to phase out the French city of Strasbourg as the seat of the European Parliament.', 'lead_paragraph': ' France’s European affairs minister scolded the British government for an “unfriendly and aggressive gesture” on Friday, after Prime Minister David Cameron and his deputy, Nick Clegg, backed a move to phase out the French city of Strasbourg as the seat of the European Parliament. In March, European legislators approved a bill to reduce time spent by the Parliament at the official chamber in Strasbourg, and allow for more time at the administrative offices in Brussels, where they conduct most of their business. The French minister, Laurent Wauquiez, said he told his British counterpart, David Lidington, that European institutions should reside in a “plurality” of locations, as doing so “reflects the diversity of the European Union.” Mr. Wauquiez said that the bill ran contrary to European treaties, and that France would contest it before the European Court of Justice. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France Chides Britain for Support of Parliament Move', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France Chides Britain for Support of Parliament Move', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legislatures and Parliaments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Parliament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Strasbourg (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T04:02:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc486f68-4fe7-5880-a889-833277e07fb1', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc486f68-4fe7-5880-a889-833277e07fb1'}, {'abstract': 'Preliminary tests have identified a case of hemorrhagic fever caused by the rare and deadly Ebola virus in central Uganda, a medical official said Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/world/africa/14briefs-Uganda.html', 'snippet': 'Preliminary tests have identified a case of hemorrhagic fever caused by the rare and deadly Ebola virus in central Uganda, a medical official said Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Preliminary tests have identified a case of hemorrhagic fever caused by the rare and deadly Ebola virus in central Uganda, a medical official said Friday. A young girl who\xa0died in a hospital in the town of Bombo, about 25 miles\xa0from the capital, Kampala, tested positive for the virus, said Dr. Nelson Mugisha, a member of the medical team working on the case.\xa0He said the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\xa0had \xa0helped the government with the initial diagnosis. The virus\xa0can lead\xa0to large outbreaks with high death rates. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uganda: Deadly Ebola Virus Confirmed Near Capital', 'kicker': 