 'headline': {'main': 'Old Man River', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T12:36:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d47846a8-2113-5e1e-8efd-e6e9895038a3', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d47846a8-2113-5e1e-8efd-e6e9895038a3'}, {'abstract': 'On forays in Connecticut and Westchester County, happening upon a carpet of oniony goodness.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/urban-forager-ramp-season/', 'snippet': 'On forays in Connecticut and Westchester County, happening upon a carpet of oniony goodness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent weeks, while traversing the wet, wooded areas of friends’ backyards in Greenwich, Conn., and Westchester County, I discovered lush carpets of two-leaved plants under the dappled light of deciduous trees. Crouching down low among last year’s foliage, I found that while the stems of the plants in Greenwich were white and the ones in Westchester were reddish, the air in both areas was filled with the distinctive fragrance of onions. Ramps. I quickly considered the culinary possibilities — savory ramp pie, scrambled eggs with ramps, ramps with morels.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Urban Forager | Ramp Season', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T12:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ava Chin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ava', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/03a047ed-8675-5460-960a-cb676926c8f5', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03a047ed-8675-5460-960a-cb676926c8f5'}, {'abstract': 'As always at Triple Crown time, the thoughts of some in the racing world turn to drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/racings-drug-problem-more-complicated-than-it-looks/', 'snippet': 'As always at Triple Crown time, the thoughts of some in the racing world turn to drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Zorn is racing manager of Castle Village Farm thoroughbred partnerships, and a director of the New York Thoroughbred Horsemen’s Association and the Backstretch Employees Service Team. He writes the Business of Racing blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Racing’s Drug Problem: More Complicated Than It Looks', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strawbridge, George Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Udall, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T13:23:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Zorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zorn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/65d4a32f-dc43-5242-91a8-b80b1ed933dd', 'word_count': 1570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65d4a32f-dc43-5242-91a8-b80b1ed933dd'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. women’s national team is breaking out its new all black World Cup uniforms for two friendlies this week against Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/basic-black-for-u-s-women/', 'snippet': 'The U.S. women’s national team is breaking out its new all black World Cup uniforms for two friendlies this week against Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States women’s national team will be wearing its new all black uniforms in Saturday’s exhibition match against Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Basic Black for U.S. Women', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T13:34:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ba3a1d0-2a0e-56e5-9c39-1df2aa791762', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ba3a1d0-2a0e-56e5-9c39-1df2aa791762'}, {'abstract': 'Jokes from TV comedy show monologues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/weekinreview/15laugh.html', 'snippet': 'Jokes from TV comedy show monologues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JAY LENO', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Laugh Lines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laugh Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T14:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a68210-46dc-597c-9970-ee1369067040', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a68210-46dc-597c-9970-ee1369067040'}, {'abstract': 'The last time he predicted the world would end (on Sept. 6, 1994, to be specific) his research was flawed, Harold Camping acknowledges. Not this time, he says.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/weekinreview/15grist.html', 'snippet': 'The last time he predicted the world would end (on Sept. 6, 1994, to be specific) his research was flawed, Harold Camping acknowledges. Not this time, he says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last time he predicted the world would end (on Sept. 6, 1994, to be specific) his research was flawed, Harold Camping acknowledges. Not this time, he says. Mr. Camping, a retired civil engineer and the leader of Family Radio Worldwide, an independent Christian ministry, says he is convinced, based on a close reading of the Bible, that the end will begin on Saturday, heralded by huge earthquakes. His group has erected billboards across the country urging people to repent. Volunteers have traveled the country and the world spreading the same message. One of them, Lincoln Ropp, a medical student in Florida, went to Bulgaria. “We definitely see a lot of scoffing and mocking,” Mr. Ropp told the South Florida Sun-Sentinel. “But really, we take this as expected. Jesus said when you speak the truth, you’ll be hated.”', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Plans? World to End', 'kicker': 'Grist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weekend Plans? World to End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T14:16:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9af4f0da-8b28-5ca9-a94a-69ee21a70006', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9af4f0da-8b28-5ca9-a94a-69ee21a70006'}, {'abstract': 'Baghdad has weathered invasion, occupation, sectarian warfare and suicide bombers. But the latest scourge, tastelessness, may prove the toughest to overcome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/world/middleeast/15baghdad.html', 'snippet': 'Baghdad has weathered invasion, occupation, sectarian warfare and suicide bombers. But the latest scourge, tastelessness, may prove the toughest to overcome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — In downtown Baghdad, a police headquarters has been painted two shades of purple: lilac and grape. The central bank, a staid building in many countries, is coated in bright red candy cane stripes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/world/BAGHDAD/BAGHDAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/world/BAGHDAD/BAGHDAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/world/BAGHDAD/BAGHDAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/world/BAGHDAD/BAGHDAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/world/BAGHDAD/BAGHDAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Baghdad Erupts in Riot of Color, Calls to Tone It Down', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Baghdad Erupts in Riot of Color, Calls to Tone It Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics an