 None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ec91fd7-52d4-51df-9e2f-93417e040ddf', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ec91fd7-52d4-51df-9e2f-93417e040ddf'}, {'abstract': 'Why elevated parks might not work outside Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/opinion/15Rybczynski.html', 'snippet': 'Why elevated parks might not work outside Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE second section of the High Line, the park built atop an abandoned elevated rail trestle on the west side of Manhattan, is scheduled to open next month. Like a movie sequel, High Line II will have some things that are the same — more of those neat high-tech concrete planks underfoot and “peel-up” benches — as well as some things that are different: a “woodland flyover” of dense vegetation; a lawn; and a dramatic glass cutout exposing traffic on the street below. Food carts and something called a wine porch are promised, as well as a Renzo Piano-designed restaurant.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15rybcznskiimg/15rybcznskiimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15rybcznskiimg/15rybcznskiimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15rybcznskiimg/15rybcznskiimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15rybcznskiimg/15rybcznskiimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType'