antly mimic the soothing effects of hash brownies — and do so legally. At least for now.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/us/LAZYCAKES-2/LAZYCAKES-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/us/LAZYCAKES-2/LAZYCAKES-2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/us/LAZYCAKES-2/LAZYCAKES-2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dessert, Laid-Back and Legal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dessert, Laid-Back and Legal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Melatonin (Hormone)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dietary Supplements and Herbal Remedies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T22:33:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Saint Louis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': 'Saint', 'lastname': 'Louis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f150e84-da30-5665-a125-fd729ae6be0e', 'word_count': 1144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f150e84-da30-5665-a125-fd729ae6be0e'}, {'abstract': 'New research questions how much you really learn in college.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/opinion/15arum.html', 'snippet': 'New research questions how much you really learn in college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COMMENCEMENT is a special time on college campuses: an occasion for students, families, faculty and administrators to come together to celebrate a job well done. And perhaps there is reason to be pleased. In recent surveys of college seniors, more than 90 percent report gaining subject-specific knowledge and developing the ability to think critically and analytically. Almost 9 out of 10 report that overall, they were satisfied with their collegiate experiences. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15sharotimg/15sharotimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15sharotimg/15sharotimg-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/opinion/15sharotimg/15sharotimg-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your So-Called Education', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Your So-Called Education', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T22:38:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Josipa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roksa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0acb4b7b-b71c-5556-bbc8-c49cf29b29f9', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0acb4b7b-b71c-5556-bbc8-c49cf29b29f9'}, {'abstract': 'A hotplate sets a bed ablaze, and a building is soon engulfed in flames.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/site-of-fatal-fire-was-a-boarding-house-fire-officials-say/', 'snippet': 'A hotplate sets a bed ablaze, and a building is soon engulfed in flames.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Brooklyn row house where two men died in a fire on Friday night was an improvised boarding house, fire officials said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Site of Fatal Fire Was a Boarding House, Fire Officials Say', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Single-Room Occupancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bushwick (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T22:39:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9bd583e-19a7-5d64-bfb9-5ac812b55327', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9bd583e-19a7-5d64-bfb9-5ac812b55327'}, {'abstract': 'Readers weigh in on my column about Richard Holbrooke’s views on Pakistan and Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/readers-comment-on-my-holbrooke-column/', 'snippet': 'Readers weigh in on my column about Richard Holbrooke’s views on Pakistan and Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column offers advice from Ambassador Richard Holbrooke, the late American envoy to Afghanistan and Pakistan, for the crisis we now face in relations with that region. Holbrooke couldn’t speak out publicly at the time, but his posthumous views could not be more relevant at a time when we face a crisis in relations with Pakistan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers Comment on my Holbrooke Column', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T22:52:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/be0f12c2-7355-5f60-b8cb-9d3777a2d036', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be0f12c2-7355-5f60-b8cb-9d3777a2d036'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Agriculture may issue a contract to a group co-founded by Shirley Sherrod, a black employee who resigned last year after an edited video falsely suggested she had discriminated against a white farmer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/us/15sherrod.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Agriculture may issue a contract to a group co-founded by Shirley Sherrod, a black employee who resigned last year after an edited video falsely suggested she had discriminated against a white farmer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The federal Department of Agriculture may soon issue a contract to a community organization co-founded by Shirley Sherrod, the former department employee who was asked to resign last year after a conservative activist issued an edited videotape that appeared to show her making racially insensitive remarks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S.D.A. May Hire an Ex-Employee’s Group', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S.D.A. May Hire an Ex-Employee’s Group', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherrod, Shirley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE DEPARTMENT', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T22:58:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gardiner Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gardiner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1dd1d66-628f-55d4-a235-68dd528a62b7', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1dd1d66-628f-55d4-a235-68dd528a62b7'}, {'abstract': 'In the South of the 18th and 19th century, one family’s journey from black to white.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/14/black-or-white/', 'snippet': 'In the South of the 18th and 19th century, one family’s journey from black to white.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February 1861, just weeks after Louisiana seceded from the Union, Randall Lee Gibson enlisted as a private in a state army regiment. The son of a wealthy sugar planter and valedictorian of Yale’s Class of 1853, Gibson had long supported secession. Conflict was inevitable, he believed, not because of states’ rights or the propriety or necessity of slavery. Rather, a war would be fought over the inexorable gulf between whites and blacks, or what he called “the most enlightened race” and “the most degraded of all the races of men.” Because Northern abolitionists were forcing the South to recognize “the political, civil, and social equality of all the races of men,” Gibson wrote, the South was compelled to enjoy “independence out of the Union.” (Read Randall Lee Gibson’s article, “Our Federal Union.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black or White?', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T23:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Sharfstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Sharfstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5459c6c-9b9e-5979-82b9-2327a637cb41', 'word_count': 1588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5459c6c-9b9e-5979-82b9-2327a637cb41'}, {'abstract': 'R.A. Dickey’s record fell to 1-5 as he gave up 11 hits to the Astros in the Mets loss to Houston on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/sports/baseball/dickey-continues-to-struggle-as-mets-fall-to-astros.html', 'snippet': 'R.A. Dickey’s record fell to 1-5 as he gave up 11 hits to the Astros in the Mets loss to Houston on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — If there is one thing that R. A. Dickey has never been over his 12-month Mets career, it is conventional. An avid reader who yearns to climb mountains and to be a ball boy at the United States Open, Dickey is unique among professional athletes.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/15mets-2/15mets-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 713, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/15mets-2/15mets-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/15mets-2/15mets-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Struggling Again, Dickey Gives Knuckleball a Day Off', 'kicker': 'Astros 7, Mets 3', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Struggling Again, Dickey Gives Knuckleball a Day Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickey, R A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Astros', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Carlos', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-14T23:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4bcbc59-39cc-5ead-8034-270c0d9b5b58', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4bcbc59-39cc-5ead-8034-270c0d9b5b58'}, {'abstract': 'In the latest twist to his increasingly frustrating season, Jorge Posada was scheduled to hit ninth for the Yankees against the Red Sox on Saturday before he removed himself from the lineup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/sports/baseball/posada-drops-to-ninth-then-out-of-the-lineup.html', 'snippet': 'In the latest twist to his increasingly frustrating season, Jorge Posada was scheduled to hit ninth for the Yankees against the Red Sox on Saturday before he removed himself from the lineup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 4 p.m. Saturday, Jorge Posada said he understood why Manager Joe Girardi had dropped him to ninth in the Yankees’ batting order. “I put myself in this spot,” Posada said. Two hours later, Posada marched into Girardi’s office and told him that he felt disrespected. Rather than suffer the ignominy of hitting last in a nationally televised game against the Boston Red Sox, Posada pulled himself out of the lineup.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/yankees-2/yankees-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/yankees-2/yankees-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/15/sports/yankees-2/yankees-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Posada Sits Rather Than Hit Ninth in the Lineup', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Posada Takes Himself Out Instead of Batting Ninth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Red Sox', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Posada, Jorge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '