ins of her first feature, “Sleeping Beauty,” to what she calls a bout of “self-exposure.” The attention she received for her well-received first novel, “The Hunter” (1999), led to a recurring nightmare about being watched in her sleep.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cannes Q. and A.: Julia Leigh on a Modern-Day ‘Sleeping Beauty’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T15:06:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Lim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/26c7ead3-74a4-57af-b42d-d636a1a96e3a', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26c7ead3-74a4-57af-b42d-d636a1a96e3a'}, {'abstract': 'Israel’s borders erupted into deadly clashes as thousands of Palestinians marched from Syria, Lebanon, Gaza and the West Bank.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/world/middleeast/16mideast.html', 'snippet': 'Israel’s borders erupted into deadly clashes as thousands of Palestinians marched from Syria, Lebanon, Gaza and the West Bank.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israel’s borders erupted in deadly clashes on Sunday as thousands of Palestinians — marching from Syria, Lebanon, Gaza and the West Bank — confronted Israeli troops to mark the anniversary of Israel’s creation. More than a dozen people were reported killed and scores injured.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/world/middleeast/mideast/mideast-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/world/middleeast/mideast/mideast-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/world/middleeast/mideast/mideast-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Israeli Troops Fire as Marchers Breach Borders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israeli Troops Fire as Marchers Breach Borders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T15:14:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86c0bb8b-1f75-516e-8079-7ac658455a8e', 'word_count': 1377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86c0bb8b-1f75-516e-8079-7ac658455a8e'}, {'abstract': 'Taking great landscape photos isn’t easy, but there are some guidelines that can improve anyone’s skill. Photo pro Tom Bol shares a few.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/seven-tips-to-better-landscape-photos/', 'snippet': 'Taking great landscape photos isn’t easy, but there are some guidelines that can improve anyone’s skill. Photo pro Tom Bol shares a few.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photographer Ansel Adams is reputed to have said “Landscape photography is the supreme test of the photographer — and often the supreme disappointment.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seven Tips to Better Landscape Photos', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T15:16:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/56386dac-f840-5dc5-a6fd-d28f1ff12d43', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56386dac-f840-5dc5-a6fd-d28f1ff12d43'}, {'abstract': 'The landscape of the Netherlands and its influence on Dutch soccer.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/landscape-with-flowers/', 'snippet': 'The landscape of the Netherlands and its influence on Dutch soccer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Look features tulips seen from such a height that we see not flowers but a colorful abstraction. That picture above looks more like a worn throw rug from a northern European department store than a planted field. The tulip, of course, is a signature icon of Dutchness, along with windmills, clogs, pointy bonnets, dark Rembrandt portraits/bright Van Gogh craziness, certain Amsterdam liberties and — on my altar, at least — soccer wizards like Johann Cruyff and Dennis Bergkamp. And Cruyff has his own move named after him and is one of the architects behind totaalvoetbal; Bergkamp did this (and this).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Landscape, With Flowers', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T16:24:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/196e2035-fe27-594f-b8fd-eb64b2b423d9', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/196e2035-fe27-594f-b8fd-eb64b2b423d9'}, {'abstract': 'A security guard at a Staten Island library is sharing his extensive collection of mementos from a career as a referee of professional wrestling bouts, where he rarely blew his whistle.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/he-saw-plenty-before-the-bout-not-so-much-during-it/', 'snippet': 'A security guard at a Staten Island library is sharing his extensive collection of mementos from a career as a referee of professional wrestling bouts, where he rarely blew his whistle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time, a few decades ago, when pro wrestling thrived in New York City, whether at Madison Square Garden, or in high school gyms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'He Saw Plenty From the Ring, Not So Much in It', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memorabilia and Souvenirs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wrestling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albano, Louis Vincent', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York State Athletic Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T16:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/29e8c2eb-cf77-5b46-88d8-3c0ded19dbf4', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29e8c2eb-cf77-5b46-88d8-3c0ded19dbf4'}, {'abstract': 'Breaking the connection.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/update-on-verizon/', 'snippet': 'Breaking the connection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, with regard to this post, I’m on Amtrak now, and my connection seems OK. In fact, I’m sure that it’s perfectly reliable; there’s no chance whatsoever that I will be cut off in the middle of a', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Update on Verizon', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T16:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee16fae1-3938-5c97-9cad-bfb63383f3ba', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee16fae1-3938-5c97-9cad-bfb63383f3ba'}, {'abstract': 'The Morning Line for May 15, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/the-morning-line-motion-and-fair-hill-are-hot-topics/', 'snippet': 'The Morning Line for May 15, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost all of the Triple Crown news today involves varying aspects of Kentucky Derby winner Animal Kingdom.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: Motion and Fair Hill Are Hot Topics', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T17:03:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb028ffa-9320-5123-b18f-0c62ba719dcd', 'word_count': 890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb028ffa-9320-5123-b18f-0c62ba719dcd'}, {'abstract': 'The star of movies including “Chinatown” and “Network,” her image gracing the festival’s program guides and advertisements, bristles at the notion that Cannes might have a commercial bent.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/faye-dunaway-defends-canness-artistic-soul/', 'snippet': 'The star of movies including “Chinatown” and “Network,” her image gracing the festival’s program guides and advertisements, bristles at the notion that Cannes might have a commercial bent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France— International sex scandals with likely candidates for the French presidency have not disrupted proceedings at the film festival here. On Sunday afternoon, Frédéric Mitterrand, the French minister of culture, gave Faye Dunaway a medal and the honor of being an officer in “L’Ordre des Arts et des Lettres.” Ms. Dunaway, whose kohl-eyed, sex-kitten image, photographed by Jerry Schatzberg in 1970, graces program guides and advertisements all over town, and hangs stories high above the Palais des Festivals, seemed to deserve the title, or at least she was ready to defend it. At the opening night dinner following the premiere last Wednesday of Woody Allen’s “Midnight in Paris,” she bristled at the notion that Cannes might have a commercial bent. “Don’t say that, because it’s wrong,” she said. “Cannes is not commercial, for God’s sake. Cannes is the only festival in the world, and the only entity in the world, that honors great film, from all over the world.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Faye Dunaway Defends Cannes’s Artistic Soul', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunaway, Faye', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schatzberg, Jerry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T17:38:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9656c210-bc03-53dc-8781-922b8b8c2741', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9656c210-bc03-53dc-8781-922b8b8c2741'}, {'abstract': 'By building and hiring in Europe, the American Internet giant is trying to win hearts and minds as it faces legal, regulatory and political challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/technology/16iht-google16.html', 'snippet': 'By building and hiring in Europe, the American Internet giant is trying to win hearts and minds as it faces legal, regulatory and political challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Chiseled into the stone facade of the vacant Second Empire building at 8, rue de Londres here is the name of the French national railroad, a former occupant. Most recently, the site housed another conservative pillar of the French corporate establishment, the insurer AXA. The proud new owner is a company with a very different image in France, more bomb-thrower than bourgeois: Google. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/business/global/16google/16google-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Google Turns On Charm to Win Over Europeans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Google Turns On Charm To Win Over Europeans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T17:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c038c6c-691a-544d-ad8f-3fda02ee2e8c', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c038c6c-691a-544d-ad8f-3fda02ee2e8c'}, {'abstract': 'NBC will count heavily on its lineup of talent competitions to set up a new fall season packed with 12 new scripted series.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/nbc-bets-on-talent-shows-and-scripted-series/', 'snippet': 'NBC will count heavily on its lineup of talent competitions to set up a new fall season packed with 12 new scripted series.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:52 p.m. | Updated NBC, hoping to rebound from almost a decade of conspicuous failure, will count heavily on its lineup of talent competitions to set up a new season packed with 12 new scripted series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Upfronts: NBC Bets on Talent Shows and Scripted Series', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T17:48:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/61c19a50-ea59-5fb4-bd18-8872912a032d', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61c19a50-ea59-5fb4-bd18-8872912a032d'}, {'abstract': 'Until now, Rick Welts, the president and chief executive of the Phoenix Suns, had not felt comfortable enough in his chosen field to be open about his sexuality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/sports/basketball/nba-executive-says-he-is-gay.html', 'snippet': 'Until now, Rick Welts, the president and chief executive of the Phoenix Suns, had not felt comfortable enough in his chosen field to be open about his sexuality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, in a Midtown office adorned with sports memorabilia, two longtime friends met for a private talk. David Stern, the commissioner of the National Basketball Association, sipped his morning coffee, expecting to be asked for career advice. Across from him sat Rick Welts, the president and chief executive of the Phoenix Suns, who had come to New York not to discuss careers, but to say, finally, I am gay.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/YJPOUT1/YJPOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/YJPOUT1/YJPOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/YJPOUT1/YJPOUT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/YJPOUT1/YJPOUT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/YJPOUT1/YJPOUT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sports Executive Leaves the Safety of His Shadow Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going Public, N.B.A. Figure Sheds Shadow Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Phoenix Suns', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle SuperSonics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T18:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fa5aa9d-652a-51f6-b027-d6c6b99089a3', 'word_count': 2085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fa5aa9d-652a-51f6-b027-d6c6b99089a3'}, {'abstract': 'The arrest of the I.M.F. chief, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, for an alleged sexual attack of a maid at a New York hotel sent tremors through the French media and blogosphere on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/the-french-reaction-to-i-m-f-chiefs-arrest/', 'snippet': 'The arrest of the I.M.F. chief, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, for an alleged sexual attack of a maid at a New York hotel sent tremors through the French media and blogosphere on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The arrest of the chief of the International Monetary Fund, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, for an alleged sexual attack of a maid at a New York hotel sent tremors through the French media and blogosphere on Sunday, inspiring a mix of contempt, sympathy, expressions of national humiliation and more than a few conspiracy theories that the contender for the French presidency had been set up by his rivals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The French Reaction to I.M.F. Chief’s Arrest', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Monde', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T18:20:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Bilefsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/81d7bdbe-ee0f-5f81-9b0f-ef097cd7148c', 'word_count': 897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81d7bdbe-ee0f-5f81-9b0f-ef097cd7148c'}, {'abstract': 'The guitarist Sharon Isbin’s program with the Salomé Chamber Orchestra at the Metropolitan Museum of Art included concertos and a chamber piece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/arts/music/sharon-isbin-and-salome-chamber-orchestra-review.html', 'snippet': 'The guitarist Sharon Isbin’s program with the Salomé Chamber Orchestra at the Metropolitan Museum of Art included concertos and a chamber piece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The classical guitar is mostly a solitary instrument, partly because the greatest works written for it are in the solo repertory, but for practical reasons too: It is so soft-spoken that its voice easily gets lost alongside louder orchestral instruments and in the large halls where orchestras tend to play. So even though its concerto repertory has grown prodigiously since the late 1930s and is plentifully available on recordings, opportunities to hear guitarists play concertos in concert remain comparatively rare. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SHARON/SHARON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 992, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Classical Guitarist Making Herself Heard', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Classical Guitarist Making Herself Heard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isbin, Sharon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T18:36:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14099c79-478f-5627-813e-bc321f9fe110', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14099c79-478f-5627-813e-bc321f9fe110'}, {'abstract': 'K. J. Choi was the last man standing at the Players Championship, winning on the first hole of sudden death with a short par putt after David Toms missed his shot at par.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/sports/golf/players-championship-has-wild-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'K. J. Choi was the last man standing at the Players Championship, winning on the first hole of sudden death with a short par putt after David Toms missed his shot at par.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PONTE VEDRA BEACH, Fla. — K. J. Choi was the last man standing at the Players Championship on Sunday, winning on the first hole of sudden death with a short par putt after David Toms — who had made a 17-foot birdie putt at the 72nd hole to tie — missed a putt that was two inches shorter than Choi’s.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/golf/golf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/golf/golf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/golf/golf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Choi Wins in Playoff After 3-Putt by Toms', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Choi Wins Players in Playoff After Toms Misses Short Putt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choi, K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDowell, Graeme', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toms, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T18:39:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Dorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b940a91-6fa4-51dc-aa50-d34d4bda11ed', 'word_count': 994, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b940a91-6fa4-51dc-aa50-d34d4bda11ed'}, {'abstract': 'The International Monetary Fund named John Lipsky acting managing director after its chief, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, was arrested in connection with a sexual attack. The accuser picked him out of a lineup Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/nyregion/imf-names-replacement-as-chief-awaits-arraignment.html', 'snippet': 'The International Monetary Fund named John Lipsky acting managing director after its chief, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, was arrested in connection with a sexual attack. The accuser picked him out of a lineup Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hours after its chief, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, was arrested in connection with the alleged sexual attack of a maid at a Midtown Manhattan hotel, the International Monetary Fund on Sunday named John Lipsky as acting managing director.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/business/global/16imf-3/16imf-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I.M.F. Names New Leader', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipsky, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T19:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker and Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53afd66f-a836-5cda-a711-0acbfd66c3bb', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53afd66f-a836-5cda-a711-0acbfd66c3bb'}, {'abstract': 'Doesn’t anyone here know how to play this game?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/this-week-panel/', 'snippet': 'Doesn’t anyone here know how to play this game?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Basically, what Digby said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week Panel', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T19:31:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4142b74f-7e4a-5ea0-a89b-b40b8a1fd19d', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4142b74f-7e4a-5ea0-a89b-b40b8a1fd19d'}, {'abstract': 'We’re repeating the same mistakes.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/money-1937-slightly-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'We’re repeating the same mistakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A followup on my note on monetarist pathos: I’ve been rereading Friedman and Schwartz, and it’s striking just how much the debate in 1936-7 paralleled the debate now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money 1937 (Slightly Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T19:56:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/08610bdd-7540-579f-bbe0-0842d73e7a1a', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08610bdd-7540-579f-bbe0-0842d73e7a1a'}, {'abstract': 'The Nai-Ni Chen Dance Company performed “Dragons on the Wall (Tianji)” at Dance Theater Workshop over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/arts/dance/nai-ni-chen-dance-company-at-dance-theater-workshop-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Nai-Ni Chen Dance Company performed “Dragons on the Wall (Tianji)” at Dance Theater Workshop over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the choreographer Nai-Ni Chen, her piece “Dragons on the Wall (Tianji)” doesn’t have much to do with mythical, fire-breathing creatures. She’s more focused on what the dragon can symbolize: individual freedom. As she writes in the program notes, a dragon is about “power and tremendous energy.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/CHEN/CHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/CHEN/CHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/CHEN/CHEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/CHEN/CHEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/CHEN/CHEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Resistance and Desire, With a Little Mud Wrestling', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Resistance and Desire, With a Little Mud Wrestling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nai-Ni Chen Dance Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T19:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddd887b1-feba-505a-8154-5fcc44b3cd4a', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddd887b1-feba-505a-8154-5fcc44b3cd4a'}, {'abstract': 'A European Commission advisory group wants countries to treat the cellphone users’ location of as personal data, deserving of the highest level of privacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/technology/16locate.html', 'snippet': 'A European Commission advisory group wants countries to treat the cellphone users’ location of as personal data, deserving of the highest level of privacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — The European Commission’s advisory panel on data protection plans this week to urge governments in the European Union to treat the geographic location of cellphone users as personal data, deserving of the highest level of privacy protection. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'E.U. Panel to Propose Tighter Data Protection', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel to Urge Europe To Bolster Data Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T20:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': "By Kevin J. O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/330dd0f7-ff0d-5c89-bd9f-8502eac50bff', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/330dd0f7-ff0d-5c89-bd9f-8502eac50bff'}, {'abstract': 'Days after announcing his presidential candidacy, Newt Gingrich sharply criticized a plan to overhaul Medicare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/us/politics/16gingrich.html', 'snippet': 'Days after announcing his presidential candidacy, Newt Gingrich sharply criticized a plan to overhaul Medicare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Days after formally announcing his candidacy for the 2012 Republican presidential nomination, Newt Gingrich on Sunday sharply criticized a plan by House Republicans that would drastically overhaul Medicare, the federal health care program for retirees.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/us/GINGRICH/GINGRICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/us/GINGRICH/GINGRICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/us/GINGRICH/GINGRICH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/us/GINGRICH/GINGRICH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/us/GINGRICH/GINGRICH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gingrich Calls G.O.P.’s Medicare Plan Too Radical', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gingrich Calls G.O.P.’s Medicare Plan Too Radical', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T20:35:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Hernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b013a96-f49f-5eed-9dd9-29da3e73706d', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b013a96-f49f-5eed-9dd9-29da3e73706d'}, {'abstract': 'The top 12 films took in about $125.2 million at the domestic box office, down 5 percent from $131.4 million for the top 12 films for the same weekend last year, according to Hollywood.com’s box-office reporting service.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/hollywoods-box-office-slump-continues/', 'snippet': 'The top 12 films took in about $125.2 million at the domestic box office, down 5 percent from $131.4 million for the top 12 films for the same weekend last year, according to Hollywood.com’s box-office reporting service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— Hollywood still couldn’t shake its box-office slump, as “Thor,” the top performer for a second weekend, posted $34.5 million in North American tickets sales for Marvel and Paramount Pictures, down 34 percent from $52 million on the same weekend last year for “Iron Man 2,” another Marvel-Paramount action fantasy that was also in its second week. “Thor,” starring Chris Hemsworth, above, has had $119.3 million in domestic ticket sales to date.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hollywood’s Box-Office Slump Continues', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'APATOW, JUDD', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T20:59:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd6b69d5-41be-5802-a176-502282ef67c6', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd6b69d5-41be-5802-a176-502282ef67c6'}, {'abstract': 'The Indians, who have the best record in baseball, have maximized the return in talent while saving more money. The next step is getting Cleveland fans to believe in the team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/sports/baseball/indians-are-first-in-standings-but-last-in-attendance.html', 'snippet': 'The Indians, who have the best record in baseball, have maximized the return in talent while saving more money. The next step is getting Cleveland fans to believe in the team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CLEVELAND — Two flags hang from each lamppost on Ontario Street here, outside the home of the best team in baseball. On one side is a head shot of a smiling Cleveland Indians player. On the other is a photograph of that player shaking hands with a fan.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/yindians1/yindians1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/yindians1/yindians1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/sports/yindians1/yindians1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ahead of Schedule, Indians Rock Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ahead of Schedule, Indians Rock Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Indians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sizemore, Grady', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shapiro, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T21:21:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba3eac9e-86bb-5dd9-945e-0c25354bfef6', 'word_count': 1421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba3eac9e-86bb-5dd9-945e-0c25354bfef6'}, {'abstract': 'The first Spring for Music festival concluded with, of all things, the Beethoven Fifth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/arts/music/evolution-and-a-definitive-endpoint.html', 'snippet': 'The first Spring for Music festival concluded with, of all things, the Beethoven Fifth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Titles of concert programs can be misleading. Take “The Evolution of the Symphony,” as the conductor Kent Nagano called the program he presented with the Montreal Symphony Orchestra at Carnegie Hall on Saturday night, the final concert in the first Spring for Music festival. This sounds like the title of a music history course in college. But the actual program, which alternated performances of Bach’s keyboard sinfonias, played by the pianist Angela Hewitt, with works by Gabrieli, Webern, Stravinsky and Beethoven, was more subversive than that drab thematic heading suggested. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SUBSPRING/SUBSPRING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 311, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SUBSPRING/SUBSPRING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 311}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SUBSPRING/SUBSPRING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SUBSPRING/SUBSPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/SUBSPRING/SUBSPRING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Evolution, and a Definitive Endpoint', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Evolution, and a Definitive Endpoint', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Paul Chamber Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Montreal Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-15T21:25:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/801ddd36-f797-54d3-8815-f1d81d814f57', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/801ddd36-f797-54d3-8815-f1d81d814f57'}, {'abstract': 'Local 802 is going after “Priscilla Queen of the Desert,” hoping to undercut the use of recordings in theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/theater/broadway-union-takes-on-priscilla-over-recorded-music.html', 'snippet': 'Local 802 is going after “Priscilla Queen of the Desert,” hoping to undercut the use of recordings in theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a bid to protect jobs, the Broadway musicians’ union is waging an unusually aggressive, political-style campaign against the new musical “Priscilla Queen of the Desert” in hopes of undercutting the use of recorded music during live theatrical performances. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/JPMUSIC2/JPMUSIC2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/JPMUSIC2/JPMUSIC2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/JPMUSIC2/JPMUSIC2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/JPMUSIC2/JPMUSIC2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/16/arts/JPMUSIC2/JPMUSIC2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Broadway Union Takes On ‘Priscilla’ Over Music',