tion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/79d8bca5-af7e-51ac-9e5d-86da1e099646', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79d8bca5-af7e-51ac-9e5d-86da1e099646'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion Question | Tell us what you think of programs like the one described in this article. Did you experience anything like this as a young child yourself?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/are-adults-hurting-young-children-by-pushing-them-to-achieve/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion Question | Tell us what you think of programs like the one described in this article. Did you experience anything like this as a young child yourself?', 'lead_paragraph': 'An article this weekend described a program in New York City in which parents pay $200 to $300 a month for children as young as two and three years old to spend up to an hour twice weekly being tutored in math and reading skills to get ahead. What do you think of programs like this? Why do you think more and more parents seem to be interested in them?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Adults Hurting Young Children by Pushing Them to Achieve?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-16T08:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0048fe68-f99b-569a-bfe0-226db67ab61d', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0048fe68-f99b-569a-bfe0-226db67ab61d'}, {'abstract': 'James Whitlow Delano tells why he photographed cherry blossoms, of all things, in the midst of the devastation in Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/tsunami-sakura/', 'snippet': 'James Whitlow Delano tells why he photographed cherry blossoms, of all things, in the midst of the devastation in Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — I stood in Ofunato, in Iwate Prefecture, with a photographer friend. We were mesmerized by a scene that seemed too outlandish even to imagine. She told me how her grandmother described clothes, reduced to rags, hanging from trees after Tokyo was bombed to the ground in World War II.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tsunami/Sakura', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cherries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-16T09:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Whitlow Delano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'Whitlow', 'lastname': 'Delano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/47782672-d8fb-5bc7-bc83-df4e37be412b', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47782672-d8fb-5bc7-bc83-df4e37be412b'}, {'abstract': 'On his visit to Europe this month, Obama will find a much diminished European Union.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/the-old-worlds-growing-pains/', 'snippet': 'On his visit to Europe this month, Obama will find a much diminished European Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It makes sense to think of Europe as a family, because all its arguments are over money and children. Although the most recent economic numbers were better than expected, German politics churns on as vo