ype': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/16/business/media/16adnewsletter1-190/16adnewsletter1-190-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 708, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Campaign to Raise the Profile of Central Park’s Protector', 'kicker': 'Campaign Spotlight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Campaign to Raise the Profile of Central Park&#8217;s Protector', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Park Conservancy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-16T17:47:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d2690d1-f5b9-5359-ab5d-0e36e2f8fa1b', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d2690d1-f5b9-5359-ab5d-0e36e2f8fa1b'}, {'abstract': 'The Morning Line for Monday, May 16', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/morning-line-whats-in-a-name/', 'snippet': 'The Morning Line for Monday, May 16', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, much of the news media plays the “Name Game” with the horses likely to be entered in the Preakness. The Baltimore Sun explains how horses get their names and asks “Shouldn’t there be a hall of fame somewhere for thoroughbreds whose accomplishments are few, but whose names are marvelous?”\xa0 Apparently, “Yakahickamickadola” may be the winner of the oddest — if not greatest — horse name ever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: What’s in a Name?', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-16T17:52:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/82686d7c-b249-5e72-a555-b3fa04df3b99', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82686d7c-b249-5e72-a555-b3fa04df3b99'}, {'abstract': 'The Museum of Modern Art will pay $31.2 million for the American Folk Art Museum’s building, according to a notice the folk art museum filed to its bondholders.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/moma-to-pay-31-2-million-for-folk-art-museum-building/', 'snippet': 'The Museum of Modern Art will pay $31.2 million for the American Folk Art Museum’s building, according to a notice the folk art museum filed to its bondholders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Modern Art will pay $31.2 million for the American Folk Art Museum’s building, according to a notice the folk art museum filed to its bondholders. The two museums announced the planned sale last week but had not disclosed the price. The folk art museum took on $32 million of debt to construct the building on West 53rd Street, but attendance never met expectations, and after sustaining investment losses in the financial crisis, the museum defaulted on its debt. The folk art museum had said that the sale would allow it to retire its debt, as well as pay fees to lawyers and consultants. The numbers are close: As of June 30, 2009, the museum owed a principal of $29,945,000 on the bonds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MoMA to Pay $31.2 Million for Folk Art Museum Building', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-16T17:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/882f5cdd-a77b-5746-bf4a-1021ed3419f2', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/882f5cdd-a77b-5746-bf4a-1021ed3419f2'}, {'abstract': 'Coming to the rescue of a sleeping actor, and finding new ways to tell people that you would rather not trade seats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/17/business/17flier.html', 'snippet': 'Coming to the rescue of a sleeping actor, and finding new ways to tell people that you would rather not trade seats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BECAUSE of the sensitive nature of my work, I do face-to-face meetings. That makes my flight schedule brutal. It’s not uncommon for me to fly from Miami to New York City on Monday, go to Los Angeles on Tuesday, Moscow on Thursday and then back to Miami for my son’s lacrosse or football games on Saturday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/17/business/17flier/17flier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/17/business/17flier/17flier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2