mes.com/2011/05/17/nyregion/bard-college-given-60-million-for-global-initiatives.html', 'snippet': 'The Open Society Foundations, created by George Soros, will help the college organize and support disparate programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bard College, a small liberal arts institution in the Hudson Valley, has received a $60 million gift from the Open Society Foundations in recognition of its global involvement, which includes programs in New Orleans, Nicaragua and Russia, officials are to announce on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '$60 Million Gift to Bolster Bard College’s Global Work', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '$60 Million Gift to Bolster Bard College’s Global Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botstein, Leon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Open Society Institute', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soros, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bard College', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-17T01:20:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0435382-96d0-509e-ba66-2b7673761257', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0435382-96d0-509e-ba66-2b7673761257'}, {'abstract': 'Developments in the study of evolution suggest that the survival of the fittest depends as much on cooperation as it does on a competition between self-interests.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/17/opinion/17brooks.html', 'snippet': 'Developments in the study of evolution suggest that the survival of the fittest depends as much on cooperation as it does on a competition between self-interests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of evolution, we have been told, is the story of the survival of the fittest. The strong eat the weak. The creatures that adapt to the environment pass on their selfish genes. Those that do not become extinct.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nice Guys Finish First', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nice Guys Finish First', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keltner, Dacher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowak, Martin A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haidt, Jonathan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-17T01:27:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/deace46e-ce55-57a0-85f7-63af0bccb0e4', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deace46e-ce55-57a0-85f7-63af0bccb0e4'}, {'abstract': 'A program that helps get books to into the homes of low-income families can boost literacy, and help publishers, too.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/a-book-in-every-home-and-then-some/', 'snippet': 'A program that helps get books to into the homes of low-income families can boost literacy, and help publishers, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When we imagine people without books, we think of villagers in places like Afghanistan. But many families in the United States have no children’s books at home. In some of the poorest areas of the country, it’s hard to find books for sale. A study (pdf) of low-income neighborhoods in Philadelphia, for example, found a ratio of one book for sale for every 300 children. Tens of millions of poor Americans can’t afford to buy books at all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Book in Every Home, and Then Some', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-17T01:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7a8241e-bd32-59ab-a620-d1906ac2b79e', 'word_count': 1852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7a8241e-bd32-59ab-a620-d1906ac2b79e'}, {'abstract': 'In the Republican budget plan proposed by Representative Paul Ryan, it’s the I’ve Got Mines vs. The Left Behinds.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/16/the-need-for-greed/', 'snippet': 'In the Republican budget plan proposed by Representative Paul Ryan, it’s the I’ve Got Mines vs. The Left Behinds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bet was audacious from the beginning, and given the miserable, low-down tenor of contemporary politics, not unfathomable: Could you divide the country between greedy geezers and everyone else as a way to radically alter the social contract?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Need for Greed', 'kicker': 'Timothy Egan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': Non