Twitter, but it doesn’t work universally across all posts on the service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Twitter Posts, Public and Private', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T10:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea8610af-b59a-57d3-83f8-3c7e5055fb56', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea8610af-b59a-57d3-83f8-3c7e5055fb56'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Harford, a columnist for The Financial Times, has a book out this month, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts With Failure.” It’s an argument about the virtues — and necessity, really — of trial and error.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/adaptive-bombshells/', 'snippet': 'Tim Harford, a columnist for The Financial Times, has a book out this month, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts With Failure.” It’s an argument about the virtues — and necessity, really — of trial and error.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tim Harford, a columnist for The Financial Times, has a book out this month, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts With Failure.” It’s an argument about the virtues — and necessity, really — of trial and error. In his words, there are three essential steps: “to try new things, in the expectations that some will fail; to make failure survivable, because it will be common; and to make sure that you know when you’ve failed.” It sort of seems like common sense, but the book’s wealth of fascinating case studies shows just how uncommon and difficult it often is for people and institutions to follow such a program.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adaptive Bombshells', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harford, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T10:16:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf91d427-b2c4-5399-b6b8-fa310bacc787', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf91d427-b2c4-5399-b6b8-fa310bacc787'}, {'abstract': 'Hewlett-Packard passes on Intel’s new Thunderbolt connection. For now, that’s not such a big deal.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/h-p-dodges-a-thunderbolt/', 'snippet': 'Hewlett-Packard passes on Intel’s new Thunderbolt connection. For now, that’s not such a big deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hewlett-Packard, the world’s largest personal computer manufacturer, announced Tuesday that it would not be installing Intel’s new Thunderbolt I/O connections on its desktop computers. The company said it would continue fitting USB 3.0 connections to its machines.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'H.P. Dodges a Thunderbolt', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hewlett-Packard Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T10:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f01f9cb-57c5-5caa-b3de-b0f51971ff95', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f01f9cb-57c5-5caa-b3de-b0f51971ff95'}, {'abstract': 'In Mississippi, warnings about Eagle Lake caused an exodus. The 15 residents who remained gather daily at a bar called Strick’s for beer, burgers and, inevitably, talk about the levees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/us/19bar.html', 'snippet': 'In Mississippi, warnings about Eagle Lake caused an exodus. The 15 residents who remained gather daily at a bar called Strick’s for beer, burgers and, inevitably, talk about the levees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAGLE LAKE, Miss. — If you need to get ahold of somebody here, this is how. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/us/19bar_337/19bar_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/19/us/19bar_337/19bar_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/us/19bar_337/19bar_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/us/19bar_337/19bar_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/us/19bar_337/19bar_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mississippi Town’s Holdouts Wait Out the Flood at the Bar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mississippi Town’s Holdouts Wait Out the Flood at the Bar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T10:57:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Campbell Robertson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Campbell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8af691c6-e8a3-50f6-a89c-32d7da8cec0f', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8af691c6-e8a3-50f6-a89c-32d7da8cec0f'}, {'abstract': 'When deciding what to cut to trim costs, a framing company rethinks its guarantee that frames will be ready in seven days.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/are-we-cutting-fat-or-bone/', 'snippet': 'When deciding what to cut to trim costs, a framing company rethinks its guarantee that frames will be ready in seven days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have spent many years focusing on how to increase revenue in my custom picture-framing business. Much of what we’ve done has worked, and the business is now roughly 20 times the size of the average framing shop in America. Lately, however, because of the economy, the housing crisis, and other factors, the framing industry has shrunk, and I now spend quite a bit of time looking for cost reductions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are We Cutting Fat or Bone?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T11:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Goltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e4b48c7-5e45-50e8-84d8-8024923ae6fc', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e4b48c7-5e45-50e8-84d8-8024923ae6fc'}, {'abstract': 'HRTV will broadcast the draw from Pimlico Race Course beginning at 5 p.m. Eastern on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/preakness-tv-coverage/', 'snippet': 'HRTV will broadcast the draw from Pimlico Race Course beginning at 5 p.m. Eastern on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The NBC Sports Group will not televise the post position draw ahead of the Preakness Stakes as it did for the Kentucky Derby. Instead, HRTV will broadcast the draw from Pimlico Race Course at 5 p.m. Eastern on Wednesday as part of its “Pursuit of the Crown” show. On Friday, the Animal Kingdom extravaganza will begin in earnest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Preakness TV Coverage', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T11:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Hoppert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middl