'Hospital emergency rooms are closing as need rises, suspected carcinogen used in baby products, protecting you iPad in the kitchen and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/wednesday-reading-fewer-emergency-rooms-as-need-rises/', 'snippet': 'Hospital emergency rooms are closing as need rises, suspected carcinogen used in baby products, protecting you iPad in the kitchen and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: Fewer Emergency Rooms As Need Rises', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T13:35:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1ffa1e8-e644-5f63-925d-44836bf32047', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1ffa1e8-e644-5f63-925d-44836bf32047'}, {'abstract': 'Avery DiUbaldo, a high school senior from Denver, writes: “While some teens may balk at hearing that they’ve picked up the mannerisms and habits of their parents, I see it as a compliment, and as a point of pride.”', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/cherry-creek-avery-diubaldo-5/', 'snippet': 'Avery DiUbaldo, a high school senior from Denver, writes: “While some teens may balk at hearing that they’ve picked up the mannerisms and habits of their parents, I see it as a compliment, and as a point of pride.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am an exceptionally average pool player. When faced with a game of billiards, I turn mediocrity into an art form, spinning with my cue a beautiful tapestry of mild competence. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pause, to Give Thanks', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T13:53:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Avery DiUbaldo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Avery', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DiUbaldo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7b5c3b7-aa28-5dac-b911-a187b5eb9132', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7b5c3b7-aa28-5dac-b911-a187b5eb9132'}, {'abstract': 'Submit your questions and comments on the finale of “The Good Wife” for Ginia Bellafante and check back for her responses.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/reaction-to-the-good-wife-finale-your-questions-answered/', 'snippet': 'Submit your questions and comments on the finale of “The Good Wife” for Ginia Bellafante and check back for her responses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s get right down to the brass tacks of the the season finale of “The Good Wife” on Tuesday. (Those who haven’t yet watched and intend to, read no further; those who’d like a broader look at the whole season see my Critic’s Notebook here.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaction to ‘The Good Wife’ Finale: Your Questions Answered', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T13:54:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d0777ed-a39d-5253-981d-001a5ea0c300', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d0777ed-a39d-5253-981d-001a5ea0c300'}, {'abstract': 'Carmen Callil, the founder of the feminist publishing house Virago, stormily withdrew from the panel over the decision to honor Mr. Roth for extraordinary work in fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/philip-roth-wins-man-booker-international-prize-in-disputed-decision/', 'snippet': 'Carmen Callil, the founder of the feminist publishing house Virago, stormily withdrew from the panel over the decision to honor Mr. Roth for extraordinary work in fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Calling him the “most decorated living American writer,” a panel named Philip Roth the winner of the Man Booker International Prize on Wednesday, an honor awarded every two years to an author for extraordinary work in fiction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Philip Roth Wins Man Booker International Prize in Disputed Decision', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Man Booker International Prize', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roth, Philip', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e56b73a-c172-50a5-8999-807dcc2e1097', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e56b73a-c172-50a5-8999-807dcc2e1097'}, {'abstract': 'Everything at Captains of Industry in Melbourne, Australia, from suits to sandwiches, is bespoke.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/a-handmade-tale/', 'snippet': 'Everything at Captains of Industry in Melbourne, Australia, from suits to sandwiches, is bespoke.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I wanted to do something with my hands,’’ says James Roberts, explaining how he became a shoemaker. Together with Thom Grogan, Roberts owns Captains of Industry, a ‘‘gentlemans outfitter and cafe’’ in Melbourne, Australia. Grogan, who runs the bespoke-suiting side of the business, thinks the Internet Age has inspired a craving for things that aren’t mass-produced. ‘‘People are getting pretty sick of being anonymous online and just buying stuff through online stores,’’ he says. ‘‘They want interaction.’’ Roberts and Grogan don’t stop at shoes and jams: they even make their own thread from scratch. ‘‘It’s kind of relaxing,’’ Roberts says, ‘‘if all goes well.’’ View interactive feature >>', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Handmade Tale', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Todd Selby and Ethan Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Todd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Selby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a32c437d-d201-528c-8a7e-5c24acd817ae', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a32c437d-d201-528c-8a7e-5c24acd817ae'}, {'abstract': 'New products to keep your iPad clean when you’re cooking.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/kitchen-coverup/', 'snippet': 'New products to keep your iPad clean when you’re cooking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Gadgetwise, Damon Darlin presents a few new ways to keep iPads and other tablet computers clean and neat while you’re cooking. His favorite is Chef Sleeve, a clear plastic envelope that costs less than a buck and lets you use the touch screen with greasy fingers. Plus its box doubles as an iPad stand. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kitchen Coverup', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:13:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/87cd73bb-85bb-58f0-a4f4-2a2f9f2886fd', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87cd73bb-85bb-58f0-a4f4-2a2f9f2886fd'}, {'abstract': 'A backscatter X-ray scanner produced a haunting, powerful image of a crowd of migrants crammed in a cargo truck.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/x-ray-scan-reveals-513-mexican-migrants-in-2-trucks/', 'snippet': 'A backscatter X-ray scanner produced a haunting, powerful image of a crowd of migrants crammed in a cargo truck.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When police ran X-ray scanners over two cargo trucks at a checkpoint in southeastern Mexico on Tuesday, they made a surprising discovery: Inside the trailer were the ghostly shadows of 513 migrants — some suffering from dehydration — packed together in near-suffocating conditions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'X-Ray Scan Reveals 513 Migrants in 2 Trucks', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smuggling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'X-Rays', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:13:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0948595b-efb0-5a8a-8a23-04899dce05a2', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0948595b-efb0-5a8a-8a23-04899dce05a2'}, {'abstract': 'The appointment of Sheila Birnbaum represents a major step in the implementation of a law Congress passed late last year to reopen the Sept. 11th Victim Compensation Fund.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/nyregion/overseer-of-911-health-fund-is-chosen.html', 'snippet': 'The appointment of Sheila Birnbaum represents a major step in the implementation of a law Congress passed late last year to reopen the Sept. 11th Victim Compensation Fund.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Justice Department on Wednesday chose a special master to administer a multibillion-dollar fund created to provide compensation to rescue workers and others sickened from exposure to toxic fumes, dust and smoke from ground zero after the 2001 terrorist attack. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/nyregion/BIRNBAUM/BIRNBAUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/nyregion/BIRNBAUM/BIRNBAUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/18/nyregion/BIRNBAUM/BIRNBAUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Overseer of 9/11 Health Fund Is Chosen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Step to Reopen 9/11 Health Fund, Administrator Is Named', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'James Zadroga 9-11 Health and Compensation Act', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Hernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23d61039-384e-5db5-97d6-c41561c027cc', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23d61039-384e-5db5-97d6-c41561c027cc'}, {'abstract': 'A writer at a the Chronicle of Higher Education blog delves into economic differences and theological differences among religions.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/the-economics-of-a-vengeful-deity/', 'snippet': 'A writer at a the Chronicle of Higher Education blog delves into economic differences and theological differences among religions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Naomi Schaefer Riley continues the discussion of religion and income at the Chronicle of Higher Education’s Brainstorm blog:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Economics of a Vengeful Deity', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T14:18:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/efe569f7-483e-55c5-8d02-4224c45fb73b', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efe569f7-483e-55c5-8d02-4224c45fb73b'}, {'abstract': 'Tokyo Electric Power Company, which operates the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, has released new video of conditions at the facility.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/new-video-of-japanese-nuclear-plant/', 'snippet': 'Tokyo Electric Power Company, which operates the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, has released new video of conditions at the facility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My colleagues Hiroko Tabuchi, Keith Bradsher and Matthew Wald report on the front page of Wednesday’s Times on a critical failure that led to the explosions at the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, after the earthquake and tsunami in March.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Video of Japanese Nuclear Plant', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 1, 'major'