 conceptual artist Tania Bruguera is sharing a tiny Queens apartment with 11 other people and scraping by on the minimum wage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tania Bruguera has eaten dirt, hung a dead lamb from her neck and served trays of cocaine to a gallery audience, all in the name of art. She has shown her work at the Venice Biennale, been feted at the Pompidou Center in Paris and landed a Guggenheim fellowship. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/nyregion/19performance_337/19performance_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/19/nyregion/19performance_337/19performance_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/nyregion/19performance_337/19performance_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/nyregion/19performance_337/19performance_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/nyregion/19performance_337/19performance_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artist’s Performance: A Year as a Poor Immigrant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She Calls It Art. They Call It, Well, Life.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Corona (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Queens Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creative Time', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T16:29:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Dolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/731cb279-a671-53ea-9479-be28f3947395', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/731cb279-a671-53ea-9479-be28f3947395'}, {'abstract': 'One of the scientists who designed a popular way to break the climate challenge into pieces says it was misused by climate campaigners.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/wishful-wedges-and-the-energy-quest/', 'snippet': 'One of the scientists who designed a popular way to break the climate challenge into pieces says it was misused by climate campaigners.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:02 p.m. | UpdatedRob Socolow of Princeton University is quoted by National Geographic as saying the popular “wedges” approach to charting climate progress that he proposed with his colleague Stephen Pacala in 2004 was misused by climate campaigners to overstate the ease with which the world could cut tens of billions of tons of heat-trapping carbon dioxide emissions out of its energy menu.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wishful Wedges and the Energy Quest', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romm, Joe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Princeton (NJ)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T16:32:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0ab6f95-f3ec-5f72-a41f-611ec49aa445', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0ab6f95-f3ec-5f72-a41f-611ec49aa445'}, {'abstract': 'The Italian director Nanni Moretti has not shied away from controversial subjects, and his new film, about a newly elected Pope suffering from existential panic attacks, is no exception.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/cannes-q-and-a-nanni-moretti-on-tackling-big-game/', 'snippet': 'The Italian director Nanni Moretti has not shied away from controversial subjects, and his new film, about a newly elected Pope suffering from existential panic attacks, is no exception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France— Outspoken in interviews and in his offscreen role as a political activist, the Italian writer, director and actor Nanni Moretti tends to take a lighter touch in his films. “Habemus Papam” (“We Have a Pope”), screening in the official competition at the Cannes Film Festival, concerns a newly elected pope named Melville (the French actor Michel Piccoli), whose existential panic attack prompts the cardinals to call in a psychoanalyst (Mr. Moretti). But while the shrink is sequestered at the Vatican, the pope goes on a walkabout through the streets of Rome, rekindling an ol