conflict, Meryl Streep has dropped out of a reading of Alan Alda’s new play about Marie Curie at the World Science Festival gala at Alice Tully Hall on June 1. Maggie Gyllenhaal, whose most recent appearance on a New York Stage was in “Three Sisters,” is taking on the role of that pioneering scientist in “Radiance: The Passion of Marie Curie” at the fund-raising event. Bob Balaban is directing the reading, which will also feature Amy Adams, Bill Camp, Allison Janney, David Morse, Liev Schreiber and Brent Sexton. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maggie Gyllenhaal to Replace Streep in Reading of Play About Marie Curie', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gyllenhaal, Maggie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T16:56:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf9f2207-7c7a-58ce-92d0-b54768485af4', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf9f2207-7c7a-58ce-92d0-b54768485af4'}, {'abstract': 'Morning Line for Wednesday, May 18', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/morning-line-full-field-for-preakness-minus-nehro/', 'snippet': 'Morning Line for Wednesday, May 18', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lead story on the Triple Crown trail is the decision of the owner Amed Zayat not to enter his horse Nehro in the Preakness. Nehro finished second in the Kentucky Derby. Zayat’s racing manager said in the New York Post, “The main decision came down to Mr. Zayat feeling it was a little much to ask the horse to run four great races in an eight-week time frame.”\xa0 Zayat told the Daily News, “”I let my horse tell me what the next step is. In the last six weeks he’s run a lot. I’ve got to be kind to him. Patience rewards you. I don’t want to get my ego involved. You do what is right by the horse.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: Full Field for Preakness, Minus Nehro', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T17:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9002fb16-7fb3-5da0-8815-493bbf847f13', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9002fb16-7fb3-5da0-8815-493bbf847f13'}, {'abstract': 'Japanese term for “energy saving,” which became a buzzword after the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/setsuden/', 'snippet': 'Japanese term for “energy saving,” which became a buzzword after the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Japanese term for “energy saving,” which became a buzzword after the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Setsuden', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T17:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fc1f717-b504-5e79-883d-72022fc63598', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fc1f717-b504-5e79-883d-72022fc63598'}, {'abstract': 'Is it really true that voters will overlook almost anything if they think the sinning political figures will carry out the policies they support?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/sex-and-the-politician/', 'snippet': 'Is it really true that voters will overlook almost anything if they think the sinning political figures will carry out the policies they support?', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Brooks: Gail, can I talk to you about that hoary old intersection between private life and politics?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sex and the Politician', 'kicker': 'The Conversation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T17:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6a475d8-0d70-52ab-b6de-c8aeff351df3', 'word_count': 837, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6a475d8-0d70-52ab-b6de-c8aeff351df3'}, {'abstract': 'Please tell us what you think of these two sites, JetBlue.com and Continental.com. Put yourself in the shoes of someone planning a trip and ask yourself, does this site offer the information you were looking for?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/whats-wrong-and-whats-right-with-these-web-sites/', 'snippet': 'Please tell us what you think of these two sites, JetBlue.com and Continental.com. Put yourself in the shoes of someone planning a trip and ask yourself, does this site offer the information you were looking for?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing I’ve learned over the years is that the quality of a Web site depends as much on the quality of the design and the effectiveness of the messaging as it does on the amount of money invested in technology and features. That’s why there are lots of major companies that have invested millions in their online business but have Web sites that fail miserably.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Wrong and What’s Right With These Web Sites?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T17:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gabriel Shaoolian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabriel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shaoolian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a49b8f7-5709-5fe4-8282-be57c766af90', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a49b8f7-5709-5fe4-8282-be57c766af90'}, {'abstract': 'A jeweler in Paris finds inspiration in dollhouse furniture.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/tiny-treasures-missbibis-dollhouse-gems/', 'snippet': 'A jeweler in Paris finds inspiration in dollhouse furniture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 43 square feet, Missbibi, the recently opened jewelry store in Paris’s Palais Royal, is so small, if you blink on your way down the arcade you might miss it. This suits Brigitte Giraudi, 34, who is known as Miss Bibi, just fine. “I’ve always appreciated small, delicate, beautifully made things,” says the jeweler, who turned from stop-motion animation and miniature model construction to sautoirs and broaches. Oddly enough it was Giraudi’s fascination with dollhouse furniture that spurred her move to jewelry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tiny Treasures | Missbibi’s Dollhouse Gems', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T17:01:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Voight', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Voight', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/42235cfa-ff68-5cd5-9e21-b95c90dbf422', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42235cfa-ff68-5cd5-9e21-b95c90dbf422'}, {'abstract': 'With the military jealously guarding its control of the airspace, wealthy Chinese hobbyists fly clandestinely.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/asia/19china.html', 'snippet': 'With the military jealously guarding its control of the airspace, wealthy Chinese hobbyists fly clandestinely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WENZHOU, China — Here in this smoggy coastal metropolis, the nouveau-riche heart of entrepreneurial China, the latest sign that one has really made it is not a Benz, or even a 