9'}, {'abstract': 'The lawsuit, joined by the N.A.A.C.P., says the city hasn’t fulfilled its obligations to help the schools it wants to shut down. It also accuses the city of favoring charter schools in allotting space.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/teachers-union-sues-to-stop-school-closings/', 'snippet': 'The lawsuit, joined by the N.A.A.C.P., says the city hasn’t fulfilled its obligations to help the schools it wants to shut down. It also accuses the city of favoring charter schools in allotting space.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:51 p.m. | Updated The United Federation of Teachers filed a lawsuit on Wednesday in an effort to halt two tools the city’s Education Department uses to change the school system: closing schools for poor performance and giving charter schools space in buildings occupied by traditional public schools.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teachers’ Union Sues to Stop School Closings', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulgrew, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walcott, Dennis M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn for the Advancement of Colored People', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Federation of Teachers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/db408805-50c8-5d02-bcb4-11603ba1270b', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db408805-50c8-5d02-bcb4-11603ba1270b'}, {'abstract': 'A majority of Federal Reserve policy makers said that the best next step to stimulate the economy would be to reduce the balance sheet', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/business/economy/19fed.html', 'snippet': 'A majority of Federal Reserve policy makers said that the best next step to stimulate the economy would be to reduce the balance sheet', 'lead_paragraph': 'A majority of Federal Reserve policy makers said at their most recent meeting that the best next step in their program to stimulate the economy would be to reduce the balance sheet and then focus on interest rates, according to minutes of the April meeting released on Wednesday. But they also said that simply discussing interest rates and other options did not mean that any move “would necessarily begin soon.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/business/FED/FED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/business/FED/FED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Stimulus Nears End, Fed Signals Focus on Rates', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Stimulus Nears End, Fed Signals Focus on Rates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:24:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64d82ff5-b408-5497-a414-c4b7e4683cd5', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64d82ff5-b408-5497-a414-c4b7e4683cd5'}, {'abstract': 'Now, instead of dictating taste, Gap hopes to catch the indiscriminate shopper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/fashion/gap-now-casts-a-wide-net-critical-shopper.html', 'snippet': 'Now, instead of dictating taste, Gap hopes to catch the indiscriminate shopper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the early 1990s, when I was in high school, one of my best friends got an after-school job at his local Gap. I could only imagine the glories, which seemed as foreign as Gstaad or Marrakesh: the folding, making those stacks of T-shirts perfectly neat! The chance to listen to the company-issue store soundtracks, filled with hits of the day, over and over! The discounts!', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/19/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do My High School Jeans Still Fit?', 'kicker': 'Critical Shopper', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do My High School Jeans Still Fit?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jeans (Apparel)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gap Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:29:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93d3a5ca-38e8-591c-b98a-caf7eb00b559', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93d3a5ca-38e8-591c-b98a-caf7eb00b559'}, {'abstract': 'A Japanese photographer’s latest body of work is resplendent in small things, as seen in a show opening on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/everything-is-illuminated-rinko-kawauchi-at-the-hermes-gallery/', 'snippet': 'A Japanese photographer’s latest body of work is resplendent in small things, as seen in a show opening on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Japanese photographer Rinko Kawauchi is finely tuned to what she likes to call the small voices in our world — those that whisper. Her eyes, she says, naturally focus on small things. And her latest body of work, “Illuminance,” which provides the content for both an exhibition, opening Friday at the Hermès gallery, and a lush book published by Aperture, is resplendent in them. A tiny green frog perches on the palm of a seemingly enormous hand; a lit cigarette, its ash a fiery orange, juts from the fingers of another. A slab of raw tuna is approached in such proximity that it takes on the monumentality of landscape. An image of goldfish packed to the gills in a plastic bag brings to mind the Tokyo subway during rush hour.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything Is Illuminated | Rinko Kawauchi at the Hermès Gallery', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:37:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/12d20077-b1c9-5468-8e06-0d48ac0651de', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12d20077-b1c9-5468-8e06-0d48ac0651de'}, {'abstract': 'The new federal consumer watchdog announced plans to revamp crucial mortgage documents that have long confused would-be home buyers.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/consumer-bureau-plans-new-mortgage-form/', 'snippet': 'The new federal consumer watchdog announced plans to revamp crucial mortgage documents that have long confused would-be home buyers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mountains of paperwork are one of the few certainties of home buying.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Consumer Bureau Plans New Mortgage Form', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:44:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7c67ee6-1a0f-5cc4-9e19-9c65f0f52234', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7c67ee6-1a0f-5cc4-9e19-9c65f0f52234'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Abrams and Howard Beck, The Times’s N.B.A. writers, respond to questions from readers about the playoffs, the Knicks and why no one plays defense anymore.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/answers-about-the-n-b-a-playoffs-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Abrams and Howard Beck, The Times’s N.B.A. writers, respond to questions from readers about the playoffs, the Knicks and why no one plays defense anymore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The following are the responses from Jonathan Abrams and Howard Beck, who took questions from readers this week about the N.B.A. playoffs, which head into the conference finals, as well as the Knicks and Nets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answers About the N.B.A. Playoffs and More', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:45:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ffdc471-8aaa-5c22-a730-856228d0c41c', 'word_count': 3194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ffdc471-8aaa-5c22-a730-856228d0c41c'}, {'abstract': 'While some startups are getting bought for their talent, many others are toiling away in obscurity.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/are-talent-acquisitions-a-sign-of-a-new-bubble/', 'snippet': 'While some startups are getting bought for their talent, many others are toiling away in obscurity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s no question that the “bubbly” feeling has returned to Silicon Valley. The signs are everywhere. Lavish parties are back and so are the highly valued start-ups. There’s a seeming frenzy over the coming LinkedIn IPO. And now, there’s a surge in “talent acquisitions,” which I wrote about today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Talent Acquisitions a Sign of a New Bubble?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T18:46:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miguel Helft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miguel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Helft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ace540e-6dbd-5200-adc7-1c72478ef747', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ace540e-6dbd-5200-adc7-1c72478ef747'}, {'abstract': 'An illustration of the method by which astronomers using a ground-based telescope found evidence for 10 free-floating, Jupiter-mass planets in space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/science/100000000824755/under-a-cosmic-magnifying-glass.html', 'snippet': 'An illustration of the method by which astronomers using a ground-based telescope found evidence for 10 free-floating, Jupiter-mass planets in space.', 'lead_paragr