hion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By ERICA M. BLUMENTHAL and LINDA CONTE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7b5ff87b-c22b-5690-bcee-a1075c6510f4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7b5ff87b-c22b-5690-bcee-a1075c6510f4'}, {'abstract': 'Scott Brooks is faced with the daunting task of orchestrating a defense against Dirk Nowitzki, but in that challenge lies an opportunity for redemption.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/scott-brooks-and-the-task-of-stopping-nowitzki/', 'snippet': 'Scott Brooks is faced with the daunting task of orchestrating a defense against Dirk Nowitzki, but in that challenge lies an opportunity for redemption.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scott Brooks’s team faces an impossible riddle in defending Dirk Nowitzki, but on a micro level, Brooks has stumbled into an interesting opportunity for redemption. No coach has taken a bigger hit to his reputation over the course of this postseason than Brooks. Phil Jackson was ushered out gracefully, despite his team’s being swept by the Dallas Mavericks in the second round. Gregg Popovich did what he could, but succumbed to the fate of an aging roster. Doc Rivers coached admirably in the face of injuries to his team, but a series win against the Heat wasn’t in the cards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scott Brooks and the Task of Stopping Nowitzki', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Scott', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durant, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'r