Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aa5ccee-c680-52c8-81ee-9561f4501669', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aa5ccee-c680-52c8-81ee-9561f4501669'}, {'abstract': 'Boxwood is a good option for gardens under assault from deer; it comes in a range of shades and shapes, and deer won’t eat it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/garden/boxwood-varieties.html', 'snippet': 'Boxwood is a good option for gardens under assault from deer; it comes in a range of shades and shapes, and deer won’t eat it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you love green, and the deer are eating everything in your garden, boxwood — which comes in a range of shades, shapes and sizes — is a lovely alternative. Unlike English boxwood, which prefers partial shade, most will tolerate sun without turning yellow, but almost all are darker green in the shade.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19garden-side/GSide-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19garden-side/GSide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19garden-side/GSide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Boxwood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exploring Boxwood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T21:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Raver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd692604-db9c-5738-9f6e-342dce81f946', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd692604-db9c-5738-9f6e-342dce81f946'}, {'abstract': 'Just months after the director Kate Whoriskey became artistic director of Intiman Theater, she learned it was broke. In April the theater’s board of trustees voted to close it and layoff the staff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/theater/intiman-theater-in-seattle-and-its-financial-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'Just months after the director Kate Whoriskey became artistic director of Intiman Theater, she learned it was broke. In April the theater’s board of trustees voted to close it and layoff the staff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — When Bartlett Sher handpicked Kate Whoriskey to succeed him as artistic director of Intiman Theater here in 2009, he appeared to be giving her a dream job. Under Mr. Sher, Intiman had become one of the country’s most prestigious theaters, nurturing a growing audience while basking in the reflected glow of Mr. Sher’s increasing fame as a freelance director of operas and musicals, including an acclaimed Broadway revival of “South Pacific.” For Ms. Whoriskey, then 38 and best known for directing Lynn Nottage’s Pulitzer Prize-winning play, “Ruined,” Intiman seemed like the perfect artistic home.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/arts/intiman-1/intiman-1-articleL