imes.com/2011/05/19/arts/dance/dance-modern-angles-on-classical.html', 'snippet': 'New York City Ballet performed Balanchine’s “Divertimento No. 15,” Robbins’s “Opus 19: The Dreamer” and Martins’s “Fearful Symmetries” on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A dancer extends a leg forward above hip-height, then, keeping that leg outstretched, starts to fall forward in the direction it is pointing. By the time he has brought the raised foot down to the floor, he has transferred much of his weight to it. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/arts/nycb1/nycb1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/18/arts/nycb1/nycb1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/arts/nycb1/nycb1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/arts/nycb1/nycb1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/18/arts/nycb1/nycb1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Three Twists on Classical Style, in Movement and in Character', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Twists on Classical Style, In Movement and in Character', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T21:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/68069ce7-0624-52f9-8e1b-ca9e35434791', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68069ce7-0624-52f9-8e1b-ca9e35434791'}, {'abstract': 'Citing a scheduling conflict, Meryl Streep has dropped out of a reading of Alan Alda’s new play about Marie Curie at the World Science Festival gala at Alice Tully Hall on June 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/theater/footnote.html', 'snippet': 'Citing a scheduling conflict, Meryl Streep has dropped out of a reading of Alan Alda’s new play about Marie Curie at the World Science Festival gala at Alice Tully Hall on June 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Citing a scheduling conflict, Meryl Streep has dropped out of a reading of Alan Alda’s new play about Marie Curie at the World Science Festival gala at Alice Tully Hall on June 1. Maggie Gyllenhaal is taking on the role of that pioneering scientist in “Radiance: The Passion of Marie Curie” at the fund-raising event. Other readers are Amy Adams, Bill Camp, Allison Janney, David Morse, Liev Schreiber and Brent Sexton.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Streep, Meryl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T21:52:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a5f476e-8631-587f-8d97-a4e0c7d05580', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a5f476e-8631-587f-8d97-a4e0c7d05580'}, {'abstract': 'Under pressure from the City Council, the Bloomberg administration reveals which fire companies are on the chopping block.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/city-releases-list-of-20-fire-companies-slated-to-close/', 'snippet': 'Under pressure from the City Council, the Bloomberg administration reveals which fire companies are on the chopping block.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The city on Wednesday afternoon released a list of the 20 fire companies it is considering closing to help fill a $600 million budget gap. The city had initially refused to release the list, prompting the City Council to threaten to subpoena it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Releases List of 20 Fire Companies Slated to Close', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fire Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T21:57:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman and Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ff4e6c1-063e-50ea-8173-d8901bd213a0', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ff4e6c1-063e-50ea-8173-d8901bd213a0'}, {'abstract': 'A statue of a cult deity that was once the subject of litigation between the Italian government and the J. Paul Getty Museum of Los Angeles, was publicly unveiled on Tuesday in its new home in Aidone, Sicily.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/arts/disputed-statue-is-back-home-in-italy.html', 'snippet': 'A statue of a cult deity that was once the subject of litigation between the Italian government and the J. Paul Getty Museum of Los Angeles, was publicly unveiled on Tuesday in its new home in Aidone, Sicily.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A statue of a cult deity, below left, that was once the subject of litigation between the Italian government and the J. Paul Getty Museum of Los Angeles, was publicly unveiled on Tuesday in its new home in Aidone, Sicily. The Getty bought the seven-foot-tall limestone-and-marble statue in 1988 for $18 million, giving rise to a two-decade legal battle after Italian authorities determined it had been looted from Italy. The museum agreed to return the statue, often identified as Aphrodite, as part of a 2007 accord with the Italian government for the restitution of 40 illegally excavated artifacts. The statue was finally shipped to Rome in March. It has been installed in what is expected to be its permanent home, the archaeological museum in Aidone, a hilltop town near the ruins of the ancient Greek colony of Morgantina, where some experts believe the statue once stood.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disputed Statue Is Back Home in Italy', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disputed Statue Is Back Home in Italy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': 