ingrich, Newt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huckabee, Mike', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Roy S', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roemer, Buddy', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanford, Mark', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intrade', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:25:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2d0080e-6bd1-5dd5-aee2-d7d502d59e36', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2d0080e-6bd1-5dd5-aee2-d7d502d59e36'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Rye Barcott addresses how young people can make a difference in the world.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/when-it-comes-to-helping-others-just-do-it/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Rye Barcott addresses how young people can make a difference in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s graduation season, and for the past two months I’ve been traveling to campuses in the United States on a book tour to talk about service in the Marines and social entrepreneurship in Africa. One point seems to resonate with students above all of the others. From a commuter college in the plains of Indiana to Stanford and MIT, students have been latching onto one simple sentence: be a doer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When It Comes to Helping Others: Just Do It', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rye Barcott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rye', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barcott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/26d24e4f-71f0-5aab-a801-d52f972608b1', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26d24e4f-71f0-5aab-a801-d52f972608b1'}, {'abstract': 'After unveiling the 2012 Jaguar XF, the car’s chief designer took a trip in search of more of the 1960s silhouettes that inspire him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/garden/midcentury-modern-shopping-with-wayne-burgess.html', 'snippet': 'After unveiling the 2012 Jaguar XF, the car’s chief designer took a trip in search of more of the 1960s silhouettes that inspire him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1961, Jaguar introduced the E-Type, a low-slung sports car with a long hood and seductively curvaceous lines. Many enthusiasts still consider it one of the most beautiful cars of all time, an opinion shared by the Museum of Modern Art, which acquired one for its permanent collection in 1996.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/garden/19shop-span/19shop-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/18/garden/19shop-span/19shop-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/garden/19shop-span/Shop-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/18/garden/19shop-span/19shop-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/18/garden/19shop-span/19shop-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '1960s Design', 'kicker': 'Shopping With Wayne Burgess', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curves Preferred', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:27:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim McKeough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKeough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c64c73ff-ddee-5a66-a3ff-cd828e19aaba', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c64c73ff-ddee-5a66-a3ff-cd828e19aaba'}, {'abstract': 'American Ballet Theater’s “Don Quixote” at the Metropolitan Opera on Tuesday was appropriately light-hearted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/arts/dance/american-ballet-theaters-don-quixote-at-met-opera-review.html', 'snippet': 'American Ballet Theater’s “Don Quixote” at the Metropolitan Opera on Tuesday was appropriately light-hearted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s not much of Cervantes’s picaresque tale left in the ballet version of “Don Quixote.” The plot, taken from Book 2 of the 1615 novel, is barely there, boiled down to the aged don’s quest for his dream woman, Dulcinea, and his belief that he has found her in the earthy peasant girl, Kitri. She, however, has her own problems, since her father wants her to marry a rich nobleman rather than her penniless sweetheart, the barber Basilio. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/arts/SUBQUIXOTE1/SUBQUIXOTE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/arts/SUBQUIXOTE1/SUBQUIXOTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/arts/SUBQUIXOTE1/SUBQUIXOTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With the Matadors, Capes, Gypsies and Dancing, Who Needs a Plot?', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With the Matadors, Capes, Gypsies And Dancing, Who Needs a Plot?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d13412be-0a72-536d-8c5f-015dc487af28', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d13412be-0a72-536d-8c5f-015dc487af28'}, {'abstract': 'Handel’s rarely performed “Judas Maccabaeus” made an appearance on Tuesday evening, performed by the Clarion Choir and Orchestra at Park Avenue Christian Church.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/arts/music/handels-judas-maccabaeus-review.html', 'snippet': 'Handel’s rarely performed “Judas Maccabaeus” made an appearance on Tuesday evening, performed by the Clarion Choir and Orchestra at Park Avenue Christian Church.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You would think that because Handel’s “Judas Maccabaeus” tells a vague approximation of the story of Hanukkah — or at least it portrays the Maccabees, the story’s central characters — it should have become part of the seasonal repertory, as a Jewish alternative to “Messiah.” But it remains a rarity, and when it is performed at all — as it was on Tuesday evening by the Clarion Choir and Orchestra at Park Avenue Christian Church — it is calendar pages away from the holiday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/arts/ALTJUDAS/ALTJUDAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ancient Victory, Honoring a Later One', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Ancient Victory, Honoring a Later One', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:31:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5518d771-e42b-5eff-b0f3-5c616c3c7c89', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5518d771-e42b-5eff-b0f3-5c616c3c7c89'}, {'abstract': 'Ruby Beets now custom-makes simple and sturdy modern furniture, pieces to mix in with the old.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/garden/ruby-beets-made-to-order-furniture.html', 'snippet': 'Ruby Beets now custom-makes simple and sturdy modern furniture, pieces to mix in with the old.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tiny Ruby Beets, tucked into an old silent-film theater on a side street in Sag Harbor, N.Y., is all of a sudden not so tiny, having doubled in size this month to accommodate a new line of furniture, Ruby Beets Made to Order. Sharone Einhorn and Honey Wolters, the store’s owners, have long filled the place with antiquarian goods (like apothecary cabinets, bottom right) and artisanal objects (like Moroccan blankets), as well as clean-lined modern and estate-sale pieces and contemporary artwork (like seascapes by the local photographer Mary Ellen Bartley), all in a muted palette.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19furniture/19furniture-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19furniture/19furniture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19furniture/19furniture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Made to Order, by Ruby Beets', 'kicker': 'Currents | Furniture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Made to Order, by Ruby Beets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:31:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b947d9e-5ba1-5330-8142-4585ae2def0a', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b947d9e-5ba1-5330-8142-4585ae2def0a'}, {'abstract': 'Presenting some basics of modern technology that may help any BlackBerry or PC user.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/technology/personaltech/19pogue.html', 'snippet': 'Presenting some basics of modern technology that may help any BlackBerry or PC user.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every time a reader asks me a basic question, struggles with a computer or lets a cellphone keep ringing at a performance, I have the same thought: There ought to be a license to use technology.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/business/POGUE/POGUE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/business/POGUE/POGUE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/business/POGUE/POGUE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ins and Outs of Using Gadgetry', 'kicker': 'State of the Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ins and Outs Of Using Gadgetry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T22:32:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e2206f3-d6b2-55c3-9f39-9aa0ad5d5b6b', 'word_count': 1309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e2206f3-d6b2-55c3-9f39-9aa0ad5d5b6b'}, {'abstract': 'A task light with sleek styling and a tiny eco-footprint.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/garden/cool-and-eco-friendly-task-lighting-currents.html', 'snippet': 'A task light with sleek styling and a tiny eco-footprint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dash task light, a collaboration between the London-based architects Foster & Partners and designers at the office furniture company Steelcase, combines sleek styling with a tiny eco-footprint.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19lighting/Currents-7-1305578100117-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19lighting/19lighting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/19/garden/19lighting/19lighting-thumbStandard.j