le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/6c73903b-f38e-5e68-b766-2e716ae12058', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/6c73903b-f38e-5e68-b766-2e716ae12058'}, {'abstract': 'Can composers embrace politics in their work without music taking a back seat to message?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/until-the-next-revolution/', 'snippet': 'Can composers embrace politics in their work without music taking a back seat to message?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was 19 years old when I first heard the Kronos Quartet’s “Howl, U.S.A.”\xa0 The album dons as its cover Robert Mapplethorpe’s stunning portrait of a tattered, weatherworn and ever-so-slightly transparent American flag.\xa0 The image seemed symbolic of the music — political works by Scott Johnson, Michael Daugherty, Harry Partch (arr. Ben Johnston) and Lee Hyla — which struck me as an honest, direct and sometimes difficult-to-hear assessment of a country in tatters.\xa0 I was enthralled.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Until the Next Revolution', 'kicker': 'The Score', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rzewski, Frederic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-18T23:15:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David T. Little', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Little', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/08bc5ace-990a-520a-aba4-a614ad3590cb