 relatively wealthy Detroit suburb, has not escaped the financial problems of other cities across the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/us/19questions.html', 'snippet': 'Troy, Mich., a relatively wealthy Detroit suburb, has not escaped the financial problems of other cities across the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Troy, Mich., a Detroit suburb where the median family income is more than $90,000, may be one of the more unlikely American cities to find itself in fiscal distress. On paper, it is difficult to understand how this city of 80,000 is struggling: only about 2 percent of its families live below the poverty line; 92 percent of its population has a high school diploma; about half of its adults have at least a bachelor’s degree; it is one of the safest cities in the country; and the median value of a house is more than $200,000. Still, Troy’s wealth has proved poor inoculation against the financial problems of cities and states across the country. John Szerlag, Troy’s city manager, discusses.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scraping and Cutting to Find $22 Million', 'kicker': 'Asked and Answered | John Szerlag', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scraping and Cutting To Find $22 Million', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Property Taxes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T00:33:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'la