yt://article/8e684a1e-1e5c-57ba-a309-275acc87c054'}, {'abstract': 'Israel’s military attaché in Russia was expelled this week on suspicion of spying, though Israel added that its own investigation into the allegations had found them to be baseless.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/middleeast/19expel.html', 'snippet': 'Israel’s military attaché in Russia was expelled this week on suspicion of spying, though Israel added that its own investigation into the allegations had found them to be baseless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israel’s military attaché in Russia was expelled this week on suspicion of spying, the Israeli Defense Ministry announced on Wednesday, adding that its own investigation into the allegations had found them to be baseless.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel Bares Russia Spy Claim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israel Bares Russia Spy Claim', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T01:47:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ece62bd-d781-5c2c-91ea-65a8b8130acd', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ece62bd-d781-5c2c-91ea-65a8b8130acd'}, {'abstract': 'Community Roots Charter School in Brooklyn has been seen as a success, but its plans to add sixth, seventh and eighth grades caused a stir.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/nyregion/upset-over-community-roots-charter-schools-expansion.html', 'snippet': 'Community Roots Charter School in Brooklyn has been seen as a success, but its plans to add sixth, seventh and eighth grades caused a stir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a parent of a student who started in the inaugural first-grade class at Community Roots Charter School in Brooklyn in 2006, Chris Thompson has watched his son grow up in an elementary school that has transformed itself from an experiment in progressive education to one of District 13’s most popular destinations for young families.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Upset Over Community Roots Charter School’s Expansion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Battle for Space by Charter and Traditional Schools Upsets an Expansion Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Charter Schools', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T01:48:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoffrey Decker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Decker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02c57ab9-85ad-5816-96bd-23d60d772ce7', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02c57ab9-85ad-5816-96bd-23d60d772ce7'}, {'abstract': 'There were conflicting reports on Wednesday about a shooting at the border between Georgia and South Ossetia, a breakaway enclave under Russia’s military protection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/europe/19briefs-georgiaenclave.html', 'snippet': 'There were conflicting reports on Wednesday about a shooting at the border between Georgia and South Ossetia, a breakaway enclave under Russia’s military protection.', 'lead_paragraph': ' There were conflicting reports on Wednesday about a shooting at the border between Georgia and South Ossetia, a breakaway enclave under Russia’s military protection. The government of Georgia accused “occupation forces” of firing on unarmed civilians it said were gathering herbs near the boundary, wounding two, one of them seriously. But South Ossetian officials said that members of the enclave’s K.G.B., which patrols the boundary, discovered a group of Georgian youths inside the border. According to Res, an Ossetian news service, a firefight was begun from the Georgian side and Georgian citizens were arrested. South Ossetia gained de facto independence in 2008 after Russia and Georgia fought a short war over it and another rebellious territory. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: Conflicting Reports Surround Shooting on Border With an Enclave', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: Conflicting Reports Surround Shooting on Border With an Enclave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Ossetia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T01:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/41152316-cd30-5e1e-87f0-89b171458a5f', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41152316-cd30-5e1e-87f0-89b171458a5f'}, {'abstract': 'A government agency in Beijing has said it will investigate a company that tried to open a luxury private club inside the Forbidden City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/asia/19briefs-chinaclub.html', 'snippet': 'A government agency in Beijing has said it will investigate a company that tried to open a luxury private club inside the Forbidden City.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A government agency in Beijing has said it will investigate a company that tried to open a luxury private club inside the Forbidden City, according to a report on the Web site of People’s Daily, the official newspaper of the Chinese Communist Party. The agency, the Beijing Administration of Industry and Commerce, is looking into wrongdoing by the Forbidden City Cultural Development Company, which had been charged by the palace’s management office with organizing salons and lectures. Instead, the company began handing out “membership sign-up forms” without telling the managers, a palace spokesman said. The company reportedly wanted to sign up 500 members at a charge of one million renminbi each, or about $154,000. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Company That Tried to Open Forbidden City Club to Be Investigated', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Company That Tried to Open Forbidden City Club to Be Investigated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T01:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf5477cb-972c-52d1-8609-f4c76a0d6b7d', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf5477cb-972c-52d1-8609-f4c76a0d6b7d'}, {'abstract': 'A frosty Thursday from Paula Gamache.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/winner/', 'snippet': 'A frosty Thursday from Paula Gamache.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THURSDAY’S PUZZLE — Hey there, come on in and have a seat. Make yourself comfortable. May I get you a refreshing beverage? How about a nice, tall cold one? There’s plenty of stuff to cool you down in Paula Gamache’s puzzle. We’ll just chisel some off and put it in our drinks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday: Three Time Tour de France Winner', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6963b03d-191f-5558-9b56-89e1b4bc73a2', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6963b03d-191f-5558-9b56-89e1b4bc73a2'}, {'abstract': 'Tell your story in your own Vows video. <a href=" http://www.nytimes.com/ref/fashion/weddings/howtosubmitwedding.html"> Click here</a> for more information. Here are some of our first and best submissions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/fashion/weddings/Vows-user-gen.html', 'snippet': 'Tell your story in your own Vows video. <a href=" http://www.nytimes.com/ref/fashion/weddings/howtosubmitwedding.html"> Click here</a> for more information. Here are some of our first and best submissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tell your story in your own Vows video. <a href=" http://www.nytimes.com/ref/fashion/weddings/howtosubmitwedding.html"> Click here</a> for more information. Here are some of our first and best submissions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Produce Your Own Vows', 'kicker': 'Weddings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7335f443-ce04-5ba6-b600-f96071267be6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7335f443-ce04-5ba6-b600-f96071267be6'}, {'abstract': 'On Saturday, at Brisbane’s Doomben track, Black Caviar blitzed a field of top sprinters to record her 13th consecutive win and confirm her Timeform rating as the word’s best horse.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/18/the-worlds-best-horse-shes-in-australia/', 'snippet': 'On Saturday, at Brisbane’s Doomben track, Black Caviar blitzed a field of top sprinters to record her 13th consecutive win and confirm her Timeform rating as the word’s best horse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kerry Lonergan has kept his bookmaker driving a Mercedes Benz for years. He’s also had shares in many slow racehorses, and like all owners, remains convinced that the next one he buys will be the next Secretariat. He lives in Brisbane, Australia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The World’s Best Horse? She’s in Australia', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerry Lonergan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lonergan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec91b4ce-10b2-59e8-adfb-6019ffa377fe', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec91b4ce-10b2-59e8-adfb-6019ffa377fe'}, {'abstract': 'Brazilian government officials said that they would crack down on cattle ranchers in the Amazon after new data showed that deforestation there had increased by 26 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/americas/19briefs-Brazil.html', 'snippet': 'Brazilian government officials said that they would crack down on cattle ranchers in the Amazon after new data showed that deforestation there had increased by 26 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Brazilian government officials said Wednesday that they would crack down on cattle ranchers in the Amazon after new data showed that deforestation there had increased by 26 percent in the nine months that ended in April, compared to with the same period a year before. The data showed to that about 710 square miles had been deforested in that time. Izabella Teixeira, Brazil’s environment minister, called the jump “unacceptable” and vowed to seize the cattle of farmers who are practicing deforestation to cultivate new pastureland. Ibama, Brazil’s environmental protection agency, more than doubled police operations this year intended to deter deforestation. Despite those efforts, Mato Grosso, Brazil’s leading soybean-producing state, saw a 43 percent increase in deforestation in the nine-month period that ended in April, compared to with the same period last year. Leading environmentalists said some farmers had cut down trees because they expected they would be allowed to do so anyway under a bill being considered in Brazil’s Congress to ease restrictions on the amount of land that needed to remain forested. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil: Government Vows Crackdown on Cattle Ranchers in the Amazon', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | THE AMERICAS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil: Government Vows Crackdown On Cattle Ranchers in the Amazon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cattle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:03:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexei Barrionuevo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrionuevo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2193eb5-ade1-5482-a492-1ecb8275505a', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2193eb5-ade1-5482-a492-1ecb8275505a'}, {'abstract': 'Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce of Jamaica and Carmelita Jeter of the United States, who was ranked No. 1 in the world in the 100 last year, will compete in the 200 on June 11 at the Adidas Grand Prix on Randalls Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/sports/field-in-womens-200-bolstered-for-grand-prix.html', 'snippet': 'Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce of Jamaica and Carmelita Jeter of the United States, who was ranked No. 1 in the world in the 100 last year, will compete in the 200 on June 11 at the Adidas Grand Prix on Randalls Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce of Jamaica, the reigning world and Olympic champion in the 100 meters, and Carmelita Jeter of the United States, who was ranked No. 1 in the world in the 100 last year, will compete in the 200 on June 11 at the Adidas Grand Prix on Randalls Island, meet organizers announced. Allyson Felix of the United States and Sherone Simpson of Jamaica had already entered.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Field in Women’s 200 Bolstered for Grand Prix', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Track and Field', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Field in Women’s 200 Bolstered for Grand Prix', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Track and Field', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fraser, Shelly-Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Carmelita', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By NYT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nyt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e0d6aa-1fe2-5ce6-ae51-e7f24dfc6287', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e0d6aa-1fe2-5ce6-ae51-e7f24dfc6287'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators will be assigned to monitor the city’s traffic courts to determine whether police officers are intentionally losing cases as favors to colleagues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/nyregion/new-unit-will-monitor-traffic-courts-for-ticket-fixing-favors.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators will be assigned to monitor the city’s traffic courts to determine whether police officers are intentionally losing cases as favors to colleagues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Investigators from the Internal Affairs Bureau will be assigned to monitor the city’s traffic courts in an effort to determine whether police officers are intentionally losing cases as favors to colleagues, police officials said Wednesday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Police Unit Will Monitor City Courts for Ticket-Fixing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Police Unit Will Monitor City Courts for Ticket-Fixing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality and Misconduct', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15cfb9b5-11e9-5296-b9b7-7c774b1a3386', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15cfb9b5-11e9-5296-b9b7-7c774b1a3386'}, {'abstract': 'Initiatives from the CW network will let advertisers follow viewers onto social media Web sites and reward them for watching.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/business/media/19adco.html', 'snippet': 'Initiatives from the CW network will let advertisers follow viewers onto social media Web sites and reward them for watching.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT almost every turn this week, television networks are talking about being liked — not only in the ratings, but also on Facebook and Twitter.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/business/Adco1/Adco1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 593, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Networks Try a Social Media Spin at the Upfronts', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Networks Try A Social Spin At the Upfronts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Univision', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shopkick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CW Television Network', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TBS Network', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roeding, Cyriac', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter and Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39dbed8e-84c5-58b3-8fa0-78d5bc30e4eb', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39dbed8e-84c5-58b3-8fa0-78d5bc30e4eb'}, {'abstract': 'Libyan officials appear to have settled on a middle course, part denial and part admission, that Shukri Ghanem may have abandoned the government’s cause.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/world/africa/19minister.html', 'snippet': 'Libyan officials appear to have settled on a middle course, part denial and part admission, that Shukri Ghanem may have abandoned the government’s cause.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya — After the NATO bombing and the rebel forces that have pushed forward at Misurata, only 130 miles from Tripoli, the greatest threat facing the Libyan government may lie in high-level defections that undermine morale and set senior officials to wondering who may be next to abandon Colonel Muammer el-Qaddafi’s elite.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Status of Libya’s Oil Minister Remains Uncertain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Status of Libya’s Oil Minister Remains Uncertain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defectors (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T02:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'o