ow as the civilian-military divide.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Military Covenant’ for America', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T14:43:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Don Gomez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Don', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gomez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/056fa382-1f7a-5a7d-a59a-3bb90f066224', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/056fa382-1f7a-5a7d-a59a-3bb90f066224'}, {'abstract': 'Speaking on Fox’s “Hannity” program, Sarah Palin accused Newt Gingrich of retreating from what she said were clearly his true beliefs about the Republican budget plan.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/palin-accuses-gingrich-of-caving-to-the-media/', 'snippet': 'Speaking on Fox’s “Hannity” program, Sarah Palin accused Newt Gingrich of retreating from what she said were clearly his true beliefs about the Republican budget plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watch the latest video at video.foxnews.com', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Palin Accuses Gingrich of Caving to the Media', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T14:43:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a42e685-1db1-5bfb-89d6-c5fa5e203857', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a42e685-1db1-5bfb-89d6-c5fa5e203857'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s magazine, Daniel Bergner writes about the Metropolitan Opera’s annual National Council Auditions.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/the-american-idol-of-opera/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s magazine, Daniel Bergner writes about the Metropolitan Opera’s annual National Council Auditions.', 'lead_paragraph': "In this week’s magazine, Daniel Bergner writes about the “American Idol” of opera — the Metropolitan Opera’s annual National Council Auditions. These tryouts are open to anyone between 20 and 30 and take place at venues across the country. This year more than 1,200 aspiring singers took part. Daniel started reporting last fall when he sat in on some of the early rounds. One enduring memory was his time in Lexington, Ky., where 30-odd singers were auditioning in a small theater at the University of Kentucky. Before the proceedings began, the crowd — voice teachers and coaches, friends, family — “stood up and sang a tremendously rousing version of ‘The Star Spangled Banner.'”", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘American Idol’ of Opera', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T14:44:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cbb033c-eeff-5c20-b86a-0174cf1ac30f', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cbb033c-eeff-5c20-b86a-0174cf1ac30f'}, {'abstract': 'A once-popular Swedish liqueur is coming back.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/how-about-a-nice-swedish-punsch/', 'snippet': 'A once-popular Swedish liqueur is coming back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The passion for resurrecting pre-Prohibition cocktails has helped fetch out of history’s dustbin several forgotten elixirs, including Crème Yvette, allspice dram, orange bitters and, most famously, absinthe. The latest one to be rehabilitated is Swedish punsch. Beginning this summer, the sweet liqueur will return to liquor stores courtesy of Eric Seed, the owner of Haus Alpenz, a Minnesota-based importing company that specializes in unique and arcane liquors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How About a Nice Swedish Punsch?', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T14:50:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Simonson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simonson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba828eba-df80-5fba-a1c5-57260414ced9', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba828eba-df80-5fba-a1