ns', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T15:36:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfd0c34b-ec52-5ab0-8282-a9550a39ede6', 'word_count': 54, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfd0c34b-ec52-5ab0-8282-a9550a39ede6'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s podcast includes a discussion about the ENIAC Programmers Project, cloud storage and Slacker Radio.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/tech-talk-podcast-female-programmers-in-history/', 'snippet': 'This week’s podcast includes a discussion about the ENIAC Programmers Project, cloud storage and Slacker Radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Contrary to popular stereotype, computer programmers have not always been caffeine-fueled men in T-shirts coding deep into the night. In fact, some of the earliest programmers were female mathematicians, originally recruited to calculate ballistics trajectories for the Army during World War II.\xa0 Several of these pioneering women went on to program ENIAC, the world’s first all-electronic computer. In this week’s Tech Talk podcast, Bettina Edelstein speaks with Kathy Kleiman of the ENIAC Programmers Project about their valuable contributions to computing history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Female Programmers in History', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T15:41:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d286eb7f-3611-57b0-af9a-f56ef7200266', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d286eb7f-3611-57b0-af9a-f56ef7200266'}, {'abstract': 'eMusic on Thursday will begin the last phase of its integration of the major label catalogs when it adds 15,000 albums on EMI.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/an-indie-music-retailer-embraces-the-mainstream/', 'snippet': 'eMusic on Thursday will begin the last phase of its integration of the major label catalogs when it adds 15,000 albums on EMI.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s the challenge faced by every indie band that ventures up to the big leagues: can it stay cool — and independent — once it goes into business with the major record labels?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Indie Music Retailer Embraces the Mainstream', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'eMusic.com Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T15:55:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/346571ec-864f-5542-b795-81aefb247e96', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/346571ec-864f-5542-b795-81aefb247e96'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Sisario and Bruce Headlam on the sale of Warner Music Group; Jon Pareles on the muchness of Lady Gaga; new CD releases by Raphael Saadiq, Colin Vallon and Telebossa. Ben Ratliff, host.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/201