s', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milan (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T20:51:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ingrid K. Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ingrid', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bebe6164-2726-53a6-bfba-0ef5316bc579', 'word_count': 1428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bebe6164-2726-53a6-bfba-0ef5316bc579'}, {'abstract': 'Life imitates art again in the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/calling-a-rape-a-rape/', 'snippet': 'Life imitates art again in the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading the story in The Times earlier this week about Tristane Banon, a 31-year-old journalist who said that Dominique Strauss-Kahn tried to rape her during an interview in 2002, I was struck by the conversation she had soon after with her mother, who discouraged her from filing charges against a powerful man who was a friend of the family. As Banon’s mother, Anne Mansouret, a Socialist Party official, recounted the conversation, she told her daughter: “Listen, you know, if he had raped you, I wouldn’t have any hesitation, but that wasn’t the case. He sexually assaulted you, there wasn’t any rape per se; so until the end of your life, you’re going to have on your résumé, you know, Tristane Banon is the girl who. …” Banon ultimately decided not to press charges.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Calling a Rape a Rape', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T20:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bb72947-4ce9-51ca-bb24-25a00b8d6a91', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bb72947-4ce9-51ca-bb24-25a00b8d6a91'}, {'abstract': 'If a handful of companies refused to authorize online credit card payments for spam-advertised products, the money supporting the system would be cut off, a researcher said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/technology/20spam.html', 'snippet': 'If a handful of companies refused to authorize online credit card payments for spam-advertised products, the money supporting the system would be cut off, a researcher said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, a team of computer scientists at two University of California campuses has been looking deeply into the nature of spam, the billions of unwanted e-mail messages generated by networks of zombie computers controlled by the rogue programs called botnets. They even coined a term, “spamalytics,” to describe their work.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Sees Way to Win Spam Fight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Sees Way to Win Spam Fight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spam (Electronic Mail)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T20:51:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Markoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Markoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ece81758-c4aa-5477-968d-8f656c1b7a5e', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ece81758-c4aa-5477-968d-8f656c1b7a5e'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese characters carved and painted by Xu Bing are displayed at the Morgan Libray & Museum; the Brooklyn Museum plans to host “Hide/Seek,” the controversial portraiture exhibition; and Italian-influenced works from the Sonnabend collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/design/chinese-characters-and-matisse-at-the-morgan-hideseek-heads-to-the-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese characters carved and painted by Xu Bing are displayed at the Morgan Libray & Museum; the Brooklyn Museum plans to host “Hide/Seek,” the controversial portraiture exhibition; and Italian-influenced works from the Sonnabend collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A “floating, iridescent cloud of calligraphy” is how the conceptual artist Xu Bing describes “The Living Word,” his installation of about 400 carved and painted acrylic Chinese characters. This summer, “The Living Word 3” — the latest version in a series that began in 2001 — will rise to the highest point of the Morgan Library & Museum’s glass atrium.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20vogel/vogel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20vogel/vogel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20vogel/vogel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbn