hts to Remember, and Nightmares', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Proms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:26:39+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/bec381b4-b26e-5a1e-ad9d-47a3dee60c36', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/bec381b4-b26e-5a1e-ad9d-47a3dee60c36'}, {'abstract': 'The Seattle band Fleet Foxes called up blissful memories in concerts at the United Palace in Washington Heights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/music/fleet-foxes-at-united-palace-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Seattle band Fleet Foxes called up blissful memories in concerts at the United Palace in Washington Heights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The oohs have it — along with that ahs, ohs, and whoas — in songs by Fleet Foxes, a band formed in Seattle that started a two-night stand at the United Palace on Wednesday night. Sooner or later, nearly every Fleet Foxes song gives way to a stretch of wordless vocal harmonies shared by three, four, perhaps five voices, moving together in chords or interweaving in counterpoint. Those harmonies are many things at once: a show of skill and preparation, an offering of sweetness, a utopian communal project and, perhaps most of all, a temporary paradise, a refuge from any kind of discord. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20FLEET-span/FLEET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20FLEET-span/FLEET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20FLEET-span/FLEET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20FLEET-span/FLEET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20FLEET-span/FLEET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Close to Eden, Where the Music Is Artisanal', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Close to Eden, Where the Music Is Artisanal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fleet Foxes (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:27:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcebf899-59d5-51a0-bf97-9b6052b35f28', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcebf899-59d5-51a0-bf97-9b6052b35f28'}, {'abstract': 'New York City Ballet performed Balanchine’s “Apollo” and “Sonnambula” and Robbins’s “Afternoon of a Faun” and “Antique Epigraphs” on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-performs-balanchine-robbins-review.html', 'snippet': 'New York City Ballet performed Balanchine’s “Apollo” and “Sonnambula” and Robbins’s “Afternoon of a Faun” and “Antique Epigraphs” on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some things that happened for the first time seem to be happening again. The first three ballets of Wednesday’s program at New York City Ballet — George Balanchine’s “Apollo,” and Jerome Robbins’s “Afternoon of a Faun” and “Antique Epigraphs” — all address different aspects of innocence, with the world seeming new; it’s heartening to see them all in good shape. The fourth, Balanchine’s “Sonnambula,” alternately suggests a society both old and sophisticated, death and a dark form of transcendence. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/APOLLO/APOLLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/APOLLO/APOLLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/APOLLO/APOLLO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/APOLLO/APOLLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/APOLLO/APOLLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Classical World of Apollos and Fauns to a Romantic Passion', 'kicker': 'Dance Review | New York City Ballet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From the Classical World of Apollos and Fauns to a Romantic Passion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:29:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/91fe3b8c-afbc-5d1e-b24a-1c08fe285dc5', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91fe3b8c-afbc-5d1e-b24a-1c08fe285dc5'}, {'abstract': 'The author’s tour of Iowa takes him to a Catholic Church, Iowa’s only milling bakery and Cherokee County, the 2,229th most populous county in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/from-thornton-to-sioux-city/', 'snippet': 'The author’s tour of Iowa takes him to a Catholic Church, Iowa’s only milling bakery and Cherokee County, the 2,229th most populous county in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I spent yesterday with Linus Solberg, a soybean farmer in Cylinder, Iowa, no doubt ruining his day – he was trying to get his beans in the ground – and compressing the rest of mine. But it’s impossible to leave when someone is friendly, warm and funny. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Thornton to Sioux City', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5130fb9d-350a-50b0-af7f-ba2213e745c9', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5130fb9d-350a-50b0-af7f-ba2213e745c9'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists counting bowhead whales in Arctic Alaska have a rare opportunity to observe polar bears in their natural habitat.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/in-polar-bear-territory/', 'snippet': 'Scientists counting bowhead whales in Arctic Alaska have a rare opportunity to observe polar bears in their natural habitat.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Kate Stafford, an oceanographer at the Applied Physics Laboratory at the University of Washington, writes from Alaska, where she is participating in a visual census of bowhead whales.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Polar Bear Territory', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polar Bears', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Stafford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stafford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/980773a2-f822-59da-9126-7a37c1a7a7b3', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/980773a2-f822-59da-9126-7a37c1a7a7b3'}, {'abstract': 'Judy Rosenblatt, starring in this revival about Peggy Guggenheim, a rich woman who did what she wanted, has the most important quality for the solo role: attitude.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/theater/reviews/woman-before-a-glass-peggy-guggenheim-review.html', 'snippet': 'Judy Rosenblatt, starring in this revival about Peggy Guggenheim, a rich woman who did what she wanted, has the most important quality for the solo role: attitude.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peggy Guggenheim did what she wanted, which was fairly unusual for women born in 1898. A rich New Yorker (although not as rich as some of the other Guggenheims), she married twice and had children but devoted most of her adult life to her modern-art collection and to love affairs, often with men who made art.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'She Loved Herself, Yes, but She Also Loved Art', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Woman Before a Glass'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She Loved Herself, Yes, But She Also Loved Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pendleton, Austin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robertson, Lanie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Peggy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:51:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/43b3ed2a-15dd-5eda-8740-8dfa676d8d18', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43b3ed2a-15dd-5eda-8740-8dfa676d8d18'}, {'abstract': 'Related by marriage and co-op board; a condo owner rents a unit; after the death of a stabilized tenant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/realestate/q-a.html', 'snippet': 'Related by marriage and co-op board; a condo owner rents a unit; after the death of a stabilized tenant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Related by Marriage and Co-op Board', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q & A', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Q & A', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rent Control and Stabilization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooperatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:54:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Romano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/36a271fc-abda-5a80-aae7-4cddab5381e6', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36a271fc-abda-5a80-aae7-4cddab5381e6'}, {'abstract': 'A “senior move manager” assists with the demands of relocating from a longtime family home into an apartment, age-restricted condo or assisted-living facility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/realestate/help-for-the-older-downsizer-in-the-regionlong-island.html', 'snippet': 'A “senior move manager” assists with the demands of relocating from a longtime family home into an apartment, age-restricted condo or assisted-living facility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JAY GOLDSTEIN and his wife, Carrie, accumulated 15,000 books in the 46 years they lived\xa0in the Glen Cove split ranch where they raised their four children. Its sixth bedroom became the second story of a library. All sorts of memorabilia and other hard-to-toss belongings accumulated in the attic and basement. \xa0In February, when their home went into contract for $550,000, deciding what to take, what to dispose of and how to lay out their new $8,050-a-month two-bedroom rental apartment at the Atria on Roslyn Harbor, an independent senior community in Roslyn, seemed overwhelming.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/lizo/lizo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/lizo/lizo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/lizo/lizo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/lizo/lizo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/lizo/lizo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leave the Stuff, Keep the Memories', 'kicker': 'In the Region | Long Island', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leave the Stuff, Keep the Memories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Moving and Moving Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-19T21:55:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcelle S. Fischler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcelle', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fischler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d43223-2050-58c3-97d5-412251bf1e59', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d43223-2050-58c3-97d5-412251bf1e59'}, {'abstract': 'Brokers have a formula for motivated sellers to live by: Assign the house’s flaws a dollar value and subtract that sum from the price.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/realestate/in-house-pricing-own-up-to-flaws-in-the-regionwestchester.html', 'snippet': 'Brokers have a formula for motivated sellers to live by: Assign the house’s flaws a dollar value and subtract that sum from the price.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BROKERS say conventional wisdom still holds: Price a house right, and do so from the start. But even when a seller is willing to follow that advice, it’s not always obvious what the right price is. The challenge seems to lie in accurately assessing what factors might make a house less than immediately saleable — and in knowing just how much money those negative factors should chop from the price tag.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/wczo1/wczo1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/realestate/wczo1/wczo1-articleLarge.jpg',