 importance of network news.', 'lead_paragraph': '“CBS Evening News” bade farewell to its anchor, Katie Couric, on Thursday with a five-minute highlights reel and a rendition of the Beatles ballad “In My Life,” but there wasn’t much sadness on the set. Ms. Couric, brisk and dry-eyed, looked almost relieved as she thanked viewers for, as she put it, “coming along with me on this incredible journey.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/us/subWATCH/subWATCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/us/subWATCH/subWATCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/us/subWATCH/subWATCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quiet Departure Is Stark Contrast to Heralded Arrival', 'kicker': 'The TV Watch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quiet Departure Is Stark Contrast to Heralded Arrival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Couric, Katie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T02:50:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0adbffd-0025-5214-9bc5-2e0263db7909', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0adbffd-0025-5214-9bc5-2e0263db7909'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon.com said that it sold 105 e-books for every 100 printed books, a milestone that its chief executive, Jeff Bezos, said had occurred sooner than expected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/technology/20amazon.html', 'snippet': 'Amazon.com said that it sold 105 e-books for every 100 printed books, a milestone that its chief executive, Jeff Bezos, said had occurred sooner than expected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest chapter in the unfolding tale of the book evolution from ink to pixels, Amazon.com said Thursday that its customers now buy more e-books than print books. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'E-Books Outsell Print Books at Amazon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'E-Books Outsell Print Books at Amazon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T02:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller and Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4f2de3c-a961-5f8d-b64e-ae4e469a8a8d', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4f2de3c-a961-5f8d-b64e-ae4e469a8a8d'}, {'abstract': 'The American Classical Orchestra revived André-Ernest Modeste Grétry’s “Richard Coeur de Lion” at the New York Society for Ethical Culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/music/richard-coeur-de-lion-review.html', 'snippet': 'The American Classical Orchestra revived André-Ernest Modeste Grétry’s “Richard Coeur de Lion” at the New York Society for Ethical Culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why certain composers who were popular in their day become marginalized over time is often inexplicable. Somehow this happens especially in opera, perhaps because opera composers so often tailored their works to the specific tastes of the theaters and audiences for which they were writing. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/Lionheart/Lionheart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviving a Rarity, Right on Time', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reviving a Rarity, Right on Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crawford, Thomas Charles (1956- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Society for Ethical Culture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Classical Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T02:59:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/97908d38-2fc3-5bd1-ac7b-210586ac952c', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97908d38-2fc3-5bd1-ac7b-210586ac952c'}, {'abstract': 'The decision to wind down and restructure its business is a surprising reversal of fortune for FrontPoint, which had appeared to have weathered the scandal.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/19/frontpoint-to-shut-most-funds-after-insider-charges/', 'snippet': 'The decision to wind down and restructure its business is a surprising reversal of fortune for FrontPoint, which had appeared to have weathered the scandal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FrontPoint Partners, once a multibillion-dollar hedge fund before it was battered by allegations of insider trading, will shut down most of its funds by the end of the month.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'FrontPoint to Shut Most Funds After Insider Trading Charges', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman and Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e91f3e7b-24d3-567e-aeab-bbae9f73edbf', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e91f3e7b-24d3-567e-aeab-bbae9f73edbf'}, {'abstract': 'The clarinetist David Shifrin and the pianist Gloria Chien performed Debussy’s Rhapsody for Clarinet and Piano in a Lyric Chamber Music Society of New York concert.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/music/clarinetist-david-shifrin-with-lyric-chamber-music-society-review.html', 'snippet': 'The clarinetist David Shifrin and the pianist Gloria Chien performed Debussy’s Rhapsody for Clarinet and Piano in a Lyric Chamber Music Society of New York concert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introducing a performance of Debussy’s Rhapsody for Clarinet and Piano at the Kosciuszko Foundation on East 65th Street in Manhattan on Wednesday night, the distinguished clarinetist David Shifrin mused to the audience that Debussy probably first heard the piece played in a similarly intimate space. While listening to Mr. Shifrin’s seductive warble and the pianist Gloria Chien’s elegant ripples and shadings, your eyes might wander across warm, wood-paneled walls bedecked with old oil paintings; an ornately sculptured ceiling; and vintage tapestries hung over the windows.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/LYRIC/LYRIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/LYRIC/LYRIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/LYRIC/LYRIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/LYRIC/LYRIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/LYRIC/LYRIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Debussy Flows in Ripples and Seductive Warbles', 'kicker': 'Music Review | Lyric Chamber Music Society of New York', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debussy Flows in Ripples And Seductive Warbles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lyric Chamber Music Society', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shifrin, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/43b191ea-0313-586e-bd57-4d282f4064ac', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43b191ea-0313-586e-bd57-4d282f4064ac'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Motian is leading a kind of tribute band at the Village Vanguard this week, paying homage to the Modern Jazz Quartet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/music/paul-motian-quartets-tribute-to-modern-jazz-quartet-review.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Motian is leading a kind of tribute band at the Village Vanguard this week, paying homage to the Modern Jazz Quartet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One way jazz can seem from the outside is as a never-ending eulogy. This week in New York there are or have been concerts paying homage to or performing the repertory of: Count Basie, Clifford Jordan, Thelonious Monk, Dizzy Gillespie, Miles Davis and the Modern Jazz Quartet. It’s just an average week. Is that O.K.? Is that strange?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20MOTIAN-span/MOTIAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20MOTIAN-span/MOTIAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20MOTIAN-span/MOTIAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20MOTIAN-span/MOTIAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20MOTIAN-span/MOTIAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'School’s in Session, but Don’t You Dare Take Notes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'School’s in Session, but Don’t You Dare Take Notes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:02:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ff88d4a-b4c5-5d41-b53e-5b05251d2ccb', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ff88d4a-b4c5-5d41-b53e-5b05251d2ccb'}, {'abstract': 'Betrayal, lust and suicide are among the “adult themes” in a very early Mozart opera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/music/mozarts-mitridate-by-little-opera-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Betrayal, lust and suicide are among the “adult themes” in a very early Mozart opera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mozart’s opera “Mitridate, Re di Ponto,” had its premiere in Milan on Dec. 26, 1770. A month later, Mozart turned 15.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20LITTLE/LITTLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20LITTLE/LITTLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/arts/20LITTLE/LITTLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Milan to New York in 240 Years', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Milan to New York in 240 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:04:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8504f54-a443-5105-94ff-52220fd6b2dd', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8504f54-a443-5105-94ff-52220fd6b2dd'}, {'abstract': 'Now the New York Public Library is creating iPad apps that bring its research collections into “the palms of the public’s hand.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/design/new-york-public-library-introduces-ipad-apps.html', 'snippet': 'Now the New York Public Library is creating iPad apps that bring its research collections into “the palms of the public’s hand.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here an app, there an app, everywhere an apt app. Now the New York Public Library is creating iPad apps that bring its research collections into “the palms of the public’s hand,” as library officials put it in a statement. Biblion: The Boundless Library is the name for a series of apps available on Apple’s tablet computer that highlight different elements in the library’s collection. It was developed by the library and the design firm Potion. The first edition showcases the library’s holdings about the 1939-40 New York World’s Fair, which are among those most consulted by researchers. Users will be able to turn over, zoom in on and roam through hundreds of items. At the end of this week the library is putting out an app that lets the public play Find That Future, from the game designer Jane McGonigal. This summer, look for an app enabling people to reserve books. The first app is available free at Apple’s iTunes store and nypl.org.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Public Library Introduces iPad Apps', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Public Library Introduces iPad Apps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ef85bd4-df0c-5c5c-b29c-e7c9294fdedb', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ef85bd4-df0c-5c5c-b29c-e7c9294fdedb'}, {'abstract': 'The family of Anton Hammerl, a South African photojournalist who has been missing since early last month, said that they think he died after being shot on April 5 in the desert.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/world/africa/20journalist.html', 'snippet': 'The family of Anton Hammerl, a South African photojournalist who has been missing since early last month, said that they think he died after being shot on April 5 in the desert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The family of Anton Hammerl, a South African photojournalist who has been missing in Libya since early last month, said late Thursday that they think he died after being shot on April 5 in the desert.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missing Photographer in Libya Is Presumed Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missing Photographer is Presumed Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:11:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96926cf9-95f1-55ec-a957-51953df70809', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96926cf9-95f1-55ec-a957-51953df70809'}, {'abstract': 'A member of the Madoff family is telling all in a memoir to be released in December.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/books/widow-of-bernard-madoffs-son-signs-a-deal-for-a-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'A member of the Madoff family is telling all in a memoir to be released in December.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A member of the Madoff family is telling all in a memoir to be released in December, its publisher said on Wednesday. Stephanie Madoff-Mack, the widow of Mark Madoff, the son of Bernard L. Madoff who committed suicide last year, has sold her book to Blue Rider Press, an imprint of Penguin Group USA. Terms of the deal were not disclosed. David Rosenthal, the publisher of Blue Rider Press, called it the “first genuine inside account” from a Madoff family member, a memoir that begins with the unraveling of Mr. Madoff’s Ponzi scheme. Ms. Madoff-Mack’s story, he said in a statement, is “heartbreaking, compelling and ultimately courageous.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Widow of Bernard Madoff’s Son Signs a Deal for a Memoir', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Widow of Bernard Madoff’s Son Signs a Deal for a Memoir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:13:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0acc81c9-809e-5045-a8b2-173a358664f4', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0acc81c9-809e-5045-a8b2-173a358664f4'}, {'abstract': 'The three “American Idol” finalists still standing competed on Wednesday night for a chance to advance to next week’s finale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/arts/television/american-idol-again-by-a-mile.html', 'snippet': 'The three “American Idol” finalists still standing competed on Wednesday night for a chance to advance to next week’s finale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three “American Idol” finalists still standing (Haley Reinhart, Scotty McCreery and Lauren Alaina, below from left) competed on Wednesday night for a chance to advance to next week’s finale as 23 million viewers tuned in to Fox’s two-hour reality show broadcast, according to Nielsen’s estimates. No other network came close to matching Fox’s ratings. CBS placed second, earning its best ratings at 9 p.m. with “Criminal Minds” (12.5 million) followed by “Criminal Minds: Suspect Behavior” at 10 (8.4 million). At 8, CBS’s rerun of “Criminal Minds” (6.3 million) lagged behind ABC’s sitcom “The Middle” (7.7 million). ABC finished in third place over all, attracting 10.1 million viewers at 9 for “Modern Family” but more modest results for “Cougar Town” at 9:30 (5.4 million) and “Happy Endings” at 10 (3.8 million) and 10:30 (3.1 million). NBC was fourth, leading the 10 p.m. hour with “Law & Order: SVU” (8.7 million) but struggling earlier in the night with back-to-back episodes of “Minute to Win It” at 8 (3.5 million) and at 9 (2.9 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘American Idol’ Again, by a Mile', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘American Idol’ Again, by a Mile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:14:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/061ed2e2-d464-5b0b-b9d5-027e6f5165ea', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/061ed2e2-d464-5b0b-b9d5-027e6f5165ea'}, {'abstract': 'In case you ever doubted it, James Cameron is truly the king of the three-dimensional world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/movies/a-2012-release-date-is-set-for-a-titanic-in-3-d.html', 'snippet': 'In case you ever doubted it, James Cameron is truly the king of the three-dimensional world.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In case you ever doubted it, James Cameron is truly the king of the three-dimensional world. Or he will be when his “Titanic” is re-released by Paramount Pictures and 20th Century Fox on April 6 in 3-D. Mr. Cameron, whose 3-D “Avatar,” with almost $2.8 billion in ticket sales at the worldwide box office, is the highest-grossing film in history, will revise the record books when “Titanic,” the second-highest-grossing film of all time, with $1.8 billion in worldwide sales, returns to theaters in 3-D. Fox and Paramount joined Mr. Cameron’s Lightstorm Entertainment in announcing the re-release date on Thursday. The next frontier in 3-D may well involve not fantasies and cartoons but naturalistic dramas like “Titanic” (starring Leonardo DiCaprio and Kate Winslet, left). Steven Soderbergh, for instance, had planned to shoot his coming “Contagion,” a disease drama, in 3-D, but backed off when he couldn’t quite get the results he needed. Still, 3-D thrillers, love stories and raunchy teen comedies (shudder) clearly are just a matter of time. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A 2012 Release Date Is Set for a ‘Titanic’ in 3-D', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A 2012 Release Date Is Set for a ‘Titanic’ in 3-D', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T03:17:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'perso