material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3f0f17e-f6db-5e86-b475-1cedd9a8f932', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3f0f17e-f6db-5e86-b475-1cedd9a8f932'}, {'abstract': 'I would have never thought that an article on an air disaster would so insightfully touch on so many aspects of the human heart and mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/the-deepest-end.html', 'snippet': 'I would have never thought that an article on an air disaster would so insightfully touch on so many aspects of the human heart and mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wil S. Hylton’s article on Air France Flight 447 was informative, fair and moving. I would have never thought that an article on an air disaster would so insightfully touch on so many aspects of the human heart and mind. Hylton’s exploration captured in words what the cover photograph so chillingly represented to me: the unfathomable scope of nature’s capacity to challenge man’s quest to advance and the heartbreaking tragedies that will invariably accompany that contest.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Deepest End', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Deepest End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Tina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/46312dd7-a452-52ae-99ba-0fd3f4be1786', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46312dd7-a452-52ae-99ba-0fd3f4be1786'}, {'abstract': 'Asleep at the wheel; against the grain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/the-ethicist-unsafe-at-any-speed.html', 'snippet': 'Asleep at the wheel; against the grain.', 'lead_paragraph': ' When my family rents a car, the rental company sends someone to pick us up. The driver they send has sleep apnea (as do I), and he is not getting any treatment. I haven’t seen him drive dangerously, but drivers with sleep apnea are as much as five times more likely to be involved in car accidents. I doubt I can persuade him to get treatment. I am also uncomfortable tattling on him to his employer. What should I do? A. G., MASSACHUSETTS', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unsafe at Any Speed?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'UNSAFE AT ANY SPEED?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APNEA (SLEEP DISORDER)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/6257dc68-45ab-5909-bbcf-72bd51e05fd3', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6257dc68-45ab-5909-bbcf-72bd51e05fd3'}, {'abstract': 'Germany’s Departments of Order have ways of making you dismount your bike.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/you-are-here-germanys-anti-chaos-crusaders.html', 'snippet': 'Germany’s Departments of Order have ways of making you dismount your bike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Knuth Kaufmann, a 41-year-old former accountant for the German Air Force and current member of the local Ordnungsbehörde, or Department of Order, eyed a young woman bicycling down the cobblestone lane. As she approached, he ordered her to dismount, and he reached into his pocket. Out came a yellow card with a large red circle around a picture of a bicycle, a reminder that it’s against the rules to ride in a pedestrian-only shopping zone.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22here_span/22here_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22here_span/22here_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22here_span/22here_span-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany’s Anti-Chaos Crusaders', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE ANTI-CHAOS CRUSADERS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dad0909a-4c91-53a0-a342-cbae5ab363bf', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dad0909a-4c91-53a0-a342-cbae5ab363bf'}, {'abstract': 'A gay couple, a surrogate mother and some surprising Southern hospitality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/lives-my-new-kentucky-baby.html', 'snippet': 'A gay couple, a surrogate mother and some surprising Southern hospitality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We came to Bowling Green, Ky., home of our good-humored surrogate, Gail, with a court order from California designating me and Richard — my husband in some states, though not in Kentucky — as the future baby’s legal parents. I’d been hoping to avoid Kentucky. Its laws make it seem unwelcoming to gay people and ambivalent about surrogacy. I figured that culturally it would be red-statey too, full of homophobia, guns and fatty foods. The coasts seemed safer, especially for a black man, a Jew and their black-Jewish daughter. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22lives/22lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 576, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22lives/22lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22lives/22lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My New Kentucky Baby', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My New Kentucky Baby', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surrogate Motherhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Gamson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gamson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b6f0201-27d7-52fc-a92a-6ac5986ba89f', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b6f0201-27d7-52fc-a92a-6ac5986ba89f'}, {'abstract': 'One of Asia’s biggest media moguls on the art of re-enacting scandals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/questions-for-jimmy-lai-asias-rupert-murdoch.html', 'snippet': 'One of Asia’s biggest media moguls on the art of re-enacting scandals.', 'lead_paragraph': ' You own magazines and newspapers in Hong Kong and Taiwan and have been called the Rupert Murdoch of Asia — and yet you’re best known here for your company’s very enjoyable and weird animated re-enactments of news events. The one you did for Tiger Woods’s Thanksgiving-weekend car accident got more than five million YouTube views. Why did you get into animation?  I started the animations because print media was going into a sunset business environment. It’s obvious that we have arrived at an era of images. I thought that if I could speed up the production of animation, I could make a big business out of recreating the amazing images of the news, because what we get on TV is always the last bit of image. What happened before that image is always missing. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22talk/mag-22Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 571, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22talk/22talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22talk/22talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jimmy Lai Animates the News', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'JIMMY LAI ANIMATES THE NEWS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lai, Jimmy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/516b6fab-9bf4-5b36-9554-f6532ac4c491', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/516b6fab-9bf4-5b36-9554-f6532ac4c491'}, {'abstract': 'Warning: This essay does not include the single most reprehensible, shocking, forbidden expletive in the language. Maybe it should.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/an-unspeakable-word-is-the-word-that-has-to-be-spoken.html', 'snippet': 'Warning: This essay does not include the single most reprehensible, shocking, forbidden expletive in the language. Maybe it should.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What have writers got but words? And what have people got but their own bodies to inhabit?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22riff_span/mag-22Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 119, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22riff_span/mag-22Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 119}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22riff_span/mag-22Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 244, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22riff_span/22riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/magazine/22riff_span/22riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘An Unspeakable Word Is the Word That Has to Be Spoken’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘An Unspeakable Word Is the Word That Has to Be Spoken’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indecency, Obscenity and Profanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vagina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Diski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Diski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b768a01e-c794-5e67-80df-6d2ef5242fe9', 'word_count': 1727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b768a01e-c794-5e67-80df-6d2ef5242fe9'}, {'abstract': 'Discussion about an expletive does not end with the decision against using it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/magazine/block-quote-the-case-against-cursing.html', 'snippet': 'Discussion about an expletive does not end with the decision against using it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times writes unblushingly about sexual behavior, arts censorship, science, health, crime and similar subjects, opening its columns to any newsworthy detail, however disturbing, provided the approach is dignified and the vocabulary clinical rather than coarse. In these situations, the paper rejects evasiveness and euphemism, which would be a disservice to readers who need to understand issues. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '47', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Block Quote: The Case Against Cursing', 'kicker': 'Bonus Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'BLOCK QUOTE: The Case Against Cursing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indecency, Obscenity and Profanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0de3a23-873c-5c44-8d7e-38ae9983234e', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0de3a23-873c-5c44-8d7e-38ae9983234e'}, {'abstract': 'The  conglomerate’s three-way split moves forward with the purchase by  Fila Korea and the largest private equity firm in South Korea.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/fortune-brands-sells-golf-business-for-1-23-billion/', 'snippet': 'The  conglomerate’s three-way split moves forward with the purchase by  Fila Korea and the largest private equity firm in South Korea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fortune Brands said on Friday that it had agreed to sell its golf business to a group led by the owner of the Fila sports brand for $1.23 billion in cash. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fortune Brands Sells Golf Business for $1.23 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortune Brands Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:18:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8dd086c-2a09-566c-a705-d9dab0d591d7', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8dd086c-2a09-566c-a705-d9dab0d591d7'}, {'abstract': 'The billionaire chairman of Berkshire Hathaway made a cameo appearance on NBC’s “The Office” on Thursday night to interview for a job.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/warren-buffett-regional-manager-scranton-pa/', 'snippet': 'The billionaire chairman of Berkshire Hathaway made a cameo appearance on NBC’s “The Office” on Thursday night to interview for a job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a new job in Warren E. Buffett’s future?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Warren Buffett: Regional Manager, Scranton, Pa.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buffett, Warren E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:23:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9b69e77-3d8e-55cf-aa2d-9a05dc2e3b37', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9b69e77-3d8e-55cf-aa2d-9a05dc2e3b37'}, {'abstract': 'At the age of 80, with almost 40 books behind him and nearly as many accumulated honors, Harold Bloom has written a kind of summing-up of his monumental career as a critic and scholar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-the-anatomy-of-influence-by-harold-bloom.html', 'snippet': 'At the age of 80, with almost 40 books behind him and nearly as many accumulated honors, Harold Bloom has written a kind of summing-up of his monumental career as a critic and scholar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the age of 80, with almost 40 books behind him and nearly as many accumulated honors, Harold Bloom has written, in “The Anatomy of Influence,” a kind of summing-up — or, as he puts it in his distinctive idiom, mixing irony with histrionism, “my virtual swan song,” born of his urge “to say in one place most of what I have learned to think about how influence works in imaginative literature.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/tanenhaus/tanenhaus-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/tanenhaus/tanenhaus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/tanenhaus/tanenhaus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harold Bloom: An Uncommon Reader', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Uncommon Reader', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloom, Harold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:24:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Tanenhaus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanenhaus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/60db010a-cc1c-5565-b6ba-39f841e431c8', 'word_count': 3665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60db010a-cc1c-5565-b6ba-39f841e431c8'}, {'abstract': 'Jon-Jon Goulian has a peculiar strategy for coping with physical insecurity, lofty expectations and other “pressures of modern life.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-the-man-in-the-gray-flannel-skirt-by-jon-jon-goulian.html', 'snippet': 'Jon-Jon Goulian has a peculiar strategy for coping with physical insecurity, lofty expectations and other “pressures of modern life.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jon-Jon Goulian’s eyes are beady and his head is small, at least in proportion to the rest of him. He went prematurely bald. He has seriously bowed legs and exasperating skin, inasmuch as it long served as the bumpy battleground between him and his acne. But his nose, well, that’s quite possibly the worst of him. He has had it surgically altered not once but twice, and a third intervention may well be in order. It’s no longer huge but not yet acceptable.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/BRUNI/BRUNI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 545, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/BRUNI/BRUNI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/BRUNI/BRUNI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Growing Up Anxious and Androgynous', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Body of Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ab9df2a-d743-5265-b0bc-c13960b16896', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ab9df2a-d743-5265-b0bc-c13960b16896'}, {'abstract': '“September 1972. It was the first week of eighth grade, and I sat alone near the back of the school bus: a short, scrawny honor-roll boy with small hands and big ears.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/excerpt-swim-back-to-me-by-ann-packer.html', 'snippet': '“September 1972. It was the first week of eighth grade, and I sat alone near the back of the school bus: a short, scrawny honor-roll boy with small hands and big ears.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Walk for Mankind', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Swim Back to Me’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:31:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Packer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Packer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e68aeff-a45d-55da-9864-30ee231bc1b1', 'word_count': 2121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e68aeff-a45d-55da-9864-30ee231bc1b1'}, {'abstract': 'In the stories of Ann Packer, individuals struggle against personal devastation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-swim-back-to-me-by-ann-packer.html', 'snippet': 'In the stories of Ann Packer, individuals struggle against personal devastation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oh, California, you aren’t as young as you used to be. Your years of bad decisions and fast living have finally caught up with you. Not to mention the sting of the past decade’s tumultuous relationships: that nasty breakup with Gray Davis; the fling with the former bodybuilder; and now an 11th-hour reconciliation with an ex, both of you silver at the temples and ready to be done with the drama. You’ve seen your share of crises and ruin, but it’s time to pick up the pieces.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Peelle/Peelle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Peelle/Peelle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Peelle/Peelle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ann Packer’s Unhappy Heroines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Breaking Points', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Packer, Ann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:31:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Peelle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peelle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2c5e74e-8d23-55e1-be5d-9bdc774922f1', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2c5e74e-8d23-55e1-be5d-9bdc774922f1'}, {'abstract': 'A novel ties the fate of Aaron Burr’s daughter to a cloistered community on the Outer Banks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-the-watery-part-of-the-world-by-michael-parker.html', 'snippet': 'A novel ties the fate of Aaron Burr’s daughter to a cloistered community on the Outer Banks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American history brims with stories of mysterious disappearances. We still wonder what happened to Virginia Dare, the first English infant born in this country, in 1587, in the lost Roanoke settlement of what would become North Carolina. Also unknown is the fate of a woman who disappeared in the same region generations later: Theodosia Burr Alston, daughter of Aaron Burr and wife of the governor of South Carolina, whose ship sank off the coast in January 1813.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Barton/Barton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Barton/Barton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Barton/Barton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Was Theodosia Burr?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Island of Their Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'PARKER, MICHAEL', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Barton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c365b70d-61dc-58d0-9b5b-9ed1716e9513', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c365b70d-61dc-58d0-9b5b-9ed1716e9513'}, {'abstract': '“It’s no mean feat to produce a powerful essay on a highly personal subject,” the author Alan Gelb writes. “But the good news is that we all have stories within us that deserve to be told. In the quieter, less pressured time of summer, those stories stand a better chance of coming into their own.”', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/tipsheetgelb/', 'snippet': '“It’s no mean feat to produce a powerful essay on a highly personal subject,” the author Alan Gelb writes. “But the good news is that we all have stories within us that deserve to be told. In the quieter, less pressured time of summer, those stori...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mr. Gelb is the author of “Conquering the College Admissions Essay in 10 Steps.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Juniors: In the Quiet of Summer, Start Your Essays', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:45:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Gelb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gelb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed938743-633b-508b-ad26-f1c746d6d697', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed938743-633b-508b-ad26-f1c746d6d697'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Barnes’s stories cover loss, friendship, sex and what it takes for two people to click.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-pulse-by-julian-barnes.html', 'snippet': 'Julian Barnes’s stories cover loss, friendship, sex and what it takes for two people to click.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Couples are the beating heart, the systole and diastole, of “Pulse,” Julian Barnes’s diverting third collection of stories. Author of the best-selling “Arthur & George” (an odd-couple historical novel in which “Arthur” is the creator of Sherlock Holmes) and the unclassifiable masterpiece “Flaubert’s Parrot,” Barnes is among the most adventurous writers — in style, versatility and narrative structure — of his Amis-McEwan-Hitchens generation. Another of his beguiling novels, “Talking It Over,” was about a loquacious love triangle, and what the characters mainly talk over in “Pulse” is what makes couples tick. “What do we look for in a partner?” the puzzled narrator, who was “still unmarried at 30,” asks in the title story. Someone who “matches us”? Perhaps. But is it possible that “we’re sometimes driven towards people with the same faults as we have?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 613, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0/bkr-Benfey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Julian Barnes on Not Talking About Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Significant Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnes, Julian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:47:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19f2a360-2cca-5f8d-9fda-76a7c6078af1', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19f2a360-2cca-5f8d-9fda-76a7c6078af1'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist argues that social networks can mitigate social ills.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-join-the-club-by-tina-rosenberg.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist argues that social networks can mitigate social ills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Man, Aristotle said, is by nature a social animal. This is the premise of Tina Rosenberg’s new and important book, “Join the Club,” which examines the idea that human behavior is defined by our relationships with our acquaintances. Peer pressure, she finds, can lead to acts of great courage, or to great harm. But Rosenberg’s particular ambition is to unlock the secret of using it to “transform the world,” in the words of her subtitle. Calling this approach the “social cure,” she examines how social networks can address some of the world’s most recalcitrant problems — from adolescent cigarette smoking to despotism.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Sachs/Sachs-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Sachs/Sachs-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Sachs/Sachs-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Social Networking Cure Social Ills?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Help From the Herd', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Tina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:49:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By JEFFREY D. SACHS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa2829e1-4d3d-5556-bc6f-c7cee90ad0cd', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa2829e1-4d3d-5556-bc6f-c7cee90ad0cd'}, {'abstract': 'How a Jewish girl from Larchmont became an Islamic polemicist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-the-convert-by-deborah-baker.html', 'snippet': 'How a Jewish girl from Larchmont became an Islamic polemicist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deborah Baker is a serious biographer who specializes in fairly crazy writers. Her study of the poet Laura Riding, who survived a suicide attempt in 1929, during her 14-year ménage-à-trois with Robert Graves and his wife, was a Pulitzer finalist. Next came “A Blue Hand,” her portrait of the Beats in India, few of whom were in robust mental health. Yet even these have nothing on Maryam Jameelah, a New York Jewish convert to Islam, who — as a disciple of Pakistan’s most world-renowned fundamentalist — made a career out of condemning the West in dozens of books and pamphlets.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/ADAMS-1305056430084/ADAMS-1305056430084-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 759, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/ADAMS-1305056430084/ADAMS-1305056430084-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/ADAMS-1305056430084/ADAMS-1305056430084-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New York Jewish Girl Becomes an Islamist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crossing Over', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T13:55:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lorraine Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lorraine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/641dccfd-098d-51fe-b345-db393801db2d', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/641dccfd-098d-51fe-b345-db393801db2d'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Aalbaek Jensen, who founded Zentropa with Mr. von Trier in 1992, said he did not share the director’s “view of what might be funny to say at a press conference,” adding that “his comments are a direct contradiction of Zentropa’s values.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/von-triers-company-apologizes-for-directors-remarks-at-cannes/', 'snippet': 'Peter Aalbaek Jensen, who founded Zentropa with Mr. von Trier in 1992, said he did not share the director’s “view of what might be funny to say at a press conference,” adding that “his comments are a direct contradiction of Zentropa’s values.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' The production company that the Danish filmmaker Lars von Trier helped found apologized on Friday for remarks the director made at the Cannes Film Festival on Wednesday in which he said he understood and sympathized with Hitler.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Von Trier’s Company Apologizes for Director’s Remarks at Cannes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bier, Susanne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'von Trier, Lars', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cannes (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_d