 from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: A New App Helps Used-Car Buyers', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:07:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec210ef8-acc2-58be-9596-2c191a86066b', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec210ef8-acc2-58be-9596-2c191a86066b'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/what-were-reading-187/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b1c8ce7-3c6e-5eb8-8973-742864b89804', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b1c8ce7-3c6e-5eb8-8973-742864b89804'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/morning-take-out-248/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOP STORIES ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:22:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5cdd53f-fd6c-5f73-a458-a2e71b6644ab', 'word_count': 1782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5cdd53f-fd6c-5f73-a458-a2e71b6644ab'}, {'abstract': 'The project was initiated as an effort to better understand this generation of gay, lesbian, bisexual and transgender youths, and to explore its realities and expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/us/23out.html', 'snippet': 'The project was initiated as an effort to better understand this generation of gay, lesbian, bisexual and transgender youths, and to explore its realities and expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The suicide of Tyler Clementi, a Rutgers University freshman who jumped from the George Washington Bridge last year after discovering that his roommate had secretly streamed his romantic interlude with another man on the Internet, captured worldwide  attention. In the wake of his death, stories of gay youths being bullied and taking their own lives proliferated.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Coming Out’: Gay Teenagers, in Their Own Words', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Coming Out’: Gay Teenagers, in Their Own Words', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:34:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9edc9415-5144-5ade-a9ee-06577897be9d', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9edc9415-5144-5ade-a9ee-06577897be9d'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Hogan was quite successful after a car accident led him to reduce his playing schedule, and the much-injured Tiger Woods might benefit from playing less in an effort to win more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/sports/golf/to-challenge-nicklaus-woods-could-adopt-hogans-schedule.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Hogan was quite successful after a car accident led him to reduce his playing schedule, and the much-injured Tiger Woods might benefit from playing less in an effort to win more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until now, most comparisons between Tiger Woods and golfers from other eras have focused on Jack Nicklaus, whose record of 18 major championship victories has been circled on Woods’s to-do list since he was a skinny kid living in the Los Angeles suburb of Cypress, Calif.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/tiger-1/tiger-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/tiger-1/tiger-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/tiger-1/tiger-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Win More, Perhaps Woods Should Play Less', 'kicker': 'On Golf', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Challenge Nicklaus, Woods Could Adopt Hogan’s Playing Schedule', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hogan, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PGA Tour Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:39:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Dorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/748f779f-e224-5c89-8f2b-f43683dfbc9e', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/748f779f-e224-5c89-8f2b-f43683dfbc9e'}, {'abstract': 'An Iraqi-born physicist recalls the golden age of Islamic astronomy, mathematics, medicine and philosophy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-the-house-of-wisdom-by-jim-al-khalili.html', 'snippet': 'An Iraqi-born physicist recalls the golden age of Islamic astronomy, mathematics, medicine and philosophy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the thousand years between the decline of Rome and the springtime of the Renaissance, science and other branches of learning took a holiday throughout Europe. It was a benighted time in the history most of us raced through in school, skipping lightly through Charlemagne and Richard the Lion-Hearted, the Norman Conquest and the Crusades, and arriving none too soon at the time of Leonardo and Michelangelo, Columbus and da Gama, Erasmus and Luther.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Wilford-1304730724441/Wilford-1304730724441-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Wilford-1304730724441/Wilford-1304730724441-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Wilford-1304730724441/Wilford-1304730724441-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Muslim Art of Science', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Muslim Art of Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:45:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Noble Wilford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Noble', 'lastname': 'Wilford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/adf1618c-5cb4-5dcf-9631-437679605b4a', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adf1618c-5cb4-5dcf-9631-437679605b4a'}, {'abstract': 'Because the Big Three’s light trucks and larger cars have farther to go, they have greater potential to add consumer value through improved fuel economy, a report says.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/who-will-reap-the-dividends-of-fuel-economy/', 'snippet': 'Because the Big Three’s light trucks and larger cars have farther to go, they have greater potential to add consumer value through improved fuel economy, a report says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two basic arguments over whether and how the country should respond to climate change and other environmental challenges. One focuses on government’s right to regulate industry, and the other on the costs and benefits of such regulation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Will Reap the Dividends of Fuel Economy?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trucks and Trucking', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nichols, Mary D', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:46:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e6d445f-62d8-51aa-bd22-a417e62d5f69', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e6d445f-62d8-51aa-bd22-a417e62d5f69'}, {'abstract': 'A psychology professor offers the theory that consciousness is a show we stage for ourselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-soul-dust-the-magic-of-consciousness-by-nicholas-humphrey.html', 'snippet': 'A psychology professor offers the theory that consciousness is a show we stage for ourselves.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Soul Dust,” Nicholas Humphrey’s new book about consciousness, is seductive — early 1960s, “Mad Men” seductive. His writing is as elegant, and hypnotic, as that cool jazz stacked on the record player. His argument feels as crystalline and bracing as that double martini going down, though you might find yourself a little woozy afterward. And his tone is as warm and inviting as that big, crackling fire, even if the dim flicker does leave things a bit obscure in the corners. I mean, the man recites Rupert Brooke love poetry at length. Very different from the usual awkward geeks of academic philosophy and psychology.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Gopnik/Gopnik-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Gopnik/Gopnik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Gopnik/Gopnik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Consciousness: The Great Illusion?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Great Illusion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:48:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Gopnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gopnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/738df23b-6da4-5a2e-9a5f-65edb35dc9cf', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/738df23b-6da4-5a2e-9a5f-65edb35dc9cf'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of essays expands our textbook view of the American Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-revolutionary-founders-rebels-radicals-and-reformers-in-the-making-of-the-nation.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of essays expands our textbook view of the American Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Historians refer to the trend as Founders’ Chic: the recent flurry of interest in biographies of the “great white men” of our nation’s founding generation. Although George Washington has consistently attracted Americans’ attention since Parson Weems’s biography appeared in 1800, during the last decade published examinations of Washington’s contemporaries have multiplied dramatically. Take John Adams. By my rough count, he alone or in conjunction with other men has been the subject of 19 biographical studies and five documentary collections since 2001. Two more books have examined his marriage to Abigail Smith; she herself has warranted two separate biographies. Remarkably, a recent search in the Cornell library catalog for the phrase “founding fathers” in titles of books on American history published since 2001 identified 37 volumes. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/NORTON/NORTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 812, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/NORTON/NORTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/NORTON/NORTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding More Founders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding More Founders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:50:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Beth Norton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Beth', 'lastname': 'Norton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ac2351b-b8ca-5255-b1d1-05e839a8db55', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ac2351b-b8ca-5255-b1d1-05e839a8db55'}, {'abstract': 'Owners allege that when idling, their crossovers lunged forward by as much as 10 feet without a foot on the gas pedal.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/agency-investigates-170000-ford-freestyle-crossovers-for-lunging-condition/', 'snippet': 'Owners allege that when idling, their crossovers lunged forward by as much as 10 feet without a foot on the gas pedal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An investigation is under way into the possibility that 170,000 Ford Freestyles have a tendency to accelerate without the driver’s foot on the gas pedal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Agency Investigates 170,000 Ford Freestyle Crossovers for ‘Lunging’ Condition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AB Volvo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:51:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b495f03e-9015-52d3-8f87-344f65dc55b7', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b495f03e-9015-52d3-8f87-344f65dc55b7'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Barry finds layers of meaning in baseball’s longest game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-bottom-of-the-33rd-by-dan-barry.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Barry finds layers of meaning in baseball’s longest game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you wanted to write a novel about the redemptive power of America’s pastime, you would imagine a Midwestern cornfield sculptured into a diamond that brought back the dead and healed generational wounds. Then you would remember that this idea has been taken, and instead you would invent the tale of the longest professional game ever played. You would make it a minor league contest, featuring players with mostly faint hopes of getting to the majors; they would absurdly play into the wee hours with no scouts in sight in April’s irrelevant cold. Therefore — and here’s your grand novelistic gesture — they must be persevering for some purpose larger than themselves. It would take place on Easter, the day the dead are reborn, in a decaying Northeastern mill town peopled by working-class Catholics who believe that unimportant things like wafers, wine and baseball can be made holy through faith. If you wished to be really obvious, the game would last 33 innings — one for each of Jesus’ years on earth. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/TRACY/TRACY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/TRACY/TRACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/TRACY/TRACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Extra Innings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Extra Innings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barry, Dan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:51:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Tracy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tracy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37fea582-1b3c-59e8-aafb-56a676894fb4', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37fea582-1b3c-59e8-aafb-56a676894fb4'}, {'abstract': 'A New York murder tale unfolds over the 24-hour news cycle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/book-review-tabloid-city-by-pete-hamill.html', 'snippet': 'A New York murder tale unfolds over the 24-hour news cycle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Granted, as silver linings go this may be scant consolation, but the decline and demise of newspapers seems to be ushering in a raft of good novels by journalists who miss the old ink and newsprint. Reporters harking back to footloose times are not new (think of Evelyn Waugh’s “When the Going Was Good”), but now that end days may actually be upon us, we may be facing a complete subgenre: Où sont les news d’antan? We’ve already had Tom Rachman’s book “The Imperfectionists.” Now comes Pete Hamill’s “Tabloid City,” which sets a high bar for those that \xadfollow.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Darnton/Darnton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Darnton/Darnton-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Darnton/Darnton-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pete Hamill’s Tabloid Thriller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peeled Apple', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamill, Pete', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:53:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Darnton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Darnton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8b39870-ddd6-5bae-8330-babc6142a985', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8b39870-ddd6-5bae-8330-babc6142a985'}, {'abstract': 'For a writer like me — midcareer, midlist, more or less middlebrow — self-publishing seems to make a lot of sense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/the-case-for-self-publishing.html', 'snippet': 'For a writer like me — midcareer, midlist, more or less middlebrow — self-publishing seems to make a lot of sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many thousands of years ago, when I was still a whelpish newspaper reporter in Chicago, I did a short profile of a retired television repairman who’d written a memoir entitled “The Perilous Life of Boris B. Gursky.” As a boy in Ukraine, Gursky said, he was stepped on by a horse; when he was a young man in Mexico, his stepfather had tried to murder him; and when he was a middle-aged man in Chicago, his ex-wife had clawed his face with her fingernails and threatened him with a loaded pistol. Most perilously, Gursky suffered from prostate cancer. He wrote, “My body had become so sensitive from staying too long in an environment surrounded with radiation from television sets.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Pollack/Pollack-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Pollack/Pollack-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Pollack/Pollack-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case for Self-Publishing', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peddling Your Prose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:55:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neal Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neal', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ea1b0b0-4626-59a8-bc4c-9fe9bae8a3db', 'word_count': 1165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ea1b0b0-4626-59a8-bc4c-9fe9bae8a3db'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Taylor said his invitation to Oxford was “very ironic seeing as I didn’t graduate from high school,” adding that in his appearance, “I’m probably going to swear and sweat a lot.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/people-surprised-corey-taylor-of-slipknot-to-speak-at-oxford-union/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Taylor said his invitation to Oxford was “very ironic seeing as I didn’t graduate from high school,” adding that in his appearance, “I’m probably going to swear and sweat a lot.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a centuries-long tradition of gussying up its debates with a little pomp and circumstance, one of Britain’s foremost institutions of oratory apparently has decided that gowns and powdered wigs won’t cut it anymore, and has turned to a man known for performing in jumpsuits and fetishistic masks. Corey Taylor, the lead singer of the heavy-metal band Slipknot, will be among the speakers to address the Oxford Union in the coming weeks, according to the schedule published on the union’s Web site. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corey Taylor of Slipknot to Speak at Oxford Union', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caan, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferguson, Niall', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Slipknot', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oxford (England)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T14:56:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7412776-49f6-5f79-bbe7-c73954903eb4', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7412776-49f6-5f79-bbe7-c73954903eb4'}, {'abstract': 'The Democratic National Committee significantly outraised its Republican counterpart in April.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/d-n-c-raises-more-money-than-r-n-c-in-april/', 'snippet': 'The Democratic National Committee significantly outraised its Republican counterpart in April.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Democratic National Committee significantly outraised its Republican counterpart in April, powered in part by millions of dollars from successful fund-raisers that featured President Obama, a D.N.C. official said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'D.N.C. Raises More Money Than R.N.C. in April', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Priebus, Reince R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steele, Michael S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic National Committee', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican National Committee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T15:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6afaac4-86e7-5753-9862-e1e26e3cb6c7', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6afaac4-86e7-5753-9862-e1e26e3cb6c7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO END ALL WARS: A Story of Loyalty and Rebellion, 1914-1918,  by Adam Hochschild (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $28.) Hochschild’s stirring account concentrates on the appalling losses in the ranks and on the extraordinary courage of the dissenters.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': "Editors' Choice", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T15:02:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/828c3ac6-8128-55f2-ade8-2b80ae62cfc9', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/828c3ac6-8128-55f2-ade8-2b80ae62cfc9'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE GOOD MAN JESUS AND THE SCOUNDREL CHRIST,  by Philip Pullman (Canongate, $14.) This provocative retelling of the Gospels, by the author of the fantasy series “His Dark Materials,” separates the ethical teachings from the miracles and damnations by having the Virgin Mary give birth to twins: Jesus, wise and strong-willed; and the shrewd Christ, who is tempted by a silver-tongued stranger.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T15:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c83d1b05-49e9-53fc-833b-e17dffaffb1e', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c83d1b05-49e9-53fc-833b-e17dffaffb1e'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Emily Bazelon’s review of J