m-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/20/books/review/video-haroldbloom/video-haroldbloom-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Harold Bloom's Influence", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yale University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloom, Harold', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T16:16:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'Brent McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ddaf5428-e98b-5b9b-acf6-106f9428aaf9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ddaf5428-e98b-5b9b-acf6-106f9428aaf9'}, {'abstract': 'A gift from Kurt Andersen: a list of verboten vocabulary.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/words-we-dont-say/', 'snippet': 'A gift from Kurt Andersen: a list of verboten vocabulary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1997, when I was first hired at New York magazine, Kurt Andersen, now a best-selling novelist and radio-show host, had just been fired as editor. Everybody was grieving about this, though not me, since I wouldn’t have had a job there otherwise. And though it wasn’t until years later that I even met Kurt, he unwittingly left me a gift: tacked to the bulletin board in the office I took over was a single page titled “Words We Don’t Say.” It contained, as you might surmise, words and phrases that Kurt found annoying and didn’t want used in his magazine. Just yesterday, I rescued it from a bunch of old office stuff that I was throwing out, and I have to say, 14 years later, it’s still a pretty useful list of phony-baloney vocabulary that editors are well-advised to excise from stories.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Words We Don’t Say', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andersen, Kurt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York (Magazine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T16:17:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7a09893-42b9-57c2-97d0-4b85527f786c', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7a09893-42b9-57c2-97d0-4b85527f786c'}, {'abstract': 'Getting up to speed on big news events and topics, and preparing to follow them over the summer.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/summer-2011-tracking-topics-in-the-news/', 'snippet': 'Getting up to speed on big news events and topics, and preparing to follow them over the summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the school year is winding down, we want to encourage students to continue to watch what’s going on in the world. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Summer 2011: Tracking Topics in the News', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T16:18:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne, Holly Epstein Ojalvo and Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dba16eca-b57d-5f43-a58f-7c9ca0b6ac7f', 'word_count': 2105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dba16eca-b57d-5f43-a58f-7c9ca0b6ac7f'}, {'abstract': 'Rafael Nadal now has to worry about Novak Djokovic, who is unbeaten in 2011, in his bid to equal Bjorn Borg’s record of six French Open titles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/sports/tennis/borg-roots-for-nadal-to-join-him-as-new-rival-arises.html', 'snippet': 'Rafael Nadal now has to worry about Novak Djokovic, who is unbeaten in 2011, in his bid to equal Bjorn Borg’s record of six French Open titles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — What once seemed a foregone conclusion — that Rafael Nadal would match Bjorn Borg by winning his sixth French Open this year — now looks more like a tennis thriller in the making thanks to the rise and prodigious efforts of Novak Djokovic.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/BORG1_span/BORG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/BORG1_span/BORG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/BORG1_span/BORG1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/BORG1_span/BORG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/sports/BORG1_span/BORG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Borg Roots for Nadal to Join Him as New Rival Arises', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ROOTING FOR A RIVAL', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Borg, Bjorn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T16:20:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83424e3b-daf2-5244-b90b-f3c70e978d38', 'word_count': 1536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83424e3b-daf2-5244-b90b-f3c70e978d38'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Lawrence Block, Jo Nesbo, Stefanie Pintoff and Scott O’Connor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/books/review/touch-of-evil-books-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Lawrence Block, Jo Nesbo, Stefanie Pintoff and Scott O’Connor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The city isn’t what it used to be in A DROP OF THE HARD STUFF (Mulholland/Little, Brown, $25.99), as Matt Scudder, the independent (and unlicensed) gumshoe in Lawrence Block’s timeless New York crime novels, sits down late one night to reminisce with Mick Ballou, the proprietor of Grogan’s Open House, a bar in Hell’s Kitchen. Gentrification has gussied up that once raffish neighborhood, and “a gentler and more refined bunch” of patrons has replaced the colorful and largely criminal riffraff that lent character to the old saloon. But instead of forcing Scudder to make his peace with this new world, Block thoughtfully sends him back to the early 1970s, when he was still a working cop and crime was still “the leading occupation” in the neighborhood.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Touch of Evil', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Touch of Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Block, Lawrence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nesbo, Jo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stasio, Marilyn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T16:20:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f07d334-4eda-5123-9563-8f34cb1500f9', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f07d334-4eda-5123-9563-8f34cb1500f9'}, {'abstract': 'Aside from that, how did Domi