edia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cannes Fashion: Hurry Sundown', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T18:59:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbae57ae-1074-599f-bbc3-fb27205e3290', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbae57ae-1074-599f-bbc3-fb27205e3290'}, {'abstract': 'This is our second straight week of superabundant sentence harvesting — which is good, given that it will apparently be our final Sentence of the Week post ever. (Does Twitter still work when the moon has turned to blood?)', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/sentence-of-the-week-rapture-edition/', 'snippet': 'This is our second straight week of superabundant sentence harvesting — which is good, given that it will apparently be our final Sentence of the Week post ever. (Does Twitter still work when the moon has turned to blood?)', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is our second straight week of superabundant sentence harvesting — which is good, given that it will apparently be our final Sentence of the Week post ever. (Does Twitter still work when the moon has turned to blood?) So let’s all really settle in and savor these together, the last good sentences ever tweeted in the Cycle of Human Time:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sentence of the Week: Rapture Edition', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Sam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T18:59:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a99c5f9-8c3d-520b-86e0-5b2e739fd5c1', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a99c5f9-8c3d-520b-86e0-5b2e739fd5c1'}, {'abstract': 'From St. Bart’s to the Thames, the new Summer Travel issue of T sets sail.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/now-online-ts-summer-travel-issue-2/', 'snippet': 'From St. Bart’s to the Thames, the new Summer Travel issue of T sets sail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new Summer Travel issue of T sets sail with the model-sailor-adventurer Daria Werbowy, who skippers a sailboat from St. Bart’s into the spectacularly blue Caribbean Sea. The watery idylls continue on Favignana, a tiny island off Sicily that has lately been discovered by the fashion crowd; in the Cape Cod town of Wellfleet, where a collection of Bauhaus-style bungalows conjures a lost bohemia; and on the banks of the Thames, in which amateur archaeologists called mudlarkers dig for scraps of history. We peel back layers of the past in Kuala Lumpur, one of Asia’s most cosmopolitan cities, then get lost in time in Tangier, a last redoubt for romantic dropouts. For beach-blanket readers, there’s an excerpt from Elaine Sciolino’s timely new book, La Seduction, about the role of flirtation and provocation in French society. And for wave-riders we’ve assembled a guide to cool new surf spots around the globe. Plus: the Selby heads to Melbourne. See all stories from the issue >>', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Online | T’s Summer Travel Issue', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc83b6be-8189-5367-9ca5-0103c0ec88c3', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc83b6be-8189-5367-9ca5-0103c0ec88c3'}, {'abstract': 'Morning Line for Friday, May 20', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/morning-line-handicapping-the-preakness/', 'snippet': 'Morning Line for Friday, May 20', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a week of articles on Team Animal Kingdom (the horse; the trainer, Graham Motion; Team Valor’s chief executive, Barry Irwin; and the horse’s home at Fair Hill)\xa0on Friday attention turned to betting the Preakness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: Handicapping the Preakness', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d061426d-c7af-5292-a1b4-99c9e96a2dcd', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d061426d-c7af-5292-a1b4-99c9e96a2dcd'}, {'abstract': 'Everyone has some fascination with the combination of sex, money and power, but who would have thought they would all point to a career at the International Monetary Fund?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/will-the-i-m-f-rise-again/', 'snippet': 'Everyone has some fascination with the combination of sex, money and power, but who would have thought they would all point to a career at the International Monetary Fund?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everyone has some fascination with the combination of sex, money and power, but who would have thought they would all point to a career at the International Monetary Fund? Founded in 1945 at the Bretton Woods Conference with a mandate to prevent wild swings in national currency values, the fund, I would have imagined, would set itself against swinging of every kind. \xa0That is clearly not the case.\xa0According to Alan Beattie’s interview this morning with I.M.F. semi-renegade Simon Johnson, the fund was firstly aimed at helping struggling postwar economies in Western Europe, and its first loan (in 1947) was to France.\xa0 For 35 of the fund’s 64 years, its managing director was French. When Western Europe had recovered, the fund went on to other things and secured for itself, let’s say, a mixed reputation.\xa0 From the 1960s onward, and culminating in the period of strict I.M.F. loan “conditionality” and rigid programs of “structural adjustment,” the fund earned a reputation as a corps of technocratic drones pitilessly contented in their air-conditioned hotel rooms as they imposed cookie-cutter “solutions” on prostrate governments. You would read from time to time of “anti-I.M.F. riots” in one poor capital or another, and you could guess what it meant, and it wasn’t pretty.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0By the early part of our own century, the fund was entering a period of repentance, not least because some poorer countries were now “emerging markets” and making a big point of repaying I.M.F. loans and swearing never to take them again. Escaping the I.M.F. became a symbol of freedom. But if the fund wasn’t making loans it had no income, and we know what that means: time for reform! Emerging-market players, with a sympathetic United States, pushed for changes in voting power. In essence, the developing world would get more I.M.F. power at the expense (slightly) of the United States and (mainly) of Europe. Several reforms, first undertaken in 2006 and most recently last year, would take the voting share of “advanced economies” from 60.6 to 55.3, and of “emerging market and developing countries” from 39.4 to 44.7. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will the I.M.F. Rise Again?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bretton Woods Agreement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:12:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Malcomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malcomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/18974000-a0e3-5947-8fe6-c68e3f915851', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18974000-a0e3-5947-8fe6-c68e3f915851'}, {'abstract': 'In a meeting with President Obama, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel listed a series of nonnegotiable conditions for a deal with Palestinians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/middleeast/21prexy.html', 'snippet': 'In a meeting with President Obama, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel listed a series of nonnegotiable conditions for a deal with Palestinians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel told President Obama on Friday that he shared his vision for a peace between Israelis and Palestinians, and then promptly listed a series of nonnegotiable conditions that have kept the two sides at an impasse for years. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21prexy-span/21prexy-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21prexy-span/21prexy-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21prexy-span/21prexy-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21prexy-span/21prexy-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21prexy-span/21prexy-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Divisions Are Clear as Obama and Netanyahu Discuss Peace', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ISRAELI LEADER REBUFFS OBAMA ON ’67 BORDERS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:12:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49978ee7-d8db-57e5-85db-9ebdfcf310d4', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49978ee7-d8db-57e5-85db-9ebdfcf310d4'}, {'abstract': 'Pakistan’s leading English-language newspaper, Dawn, began publishing a selection of more than 4,000 American diplomatic cables obtained from WikiLeaks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/asia/21wikileaks.html', 'snippet': 'Pakistan’s leading English-language newspaper, Dawn, began publishing a selection of more than 4,000 American diplomatic cables obtained from WikiLeaks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — The leading English-language newspaper, Dawn, and its Web site on Friday began publishing a selection of more than 4,000 American diplomatic cables obtained from WikiLeaks that are devoted to Pakistan, opening a window onto the American-Pakistani relationship and domestic politics never before seen here. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21wikileaks/21wikileaks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21wikileaks/21wikileaks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21wikileaks/21wikileaks-thumbStandard