e': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Village (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:43:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6174786-8466-5172-a1a7-ad9f1f0de924', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6174786-8466-5172-a1a7-ad9f1f0de924'}, {'abstract': 'Kate Moss’s infamous quote is now available in needlepoint.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/eat-her-words/', 'snippet': 'Kate Moss’s infamous quote is now available in needlepoint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people put photographs of lithe supermodels on their fridges to help avert caloric mishaps. Now you can reproach yourself in any room of the house, with words from one of the slimmest supes of all. Stitched with Kate Moss’s infamous quote, Jonathan Adler’s needlepoint pillow is $119 at Kirna Zabête, 96 Greene Street (Spring Street). Or go to kirnazabete.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eat Her Words', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:45:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandra Ballentine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ballentine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/06b68367-aa06-5348-875a-feaf8e08a57e', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06b68367-aa06-5348-875a-feaf8e08a57e'}, {'abstract': 'Erik Larson’s “In the Garden of Beasts” hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4, without any help from Navy Seals or Steven Tyler’s hairdresser.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Erik Larson’s “In the Garden of Beasts” hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4, without any help from Navy Seals or Steven Tyler’s hairdresser.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CITY LIGHTS: Erik Larson’s “In the Garden of Beasts” hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4, without any help from Navy Seals or Steven Tyler’s hairdresser. Instead, it’s got Nazis, romance and old-fashioned storytelling chops, not to mention a cover that makes Berlin in 1933 look an awful lot like the Chicago of the 1893 World’s Fair depicted on the front of Larson’s blockbuster “The Devil in the White City,” which spent a total of 182 weeks on the hardcover and paperback lists starting back in 2003. Like that book, “In the Garden of Beasts” is shaped around the interlocking stories of two characters, the American ambassador William Dodd, who views Hitler’s Berlin with growing alarm, and his daughter, Martha, who finds herself rather enchanted by all the glamorous men in uniform. (“Think Carrie Bradshaw in Berlin,” as USA Today’s reviewer, Deirdre Donahue, put it.) In an interview with The Huffington Post Chicago, Larson, inset, explained his decision to give so much space to Martha, a former assistant literary editor at The Chicago Tribune who left her husband behind to gallivant with French diplomats, Russian agents and the head of the Gestapo: “If I’d hung the entire book on Dodd, it might have been interesting, but it would have lapsed into a diplomatic history. I find diplomatic histories the dullest of histories.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/books/22inside-pic/22inside-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/books/22inside-pic/22inside-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:49:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92fe9ba3-0c41-55d8-b793-7e4ab75614b4', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92fe9ba3-0c41-55d8-b793-7e4ab75614b4'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama and Benjamin Netanyahu have had a somewhat rocky relationship, but had a publicly cordial meeting at the White House on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/talking-and-listening-in-the-oval-office/', 'snippet': 'President Obama and Benjamin Netanyahu have had a somewhat rocky relationship, but had a publicly cordial meeting at the White House on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When President Obama met with Benjamin Netanyahu, the prime minister of Israel, in the Oval Office on Friday, this photo caught our eye. The two men have had a sometimes rocky relationship, (see today’s story by Helene Cooper) but they exchanged cordial words on Friday. This picture was snapped as Mr. Obama listened, almost frozen, during long remarks by Mr. Netanyahu, in which the Israeli leader pushed back against the framework for a peace deal that Mr. Obama outlined in a speech Thursday at the State Department. It made us wonder: What is Mr. Obama thinking? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking, and Listening, in the Oval Office', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T19:50:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/33e2c57f-0c34-500b-811a-8d5619540219', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33e2c57f-0c34-500b-811a-8d5619540219'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from the West Bank, Japan, Spain and Bulgaria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/20/blogs/20110520POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from the West Bank, Japan, Spain and Bulgaria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from the West Bank, Japan, Spain and Bulgaria.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/blogs/20110520POD-slide-PA6D/20110520POD-slide-PA6D-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/20/blogs/20110520POD-slide-PA6D/20110520POD-slide-PA6D-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail