ring back problems, James Levine should be feeling some contentment right now. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/LEVINE-1/LEVINE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/LEVINE-1/LEVINE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/LEVINE-1/LEVINE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For James Levine, the Twilight of a Conductor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Levine’s Next Act: Give Up the Role Of Music Director', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levine, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T20:25:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddbf700d-f20f-56a1-904e-a7321a1f0ff9', 'word_count': 1615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddbf700d-f20f-56a1-904e-a7321a1f0ff9'}, {'abstract': 'Singers are hoping that a short burst of television exposure can do what the music business often can’t or won’t: make them a star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/arts/television/singers-favor-tv-show-competitions-over-record-companies.html', 'snippet': 'Singers are hoping that a short burst of television exposure can do what the music business often can’t or won’t: make them a star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BETWEEN 2003 and 2006 Javier released two albums and a live EP on Capitol Records, a fair shot by any music industry standard. He was an amiable and warm singer, precise and flexible, with a crisp pop voice and smooth R&B inflections. And yet that voice, perhaps too clean and pretty, made for unremarkable records that left little impact. Soon after his second album Javier and Capitol parted ways, another quiet casualty of the record business.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/SINGING-1/SINGING-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/SINGING-1/SINGING-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/SINGING-1/SINGING-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/SINGING-1/SINGING-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/SINGING-1/SINGING-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Singers Embrace the TV Road to Stardom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Singers Embrace The TV Road To Stardom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T20:28:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a075c46-5071-574d-8c49-760850247600', 'word_count': 1445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a075c46-5071-574d-8c49-760850247600'}, {'abstract': '“Los Tigres Del Norte and Friends: MTV Unplugged” features a lineup of stars including Paulina Rubio and Juanes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/arts/music/a-little-bit-country-a-little-bit-norteno.html', 'snippet': '“Los Tigres Del Norte and Friends: MTV Unplugged” features a lineup of stars including Paulina Rubio and Juanes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Los Tigres del Norte', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/PLAYLIST-1/PLAYLIST-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vampires, Bachata, Norteño and a Country Coalition-Builder', 'kicker': 'Playlist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vampires, Bachata, Norteño And a Country Coalition-Builder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Tigres del Norte', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rich, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Juanes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T20:29:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa7e7322-5e41-5b3a-8498-a21b21d7e305', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa7e7322-5e41-5b3a-8498-a21b21d7e305'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column is about the risks involved in investing in private equity and debt offerings.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/private-offerings-come-with-many-caveats/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column is about the risks involved in investing in private equity and debt offerings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column this week makes the point that some investors are not any more aware of the risks involved in private equity and debt offerings than they were nearly three years ago, in the depths of the recession. The biggest risk is that they will lose all their money. That is probably not as big a deal for the wealthy as it is for those who aspire to be rich.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Private Offerings Come With Many Caveats', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T20:29:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e064d738-5724-5183-9297-93f2e79ae8b4', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e064d738-5724-5183-9297-93f2e79ae8b4'}, {'abstract': 'Two New York-based artists take aspects of globalized culture and consumerism and trickle them down to a rural level.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/timely-jules-marquis-art-in-america/', 'snippet': 'Two New York-based artists take aspects of globalized culture and consumerism and trickle them down to a rural level.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether by filming an ode to NBC in a field or by having small-town Little Leaguers pace around a New York gallery and mutter ‘‘good game’’ to one another, Colin Snapp (left) and Daniel Turner frequently take aspects of globalized culture and consumerism and trickle them down to a rural level. The two New York-based artists, who recently began exhibiting collaborative work under the name Jules Marquis, share a Greenpoint studio but regularly shuttle to southeast Virginia, where they’ve created a nonprofit art space on a corner of the Turner family farm. Named Jericho Ditch, after a local logging canal, it serves as a venue and creative getaway for fellow artists. It also embodies the Jules Marquis ethos: from the outside, it’s a shed in a field; inside, it’s a big-city-gallery-style white box lighted by symmetrical fluorescent tubes. Snapp and Turner have two shows opening in June: at Jericho Ditch and at Martos Gallery in New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Timely | Jules Marquis • Art in America', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T20:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_de