/article/4b86b121-755c-5af0-99f3-822c3681ee23', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b86b121-755c-5af0-99f3-822c3681ee23'}, {'abstract': 'The couple were married Friday at the Columbus Citizens Foundation, a private club, in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/fashion/weddings/elaine-mcquillan-hugh-barry-wedding.html', 'snippet': 'The couple were married Friday at the Columbus Citizens Foundation, a private club, in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elaine Marie McQuillan and Hugh John Barry were married Friday at the Columbus Citizens Foundation, a private club, in Manhattan. Justice Edward J. McLaughlin of State Supreme Court in Manhattan officiated. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/weddings/22MCQUILLANjpg/22MCQUILLANjpg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/weddings/22MCQUILLANjpg/22MCQUILLANjpg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/weddings/22MCQUILLANjpg/22MCQUILLANjpg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Elaine McQuillan and Hugh Barry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Elaine McQuillan, Hugh Barry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:04:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcab4d57-e9af-5ab7-a850-48628b49a42a', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcab4d57-e9af-5ab7-a850-48628b49a42a'}, {'abstract': 'This summer, two separate curatorial teams will tackle the behemoth subject of art in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/los-angeles-city-of-artists/', 'snippet': 'This summer, two separate curatorial teams will tackle the behemoth subject of art in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There’s no such thing as L.A.,” says the curator Benjamin Godsill. “There’s multiple, sometimes overlapping bits of community, ideologies and neighborhoods.” Organizing a group show around the theme of a major metropolis poses risks. Hyperbole, dime store anthropology and overlooked artists often crowd the presentation. But the best of these endeavors don’t seek to galvanize a scene, per se, but provide the most basic of contexts: place. In this way, the mercurial nature of Los Angeles provides a valid starting point for an investigation of its artists. This summer, two separate curatorial teams (both based in New York, funnily enough) will tackle the behemoth subject of art in Los Angeles with group shows staged far from home. “Greater LA,” on view until June 10 in SoHo, looks at the city’s contemporary artists, whereas “Venice in Venice,” which opens on June 4 at the Venice Biennial, revisits the city’s rebel artists from the 1960s and ’70s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Los Angeles, City of Artists', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:05:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Hruska', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hruska', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/52815bf9-ebfa-5a32-90f4-fc834cd6ac2f', 'word_count': 722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52815bf9-ebfa-5a32-90f4-fc834cd6ac2f'}, {'abstract': 'The singers and musicians in “More Than a Song: The Music That Integrated America” at the Allen Room delivered emotionally connected performances that transcended a revue format.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/music/more-than-a-song-revue-at-the-allen-room-review.html', 'snippet': 'The singers and musicians in “More Than a Song: The Music That Integrated America” at the Allen Room delivered emotionally connected performances that transcended a revue format.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Songs by African-American jazz musicians that became standards; racially conscious protest songs written by Tin Pan Alley titans and made famous by black performers: the back-and-forth crossover process that accelerated with the birth of rock ’n’ roll was the theme of “More Than a Song: The Music That Integrated America,” a sturdy, thoughtful revue that had performances at the Allen Room on Tuesday and Wednesday evenings.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/sub-Feinstein/sub-Feinstein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering the Tunes That Broke Boundaries', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remembering the Tunes That Broke Boundaries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:06:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/103e747b-63ad-52de-a9c3-d11d7d4ff3db', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/103e747b-63ad-52de-a9c3-d11d7d4ff3db'}, {'abstract': 'Using an acidic solution to remove the mineral layer that coats wild silkworms’ cocoons may make them easier to work with, Oxford researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/science/24obsilk.html', 'snippet': 'Using an acidic solution to remove the mineral layer that coats wild silkworms’ cocoons may make them easier to work with, Oxford researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most commercial silk today comes from the cocoons of domesticated silkworms, bred over thousands of years to produce cocoons that are easy to unravel into silk that is easy to dye. The use of silkworms is thought to have originated in China nearly 6,000 years ago. There are many species of wild silkworms as well, but their cocoons are more difficult to unravel. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/science/24silkspan/24silkspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/24/science/24silkspan/24silkspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/science/24silkspan/24silkspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/science/24silkspan/24silkspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/24/science/24silkspan/24silkspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silk Production Takes a Walk on the Wild Side', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silk Production Takes a Walk on the Wild Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b206f86b-c430-58a8-8649-40a950e731b5', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b206f86b-c430-58a8-8649-40a950e731b5'}, {'abstract': 'The advance of the young guard. That would be a fair headline for the final of the open trial in Henderson, Nev., this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/crosswords/bridge/aggressive-bid-leads-to-a-slam.html', 'snippet': 'The advance of the young guard. That would be a fair headline for the final of the open trial in Henderson, Nev., this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The advance of the young guard. That would be a fair headline for the final of the open trial in Henderson, Nev., this week. The average age of the winning team of Kevin Bathurst, Daniel Zagorin, Joe Grue, Justin Lall, John Hurd and Joel Wooldridge was 31, the youngest being Lall, at 24.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aggressive Bid Leads to a Slam', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aggressive Bid Leads to a Slam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:11:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0815353f-8511-54a8-89eb-7b337c80bdc6', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0815353f-8511-54a8-89eb-7b337c80bdc6'}, {'abstract': 'Chelsea Handler’s confidence and lack of any need to please is extraordinary to watch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/fashion/comedy-with-a-side-of-disgust.html', 'snippet': 'Chelsea Handler’s confidence and lack of any need to please is extraordinary to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE few times I have seen the talk show “Chelsea Lately,” a premonition of a world flattened by small talk and Kim Kardashian’s rear, my sense of its outspoken and attractive host, Chelsea Handler, has been that she is not so much giving quarter to her guests as holding them at bay with her intelligence.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/MAINCOURSE/MAINCOURSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/MAINCOURSE/MAINCOURSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/MAINCOURSE/MAINCOURSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Comedy With a Side of Disgust', 'kicker': 'Main Course | Chelsea Handler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Comedy With a Side Of Disgust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handler, Chelsea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:11:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d1d78f6-47f8-5966-a46d-bf94c4dabbaa', 'word_count': 1560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d1d78f6-47f8-5966-a46d-bf94c4dabbaa'}, {'abstract': 'Talk about cravings. At one point in the eerily staged production of  “Sleep No More” at a mock hotel  in Chelsea, a showily pregnant Lady Macduff, hikes up her blue velvet evening dress, hops on to a bar and laps milk from a saucer.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/sleep-no-more-for-style-fanatics/', 'snippet': 'Talk about cravings. At one point in the eerily staged production of  “Sleep No More” at a mock hotel  in Chelsea, a showily pregnant Lady Macduff, hikes up her blue velvet evening dress, hops on to a bar and laps milk from a saucer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talk about cravings. At one point in the eerily staged production of “Sleep No More” at a mock hotel in Chelsea, a showily pregnant Lady Macduff hikes up her blue velvet evening dress, hops on to a bar and laps milk from a saucer. At another moment in this crazily meandering interpretation of “Macbeth” by Punchdrunk, the British experimental theater company, her dress is laid out on a bed, leaving masked spectators free to pore over its capacious folds and seams. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sleep No More’ Is a Feast for Style Fanatics', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sleep No More (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Punchdrunk Theatricals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sleep No More (Play)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:13:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/36b12442-0e25-54c6-8fc3-83b3a35a30b9', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36b12442-0e25-54c6-8fc3-83b3a35a30b9'}, {'abstract': 'Selections from classic musicals were on offer at a Carnegie Hall program on Thursday benefiting the Collegiate Chorale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/music/deborah-voigt-sings-with-collegiate-chorale.html', 'snippet': 'Selections from classic musicals were on offer at a Carnegie Hall program on Thursday benefiting the Collegiate Chorale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No question: the musical forces on the stage of Carnegie Hall on Thursday evening were mighty. Take the 180-voice Collegiate Chorale, add a real-life Brünnhilde in the person of the great dramatic soprano Deborah Voigt, and you should expect nothing less than thunder and lightning. But it was not to be. A distant rumble, a flicker on the horizon, a spritz of rain, yes. But except for a rip-roaring “Oklahoma!” when everything suddenly converged, there was only a hint of a storm. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/voight/voight-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/voight/voight-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/voight/voight-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brünnhilde Meets Broadway at Benefit for Collegiate Chorale', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brünnhilde Meets Broadway at Benefit For Collegiate Chorale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Szot, Paulo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Collegiate Chorale', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Voigt, Deborah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sperling, Ted', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d871ebf4-72f4-52f4-872a-585d123ae47b', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d871ebf4-72f4-52f4-872a-585d123ae47b'}, {'abstract': 'For its commencement concert at Alice Tully Hall on Thursday, the Juilliard Orchestra went beyond the usual Elgar to play Brahms and Saint-Saëns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/music/juilliard-orchestra-commencement-concert-at-tully-hall.html', 'snippet': 'For its commencement concert at Alice Tully Hall on Thursday, the Juilliard Orchestra went beyond the usual Elgar to play Brahms and Saint-Saëns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The music surrounding graduation festivities at most colleges begins and ends with a weak version of Elgar’s famous “Pomp and Circumstance” March. At the Juilliard School, as you might imagine, things are a bit different.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/Juilliard/Juilliard-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Saying Farewell to School, With Some Help From Brahms', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saying Farewell to School, With Some Help From Brahms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juilliard Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:22:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/243b8d2c-b804-5a73-a181-76a3c784d0fd', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/243b8d2c-b804-5a73-a181-76a3c784d0fd'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Your Money column is about the growing number of companies that are covering the extra taxes that their same-sex employees must pay for health insurance.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/more-employers-equalize-health-benefits-for-gay-workers/', 'snippet': 'This week’s Your Money column is about the growing number of companies that are covering the extra taxes that their same-sex employees must pay for health insurance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Your Money column looks at the growing number of companies that have decided to equalize the cost of health benefits for their gay employees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Employers Equalize Health Benefits for Gay Workers', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teach for America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Your Money', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:23:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/95c0527f-1297-54a1-861d-6cab2aedebe0', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95c0527f-1297-54a1-861d-6cab2aedebe0'}, {'abstract': 'More businesses are reaching into their own pockets to pay for an extra tax that gay employees owe on their partners’ health insurance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/your-money/health-insurance/21money.html', 'snippet': 'More businesses are reaching into their own pockets to pay for an extra tax that gay employees owe on their partners’ health insurance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The battle to legalize same-sex marriage may be dominating the headlines, but that issue could take years to resolve. More immediately, a growing number of companies have taken it upon themselves to make life a little more equal for their gay employees.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/business/21MONEY/21MONEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 815, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/business/21MONEY/21MONEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/business/21MONEY/21MONEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Gay Employees, an Equalizer', 'kicker': 'Your Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Gay Employees, An Equalizer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mack, John J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teach for America', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skadden Arps Slate Meagher & Flom', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Health Insurance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75c8334d-064e-523e-9605-6d67c4beaef1', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75c8334d-064e-523e-9605-6d67c4beaef1'}, {'abstract': 'Video of Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu telling President Barack Obama that Israel “cannot go back to the 1967 lines, because these lines are indefensible.”', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/israel-cannot-go-back-to-the-1967-lines-netanyahu-tells-obama/', 'snippet': 'Video of Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu telling President Barack Obama that Israel “cannot go back to the 1967 lines, because these lines are indefensible.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague Steven Lee Myers reports from Washington, “Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel told President Obama on Friday that he shared his vision for a peace between Israelis and Palestinians and then promptly listed a series of nonnegotiable conditions that have kept the two sides at an impasse for years.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel ‘Cannot Go Back to the 1967 Lines,’ Netanyahu Tells Obama', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4889dba8-6e5c-5f74-8ffb-99c42e90cf7d', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4889dba8-6e5c-5f74-8ffb-99c42e90cf7d'}, {'abstract': 'Zoe Saldana, star of this fall’s thriller “Colombiana,” blossoms in a Vionnet black silk organza dress.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/look-of-the-moment-zoe-saldana/', 'snippet': 'Zoe Saldana, star of this fall’s thriller “Colombiana,” blossoms in a Vionnet black silk organza dress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Look: Miracle Grow – with a nod to the natural this floor-length bat-sleeve number blooms with flowers and a striped, color-blocked belt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look of the Moment | Zoe Saldana', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa6a98f8-7d0e-5de0-bfff-c6882013fb40', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa6a98f8-7d0e-5de0-bfff-c6882013fb40'}, {'abstract': 'Private placement investments, which hold the possibility of big returns, are not meant for people who cannot afford to lose large sums of money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/your-money/21wealth.html', 'snippet': 'Private placement investments, which hold the possibility of big returns, are not meant for people who cannot afford to lose large sums of money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INVESTOR interest in private equity and debt offerings continues to increase a mere three years after these illiquid investments caused a lot of anxiety, if not substantial financial losses. The problem is there is little sign that investors are any more aware now of the risks involved in these deals than they were then. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/business/Wealth/Wealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Investment Asks, How Much Can You Afford to Lose?', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Investment Asks, How Much Can You Afford to Lose?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T21:48:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e6d21a9-2cef-51f5-9fca-0ea88a10ba52', 'word_count': 1231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e6d21a9-2cef-51f5-9fca-0ea88a10ba52'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis take on questions about the value of movie remakes, finding the quality films at the local theater and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/movies/questions-for-the-film-critics-scott-and-dargis.html', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis take on questions about the value of movie remakes, finding the quality films at the local theater and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis, the co-chief film critics of The New York Times, answer your questions in a monthly column that appears in print and online. This month readers asked about remakes (pro and con), big-screen films on small-screen devices, and more. You can write Ms. Dargis and Mr. Scott at askthefilmcritics@nytimes.com. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'cro