, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gruber, Ruth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac08b5ac-ae07-5a7c-91fe-cf768134b2dd', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac08b5ac-ae07-5a7c-91fe-cf768134b2dd'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the case of a psychic accused of having persuaded a client to hand over thousands of dollars, the Newark Peace Education Summit, and a fire in Bushwick, Brooklyn, that killed two people.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/the-week-in-pictures-for-may-20/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the case of a psychic accused of having persuaded a client to hand over thousands of dollars, the Newark Peace Education Summit, and a fire in Bushwick, Brooklyn, tha...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the case of a psychic who is accused of having persuaded a client to hand over thousands of dollars to cure herself of an unhealthy attachment to money, the Newark Peace Education Summit, a Brooklyn fire that killed two people and former Gov. James E. McGreevey of New Jersey, who hopes to be accepted as a candidate for the priesthood in the Episcopal Church.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Pictures for May 20', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:05:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/59fadbf6-aa1d-5f4b-9d01-0b3ffadef821', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59fadbf6-aa1d-5f4b-9d01-0b3ffadef821'}, {'abstract': 'The president tells a jubilant crowd at the spy agency that the Bin Laden operation was ‘one of the greatest intelligence successes in American history.’', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/obama-praises-c-i-a-for-bin-laden-raid/', 'snippet': 'The president tells a jubilant crowd at the spy agency that the Bin Laden operation was ‘one of the greatest intelligence successes in American history.’', 'lead_paragraph': ' Praising them for what he called “one of the greatest intelligence successes in American history,” President Obama told a jubilant crowd at the Central Intelligence Agency on Friday that the tracking and killing of Osama bin Laden was a crucial milestone in the larger American war to “defeat al Qaeda.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Applauds C.I.A. for Bin Laden Raid', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:06:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Mazzetti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazzetti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d4e53c8-26fc-53df-8312-8429207855db', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d4e53c8-26fc-53df-8312-8429207855db'}, {'abstract': 'Nabil Shaath, a leader of the Palestinian president’s party, said President Obama’s speech had “contained little hope for the Palestinians.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/middleeast/21palestinian.html', 'snippet': 'Nabil Shaath, a leader of the Palestinian president’s party, said President Obama’s speech had “contained little hope for the Palestinians.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — After President Obama’s high-profile speech on Thursday in which he laid out broad principles for reaching an Israeli-Palestinian deal, the Palestinian president, Mahmoud Abbas, called an emergency meeting at his headquarters in Ramallah in the West Bank. He advised his associates not to comment on the speech, according to a senior Palestinian official who attended the meeting, but to wait instead for Mr. Obama’s meeting with the prime minister of Israel in the White House “and see if there are any positive signs.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21palestinians_337/21palestinians_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21palestinians_337/21palestinians_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21palestinians_337/21palestinians_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/world/21palestinians_337/