ne, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/JP-PURIFICATION/JP-PURIFICATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/JP-PURIFICATION/JP-PURIFICATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/JP-PURIFICATION/JP-PURIFICATION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/JP-PURIFICATION/JP-PURIFICATION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/fashion/JP-PURIFICATION/JP-PURIFICATION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Age of Purification', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Age of Purification', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Trebay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bb6c8de-65af-5502-9e4d-f81e55bc9f5c', 'word_count': 2003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bb6c8de-65af-5502-9e4d-f81e55bc9f5c'}, {'abstract': 'N.Y. See: a colossal head in the park draws curious photographers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/madison-square-park-1208-p-m/', 'snippet': 'N.Y. See: a colossal head in the park draws curious photographers.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Madison Square Park, 12:08 P.M.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:32:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8a08c30-0fc6-586d-8528-a6614a495325', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8a08c30-0fc6-586d-8528-a6614a495325'}, {'abstract': 'Though there is great stuff to read this week (I promise!), I am going to focus on photography.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/this-weeks-magazine-a-picture-show/', 'snippet': 'Though there is great stuff to read this week (I promise!), I am going to focus on photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though there is great stuff to read this week (I promise!), I am going to focus on photography. Just last week, we won a National Magazine Award for a photo essay by Ashley Gilbertson on the bedrooms of dead U.S. soldiers. But as rewarding as the big events like that are, it’s the week-to-week excellence of our photo director, Kathy Ryan, and her staff that impresses me even more. They don’t pursue a particular style or idea about pictures. Their interests are gloriously catholic. They pull stuff out of archives. They come up with stunning ideas that enliven subjects that have been photographed a million times before. They scour the earth for new talent. And they are always receptive to ideas that photographers bring to them, a more important skill than you might think.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Magazine: A Picture Show', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:32:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/de18ba47-9064-56d7-84ea-795842da6602', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de18ba47-9064-56d7-84ea-795842da6602'}, {'abstract': 'Haley Reinhart received the fewest votes on Thursday night’s elimination episode of “American Idol.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/television/idol-down-to-two-makes-fox-no-1.html', 'snippet': 'Haley Reinhart received the fewest votes on Thursday night’s elimination episode of “American Idol.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Haley Reinhart, above, received the fewest votes on Thursday night’s elimination episode of “American Idol,” as 21.2 million viewers tuned in to the Fox reality show, according to Nielsen’s estimates. Lauren Alaina and Scotty McCreery advanced to next week’s final round. Fox led the night as the top-rated network, garnering 10 million viewers at 9 for “Bones,” the crime drama’s highest ratings for a season finale in three years. But CBS’s “Mentalist” led the time slot with the first half of its two-hour finale, which averaged 13.8 million viewers from 9 to 11. CBS was second, drawing 11.1 million at 8 for “The Big Bang Theory” and 8.8 million at 8:30 for “Rules of Engagement.” ABC finished third with finales of “Wipeout” (5.6 million), “Grey’s Anatomy” (9.6 million) and “Private Practice” (7.5 million). NBC was fourth, averaging 7.1 million viewers for its hourlong season finale of “The Office” at 9 but had lower ratings for “Parks and Recreation” at 10 (4.2 million) and 10:30 (3.7 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Idol,’ Down to Two, Makes Fox No. 1', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Idol,’ Down to Two, Makes Fox No. 1', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5cc4157-77c3-5fdc-a5f3-3591b0c3fadf', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5cc4157-77c3-5fdc-a5f3-3591b0c3fadf'}, {'abstract': 'The PEN American Center will administer the PEN/Bellwether Prize for Socially Engaged Fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/books/footnote.html', 'snippet': 'The PEN American Center will administer the PEN/Bellwether Prize for Socially Engaged Fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The PEN American Center will administer the PEN/Bellwether Prize for Socially Engaged Fiction, an award that was originally founded by Barbara Kingsolver in 2000 as the Bellwether Prize, the organization said on Friday. Beginning in 2012 it will award the prize to the author of a new novel that “exemplifies the prize’s founding principles.” Algonquin Books has agreed to give a publishing contract to at least the next two winners. Going forward, the prize will be awarded every other year.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:42:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/de1ba4ca-7cae-5d49-90fd-2e587b74d40f', 'word_count': 80, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de1ba4ca-7cae-5d49-90fd-2e587b74d40f'}, {'abstract': 'Scandal at the International Monetary Fund, the fiscal crisis in Greece, rising gas prices and continuing problems at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex in Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/21/business/weekly-business-photos.html', 'snippet': 'Scandal at the International Monetary Fund, the fiscal crisis in Greece, rising gas prices and continuing problems at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex in Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scandal at the International Monetary Fund, the fiscal crisis in Greece, rising gas prices and continuing problems at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex in Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/business/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 859, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/business/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/business/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM/weekly-business-photo-slide-KKZM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Business News in Pictures', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:48:26+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8bb71d71-7cee-5a57-86e1-8addab859353', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8bb71d71-7cee-5a57-86e1-8addab859353'}, {'abstract': '“Nameless forest,” choreographed by Dean Moss, uses audience members who sit onstage and join in the performance at the Kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/dance/dean-mosss-nameless-forest-opens-at-the-kitchen.html', 'snippet': '“Nameless forest,” choreographed by Dean Moss, uses audience members who sit onstage and join in the performance at the Kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps nothing is more controversial in experimental dance today than performance style. Most of “Nameless forest,” a new piece by the choreographer Dean Moss that opened at the Kitchen on Thursday night, was unimpeachable. Trying to create a world of human behavior in a three-dimensional set, it involved a number of theater games for its six dancers and for 12 members of the audience, who, invited to sit onstage, at times became involved in both the choreography and some interviews. But it was tediously performed. At Thursday night’s show some of the participating innocent bystanders proved more stageworthy than any of the performers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/FOREST/FOREST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/FOREST/FOREST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/FOREST/FOREST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where an Audience Becomes the Star', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where An Audience Becomes The Star', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moss, Dean', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kitchen, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:49:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/29aeeed3-2521-50f8-81eb-cd6aa57d60bc', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29aeeed3-2521-50f8-81eb-cd6aa57d60bc'}, {'abstract': 'The TMX Group, parent of the Toronto Stock Exchange, said the offer by Canadian banks and pension funds was “inadequate” and not superior to its deal with the London Stock Exchange.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/toronto-exchange-rejects-rival-canadian-bid/', 'snippet': 'The TMX Group, parent of the Toronto Stock Exchange, said the offer by Canadian banks and pension funds was “inadequate” and not superior to its deal with the London Stock Exchange.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The TMX Group, the parent company of the Toronto Stock Exchange, said late on Friday that it had rejected a unsolicited takeover bid from a consortium of Canadian banks and pension funds, saying the offer was “inadequate” and not superior to its merger agreement with the London Stock Exchange.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toronto Exchange Rejects Rival Bid by Canadian Banks', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of Nova Scotia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London Stock Exchange', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Bank of Canada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Ontario Teachers' Pension Plan Board", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TMX Group', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Dominion Bank', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Stock Exchange', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T22:52:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/83bbc8d9-6254-5dec-98f4-fcfbf77fb684', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83bbc8d9-6254-5dec-98f4-fcfbf77fb684'}, {'abstract': 'The Look & Listen Festival included, to look at and to play, a microsonophone, a homemade instrument made of blocks of wood, strung with rubber bands and a stethoscope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/music/look-listen-festival-at-chelsea-art-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Look & Listen Festival included, to look at and to play, a microsonophone, a homemade instrument made of blocks of wood, strung with rubber bands and a stethoscope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wonderfully eclectic Look & Listen Festival opened on Thursday evening with a program that prized vigor and muscularity over intricacy and nuance: a victory lap, of sorts, in celebration of the festival’s 10th anniversary. The point of Look & Listen, as its title suggests, is to bring together music and visual arts, and this year the concerts are in the large, resonant gallery of the Chelsea Art Museum, where an exhibition of colorful abstracts by the painter Jean Miotte surrounds the musicians and audience. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/look-3/look-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/look-3/look-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/arts/look-3/look-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Experiencing Art With Eyes Peeled, Ears Trained and Hands On', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Experiencing Art With Eyes Peeled, Ears Trained and Hands On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JACK Quartet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chelsea Art Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-20T23:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d35b351-ffb4-5231-84bb-b32441d87450', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d35b351-ffb4-5231-84bb-b32441d87450'}, {'abstract': 'Novus NY, Trinity Church’s newly formed new-music ensemble, gave its first formal concert on Thursday night, in a program devoted to Elena Ruehr.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/arts/music/trinity-choir-and-novus-ny-review.html', 'snippet': 'Novus NY, Trinity Church’s newly formed new-music ensemble, gave its first formal concert on Thursday night, in a program devoted to Elena Ruehr.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Trinity Choir, the superb resident ensemble of Trinity Church in Lower Manhattan, is rightly renowned for its polished, spirited renditions of major works from the classical choral repertory. Its activities in contemporary music, though distinguished, have been emphasized less. That changed on Thursday night, when Julian Wachner, Trinity’s director of music and the arts, conducted the first formal concert by Novus NY, the church’s newly formed new-music ensemble.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/ar