ies foisted upon the author by network censors.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/20/the-first-shall-be-last-or-anyway-second/', 'snippet': 'Postponing a talk-show debut was only one of the indignities foisted upon the author by network censors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whoops. I’ve misinformed you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The First Shall Be Last — Or, Anyway, Second', 'kicker': 'Dick Cavett', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T01:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dick Cavett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cavett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee10d950-7d3e-53ba-9c5f-25bc4f16337c', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee10d950-7d3e-53ba-9c5f-25bc4f16337c'}, {'abstract': 'Fortune Brands said on Friday that it had agreed to sell its golf business to a group led by the owner of the Fila sports brand for $1.23 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/business/global/21bizbriefs-FORTUNEBRAND_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Fortune Brands said on Friday that it had agreed to sell its golf business to a group led by the owner of the Fila sports brand for $1.23 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fortune Brands said on Friday that it had agreed to sell its golf business to a group led by the owner of the Fila sports brand for $1.23 billion. The sale of the business, which includes the Titleist and FootJoy brands, is the first big step toward a planned breakup of Fortune Brands, long an unusual conglomerate with a big spirits portfolio and home and hardware business, in addition to golf products. The buyers are led by Fila Korea and Mirae Asset Private Equity, the largest private equity firm in South Korea. Fortune Brands said it expected to net $1.1 billion after taxes and expenses. The golf business accounted for nearly a fifth of the total revenue of Fortune Brands. Nearly half of the $1.2 billion in net sales for the company’s golf products came in markets outside the United States. The business had operating income of $89 million in 2010. After the split, Fortune Brands will be a liquor company, with brands like Jim Beam, Canadian Club, Courvoisier, Maker’s Mark and Laphroaig. Fortune said it expected to complete the breakup in the fourth quarter. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fortune Brands Sells Its Golf Line for $1.2 Billion', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Leisure Goods', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fortune Brands Sells Its Golf Line for $1.2 Billion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortune Brands Incorporated', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T01:05:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By NYT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nyt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a3c0ebc-ab13-515d-9278-37381c88854b', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a3c0ebc-ab13-515d-9278-37381c88854b'}, {'abstract': 'Acorda Therapeutics won the backing of European Union regulators for a drug it makes with Biogen Idec to improve walking in multiple sclerosis patients, setting off a 14 percent gain in its share price.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/business/21bizbriefs-BACKINGFORMU_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Acorda Therapeutics won the backing of European Union regulators for a drug it makes with Biogen Idec to improve walking in multiple sclerosis patients, setting off a 14 percent gain in its share price.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Acorda Therapeutics, a biotechnology company, won the backing of European Union regulators for a drug it makes with Biogen Idec to improve walking in multiple sclerosis patients, setting off a 14 percent gain in its share price. The regulators, at the European Medicines Agency’s Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, recommended conditional marketing authorization for the drug, Fampyra, and asked for further study of the treatment. The drug was approved in the United States in January 2010 and generated $133.1 million last year for Acorda. Biogen markets the medicine outside the United States and pays Acorda royalties. Shares of Acorda, which is based in Hawthorne, N.Y., rose $3.67, or 13.7 percent, to $30.38. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Backing for Multiple Sclerosis Drug Lifts Stock', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Backing for Multiple Sclerosis Drug Lifts Stock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Biogen Idec Incorporated', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Acorda Therapeutics Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Medicines Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Sclerosis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T01:05:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3405fa97-55ec-54b0-adbd-2f4ec7d1cc22', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3405fa97-55ec-54b0-adbd-2f4ec7d1cc22'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators believe that the burglaries of a Knights of Columbus lodge and a nearby tattoo shop in Brooklyn may have been committed by the same person.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/nyregion/brooklyn-burglaries-link-knights-of-columbus-and-tattoo-parlor.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators believe that the burglaries of a Knights of Columbus lodge and a nearby tattoo shop in Brooklyn may have been committed by the same person.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While politics is usually the matchmaker in these things, this time it had nothing to do with the strange bedfellows found at “The Beach.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/nyregion/CRIMESCENE1/CRIMESCENE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/21/nyregion/CRIMESCENE1/CRIMESCENE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/21/nyregion/CRIMESCENE1/CRIMESCENE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Burglar Led Into Temptation by Catholics and Tattoos', 'k