International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagarde, Christine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fraga Neto, Arminio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T02:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/081a9060-fbf8-59a5-9163-510ae1d0c53a', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/081a9060-fbf8-59a5-9163-510ae1d0c53a'}, {'abstract': 'The German Defense Ministry acknowledged that its troops had fired on demonstrators during a violent protest in the northern Afghan city of Taliqan this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/asia/21briefs-Afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'The German Defense Ministry acknowledged that its troops had fired on demonstrators during a violent protest in the northern Afghan city of Taliqan this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The German Defense Ministry acknowledged Friday that its troops had fired on demonstrators during a violent protest in the northern Afghan city of Taliqan this week, wounding several civilians and possibly killing some. The demonstrations erupted Wednesday after a joint American and Afghan night raid at a family compound just outside the city that left four people dead, two of them women. NATO said the four were insurgents who tried to fire at its troops, but local officials insisted they were civilians. When the protesters tried to attack a small German-run NATO base on the outskirts of the city, the ministry said, German troops fired warning shots in the air before shooting at the legs of the attackers. Some protesters were hit in the torso and at least one was hit in the neck and head region, the statement said. At least 12 people were killed in the two days of protests. German authorities said they were investigating the shootings. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan: German Troops Fired on Protesters', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan: German Troops Fired on Protesters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T02:26:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Rivera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa22143e-7ae4-5b55-8149-42630c682052', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa22143e-7ae4-5b55-8149-42630c682052'}, {'abstract': 'He has vowed to remake his country. Doing so will mean making difficult decisions, even if they displease entrenched elites, and delivering tangible improvements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/opinion/21sat3.html', 'snippet': 'He has vowed to remake his country. Doing so will mean making difficult decisions, even if they displease entrenched elites, and delivering tangible improvements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The peaceful inauguration this month of Haiti’s new president, Michel Martelly, should give Haitians cause for pride and cautious hope that their country can move beyond mere survival and start rebuilding. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Haiti’s President Martelly', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Haiti’s President Martelly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martelly, Michel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T02:27:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/75a09ae2-eb1c-5161-8527-90215abf9f0f', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75a09ae2-eb1c-5161-8527-90215abf9f0f'}, {'abstract': 'Former President Jacques Chirac, 78, is accused of embezzling public funds as Paris mayor to pay people working for his party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/europe/21briefs-France.html', 'snippet': 'Former President Jacques Chirac, 78, is accused of embezzling public funds as Paris mayor to pay people working for his party.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The corruption trial of former President Jacques Chirac, suspended in March, can go ahead, France’s highest appeals court ruled Friday. Mr. Chirac, 78, the first former French president to go on trial since Marshal Philippe Pétain after World War II, is accused of embezzling public funds as Paris mayor to pay people working for his party. He has denied knowledge of any illegal payments. Co-defendants had argued in vain that some charges were unconstitutional under the statute of limitations. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France: Chirac Corruption Trial to Go Forward', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France: Chirac Corruption Trial to Go Forward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chirac, Jacques', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T02:27:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/29293933-efae-500b-8ffc-787fb9ee3668', 'word_count': 81, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29293933-efae-500b-8ffc-787fb9ee3668'}, {'abstract': 'A company controlled by the dissident artist Ai Weiwei evaded a “huge amount” of taxes and intentionally destroyed financial documents, the government’s official news agency, reported Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/world/asia/21briefs-China.html', 'snippet': 'A company controlled by the dissident artist Ai Weiwei evaded a “huge amount” of taxes and intentionally destroyed financial documents, the government’s official news agency, reported Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A company controlled by the dissident artist Ai Weiwei evaded a “huge amount” of taxes and intentionally destroyed financial documents, Xinhua, the government’s official news agency, reported Friday. Xinhua, quoting the police, said that the authorities were focusing on Beijing Fake Cultural Development Ltd. The artist’s sister, Gao Ge, told Reuters that Mr. Ai is neither the company’s legal representative nor its chief executive. “So even if the company is accused of these crimes, Ai Weiwei should not be detained,” she said. Mr. Ai has been detained at an undisclosed location since April 3.', 'print_se