cument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Rivera and Sharifullah Sahak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sharifullah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sahak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a09ad06-93ea-527b-856b-47ad3f520c88', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a09ad06-93ea-527b-856b-47ad3f520c88'}, {'abstract': 'With “Miss Bala,” the Mexican director Gerardo Naranjo aims to leave people as confused about that country’s drug war as he is as Mexican reading the news.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/cannes-q-and-a-gerardo-naranjo-and-mexicos-state-of-fear/', 'snippet': 'With “Miss Bala,” the Mexican director Gerardo Naranjo aims to leave people as confused about that country’s drug war as he is as Mexican reading the news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France— In his new film “Miss Bala,” the director Gerardo Naranjo, tackles a topical issue — drug violence in Mexico — though an unlikely protagonist: an aspiring Tijuana beauty queen (Stephanie Sigman) who gets caught up with the local kingpins. Instead of delving into the social or political aspects of the problem, docudrama-style, Mr. Naranjo fashions a tense mood piece steeped in confusion and anxiety. He sticks to the captive heroine’s frequently befogged point of view while ruthless cartel operatives and corrupt cops remain on the periphery or outside the frame, their actions often unseen and their motivations murky. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cannes Q. and A.: Gerardo Naranjo and Mexico’s State of Fear', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Naranjo, Gerardo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Lim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4b533bf-063b-5735-b082-d9d009611baa', 'word_count': 1088, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4b533bf-063b-5735-b082-d9d009611baa'}, {'abstract': 'Some tennis followers are shoveling dirt on 29-year-old Roger Federer’s chances of winning Roland Garros and the other majors.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/at-french-open-this-plot-looks-familiar/', 'snippet': 'Some tennis followers are shoveling dirt on 29-year-old Roger Federer’s chances of winning Roland Garros and the other majors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Being the third wheel, almost an afterthought, going into Slam tournaments can’t be easy if you’re a player who has gone into such events as a favorite for nearly a decade. I mean, imagine if you started winning Slam titles when you were 21. At one point you were No. 1 on the computer for so many consecutive weeks that people had a hard time remembering who held the spot before you did.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At French Open, This Plot Looks Familiar', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'secti