anization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b728446-6e6e-5f48-b8cf-42be68e3dece', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b728446-6e6e-5f48-b8cf-42be68e3dece'}, {'abstract': 'Curse words litter play titles, pop songs and everyday talk. But a new book shows where they’re still subversive: in parenthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/weekinreview/22curse.html', 'snippet': 'Curse words litter play titles, pop songs and everyday talk. But a new book shows where they’re still subversive: in parenthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OF the many moments during the day in which parents and children operate at cross purposes, none are so fraught as bedtime. After a certain amount of hair detangling, into-your-pajamas-now cajoling, recitations of “Goodnight Moon” and singing of shopworn lullabies, the parent is done. Done! And this moment is precisely when the child gets started: the repeat trip to the bathroom, the urgent request for water, the sudden fear of the radiator, the artfully misplaced lovey. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/opinion/2paulimg/2paulimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/opinion/2paulimg/2paulimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 530}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/opinion/2paulimg/2paulimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/opinion/2paulimg/2paulimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/02/opinion/2paulimg/2paulimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raising Children Is Heck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raising Children Is Heck', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mansbach, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indecency, Obscenity and Profanity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T20:54:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9944b35-1b2e-5b30-acda-1bf91b322790', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9944b35-1b2e-5b30-acda-1bf91b322790'}, {'abstract': 'The Preakness mascot, Kegasus, might have drawn the ire of legislators and traditionalists within the industry, but the ad campaign appears to have worked.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/mythical-creatures-in-the-infield-calm-crowds/', 'snippet': 'The Preakness mascot, Kegasus, might have drawn the ire of legislators and traditionalists within the industry, but the ad campaign appears to have worked.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:59 p.m. | Updated Attendance was 107,398, the sixth largest in Preakness history. Last year’s attendance was 95,760. The overall handle was $76,376,689. The last time the attendance was over 100,000 was in 2008, the last year alcoholic beverages were allowed to be brought into the infield.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mythical Creatures in the Infield: Calm Crowds', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T20:56:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Hoppert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoppert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7581360a-3eb1-55fa-8254-de407078b7fa', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7581360a-3eb1-55fa-8254-de407078b7fa'}, {'abstract': 'New studies have raised hopes for curbing transmission — if H.I.V. patients take treatment long before they feel sick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/weekinreview/22aids.html', 'snippet': 'New studies have raised hopes for curbing transmission — if H.I.V. patients take treatment long before they feel sick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE is now, for the first time, hard clinical evidence of an effect that AIDS doctors have suspected for years: If you are H.I.V.-positive, being on antiretroviral drugs will probably save not only your life, but also the lives of your sexual partners. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/McNIELalt/McNIELalt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/McNIELalt/McNIELalt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/McNIELalt/McNIELalt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 978, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/McNIELalt/McNIELalt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/McNIELalt/McNIELalt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drugs Stop AIDS. Take Your Medicine.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drugs Stop AIDS. Take Your Medicine.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bayer, Ronald', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexually Transmitted Diseases', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Markel, Howard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'El-Sadr, Wafaa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bentham, Jeremy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frieden, Thomas R', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T20:57:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14219a64-813c-527f-b286-458094a1147b', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14219a64-813c-527f-b286-458094a1147b'}, {'abstract': '27: The number of sequels on tap from Hollywood this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/weekinreview/22prime.html', 'snippet': '27: The number of sequels on tap from Hollywood this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The number of sequels on tap from Hollywood this year, many of them coming out this summer, according to a tally by Brandon Gray of Box Office Mojo, cited by the critic Roger Ebert in Newsweek. That is a record, topping the previous high of 24 sequels in 2003, Mr. Gray says. The trips back to the cinematic well (or chum bucket, as the case may be) include “Kung Fu Panda 2,” “Paranormal Activity 3,” “Scream 4,” “Fast Five,” and the eighth Harry Potter movie, “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows (Part Two).” You might call the latter a sequel of a sequel of a sequel of a sequel of a sequel of a sequel of a sequel.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prime Number', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prime Number: 27', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ebert, Roger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T21:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b58f71a4-93c8-51d6-a2a6-150114e48ca8', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b58f71a4-93c8-51d6-a2a6-150114e48ca8'}, {'abstract': 'The series featuring the writings of philosophers on topics both timely and timeless returns to Opinionator. But why?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/the-stone-returns/', 'snippet': 'The series featuring the writings of philosophers on topics both timely and timeless returns to Opinionator. But why?', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re delighted to be bringing back The Stone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stone Returns', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T21:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Critchley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Critchley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a00870c6-1f92-553d-8a8c-fe84d381bf1f', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a00870c6-1f92-553d-8a8c-fe84d381bf1f'}, {'abstract': 'Readers offer their reaction to my quiz on religion and sex — and tell me what questions I should have asked.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/reader-comments-on-my-religion-quiz/', 'snippet': 'Readers offer their reaction to my quiz on religion and sex — and tell me what questions I should have asked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The back story behind my Sunday column is a measure of irritation that the Bible is so often cited as a basis for what seem to me to be archaic or backward positions on social issues — such as support for slavery in the 19th century, or opposition to gay marriage today. In particular, there’s a widespread sense that religion is deeply conservative on social and sexual issues, when in fact it’s pretty much all over the map and is much more focused on questions of social justice. So that’s why I wrote this 2011 religion quiz as my Sunday column. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Comments on my Religion Quiz', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T21:04:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd31ad96-f97c-5a54-9b21-803329ba81b3', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd31ad96-f97c-5a54-9b21-803329ba81b3'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to our coverage of the 136th running of the Preakness Stakes.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/welcome-to-the-136th-preakness-stakes/', 'snippet': 'Welcome to our coverage of the 136th running of the Preakness Stakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — Shackleford, who appeared agitated before the race, was able to edge the Derby winner Animal Kingdom by a half length to win the Preakness Stakes on Saturday at Pimlico Race Course. Astrology finished third.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shackleford Wins Preakness Stakes', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T21:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Hoppert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoppert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d50aecbe-e17d-5d6c-9af8-06344fa64de1', 'word_count': 1336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d50aecbe-e17d-5d6c-9af8-06344fa64de1'}, {'abstract': 'Queen Elizabeth II’s visit to Ireland last week seemed less tense than the last visit by a reigning British monarch, a century ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/weekinreview/22backthen.html', 'snippet': 'Queen Elizabeth II’s visit to Ireland last week seemed less tense than the last visit by a reigning British monarch, a century ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A minor bomb scare aside, Queen Elizabeth II’s visit to Ireland last week seemed less tense than the last visit by a reigning British monarch, a century ago, before the creation of the Irish Free State in 1922. The New York Times reported from Dublin on July 8, 1911, that — amid tight security in the “poorest and roughest” district — a sergeant’s arm was broken in a scuffle before King George V’s entourage arrived, at which time “men and women who had been jeering the police and soldiers broke into hearty cheers.” Elsewhere, the report said, popular sentiment was “well expressed by a banner stretched outside a City Council hall at Pembroke, a suburb of the capital, which refused to address the King, declaring, ‘Welcome. We Want Home Rule.’\xa0”', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/BACK/BACK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/BACK/BACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/weekinreview/BACK/BACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back Then: 1911', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back Then: 1911', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George V, King of Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 