: {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/122c9910-8217-5c15-891b-4678fca9d945', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/122c9910-8217-5c15-891b-4678fca9d945'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrate a New York City institution with Bob Klahn.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/21/writer/', 'snippet': 'Celebrate a New York City institution with Bob Klahn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUNDAY’S PUZZLE — As someone who grew up in New York City, one of my fondest memories was of childhood trips to the main branch of the New York Public Library (now known as the Stephen A. Schwartzman Building). I consider myself lucky to have walked among the majestic columns, up the stairs past the solemn, stone lions that still guard the entrance and through the great halls into the reading rooms. In a quasi-Proustian way, I can still remember the smell of the books and the card catalogs, a sense memory that I wonder if today’s children will ever have. Can you really dredge up the memory of how an e-book smelled?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday: Fabulous Writer?', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T22:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/30f0d08d-d68d-52f3-b017-e9f8aa3aff61', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30f0d08d-d68d-52f3-b017-e9f8aa3aff61'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of financially needy high school students hoping to receive Texas Grants have had to put their college plans on hold while the State Legislature makes decisions on budget cuts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/us/22ttgrants.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of financially needy high school students hoping to receive Texas Grants have had to put their college plans on hold while the State Legislature makes decisions on budget cuts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Texas Legislature made a promise to the state’s financially neediest high school seniors in 1999: Money will not be a barrier to your higher education at a public college or university if you stay out of trouble, complete certain courses and graduate from high school.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22TTGRANTS/22TTGRANTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22TTGRANTS/22TTGRANTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22TTGRANTS/22TTGRANTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Students Wait as Legislators Decide Cuts', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Students Wait as Legislators Decide Cuts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T22:03:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thanh Tan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thanh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfb59c06-af60-51af-9501-2e3923fe9b79', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfb59c06-af60-51af-9501-2e3923fe9b79'}, {'abstract': 'As a result of the supermajority of Republicans in the Texas House, the Tea Party’s objectives have been the top priorities of lawmakers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/us/22ttteaparty.html', 'snippet': 'As a result of the supermajority of Republicans in the Texas House, the Tea Party’s objectives have been the top priorities of lawmakers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ideological partnership of the raucous Tea Party movement and fiscally conservative Texas Republicans has dominated the agenda of the state lawmaking session that is sputtering to an end. Gov. Rick Perry embraced the Tea Party ideals before most knew the movement was brewing, and the conservative, anti-tax activism helped bring a supermajority of Republicans into the Texas House. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22TEAPARTY1/22TEAPARTY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tea Party Sets the Agenda, and Legislators Fall in Line', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tea Party Sets the Agenda, And Legislators Fall in Line', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'majo