{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buffalo (NY)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T23:50:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ecff03d-d570-5585-803b-c91437edb1b0', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ecff03d-d570-5585-803b-c91437edb1b0'}, {'abstract': 'I wonder what my horses will feel when they find themselves turned loose in a pasture they’ve spent such a long time contemplating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/opinion/22sun3.html', 'snippet': 'I wonder what my horses will feel when they find themselves turned loose in a pasture they’ve spent such a long time contemplating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the past 13 years, my horses have gazed across a gravel road toward a neighbor’s pasture, where her horses stand, gazing back. Kinship, affinity, species recognition, herd instinct, longing — I don’t know what to call this habit. But by the time you read this, my neighbor and I will have moved my horses into her pasture. She has plenty of extra grass, and my land can use a rest.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tomorrow to Pastures New', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Rural Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tomorrow to Pastures New', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-21T23:54:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2646535-e460-5afb-89dd-822062bbd38a', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2646535-e460-5afb-89dd-822062bbd38a'}, {'abstract': 'Stars and dimmer lights deal in a world of dirt and money, not always unwillingly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/us/22gossip.html', 'snippet': 'Stars and dimmer lights deal in a world of dirt and money, not always unwillingly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — In late July 2010, the Southampton, N.Y., police charged Michael Lohan, the father of the actress Lindsay Lohan, with physically harassing his fiancée. When the news hit on the gossip Web site TMZ, Mr. Lohan was at the Sunset Tower in Los Angeles, where he had been on the interview circuit discussing his daughter’s imprisonment stemming from drunken-driving charges.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22gossip-new337/22gossip-new337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22gossip-new337/22gossip-new337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/22/us/22gossip-new337/22gossip-new337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gossip Machine, Churning Out Cash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'pri