0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/becf1d46-60c6-59ac-aafc-a05181139b50', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/becf1d46-60c6-59ac-aafc-a05181139b50'}, {'abstract': 'Genre movies are typically relegated to the sidelines at major film festivals, but Nicolas Winding Refn’s “Drive,” starring Ryan Gosling, is helping to shift the balance at Cannes.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/cannes-q-and-a-driving-in-a-noir-l-a/', 'snippet': 'Genre movies are typically relegated to the sidelines at major film festivals, but Nicolas Winding Refn’s “Drive,” starring Ryan Gosling, is helping to shift the balance at Cannes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France— Genre movies are typically relegated to the sidelines at the world’s major film festivals, but Cannes this year has done its part to shift the balance. Alongside auteurs like the Dardenne brothers and Aki Kaurismaki, the main competition also features a few surprising Cannes first-timers, like Japan’s Takashi Miike, whose samurai drama “Hara-kiri” is the first 3-D movie to screen in competition, and Denmark’s Nicolas Winding Refn, best known for the violent “Pusher” trilogy and the stylized thug portrait “Bronson,” who is here with his Los Angeles-set noir “Drive.” (On Sunday, Mr. Refn won the festival’s best director prize.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cannes Q. and A.: Driving in a Noir L.A.', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosling, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'