ity of purpose in their creation of eloquent work.” If that’s a little ambiguous, this isn’t: One had more purpose and eloquence than the other. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/STENN1/STENN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 809, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/STENN1/STENN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/STENN1/STENN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Insects Flee, and Kitty Seeks Revenge', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Insects Flee, and Kitty Seeks Revenge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stenn, Rebecca', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T20:58:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/23ec2dc5-9b5b-5b3a-9279-0d64500b1739', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23ec2dc5-9b5b-5b3a-9279-0d64500b1739'}, {'abstract': 'The soprano Kate Royal packed 29 songs into “A Lesson in Love,” her recital on Friday at Weill Recital Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/music/kate-royal-in-a-lesson-in-love-at-weill-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The soprano Kate Royal packed 29 songs into “A Lesson in Love,” her recital on Friday at Weill Recital Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you consider that opera and art song tend to reach their most unforgettable heights and depths at moments of vulnerability and tragedy, it is no surprise that the final part of the soprano Kate Royal’s concert at Weill Recital Hall on Friday was the strongest.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/2NDSUBKATE/2NDSUBKATE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/2NDSUBKATE/2NDSUBKATE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/2NDSUBKATE/2NDSUBKATE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/2NDSUBKATE/2NDSUBKATE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/2NDSUBKATE/2NDSUBKATE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Meeting, Marriage and Then Trouble', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Meeting, Marriage and Then Trouble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Royal, Kate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f4a2fda-eedb-53fc-ba53-022ab93fcdab', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f4a2fda-eedb-53fc-ba53-022ab93fcdab'}, {'abstract': 'The HBO film “Too Big to Fail,” based on a book about the financial crisis of 2008, uses every cinematic trick in the book, but ultimately succeeds because we know that the danger was real.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/television/too-big-to-fail-on-hbo-review.html', 'snippet': 'The HBO film “Too Big to Fail,” based on a book about the financial crisis of 2008, uses every cinematic trick in the book, but ultimately succeeds because we know that the danger was real.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It can’t be easy to create a financial thriller. There’s no blood, there are (usually) no bodies. How do you create excitement when most of the action consists of middle-aged white men in conservative suits talking on the phone, and the closest thing to a car chase is a stately procession of big black Town Cars? How often can you show people peering at spreadsheets and recoiling in alarm? What do you do when the scandal itself might as well be labeled “Too Complicated to Understand”? The HBO film “Too Big to Fail,” based on the book of the same name by the reporter Andrew Ross Sorkin of The New York Times, about the financial crisis of 2008, uses every cinematic trick in the book, but ultimately succeeds because we know that the danger was real.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/Big/Big-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 747, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Crisis Unfurls in Hushed Suspense', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Economic Crisis Unfurls In Hushed Suspense', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorkin, Andrew Ross', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:06:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kinsley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kinsley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3328afd8-34cf-5980-8958-209ac9b73cc4', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3328afd8-34cf-5980-8958-209ac9b73cc4'}, {'abstract': 'Wordless Music, a series run by Ronen Givony, is devoted to showing fans of indie rock and contemporary classical music that the two genres have a common appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/music/wordless-music-orchestra-and-ensemble-signal-review.html', 'snippet': 'Wordless Music, a series run by Ronen Givony, is devoted to showing fans of indie rock and contemporary classical music that the two genres have a common appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wordless Music, the concert series run by Ronen Givony when he is not overseeing the classical and new-music programming at Le Poisson Rouge, is devoted to showing fans of indie rock and contemporary classical music that the two genres have a common appeal. That argument no longer requires special pleading, partly because Mr. Givony’s thoughtful juxtapositions have made the point so persuasively but also because so many young composers (and some of their elders) draw freely on both their classical and pop antecedents. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/WORDLESS/WORDLESS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rock and Classical Collide With Bowie and a Score by Radiohead Star', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rock and Classical Collide With Bowie and a Score by Radiohead Star', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenwood, Jonny (1971- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Givony, Ronen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Worldess Music Series', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lubman, Brad', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/21e16a54-53d6-5014-b3ad-2b543ae1f586', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21e16a54-53d6-5014-b3ad-2b543ae1f586'}, {'abstract': 'A slew of new shows feature the fantastic, including “Grimm” on NBC, and “Once Upon a Time” on ABC.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/business/media/23adco.html', 'snippet': 'A slew of new shows feature the fantastic, including “Grimm” on NBC, and “Once Upon a Time” on ABC.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was not so long ago that commercials were filled with fantastical characters like green giants, flying maids and white knights. For the 2011-12 television season, it will be the shows, not the ads, that feature creatures out of fairy tales and ghost stories.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/business/ADCO-1/ADCO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/23/business/ADCO-1/ADCO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/business/ADCO-1/ADCO-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/business/ADCO-1/ADCO-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/business/ADCO-1/ADCO-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In TV Pilots, Paranormal Is the New Normal', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In TV Pilots, Paranormal Is the New Normal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fairy Tales', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:11:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb876818-656d-5218-a909-f6b46ca300a6', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb876818-656d-5218-a909-f6b46ca300a6'}, {'abstract': 'The grave of Robert Selden Garnett, killed in Virginia in 1861, has been found in Green-Wood Cemetery, where a Civil War sesquicentennial commemoration is planned.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/confederate-general-buried-far-from-the-battlefield/', 'snippet': 'The grave of Robert Selden Garnett, killed in Virginia in 1861, has been found in Green-Wood Cemetery, where a Civil War sesquicentennial commemoration is planned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Selden Garnett, the first general killed in the Civil War, was buried in Green-Wood Cemetery in Brooklyn, but his family did not want visitors to the cemetery to know it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confederate General Buried Far From the Battlefield', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Green-Wood Cemetery (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Rex Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'Rex', 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/85d7ff80-2809-5f81-a14d-e677e5e10fbc', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85d7ff80-2809-5f81-a14d-e677e5e10fbc'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter 2011 is a wiki-wit machine specializing in one-liners in the spirit of Dave Chappelle, Chris Rock and Wanda Sykes.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/making-a-hashtag-of-it/', 'snippet': 'Twitter 2011 is a wiki-wit machine specializing in one-liners in the spirit of Dave Chappelle, Chris Rock and Wanda Sykes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve been watching media debaters flip the pro-con coin about the Internet lately, you know by now that Twitter is either a noble catalyst of the Arab Spring or an atomizer of noxious moronism whose users might as well be huffing computer duster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making a Hashtag of It', 'kicker': 'Virginia Heffernan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/489ac91c-2390-589c-addd-c8a5960462f6', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/489ac91c-2390-589c-addd-c8a5960462f6'}, {'abstract': 'Two renowned Moroccan maalems (master musicians) made their United States debuts: Hassan Zgarhi from Marrakesh, who is known as a staunch traditionalist, and Mahmoud Guinea from Essaouira.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/music/master-gnaoua-musicians-in-morocco-festival-review.html', 'snippet': 'Two renowned Moroccan maalems (master musicians) made their United States debuts: Hassan Zgarhi from Marrakesh, who is known as a staunch traditionalist, and Mahmoud Guinea from Essaouira.', 'lead_paragraph': 'West African slaves, transported far from home, held on to rituals that praise saints and spirits with songs, dances, galvanizing rhythms and trance possession. That’s the story of voodoo and Santería in the Americas, and it’s also the story of Gnawa music in Morocco, made by descendants of slaves brought north from what is now Mali. As part of its World Nomads Morocco Festival, the French Institute Alliance Française brought “Master Gnaoua Musicians” to Florence Gould Hall on Saturday night. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/SUBMASTERS/SUBMASTERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/SUBMASTERS/SUBMASTERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/SUBMASTERS/SUBMASTERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/SUBMASTERS/SUBMASTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/SUBMASTERS/SUBMASTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mystical Morocco Bursting With Tribal Rhythm', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mystical Morocco Bursting With Tribal Rhythm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:35:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3327ab5c-1061-5c36-b59b-0ca30a222567', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3327ab5c-1061-5c36-b59b-0ca30a222567'}, {'abstract': 'Why historians of philosophy must resist the contemporary demand for relevance.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/22/the-flight-of-curiosity/', 'snippet': 'Why historians of philosophy must resist the contemporary demand for relevance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Must one be endowed with curiosity in order to become a philosopher?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Flight of Curiosity', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:35:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin E.h. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'h', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/efcdfff8-90ec-5f97-b3fd-9d39ed477529', 'word_count': 2286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efcdfff8-90ec-5f97-b3fd-9d39ed477529'}, {'abstract': 'Marking the 100th anniversary of Mahler’s death, the New Jersey Symphony Orchestra played the Third Symphony.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/music/new-jersey-symphony-orchestra-plays-mahlers-third-review.html', 'snippet': 'Marking the 100th anniversary of Mahler’s death, the New Jersey Symphony Orchestra played the Third Symphony.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — One hundred years ago last Wednesday, Gustav Mahler died of a heart infection. He was living in Vienna, but three months earlier he had conducted the New York Philharmonic at Carnegie Hall in his final appearance on a podium. That February concert ended a three-year period in which he reigned in New York, first as a conductor at the Metropolitan Opera and then as the Philharmonic’s music director.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/MAHLER/MAHLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/MAHLER/MAHLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/MAHLER/MAHLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Subtlety and Power, Observing Mahler’s Moment', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Subtlety and Power, Observing Mahler’s Moment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Gustav', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacombe, Jacques', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:36:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5c3a995-fa0d-5ed4-8015-014e4c8f45b7', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5c3a995-fa0d-5ed4-8015-014e4c8f45b7'}, {'abstract': 'With Lady Gaga performing and Justin Timberlake hosting over the weekend, “Saturday Night Live” recorded its highest ratings for a season finale in seven years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/arts/television/pop-stars-lift-snl-ratings.html', 'snippet': 'With Lady Gaga performing and Justin Timberlake hosting over the weekend, “Saturday Night Live” recorded its highest ratings for a season finale in seven years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe it was the Gaga effect, or maybe it was J. T., or most likely a bit of both: with Lady Gaga performing and Justin Timberlake, together below, hosting over the weekend, “Saturday Night Live” recorded its highest ratings for a season finale in seven years. Nielsen reported that the show averaged a 7.0 rating in major local markets — meaning about 7 percent of homes with televisions watched the episode — up from a 5.8 rating for the same episode last season. NBC said that rating was high enough to match the season finale in 2004, when the Olsen twins, Mary-Kate and Ashley, were the hosts and J-Kwon was the performer. Apart from a night in January when the show was preceded by a major football game, Saturday’s episode also received a season-high rating. The episode was particularly well-received online for its revival of several past Timberlake sketches and for Lady Gaga’s appearances in several of the sketches.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Stars Lift ‘SNL’ Ratings', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop Stars Lift ‘SNL’ Ratings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Timberlake, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:37:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b397425f-b4f5-537c-bd03-0d27d955bfa1', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b397425f-b4f5-537c-bd03-0d27d955bfa1'}, {'abstract': 'HarperCollins plans to publish a new novel by Michael Crichton, the best-selling author of “Jurassic Park” and “The Andromeda Strain,” who died of cancer in November 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/books/nonfiction-writer-finishes-crichton-novel.html', 'snippet': 'HarperCollins plans to publish a new novel by Michael Crichton, the best-selling author of “Jurassic Park” and “The Andromeda Strain,” who died of cancer in November 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HarperCollins plans to publish a new novel by Michael Crichton, the best-selling author of “Jurassic Park” and “The Andromeda Strain,” who died of cancer in November 2008. The book is to be released in November with assistance from the noted nonfiction author Richard Preston, The Associated Press reported. Mr. Crichton wrote about one-third of “Micro,” a technological thriller, before his death. Mr. Preston, best known for his book “The Hot Zone,” about the Ebola virus, used Mr. Crichton’s personal reference material, outline and notes to finish the novel. “When I first read Michael Crichton’s unfinished manuscript, I could practically feel his excitement filling the pages,” Mr. Preston said in a news release from the publisher. “Michael was writing at the top of his game, with a grand sense of adventure, into an eerie world that seems almost beyond imagining.” He added that he sought “to honor the work and imagination of one of our time’s most visionary and creative authors.” In 2009 HarperCollins also published “Pirate Latitudes,” which Mr. Crichton had finished.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonfiction Writer Finishes Crichton Novel', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonfiction Writer Finishes Crichton Novel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HarperCollins Publishers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crichton, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:38:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5311b36-4f35-5cd6-8890-2de78378524f', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5311b36-4f35-5cd6-8890-2de78378524f'}, {'abstract': 'St. Ann’s Warehouse in Dumbo, Brooklyn, will host Labapalooza!, an annual minifestival celebrating new puppet and object theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/theater/festival-for-puppets.html', 'snippet': 'St. Ann’s Warehouse in Dumbo, Brooklyn, will host Labapalooza!, an annual minifestival celebrating new puppet and object theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'St. Ann’s Warehouse in Dumbo, Brooklyn, will host Labapalooza!, an annual minifestival celebrating new puppet and object theater. Guided by the directors Matthew Acheson and Tom Lee, St. Ann’s Puppet Lab artists will present a range of works including “Interference and Collapse,” a look at the death of the R&B singer Sam Cooke, and “Senseless! A Brick Foley Adventure,” a hard-boiled tale investigating a series of murders at the Helen Keller School of Music, the theater announced. Financed by the Jim Henson Foundation, the New York City Department of Cultural Affairs and the New York State Council on the Arts, Labapalooza!, in its 13th year, will be presented in two programs running from June 2 to 5.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Festival for Puppets', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Festival for Puppets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Ann's Warehouse", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puppets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:39:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec58fbc8-d9ce-5022-8868-1138c294ba92', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec58fbc8-d9ce-5022-8868-1138c294ba92'}, {'abstract': 'In Chicago, Orlando and Los Angeles, markets with more than one PBS station, the cost of programming may be unreasonable as pledge contributions dwindle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/business/media/23pbs.html', 'snippet': 'In Chicago, Orlando and Los Angeles, markets with more than one PBS station, the cost of programming may be unreasonable as pledge contributions dwindle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORLANDO, Fla. — Television executives who gathered here last week for PBS’s annual meeting enthusiastically embraced projects such as the five-part “Women, War and Peace” and heard the actress Anna Deavere Smith speak so passionately of PBS’s role in bringing the arts to Americans that Paula Kerger, PBS’s president and chief executive, teared up.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/business/PBS-1/PBS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/business/PBS-1/PBS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/business/PBS-1/PBS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Burdened by PBS Dues, Stations Consider Withdrawing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Burdened by PBS Dues, Stations Consider Withdrawing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34933059-8098-50fc-9b52-ccb8ed42375d', 'word_count': 1098, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34933059-8098-50fc-9b52-ccb8ed42375d'}, {'abstract': 'The historian David McCullough’s latest volume begins in the 1830s and follows waves of young Americans who would become important in the arts, education and technological innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/books/the-greater-journey-david-mcculloughs-latest-review.html', 'snippet': 'The historian David McCullough’s latest volume begins in the 1830s and follows waves of young Americans who would become important in the arts, education and technological innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Greater Journey” is an important-looking book by the august historian David McCullough, who has twice won the Pulitzer Prize (for “Truman” and “John Adams”) and is a regular source of Father’s Day gifts for distinction-loving dads. His latest lavishly produced volume has a handsome collection of color photographs, a stellar cast of historically important American characters and a long, impressive bibliography. What it does not have is a unifying premise.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 596, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/23/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Parisian Experience of American Pioneers', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Parisian Experience Of American Pioneers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCullough, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-22T21:47:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c597a0a7-1b12-5631-9bca-b3f3b2327b09', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c597a0a7-1b12-5631-9bca-b3f3b2327b09'}, {'abstract': 'Intricate bidding by John Hurd and Joel Wooldridge on a slam helped bring Kevin Bathurst’s team a victory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/crosswords/bridge/bermuda-bowl-trial-brings-bathurst-a-victory-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'Intricate bidding by John Hurd and Joel Wooldridge on a slam helped bring Kevin Bathurst’s team a victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The higher the level of the game, the more important bidding has become. And for getting to the correct games and, especially, slams, describing opener’s hand type as quickly as possible is a big plus. S