m/2011/05/24/world/asia/24omar.html', 'snippet': 'Reports from inside Afghanistan’s intelligence agency had indicated that Mullah Muhammad Omar was dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — The Taliban said Monday that their reclusive leader, Mullah Muhammad Omar, was still very much alive and “leading the mujahedeen in all aspects,” disputing reports from inside Afghanistan’s intelligence agency indicating that he had been killed in Pakistan last week. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rebutting Afghan Spy Agency, Taliban Say Their Leader Isn’t Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rebutting Afghan Spy Agency, Taliban Say Their Leader Isn’t Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Omar, Muhammad (Taliban Leader)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T09:52:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Rivera and Carlotta Gall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Carlotta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d5c96d8-3b46-5651-8e3c-447a37b19eb2', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d5c96d8-3b46-5651-8e3c-447a37b19eb2'}, {'abstract': 'Amanda Wachob and the abstract expressionist tattoo.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/who-made-that-tattoo/', 'snippet': 'Amanda Wachob and the abstract expressionist tattoo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a recent Harris poll indicating that 16 percent of the people in the United States have at least one tattoo, chances are, in the course of your lifetime, you’ve known someone who has one. According to the same poll, if you’re currently between 18 and 29, there’s a 49 percent chance that you have one yourself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Tattoo?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tattoos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c74d0aa-bda4-5dc5-a866-e71ee8aa3ba7', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c74d0aa-bda4-5dc5-a866-e71ee8aa3ba7'}, {'abstract': 'The latest political news from today’s Times, around the Web and happenings in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/the-early-word-tension/', 'snippet': 'The latest political news from today’s Times, around the Web and happenings in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times•President Obama, in a speech to the American Israel Public Affairs Committee, an influential pro-Israel lobbying group, struck back at critics of his declaration last week that Israeli-Palestinian peace negotiations should be focused on Israel’s pre-1967 borders, along with negotiated land swaps, Helene Cooper of The Times reports. Mr. Obama called his proposal the best chance Israel has to avoid growing isolation amid rising political upheaval near its borders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Tension', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b9e3d63-2a43-5bcb-b94e-0e60a5c913df', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b9e3d63-2a43-5bcb-b94e-0e60a5c913df'}, {'abstract': 'As London’s many gardens enter their peak bloom period, approximately 100 of the ones typically closed to the public — and over 200 in total — will be accessible for the weekend of June 11 and 12.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/seeing-londons-hidden-gardens/', 'snippet': 'As London’s many gardens enter their peak bloom period, approximately 100 of the ones typically closed to the public — and over 200 in total — will be accessible for the weekend of June 11 and 12.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As London’s many gardens enter their peak bloom period, approximately 100 of the ones typically closed to the public — and over 200 in total — will be accessible for the weekend of June 11 and 12. Called Open Squares Garden Weekend, this annual event provides a rare opportunity to see into spots like the Tate Modern Community Garden, the three gardens of artist William Morris’s London home, and the Lincoln’s Inn Gardens, which date back to 1422.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing London’s Hidden Gardens', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Rose Dobson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Dobson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/850251c6-02c3-5329-8462-1111bd512e9b', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/850251c6-02c3-5329-8462-1111bd512e9b'}, {'abstract': 'An economist explores the debate over a proposal for a minimum wage of $10 an hour on all large projects that receive public subsidies.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/a-new-york-city-living-wage/', 'snippet': 'An economist explores the debate over a proposal for a minimum wage of $10 an hour on all large projects that receive public subsidies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New York City ‘Living Wage’?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4aaee90-4e94-5fca-b7b1-d31c73e35af8', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4aaee90-4e94-5fca-b7b1-d31c73e35af8'}, {'abstract': 'Boris, the cult Japanese heavy music band, has four albums coming out this year.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/japanese-heady-metal-boris-is-back/', 'snippet': 'Boris, the cult Japanese heavy music band, has four albums coming out this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Boris, the cult Japanese heavy music band, has four albums coming out this year, NPR reports: “‘Attention Please’ is just one of four — four! — Boris albums coming out this spring. Its release coincides with that of ‘Heavy Rocks’ (not to be confused with the 2002 album of the same name) and two Japanese-only titles: another collaboration with noise master Merzbow called ‘Klatter,’ as well as ‘New Album,’ which frustratingly mixes tracks from ‘Attention Please’ and the new ‘Heavy Rocks’ with other material.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japanese Heady Metal: Boris is Back', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wray, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:09:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f88513b3-5788-5769-8ec0-4e9859d5d802', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f88513b3-5788-5769-8ec0-4e9859d5d802'}, {'abstract': 'How our future robot working class will affect Communism.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/cylons-of-the-world-unite/', 'snippet': 'How our future robot working class will affect Communism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Economic theory is always easier to fathom when concrete examples accompany airy concepts. For instance, I was missing the point of Brian Caplan’s wonky vision of long-term economic development. Something about subsistence levels and intelligent machines and this baffling summation: “In slogan form: Simon for people, Malthus for robots.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cylons of the World, Unite!', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T10:15:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wm. Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wm', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7577162-eadb-5b37-b091-40d1c220ae25', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7577162-eadb-5b37-b091-40d1c220ae25'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama began a four-nation tour in Ireland, where he will explore his roots there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/world/europe/24prexy.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama began a four-nation tour in Ireland, where he will explore his roots there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONEYGALL, Ireland — President Obama may not be related to everybody in this postage-stamp Irish hamlet of 300, which claims him as its native son. But on Monday, Mr. Obama appeared determined to hug, kiss, shake hands or raise a glass with every last one of them.', 'print_section