klyn Flea. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Smorgasburg’s Debut Draws a Crowd', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T12:12:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b20a8a7-f70b-5456-bda5-00ac807577d1', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b20a8a7-f70b-5456-bda5-00ac807577d1'}, {'abstract': 'The problem is not just one of unsightliness, or of sea life getting caught up in plastic grocery bags or choking on plastic bottle tops or cigarette lighters. There are also the tiny fragments formed by disintegrating items, which threaten to contaminate the food chain.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/pity-the-oceans-vast-stews-of-plastic-soup/', 'snippet': 'The problem is not just one of unsightliness, or of sea life getting caught up in plastic grocery bags or choking on plastic bottle tops or cigarette lighters. There are also the tiny fragments formed by disintegrating items, which threaten to con...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overfishing, oil slicks, acidification — the world’s oceans, which cover 71 percent of the planet’s surface, face plenty of environmental problems. As I note in this Green Column, we’ve added another serious one to the list: the vast amount of trash, 80 percent of it plastic, that ends up in the seas from year to year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Rid the Seas of ‘Plastic Soup’?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mediterranean Sea', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T12:15:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bettina Wassener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bettina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wassener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f166a3b6-8085-5028-8302-b357d4b088dc', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f166a3b6-8085-5028-8302-b357d4b088dc'}, {'abstract': 'While Morgan Stanley and Bank of America did not get everything perfect with the LinkedIn I.P.O., they didn’t “scam” the social networking company, as The New York Times’s Joe Nocera suggested in his recent column.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/why-linkedins-price-was-appropriate/', 'snippet': 'While Morgan Stanley and Bank of America did not get everything perfect with the LinkedIn I.P.O., they didn’t “scam” the social networking company, as The New York Times’s Joe Nocera suggested in his recent column.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:16 a.m. | Updated Banker bashing has become a national pastime.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wh