its star, Toni Collette, but ratings for the series have fallen.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/showtime-cancels-united-states-of-tara-and-renews-nurse-jackie/', 'snippet': '“Tara” won an Emmy Award and a Golden Globe for its star, Toni Collette, but ratings for the series have fallen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With one fell swoop, Showtime has eliminated several female personalities from its lineup of original programming. On Monday it announced the cancellation of “The United States of Tara,” the comedy-drama starring Toni Collette as a housewife with dissociative identity disorder (commonly known as multiple personality disorder); the current season will be its last. “Tara,” which made its debut in 2009, came with an impressive pedigree, which included its creator, Diablo Cody, the Academy Award-winning screenwriter of “Juno,” and the executive producer Steven Spielberg. And Ms. Collette was widely praised for her performance in the title role, winning an Emmy Award in 2009 and a Golden Globe in 2010. But ratings for the series have diminished, from 816,000 viewers for the premiere of Season 2 to 411,000 viewers for the debut of Season 3 in March, The Hollywood Reporter reported. By comparison, the season finale of Showtime’s historical drama “The Borgias” was watched by 810,000 viewers at 10 p.m. on Sunday, the network said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Showtime Cancels ‘United States of Tara’ and Renews ‘Nurse Jackie’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collette, Toni', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Showtime', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-23T18:41:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itz