arrested, but did note the police action in Georgia could be interpreted as violating a European human rights convention. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Harsh Words for Georgia', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Harsh Words for Georgia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T00:56:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6f0517a-258a-5aaa-b2a3-254c46181118', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6f0517a-258a-5aaa-b2a3-254c46181118'}, {'abstract': 'Theodore Forstmann, who helped create the leveraged buyout industry, has brain cancer, and his latest business goal is to make a lot of money to help children worldwide.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/philanthropy-is-given-focus-by-brain-cancer/', 'snippet': 'Theodore Forstmann, who helped create the leveraged buyout industry, has brain cancer, and his latest business goal is to make a lot of money to help children worldwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Theodore J. Forstmann is tired.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Philanthropy Given Focus by Brain Cancer', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forstmann, Theodore J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'IMG Worldwide', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T00:59:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/373e69d0-7baa-59f0-a588-c3162ed52f6c', 'word_count': 1089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/373e69d0-7baa-59f0-a588-c3162ed52f6c'}, {'abstract': 'Recent events have proven that historical attitudes about Jews, especially negative ones, continue to flourish.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/23/whats-up-with-the-jews/', 'snippet': 'Recent events have proven that historical attitudes about Jews, especially negative ones, continue to flourish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been an interesting week or two for Jews. Mel Gibson’s new film, “The Beaver,” opens nationwide in theaters and Jews must decide whether to pay good money to see a movie starring someone whose father is a Holocaust denier, and who has himself vilified Jews in public. In Cannes, the Danish movie director Lars von Trier rambles on at a press conference about sympathizing with Hitler, being annoyed with Israel and admiring Hitler’s architect, Albert Speer. Next day he recants, but it’s too late; he’s declared persona non grata at the film festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Up With the Jews?', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T01:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/51b62069-fa45-5a85-ae62-7c892f3af67f', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51b62069-fa45-5a85-ae62-7c892f3af67f'}, {'abstract': "While locked out from the N.F.L., Indianapolis Colts' Taj Smith trained at his former high school in Newark and talked with kids about life beyond Newark's mean streets.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/sports/100000000821301/home-for-the-nfl-lockout.html', 'snippet': "While locked out from the N.F.L., Indianapolis Colts' Taj Smith trained at his former high school in Newark and talked with kids about life beyond Newark's mean streets.", 'lead_paragraph': "While locked out from the N.F.L., Indianapolis Colts' Taj Smith trained at his former high school in Newark and talked with kids about life beyond Newark's mean streets.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/sports/video-newark-nfl/video-newark-nfl-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/19/sports/video-newark-nfl/video-newark-nfl-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/19/sports/video-newark-nfl/video-newark-nfl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'