 thoughts on the way things are going.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Owner Sets Goals for the Company and Himself', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T11:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2376de1-13e4-515d-8886-fd8ab953ff2f', 'word_count': 579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2376de1-13e4-515d-8886-fd8ab953ff2f'}, {'abstract': 'A singer who lives in public housing but takes on the spirit of a jazz legend when she steps onto a stage.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/keeping-billie-holiday-alive-at-the-cotton-club/', 'snippet': 'A singer who lives in public housing but takes on the spirit of a jazz legend when she steps onto a stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Around Harlem, Linda Hutchinson is much better known by her stage name, Billie Holiday Jr. — that’s Princess Billie to you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping Billie Holiday Alive at the Cotton Club', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holiday, Billie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T11:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'origi