es', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Glencore Sinks Below Offering Price', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rich, Marc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glencore International AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London Stock Exchange', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T12:44:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/80c0bd27-3246-5d5a-9408-967efa0b51d6', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80c0bd27-3246-5d5a-9408-967efa0b51d6'}, {'abstract': 'As part of its push into health care, Nestlé said on Tuesday that it had agreed to purchase Prometheus Laboratories, an American company specializing in gastrointestinal and cancer diagnostics.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/nestle-to-buy-diagnostics-firm/', 'snippet': 'As part of its push into health care, Nestlé said on Tuesday that it had agreed to purchase Prometheus Laboratories, an American company specializing in gastrointestinal and cancer diagnostics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nestlé said on Tuesday that it had agreed to purchase Prometheus Laboratories, an American company specializing in gastrointestinal and cancer diagnostics, for an undisclosed sum as the Swiss food giant moved further into health care.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nestlé to Buy Diagnostics Firm', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T12:59:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e38fbb4b-cece-5bd1-9648-d8d33d640520', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e38fbb4b-cece-5bd1-9648-d8d33d640520'}, {'abstract': 'We talk to the writer Lila Azam Zanganeh on the personal history excluded from her new book, “The Enchanter: Nabokov and Happiness.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/reading-lolita-forgetting-tehran/', 'snippet': 'We talk to the writer Lila Azam Zanganeh on the personal history excluded from her new book, “The Enchanter: Nabokov and Happiness.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the commodities exchange of American letters, Lila Azam Zanganeh is an easy buy: She’s got that appealing balance of Near Eastern extraction, European education and Yankee ambition. Born in Paris to Iranian exiles, she studied at the prestigious École Normale Supérieure before teaching literary seminars at Harvard. Now she lives in New York and is something of a fixture at book parties and publishing events. (She was just on the cover of Thursday Styles, giving Jon-Jon Goulian, another of the season’s publishing darlings, a protracted peck.) She speaks six languages, sings opera and is quite beautiful, although even her sultry glamour is tempered a bit by a childlike innocence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading ‘Lolita.’ Forgetting Tehran.', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/731d5c8b-4f16-5777-abd3-fa3b6105d40b', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/731d5c8b-4f16-5777-abd3-fa3b6105d40b'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Train service isn’t what it was as recently as a year ago, conspicuously on the numbered lines that the transportation authority still quaintly lumps together as the IRT. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/paying-more-to-wait-longer-for-the-subway/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Train service isn’t what it was as recently as a year ago, conspicuously on the numbered lines that the transportation authority still quaintly lumps together as the IRT. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s dispense first with a few housekeeping details: The future Lady McCartney did her duty as a board member of the Metropolitan Transportation Authority by attending committee hearings on Monday. Some had wondered if she would show up. She did.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paying More to Wait Longer for the Subway', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City