urkle, Ronald W', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malone, John C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberty Media Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yucaipa Cos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:33:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2e24464-5dd2-5cd3-be93-8b84a9f12cd8', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2e24464-5dd2-5cd3-be93-8b84a9f12cd8'}, {'abstract': 'With Libya, Afghanistan, and Mideast peace on the agenda during a two-day visit, President Obama was welcomed with a ceremony at Buckingham Palace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/europe/25prexy.html', 'snippet': 'With Libya, Afghanistan, and Mideast peace on the agenda during a two-day visit, President Obama was welcomed with a ceremony at Buckingham Palace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Queen Elizabeth II welcomed President Obama to Britain on Tuesday with pomp and ceremony, with the Scots Guards’ band playing “The Star-Spangled Banner” and the firing of a 41-gun cannon salute that resounded across the gardens of Buckingham Palace.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/PREXY/PREXY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/world/PREXY/PREXY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/PREXY/PREXY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/PREXY/PREXY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/world/PREXY/PREXY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Britain, Pomp for Obama but Serious Business Too', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain Welcomes Obama as Concerns Stay in the Background', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Buckingham Palace', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:34:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb5a6a24-5390-58a3-be44-320dab37dc76', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb5a6a24-5390-58a3-be44-320dab37dc76'}, {'abstract': 'Humans are distinguished from other animals in that we developed qualitatively different sorts of social abilities—involving teaching, altruism and morality.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/getting-together/', 'snippet': 'Humans are distinguished from other animals in that we developed qualitatively different sorts of social abilities—involving teaching, altruism and morality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week I wrote a column citing a gradual shift in the way we think about evolution—that it’s less strictly a competitive struggle for survival among the fittest and involves a lot more cooperation and altruism. A study by Kim R. Hill, Robert Walker and others in the journal Science plays into that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Together', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:36:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab54a807-fc33-5c14-950a-e274576f4047', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab54a807-fc33-5c14-950a-e274576f4047'}, {'abstract': 'People naturally desire complete group cohesion.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/everybody-plays/', 'snippet': 'People naturally desire complete group cohesion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the way, Daniel Klein conducted an experiment for a George Mason University working paper that illustrates our hunger for cooperation. He asked groups of people in a room to sing a song together. But one person in the room was instructed not to sing unless the other people paid him some money. The question was: Do people desire complete group cohesion so much they are willing to pay to avoid having an outlier/dissident in their group. The groups decided to pay money to achieve what Klein calls “encompassment” 47.4 percent of the time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everybody Plays', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'George Mason University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/be8df7ea-1ca2-59a9-ae98-85dcdd194cd2', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be8df7ea-1ca2-59a9-ae98-85dcdd194cd2'}, {'abstract': 'We sit closer to people who share our physical traits.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/girls-and-guys-who-wear-glasses/', 'snippet': 'We sit closer to people who share our physical traits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suppose you are walking into a waiting room and you see a bunch of strangers. How close are you going to sit to one of the strangers? Well, do you wear glasses?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Girls and Guys Who Wear Glasses…', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:44:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/29354d5e-c2b0-5e09-9f98-4c8f71ed4ed9', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29354d5e-c2b0-5e09-9f98-4c8f71ed4ed9'}, {'abstract': 'Midlife fitness predicts long-term heart health, Bank of America settles overdraft fee case, Do@ tries to speed mobile searches and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/tuesday-reading-midlife-fitness-can-predict-long-term-heart-health/', 'snippet': 'Midlife fitness predicts long-term heart health, Bank of America settles overdraft fee case, Do@ tries to speed mobile searches and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Midlife Fitness Can Predict Long-Term Heart Health', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:53:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a599f5ae-af95-5685-bf98-0e8fdea9e5a3', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a599f5ae-af95-5685-bf98-0e8fdea9e5a3'}, {'abstract': 'For good or ill, the Republicans are probably stuck with Paul Ryan’s budget plan, with its controversial Medicare overhaul, through the 2012 election. That they stuck together on it in the House is probably to their advantage.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/the-ryan-budget-tipping-point/', 'snippet': 'For good or ill, the Republicans are probably stuck with Paul Ryan’s budget plan, with its controversial Medicare overhaul, through the 2012 election. That they stuck together on it in the House is probably to their advantage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps this is obvious, but Republican strategy on Rep. Paul Ryan’s controversial budget plan represents a classic tipping point scenario — enough so that it could be a case study for Malcolm Gladwell’s next book.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ryan Budget Tipping Point', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gladwell, Malcolm', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons'