y its leash against an elevator wall and also kicked the animal was charged with aggravated animal cruelty on Monday, the police said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Man Is Charged With Abusing Dog in East Harlem Elevator', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruelty to Animals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Harlem (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T13:56:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/99ff8518-9432-532c-91cf-f1b756fa0021', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99ff8518-9432-532c-91cf-f1b756fa0021'}, {'abstract': 'Business acumen and experience are being emphasized by the early presidential contenders.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/c-e-o-of-the-u-s-a/', 'snippet': 'Business acumen and experience are being emphasized by the early presidential contenders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The job of the president is technically that of the chief executive of the United States. But I’m still fascinated by the extent to which business acumen is being emphasized by the early presidential contenders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'C.E.O. of the U.S.A.', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7535901e-878f-525b-9dd1-7a0628180f4c', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7535901e-878f-525b-9dd1-7a0628180f4c'}, {'abstract': 'Explaining war to kids is always hard, but when you’re part of a military family, the task is even more difficult because it’s so personal.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/explaining-war-to-our-children/', 'snippet': 'Explaining war to kids is always hard, but when you’re part of a military family, the task is even more difficult because it’s so personal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Explaining war to kids is always hard, but when you’re part of a military family, the task is even more difficult because it’s so personal. When my sons were little, their father was in the Navy Reserve as an intelligence officer with the Seabees, the Navy’s construction division. The boys understood what the military was in a very hazy “It must involve a ship, Daddy going away, and a large yellow bee” kind of way. But because their father wasn’t directly in harm’s way, I was able to gloss over hard questions about war and just concentrate on the fun parts of having a dad in the military from a toddler’s perspective — serving the country and wearing really cool hats.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Explaining War to Our Children', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:08:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Himel-Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Himel-Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6739d48-b4dc-568e-9aee-0e3bf3fe2970', 'word_count': 1343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6739d48-b4dc-568e-9aee-0e3bf3fe2970'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott examines the aesthetics of failure in Bruce Robinson’s film about two young actors in 1969 London.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/critics-picks-video-withnail-and-i/', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott examines the aesthetics of failure in Bruce Robinson’s film about two young actors in 1969 London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, A. O. Scott looks back at Bruce Robinson’s 1987 British comedy “Withnail and I.” The film revolves around the relationship between its two central characters (Richard E. Grant and Paul McGann), two out of work actors who spend most of their time drinking, fighting and getting very little accomplished. Mr. Scott calls the movie “charming, hilarious and ultimately heartbreaking.” Have you seen “Withnail and I?” Please share your thoughts in the comments area below.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ‘Withnail and I’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Richard E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGann, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d12de51d-115e-54d5-8f86-c5a018d1a4a4', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d12de51d-115e-54d5-8f86-c5a018d1a4a4'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/what-were-reading-189/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd27ba7f-d992-5b9f-b7ae-72c34fb5f1a6', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd27ba7f-d992-5b9f-b7ae-72c34fb5f1a6'}, {'abstract': 'The first lady chose a summery vintage look to meet Queen Elizabeth and the Duchess of Cambridge, Kate Middleton.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/michelle-obama-meets-the-new-duchess/', 'snippet': 'The first lady chose a summery vintage look to meet Queen Elizabeth and the Duchess of Cambridge, Kate Middleton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the sidelines, London fashion writers pondered whether the first lady, Michelle Obama, would favor a British designer during her and President Obama’s state visit. Meanwhile, an Irish journalist observed that “all the stylists in the world couldn’t save the incredibly chic Michelle Obama” from a battle of a bad hair day in windy Dublin, where Mrs. Obama wore a dotted Marc Jacobs dress and a lightweight coat. Even the length of her eyelashes was closely observed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Michelle Obama Meets the New Duchess', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:17:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bfbbc15-a488-5528-a79a-d6958ec0cd17', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bfbbc15-a488-5528-a79a-d6958ec0cd17'}, {'abstract': 'Yarn weaved from dog hair. (Chien [Dog] + Angora.)', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/chiengora/', 'snippet': 'Yarn weaved from dog hair. (Chien [Dog] + Angora.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yarn weaved from dog hair.  (Chien [Dog] + Angora.)', 'sour