nly four lenders were added to the government list of so-called problem banks in the first quarter, the smallest increase since the financial crisis began.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:23 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Bank Woes Ease, the F.D.I.C.’s Fund Is Building Up', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:37:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2970f8c2-c592-5899-9d37-2fff519671d0', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2970f8c2-c592-5899-9d37-2fff519671d0'}, {'abstract': '“I am not quite sure what to make of this magazine cover.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/magazine/gold-getter.html', 'snippet': '“I am not quite sure what to make of this magazine cover.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am not quite sure what to make of this magazine cover. On one hand, it is gold on the cover of a mainstream magazine. But it’s less convincing as a magazine-cover contrary indicator. Compare it to the housing-peak covers in Fortune (May 2005) or Time (June 2005). The focus here is not on the investment aspect, but rather is a profile on a gold surveyor and on gold “as the world’s most primitive form of wealth.” Hardly the gushing endorsements we typically like to see as contrary indicators.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gold Getter', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'GOLD GETTER', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:40:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/372f26b7-0664-5df6-a718-111bc215c646', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/372f26b7-0664-5df6-a718-111bc215c646'}, {'abstract': 'Former President Hosni Mubarak will be tried for conspiring to kill protesters, Egypt’s top prosecutor announced, yielding to demands for accountability.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/middleeast/25egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Former President Hosni Mubarak will be tried for conspiring to kill protesters, Egypt’s top prosecutor announced, yielding to demands for accountability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Former President Hosni Mubarak will be put on trial for conspiring to kill unarmed protesters, Egypt’s top prosecutor announced Tuesday, yielding to public demands for accountability and setting an example that could rattle autocrats around the region. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt Is Moving to Try Mubarak in Fatal Protests', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'EGYPT IS MOVING TO TRY MUBARAK IN FATAL PROTESTS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption in Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Gamal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Alaa', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:43:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e589221b-a7ec-5143-aace-c5eb14f88501', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e589221b-a7ec-5143-aace-c5eb14f88501'}, {'abstract': '“David Leonhardt’s presentation of the Pew data on religious affiliation and income in the United States neglects the way that religious groups are distributed geographically.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/magazine/top-down.html', 'snippet': '“David Leonhardt’s presentation of the Pew data on religious affiliation and income in the United States neglects the way that religious groups are distributed geographically.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Leonhardt’s presentation of the Pew data on religious affiliation and income in the United States (“Is Your Religion Your Financial Destiny?”) neglects the way that religious groups are distributed geographically. The groups with the highest average household incomes tend disproportionately to live where costs of living and therefore salaries are highest. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top Down', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TOP DOWN', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:44:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/21f57e4b-5734-5d80-983b-356c415f55e0', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21f57e4b-5734-5d80-983b-356c415f55e0'}, {'abstract': '“As the first-generation daughter of an Icelandic immigrant to the United States, I was never able to tap into the psyche of my father’s native land until I read Jake Halpern’s article.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/magazine/the-big-thaw.html', 'snippet': '“As the first-generation daughter of an Icelandic immigrant to the United States, I was never able to tap into the psyche of my father’s native land until I read Jake Halpern’s article.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the first-generation daughter of an Icelandic immigrant to the United States, I was never able to tap into the psyche of my father’s native land until I read Jake Halpern’s article. The dry humor, honesty and independent spirit of the Icelanders, born from living on a rock in the middle of the north seas, were captured brilliantly. Halpern helped me understand what seemed like cavalier financial behavior and heal my embarrassment over their decisions. Thank you for helping me understand my people.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Big Thaw', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE BIG THAW', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iceland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:46:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b12807e-25cb-56da-b9d1-3e28e473527f', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b12807e-25cb-56da-b9d1-3e28e473527f'}, {'abstract': '“The bonus feature by Adam Sternbergh was worth the price of my annual subscription.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/magazine/riff.html', 'snippet': '“The bonus feature by Adam Sternbergh was worth the price of my annual subscription.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a clever line by Sam Anderson — “if novels are mirrors held up to nature, memoirs are mirrors held up to mirrors.” But most fiction actually takes the form either of biography (third-person narrative) or autobiography (first person). The relationship between these sibling genres has been complicated and competitive for at least 200 years. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Riff', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'RIFF', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Sam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:47:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b682724-5b91-533c-b550-6a77a8a28277', 'word_count': 81, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b682724-5b91-533c-b550-6a77a8a28277'}, {'abstract': '“In Andrew Goldman’s interview of Larry Summers, my colleague Joseph Stiglitz is cited for saying Summers ignores arguments that he doesn’t like.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/magazine/talk.html', 'snippet': '“In Andrew Goldman’s interview of Larry Summers, my colleague Joseph Stiglitz is cited for saying Summers ignores arguments that he doesn’t like.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Andrew Goldman’s interview of Larry Summers, my colleague Joseph Stiglitz is cited for saying Summers ignores arguments that he doesn’t like. I have disagreed with Summers on several issues, but I have always found him ready to debate my views, even at Columbia, when, on the celebration of my 75th birthday, he took on me, Paul Krugman and others. He shies away from shoddy arguments that are simply populist, not from debating serious differences with critics his own size. By contrast, I have never succeeded in getting a debate, on or off campus, between Stiglitz and myself.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talk', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TALK', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Summers, Lawrence H', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:48:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c98a544-7f84-5409-95d5-6b5b3adaff5d', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c98a544-7f84-5409-95d5-6b5b3adaff5d'}, {'abstract': 'Days after LinkedIn’s surge, a Russian search engine company grabbed the limelight as the hot Internet I.P.O., as shares of Yandex climbed $13.84 to close at $38.84 on the Nasdaq stock market.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/yandex-shares-surge-on-debut/', 'snippet': 'Days after LinkedIn’s surge, a Russian search engine company grabbed the limelight as the hot Internet I.P.O., as shares of Yandex climbed $13.84 to close at $38.84 on the Nasdaq stock market.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:17 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yandex Shares Soar 55% in Market Debut', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T14:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/612c1f47-4b70-5e7a-8d91-a3957716b0a0', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/612c1f47-4b70-5e7a-8d91-a3957716b0a0'}, {'abstract': 'Some bonuses to British bankers were partly paid with government funds supplied to the industry, said John Vickers, chairman of a government-backed banking commission.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/british-banks-paid-bonuses-with-government-aid/', 'snippet': 'Some bonuses to British bankers were partly paid with government funds supplied to the industry, said John Vickers, chairman of a government-backed banking commission.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Some bonuses to British bankers were partly paid using government aid supplied to the beleaguered industry, John Vickers, chairman of a government-backed banking commission, said on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Banks Paid Bonuses Using Government Aid', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonuses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hester, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lloyds Banking Group Plc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Bank of Scotland PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:02:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/67a9b076-7a27-5423-ab67-b09d0ac8efb9', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67a9b076-7a27-5423-ab67-b09d0ac8efb9'}, {'abstract': 'Plays are doing solid box-office business during Tony season.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/tony-nominated-plays-reap-box-office-benefits-and-spider-man-returns-with-a-bang/', 'snippet': 'Plays are doing solid box-office business during Tony season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do Tony Award nominees for best play sell unusually well during the weeks between the nominations (this year, May 3) and the awards ceremony (June 12)? Not always. This time last year, for instance, two of the four best-play nominees (“In the Next Room, or the Vibrator Play” and “Time Stands Still”) had already closed, while “Next Fall” earned $150,982 and “Red” earned $463,639 for performances between May 17-23.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony-Nominated Plays Reap Box-Office Benefits, and ‘Spider-Man’ Returns With a Bang', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Broadway League', 'rank': 3, 'major':