 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1ac768f-b86c-5789-98ab-577f426b8fb1', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1ac768f-b86c-5789-98ab-577f426b8fb1'}, {'abstract': 'The Tokyo Electric Power Company on Tuesday offered a more severe assessment of the nuclear disaster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/asia/25nuclear.html', 'snippet': 'The Tokyo Electric Power Company on Tuesday offered a more severe assessment of the nuclear disaster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — In a belated acknowledgment of the severity of Japan’s nuclear disaster, the Tokyo Electric Power Company said Tuesday that three of the stricken Fukushima Daiichi plant’s reactors most likely suffered fuel meltdowns in the early days of the crisis.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Company Believes 3 Reactors Melted Down in Japan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Sees Signs of 2 More Meltdowns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tokyo Electric Power Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:16:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cef362b1-c0e6-5bce-800c-600a04e80c80', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cef362b1-c0e6-5bce-800c-600a04e80c80'}, {'abstract': 'What’s really new about the social networking-obsessed “What’s Trending” is the fact that it’s a live, scheduled half-hour Web show being done on the site of a major broadcast television network, and with a major corporate sponsor in AT&T.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/watchlist-a-self-conscious-web-experiment-from-cbs-news/', 'snippet': 'What’s really new about the social networking-obsessed “What’s Trending” is the fact that it’s a live, scheduled half-hour Web show being done on the site of a major broadcast television network, and with a major corporate sponsor in AT&T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The live Web show “What’s Trending” at cbsnews.com, an attempt to build a news-and-chat program driven by social networking — sort of a digital “Today” — is a self-consciously experimental project. That self-consciousness registers throughout the half-hour show; it rang through clearly in the premiere episode last Tuesday, when the host, the blogger and online reporter Shira Lazar, announced: “We are all about the trending topics and stories the world is sharing. It’s what you need to know to be in the now.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watchlist: A Self-Conscious Web Experiment From CBS News', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kweli, Talib', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:19:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/79773588-8c98-5f14-9bfb-5acef5912073', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79773588-8c98-5f14-9bfb-5acef5912073'}, {'abstract': 'The New Jersey Supreme Court ruling ordered that financing to Abbott districts must be made. Issued May 5, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/25/education/20110525-education-abbott-ruling.html', 'snippet': 'The New Jersey Supreme Court ruling ordered that financing to Abbott districts must be made. Issued May 5, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Jersey Supreme Court ruling ordered that financing to Abbott districts must be made. Issued May 5, 2011.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision on New Jersey Schools Abbott Financing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Secondary)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:19:45+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Education', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/e317eb7c-6188-5a8f-9569-7f48b3f28cf0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/e317eb7c-6188-5a8f-9569-7f48b3f28cf0'}, {'abstract': 'Volkswagen inaugurates a new plant in Chattanooga, Tenn., while Nissan becomes embroiled in Tennessee state politics.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/wheelies-the-volunteer-state-edition/', 'snippet': 'Volkswagen inaugurates a new plant in Chattanooga, Tenn., while Nissan becomes embroiled in Tennessee state politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The Volunteer State Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saab Automobile AB', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chattanooga (Tenn)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:20:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cacab48b-0e8d-56ab-ab4d-4698529f9e0d', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cacab48b-0e8d-56ab-ab4d-4698529f9e0d'}, {'abstract': 'The efforts to negotiate a settlement of the political crisis appeared more remote than ever after the clashes in Sana.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/middleeast/25yemen.html', 'snippet': 'The efforts to negotiate a settlement of the political crisis appeared more remote than ever after the clashes in Sana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Hopes for a peaceful settlement of Yemen’s political crisis receded further on Tuesday as street battles intensified between government security forces and opposition tribesmen, leaving at least two dozen people dead and turning part of the Yemeni capital, Sana, into a war zone. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/YEMEN/YEMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/world/YEMEN/YEMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/YEMEN/YEMEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/YEMEN/YEMEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/world/YEMEN/YEMEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting Intensifies in Yemen as the Government Battles Tribal Groups', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fighting Intensifies in Yemen as the Government Battles Tribal Groups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gulf Cooperation Council', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saleh, Ali Abdullah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T15:23:08+0000', 'document_ty