 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/da08f2f6-0717-5b4c-8ad7-267c97372bf0', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da08f2f6-0717-5b4c-8ad7-267c97372bf0'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights of this year’s festival include “The Infernal Comedy: Confessions of a Serial Killer,” starring Mr. Malkovich as the Austrian criminal and author Jack Unterweger; and the last planned BAM appearances by the Merce Cunningham Dance Company.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/a-murderous-malkovich-in-lineup-for-bams-2011-next-wave-festival/', 'snippet': 'Highlights of this year’s festival include “The Infernal Comedy: Confessions of a Serial Killer,” starring Mr. Malkovich as the Austrian criminal and author Jack Unterweger; and the last planned BAM appearances by the Merce Cunningham Dance Compan...', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Malkovich‘s latest turn as a psychopath, new productions of “The Threepenny Opera” and “Krapp’s Last Tape,” a stage adaptation of Ingmar Bergman’s “Cries and Whispers” and the last planned appearance by the Merce Cunningham Dance Company at the Brooklyn Academy of Music are among the eclectic events planned for BAM’s 2011 Next Wave Festival, organizers announced on Tuesday. The series, during which BAM will begin a 16-month celebration of its 150th anniversary, starts on Sept. 21 with the New York premiere of “Awakening: A Musical Meditation on the Anniversary of 9/11,” a work that will be performed by the Kronos Quartet, featuring the Brooklyn Youth Chorus. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Murderous Malkovich in Lineup for BAM’s 2011 Next Wave Festival', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Next Wave Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Samuel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bergman, Ingmar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurt, John', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malkovich, John', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Hove, Ivo', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Robert', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Youth Chorus', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce, Dance Co', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kronos Quartet', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austria', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc657136-b238-50da-91bc-1b3ec85c6095', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc657136-b238-50da-91bc-1b3ec85c6095'}, {'abstract': 'Updated with adorable video of hawk testing wings: Baby hawk walks out of nest and out of frame of webcam. Human hawk fans panic. Prodigal bird returns.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/hawk-cam-pip-goes-for-a-walk/', 'snippet': 'Updated with adorable video of hawk testing wings: Baby hawk walks out of nest and out of frame of webcam. Human hawk fans panic. Prodigal bird returns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 4:59 p.m. |  Pip the hawk hatchling reached another milestone this morning: Sometime shortly before 10 a.m., the little one hopped out of the nest — and out of the frame of the Hawk Cam. Suddenly the camera just showed Violet’s lower body and the two inert leftover eggs in the nest. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hawk Cam | Pip Goes for a Walk', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hawks (Birds)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:13:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman and Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ff9c03-0eee-58d9-8e17-b59b2e9d2149', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ff9c03-0eee-58d9-8e17-b59b2e9d2149'}, {'abstract': 'Staff members were asked to identify things that differentiated their taste from that of their friends and colleagues: What do we like that others in our lives either don’t like or don’t know about? What is idiosyncratically ours?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/stuff-we-like-that-maybe-you-wont/', 'snippet': 'Staff members were asked to identify things that differentiated their taste from that of their friends and colleagues: What do we like that others in our lives either don’t like or don’t know about? What is idiosyncratically ours?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This list of recommendations was generated with a twist. Staff members were asked to identify things that differentiated their taste from that of their friends and colleagues: What do we like that others in our lives either don’t like or don’t know about? What is idiosyncratically ours? Some, you’ll see, took this occasion to reveal their particular guilty pleasures. Others showed us a side of themselves we didn’t know existed — is there really among us someone who enjoys “Alf”? And who watches shark videos? While we figure out what to do with these outliers, read the list and please add your own off-kilter picks in the comments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stuff We Like That Maybe You Won’t', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a83593ef-6c29-5033-acdd-d01f61ff1630', 'word_count': 1421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a83593ef-6c29-5033-acdd-d01f61ff1630'}, {'abstract': 'Census undercounts a Brooklyn block.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/05/24/nyregion/nycensus.html', 'snippet': 'Census undercounts a Brooklyn block.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Census undercounts a Brooklyn block.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Block in Brooklyn: Exodus or Undercount?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:17:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/70d27544-02b4-5e2d-97b9-c8f7b39bc8d8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/70d27544-02b4-5e2d-97b9-c8f7b39bc8d8'}, {'abstract': 'In a speech to Congress, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu set several significant limits on what Israel would accept in a deal with the Palestinians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/middleeast/25diplo.html', 'snippet': 'In a speech to Congress, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu set several significant limits on what Israel would accept in a deal with the Palestinians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel, broadly laying out the Israeli response to President Obama’s peace proposals, called on the Palestinian Authority president, Mahmoud Abbas, on Tuesday to accept what Mr. Netanyahu framed as a tenet: that Palestinians will not get a right of return to Israel. In so doing, he made clear that he was giving no ground on the major stumbling blocks to a peace agreement.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/25diplo5_cnd/25diplo5_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/world/25diplo5_cnd/25diplo5_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/25diplo5_cnd/25diplo5_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/world/25diplo5_cnd/25diplo5_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/world/25diplo5_cnd/25diplo5_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Netanyahu Gives No Ground in Congress Speech', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Netanyahu Gives No Ground in Congress Speech', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:18:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper and Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf861165-db37-5787-9fd6-468d03ab675a', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf861165-db37-5787-9fd6-468d03ab675a'}, {'abstract': 'Fill in the blanks in the paragraphs below taken from the article, “In Florida, the Natives Are Restless.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/fill-in-alligators-everywhere/', 'snippet': 'Fill in the blanks in the paragraphs below taken from the article, “In Florida, the Natives Are Restless.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fill-In | Alligators Everywhere', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:20:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a59d87b-ba56-5832-83d9-1e342f1c2682', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a59d87b-ba56-5832-83d9-1e342f1c2682'}, {'abstract': 'Social media, eco-lodging and a D.I.Y. ethic intersect at a new hostel hybrid in the hip Paris neighborhood of EsBas (East Bastille).', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/french-connection-hi-matic/', 'snippet': 'Social media, eco-lodging and a D.I.Y. ethic intersect at a new hostel hybrid in the hip Paris neighborhood of EsBas (East Bastille).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Social media, eco-lodging and a D.I.Y. ethic intersect at HI Matic, a new B&B-hostel hybrid in the hip Paris neighborhood of EsBas (East Bastille). The hotel takes a plugged-in approach to hospitality, eschewing full-service concierge and staff for a digitally customizable experience. Not only can guests tailor nearly every detail of their stay on touch-screens throughout the building, but they can also share information with the room next door – or, if they want, with all 42 rooms at once. The organic breakfast is served through futuristic vending machines, which also offer books, aromatherapy diffusers and other goodies. Each visitor receives a personalized list of exhibitions and shows recommended by local experts, and two electronic music stations – one mellow, one groovier – are available on every floor, courtesy of the German record label Kompakt. Matali Crasset, a Philippe Starck protégée who has opened three other hotels with the HI group (two in Nice and one in Nefta, Tunisia), created Ryokan-inspired rooms with modular furniture and floor mattresses. She chose the area for its authenticity and charm. “There are lots of bars and it’s fun, but the neighborhood is not touristy,” she says. “It has kept its local flavor, with mom-and-pop shops and cafés.” Among her favorite spots is Septime (80 rue de Charonne), a new foodie hotspot directly across the street from the hotel where the star chef Bertrand Grébaut concocts simple but sophisticated dishes that le tout Paris is raving about.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'French Connection | HI Matic', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shirine Saad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shirine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0992a50c-7e60-5c73-b8ce-73cc05feaf02', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0992a50c-7e60-5c73-b8ce-73cc05feaf02'}, {'abstract': 'My wife and I will be attending a medical conference in Honolulu next October. We have never been to Hawaii and would like to spend a few days before or after the meeting in a more rural location, preferably at a beachside location and near good hiking. We are not surfers. Can we do this on Oahu, or do we need to fly to another island?', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/qa-hiking-and-dining-on-oahu/', 'snippet': 'My wife and I will be attending a medical conference in Honolulu next October. We have never been to Hawaii and would like to spend a few days before or after the meeting in a more rural location, preferably at a beachside location and near good h...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Hiking and Dining on Oahu', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honolulu (Hawaii)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oahu (Hawaii)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:23:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Rivas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0de9b2b-d9c4-5103-a520-f4c6e49d2d71', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0de9b2b-d9c4-5103-a520-f4c6e49d2d71'}, {'abstract': 'Rafael Nadal recovered his balance in the fourth set and prevailed after four hours against John Isner of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/2011-french-open-nadal-survives-a-scare-from-isner.html', 'snippet': 'Rafael Nadal recovered his balance in the fourth set and prevailed after four hours against John Isner of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Rafael Nadal has made a habit of making victory look easy at Roland Garros. He arrived this time as a five-time champion with a 38-1 record. And in all those wins since 2005, the Spaniard with the steely nerve and bending shots never needed a fifth and final set.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/25nadal1/25nadal1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/25nadal1/25nadal1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/25nadal1/25nadal1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/25nadal1/25nadal1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/sports/tennis/25nadal1/25nadal1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No. 1 Nadal, Stretched to Five Sets by Isner, Bends but Never Buckles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No. 1 Nadal, Stretched to Five Sets by Isner, Bends but Never Buckles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isner, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:24:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b27d1df-73de-59e9-bcbd-fd99ff85aa7d', 'word_count': 861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b27d1df-73de-59e9-bcbd-fd99ff85aa7d'}, {'abstract': 'In Beijing, the Foreign Ministry said that “technical staff of a certain enterprise” were taken hostage in a bloody 16-hour siege at a naval base in Karachi, Pakistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/world/asia/25china.html', 'snippet': 'In Beijing, the Foreign Ministry said that “technical staff of a certain enterprise” were taken hostage in a bloody 16-hour siege at a naval base in Karachi, Pakistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A day after denying that any of its citizens had been involved, China confirmed Tuesday that Chinese technicians were taken hostage during a militant attack on a Pakistani naval base.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Admits Its Technicians Were Held in Pakistan Base Attack', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Admits Its Technicians Were Held in Pakistan Base Attack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe74ea67-b6b1-5553-92ed-f3bb4388c5ad', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe74ea67-b6b1-5553-92ed-f3bb4388c5ad'}, {'abstract': 'The Clive Davis Department will become the Clive Davis Institute.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/music-executive-giving-5-million-to-n-y-u-to-expand-music-business-program/', 'snippet': 'The Clive Davis Department will become the Clive Davis Institute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The music executive Clive Davis is giving an additional $5 million gift to the Tisch School of the Arts at New York University, allowing the expansion of the Clive Davis Department of Recorded Music into the Clive Davis Institute of Recorded Music, school officials announced Tuesday. The gift will make it possible for the school to recruit additional working professionals as faculty members, establish a generous scholarship fund, facilitate the recruitment of young talent from around the world and increase the number of students, university officials said in a statement. Two new programs will be created to bring in talented high school students. Future Music Moguls will be a free program for students in the New York City area, and the Summer High School Programs in Recorded Music will be open to students everywhere. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Executive Giving $5 Million to N.Y.U. to Expand Music Business Program', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houston, Whitney', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joplin, Janis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keys, Alicia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BMG Entertainment', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia Records', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'J Records', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and Museum', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tisch School of the Arts', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:27:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a9b927c-b1e3-5cd3-9d7a-f34faef90fc7', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a9b927c-b1e3-5cd3-9d7a-f34faef90fc7'}, {'abstract': 'A new study seeks to reduce the value of one’s college major to dollars and cents, and finds that the earnings of those who studied engineering far outpace those who majored in counseling and education. But is that definition of value too narrow?', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/whats-it-worth/', 'snippet': 'A new study seeks to reduce the value of one’s college major to dollars and cents, and finds that the earnings of those who studied engineering far outpace those who majored in counseling and education. But is that definition of value too narrow?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the cost of college climbs ever higher each year, amid a national economic forecast that remains cloudy, questions about the value of a four-year degree are being raised with increased urgency.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Calculating the Potential Return on Your Major', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:31:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e5717a5-466c-59af-8dfd-464d0e4c0f6e', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e5717a5-466c-59af-8dfd-464d0e4c0f6e'}, {'abstract': 'A dispute spurred by rising coal prices indicates that China’s unique marriage of market competition and government oversight may be starting to fray.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal.html', 'snippet': 'A dispute spurred by rising coal prices indicates that China’s unique marriage of market competition and government oversight may be starting to fray.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YIYANG, China — It is a power struggle that is causing a power shortage — one that has begun to slow China’s mighty economic growth engine.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal-span/25coal-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal-span/25coal-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal-span/25coal-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal-span/25coal-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/business/energy-environment/25coal-span/25coal-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Utilities Cut Energy Production, Defying Beijing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Power vs. Profit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rationing and Allocation of Resources', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:31:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cca3d54-e115-558a-b01d-fd74426c4a99', 'word_count': 1559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cca3d54-e115-558a-b01d-fd74426c4a99'}, {'abstract': 'Robert M. Parker Jr. and John Gilman have diametrically opposed reactions to their barrel tastings of a controversial St.-Émilion.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/sensational-or-just-bad-on-2010-pavie-two-critics-disagree/', 'snippet': 'Robert M. Parker Jr. and John Gilman have diametrically opposed reactions to their barrel tastings of a controversial St.-Émilion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One wine. One vintage. Two critics. Two reviews that couldn’t possibly be more different.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sensational’ or Just ‘Bad’? On 2010 Pavie, Two Critics Disagree', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Robert M Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bordeaux (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:37:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/93b71a7d-4785-5ca6-a1a7-612d4cab0ecb', 'word_count': 1036, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93b71a7d-4785-5ca6-a1a7-612d4cab0ecb'}, {'abstract': 'The 261 m.p.g. prototype worked the red carpet at a gala held at the Museum of Modern Art in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/at-moma-volkswagen-formula-xl1-makes-u-s-debut/', 'snippet': 'The 261 m.p.g. prototype worked the red carpet at a gala held at the Museum of Modern Art in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Martin Winterkorn, the Volkswagen Group chief executive, showed off the latest XL1 Formula prototype, as well as the actress Lucy Liu, on Monday night in Manhattan. Volkswagen claims that the 1.0-liter diesel hybrid can achieve 261 m.p.g.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At MoMA, Volkswagen Formula XL1 Makes U.S. Debut', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu, Lucy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winterkorn, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T16:39:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8287e205-fd1a-536c-a540-a78758ab3a2c', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8287e205-fd1a-536c-a540-a78758ab3a2c'}, {'abstract': 'Whether Monday’s Game 4 was an epic comeback by the Mavericks or an epic collapse by the Thunder, Dirk Nowitzki came out a winner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/sports/nowitzkis-grand-stand-leads-mavericks-comeback.html', 'snippet': 'Whether Monday’s Game 4 was an epic comeback by the Mavericks or an epic collapse by the Thunder, Dirk Nowitzki came out a winner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a Western Conference finals without the melodrama-a-minute Lakers and formerly age-defying Spurs, the Thunder and the Mavericks are filling in the dramatic narrative nicely. It was hard to ask for much more than Dallas erasing a 15-point deficit in the final five minutes in Monday night’s latest N.B.A. epic overtime victory. Yes, this is roughly the same cast of Dallas characters that made an art form of face-planting in the playoffs over the years and yes, the hero was the same Dirk Nowitzki who habitually seems to shrink about half a foot this time of year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/sports/24leading1/24leading1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 864, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/sports/24leading1/24leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/24/sports/24leading1/24leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 