media', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/46378ba3-4239-50fc-963f-1ba0ab30c784', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/46378ba3-4239-50fc-963f-1ba0ab30c784'}, {'abstract': 'The authorities are suing three companies and two individuals for reportedly manipulating the crude oil market in early 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/business/global/25oil.html', 'snippet': 'The authorities are suing three companies and two individuals for reportedly manipulating the crude oil market in early 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After oil prices surged past $100 a barrel in 2008, suspicions that traders had manipulated the market led to Congressional hearings and regulatory investigations. But they produced no solid cases in the record run-up in gasoline prices.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/business/oil/oil-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/business/oil/oil-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/business/oil/oil-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Suit Sees Manipulation of Oil Trades', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Suit Sees Manipulation of Oil Trades in 2008', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:35:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Bowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56ce1e13-cb40-54f1-96dc-0350f3ad76f3', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56ce1e13-cb40-54f1-96dc-0350f3ad76f3'}, {'abstract': 'A clothing designer applies his love for color and for organic products in another medium: painting.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/jack-of-all-trades-organic-by-john-patrick/', 'snippet': 'A clothing designer applies his love for color and for organic products in another medium: painting.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve been creating China ink paintings pretty steadily for the eight past years,” says John Patrick, the designer of Organic by John Patrick. But they were done only as gifts, not as a way of making money. All of that changed when Alicia Lubowski-Jahn of Fair Folks & a Goat, the artsy specialty store, contacted Patrick to sign him up for an art show to promote sustainable and socially responsible designs. There were two problems. Lubowski-Jahn, who organized the show, “Leaf // Cloud: Nature Tangible and Transcendent,” loves color, but Patrick’s paintings are black and white. Also, Patrick, who is an environmentalist, did not previously use organic paint.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jack of All Trades | Organic by John Patrick', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:39:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4160a857-8bfe-5676-afd8-1d957c2861e1', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4160a857-8bfe-5676-afd8-1d957c2861e1'}, {'abstract': 'Dell offers a $999 laptop with an aluminum and magnesium-alloy body that it hopes will compete with the thin models Apple sells.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/dell-tries-again-with-a-thin-laptop/', 'snippet': 'Dell offers a $999 laptop with an aluminum and magnesium-alloy body that it hopes will compete with the thin models Apple sells.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dell’s mixed results selling consumer products has not stopped it from trying yet again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dell Tries Again With a Thin Laptop', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dell Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:40:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verne G. Kopytoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verne', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Kopytoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b03cd04d-7dae-5774-95e3-ea5f60d7fa06', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b03cd04d-7dae-5774-95e3-ea5f60d7fa06'}, {'abstract': 'Experimentation with the fiano grape has produced some eye-catching results.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/dining/reviews/fiano-enjoys-a-renaissance-wines-of-the-times.html', 'snippet': 'Experimentation with the fiano grape has produced some eye-catching results.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR one of the more dramatic examples of how the world of wine has changed in the last 25 years, consider the white wines of Italy.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25wine/WINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 861, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25wine/WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25wine/WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiano Enjoys a Renaissance', 'kicker': 'Wines of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Revival of Italian White Wines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:43:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Dining & Wine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85035b1-9385-56ae-9682-08c4211169d8', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85035b1-9385-56ae-9682-08c4211169d8'}, {'abstract': 'Bien Cuit will open this summer; Hakkasan will open in the theater district; and Jordan Frosolone is the executive chef at August, in the West Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/dining/off-the-menu.html', 'snippet': 'Bien Cuit will open this summer; Hakkasan will open in the theater district; and Jordan Frosolone is the executive chef at August, in the West Village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOSPODA  The dramatically decorated dining room, with soaring ceilings and backlighted images of beer, food and people engraved in dark wood, is the final element of a 10-year restoration of the historic Bohemian National Hall, owned by the Czech Republic. Oldrich Sahajdak, the executive chef, who also works at the parent company’s restaurants in Prague and elsewhere, has overseen a menu of lightened dishes rooted in tradition, like butter-poached Prague ham with horseradish foam, and flatiron steak with dill sauce and barley dumplings. To the concise menu of beer snacks and smallish plates, a tasting menu will be added this summer. Marek Sada is chef de cuisine. The beer is Pilsner Urquell but is dispensed in four styles led by draught master Lukas Svoboda: 321 East 73rd Street, (212) 861-1038. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/dining/25off/25OFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/24/dining/25off/25off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/24/dining/25off/25off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Menu', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off the Menu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:48:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98dfe240-4e32-573c-ae6b-efb8b3c7d9b6', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98dfe240-4e32-573c-ae6b-efb8b3c7d9b6'}, {'abstract': 'At G-Free NYC, Lynn Shuter sells breads, granolas, cookies, cakes, pastas, kale chips, popcorn and other items.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/dining/g-free-nyc-a-shop-for-products-without-gluten-food-stuff.html', 'snippet': 'At G-Free NYC, Lynn Shuter sells breads, granolas, cookies, cakes, pastas, kale chips, popcorn and other items.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gluten-free products have come so far in the last few years that Lynn Shuter decided she could open a whole shop devoted to them. At G-Free NYC, she sells breads, granolas, cookies and cakes as well as more commonplace snacks that people who are gluten-intolerant can enjoy, like kale chips and popcorn. Fresh baked goods from a number of sources are set out on a display table, some offered for tasting. The shelves and freezers are filled with other foods, including biscotti, pastas and frozen pita breads and boxes of small pizza crusts. Many of the products are dairy-free and some are vegan. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-gluten/25stuff-gluten-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-gluten/25stuff-gluten-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-gluten/25stuff-gluten-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-gluten/25stuff-gluten-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-gluten/25stuff-gluten-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Avoiding Gluten? There’s a Whole Shop for You', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Avoiding Gluten? There’s a Whole Shop for You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gluten', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:48:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0bbd49e-4f8a-5464-97d6-273362f2ad52', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0bbd49e-4f8a-5464-97d6-273362f2ad52'}, {'abstract': 'Shane Welch, the founder and brewmaster of Sixpoint Craft Ales, said aluminum cans keep beer fresher than bottles because they block out light and have no air space on top.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/dining/sixpoint-craft-ales-sells-beer-in-cans-food-stuff.html', 'snippet': 'Shane Welch, the founder and brewmaster of Sixpoint Craft Ales, said aluminum cans keep beer fresher than bottles because they block out light and have no air space on top.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After six years of brewing ales in more than 200 styles for bars and restaurants to sell on draught, Sixpoint Craft Ales in Red Hook, Brooklyn, is selling beer in cans. Shane Welch, the founder and brewmaster, said aluminum cans keep beer fresher than bottles: they block out light and have no air space on top. They are also lighter to ship. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-beer/25stuff-beer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-beer/25stuff-beer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-beer/25stuff-beer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sixpoint Brews Are Now in Cans', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sixpoint Brews Are Now in Cans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:48:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e719fc7a-c03c-552d-beb2-ed6953e88a6d', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e719fc7a-c03c-552d-beb2-ed6953e88a6d'}, {'abstract': 'Two places in Brooklyn and one in Greenwich Village offer maple-bacon, chocolate stout with pretzels, black sesame-seaweed, mixed vegetable and goat milk (ice cream).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/dining/ample-hills-creamery-sky-ice-and-victory-garden-food-stuff.html', 'snippet': 'Two places in Brooklyn and one in Greenwich Village offer maple-bacon, chocolate stout with pretzels, black sesame-seaweed, mixed vegetable and goat milk (ice cream).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brian Smith, the owner of Ample Hills Creamery, right, a bright shop that just opened in Prospect Heights, Brooklyn, is scooping salted caramel, maple-bacon, chocolate stout with pretzels, all-natural bubble gum tinted with beet juice and 19 other flavors, including chocolate and vanilla. He custom blends his bases (some with eggs, some not), so by law he needs to pasteurize his mixtures of milk and cream from local dairies on site. Homemade cones, and cakes with ice cream and blondies or brownies are also on the menu. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-icecream/STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-icecream/25stuff-icecream-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/dining/25stuff-icecream/25stuff-icecream-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Satisfy That Hankering for Mixed-Vegetable Ice Cream', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Satisfy That Hankering for Mixed Vegetable Ice Cream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ICE CREAM', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:48:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc285a60-eda1-526d-9da9-6f12cfd6311e', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc285a60-eda1-526d-9da9-6f12cfd6311e'}, {'abstract': 'Keeping four-month-old Storm’s sex a secret.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/is-this-baby-a-boy-or-girl/', 'snippet': 'Keeping four-month-old Storm’s sex a secret.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When we discussed the trend toward “gender neutral parenting” on Motherlode earlier this month,we used the term to mean things like not finding out the sex of the baby before birth, allowing children to choose clothes from either department at the store, and not limiting choice of play in any way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is This Baby a Boy or Girl?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:49:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26200c7e-d3a8-53af-9041-ae1e13ffddf2', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26200c7e-d3a8-53af-9041-ae1e13ffddf2'}, {'abstract': 'Tiger Woods, who has spent the last week wearing a protective boot on his left leg to immobilize his sore left Achilles’ tendon, said he expected to be ready for the United States Open from June 16-19 at Congressional Country Club in Bethesda, Md.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/woods-says-injury-is-a-cakewalk-compared-with-2008-ordeal/', 'snippet': 'Tiger Woods, who has spent the last week wearing a protective boot on his left leg to immobilize his sore left Achilles’ tendon, said he expected to be ready for the United States Open from June 16-19 at Congressional Country Club in Bethesda, Md.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tiger Woods said Tuesday that he spent the last week wearing a protective boot on his left leg to immobilize his sore left Achilles’ tendon, and hobbled around on crutches to protect the strained medial collateral ligament in his knee and prevent further pain to his sore back. He also said he expected to be ready for the United States Open from June 16-19 at Congressional Country Club in Bethesda, Md.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Woods Expects to Play U.S. Open', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Country Club', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T18:52:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Dorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38846119-f4b5-519a-866b-e8947632b187', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38846119-f4b5-519a-866b-e8947632b187'}, {'abstract': 'The second episode of “What’s Trending,” the new Web series from CBS News, benefited from funnier, livelier guests than the first.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/watchlist-a-second-look-at-whats-trending/', 'snippet': 'The second episode of “What’s Trending,” the new Web series from CBS News, benefited from funnier, livelier guests than the first.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier today, Mike Hale reviewed the premiere episode of “What’s Trending,” the new Web series from CBS News. He took in the second episode this afternoon:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watchlist: A Second Look at ‘What’s Trending’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T19:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed99abd1-6d0d-56cc-9570-1a229c98e3cf', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed99abd1-6d0d-56cc-9570-1a229c98e3cf'}, {'abstract': 'The union representing N.B.A. players announced that it had filed a complaint with the National Labor Relations Board accusing league officials of failing to negotiate in good faith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/sports/basketball/players-accuse-nba-of-failing-to-bargain-in-good-faith.html', 'snippet': 'The union representing N.B.A. players announced that it had filed a complaint with the National Labor Relations Board accusing league officials of failing to negotiate in good faith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The legal maneuvering in the N.B.A.’s labor battle began in earnest Tuesday, when the players union filed a complaint with the National Labor Relations Board, accusing league officials of failing to negotiate in good faith.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turning to Labor Board, N.B.A. Union Fires First', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turning to Labor Board, N.B.A. Union Fires First', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Players Assn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T19:04:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b526d2ef-cfbe-5b79-b00d-99d3a2c1bbde', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b526d2ef-cfbe-5b79-b00d-99d3a2c1bbde'}, {'abstract': 'More about “legislating parenting,” and an update on my ankle.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/parenting-tornadoes-and-surgical-boots/', 'snippet': 'More about “legislating parenting,” and an update on my ankle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was on the “Today” show on Monday discussing my Sunday column about “legislating parenting.” It was an interesting day to be in the studio, with news from Joplin, Mo.,\xa0 breaking all around. The everyday struggles of parenting get put in their place when you know there are parents out there who have lost everything.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parenting, Tornadoes and Surgical Boots', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T19:05:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e2f315b-60dd-5ee5-acde-61cdeb98f28a', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e2f315b-60dd-5ee5-acde-61cdeb98f28a'}, {'abstract': 'Afghans seemed content to let reports of Mullah Muhammad Omar’s possible death vanish with the news cycle.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/24/a-rumor-from-the-roundabout/', 'snippet': 'Afghans seemed content to let reports of Mullah Muhammad Omar’s possible death vanish with the news cycle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — There’s an old saying here that goes “awaza sar-e chowk,” a rumor from the roundabout. If a bit of news seems hard to believe, you might hear it. Kabul residents found themselves saying it plenty on Monday when Afghan media reported the news that Mullah Muhammad Omar, the Taliban’s reclusive, one-eyed leader, was dead. The news sprang from the National Directorate of Security, the country’s main intelligence agency. Yes, it came from unnamed sources inside the agency, which publicly would say only that Mullah Omar was missing, but it carried enough currency that Western officials, though skeptical, would not come right out and say it was wrong. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rumor From the Roundabout', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Omar, Muhammad (Taliban Leader)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-24T19:09:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Rivera and Sangar Rahimi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sangar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rahimi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/335e45bd-ee60-5510-a901-009cb7b66af0', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/335e45bd-ee60-5510-a901-009cb7b66af0'}, {'abstract': 'Patrick Widmann measuring the knee joint of a fossil, and then exposing more of the bone; he appears to be quite surprised by what he uncovers.', '