ty. But he had died at ground zero, trying to help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If memory serves, the police captain met me over coffee on a Wednesday night in October 2001, when the world was dizzy with menace. He passed along word of a fresh worry. A young Muslim chemist, who worked in an advanced biochemistry laboratory in Midtown, had vanished on the morning of Sept. 11. Because he had once been a member of a police cadet program, he had an ID card that gave him access to police facilities. His name was Mohammad Salman Hamdani, and the captain had just been faxed a flier about him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/nyregion/ABOUT2/ABOUT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/25/nyregion/ABOUT2/ABOUT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/25/nyregion/ABOUT2/ABOUT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Legacy for a Son Whose Character, After 9/11, Was Misjudged', 'kicker': 'About New York', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Legacy for a Son Whose Character, After 9/11, Was Misjudged', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Queens College, City University of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T01:06:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Dwyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dwyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12c4e65d-d2ce-5733-9bfd-44948bb1639e', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12c4e65d-d2ce-5733-9bfd-44948bb1639e'}, {'abstract': 'The G-8 seems determined to fudge the numbers rather than admit to a broken promise.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/opinion/25wed3.html', 'snippet': 'The G-8 seems determined to fudge the numbers rather than admit to a broken promise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final communiqués haven’t been written. But the word on the street is that when leaders of the Group of 8 industrialized countries meet in France this week, they will claim that wealthy countries have come close to fulfilling their 2005 promise to boost annual development aid by $50 billion by 2010. They are not even in the ballpark. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The G-8’s Self-Serving Math', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The G-8’s Self-Serving Math', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group of Eight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T01:07:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c18