{'abstract': 'Tell us what you think about this writer’s theory that 14 is a “magic age” for developing cultural tastes that can last a lifetime. What music, movies or books did you love at that age?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/is-14-a-magic-age-for-forming-cultural-tastes/', 'snippet': 'Tell us what you think about this writer’s theory that 14 is a “magic age” for developing cultural tastes that can last a lifetime. What music, movies or books did you love at that age?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an Op-Ed celebrating Bob Dylan’s 70th birthday Tuesday, one writer suggests that the cultural tastes you develop at age 14, especially in music, can influence your tastes for the rest of your life. What music did you love at 14? What books, movies, television shows or video games? How do you think these artistic tastes will continue to influence you? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is 14 a ‘Magic Age’ for Forming Cultural Tastes?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T08:07:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0aa9b5b-67fa-5896-aa76-c154014c5a03', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0aa9b5b-67fa-5896-aa76-c154014c5a03'}, {'abstract': 'A series of storms struck Oklahoma, Kansas and Arkansas late Tuesday and early Wednesday, wiping out homes and businesses and killing at least 14 people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/us/26storm.html', 'snippet': 'A series of storms struck Oklahoma, Kansas and Arkansas late Tuesday and early Wednesday, wiping out homes and businesses and killing at least 14 people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Residents of the South and Midwest braced for another round of severe weather on Wednesday, only hours after at least 15 people were killed in a series of storms that struck portions of Arkansas, Kansas and Oklahoma, where nine people died.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/us/26stormspan/26stormspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/26/us/26stormspan/26stormspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/us/26stormspan/26stormspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/us/26stormspan/26stormspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/26/us/26stormspan/26stormspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Least 14 People Are Killed in Storms in 3 States', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Storms Kill at Least 14 People in 3 States', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Worth (Tex)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dallas (Tex)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARKANSAS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T08:43:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94ae9564-9c90-559d-bf0b-068bbfc0d0dc', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94ae9564-9c90-559d-bf0b-068bbfc0d0dc'}, {'abstract': 'In this essay, Stephen Crowley sees the problem of Washington photo coverage as far broader than President Obama’s re-enacted speech.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/whose-eye-what-beholder/', 'snippet': 'In this essay, Stephen Crowley sees the problem of Washington photo coverage as far broader than President Obama’s re-enacted speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel were clearly of two very different minds about the prospect of Israel reverting to its pre-1967 borders. The official White House photo by Pete Souza — showing the two leaders huddling outside the Oval Office on Friday — didn’t make life easy for picture editors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whose Eye? What Beholder?', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'White House (Washington, DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T09:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Crowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3fdb50f-6181-5485-a8e9-59186a710331', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3fdb50f-6181-5485-a8e9-59186a710331'}, {'abstract': 'Transportation disruptions caused by an Icelandic volcano spread on Wednesday to northern Germany. British air traffic was expected to return to normal amid signs that the volcano was no longer erupting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/world/europe/26volcano.html', 'snippet': 'Transportation disruptions caused by an Icelandic volcano spread on Wednesday to northern Germany. British air traffic was expected to return to normal amid signs that the volcano was no longer erupting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — A dense cloud of volcanic ash that forced the brief morning closings of airports in Berlin and other German cities blew farther east on Wednesday, allowing most commercial air traffic to resume as the second major eruption of an Icelandic volcano in two years appeared to be sputtering to an end. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/world/26volcanospan/26volcanospan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/26/world/26volcanospan/26volcanospan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/world/26volcanospan/26volcanospan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/world/26volcanospan/26vo