n-loans/', 'snippet': 'GE Capital said on Wednesday that it had agreed to sell mortgages worth about 5 billion Australian dollars to Pepper Homeloans for an undisclosed sum, as high prices feed concern about a bubble in Australia’s housing market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GE Capital said on Wednesday that it had agreed to sell mortgages worth about 5 billion Australian dollars ($5.2 billion) to Pepper Homeloans for an undisclosed sum, as high prices in the country’s housing market reinforce fears about its real estate sector.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'GE Capital Unloads $5.2 Billion in Australian Loans', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T09:40:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d696d65-8dbf-513b-b73f-de13cb03e89b', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d696d65-8dbf-513b-b73f-de13cb03e89b'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama told the Parliament that Britain and America were the “greatest catalysts for global action.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/world/europe/26prexy.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama told the Parliament that Britain and America were the “greatest catalysts for global action.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Britain and the United States remain “indispensable” partners, President Obama declared Wednesday in an address to the British Parliament, even as he and Prime Minister David Cameron revealed subtle differences over issues like the military campaign in Libya and the Middle East peace process.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'sou