Taliban', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kunduz (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T13:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21504683-3ae0-5063-b6a2-330b44a9ba07', 'word_count': 1548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21504683-3ae0-5063-b6a2-330b44a9ba07'}, {'abstract': 'An oyster house in Seattle proves that the beach-to-bar dining scene is thriving in the Pacific Northwest.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/aw-shucks-elliotts-oyster-house/', 'snippet': 'An oyster house in Seattle proves that the beach-to-bar dining scene is thriving in the Pacific Northwest.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There’s a renaissance in the shellfish world right now, especially oysters,” says Robert Spaulding, the executive chef at Elliott’s Oyster House in Seattle. Elliott’s considers the oyster to be emblematic of the Pacific Northwest’s thriving farm-to-table — or, in this case, beach-to-bar — dining scene. Elliott’s has an expansive raw bar and many direct relationships with Northwestern oyster growers. As Spaulding puts it, shucking knife in hand and flanked by a seemingly endless battalion of shells on ice, “It’s part of that movement to get out there and create something yourself.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aw, Shucks | Elliott’s Oyster House', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T13:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byli