n, but his clumsy performance is eclipsed by his giant smile. Across the lofty room, a group of young men practice balancing on two piles of rough-edged bricks — on two hands, on one hand, legs moving in all directions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Up | GoDown Arts Centre', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T15:59:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marina Cashdan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cashdan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fa981ae-cb16-5cb9-ab57-5ae647f4fd2a', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fa981ae-cb16-5cb9-ab57-5ae647f4fd2a'}, {'abstract': 'In 2006, a hatchling hawk was banded near the Delaware Water Gap for a study of hawk migration patterns. She grew up to be Violet, the Hawk Cam mom, and nearly five years later, the band is at the center of the debate about her health.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/hawk-cam-the-story-of-violets-leg-band/', 'snippet': 'In 2006, a hatchling hawk was banded near the Delaware Water Gap for a study of hawk migration patterns. She grew up to be Violet, the Hawk Cam mom, and nearly five years later, the band is at the center of the debate about her health.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:41 p.m. | Bits and pieces of this story have been bouncing around the Web and the airwaves for a while now, but not yet formally reported in this space, so here goes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hawk Cam | The Story of Violet’s Leg Band', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'k