}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc1b2155-0a82-5a67-8be9-b2c6debe79e2', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc1b2155-0a82-5a67-8be9-b2c6debe79e2'}, {'abstract': 'A blog post by Yasmine El Rashidi is a reminder that the bogeymen in Egypt are not all political nor are threats there necessarily hypothetical. Religious clashes have already killed more than a dozen people in the Egyptian capital this month.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/tahrir-square-is-it-good-for-the-christians/', 'snippet': 'A blog post by Yasmine El Rashidi is a reminder that the bogeymen in Egypt are not all political nor are threats there necessarily hypothetical. Religious clashes have already killed more than a dozen people in the Egyptian capital this month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the flags were packed away, the euphoria faded and protesters swept all of the dropped signs and cigarette butts out of Tahrir Square, politicians around the world started to ask “what if?” What if the Muslim Brotherhood gains power? What if they stifle freedoms in Egypt or threaten peace with Israel? President Obama recently announced $1 billion of debt relief and $1 billion in loans for Egypt. But as David E. Sanger reported this weekend, one of Obama’s strategists said, “We think there’s a ‘Muslim Brotherhood exception’ to the aid package.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tahrir Square: Is It Good for the Christians?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tahrir Square (Cairo)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T17:09:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34b27186-bbbe-5be9-a7f7-0225a7ca0945', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34b27186-bbbe-5be9-a7f7-0225a7ca0945'}, {'abstract': 'Bad fortune seems to befall Mets players who are honored with a bobblehead doll sponsored by Gold’s Horseradish.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/the-mets-bobblehead-curse-lives/', 'snippet': 'Bad fortune seems to befall Mets players who are honored with a bobblehead doll sponsored by Gold’s Horseradish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest issue of New Yorker magazine, Fred Wilpon said that the Mets were “snakebitten” because of the rash of player injuries, last-season meltdowns and financial calamities to befall the club the last few seasons.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mets’ Bobblehead Curse Lives', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bay, Jason', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duca, Paul Lo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franco, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martinez, Pedro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piazza, Mike', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Francisco', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santana, Johan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}]