83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b50f2e7-69bb-5a01-987e-f0404f3f8aeb'}, {'abstract': 'Term used by a team of computer scientists investigating spam emails. (Spam + analytics.)', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/spamalytics/', 'snippet': 'Term used by a team of computer scientists investigating spam emails. (Spam + analytics.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'Term used by a team of computer scientists investigating spam emails.(Spam + analytics.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spamalytics', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spam (Electronic Mail)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T17:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d30f4ce-7ee9-5116-9278-029cc1689137', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d30f4ce-7ee9-5116-9278-029cc1689137'}, {'abstract': 'Paulick Report talked with Jimmy Learned, the founder and president of the advertising agency Elevation Ltd., which created “Get Your Preak On” and “Kegasus” campaigns for the Preakness in 2010 and 2011:', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/creating-kegasus/', 'snippet': 'Paulick Report talked with Jimmy Learned, the founder and president of the advertising agency Elevation Ltd., which created “Get Your Preak On” and “Kegasus” campaigns for the Preakness in 2010 and 2011:', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Paulick Report talked with Jimmy Learned, the founder and president of the advertising agency Elevation Ltd., which created the “Get Your Preak On” and “Kegasus” campaigns for the Preakness in 2010 and 2011: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Creating Kegasus', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T17:40:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5eab980-d654-5388-a5db-32eca154c201', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5eab980-d654-5388-a5db-32eca154c201'}, {'abstract': 'Not many people may have been watching, but the Canucks’ Ryan Kesler, playing essentially on one leg, put Vancouver in the Stanley Cup finals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/sports/american-hero-in-canada.html', 'snippet': 'Not many people may have been watching, but the Canucks’ Ryan Kesler, playing essentially on one leg, put Vancouver in the Stanley Cup finals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are a variety of possible reactions to the Miami Heat appearing poised to crash the N.B.A. finals after Tuesday night’s victory over the Chicago Bulls: joy, grudging acceptance, pure unadulterated misery (headquarters: Cleveland) and just plain fatigue. Yes, the Big Three, above all its other attributes, is overexposed, every sigh and hangnail analyzed for social and political implications. We’ll get to today’s overexposure, but first, perhaps, a bit of an antidote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Hero in Canada', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Hero in Canada', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vancouver Canucks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T17:43:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96d023f3-5fad-5c2b-9c8a-7c3e277c0245', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96d023f3-5fad-5c2b-9c8a-7c3e277c0245'}, {'abstract': 'As our culture increasingly enshrines physical perfection, the bikini has come to inspire dread and awe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/fashion/the-bikini-as-a-badge-of-fitness.html', 'snippet': 'As our culture increasingly enshrines physical perfection, the bikini has come to inspire dread and awe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERY spring, as sure as cherry blossoms bloom, so does the notion that women should diet, exercise and even liposuction their way to bikini-ready bodies. Magazines like Shape and Self reinforce the idea that preparation is essential, with months-long bikini body countdowns (100-calorie snack tips included) and workout DVDs like “Bikini Ready Fast!” as if the beach reveal were a test on par with the MCAT and only a slacker would settle for a one-piece.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26SKIN-1/Z-SKIN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 719, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26SKIN-1/Z-SKIN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26SKIN-1/Z-SKIN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Bikini-Ready’? Who’s Judging?', 'kicker': 'Skin Deep', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Bikini-Ready’? Who’s Judging?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathing Suits', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T17:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Saint Louis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': 'Saint', 'lastname': 'Louis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d386643-064a-578a-89d2-ad88e03f81f3', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d386643-064a-578a-89d2-ad88e03f81f3'}, {'abstract': 'The filmmakers of “Kung Fu Panda 2” explored more of China for inspiration in scenery and design.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/movies/kung-fu-panda-2-design-choices.html', 'snippet': 'The filmmakers of “Kung Fu Panda 2” explored more of China for inspiration in scenery and design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW hard is it to maintain a life of awesomeness? It’s not as simple as one particular panda skilled in the martial arts would like. In “Kung Fu Panda 2,” the follow-up to Dreamworks Animation’s 2008 hit, Jack Black returns as the voice of Po, the clownish panda with a laid-back vibe who becomes the Dragon Warrior, keeping order in the Valley of Peace with help from his fellow kung fu masters, the Furious Five. But when a villainous peacock, Lord Shen (Gary 