tion_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19897fec-d48c-5942-bc16-bd959f15e11f', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19897fec-d48c-5942-bc16-bd959f15e11f'}, {'abstract': 'Thursday’s schedule includes No. 1 Rafael Nadal, coming off his five-set struggle with John Isner, playing his fellow Spaniard Pablo Andujar. That match will be played on Court Suzanne Lenglen, the second show court, following No. 5 Robin Soderling versus Albert Ramos, and No. 6 Li Na versus Silvia Soler-Espinosa.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/what-to-watch-thursday-at-the-french-open/', 'snippet': 'Thursday’s schedule includes No. 1 Rafael Nadal, coming off his five-set struggle with John Isner, playing his fellow Spaniard Pablo Andujar. That match will be played on Court Suzanne Lenglen, the second show court, following No. 5 Robin Soderlin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS -– The weather at the French Open, near perfect and sunny through the first four days –- there were rumors of a cloud somewhere over Paris Wednesday, but that has not been confirmed — is expected to turn a bit cool and blustery on Thursday. But the tennis should remain plenty hot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What to Watch Thursday at the French Open', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-25T21:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28af031d-b7d1-53cd-a071-70bcb6e61b1f', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28af031d-b7d1-53cd-a071-70bcb6e61b1f'}, {'abstract': 'Forward-thinking merchants are championing scarves’ versatility, anointing this once-humble wardrobe understudy as the unlikely diva of the spring and summer season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/fashion/scarves-succeed-as-the-ultimate-fashion-multitaskers.html', 'snippet': 'Forward-thinking merchants are championing scarves’ versatility, anointing this once-humble wardrobe understudy as the unlikely diva of the spring and summer season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MARIANGELA MELATO could work a scarf like Salome. In “Swept Away,” Lina Wertmüller’s 1974 screen exploration of class and gender warfare, her character, a castaway, makes do mostly with a single wardrobe item, an outsize scarf that she presses into service as a head wrap, a sultry halter dress, a lens-fogging slit-to-the-hip sarong and, when the waters get choppy, a sheltering cocoon.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26scarves-span/26scarves-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26scarves-span/26scarves-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/fashion/26scarves-span/26scarves-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 757, 'width': 791, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image