rint_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:13:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f083fe6-4fa0-5c1f-a271-e98f629c87be', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f083fe6-4fa0-5c1f-a271-e98f629c87be'}, {'abstract': 'A cap on New York property taxes would devastate schools and is no substitute for leadership.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/opinion/26thu1.html', 'snippet': 'A cap on New York property taxes would devastate schools and is no substitute for leadership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo and the New York State Legislature have already adopted a state budget that drastically cuts funds to schools and local communities — cuts that were far deeper than needed to balance the budget because of Mr. Cuomo’s indefensible refusal to extend a tax surcharge on New York’s wealthiest residents. Now they want to adopt a cheap political tool — a 2 percent property tax cap — that would only further devastate communities around the state that can least afford it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reject the Tax Cap', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reject the Tax Cap: A cap on New York property taxes would devastate schools and is no substitute for leadership', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Property Taxes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/182e3939-bdeb-581e-87bd-9a57a4a38243', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/182e3939-bdeb-581e-87bd-9a57a4a38243'}, {'abstract': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau needs to rewrite the credit reporting rules so that consumers have a way of exonerating themselves without hiring lawyers and going to court.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/opinion/26thu3.html', 'snippet': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau needs to rewrite the credit reporting rules so that consumers have a way of exonerating themselves without hiring lawyers and going to court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The credit reporting industry has vast reach and power. The information it gathers and sells is used to determine eligibility for credit cards, mortgages and even some jobs. Federal regulators haven’t had the authority or the means to protect consumers who have been victims of inaccurate reports. The new Consumer Financial Protection Bureau will have that authority. It has a big job ahead.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kafka Couldn’t Have Built a Better System', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kafka Couldn’t Have Built a Better System', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Scores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:18:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb732a8d-d94d-5c85-9f30-6942af3b353b', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb732a8d-d94d-5c85-9f30-6942af3b353b'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s podcast is about mobile apps for travel on the road, in the air and with children.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/25/tech-talk-podcast-vacation-apps/', 'snippet': 'This week’s podcast is about mobile apps for travel on the road, in the air and with children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time to start getting ready for that summer vacation! On this week’s episode of Bits: Tech Talk, Bettina Edelstein, J.D. Biersdorfer and Pedro Rafael Rosado chat about mobile apps for travel on the road, in the air and with children.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Vacation Apps', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9662179-4dc0-5afc-a234-954bf74dc6ac', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9662179-4dc0-5afc-a234-954bf74dc6ac'}, {'abstract': 'A new Republican Super PAC will compound the damage of the Supreme Court’s Citizens United decision on corporate donations. Just how low will a candidate go for fat-cat money?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/opinion/26thu4.html', 'snippet': 'A new Republican Super PAC will compound the damage of the Supreme Court’s Citizens United decision on corporate donations. Just how low will a candidate go for fat-cat money?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since Watergate, federal office holders and candidates have been barred from soliciting unlimited money for partisan campaign machines. Now three members of the Republican National Committee are trying to turn the law on its head with a new Republican Super PAC. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Republicans’ ChutzPAC', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Republicans’ ChutzPAC', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican National Committee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:21:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcf7795d-39ef-5e0f-9b78-21cc7c48bb9e', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcf7795d-39ef-5e0f-9b78-21cc7c48bb9e'}, {'abstract': 'Democrats cannot spent their entire campaign attacking Paul Ryan’s budget plan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/opinion/26thu2.html', 'snippet': 'Democrats cannot spent their entire campaign attacking Paul Ryan’s budget plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After their surprise victory in a heavily Republican Congressional district of western New York, Democrats believe they have the magic formula for winning back the House next year. “Medicare, Medicare, and Medicare,” said Representative Steve Israel of Long Island, who runs the Democratic election effort in the House. There is enormous power in Mr. Israel’s new cudgel. Even Paul Ryan, the Wisconsin Republican who wrote the plan to privatize Medicare and end its guarantee to the elderly, admitted that it played a significant role in the victory by Kathy Hochul, the Democrat. Nonetheless, effective as the playbook was in one special election, Democrats cannot expect to build their entire 2012 campaign around attacking the Ryan plan. It’s a useful reminder of the vast differences between the parties, but defines only the Republicans, not the Democrats. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Running on Medicare the Right Way', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Running on Medicare the Right Way: Democrats cannot spent their entire campaign attacking Paul Ryan’s budget plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T02:21:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/716fad72-1531-5018-ab38-7eab70efa0cf', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/716fad72-1531-5018-ab38-7eab70efa0cf'}, {'abstract': 'The organizers of Georgia’s protest movement were demanding the ouster of President Mikheil Saakashvili.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/world/europe/26georgia.html', 'snippet': 'The organizers of Georgia’s protest movement were demanding the ouster of President Mikheil Saakashvili.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — An opposition protest in the Georgian capital of Tbilisi was violently broken up by riot police officers late Wednesday, scattering about a thousand demo