': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilpon, Fred', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T14:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir and Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/709cbff2-4105-5902-8760-67005f9c6fcc', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/709cbff2-4105-5902-8760-67005f9c6fcc'}, {'abstract': 'The state would like charge childless adults on Medicaid who smoke or are obese $50. “What we want to test is whether making people pay is going to affect behavior,” said a spokeswoman for Arizona’s Medicaid program. “We think it will.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/us/27questions.html', 'snippet': 'The state would like charge childless adults on Medicaid who smoke or are obese $50. “What we want to test is whether making people pay is going to affect behavior,” said a spokeswoman for Arizona’s Medicaid program. “We think it will.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arizona, like many others states, says it is no longer able to adequately finance its Medicaid program. As part of a plan to cut costs, the state has proposed imposing a $50 fee on childless adults on Medicaid who are either obese or who smoke. In Arizona, almost half of all Medicaid recipients smoke; while the number of obese people is unclear, about one-in-four Arizonans is overweight, according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. The state’s plan must ultimately be approved by the federal government. Monica Coury, spokeswoman for Arizona’s Medicaid program, discusses. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under an Arizona Plan, Smokers and Obese Would Pay Fee for Medicaid', 'kicker': 'Asked And Answered | Monica Coury', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under an Arizona Plan, Smokers and Obese Would Pay Fee for Medicaid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T14:20:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c827cce5-e0fe-5388-9f9d-f41aad2a46a4', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c827cce5-e0fe-5388-9f9d-f41aad2a46a4'}, {'abstract': 'Birreria will have its own craft beers, brewed there.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/eatalys-beer-garden-opens-to-the-sky-soon-to-the-public/', 'snippet': 'Birreria will have its own craft beers, brewed there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could not have picked a better evening than last night to exhibit the allure of Birreria, the rooftop beer garden at Eataly. At a preview party\xa0 for journalists and other invited guests,\xa0 Birreria’s retractable roof, rimmed with greenery, was open on a clear warm night to a great view of the clock on the Met Life tower across Madison Square Park.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eataly’s Beer Garden Opens to the Sky; Soon, to the Public', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eataly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T14:20:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8300984d-f5c5-5d14-9890-faecd41642ae', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8300984d-f5c5-5d14-9890-faecd41642ae'}, {'abstract': 'Before he went into hiding, Ratko Mladic, the former commander of the Bosnian Serb Army who was arrested on Thursday in connection with the 1995 massacre of nearly 8,000 men and boys at Srebrenica, embraced the spotlight.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/a-bosnian-serb-general-who-loved-the-cameras-returns-to-the-spotlight/', 'snippet': 'Before he went into hiding, Ratko Mladic, the former commander of the Bosnian Serb Army who was arrested on Thursday in connection with the 1995 massacre of nearly 8,000 men and boys at Srebrenica, embraced the spotlight.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 2:45 p.m. To journalists and investigators poring over brief snatches of video shot recently in Libya or Syria for possible evidence of war crimes committed this year, it comes as something of a shock to be confronted with footage from the 1990s shot by the television cameras that accompanied Ratko Mladic, then the commander of the Bosnian Serb Army, as he entered Srebrenica in 1995. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bosnian Serb General Who Loved the Cameras Returns to the Spotlight', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T14:21:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'sec