ive is the biggest overhang on Microsoft’s stock price.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:53 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Microsoft Shares Rise After Call to Oust Ballmer', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ballmer, Steven A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einhorn, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:37:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86bc5e8b-ac46-5f40-ba44-3df749c432b1', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86bc5e8b-ac46-5f40-ba44-3df749c432b1'}, {'abstract': '“The Tree of Life,” directed by Terrence Malick, envisions the origin of the universe and ponders some of life’s deepest questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/movies/the-tree-of-life-from-terrence-malick-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Tree of Life,” directed by Terrence Malick, envisions the origin of the universe and ponders some of life’s deepest questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Day of Judgment, prophesied for last weekend, has apparently been postponed, but moviegoers eager for rapture can find consolation — to say nothing of awe, amazement and grist for endless argument — in “The Tree of Life,” Terrence Malick’s new film, which contemplates human existence from the standpoint of eternity. Recently showered with temporal glory at the Cannes Film Festival, where it won the Palme d’Or, this movie, Mr. Malick’s fifth feature in 38 years, folds eons of cosmic and terrestrial history into less than two and a half hours. Its most provocative sequences envision the origin of the universe, the development of life on earth (including a few soulful dinosaurs) and then, more concisely and less literally, the end of time, when the dead of all the ages shall rise and walk around on a heavenly beach.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/movies/27tree-span/27tree-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/movies/27tree-span/27tree-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/movies/27tree-span/27tree-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/movies/27tree-span/27tree-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/movies/27tree-span/27tree-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Heaven, Texas and the Cosmic Whodunit', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Tree of Life'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heaven, Texas And the Cosmic Whodunit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malick, Terrence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisk, Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pitt, Brad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penn, Sean', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Tree of Life, The (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:40:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a97784d3-c776-5706-8dde-24cdde6b4d6e', 'word_count': 1706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a97784d3-c776-5706-8dde-24cdde6b4d6e'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time again for our weekly contest: guess the location of the photograph. Your hint: One of this lake’s most popular attractions is a medieval fortress.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/guess-where-this-is-16/', 'snippet': 'It’s time again for our weekly contest: guess the location of the photograph. Your hint: One of this lake’s most popular attractions is a medieval fortress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time again for our weekly contest: guess the location of the photograph above.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guess Where This Is', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:45:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e53d7028-79b9-59f0-a109-95d2b06e52dc', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e53d7028-79b9-59f0-a109-95d2b06e52dc'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Voigt was angry. So angry that he went on a 60-day all-potato diet.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/superspud-me/', 'snippet': 'Chris Voigt was angry. So angry that he went on a 60-day all-potato diet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Voigt was angry. So angry that he went on a 60-day all-potato diet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Superspud Me', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lunch and Breakfast Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Potatoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:51:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a72d407-5123-5942-8679-d3fd8c61b781', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a72d407-5123-5942-8679-d3fd8c61b781'}, {'abstract': 'Our restaurant-owning blogger responds to his critics.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/taking-the-bait-on-prix-fixe-sustainable-seafood-and-restaurant-economics/', 'snippet': 'Our restaurant-owning blogger responds to his critics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here are some comments from all over the country and all over the culinary map. I am grateful for the responses to the posts about prix fixe policy, the talkative owner and the restaurant itself. It may be impossible to please all the people all the time, but all the people who take the time to write please me all the time. Thank you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking the Bait: On Prix Fixe, Sustainable Seafood and Restaurant Economics', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hamptons (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:53:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Buschel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buschel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/959b41a6-a330-5a84-8aa4-082fc3532938', 'word_count': 1372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/959b41a6-a330-5a84-8aa4-082fc3532938'}, {'abstract': 'Some cable companies have modified their policies on lost or damaged cable modems after the tornadoes in Alabama.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/dont-forget-the-cable-box-in-a-disaster/', 'snippet': 'Some cable companies have modified their policies on lost or damaged cable modems after the tornadoes in Alabama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many readers made thoughtful comments in response to my post earlier this week, asking what they would take if they had to leave their home quickly in a disaster. Children, pets and sentimental keepsakes topped their lists, along with wallets and credit cards. No one talked about grabbing the cable television box. But apparently, that may be a good idea.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Forget the Cable Box in a Disaster', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T16:56:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fd5103e-8b85-5751-b093-3605a1ae4974', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fd5103e-8b85-5751-b093-3605a1ae4974'}, {'abstract': 'When Kim Clijsters got the yips late in the second set against Aranxa Rus and began backing off of her forcing groundstrokes, Rus surged in to take control.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/analysis-why-clijsters-lost/', 'snippet': 'When Kim Clijsters got the yips late in the second set against Aranxa Rus and began backing off of her forcing groundstrokes, Rus surged in to take control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nature abhors a vacuum, and tennis, like physics, obeys the laws of the universe. So when Kim Clijsters got the yips late in the second set against Arantxa Rus and began backing off of her forcing groundstrokes, Rus surged in to take control. It was barely discernible for a minute or two, but when Rus saved the second match point against her with a beautiful lefty forehand cross-court winner, the energy and dominance that Clijsters showed took leave of her and the momentum shifted. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Analysis: Why Clijsters Lost', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca188427-a5d1-5a95-8cac-5a651e8447af', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca188427-a5d1-5a95-8cac-5a651e8447af'}, {'abstract': 'A young New York-based designer discusses his unisex private label line.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/stripes-haans-nicholas-mott/', 'snippet': 'A young New York-based designer discusses his unisex private label line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Haans Nicholas Mott’s traipse into designing clothes was kind of an accident. “Someone who just liked my style one day unexpectedly asked me if I wanted to work on the line with him,” says Mott. Perhaps because of his background in architecture, the New York-based designer, who is 33, is very particular about the construction of his unisex private label line. The clothes have been featured alongside Rick Owens, Ann Demeulemeester and Yohji Yamamoto in editorials for “SOME/THINGS” magazine. His collection includes hand-painted striped tops (five to six hours of painting) that are frayed in all the right places (36 hours to machine-sew and hand finish) and have the perfect dose of weight to shield against a cool spring breeze. The fit is boyish and over sized but the imperfection of the lines add a touch of whimsy. The collection also has unconventionally shaped short jackets (another three to five days of sewing) and rich-hued duffel coats with detachable hoods (hand sewn for eight to nine days). “Architecture gives you a set of problem-solving skills,” he says. “Once I started sewing, I got very into cutting patterns, developing stitches and constructing things.” Each collection has only about a dozen pieces and is only available through special order. “When things become large, you can’t control it,” says Mott. That firm control of his process, he says, is the most exciting part of clothing making. “If the process become boring, I’ll quit.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stripes | Haans Nicholas Mott', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Madison Shimoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Madison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shimoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fda0187-afa2-5214-acb4-45a9b43fb95e', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fda0187-afa2-5214-acb4-45a9b43fb95e'}, {'abstract': 'When the Lakers decided to replace the greatest coach of the modern era with one of the most widely maligned coaches of a contending team in recent memory, plenty of eyebrows were raised.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/has-mike-brown-been-vindicated/', 'snippet': 'When the Lakers decided to replace the greatest coach of the modern era with one of the most widely maligned coaches of a contending team in recent memory, plenty of eyebrows were raised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Lakers decided to replace the greatest coach of the modern era with one of the most widely maligned coaches of a contending team in recent memory, plenty of eyebrows were raised.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Has Mike Brown Been Vindicated?', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Krolik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krolik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24be5b81-8be8-58fa-8d20-fa4dc35bceb0', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24be5b81-8be8-58fa-8d20-fa4dc35bceb0'}, {'abstract': 'For the second consecutive year, the UEFA Champions League final will be carried over-the-air on the Fox network in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/fox-mothership-hovers-over-champions-league-final/', 'snippet': 'For the second consecutive year, the UEFA Champions League final will be carried over-the-air on the Fox network in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may not have the impact or appeal in the United States of the Super Bowl or the World Series, but Saturday’s UEFA Champions League final between Barcelona and Manchester United has the undivided attention on a holiday weekend of the Fox network.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fox Mothership Hovers over Champions League Final', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'UEFA Champions League (Soccer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nathanson, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FC Barcelona', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manchester United', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UEFA Champions League', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e56ad6ae-4cb6-589b-bab1-fe0b745d27ad', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e56ad6ae-4cb6-589b-bab1-fe0b745d27ad'}, {'abstract': 'Doctors are as terrified of death as any other human being, and like any other human, we use euphemism to shield us from that fear.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/doctors-and-the-d-word/', 'snippet': 'Doctors are as terrified of death as any other human being, and like any other human, we use euphemism to shield us from that fear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a first-year medical student, I earned a few extra dollars by working the 4 p.m.-to-midnight shift in our hospital’s nursing office, where I scheduled private-duty nurses. One evening, about 9 p.m., a nurse called to inform me that her patient had “expired” and that she would need to be assigned to a different patient for the next day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctors and the ‘D’ Word', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:02:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Ofri, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ofri', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7498a5e4-9da4-5779-9b43-732b30bb4ca7', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7498a5e4-9da4-5779-9b43-732b30bb4ca7'}, {'abstract': 'A 5-to-3 ruling appeared to endorse vigorous state efforts to punish employers who intentionally hire illegal workers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/us/27scotus.html', 'snippet': 'A 5-to-3 ruling appeared to endorse vigorous state efforts to punish employers who intentionally hire illegal workers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Thursday upheld an Arizona law that imposes harsh penalties on businesses that hire illegal immigrants.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Illegal Workers: Court Upholds Faulting Hirers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justices Uphold State Law Penalizing Hirers of Illegal Aliens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:04:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20e93339-7161-5935-9677-20f25a03c267', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20e93339-7161-5935-9677-20f25a03c267'}, {'abstract': 'The Dallas Mavericks, who say they are not done, will enter the N.B.A. finals as America’s darlings, especially if their opponent is the Miami Heat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/sports/mavericks-want-more-than-a-conference-crown.html', 'snippet': 'The Dallas Mavericks, who say they are not done, will enter the N.B.A. finals as America’s darlings, especially if their opponent is the Miami Heat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Mavericks felt as if they had burst through some huge barrier by reaching the N.B.A. finals again, you could not tell by their reaction Wednesday night. When they polished off their Western Conference finals series against the Thunder with another admirably gritty finish, the Mavericks celebrated with only marginally more elation than if they had scored tickets to a taping of “Glee.” Yes, they were making the point that they have been this far before, but everyone knows how long and painful the five-year gap has been. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mavs Have Title Faces On', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mavericks Have Title Faces On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tressel, Jim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Posey, Buster', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Lightning', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ohio State University', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T17:05:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1553a68-a6fa-5525-956d-145d933d981b', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1553a68-a6fa-5525-956d-145d933d981b'}, {'abstract': 'American moviegoers will soon be able to see the hit Hong Kong film “3D Sex and Zen: Extreme Ecstasy,” while dropping their kids off for a 3-D re-release of Disney’s “Lion King.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/the-circle-of-life-3-d-movies-rated-g-through-xxx/', 'snippet': 'American moviegoers will soon be able to see the hit Hong Kong film “3D Sex and Zen: Extreme Ecstasy,” while dropping their kids off for a 3-D re-release of Disney’s “Lion King.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’ll never think of the phrase “box office gross” in the same way again: a pornographic 3-D movie that has already proved a hit in Asia is headed for a theatrical release in the United States. The movie, an erotic Hong Kong period drama called “3D Sex and Zen: Extreme Ecstasy” and directed by Christopher Sun, is a remake of a 1991 film called “Sex and Zen,” which in turn is adapted from a 17th-century work of Chinese erotic fiction known as “The Carnal Prayer Mat.” (Among its characters are a lascivious scholar, his virginal bride, a duke with an estimable harem and a horse.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [],