ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Golden State Warriors', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:26:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b33e5650-500c-5325-afb6-999f8bd9d97f', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b33e5650-500c-5325-afb6-999f8bd9d97f'}, {'abstract': 'The march by tens of thousands of Iraqis loyal to the anti-American Shiite cleric Moktada al-Sadr was to warn the government against asking the United States military to stay beyond Dec. 31.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/world/middleeast/27iraq.html', 'snippet': 'The march by tens of thousands of Iraqis loyal to the anti-American Shiite cleric Moktada al-Sadr was to warn the government against asking the United States military to stay beyond Dec. 31.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — Marching in lockstep as they shouted “No, no, America,” tens of thousands of Iraqis loyal to the anti-American Shiite cleric Moktada al-Sadr poured into the streets on Thursday to warn Iraq’s government against extending the United States military presence here.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Insurgent Shot to Death in Baghdad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tens of Thousands of Sadrists in Anti-American March', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussein, Saddam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sadr, Moktada Al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Status of Forces Agreements', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mahdi Army', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baath Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:28:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy and Khalid D. Ali', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Khalid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f70ebd34-ec14-5f74-81f5-e2126e2875ed', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f70ebd34-ec14-5f74-81f5-e2126e2875ed'}, {'abstract': 'Bing Gordon says investor interest in Internet giants like Facebook and LinkedIn still leaves room for smaller companies.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/a-conversation-with-bing-gordon/', 'snippet': 'Bing Gordon says investor interest in Internet giants like Facebook and LinkedIn still leaves room for smaller companies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bing Gordon has years of experience bridging the world of technology start-ups and venture capital. In addition to having been an executive at Electronic Arts, he is a board member of Amazon and Zynga, among other firms. Now, he is a partner with the venture capital firm Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With Bing Gordon', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:29:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli and Mac William Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25892d3b-fc3f-5950-bc0c-5014f7516719', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25892d3b-fc3f-5950-bc0c-5014f7516719'}, {'abstract': 'An adult daunted by the deep end takes 10 sessions of private swimming lessons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/nyregion/adult-swimming-lessons-first-strokes-to-becoming-aquaman.html', 'snippet': 'An adult daunted by the deep end takes 10 sessions of private swimming lessons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SWIMMING and I have never had much need for each other: I grew up in Manhattan with a fire-hydrant sprinkler as my respite from the heat. Out of my parents, four older sisters and myself, only Dad can swim. It was not a skill we put much thought into acquiring; we were a poor immigrant family that spent little time around large bodies of water. There was a trip to Lake George in 1980, however, when the family was lounging by a pool. My legs were dangling in the cool water when my brother-in-law shoved me in. (He became my ex-brother-in-law years later, but my eternal trauma was probably not a factor.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Swim-span/Swim1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Swim-span/Swim1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Swim-span/Swim1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Swim-span/Swim1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Swim-span/Swim1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'First Strokes to Becoming Aquaman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'First Strokes To Becoming Aquaman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:29:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6aab567-4dbf-5d67-87f7-a4bc921d25a6', 'word_count': 1334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6aab567-4dbf-5d67-87f7-a4bc921d25a6'}, {'abstract': 'Land of fried-clam-cold-beer-live-music-and-raw-oyster gloriousness, Claudio’s Clam Bar is nestled on a wharf that juts into Peconic Bay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/nyregion/at-claudios-clam-bar-fresh-clams-and-salty-air.html', 'snippet': 'Land of fried-clam-cold-beer-live-music-and-raw-oyster gloriousness, Claudio’s Clam Bar is nestled on a wharf that juts into Peconic Bay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAMPTONS-BOUND traffic in the summer is a sweaty, hectic, BMW-infested mess. But that’s not your problem. As you soar down the Long Island Expressway, past the long line of frustrated New Yorkers waiting to disembark at Exit 70, grin and wave, because you’re not going to the Hamptons, pal. You’re headed for the North Fork — and straight to Claudio’s Clam Bar.\xa0', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29JOINT1/29JOINT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29JOINT1/29JOINT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29JOINT1/29JOINT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29JOINT1/29JOINT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29JOINT1/29JOINT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'And Now, Fresh Clams and Salty Air', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And Now, Fresh Clams And Salty Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clams', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'GREENPORT (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4b1d85c-dd26-5fa5-9053-3bbe85366c7b', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4b1d85c-dd26-5fa5-9053-3bbe85366c7b'}, {'abstract': 'Governors Island, which reopens to the public on Friday, is only 800 yards from the Battery and accessible by free ferries from Lower Manhattan and Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/travel/mark-di-suvero-sculptures-on-governors-island.html', 'snippet': 'Governors Island, which reopens to the public on Friday, is only 800 yards from the Battery and accessible by free ferries from Lower Manhattan and Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT is not Manhattan, this New York City island that features a miniature golf course designed by artists, a beer garden, trapeze performances, sunbathing on a sandy beach, snoozing on bright red Adirondack chairs, a fort restored just in time for its bicentennial this year, polo, big-name concerts, an organic farm and the largest outdoor presentation of Mark di Suvero’s monumental sculptures in the city in four decades. This is Governors Island, and it’s only 800 yards from the Battery.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Governors-span/Governors-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Governors-span/Governors-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Governors-span/Governors-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Governors-span/Governors-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Governors-span/Governors-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Island of Culture in the Harbor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Island of Culture in the Harbor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Governors Island (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:32:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce039c34-588b-52ae-84b0-2db33a7df3f1', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce039c34-588b-52ae-84b0-2db33a7df3f1'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama has shifted from protecting civilians to regime change, but NATO faces tensions over how long it will take.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/world/africa/27policy.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama has shifted from protecting civilians to regime change, but NATO faces tensions over how long it will take.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama has subtly shifted Washington’s public explanation of its goals in Libya, declaring now that he wants to assure the Libyan people are “finally free of 40 years of tyranny” at the hands of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, after first stating he wanted to protect civilians from massacres. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/world/27policy/27policy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/world/27policy/27policy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/world/27policy/27policy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Goal Shifts in Libya, Time Constrains NATO', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Goal Shifts in Libya, Time Constrains NATO', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:34:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Schmitt and David E. Sanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/780df03d-b361-53e5-b9e8-d3589f01adaf', 'word_count': 1144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/780df03d-b361-53e5-b9e8-d3589f01adaf'}, {'abstract': 'The American Guild of Musical Artists tries to block the company’s planned move from Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/new-york-city-opera-union-files-labor-complaint/', 'snippet': 'The American Guild of Musical Artists tries to block the company’s planned move from Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': ' One of New York City Opera’s key unions on Thursday charged the company with unfair labor practices and sought a court order blocking its proposed move out of Lincoln Center. The salvo from the American Guild of Musical Artists comes nearly a week after City Opera said it could no longer afford to stay at the arts complex. Opera officials have so far remained silent on what they will present next season or where. The Guild, which represents singers, chorus members, dancers, stage managers and directors, said City Opera was using the move to break its contractual obligations and had refused to negotiate or provide any information about it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City Opera Union Files Labor Complaint', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN GUILD OF MUSICAL ARTISTS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:34:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa335451-c525-5875-a536-96f9a9515d80', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa335451-c525-5875-a536-96f9a9515d80'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain, and Egypt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/05/26/blogs/20110526POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain, and Egypt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain, and Egypt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/26/blogs/20110526POD-slide-WZ8O/20110526POD-slide-WZ8O-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Libya and Elsewhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:36:26+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/92447a1d-4069-581f-ac77-66c3f19eab45', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/92447a1d-4069-581f-ac77-66c3f19eab45'}, {'abstract': 'The recent opening of Al Abayan’s Patron of the New may finally turn TriBeCa into a fashion destination.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/the-new-new-thing/', 'snippet': 'The recent opening of Al Abayan’s Patron of the New may finally turn TriBeCa into a fashion destination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though Gary Graham, Thom Browne and Steven Alan have all set up shop in TriBeCa, the neighborhood has never really gelled as a fashion destination. But the recent opening of Al Abayan’s Patron of the New may just change that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New (New) Thing', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:40:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27354635-efeb-51fa-ba45-b1bb0c1956d6', 'word_count': 1005, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27354635-efeb-51fa-ba45-b1bb0c1956d6'}, {'abstract': '“He cut the throat of the goat with a knife,” a chef and adviser says of the billionaire founder of Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/mark-zuckerberg-says-hes-only-eating-meat-he-kills/', 'snippet': '“He cut the throat of the goat with a knife,” a chef and adviser says of the billionaire founder of Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Zuckerberg has certainly been called cutthroat before, but nobody meant it literally. Yet Mr. Zuckerberg, the founder of Facebook, has told Fortune that he is only eating meat he kills himself. This news apparently came to light when he told his Facebook friends on May 4, “I just killed a pig and a goat.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark Zuckerberg Says He’s Only Eating Meat He Kills', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:41:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae5beac0-64ef-588b-8cdc-f965880b4663', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae5beac0-64ef-588b-8cdc-f965880b4663'}, {'abstract': 'Which songs would make the least appealing aspects of city life bearable, even exhilarating?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/hey-you-what-song-should-we-be-listening-to/', 'snippet': 'Which songs would make the least appealing aspects of city life bearable, even exhilarating?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Walk around Times Square, and it can sometimes seem oppressively dirty, crowded, loud, saturated in exhaust fumes. But walk around listening to the Bee Gees’ “More Than a Woman” on your headphones, and it’s a whole different story. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wanted: City Soundtrack Suggestions', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bee Gees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Times Square and 42nd Street (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:41:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14546a00-61e9-5c83-b817-24402022b704', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14546a00-61e9-5c83-b817-24402022b704'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain and Egypt.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/pictures-of-the-day-libya-and-elsewhere-25/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain and Egypt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Libya, Iraq, Spain and Egypt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Libya and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:47:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7eafea3e-7f40-5f42-bc2d-ae67395f3537', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7eafea3e-7f40-5f42-bc2d-ae67395f3537'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to events for, by and about children in the New York area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/arts/spare-times-for-children-for-may-27-june-2.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to events for, by and about children in the New York area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREAT ADAPTATIONS', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spare Times: For Children, for May 27-June 2', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spare Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puppets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:54:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ce49ede-f87b-58ed-aa82-c84c97da56fd', 'word_count': 2716, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ce49ede-f87b-58ed-aa82-c84c97da56fd'}, {'abstract': '“Zoolander,” one of the last all-out, joke-driven comedies released before the rise and stifling reign of the jokeless comedy, was a victim, in part, of terrible timing: Its release came just a few weeks after 9/11.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/the-last-great-joke-stuffed-comedy-in-america/', 'snippet': '“Zoolander,” one of the last all-out, joke-driven comedies released before the rise and stifling reign of the jokeless comedy, was a victim, in part, of terrible timing: Its release came just a few weeks after 9/11.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you see a review of a madcap comedy that ends with the words “terrorist strikes in the heart of America,” you know you probably have a problem —\xa0and that’s exactly how Elvis Mitchell’s review of “Zoolander,” which ran in The Times on Sept. 28, 2001, concluded. Mitchell fretted that elements of the film might be “a reminder of the atmosphere in this country after the terrorist strikes into the heart of America.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Last Great Joke-Stuffed Comedy in America', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiller, Ben', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T20:54:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a26d72ae-1ff8-5014-937e-988a9f80a0e6', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a26d72ae-1ff8-5014-937e-988a9f80a0e6'}, {'abstract': 'The dispute centers on the so-called Schneerson Library sought by a Hasidic group based in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/legal-wrangle-may-keep-38-works-from-russia-out-of-los-angeles-show/', 'snippet': 'The dispute centers on the so-called Schneerson Library sought by a Hasidic group based in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Los Angeles County Museum of Art is in danger of becoming the latest victim of an international diplomatic battle that has led Russian museums to hold off making loans of state-owned art to the United States, after a federal judge in the District of Columbia ordered Russia to turn over a trove of religious books and manuscripts to members of Chabad-Lubavitch, the Hasidic group based in Brooklyn.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Legal Wrangle May Keep 38 Works From Russia Out of Los Angeles Show', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hasidism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chabad-Lubavitch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Getty, J Paul, Museum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T21:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b5d2b21-78fd-549e-b8e1-b6ec7a90d207', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b5d2b21-78fd-549e-b8e1-b6ec7a90d207'}, {'abstract': 'Excerpts from an interview with Caroline Wozniacki.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/q-and-a-with-caroline-wozniacki/', 'snippet': 'Excerpts from an interview with Caroline Wozniacki.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Caroline Wozniacki, the 20-year-old Danish player ranked No. 1 in the world, is in the third round of the French Open and still looking for her first Grand Slam singles title. The daughter of Polish immigrants to Denmark, she is now based in Monaco. She spoke with Christopher Clarey of The International Herald Tribune and The New York Times last week before the French Open. The first of two excerpts from that interview:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With Caroline Wozniacki', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T21:00:45+000