nal': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4e601a8-4167-52d0-b6e4-6fdb25f2387b', 'word_count': 4897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4e601a8-4167-52d0-b6e4-6fdb25f2387b'}, {'abstract': 'In “United Red Army” Koji Wakamatsu charts the rise and self-destruction of a Japanese extremist group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/movies/united-red-army-directed-by-koji-wakamatsu-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “United Red Army” Koji Wakamatsu charts the rise and self-destruction of a Japanese extremist group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a darkly comic moment in Olivier Assayas’s film “Carlos,” about Carlos the Jackal, involving three Japanese left-wing extremists. The militants, members of the Japanese Red Army, have decided to attack the French embassy at The Hague in an attempt to liberate a comrade imprisoned elsewhere. On their way to the embassy, though, they get lost (you see them studying a map), an absurd turn of events that suggests there’s something fundamentally pathetic about them. That this is a terrorist gang that can’t shoot (steer) straight, literally doesn’t know where it’s going, finds further fuel in another movie, “United Red Army.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27UNITED-span/SUB-UNITED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27UNITED-span/SUB-UNITED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27UNITED-span/SUB-UNITED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capt