the privilege of parking where they live.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOTORISTS might be forgiven for thinking that New York has been snubbing them. Times Square has been covered with tables and chairs. Broad strips of streets have been painted green and handed over to cyclists. Builders continue to snap up parking lots for development. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/realestate/Cover-Posting/Cover-Posting-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/realestate/Cover-Posting/Cover-Posting-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/realestate/Cover-Posting/Cover-Posting-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/realestate/Cover-Posting/Cover-Posting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/realestate/Cover-Posting/Cover-Posting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Higher-Priced Developments Providing Parking', 'kicker': 'Posting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Locus For Quirky Parking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking Garages and Lots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e39f0757-c3c7-5bee-b3cf-778ec4d3f896', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e39f0757-c3c7-5bee-b3cf-778ec4d3f896'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Zuckerberg has certainly been called cutthroat before, but nobody meant it literally.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/26/zuckerberg-says-hes-only-eating-meat-he-kills/', 'snippet': 'Mark Zuckerberg has certainly been called cutthroat before, but nobody meant it literally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Zuckerberg has certainly been called cutthroat before, but nobody meant it literally. Yet Mr. Zuckerberg, the founder of Facebook, has told Fortune that he is only eating meat he kills himself. This news apparently came to light when he told his Facebook friends on May 4, “I just killed a pig and a goat.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zuckerberg Says He’s Only Eating Meat He Kills', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:04:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f04e993f-8e99-5915-8468-6218dd912e20', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f04e993f-8e99-5915-8468-6218dd912e20'}, {'abstract': 'New York Times editors and reporters discuss how Democrats in Washington celebrated a surprising victory in a heavily Republican congressional district in Upstate New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/05/26/us/politics/26caucus_pod.html', 'snippet': 'New York Times editors and reporters discuss how Democrats in Washington celebrated a surprising victory in a heavily Republican congressional district in Upstate New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York Times editors and reporters discuss how Democrats in Washington celebrated a surprising victory in a heavily Republican congressional district in Upstate New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ecstatic Democrats and Shifting Winds In the Medicare Debate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/78719688-2af7-576c-94ec-53173280daad', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/78719688-2af7-576c-94ec-53173280daad'}, {'abstract': 'The beach book this summer is likely to have new names and new twists, even when it comes to Scandinavian mysteries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/books/summers-beach-books-get-a-makeover.html', 'snippet': 'The beach book this summer is likely to have new names and new twists, even when it comes to Scandinavian mysteries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The beach book has undergone a makeover for 2011. As the season’s traditional big names and story lines run out of gas, new variations on old formulas have emerged. Want a story of power, greed and conspicuous consumption? Forget Hollywood; think hedge fund. Want a killer mystery? Forget that corpse in the opening chapter; think about the heroine who wakes up with amnesia and can’t trust anyone around her. Want a topical family drama about teenage lovers? Think “Romeo and Juliet” with sexting thrown in.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Beach-1/27Beach-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Beach-1/27Beach-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Beach-1/Beach-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Beach-1/Beach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27Beach-1/Beach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Books to Bury Yourself In', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Books to Bury Yourself In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:07:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/522a23bc-61ec-5ccf-a232-b7098025882d', 'word_count': 1968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/522a23bc-61ec-5ccf-a232-b7098025882d'}, {'abstract': 'The reunited Cars played Roseland on Wednesday, and the big difference between their oldies and songs from their new album was familiarity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/arts/music/the-cars-reunited-play-roseland-review.html', 'snippet': 'The reunited Cars played Roseland on Wednesday, and the big difference between their oldies and songs from their new album was familiarity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reunited bands face a choice. Plan A is to ignore the missing years and pick up where they left off; Plan B, to acknowledge the gap and try to update the sound. The Cars lean toward Plan A. When the band played Roseland on Wednesday night, the big difference between their oldies and songs from their new album, “Move Like This” (Hear Music), was familiarity. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27CARS-span/CARS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27CARS-span/CARS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27CARS-span/CARS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27CARS-span/27CARS-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27CARS-span/27CARS-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back to New Wave, Older and Somewhat Wiser', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back to New Wave, Older and Somewhat Wiser', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ocasek, Ric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:08:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d25b5a1-5518-5347-a4e8-dd6da305ed4b', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d25b5a1-5518-5347-a4e8-dd6da305ed4b'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibition “Illuminating Fashion,” at the Morgan Library & Museum, accompanies a definitive guide to medieval fashion. Both should prove useful for scholars and designers alike.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/arts/design/illuminating-fashion-at-morgan-library-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibition “Illuminating Fashion,” at the Morgan Library & Museum, accompanies a definitive guide to medieval fashion. Both should prove useful for scholars and designers alike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can you judge a hunter by his houpeland, or a prince by his pouleines? You certainly can in “Illuminating Fashion: Dress in the Art of Medieval France and the Netherlands” at the Morgan Library & Museum. This lively show will teach you to scrutinize centuries-old manuscripts as you would a style magazine. (For the uninitiated: a houpeland is a high-waisted, drapey gown; pouleines are shoes with long, pointy toes.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27FASHION-1/FASHION-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27FASHION-1/FASHION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/27FASHION-1/FASHION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Medieval Style Files: Tailored Artistry', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Medieval Style Files: Tailored Artistry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Library & Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:09:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/923742b0-ce6d-50de-9923-5184b28c5ff0', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/923742b0-ce6d-50de-9923-5184b28c5ff0'}, {'abstract': 'Confronted with — and handling — rude questions about race.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/fashion/questions-about-social-situations.html', 'snippet': 'Confronted with — and handling — rude questions about race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am an Asian-American woman living in a mostly white town. Every day, I am asked bluntly: “What are you?” I get it from colleagues at work and people who have known me for less than five minutes. I find it so intrusive that sometimes I respond rudely. But even then, people will say, “You sure don’t look it.” First, how should I respond to this question? And can you tell me why it upsets me so much?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Race Got to Do With It?', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s Race Got to Do With It?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-26T22:10:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/a24b0640-961e-5030-baa3-c9f7a09ccc9e', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a24b0640-961e-5030-baa3-c9f7a09ccc9e'}, {'abstract': 'The Op-Ed columnist Roger C