l events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Delaney, an Irish businessman who founded Intrade, an online prediction market that allows customers to bet on world political, entertainment and financial events, died on Saturday after coming within 50 yards of the summit of Mount Everest. He was 42.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/business/DELANEY-obit/DELANEY-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/business/DELANEY-obit/DELANEY-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John Delaney, Founder of Intrade, Dies at 42', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John Delaney, 42, Founder of Intrade', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountain Climbing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intrade', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T02:22:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Creswell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creswell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9d81b4a-15b9-5514-9c6e-7b68c0adc819', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9d81b4a-15b9-5514-9c6e-7b68c0adc819'}, {'abstract': 'A whistle-blower case charges that an education company encouraged aggressive recruitment of unqualified students for their federal student aid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/education/27edmc.html', 'snippet': 'A whistle-blower case charges that an education company encouraged aggressive recruitment of unqualified students for their federal student aid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2004, when Todd S. Nelson was chief executive of the University of Phoenix, the nation’s largest for-profit college, he signed a $9.8 million settlement with the Department of Education, which found that Phoenix had “systematically and intentionally” broken the federal rules against paying recruiters for students.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions Follow Leader of For-Profit Colleges', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Questions Follow Leader Of For-Profit Colleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Management Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Phoenix', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T02:24:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d57f1f3-fd6f-5965-81dd-d7ad14d72dee', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d57f1f3-fd6f-5965-81dd-d7ad14d72dee'}, {'abstract': 'Daren Palmer was the picture of success in his Idaho Falls hometown, but he faces prison for a $78 million Ponzi scheme.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/us/27ponzi.html', 'snippet': 'Daren Palmer was the picture of success in his Idaho Falls hometown, but he faces prison for a $78 million Ponzi scheme.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IDAHO FALLS — Never mind that he was a multimill