cast song of the week from Group Doueh. Ben Ratliff is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/popcast-idol-post-mortem/', 'snippet': 'Jon Caramanica on “American idol”; Nate Chinen on My Morning Jacket; and a Popcast song of the week from Group Doueh. Ben Ratliff is the host.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Did Scotty McCreery’s bulging eyes win him the “American Idol” title?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: ‘Idol’ Post-Mortem', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:22:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8e0729f-bdbe-5904-b76a-c0bfc0438960', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8e0729f-bdbe-5904-b76a-c0bfc0438960'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/morning-take-out-253/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former Nasdaq Executive Pleads Guilty to Insider Trading \xa0|\xa0 For years, Donald L. Johnson was a gatekeeper at the Nasdaq OMX Group, whose role at the exchange’s market intelligence unit helped him counsel companies on stock-moving strategy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:24:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9ae3625-598b-5a8a-ae90-f031c7c3d57d', 'word_count': 1787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9ae3625-598b-5a8a-ae90-f031c7c3d57d'}, {'abstract': 'David McCullough explores the intellectual legacy that France settled on its 19th-century visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-the-greater-journey-americans-in-paris-by-david-mccullough.html', 'snippet': 'David McCullough explores the intellectual legacy that France settled on its 19th-century visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David McCullough has stressed France’s pre-eminent role in American history for years. We would not, he has argued, have a country without the French, who have permanently and profoundly shaped us. If anyone could get away with suggesting that room be made on Mount Rushmore for Astérix it is McCullough. He seems to have had something else in mind, however. With “The Greater Journey: Americans in Paris,” he explores the intellectual legacy that France settled on its 19th-century visitors. The result is an epic of ideas, as well as an exhilarating book of spells.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Schiff-1305740070045/Schiff-1305740070045-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 761, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Schiff-1305740070045/Schiff-1305740070045-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Schiff-1305740070045/Schiff-1305740070045-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Paris Created America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The French Connection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCullough, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:31:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacy Schiff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61750f4c-04d0-50b4-89cb-5d7af0b3e8ce', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61750f4c-04d0-50b4-89cb-5d7af0b3e8ce'}, {'abstract': 'Against all odds and iTunes, despite the prolonged death agony of brick-and-mortar record stores and of records themselves, not to mention the endless breakneck gentrification of the East Village and Lower East Side, the Downtown Music Gallery has survived.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/downtown-and-underground-an-outpost-of-avant-garde-music/', 'snippet': 'Against all odds and iTunes, despite the prolonged death agony of brick-and-mortar record stores and of records themselves, not to mention the endless breakneck gentrification of the East Village and Lower East Side, the Downtown Music Gallery has...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beneath the incense burners and chanting celebrating the 2,555th anniversary of Buddha’s birth at a Chinese temple upstairs, an unearthly din rose from the basement of 13 Monroe Street in Chinatown the other Sunday to observe a more recent anniversary. The extended 20th birthday party of the Downtown Music Gallery, purveyors of progressive music of all stripes since 1991, was in full, lurching swing, and more than a dozen brave souls huddled around a makeshift stage for another weekly ritual, a free performance series that gives literal meaning to the term underground music. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Downtown and Underground, an Outpost of Noise', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Downtown Music Gallery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:46:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aidan Levy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aidan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Levy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab722949-ff07-527f-9993-fe787ecce7ae', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab722949-ff07-527f-9993-fe787ecce7ae'}, {'abstract': 'The education minister that the country would lower radiation exposure limits for schoolchildren in areas around a stricken nuclear plant and pay for schools to remove contaminated topsoil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/world/asia/28japan.html', 'snippet': 'The education minister that the country would lower radiation exposure limits for schoolchildren in areas around a stricken nuclear plant and pay for schools to remove contaminated topsoil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Responding to fury among parents in Fukushima, Japan’s education minister said Friday that the country would set a lower radiation exposure limit for schoolchildren in areas around a stricken nuclear plant and pay for schools to remove contaminated topsoil from fields and playgrounds.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Tries to Ease Fury of Parents Near Plant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Tries To Ease Fury Of Parents Near Plant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tokyo Electric Power Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:49:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00d720a5-5f73-50e5-b472-2cc3095cd4ce', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00d720a5-5f73-50e5-b472-2cc3095cd4ce'}, {'abstract': 'Play our crossword to see what you know about this topic, or find related resources.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/student-crossword-the-industrial-revolution/', 'snippet': 'Play our crossword to see what you know about this topic, or find related resources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Play our crossword (PDF) to see what you know about the Industrial Revolution. You can find the answers here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Crossword | The Industrial Revolution', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:50:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Longo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50f2ce39-57e3-5286-a630-a0fde8e9df2e', 'word_count': 54, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50f2ce39-57e3-5286-a630-a0fde8e9df2e'}, {'abstract': 'Study casts doubt on benefit of raising good cholesterol levels, fish is frequently mislabeled, Google’s new app for paying with your phone and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/friday-reading-raising-good-cholesterol-may-not-help-fight-heart-attacks/', 'snippet': 'Study casts doubt on benefit of raising good cholesterol levels, fish is frequently mislabeled, Google’s new app for paying with your phone and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Raising Good Cholesterol May Not Help Fight Heart Attacks', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:52:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/502cd56b-0c4b-541b-b4db-0c45fc27a5d2', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/502cd56b-0c4b-541b-b4db-0c45fc27a5d2'}, {'abstract': 'Tattered documents, dating back centuries, endure in a synagogue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-sacred-trash-the-lost-and-found-world-of-the-cairo-geniza-by-adina-hoffman-and-peter-cole.html', 'snippet': 'Tattered documents, dating back centuries, endure in a synagogue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 120 years ago, a cache of manuscripts, mostly fragments, was discovered in the storeroom of an old Cairo synagogue. Its members had deposited them there over many centuries. This collection of documents managed to be both heterogeneous and comprehensive at the same time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Julius/Julius-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Julius/Julius-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Julius/Julius-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Secret Life of Cairo’s Jews', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Pileup of Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoffman, Adina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:55:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Julius', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Julius', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cfcd4e9-8911-5177-b3bf-d57a57043168', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cfcd4e9-8911-5177-b3bf-d57a57043168'}, {'abstract': 'The narrator of Jim Krusoe’s novel tries to find a way for the living to get through to the dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-toward-you-by-jim-krusoe.html', 'snippet': 'The narrator of Jim Krusoe’s novel tries to find a way for the living to get through to the dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bob, the first-name-only protagonist of “Toward You,” Jim Krusoe’s unsettling fourth novel, is a man much obsessed with death. A reupholsterer of antiques by trade and a congenital loser by nature, Bob spends his free hours in the (amazingly named) fictional town of St. Nils tinkering with a device he calls the Communicator, whose purpose is to breach the wall separating the living and the dead. Bob’s vanity and his avarice — both seemingly small-time — drive him to this work, and faint dreams of wealth, fame and sexual satisfaction pervade his quotidian existence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Munson/Munson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Munson/Munson-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Munson/Munson-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Surreal America, with Rabid Dog', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beware of Dog', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krusoe, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:56:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Munson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Munson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6734c6b0-93e4-5d07-bf74-9f44cbc91c0e', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6734c6b0-93e4-5d07-bf74-9f44cbc91c0e'}, {'abstract': 'Tessa Hadley’s novel is divided between two characters who once intersected for an affair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-the-london-train-by-tessa-hadley.html', 'snippet': 'Tessa Hadley’s novel is divided between two characters who once intersected for an affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The people in Tessa Hadley’s latest novel, “The London Train,” seem to be managing the trials of adulthood reasonably well, right up until the moment their lives jump the tracks. Their problems are the familiar and expected ones — grief at the death of a parent, career disappointments, marital friction — although that makes them no less difficult or wrenching. No one is more surprised than Hadley’s characters when they act on irrational impulse. She sets out to make these blinkered lives plausible, and then to subvert our natural sympathy into something more resembling judgment.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Thompson/Thompson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 570, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Thompson/Thompson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Thompson/Thompson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adultery in the U. K.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rail Car Named Desire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadley, Tessa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b01554b1-ca25-5d7b-92cc-38ae161be3af', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b01554b1-ca25-5d7b-92cc-38ae161be3af'}, {'abstract': 'A new translation brings a revered body of Indian verse into sharper relief.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-songs-of-kabir-by-translated-by-arvind-krishna-mehrotra.html', 'snippet': 'A new translation brings a revered body of Indian verse into sharper relief.', 'lead_paragraph': "Arvind Krishna Mehrotra's new translation of Kabir brings the poetry of the great 15th-century Indian poet and holy man to life in English for the first time. Not that others haven’t tried: Pound, Robert Bly and, most notably, Rabindranath Tagore in 1915, with a version consisting of thees, thous and thines, delivered in a sandalwood-scented prayer-book-ese that would not have been out of place atop a teak sidetable at one of Mme. Blavatsky’s legendary seances. But it is Mehrotra who has succeeded in capturing the ferocity and improvisational energy of Kabir’s poetry.", 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/KLEINZAHLER/KLEINZAHLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/KLEINZAHLER/KLEINZAHLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/KLEINZAHLER/KLEINZAHLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rebirth of a Poet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rebirth of a Poet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:56:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By August Kleinzahler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'August', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kleinzahler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6965456c-4ae9-5603-972e-065a9e1f562c', 'word_count': 1158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6965456c-4ae9-5603-972e-065a9e1f562c'}, {'abstract': 'In Roddy Doyle’s stories, characters struggle with the funk brought on by middle age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-bullfighting-by-roddy-doyle.html', 'snippet': 'In Roddy Doyle’s stories, characters struggle with the funk brought on by middle age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four Irishmen walk into a pub and start talking international affairs. “Poor oul’ Benazir.” “What a place.” “Mad. Would you have given her one?” “Oh, yeah. Absolutely.” But you’ve heard this one before: we’re in a story by Roddy Doyle — the title story, in fact, of his new collection, “Bullfighting.” Old friends. Pints of Guinness. The running patter of filthy jokes. We could almost be back in Barrytown, the fictional Dublin suburb whose inhabitants Doyle brought to such boisterous, profanity-fueled life back in the late 1980s and early ’90s. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/bkr-Shone-t_CA0/bkr-Shone-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/bkr-Shone-t_CA0/bkr-Shone-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/bkr-Shone-t_CA0/bkr-Shone-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roddy Doyle’s Dubliners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Irishmen Unmoored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doyle, Roddy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:57:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Shone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a00d5bc-0a8b-5fdd-85e8-fb93a2b24647', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a00d5bc-0a8b-5fdd-85e8-fb93a2b24647'}, {'abstract': 'Admitting he’s been bored for large tracts of his life, a classicist offers a history of his affliction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-boredom-a-lively-history-by-peter-toohey.html', 'snippet': 'Admitting he’s been bored for large tracts of his life, a classicist offers a history of his affliction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 13th canto of Byron’s “Don Juan,” our usually adventurous hero finds himself becalmed in English society, which, Byron reports, “ . . . is now one polish’d horde, / Form’d of two mighty tribes, the Bores and Bored.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Gottleib/Gottleib-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Gottleib/Gottleib-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Gottleib/Gottleib-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Life is So Boring', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wake Me When It’s Over', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:57:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Gottlieb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gottlieb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b157006e-eaf3-5cc2-b066-fc5aae5904a8', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b157006e-eaf3-5cc2-b066-fc5aae5904a8'}, {'abstract': 'Noah Webster was a journalist, reformer and lexicographer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-the-forgotten-founding-father-noah-websters-obsession-and-the-creation-of-an-american-culture-by-joshua-kendall.html', 'snippet': 'Noah Webster was a journalist, reformer and lexicographer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even if he had never written his famous dictionary, Noah Webster (1758-1843) would be regarded as one of the most interesting and influential figures of the early days of the American republic. His phenomenally popular “American Spelling Book,” first published in 1783, outsold every book in the 19th century except the Bible. His relentless book promotion pioneered now common techniques like the author tour, the fabricated blurb and the aggressive stoking of manufactured controversy. His efforts to protect his work from piracy contributed to the development of American copyright laws. In 1793 he founded American Minerva, the first daily newspaper in New York City, eight years before Alexander Hamilton started The New York Post. He helped to establish Amherst College.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Sheidlower-1305570936224/Sheidlower-1305570936224-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Sheidlower-1305570936224/Sheidlower-1305570936224-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Sheidlower-1305570936224/Sheidlower-1305570936224-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Noah Webster, Founding Father', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Colonial Polymath', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kendall, Joshua', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:57:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Sheidlower', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheidlower', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/57f49b58-0a71-514e-a5aa-9991abed8131', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57f49b58-0a71-514e-a5aa-9991abed8131'}, {'abstract': 'Jane Gross recounts her struggle to help an infirm parent and offers practical advice on eldercare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/book-review-a-bittersweet-season-caring-for-our-aging-parents-and-ourselves-by-jane-gross.html', 'snippet': 'Jane Gross recounts her struggle to help an infirm parent and offers practical advice on eldercare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Combining personal narrative with practical advice, as Jane Gross does in “A Bittersweet Season,” is a tricky business. A reader swept up in a story is apt to resent the intrusion of brass tacks. And a reader looking for how-tos will have little use for the details of an author’s own tale. Particularly perilous are the transitions between the instructional and the essayistic — passages reminiscent of the fraught moments in Broadway musicals when ordinary speech must lift into song. There is the actor, speaking his lines; suddenly he leans on his pitchfork, squints into the distance and breaks into a soaring rendition of “Oh, What a Beautiful Morning.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/PAUL/PAUL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 886, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/PAUL/PAUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/PAUL/PAUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Care for Your Mother', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Final Passage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bcb2d6c-4096-5ce7-a25f-fd45ed81e79a', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bcb2d6c-4096-5ce7-a25f-fd45ed81e79a'}, {'abstract': 'The announcement, which came after intensive talks between President Obama and President Dmitri A. Medvedev, represents a marked shift in Russia’s tone on Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/world/europe/28russia.html', 'snippet': 'The announcement, which came after intensive talks between President Obama and President Dmitri A. Medvedev, represents a marked shift in Russia’s tone on Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — President Dmitri A. Medvedev on Friday offered to leverage Russia’s relationships in Libya to try to persuade Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi to leave power, an act of long-shot diplomacy that for the first time casts Russia as a central player in events unfolding in North Africa.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Shift, Russia Agrees to Try to Talk Qaddafi Into Leaving', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Diplomatic Reversal, Russia Offers to Try to Persuade Qaddafi to Leave Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group of Eight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2bc8b73-3847-5496-acbb-66f6ac01e48f', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2bc8b73-3847-5496-acbb-66f6ac01e48f'}, {'abstract': 'With new books challenging our collective memory, can we still take pride in World War II?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/adam-kirsch-on-new-books-about-world-war-ii.html', 'snippet': 'With new books challenging our collective memory, can we still take pride in World War II?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February, the last surviving American veteran of the First World War died. It is hard to imagine the day when we say goodbye to the last survivor of the Second World War, so large do the “good war” and the “greatest generation” still loom in the national imagination. But the calendar and the census do not lie. Some 16 million Americans served in the military during World War II. On the 60th anniversary of the attack on Pearl Harbor in 2001, about 5.5 million were still living. This year, as we prepare to mark the 70th anniversary, the number is closer to 1.5 million, and it drops by almost a thousand a day.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Kirsh/Kirsh-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 356, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Kirsh/Kirsh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Kirsh/Kirsh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is World War II Still ‘the Good War’?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Battle for History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burleigh, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:58:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Kirsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a47821f0-fc9a-5580-8993-a4c54921dcc8', 'word_count': 2900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a47821f0-fc9a-5580-8993-a4c54921dcc8'}, {'abstract': 'Qaddafi’s Green Book mixes utopian socialism and Arab nationalism with a streak of Bedouin supremacism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/what-did-qaddafis-green-book-really-say.html', 'snippet': 'Qaddafi’s Green Book mixes utopian socialism and Arab nationalism with a streak of Bedouin supremacism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Libya’s ragtag coalition of rebels manages to dislodge Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi from power it will mean not only the fall of the longest reigning dictator in the Arab world but also the end of the ambitious ideological system that he dreamed would supersede all rivals. While much of the world sees him as an eccentric and brutal demagogue, Qaddafi has tried for decades to portray himself as a statesman-philosopher. It’s an aspiration best embodied in the Green Book, his notorious three-part meditation on politics, economics and everything from the evils of mechanized poultry farming to the importance of owning one’s car. In Libya, the text is omnipresent: several generations of students grew up studying it, and the government even constructed sculptures in its image.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Bazzi/Bazzi-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Bazzi/Bazzi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Bazzi/Bazzi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Did Qaddafi’s Green Book Really Say?', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Required Reading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohamad Bazzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohamad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d197e594-a268-50c4-bbd5-032cabc4fa43', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d197e594-a268-50c4-bbd5-032cabc4fa43'}, {'abstract': 'It shouldn’t be surprising that a writer whose work includes a story collection called “Throw Like a Girl” should have strong opinions on the pigeonholing of writers by gender.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'It shouldn’t be surprising that a writer whose work includes a story collection called “Throw Like a Girl” should have strong opinions on the pigeonholing of writers by gender.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It shouldn’t be surprising that a writer whose work includes a story collection called “Throw Like a Girl” and a novel called “The Woman Driver” should have strong opinions on the pigeonholing of writers by gender. Jean Thompson, whose latest novel, “The Year We Left Home,” has just been published, is a longtime practitioner of what she described in a recent e-mail as “the sturdy realistic tradition” that she shares with the British writer Tessa Hadley, whose novel “The London Train” she reviews on Page 13. Hadley, Thompson noted, is a “first-rate psychologist” who immerses the reader in her characters using “a particularly intimate form of third-person narrator.” And Hadley has presented half of her novel — which delves into some of the familiar problems of modern life also found in the “women’s novels” one of the characters reads — from the perspective of a man. “When I taught writing,” Thompson recalled, “inevitably students loved to worry this point, how you could identify ‘male’ or ‘female’ writing. So I brought in some blind samples: women writing about sea voyages, men writing about intense emotional experiences. When I asked them to guess the author’s gender, they fell right into that famous trap.” The Editors ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a52657bd-b439-54d8-a606-6973a53cdd42', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a52657bd-b439-54d8-a606-6973a53cdd42'}, {'abstract': 'Recent books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recent books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE ANATOMY OF INFLUENCE: Literature as a Way of Life,  by Harold Bloom (Yale University, $32.50.) Bloom, now 80, offers a kind of summing-up of ideas from his sterling career in criticism, and \xadreaffirms his vows of fidelity to Shakespeare. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:59:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bcbc1d3-963d-56ad-bec3-6badf7134f24', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bcbc1d3-963d-56ad-bec3-6badf7134f24'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': ' WHAT IS LEFT THE DAUGHTER,  by Howard Norman (Mariner/Houghton Mifflin, $13.95.) In this epistolary novel, set in Nova Scotia during World War II, Wyatt Hillyer reaches out to a 21-year-old daughter he hasn’t seen or spoken to since 1948, when she was 2. Beginning with the day his mother and father commit suicide within hours of each other, Wyatt’s layered tale encompasses unrequited love, murder and German U-boats prowling Canada’s eastern shore. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T13:59:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9df6e27-1930-55db-bfca-a6df428324e3', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9df6e27-1930-55db-bfca-a6df428324e3'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Romer, a Stanford professor, will join New York University’s economics department and run the new Urban System Project.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/n-y-u-lands-top-economist-for-cities-project/', 'snippet': 'Paul Romer, a Stanford professor, will join New York University’s economics department and run the new Urban System Project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York University has landed a star economist: Paul Romer, a leading expert on economic growth, who has been at Stanford for the last 15 years. Mr. Romer has been a visiting professor at N.Y.U. this year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.Y.U. Lands Top Economist for Cities Project', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romer, Paul M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T14:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e328e0d-b6d9-597e-aa53-9abcf8d3eaff', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e328e0d-b6d9-597e-aa53-9abcf8d3eaff'}, {'abstract': 'The British police code name for President Obama’s state visit to the United Kingdom.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/chalaque/', 'snippet': 'The British police code name for President Obama’s state visit to the United Kingdom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British police code name for President Obama’s state visit to the United Kingdom.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chalaque', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'maj