humbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/books/review/bkr-Reich-t_CA0/bkr-Reich-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington and Wall Street: The Revolving Door', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting Away With It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Association', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Freddie Mac', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgenson, Gretchen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, James A', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T14:38:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROBERT B. REICH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f2a63ff-6126-5bcf-8e6e-646a4c68d094', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f2a63ff-6126-5bcf-8e6e-646a4c68d094'}, {'abstract': 'Gerard Denault is accused of having received at least $5 million in illegal kickbacks.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/consultant-on-payroll-project-is-arrested/', 'snippet': 'Gerard Denault is accused of having received at least $5 million in illegal kickbacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A former consulting company executive was charged on Friday with receiving at least $5 million in illegal kickbacks in connection with his work as a project manager on the CityTime automated payroll project, people briefed on the matter said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Consultant on Payroll Project Is Arrested', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denault, Gerard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Investigation (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T14:40:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By William K. Rashbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Rashbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cbfe19f-d96f-50c8-bf3f-752bfc2c0623', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cbfe19f-d96f-50c8-bf3f-752bfc2c0623'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrities may rule the best-seller list, but psychopaths rule the world — or so suggests Jon Ronson, author of “The Psychopath Test.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Celebrities may rule the best-seller list, but psychopaths rule the world — or so suggests Jon Ronson, author of “The Psychopath Test.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LET’S GO CRAZY: Celebrities may rule the best-seller list, but psychopaths still rule the world. At least that’s the hypothesis floated by Jon Ronson in “The Psychopath Test,” new at No. 11 on the hardcover nonfiction list. In the book, Ronson (inset) consorts with psychologists, corporate titans, madmen, Scientologists and a cabal of “giant, blood-drinking, child-\xadsacrificing pedophile lizards”—oh, sorry, those guys were actually from Ronson’s earlier book “Them” — in an effort to prove that those afflicted with “mad striving for unlimited success and excessive admiration” profoundly shape society and, more important, can be identified with the help of a simple 20-item checklist. Alas, Ronson finds only one high-profile potential psychopath willing to take the test: Albert (Chainsaw Al) Dunlap, the corporate cost-cutting king. (“You know how I said in my e-mail that your amygdala might not shoot the requisite signals of fear to your central nervous system and that’s perhaps why you’ve been so successful?” Ronson asks gingerly.) Not that Ronson presents himself as the picture of mental health. After consulting the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual IV, he concludes that he may suffer from no fewer than 12 mental ailments, including general anxiety disorder, arithmetic learning disorder, caffeine induced disorder, and malingering.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/books/bestseller/bestseller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/27/books/bestseller/bestseller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Akpan, Uwem', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frey, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronson, Jon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobsen, Annie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Cormac', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiesel, Elie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T14:42:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b49987b9-83b4-515d-93fa-d92374a1c5e1', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b49987b9-83b4-515d-93fa-d92374a1c5e1'}, {'abstract': 'In a revival of an attraction said to date to 1824 in New York City, there are now more than 50 beer gardens scattered in its neighborhoods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/nyregion/beer-gardens-growing-steadily-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'In a revival of an attraction said to date to 1824 in New York City, there are now more than 50 beer gardens scattered in its neighborhoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE are some who thought, prematurely, that 2010 was New York’s summer of the beer garden, what with the World Cup and the opening of a half-dozen outdoor, German-style drinking establishments. But not unlike some genetically altered superweed, these ale-and-oompah joints have continued even this year to crop up everywhere you look. They have grown so thick, so fast, that certain neighborhoods (Astoria in Queens and Williamsburg in Brooklyn come to mind) could, with the proper vantage and the help of several pilsners, be mistaken for Bavaria.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima