 Wall Street implosion; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Jennifer Schuessler has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "David McCullough's New Book; the Wall Street Implosion; Best Seller News", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T15:41:06+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/79ebe64f-505e-5a4d-8a55-f00236f930b9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/79ebe64f-505e-5a4d-8a55-f00236f930b9'}, {'abstract': 'Samir Barai, who is accused of swapping illegal stock tips with two portfolio managers at SAC Capital, is expected to plead guilty.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/guilty-plea-expected-in-hedge-fund-case/', 'snippet': 'Samir Barai, who is accused of swapping illegal stock tips with two portfolio managers at SAC Capital, is expected to plead guilty.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:46 a.m. | Updated Samir Barai, a former Citigroup executive who ran Barai Capital Management, is expected to plead guilty on Friday afternoon to insider trading before a magistrate at the Federal District Court in Manhattan, a person briefed on the matter told DealBook. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guilty Plea Expected in Hedge Fund Case', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barai, Samir', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barai Capital Management', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Primary Global Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SAC Capital Advisors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T15:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b088d92c-7a83-5690-a5f5-ebe668655514', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b088d92c-7a83-5690-a5f5-ebe668655514'}, {'abstract': 'DVD Extras From the David Mamet Interview', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/mo-mamet-mo-mamet/', 'snippet': 'DVD Extras From the David Mamet Interview', 'lead_paragraph': 'The absolute worst part of my new job doing the weekly Q & A in the magazine is editing the hour-long conversations down to the paltry 700 words allotted for my interview. So many darlings. So many grisly deaths. This was especially true of my conversation with Pulitzer-winning playwright and nouveau-grumpy right-winger David Mamet, who spent the first 10 minutes of our conversation outlining in detail the entire plot of his unproduced screenplay for “The Diary of Anne Frank.” (In a particularly wonderful bit of Hollywood absurdity, he says that Disney, who commissioned the script, deemed Mamet’s take “too dark” before putting it in turnaround.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mo’ Mamet, Mo’ Mamet', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mamet, David', 'rank': 1, 'major'