ne, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6116ff47-148f-50dd-923d-ea2c792d670e', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6116ff47-148f-50dd-923d-ea2c792d670e'}, {'abstract': 'Nina Arianda, nominated for a best actress Tony for “Born Yesterday,” has screeched, pouted and purred her way to roles in film and on Broadway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/theater/nina-arianda-a-rising-star-in-born-yesterday.html', 'snippet': 'Nina Arianda, nominated for a best actress Tony for “Born Yesterday,” has screeched, pouted and purred her way to roles in film and on Broadway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Meeting Nina Arianda in person for the first time is very different from meeting her onstage. Instead of the high-pitched “WHA-A-A-A-T?” with which she announces her entrance as Billie Dawn, the classic dumb blonde she plays in the revival of “Born Yesterday” at the Cort Theater, Ms. Arianda offers a low-key, low-throated pleased-to-meet-you. Her honking version of hello — which Charles Isherwood described in The New York Times as a cross between Jean Harlow and Cyndi Lauper — has helped to turn Ms. Arianda, 26, into Broadway’s newest darling and won her a Tony nomination for best actress. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/arianda1/arianda1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/arianda1/arianda1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cred