f Ravel, Rachmaninoff, Debussy and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/arts/music/marouan-benabdallahs-american-debut-music.html', 'snippet': 'In his American debut at Zankel Hall, the Hungarian-Moroccan pianist Marouan Benabdallah performed a program of Ravel, Rachmaninoff, Debussy and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The recital that the Moroccan pianist Marouan Benabdallah gave at Zankel Hall on Thursday night, his American debut, was naturally a personal milestone for this 29-year-old artist. From the demanding program he played, with nearly two hours of virtuoso works from the late 19th and early 20th centuries, he seemed determined to make a strong impression. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/BEN/BEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/BEN/BEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/BEN/BEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/BEN/BEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/BEN/BEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hungarian Tradition, African Lilt', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hungarian Tradition, African Lilt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:39:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2233a62a-657d-5387-8e45-f75609d650e9', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2233a62a-657d-5387-8e45-f75609d650e9'}, {'abstract': 'The crew behind a reality show try to control their own harried lives in the play “Cut.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/theater/reviews/cut-by-crystal-skillman-at-under-st-marks-review.html', 'snippet': 'The crew behind a reality show try to control their own harried lives in the play “Cut.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades Hollywood’s sick soul has provided cannon fodder for novelists and screenwriters. With “Cut,” a Horse Trade Theater Group and Management Theater Company production at Under St. Marks, the playwright Crystal Skillman presents a modest “Hurlyburly” for our time, taking aim at reality television, as deserving a target — with as Orwellian a name — as show business currently offers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/CUT/CUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/CUT/CUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/CUT/CUT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/CUT/CUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/CUT/CUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Scenes, Action and Angst', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Cut'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Behind the Scenes, Action and Angst', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Horse Trade Theater Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skillman, Crystal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed14b7c1-7d0f-5d02-b53b-84cc7139fb94', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed14b7c1-7d0f-5d02-b53b-84cc7139fb94'}, {'abstract': 'An art show and contest highlights the artists who create mesmerizing scenes for today’s videogames.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/the-art-of-the-videogame/', 'snippet': 'An art show and contest highlights the artists who create mesmerizing scenes for today’s videogames.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artwork that goes into today’s videogames can be museum-worthy. Many game scenes look completely photorealistic, created with crisp high-end graphics; others are highly stylized cartoon-like visuals. Some games even contain scenes that are beautifully painted by hand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of the Videogame', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Entertainment Software Assn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:41:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0e9c5e6-a94b-55fe-8722-fa3ac140e506', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0e9c5e6-a94b-55fe-8722-fa3ac140e506'}, {'abstract': 'When a tennis player is at the top of his game, he is said to be in the zone. Expert bridge players experience that also, just knowing where all of the cards are lying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/crosswords/bridge/a-dandy-defensive-coup-that-few-players-would-find.html', 'snippet': 'When a tennis player is at the top of his game, he is said to be in the zone. Expert bridge players experience that also, just knowing where all of the cards are lying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a tennis player is at the top of his game, he is said to be in the zone. Expert bridge players experience that also, just knowing where all of the cards are lying. Richie Coren (East) of Delray Beach, Fla., found the “zonal” play in the diagramed deal from a game in Florida this month.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/27/arts/card1/card1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Dandy Defensive Coup That Few Players Would Find', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Dandy Defensive Coup That Few Players Would Find', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:47:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03faeee0-7ac3-574f-8b97-e84df1f322a4', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03faeee0-7ac3-574f-8b97-e84df1f322a4'}, {'abstract': 'Mobile phones replacing wallets is clearly in the future, but the type of technology we use and how long it takes for it to be adopted is still up for debate.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/waiting-for-the-new-mobile-payments-frontier/', 'snippet': 'Mobile phones replacing wallets is clearly in the future, but the type of technology we use and how long it takes for it to be adopted is still up for debate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mobile phone will eventually take over your wallet, replacing your credit cards, cash and coupons. But it could be some time before this new type of transaction becomes an everyday reality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Waiting for the New Mobile Payments Frontier', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:50:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e151ffdf-cb74-594e-8f94-995c450772dc', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e151ffdf-cb74-594e-8f94-995c450772dc'}, {'abstract': 'The popular crossover can be optioned with the latest MyFord Touch in-car connectivity system. John R. Quain wonders whether that is a good thing.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/reviewing-the-ford-edge-sport/', 'snippet': 'The popular crossover can be optioned with the latest MyFord Touch in-car connectivity system. John R. Quain wonders whether that is a good thing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sunday’s Automobiles section, John R. Quain reviews the Ford Edge Sport. For 2011, the popular crossover received a revised front fascia, more robust brakes and the stout V-6 from the base-level Mustang. Ford, however, also wants to train attention on the latest version of MyFord Touch, one of the most advanced in-car computer control systems on the market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing the Ford Edge Sport', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T21:53:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77be680f-3631-5a3d-9790-687bf754073d', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77be680f-3631-5a3d-9790-687bf754073d'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Amy Ernst, based in Congo, writes about those who take advantage of the suffering, while claiming to help them.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/notes-from-a-young-american-in-congo-orphans-on-the-edge/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Amy Ernst, based in Congo, writes about those who take advantage of the suffering, while claiming to help them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I flew to Kinshasa in order to help a man help a boy. Scott Sherman and his family had recently adopted a four-year-old boy from an orphanage in the capital city of the Democratic Republic of Congo. When Scott’s son arrived in the States, he was severely underweight, suffering from a variety of illnesses, and indicated that he had been sexually abused at the orphanage where he’d lived. Scott’s son told his new parents that one other boy, Moise, had tried to protect him from the abuse. Scott and his family asked me if I could try and help find a better situation for Moise. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From a Young American in Congo: Orphans on the Edge', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T22:04:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Ernst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ernst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06056f40-c8cd-5c1d-8a48-6f8bf8550aef', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06056f40-c8cd-5c1d-8a48-6f8bf8550aef'}, {'abstract': 'Why can’t the French and the Americans just get along?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/french-press/', 'snippet': 'Why can’t the French and the Americans just get along?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were a lot of shockers this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'French Press', 'kicker': 'The Thread', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T22:04:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Catapano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catapano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8aeae1a-31cb-52c9-84ad-781c38589851', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8aeae1a-31cb-52c9-84ad-781c38589851'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region includes images of a quaint antiques shop in the East Village, a dilapidated castle on the Hudson River, and a special Congressional election in upstate New York.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/27/the-week-in-pictures-for-may-27/', 'snippet': 'A slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region includes images of a quaint antiques shop in the East Village, a dilapidated castle on the Hudson River, and a special Congressional election in upstate New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include a quaint antiques shop in the East Village, a dilapidated castle on the Hudson River, and a special Congressional election in upstate New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Pictures for May 27', 'kicker': 'City Room',