eons of nature and decades of culture have made anything clear, it is that all creation is more or less a collage. You know, recombinant DNA. XX times XY. Boy meets girl. “Annie” meets “Aliens.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/fashion/POSSESSED1/POSSESSED1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/fashion/POSSESSED1/POSSESSED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/fashion/POSSESSED1/POSSESSED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Narrative Has Many Blanks', 'kicker': 'Possessed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Narrative Has Many Blanks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salle, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T22:07:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Colman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Colman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3508ea4-9b1e-5e00-8e6c-6813da5ca3e9', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3508ea4-9b1e-5e00-8e6c-6813da5ca3e9'}, {'abstract': 'Clara Longstreth led a program of Romanticism and tone painting in recent American choral music, with some modern reconsiderations of folk melodies as well.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/arts/music/clara-longstreth-leads-the-new-amsterdam-singers.html', 'snippet': 'Clara Longstreth led a program of Romanticism and tone painting in recent American choral music, with some modern reconsiderations of folk melodies as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Amsterdam Singers have explored music ranging from 15th-century sacred works to modern secular pieces in the 40 years since Clara Longstreth founded the group, and the choir’s performances have always been spirited and finely polished. The group’s real charm, though, is its passion for contemporary American music. Having built relationships with choral composers around the country, Ms. Longstreth has made a point of building smart thematic programs around their work. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/alt-SINGERS/alt-SINGERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/alt-SINGERS/alt-SINGERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/alt-SINGERS/alt-SINGERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Romanticism, Tone Paintings and Modern Takes on Folk Tunes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Romanticism, Tone Paintings and Modern Takes on Folk Tunes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Longstreth, Clara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Amsterdam Singers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T22:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f173fc58-b967-50ed-88dd-9ec61fe1c269', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f173fc58-b967-50ed-88dd-9ec61fe1c269'}, {'abstract': 'The violinists Ana Milosavljevic and Todd Reynolds performed as part of the Innova showcase at the Stone on Wednesday evening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/arts/music/violinists-plugged-in-bow-pluck-and-twang.html', 'snippet': 'The violinists Ana Milosavljevic and Todd Reynolds performed as part of the Innova showcase at the Stone on Wednesday evening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the final two weeks of May the programming at the Stone has been in the hands of Philip Blackburn, the director of the enterprising, polyglot record label Innova. Mr. Blackburn’s idea was to present 24 one-hour concerts by musicians who record for Innova, and on Wednesday evening he offered, as hours 13 and 14, what he called “the fiddler’s hoedown from hell”: back-to-back recitals by Ana Milosavljevic and Todd Reynolds, violinists for whom amplification and sound processing are integral to music-making. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/ana/ana-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 629, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Violinists, Plugged in, Bow, Pluck and Twang', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Violinists, Plugged In, Bow, Pluck And Twang', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reynolds, Todd', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-27T22:16:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/755d3e96-d12c-548d-a301-0220dd1fef1d', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/755d3e96-d12c-548d-a301-0220dd1fef1d'}, {'abstract': 'Paris in the 1920s, as portrayed in Woody Allen’s “Midnight in Paris,” was fertile ground for art, literature and complicated relationships, decrypted here.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/movies/midnight-in-paris-a-historical-view.html', 'snippet': 'Paris in the 1920s, as portrayed in Woody Allen’s “Midnight in Paris,” was fertile ground for art, literature and complicated relationships, decrypted here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many a writer or artist has longed to travel back in time to the sizzling Paris of the 1920s, to sip absinthe with Hemingway at Les Deux Magots or dine on choucroute garnie with Picasso at La Rotonde. Imagine the conversation! What has beguiled audiences about the new Woody Allen movie, “Midnight in Paris,” is that the protagonist, Gil, a disenchanted Hollywood screenwriter played by Owen Wilson, gets to live exactly that fantasy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/jpmidnight1/jpmidnight1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/28/arts/jpmidnight1/jpmidnight1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'c