uction now at Hartford Stage also serves as the final offering of Michael Wilson’s tenure as artistic director. As it turns out, this conjunction of Wilsons has a certain aptness: With its many comings and goings, the Pittsburgh parlor of the play’s mystical Aunt Ester provides both an essential destination and an ideal point of departure. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29STAGECO/29STAGECO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29STAGECO/29STAGECO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29STAGECO/29STAGECO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29STAGECO/29STAGECO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29STAGECO/29STAGECO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Africa to Pittsburgh, Slave Ship to 1904', 'kicker': 'Theater Review | Connecticut', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Africa to Pittsburgh, Slave Ship to 1904', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, August', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hartford Stage Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T02:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sylviane Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sylviane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b26d7db0-e1a8-5bb7-9c7d-d878b6ec96db', 'word_count': 658, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b26d7db0-e1a8-5bb7-9c7d-d878b6ec96db'}, {'abstract': '“My One and Only,” at the Goodspeed Opera House, features songs by George and Ira Gershwin and choreography by Kelli Barclay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/nyregion/my-one-and-only-review.html', 'snippet': '“My One and Only,” at the Goodspeed Opera House, features songs by George and Ira Gershwin and choreography by Kelli Barclay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What could possibly bring together a hick aviator from Texas, a comely swimmer from England, an entrepreneurial prince from Russia and an all-knowing barber from Harlem? ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29THEATCO1/29THEATCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29THEATCO1/29THEATCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29THEATCO1/29THEATCO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/nyregion/29THEATCO1/29THEATCO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumb