ract': 'Whether an investigation will result in punishment for the killers — or those who hired them — is uncertain. More than 1,000 rural activists, small farmers, religious workers and others fighting deforestation have been slain in the past 20 years, but only a handful of killers have been successfully prosecuted.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/murder-of-amazon-activists-raises-justice-questions/', 'snippet': 'Whether an investigation will result in punishment for the killers — or those who hired them — is uncertain. More than 1,000 rural activists, small farmers, religious workers and others fighting deforestation have been slain in the past 20 years, ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early last Tuesday, José Cláudio Ribeiro da Silva, a forest activist and tree nut harvester, and his wife, Maria do Espirito Santo, drove a motorcycle through Brazil’s northern Para State, in the Amazon rain forest. As they crossed a river bridge, gunmen lying in wait opened fire with a pistol and shotgun, killing them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder of Activists Raises Questions of Justice in Amazon', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Logging Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stang, Dorothy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenpeace', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T14:36:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Collins Rudolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Rudolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/734785ce-05fb-5415-9bd2-4688d095882a', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/734785ce-05fb-5415-9bd2-4688d095882a'}, {'abstract': 'Applying that can’t-do spirit.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/inflation-and-debt-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'Applying that can’t-do spirit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I mentioned in an earlier post, the latest OECD Economic Outlook is a remarkable document — and I mean that in the worst way. I’ve already pointed to the report’s insistence that we must raise interest rates, because foofa grrzt bumble shazam — OK, that’s not quite what the report says, but it makes at least as much sense as the justification the report does offer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inflation and Debt (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T14:44:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acf8d661-2f0f-5341-8312-d9bd2b6f2bf8', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acf8d661-2f0f-5341-8312-d9bd2b6f2bf8'}, {'abstract': 'Though often called the “godfather of rap,” Mr. Scott-Heron preferred to call himself a “bluesologist,” drawing on blues, jazz and Harlem Renaissance poetics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/arts/music/gil-scott-heron-voice-of-black-culture-dies-at-62.html', 'snippet': 'Though often called the “godfather of rap,” Mr. Scott-Heron preferred to call himself a “bluesologist,” drawing on blues, jazz and Harlem Renaissance poetics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gil Scott-Heron, the poet and recording artist whose syncopated spoken style and mordant critiques of politics, racism and mass media in pieces like “The Revolution Will Not Be Televised” made him a notable voice of black protest culture in the 1970s and an important early influence on hip-hop, died on Friday at a hospital in Manhattan. He was 62 and had been a longtime resident of Harlem.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/obituaries/28scott-heron_337/28scott-heron_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/28/obituaries/28scott-heron_337/28scott-heron_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/28/obituaries/28scott-heron_337/28scott-heron_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gil Scott-Heron, Voice of Black Protest Culture, Dies at 62', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gil Scott-Heron, a Voice of Protest And a Music Pioneer, Dies at 62', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blues Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott-Heron, Gil', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T14:55:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/9078b3c0-93df-538c-a5c9-a50eddb1ed1a', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9078b3c0-93df-538c-a5c9-a50eddb1ed1a'}, {'abstract': 'A recipe for spruce-tip oil.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/urban-forager-spring-sprucing/', 'snippet': 'A recipe for spruce-tip oil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some weeks ago, I started noticing nascent light-green buds on the branches of every city conifer I encountered from Central Park down to Staten Island. Unlike mature pine needle tea — a resiny, survivalist food at best — fresh, young evergreen tips are a citrusy surprise to the palate, and I couldn’t help nibbling on a choice few. At the College of Staten Island, I discovered a midsize, classic Christmas-tree-looking conifer, which friends at the Brooklyn Botanic Garden identified as a Colorado blue spruce. It had very lemony-tasting tips.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Urban Forager | Spring Sprucing', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'valu