name': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelloh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48363d84-59dd-519d-ad5b-e2db8970d9c4', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48363d84-59dd-519d-ad5b-e2db8970d9c4'}, {'abstract': 'Less physical activity at work may be a major but overlooked culprit in rising American obesity. This calls for a management solution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/weekinreview/29fat.html', 'snippet': 'Less physical activity at work may be a major but overlooked culprit in rising American obesity. This calls for a management solution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'News item: Less physical activity at work may be a major but overlooked culprit in rising American obesity, researchers reported last week.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/FAT/FAT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/FAT/FAT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/FAT/FAT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/FAT/FAT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/FAT/FAT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Office: Sedentary No More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Office: Sedentary No More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T19:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8c2f656-d325-5b15-99fd-295193120752', 'word_count': 37, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8c2f656-d325-5b15-99fd-295193120752'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t have the grades to get into Oxford? It might not be a problem if you’ve got the cash.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/weekinreview/29grist.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t have the grades to get into Oxford? It might not be a problem if you’ve got the cash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DON’T HAVE THE GRADES to get into Oxford? It might not be a problem if you’ve got the cash. The British education minister has proposed letting wealthy students pay for spots at top universities even though they failed to get in through the regular admissions process. “Rage and confusion have been the immediate results,” The Boston Globe reports. Admission to British universities is based on meritocratic competition; once students get in, they pay a low flat fee. At Oxford, it is about $6,000 a year, The Globe says. Supporters of the idea say the extra cash will bolster the educational system. Less affluent students would not lose out, they say, because these new spots would be “extra” ones. Critics say the two-tier idea is profoundly unfair. “The proposals are likely to be criticized as a means for the wealthiest to ‘buy places’ at a time when the government is to cut 10,000 publicly funded places,” The Guardian reported.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/29/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ahead of the Class', 'kicker': 'Grist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ahead of the Class', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T19:32:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c1952d9-ae84-5144-bc51-83c8a6df8e1b', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c1952d9-ae84-5144-bc51-83c8a6df8e1b'}, {'abstract': 'The Legislature’s regular session is ending as it started, with lawmakers arguing on the budget and redistricting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/us/29ttsessionweb.html', 'snippet': 'The Legislature’s regular session is ending as it started, with lawmakers arguing on the budget and redistricting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 82nd Texas Legislature’s regular session ends as it started, with lawmakers arguing about a shrunken state budget and redistricting.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27A', 