e': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE TEXAS TRIBUNE', 'person': [], 'organization': 'THE TEXAS TRIBUNE'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97e29b4c-56bb-5c4c-842d-87ae36586b01', 'word_count': 2567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97e29b4c-56bb-5c4c-842d-87ae36586b01'}, {'abstract': '48: the percentage of American households represented by married couples in 2010, the first time that figure has dropped below 50 percent', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/weekinreview/29prime.html', 'snippet': '48: the percentage of American households represented by married couples in 2010, the first time that figure has dropped below 50 percent', 'lead_paragraph': '48: THE PERCENTAGE OF American households represented by married couples in 2010, the first time that figure has dropped below 50 percent, the Census Bureau reports. That is down from 78 percent in 1950. “The days of Ozzie and Harriet have faded into the past,” said William Frey, a demographer at the Brookings Institution who analyzed the census figures. Just a fifth of households were traditional families — married couples with children. The overwhelming majority of Americans still eventually marry, though increasingly working-class people do not stay married.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prime Number', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prime Number', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T19:33:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a398c58-d0d8-5901-8495-1d9ad4801dba', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a398c58-d0d8-5901-8495-1d9ad4801dba'}, {'abstract': 'Congress agreed last week to reauthorize the Patriot Act just before the terrorist surveillance law was set to