'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendes, Chico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T21:15:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39c32511-d175-5540-bd24-69a196b42e32', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39c32511-d175-5540-bd24-69a196b42e32'}, {'abstract': 'Democrats are too afraid of political risk to put forward bold thinking of their own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/opinion/29sun1.html', 'snippet': 'Democrats are too afraid of political risk to put forward bold thinking of their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republican leaders in the Senate have spent weeks gleefully deriding the Democrats who run the chamber for not producing a budget proposal in more than two years. It is a classic tactic, designed to deflect attention from their party’s toxic plan to privatize Medicare. The truth, though, is that the Republicans also have a point.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Passive in the Senate', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Passive in the Senate: Democrats are too afraid of political risk to put forward bold thinking of their own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T21:20:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/efa381ec-8312-585c-9210-89fa2118581e', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efa381ec-8312-585c-9210-89fa2118581e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about India gaining ground on China.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/readers-comment-on-the-rise-of-india/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about India gaining ground on China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column argues that India is turning around and that its takeoff is gaining traction around the country, even in places like Bihar and West Bengal. I’ve been a bit wary of India in the past, previously noting that the tech sector accomplishments are real but have tiny employment and that solid chunks of India remain abysmally undeveloped. That’s still true, but my latest trip leaves me more optimistic. India is steadily evolving in the right direction — while in China there’s reason for more caution. Under President Hu Jintao, China’s evolution has paused, and there probably won’t be more movement until after the Communist Party Congress in late 2012 installs Xi Jinping as Hu’s successor. I still would never bet against China, but I think India may be able 