ttps://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/28/sunday-breakfast-menu-may-29/', 'snippet': 'Some of this week’s shows honor Memorial Day by focusing on the issues facing military personnel and veterans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this Memorial Day weekend, CNN’s “State of the Union” and TV One’s “Washington Watch” are recognizing the holiday by exploring the challenges facing military personnel and veterans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, May 29', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-28T22:49:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86fa6642-9c32-52e1-a1fd-99ba91e6753c', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86fa6642-9c32-52e1-a1fd-99ba91e6753c'}, {'abstract': 'The Texas novelist Stephen Harrigan has been successful, but never in fashion among the New York literary set.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/us/29ttharrigan.html', 'snippet': 'The Texas novelist Stephen Harrigan has been successful, but never in fashion among the New York literary set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To research his latest work of historical fiction, the post-World War I novel “Remember Ben Clayton,” the novelist and screenwriter Stephen Harrigan traveled to France twice so he could faithfully describe the town of Saint-Étienne and the battle that took place there in 1918. In Austin, where he lives, he spent hours poring over newspaper microfiche so he could write about what movies were playing at the time and what brands of chewing gum were being advertised. ', 