}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ccec3a9-81b1-5df6-a6ea-0612b1f5fcac', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ccec3a9-81b1-5df6-a6ea-0612b1f5fcac'}, {'abstract': 'After odd-numbered games, tennis players are allowed a 90-second break on the sidelines. What they do with their breaks says a lot about their state of mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/sports/tennis/30iht-CHANGEOVER30.html', 'snippet': 'After odd-numbered games, tennis players are allowed a 90-second break on the sidelines. What they do with their breaks says a lot about their state of mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — There was once not much of a changeover in tennis. Chairs were not used at Wimbledon until 1975, and until the demands of television had to be met, players stayed on their feet and quickly made their way to the opposite side of the court, taking a swig of water or perhaps something stronger along the way. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/YCHANGEOVER2/YCHANGEOVER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/YCHANGEOVER2/YCHANGEOVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/YCHANGEOVER2/YCHANGEOVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Changeovers Offer Glimpse Into Players’ Souls', 'kicker': 'Tennis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off to the Side, 90-Second Looks Into Players’ Souls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'