are laugher during a tear-inducing season, the Mets pounded the Phillies, knocking out 17 hits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/mets-end-rocky-week-by-thumping-phillies.html', 'snippet': 'In a rare laugher during a tear-inducing season, the Mets pounded the Phillies, knocking out 17 hits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After enduring one of the stranger and more stressful weeks in the team’s 49-year history, the Mets were eager to turn the page Sunday.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/30metspix/30metspix-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/30metspix/30metspix-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/30metspix/30metspix-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/30metspix/30metspix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/sports/baseball/30metspix/30metspix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets End Rocky Week by Thumping Phillies', 'kicker': 'Mets 9, Phillies 5', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mets End Rocky Week By Thumping Phillies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reyes, Jose', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Phillies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Niese, Jon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thole, Josh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Daniel (1985- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:14:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a90e042-0f3f-5a49-b1ee-52ea5f35549b', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a90e042-0f3f-5a49-b1ee-52ea5f35549b'}, {'abstract': 'The Morning Line for Sunday, May 29.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/the-morning-line-12-appear-ready-for-belmont/', 'snippet': 'The Morning Line for Sunday, May 29.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jerry Bossert in The Daily News reviewed the field for the Belmont Stakes to be run on June 11 and found that “a field of 12 appears set.” Mike Veitch in the Saratogian reviewed and rated the cast of 3-year-olds in the United States. His top four rated in order were Animal Kingdom, Shackleford, Nehro and Toby’s Corner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: 12 Appear Ready for Belmont', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:16:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87815872-c849-5ee1-b8ee-05b2da39628b', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87815872-c849-5ee1-b8ee-05b2da39628b'}, {'abstract': 'At Feinstein’s at Loews Regency, the singer Marilyn Maye, 83, delighted in her long love affair with her audience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/arts/music/marilyn-maye-at-feinsteins-at-loews-regency-review.html', 'snippet': 'At Feinstein’s at Loews Regency, the singer Marilyn Maye, 83, delighted in her long love affair with her audience.', 'lead_paragraph': '“As many of you know, I’ve had three husbands and one meaningful love affair,” the singer Marilyn Maye remarked. “And now that I’m too old to be humble, the good news is that none of ’em worked. This is what I love.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/arts/MAYE/MAYE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 776, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American Songbook as Fountain of Youth', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Songbook as Fountain of Youth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Feinstein's at the Regency", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maye, Marilyn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:21:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa2d9250-54a7-5f47-8c96-1c39b5d59b02', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa2d9250-54a7-5f47-8c96-1c39b5d59b02'}, {'abstract': 'Ursula Langmayr’s program at the Austrian Institute, on a nature theme, ranged from Mahler to the contemporary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/arts/music/ursula-langmayr-sings-mahler-at-austrian-institute-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ursula Langmayr’s program at the Austrian Institute, on a nature theme, ranged from Mahler to the contemporary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One hundred years after Mahler’s death his work remains a vital influence, at least when it comes to talking animals. In a revealing recital Thursday evening at the Austrian Cultural Forum the soprano Ursula Langmayr sang two sets of Mahler songs about nature and beasts, using them to frame pieces on the same themes by three more recent, less famous Austrian composers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/arts/URSULA/URSULA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 611, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music to Echo Nature’s Sounds and Voices', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music to Echo Nature’s Sounds and Voices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:22:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab757479-b67b-56f2-a5d6-965096b2025c', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab757479-b67b-56f2-a5d6-965096b2025c'}, {'abstract': 'Ranking Major League Baseball’s best ballparks shows Pittsburgh coming out on top.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/ranking-baseballs-best-ballparks/', 'snippet': 'Ranking Major League Baseball’s best ballparks shows Pittsburgh coming out on top.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m working on an article about the New York Mets — but that subject is perhaps too depressing for a beautiful Memorial Day weekend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ranking Baseball’s Best Ballparks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings and Rating Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bayless, Rick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soriano, Alfonso', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fenway Park (Boston)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yelp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:25:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2399714e-2b4b-5690-a9e0-84d261ec69a0', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2399714e-2b4b-5690-a9e0-84d261ec69a0'}, {'abstract': 'At a tournament that draws some of New York’s top bridge talent, a 95-year-old and her partner come out on top.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/crosswords/bridge/selma-schlechter-and-jacqui-mitchell-win-uja-event-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'At a tournament that draws some of New York’s top bridge talent, a 95-year-old and her partner come out on top.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s most successful single-session charity bridge tournament, the UJA-Federation of New York’s Women’s Philanthropy pro-am, was played Tuesday at the Harmonie Club in Manhattan. The winners with a huge 81 percent game were Jacqui Mitchell and Selma Schlechter of New York. Melih Ozdil and Sharon Goldman of New York finished second.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Winning Pair With Plenty of Experience', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Winning Pair With Plenty of Experience', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:27:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77be6935-ea1c-53cb-944a-29bf0250dedd', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77be6935-ea1c-53cb-944a-29bf0250dedd'}, {'abstract': 'Time to ditch the Web in favor of ‘LDL.’', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/the-trouble-with-e-mail/', 'snippet': 'Time to ditch the Web in favor of ‘LDL.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among Internet users with secrets, including bankers, lawyers, hackers and people who visit porn sites or confide in friends — so that would be all of us — there’s a widespread apprehension that the Web is no longer a safe place to spill them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With E-Mail', 'kicker': 'Virginia Heffernan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2731be1-ee01-567d-aeb5-c569f172be83', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2731be1-ee01-567d-aeb5-c569f172be83'}, {'abstract': 'The way we think about the turmoil in the Middle East and elsewhere is shaped by how we understand human rights.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/are-there-natural-human-rights/', 'snippet': 'The way we think about the turmoil in the Middle East and elsewhere is shaped by how we understand human rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This has been a year of uprisings. The series of popular revolts, struggles and crackdowns by governments, which continue to this day, began in Tunisia when protesters demanded the removal of president Zine al-Abidine Ben Ali.\xa0 The claims of the protesters were about the right to eat (the cost of food), political corruption, freedom of speech and basic political rights.\xa0 Then came Egypt, as thousands flocked to Tahrir Square in Cairo and elsewhere.\xa0 Their demands were similar to those in Tunisia. Both sets of protesters were successful: the leaders of both countries fled.\xa0 This was the first wave.\xa0 It was largely peaceful.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are There Natural Human Rights?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Boylan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boylan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12356583-899d-5841-bfa7-b7b237094488', 'word_count': 2256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12356583-899d-5841-bfa7-b7b237094488'}, {'abstract': 'The way Francesca Schiavone adapted to the emotional reality of her match against Jelena Jankovic speaks volumes about her growth as a player.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/29/schiavone-advances-by-keeping-her-cool/', 'snippet': 'The way Francesca Schiavone adapted to the emotional reality of her match against Jelena Jankovic speaks volumes about her growth as a player.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stakes were high late in the third set Sunday between Jelena Jankovic and the French Open defending champion, Francesca Schiavone. Both players had squandered opportunities to run away with the match, and neither was playing their best tennis. Schiavone had won converted just 4 of 19 break-point chances. Time and again, Schiavone set up the point, only to hit the final shot into the top of the net or just wide. For her part, Jankovic allowed her emotions to run away, especially at several critical junctures late in the match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schiavone Advances by Keeping Her Cool', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jankovic, Jelena', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schiavone, Francesca', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T21:32:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/901e683b-e6af-53da-9ac7-ddab8222727b', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/901e683b-e6af-53da-9ac7-ddab8222727b'}, {'abstract': 'DanceAfrica 2011 pays homage to “Expressions and Encounters: African, Cuban and American Rhythms.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/arts/dance/danceafrica-at-brooklyn-academy-of-music-review.html', 'snippet': 'DanceAfrica 2011 pays homage to “Expressions and Encounters: African, Cuban and American Rhythms.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s nothing like a DanceAfrica performance to send you straight to summer. It has its own flow, and you can’t fight it, no matter how chaotic, raucous and hazily dull it becomes. Chuck Davis, the group’s artistic director, loves to speechify, and during the nearly two-and-a-half hour program at the Brooklyn Academy of Music on Friday night — it was so long he had to cut the finale — there were two award presentations and a memorial service to honor those once connected to DanceAfrica. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 