trait Of a Shaker Community', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shakers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hutton, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:17:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b037978b-d59f-5c9b-8b71-482a64a9bdfc', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b037978b-d59f-5c9b-8b71-482a64a9bdfc'}, {'abstract': 'Diana Vishneva and Alina Cojocaru bring their interpretations of Giselle to American Ballet Theater on back-to-back nights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/arts/dance/american-ballet-theaters-giselle-at-the-met-review.html', 'snippet': 'Diana Vishneva and Alina Cojocaru bring their interpretations of Giselle to American Ballet Theater on back-to-back nights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Behind the story of “Giselle” lie the still dismaying, well-attested historical accounts of dance mania that caused deaths in the Rhineland in the late medieval and early Renaissance eras. Onstage love, death and dance all come together in an engrossingly beautiful vortex. Other ballets have greater music and greater choreography, yet this one still takes us deep into the classically Romantic heart of what ballet itself is all about. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Top Giselles, Back to Back', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Top Giselles, Back to Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vishneva, Diana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cojocaru, Alina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:19:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b8b815d-c833-5f89-9e02-caf82ac4f25e', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b8b815d-c833-5f89-9e02-caf82ac4f25e'}, {'abstract': 'The president of CBS Radio instructed programmers to start identifying more of the songs played on the air.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/business/media/30radio.html', 'snippet': 'The president of CBS Radio instructed programmers to start identifying more of the songs played on the air.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would seem one of the basic tasks of any disk jockey: tell listeners the name of the song they just heard. But last week the head of a major radio company felt compelled to instruct its programmers to start identifying more of the songs played on the air, by title and artist name.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/radio/radio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 655, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/radio/radio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/business/radio/radio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Radio Reminds D.J.’s to Identify Songs: ‘When You Play It, Say It’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CBS Radio Reminds D.J.’s to Identify Songs: ‘When You Play It, Say It’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disc Jockeys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:22:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/735b33a8-bb9c-5e85-b4a3-07bbbddce243', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/735b33a8-bb9c-5e85-b4a3-07bbbddce243'}, {'abstract': 'Modern dance now has a new performance space, the iPhone, with a free app called Dances for an iPhone, created by the choreographer and videographer Richard Daniels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/arts/dance/modern-dance-has-an-iphone-app.html', 'snippet': 'Modern dance now has a new performance space, the iPhone, with a free app called Dances for an iPhone, created by the choreographer and videographer Richard Daniels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Modern dance now has a new performance space, the iPhone, with a free app called Dances for an iPhone, created by the choreographer and videographer Richard Daniels. It features six short films with performances by the dancers Carmen de Lavallade, Deborah Jowitt, Regina Larkin, Christine Redpath and Megan Williams, with biographical information for each performer. Mr. Daniels said this was the first installment in a planned series meant to bring the world of dance to a digital audience. Mr. Daniels is an artist in residence at the Baryshnikov Arts Center in New York, where an update for the app, featuring seven new performances, is in production.\xa0 ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Modern Dance Has an iPhone App', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Modern Dance Has an iPhone App', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:36:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/40a70d66-dbe7-577e-b2e0-f813ce1bfa54', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40a70d66-dbe7-577e-b2e0-f813ce1bfa54'}, {'abstract': 'Austin Ratner has won the Sami Rohr Prize for Jewish Literature, the Jewish Book Council has announced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/books/jewish-prize-for-brooklyn-author.html', 'snippet': 'Austin Ratner has won the Sami Rohr Prize for Jewish Literature, the Jewish Book Council has announced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Austin Ratner has won the Sami Rohr Prize for Jewish Literature, the Jewish Book Council has announced. Mr. Ratner, who lives in Brooklyn, will receive the $100,000 award, presented each year to an emerging Jewish writer, for his novel, “The Jump Artist” (Bellevue Literary Press, 2009). Joseph Skibell, the second-place winner, will receive the Jewish Literature Choice Award of $25,000, for his novel “A Curable Romantic” (Algonquin Books, 2010). Both awards will be presented on Tuesday at the Center for Jewish History in New York. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jewish Prize for Brooklyn Author', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jewish Prize for Brooklyn Author', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skibell, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:38:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bf70cd3-acb7-572a-8207-750fe0c3d3dc', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bf70cd3-acb7-572a-8207-750fe0c3d3dc'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Musical Theater Festival has selected musicals for its 2011 Next Link Project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/theater/selections-for-next-link-project.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Musical Theater Festival has selected musicals for its 2011 Next Link Project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Musical Theater Festival has selected musicals for its 2011 Next Link Project, a program that provides visibility, financial support, training and networking for rising talent with previously unproduced work. Twelve of the festival’s fully staged slots will go to Next Link Project productions, including “The Brain That Wouldn’t Die! In 3-D!!!,” with book and lyrics by T. Sivak and E. Gelman, and “Jack Perry Is Alive (and Dating),” with book and lyrics by Harrison David Rivers and Daniella Shoshan and music by Julia Meinwald. Selections were chosen by a jury of industry professionals. \xa0The festival will run from Sept. 26 to Oct. 16.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Selections for Next Link Project', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Selections for Next Link Project', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Musical Theater Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:39:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/05249013-dd57-57fb-877c-729247222f26', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05249013-dd57-57fb-877c-729247222f26'}, {'abstract': 'There is one set of ethics guidelines for the rank-and-file staff and another for the 24 elite executive directors who oversee the organization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/business/global/30fund.html', 'snippet': 'There is one set of ethics guidelines for the rank-and-file staff and another for the 24 elite executive directors who oversee the organization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the International Monetary Fund, there is one set of ethics guidelines for the rank-and-file staff and another for the 24 elite executive directors who oversee the powerful organization.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/jp-fund/jp-fund-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At I.M.F., a Strict Ethics Code Doesn’t Apply to Top Officials', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At I.M.F., a Strict Ethics Code Doesn’t Apply to Top Officials', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Bowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f42e2444-cc0e-57a0-a1d6-70c3f3fabfd2', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f42e2444-cc0e-57a0-a1d6-70c3f3fabfd2'}, {'abstract': 'Ever wanted to dress up as Judy Garland in “The Wizard of Oz” or as Marilyn Monroe in “The Seven Year Itch”?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/movies/debbie-reynolds-auctions-costume-collection.html', 'snippet': 'Ever wanted to dress up as Judy Garland in “The Wizard of Oz” or as Marilyn Monroe in “The Seven Year Itch”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever wanted to dress up as Judy Garland in “The Wizard of Oz” or as Marilyn Monroe in “The Seven Year Itch”? Profiles in History, a California auction house, is offering enthusiasts an opportunity actually to do so when it puts the famous dresses from those movies and other famous costume pieces worn by classic film stars up for auction on June 18 in Beverly Hills, Reuters reported. The 3,500-piece collection, which is owned by Debbie Reynolds, will include a bowler worn by Charlie Chaplin, the gold velvet gown worn by Barbra Streisand in “Hello, Dolly!,” right, the Ascot dress worn by Audrey Hepburn in “My Fair Lady,” left, and several costumes for Julie Andrews in “The Sound of Music.” Ms. Reynolds said that she was sad to break up the collection but that recent attempts to open a museum to showcase the items had fallen through. “I’ve reached such a difficult time with the economic situation,” Ms. Reynolds told Reuters. “To keep them stored another 50 years didn’t make sense. I hope in the end they all find happy homes, that they will be shown, and that they might even land in museums.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debbie Reynolds Auctions Costume Collection', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debbie Reynolds Auctions Costume Collection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Profiles in History', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Costumes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-29T22:41:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3d92491-d226-57d0-89b8-0ec34ea4d6ce', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3d92491-d226-57d0-89b8-0ec34ea4d6ce'}, {'abstract': '“A Christmas Story,” the holiday film classic about a boy and his Red Ryder BB gun, will find new life on the stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/theater/pop-goes-the-bb-gun-in-tune.html', 'snippet': '“A Christmas Story,” the holiday film classic about a boy and his Red Ryder BB gun, will find new life on the stage.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A Christmas Story,” the holiday film classic about a boy and his Red Ryder BB gun, will find new life on the stage as “A Christmas Story, the Musical!,” the producers announced. The production features a score by the composer-lyricist team Benj Pasek and Justin Paul (“Roald Dahl’s James and the Giant Peach” ) and a book by Joseph Robinette (“Charlotte’s Web”) based on the writings of the radio humorist Jean Shepherd and the 1983 “Christmas Story” film. The prime mover behind the project is Warren Carlyle, choreographer of the Kennedy Center’s current production of “Follies” in Washington. “A Christmas Story, the Musical!” will begin a five-city tour on Nov. 8 in Hershey, Pa.; it ends with a three-week run at the Chicago Theater.\xa0Peter Billingsley, who starred in the film, above, as the boy who was warned about the perils of playing with the gun, has signed on as a producer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Goes the BB Gun, in Tun