 {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a3fc8a0-f88a-5822-b325-85d07cccddbf', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a3fc8a0-f88a-5822-b325-85d07cccddbf'}, {'abstract': 'A plan to privatize Medicare will hurt beneficiaries, no matter what analogies he cites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/opinion/30mon2.html', 'snippet': 'A plan to privatize Medicare will hurt beneficiaries, no matter what analogies he cites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representative Paul Ryan is rebutting critics of his plan to turn Medicare into a “premium support” program, pointing to two existing programs that he says prove his approach would be better for beneficiaries. Don’t believe it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rep. Ryan’s Dubious Sales Pitch', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rep. Ryan’s Dubious Sales Pitch: A plan to privatize Medicare will hurt beneficiaries, no matter what analogies he cites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T01:14:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f91d4374-6181-5473-bfa5-6dc0f6096552', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f91d4374-6181-5473-bfa5-6dc0f6096552'}, {'abstract': 'The American auto industry has made small cars a central part of its strategy, seeking to capitalize on a shift in the preferences of consumers in an era of high gas prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/business/economy/30auto.html', 'snippet': 'The American auto industry has made small cars a central part of its strategy, seeking to capitalize on a shift in the preferences of consumers in an era of high gas prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — After years of being outgunned by Japanese rivals, the American auto industry has made small cars a central part of its strategy, seeking to capitalize on a fundamental shift in the preferences of consumers in an era of fast-rising gas prices. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/sub-jp-auto/sub-jp-auto-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/30/business/sub-jp-auto/sub-jp-auto-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/sub-jp-auto/sub-jp-auto-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 589, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/business/sub-jp-auto/sub-jp-auto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/business/sub-jp-auto/sub-jp-auto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Detroit’s Rebound Is Built on Smaller Cars', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Rebound, Detroit Focuses On Smaller Cars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Cars (Compact, Subcompact and Microcars)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T01:14:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c52d9c3-0a01-56d8-8bf6-310dbfef5e43', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c52d9c3-0a01-56d8-8bf6-310dbfef5e43'}, {'abstract': 'Backing by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo of New York has so far overcome worries about pollution and cost of the Manhattan cable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/nyregion/power-cable-under-hudson-has-critics-in-2-states.html', 'snippet': 'Backing by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo of New York has so far overcome worries about pollution and cost of the Manhattan cable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City officials are not completely sold on it, some state lawmakers are dead-set against it and now regulators in New Jersey are trying to stop it. But New York’s governor, Andrew M. Cuomo, wants it, so a cable that would carry electricity from New Jersey to Manhattan appears to be about to make its way across the Hudson River.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hudson Power Cable Still Has Critics in 2 States', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hudson Power Cable Still Has Critics in 2 States', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Power Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T01:19:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a4c937e-9662-5def-8547-7f53c5e3511c', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a4c937e-9662-5def-8547-7f53c5e3511c'}, {'abstract': 'Kathy Hochul, the surprise winner in a special election for a Congressional seat in the Buffalo area, grew up in a family that emphasized giving back to others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/nyregion/kathy-hochul-inherited-an-eagerness-to-serve.html', 'snippet': 'Kathy Hochul, the surprise winner in a special election for a Congressional seat in the Buffalo area, grew up in a family that emphasized giving back to others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few months before Kathy Hochul was born, her family was living in a 31-by-8-foot trailer not far from the hulking Bethlehem Steel plant near Buffalo. When things got a little better, they moved to the second-floor flat of a home in working-class Woodlawn.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/nyregion/HOCHUL1/HOCHUL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/30/nyregion/HOCHUL1/HOCHUL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/nyregion/HOCHUL1/HOCHUL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/30/nyregion/HOCHUL1/HOCHUL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/30/nyregion/HOCHUL1/HOCHUL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Her Inheritance: An Eagerness to Serve', 'kicker': 'Woman in the News | Kathleen Courtney Hochul', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Her Inheritance: An Eagerness to Serve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buffalo (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochul, Kathleen C', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T01:20:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Hernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fd6aa9c-fd3f-5a4e-8210-055bb91d24c8', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fd6aa9c-fd3f-5a4e-8210-055bb91d24c8'}, {'abstract': 'States should pass a bill that limits the use of credit histories by potential employers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/opinion/30mon3.html', 'snippet': 'States should pass a bill that limits the use of credit histories by potential employers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Millions of Americans who lost their jobs to the recession and fell behind in payments to creditors are being penalized again, this time by companies that use credit records to screen job applicants. Five states have limited the use of credit histories by potential employers and about 20 are considering similar measures that deserve to become law. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Millions Need Not Apply', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Millions Need Not Apply', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Scores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T01:21:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a8fd912-8e68-53c3-9fdb-09789e6acc2f', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a8fd912-8e68-53c3-9fdb-09789e6acc2f'}, {'abstract': 'Whatever you make of the wars in which soldiers fought, whatever you make of war itself, their sacrifices are real and permanent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/opinion/30mon4.html', 'snippet': 'Whatever you make of the wars in which soldiers fought, whatever you make of war itself, their sacrifices are real and permanent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, there are families knotted in mourning for a soldier recently lost in Iraq or Afghanistan. There are families for which mourning has become an absence more persistently felt than they could ever have imagined.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Among the Graves This Memorial Day', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Among the Graves