value': 'Casey, Robert P Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Labor Statistics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T13:38:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d50f66ea-9c3b-5fb1-923d-d20cd5005135', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d50f66ea-9c3b-5fb1-923d-d20cd5005135'}, {'abstract': 'It was clear Richard Gasquet of France did not believe he had a chance to beat Serbia’s Novak Djokovic during their French Open match on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/djokovic-gasquet-the-mental-mismatch/', 'snippet': 'It was clear Richard Gasquet of France did not believe he had a chance to beat Serbia’s Novak Djokovic during their French Open match on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most matches are decided before the players take the court. Each player, according to this theory, knows who will win, and the loser spends a lot of energy playing just well enough to have a respectable showing, but without truly believing victory is possible.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Djokovic-Gasquet: the Mental Mismatch', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gasquet, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T14:14:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cec18ce-008b-5302-83c7-daeb04d41f89', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cec18ce-008b-5302-83c7-daeb04d41f89'}, {'abstract': 'Bob and Mike Bryan advanced past Teymuraz Gabashvili and Mikhail Kukushkin to reach the quarterfinals of men’s doubles at the French Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/in-a-flash-bryans-advance-in-doubles/', 'snippet': 'Bob and Mike Bryan advanced past Teymuraz Gabashvili and Mikhail Kukushkin to reach the quarterfinals of men’s doubles at the French Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS -– Workmanlike is the word that describes Bob and Mike Bryan of the United States, the world’s top-ranked team, as they move through the men’s doubles draw at the French Open.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Flash, Bryans Advance in Doubles', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T14:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51f9540a-a0fe-5598-898d-e082f37a0b97', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51f9540a-a0fe-5598-898d-e082f37a0b97'}, {'abstract': 'So it’s scare tactics to point them out.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/the-facts-have-a-liberal-bias-again/', 'snippet': 'So it’s scare tactics to point them out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Benen watches an entire panel on Meet the Press condemn Democrats for accurately describing the Ryan plan:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Facts Have A Liberal Bias, Again', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T14:26:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/206ab372-5c1d-5660-b62b-5e0cfbc536d7', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/206ab372-5c1d-5660-b62b-5e0cfbc536d7'}, {'abstract': 'With the N.C.A.A. investigating the Ohio State program, Tressel, who led the Buckeyes to their first national championship in 34 years, stepped down in “the best interest” of the university.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/sports/ncaafootball/jim-tressel-resigns-as-ohio-state-football-coach.html', 'snippet': 'With the N.C.A.A. investigating the Ohio State program, Tressel, who led the Buckeyes to their first national championship in 34 years, stepped down in “the best interest” of the university.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jim Tressel resigned as the football coach at Ohio State on Monday, an abrupt move in a long but escalating scandal that has ensnared the university in an N.C.A.A. investigation and sullied Tressel’s reputation.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type