h-cab-for-cuties-christina-perri-kina-grannis-new-cds.html', 'snippet': 'Reviews of Death Cab for Cutie, Christina Perri and Kina Grannis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEATH CAB FOR CUTIE', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/jp-choice-3/jp-choice-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/jp-choice-3/jp-choice-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/jp-choice-3/jp-choice-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooding Now Laced With Hope', 'kicker': 'Critics’ Choice: New Cds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brooding Now Laced With Hope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Death Cab for Cutie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:22:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles and Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b6b9cd8-89c3-5ce0-ae7f-984f4eb3dbff', 'word_count': 981, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b6b9cd8-89c3-5ce0-ae7f-984f4eb3dbff'}, {'abstract': 'Clubbed Thumb, the Obie Award-winning New York theater company that commissions, develops and produces provocative plays, has announced titles for the 16th annual Summerworks festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/theater/summerworks-plays-are-announced.html', 'snippet': 'Clubbed Thumb, the Obie Award-winning New York theater company that commissions, develops and produces provocative plays, has announced titles for the 16th annual Summerworks festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Clubbed Thumb, the Obie Award-winning New York theater company that commissions, develops and produces provocative plays, has announced titles for the 16th annual Summerworks festival, from Sunday through June 25, at Here Arts Center in Manhattan. The festival, which highlights original works by American playwrights, will feature three new plays, according to  Maria Striar, Clubbed Thumb’s producing artistic director. They are  Tanya Saracho’s “Enfrascada,” directed by  Jerry Ruiz, from Thursday through June 11;  Kristin Newbom and  W. David Hancock’s “Our Lot,” directed by  May Adrales, from June 12 through 18; and  Jason Grote’s “Civilization (All You Can Eat),” directed by  Seth Bockley, from June 19 through 25.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Summerworks Plays Are Announced', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Summerworks Plays Are Announced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:23:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a67a7c9e-cfd2-5e20-af5b-16f4447fd55c', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a67a7c9e-cfd2-5e20-af5b-16f4447fd55c'}, {'abstract': 'The pop star Sean Kingston was hospitalized in Miami.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/arts/music/sean-kingston-remains-in-hospital-after-crash.html', 'snippet': 'The pop star Sean Kingston was hospitalized in Miami.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pop star Sean Kingston who was hospitalized in Miami after an accident involving a personal watercraft on Sunday, was listed in stable condition on Monday, Billboard.com reported. The publicist Joseph Carozza issued a statement saying that Mr. Kingston, 21, had been moved to intensive care from the trauma unit. “Sean’s family thanks everyone for their prayers and support during this time,” Mr. Carozza said. Mr. Kingston, whose real name is Kisean Anderson, was taken to Jackson Memorial Hospital after he and a female passenger aboard his watercraft crashed into a bridge around 6 p.m. A boater rescued them from the water, and crews took them to the hospital. No details regarding the injuries have been released. Since breaking out with his debut single, “Beautiful Girls,” in 2007, Mr. Kingston has scored hits like “Take You There,” “Fire Burning” and “Eenie Meenie” with Justin Bieber, whom he joined on tour last year. Mr. Kingston received an outpouring of support by his fans and his peers on Twitter, including Mr. Bieber, Rihanna, Nicki Minaj and Chris Brown.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sean Kingston Remains in Hospital After Crash', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sean Kingston Remains In Hospital After Crash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:24:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8e1c1ed-64f9-5fe9-88c6-9a5ab8ccd861', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8e1c1ed-64f9-5fe9-88c6-9a5ab8ccd861'}, {'abstract': 'Tributes have been pouring in from around the world from musicians and friends of the poet and music pioneer Gil Scott-Heron.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/arts/music/artists-remember-gil-scott-heron.html', 'snippet': 'Tributes have been pouring in from around the world from musicians and friends of the poet and music pioneer Gil Scott-Heron.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tributes have been pouring in from around the world from musicians and friends of the poet and music pioneer Gil Scott-Heron, who died on Friday at 62. Eminem,  Talib Kweli and Snoop Dogg were among the rappers who acknowledged Mr. Scott-Heron’s early influence on hip-hop and black protest culture after hearing the news, according to BBC News. The cause of death was not immediately known, though The Associated Press reported that Mr. Scott-Heron had become ill after returning from a trip to Europe. Chuck D of Public Enemy posted on Twitter, “We do what we do and how we do because of you.” Wu-Tang Clan’s Ghostface Killah wrote: “Salute Gil Scott-Heron for his wisdom and poetry! May he rest in paradise.” Eminem wrote on Twitter: “RIP Gil Scott-Heron. He influenced all of hip-hop.” Cee Lo Green recognized “the god Gil Scott,” while Talib Kweli said he “completely influenced me as an artist.” The politically outspoken rapper Michael Franti said Mr. Scott-Heron’s talent was his ability to “make us think about the world in a different way.”  Richard Russell, who produced and released Mr. Scott-Heron’s final album, “I’m New Here in 2010,” described him as “a master lyricist, singer, orator and keyboard player.”  Lemn Sissay, a friend of Mr. Scott-Heron’s who produced a documentary on his work, told the BBC that he was “a polymath” who “spoke crucially of the issues of his people.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artists Remember Gil Scott-Heron', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Artists Remember Gil Scott-Heron', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:25:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fefdea12-6ed3-5d4e-bb72-e0902a427758', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fefdea12-6ed3-5d4e-bb72-e0902a427758'}, {'abstract': 'Now a film about the romance of Prince William and Kate Middleton is expected to be released in August on the Hallmark Channel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/arts/television/royal-love-story-heads-to-hallmark.html', 'snippet': 'Now a film about the romance of Prince William and Kate Middleton is expected to be released in August on the Hallmark Channel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You saw the wedding. You followed the postnuptials gossip. Now a film about the romance of Prince William and Kate Middleton is expected to be released in August on the Hallmark Channel, The Associated Press reported. “William & Kate: A Royal Love Story” is a mixture of fact and fiction, according to the director, Linda Yellen, who took some time out from filming at MediaPro Studios in Romania to talk with The A.P. about her project. Ms. Yellen was the producer of CBS’s film “The Royal Romance of Charles and Diana” (1982), which drew a huge audience. “William & Kate” is more than a romance; it’s also “a psychological story of the memory of the mother, in this case Princess Diana, and her legacy,” Ms. Yellen said. The film shows how William “has to choose to live his life with her memory and with the decisions he has to make,” including the decision of whom to marry. The couple were married on April 29 in a lavish ceremony at Westminster Abbey. Filming for the movie, which imagines some scenes from the couple’s romance, began in early May. Ms. Yellen, who met Princess Diana, described the project as “a very personal story.” She added, “In a way, it’s my tribute to her as well as a tribute to the young couple.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Royal Love Story Heads to Hallmark', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Royal Love Story Heads to Hallmark', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/97f5d800-6eb8-5227-ae6b-fbc7c8f919d4', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97f5d800-6eb8-5227-ae6b-fbc7c8f919d4'}, {'abstract': 'Natalia Osipova stars in “Coppélia,” broadcast live from the Bolshoi Ballet in Moscow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/arts/dance/natalia-osipova-and-the-bolshoi-live-from-moscow-review.html', 'snippet': 'Natalia Osipova stars in “Coppélia,” broadcast live from the Bolshoi Ballet in Moscow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday morning, in a live transmission of the Bolshoi Ballet’s production of the three-act “Coppélia,” the extraordinary ballerina Natalia Osipova bounded with her characteristic élan onto a New York cinema screen. The camera — though only employing close-ups for acting passages — often followed her full-frame, so that we had only a blurry sense of the space around her: disorienting but exciting. In the intimate cinema, people kept starting to applaud.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/Broadcast/Broadcast-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/Broadcast/Broadcast-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/Broadcast/Broadcast-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/Broadcast/Broadcast-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/Broadcast/Broadcast-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Feast on the Bolshoi (Popcorn, Too)', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Morning Feast On the Bolshoi (Popcorn, Too)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osipova, Natalia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolshoi Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:28:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33e42eca-b2f2-5437-abb8-4fe05fa5c4ca', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33e42eca-b2f2-5437-abb8-4fe05fa5c4ca'}, {'abstract': 'Tornado fatalities have declined steadily, but many of the circumstances that were beyond science in 1953 are still beyond science today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/science/31tornado.html', 'snippet': 'Tornado fatalities have declined steadily, but many of the circumstances that were beyond science in 1953 are still beyond science today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a warm, damp day in central Nebraska, heavy and still. The tornado arrived at the Madsen farm just before 3 p.m. Mads Madsen, 63, his wife Minnie, 59, three of their children and five of their grandchildren were in the parlor eating Sunday dinner. Twenty minutes later all 10 of them were dead.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/science/TORN-span/TORN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/science/TORN-span/TORN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/science/TORN-span/TORN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/science/TORN-span/TORN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/science/TORN-span/TORN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Advances, Tornado Forecasts Show Limits', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despite Advances, Tornado Forecasts Show Limits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forecasts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:31:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Gugliotta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gugliotta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aac6e38a-fe95-5937-95cf-43b16d086370', 'word_count': 1476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aac6e38a-fe95-5937-95cf-43b16d086370'}, {'abstract': 'This assortment of four new one-acts and one revival features the playwrights Romulus Linney, Ben Rosenthal, J. Holtham, Billy Aronson and Qui Nguyen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/theater/reviews/marathon-2011-series-a-at-ensemble-studio-theater.html', 'snippet': 'This assortment of four new one-acts and one revival features the playwrights Romulus Linney, Ben Rosenthal, J. Holtham, Billy Aronson and Qui Nguyen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Making Romulus Linney’s “Tennessee” the centerpiece of Series A of the Ensemble Studio Theater’s 33rd annual festival of one-acts was a fine idea. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/ENSEMBLE-1/ENSEMBLE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/ENSEMBLE-1/ENSEMBLE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/ENSEMBLE-1/ENSEMBLE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 883, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/ENSEMBLE-1/ENSEMBLE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/arts/ENSEMBLE-1/ENSEMBLE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Elegiac Appalachian Hollers to New Terrain', 'kicker': 'Theater Review | Marathon 2011: Series A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Elegiac Appalachian Hollers to New Terrain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Linney, Romulus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ensemble Studio Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nguyen, Qui', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARONSON, BILLY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Rooney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rooney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e7d9027-fedd-556a-bb17-14831d514fc4', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e7d9027-fedd-556a-bb17-14831d514fc4'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers in New York estimate that a newly discovered virus, canine hepacivirus, also known as CHV, shared an ancestor with the hepatitis C virus 500 to 1,000 years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/health/31hepatitis.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers in New York estimate that a newly discovered virus, canine hepacivirus, also known as CHV, shared an ancestor with the hepatitis C virus 500 to 1,000 years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hepatitis C is, in some ways, a high-profile disease. Worldwide, an estimated 200 million people are infected with the virus. Some of them will suffer cirrhosis, liver cancer and even death. Celebrities like Steven Tyler of Aerosmith and “American Idol” have spoken publicly of their infections.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Viral Outbreaks in Dogs Yield Clues on Origins of Hepatitis C', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Viral Outbreaks in Dogs Yield Clues on Origins of Hepatitis C', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liver', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hepatitis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:40:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b820bfbd-c9a0-5c81-a04a-10935c5b3cce', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b820bfbd-c9a0-5c81-a04a-10935c5b3cce'}, {'abstract': 'Forget counting sheep. Try these suggestions instead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/health/31brodybox.html', 'snippet': 'Forget counting sheep. Try these suggestions instead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists know that many people inadvertently undermine their ability to fall asleep and stay asleep for a full night. Here are some frequent suggestions:', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Rest Easy, Forget the Sheep', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Rest Easy, Forget the Sheep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-30T21:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3212dab-21f3-5a18-85c6-aa53ca1ab312', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3212dab-21f3-5a18-85c6-aa53ca1ab312'}, {'abstract': 'As the global economy recovers so, too, do greenhouse gas emissions.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/tracking-economy-co2-emissions-hit-new-high/', 'snippet': 'As the global economy recovers so, too, do greenhouse gas emissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The International Energy Agency has reported that global emissions of carbon dioxide from energy use, tracking the recovering world economy,  hit a new high of 30.6 billion metric tons of the gas last year. (Emissions from deforestation and activities like cement manufacturing are not included.) That’s 5 percent above the previous peak, measured in 2008.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracking Economy, CO2 Emissions Hit New High', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 