nyt://article/10098abf-c892-5603-879b-ecb014d3ca3c', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10098abf-c892-5603-879b-ecb014d3ca3c'}, {'abstract': 'Tizway ended a slump and gave his trainer, H. James Bond, his first Grade I victory since 2002.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/sports/tizway-runs-second-fastest-metropolitan-mile.html', 'snippet': 'Tizway ended a slump and gave his trainer, H. James Bond, his first Grade I victory since 2002.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tizway was an older horse who had a chance to distinguish himself. H. James Bond is a seasoned horseman who had a chance to return to the spotlight. It took horse and trainer only 1 minute 32.9 seconds to blitz through the second-fastest Metropolitan Mile in the race’s 118-year history on Monday and accomplish both missions.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tizway Wins Fast Met Mile', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tizway Wins Fast Met Mile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belmont (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:05:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3abda3f3-6d92-570d-8b79-4840b3b62123', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3abda3f3-6d92-570d-8b79-4840b3b62123'}, {'abstract': 'The economy is not growing nearly fast enough to dent unemployment, but help is not forthcoming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/opinion/31tue1.html', 'snippet': 'The economy is not growing nearly fast enough to dent unemployment, but help is not forthcoming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A month ago, when an initial gauge of first-quarter economic growth came in surprisingly weak, many policy makers and economists expected the bad news to prove fleeting. But when revised data were released last week, the growth estimate remained stuck at an annual rate of 1.8 percent, compared with 3.1 percent at the end of last year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Numbers Are Grim', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Numbers Are Grim: The economy needs more help, but help is not forthcoming', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3de675c-0576-5ac7-aba4-590df7f7650a', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3de675c-0576-5ac7-aba4-590df7f7650a'}, {'abstract': 'Despite a poor human rights record, the U.S. still broadly engages with the country’s dictator, Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/world/africa/31guinea.html', 'snippet': 'Despite a poor human rights record, the U.S. still broadly engages with the country’s dictator, Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MALABO, Equatorial Guinea — While the United States has turned its back on some authoritarian rulers in North Africa and the Middle East, its attitude toward strategically placed autocrats in less restive corners of Africa is more ambiguous, and perhaps nowhere more so than in this oil-rich speck of a nation in the Gulf of Guinea.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/world/GUINEA-2/GUINEA-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/world/GUINEA-2/GUINEA-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/world/GUINEA-2/GUINEA-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Engages With an Iron Leader in Equatorial Guinea', 'kicker': 'Malabo Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Iron Grip In Africa, With Ties To the U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Equatorial Guinea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obiang Nguema Mbasogo, Teodoro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:07:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f812d842-2f32-5b8e-9cc1-feb7ca87e9f1', 'word_count': 1547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f812d842-2f32-5b8e-9cc1-feb7ca87e9f1'}, {'abstract': 'Although water pipes are commonly viewed as less dangerous to health than cigarettes, they are just as bad, researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/health/31hookah.html', 'snippet': 'Although water pipes are commonly viewed as less dangerous to health than cigarettes, they are just as bad, researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kevin Shapiro, a 20-year-old math and physics major at the University of Pennsylvania, first tried a hookah at a campus party. He liked the exotic water pipe so much that he chipped in to buy one for his fraternity house, where he says it makes a useful social lubricant at parties.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/us/HOOKAH/HOOKAH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/us/HOOKAH/HOOKAH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/us/HOOKAH/HOOKAH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/us/HOOKAH/HOOKAH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/us/HOOKAH/HOOKAH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting a Crimp in the Hookah', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putting a Crimp in the Hookah', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hookahs (Water Pipes)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:07:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f4f1f73-0124-5a9b-906c-66bcb0284d6f', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f4f1f73-0124-5a9b-906c-66bcb0284d6f'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Sass runs a boutique investment firm with $8 billion in assets. He doesn’t believe in get-rich-quick schemes and has “a healthy degree of paranoia.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/business/31sass.html', 'snippet': 'Martin Sass runs a boutique investment firm with $8 billion in assets. He doesn’t believe in get-rich-quick schemes and has “a healthy degree of paranoia.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The money manager Martin D. Sass loves a good bargain.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/31SASS/31SASS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/business/31SASS/31SASS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/31SASS/31SASS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/31SASS/31SASS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/business/31SASS/31SASS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Not a Flashy Investor, Just Successful', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Flashy, Just Successful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Macquarie Bank Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:10:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Creswell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creswell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76fe570a-1516-5bab-b0c3-aaf72bd63f9c', 'word_count': 1472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76fe570a-1516-5bab-b0c3-aaf72bd63f9c'}, {'abstract': 'New Jersey’s self-proclaimed “clean-energy advocate” has proved to be anything but.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/opinion/31tue2.html', 'snippet': 'New Jersey’s self-proclaimed “clean-energy advocate” has proved to be anything but.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Running for governor in 2009, Chris Christie vowed to become “New Jersey’s No. 1 clean-energy advocate.” That was a hollow promise. As governor, Mr. Christie proceeded to cut all the money for the Office of Climate and Energy. He raided $158 million from the clean energy fund, meant for alternative energy investments, and spent it on general programs. He withdrew the state from an important lawsuit against electric utilities to reduce emissions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gov. Christie Abandons a Good Idea', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gov. Christie Abandons a Good Idea: New Jersey’s self-proclaimed ‘clean-energy advocate’ has proved to be anything but', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:11:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/57411072-1c58-53d1-955a-11f2979e2192', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57411072-1c58-53d1-955a-11f2979e2192'}, {'abstract': 'Sarah Palin held an impromptu question-and-answer session in Gettysburg, Pa., but it did not last long.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/30/palin-talks-to-the-media-for-three-minutes/', 'snippet': 'Sarah Palin held an impromptu question-and-answer session in Gettysburg, Pa., but it did not last long.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GETTYSBURG, Pa. — Sarah Palin promised reporters on Monday night that if she runs for president, she will break from the usual approaches taken by presidential hopefuls.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Palin Talks to the Media, for Three Minutes', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T01:14:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf9aa81f-5d54-5e94-a12d-818c49c9ed4d', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf9aa81f-5d54-5e94-a12d-818c49c9ed4d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Clements founded an international drilling company before going into politics and breaking the Democrats’ stranglehold on the Texas governor’s office in 1978.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/us/politics/31clements.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Clements founded an international drilling company before going into politics and breaking the Democrats’ stranglehold on the Texas governor’s office in 1978.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — Bill Clements, a brash oilman who founded an international drilling company before going into politics and breaking the Democrats’ stranglehold on the Texas governor’s office in 1978, died Sunday at a Dallas-area hospital. He was 94. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/us/CLEMENTS-obit/CLEMENTS-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/us/CLEMENTS-obit/CLEMENTS-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/us/CLEMENTS-obit/CLEMENTS-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Clements Dies at 94;