South Africa President Jacob Zuma, the Libya leader Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi “emphasized” that he will not leave Libya despite air attacks and international pressure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHANNESBURG — In talks with South Africa President Jacob Zuma, the Libya leader Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi “emphasized” that he will not leave Libya despite air attacks and international pressure, according to a statement released Tuesday by Mr. Zuma’s office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zuma’s Office Says Qaddafi Intent on Staying in Libya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Zuma&#8217;s Office Says Qaddafi Intent on Staying in Libya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuma, Jacob G', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T15:54:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Bearak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bearak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/315b4ac7-26e3-54c6-ada2-5fe1908b73c9', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/315b4ac7-26e3-54c6-ada2-5fe1908b73c9'}, {'abstract': 'Morning Line for Tuesday, May 31', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/morning-line-shackleford-settles-in-at-belmont/', 'snippet': 'Morning Line for Tuesday, May 31', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shackleford, the winner of the Preakness, arrived at Belmont Park Monday morning. He arrived after an overnight 15-hour van ride from Churchill Downs. The New York Daily News reported that “the 3-year-old colt is expected to gallop over Belmont’s main oval Wednesday, before having an official workout over the strip Saturday.” The Associated Press said that the trainer Dale Romans “wants to make sure the 3-year-old colt is in top condition before committing to the Belmont.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morning Line: Shackleford Settles In at Belmont', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bennett Liebman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bennett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liebman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/745fc57d-e0df-5cca-a60a-50f4aae2242b', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/745fc57d-e0df-5cca-a60a-50f4aae2242b'}, {'abstract': 'The EA Sports simulation engine has been on a roll, predicting Boston would meet Vancouver for the Stanley Cup — back in October 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/video-game-decides-canucks-will-win-stanley-cup/', 'snippet': 'The EA Sports simulation engine has been on a roll, predicting Boston would meet Vancouver for the Stanley Cup — back in October 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t mess with the video game simulation, as it appears to know all, at least when it comes to hockey.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video Game Decides Canucks Will Win Stanley Cup', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Bruins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vancouver Canucks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:08:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/731d4e2a-6fa5-52fc-ae90-cabc0bb8ecba', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/731d4e2a-6fa5-52fc-ae90-cabc0bb8ecba'}, {'abstract': 'Neuroscientists theorize about Tatiana and Krista Hogan’s unique connection.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/the-twins-connection-other-theories/', 'snippet': 'Neuroscientists theorize about Tatiana and Krista Hogan’s unique connection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I wrote in Sunday’s cover story, 4-year-old conjoined twins Tatiana and Krista Hogan seem — based on casual observation and (as yet unpublished) scientific research — to share some sensory perception. Their neurosurgeon, Douglas Cochrane of British Columbia Children’s Hospital, believes that a neural bridge that links the thalamus of one girl to that of the other could explain why, for example, Tatiana would be able to see the stuffed animal Krista is holding, even when Tatiana’s eyes are closed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Twins’ Connection: Other Theories', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Twins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:09:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastna