 Green Beret killed on a secret mission in Laos is laid to rest 41 years later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thousands of people in the quiet towns of Concord and Kannapolis, N.C., will remember the weekend of April 30 and May 1 for a long time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Homecoming for a Veteran of the ‘Secret War’ in Southeast Asia', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Special Operations Command', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:11:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Stryker Meyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Stryker', 'lastname': 'Meyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f50343b1-35a9-5baf-b462-457c002977c3', 'word_count': 1451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f50343b1-35a9-5baf-b462-457c002977c3'}, {'abstract': 'Did you think stocks were more risky in early 2009 than you do today? If so, your emotions may be getting in the way.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/how-rising-stock-prices-can-fool-you/', 'snippet': 'Did you think stocks were more risky in early 2009 than you do today? If so, your emotions may be getting in the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah. His sketches are archived here on the Bucks blog and on his personal Web site, BehaviorGap.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Rising Stock Prices Can Fool You', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:14:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d8bb5b3-fbec-509d-91e3-616a444a6a89', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d8bb5b3-fbec-509d-91e3-616a444a6a89'}, {'abstract': 'Georgina Bloomberg’s new book, “The A Circuit,” is about a family headed by a blunt-talking Wall Street billionaire who lives in a Manhattan town house and “owns half of New York.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/nyregion/book-by-georgina-bloomberg-is-fiction-with-tell-all-references.html', 'snippet': 'Georgina Bloomberg’s new book, “The A Circuit,” is about a family headed by a blunt-talking Wall Street billionaire who lives in a Manhattan town house and “owns half of New York.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those who inhabit the rarefied world of Michael R. Bloomberg, with all its perquisites and payouts, have long adhered to a strict code of silence about his personal life: jaunts to Bermuda are cloaked in secrecy, tax returns are heavily redacted and even his golf handicap is off limits. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/nyregion/01georgina2_cnd/01georgina2_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/nyregion/01georgina2_cnd/01georgina2_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/01/nyregion/01georgina2_cnd/01georgina2_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Novel by Mayor’s Daughter, Hints of Family Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Novel by Mayor’s Daughter, Hints of Family Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Georgina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman and Michael Barbaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/827923ac-1bae-5f30-9acf-e12b32ecf952', 'word_count': 1345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/827923ac-1bae-5f30-9acf-e12b32ecf952'}, {'abstract': 'The hearing on Friday in St. Louis, before the Eighth Circuit, has loomed large in the fight for leverage between N.F.L. players and owners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/sports/football/lockout-hearing-may-give-nfl-clarity.html', 'snippet': 'The hearing on Friday in St. Louis, before the Eighth Circuit, has loomed large in the fight for leverage between N.F.L. players and owners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the N.F.L. owners meeting last week, John Mara, the president of the Giants, was asked if the Super Bowl, scheduled for February in Indianapolis, would be played. Yes, he told a reporter, he was confident Indianapolis would have its Super Bowl. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Headed Back to Court, N.F.L. May Gain Clarity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Headed Back to Court, N.F.L. May Gain Clarity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League Players Assn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olson, Theodore B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clement, Paul D', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:22:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c520e3f-9218-5158-8661-06721a870b23', 'word_count': 1291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c520e3f-9218-5158-8661-06721a870b23'}, {'abstract': 'The rising labor costs for companies that supply Chinese goods to the West may result in higher consumer prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/business/global/01wages.html', 'snippet': 'The rising labor costs for companies that supply Chinese goods to the West may result in higher consumer prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Wages are surging this year in China and among its main low-wage Asian rivals, benefiting workers across the region. But the increases confront trading companies and Western retailers with cost increases and are making higher prices likely for American and European consumers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/business/Wages/Wages-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/01/business/Wages/Wages-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/business/Wages/Wages-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/business/Wages/Wages-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/01/business/Wages/Wages-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As China’s Workers Get a Raise, Companies Fret', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As China’s Workers Get a Raise, Companies and Consumers Face Higher Prices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2601c4a-c562-5faa-b0bb-47b44147a235', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2601c4a-c562-5faa-b0bb-47b44147a235'}, {'abstract': 'FIFA’s president pushed aside allegations of vote-buying, while a tattoo parlor scandal led Ohio State’s football coach to resign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/sports/crises-at-fifa-and-ohio-state.html', 'snippet': 'FIFA’s president pushed aside allegations of vote-buying, while a tattoo parlor scandal led Ohio State’s football coach to resign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Memorial Day is normally a placid day in sports that allows us to remember what we’re supposed to be remembering, and no, not just how long to grill the burgers. But on Monday the whole metaphorical barbecue flared so badly that all our eyebrows got singed: Ohio State Coach Jim Tressel felt the flames lapping at his sweater vest and quit amid a deepening N.C.A.A. scandal, and Sepp Blatter, the president of FIFA, called a nonsensical news conference to proclaim there is no scandal in his organization because he says so and because he is FIFA’s president, “You cannot question me.” Feel free to imagine him shaking a spatula at you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crises at FIFA and Ohio State', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crises at FIFA and Ohio State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tressel, Jim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ohio State University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlanta Thrashers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blatter, Sepp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dohrmann, George', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wade, Dwyane', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/347f82ee-7995-5a56-bb02-b55252a9bc96', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/347f82ee-7995-5a56-bb02-b55252a9bc96'}, {'abstract': 'A Saudi woman who was detained for more than a week for driving released a statement on Tuesday, after she was allowed to leave prison.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/a-saudi-activist-in-her-own-words/', 'snippet': 'A Saudi woman who was detained for more than a week for driving released a statement on Tuesday, after she was allowed to leave prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Arab News reported, a Saudi woman who was detained for more than a week for defying Saudi Arabia’s ban on female drivers was released on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Saudi Activist, in Her Own Words', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a54ae5c-7042-5cf6-93d3-c6d63b4e7c86', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a54ae5c-7042-5cf6-93d3-c6d63b4e7c86'}, {'abstract': 'Joao Silva was among the patients at Walter Reed on whom Michelle Obama paid a quiet call on Memorial Day.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/standing-when-the-first-lady-pays-a-call/', 'snippet': 'Joao Silva was among the patients at Walter Reed on whom Michelle Obama paid a quiet call on Memorial Day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Memorial Day, Michelle Obama made an unannounced trip to visit the wounded at Walter Reed Army Medical Center. Joao Silva of The New York Times was among those on whom she paid a call. “She came across as a kind and attentive person,” Mr. Silva said. She asked about the progress of his recovery and gave yo-yos, Frisbees and White House candies to his children, Isabel, 6, and Gabriel, 5.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Standing When the First Lady Pays a Call', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silva, Joao', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T16:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb03e17b-faa1-5106-aae1-a85f342f10a1', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb03e17b-faa1-5106-aae1-a85f342f10a1'}, {'abstract': 'Apple is usually ultrasecretive about coming product announcements, but on Tuesday the company pre-announced a number of new software plans a week before a presentation that will be led by Steve Jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/why-did-apple-pre-announce-its-announcement/', 'snippet': 'Apple is usually ultrasecretive about coming product announcements, but on Tuesday the company pre-announced a number of new software plans a week before a presentation that will be led by Steve Jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New product announcements by Apple usually take place with the secrecy of a clandestine military operation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Did Apple Pre-Announce Its Announcement?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 