e', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Rivas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7881a371-a3a7-5d3d-89c7-419c1afe80de', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7881a371-a3a7-5d3d-89c7-419c1afe80de'}, {'abstract': 'Ermilio, whose handmade dresses caused a stir in the offices of Vogue, designs with a classically tailored Gossip Girl in mind.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/the-vogue-intern-katie-ermilio/', 'snippet': 'Ermilio, whose handmade dresses caused a stir in the offices of Vogue, designs with a classically tailored Gossip Girl in mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a well-known fact that Vogue assistants often move on to become designers: Irini Arakas of Prova, Aimee Cho of Gryphon and Eviana Hartman of Bodkin. But who knew that Vogue interns also graduate from the 12th floor right into their own fashion labels? In fairness, Katie Ermilio, who interned at Vogue, had a stint at Teen Vogue before leaving to devote herself full time to making clothes. “I grew up in the business,” says Ermilio, whose dad owns Ermilio Clothiers, a bespoke men’s-wear shop in Philadelphia. “But I always thought I was going to be a fashion editor — I had no interest in becoming a designer at all.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Vogue Intern | Katie Ermilio', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T17:08:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/873b18e3-a837-5f0e-827b-0d44daf3b827', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/873b18e3-a837-5f0e-827b-0d44daf3b827'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Hertog, the New-York Historical Society’s chairman, bought two of John Lansing Jr.’s notebooks at auction for $902,500.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/historical-society-snags-a-founding-fathers-notebooks/', 'snippet': 'Roger Hertog, the New-York Historical Society’s chairman, bought two of John Lansing Jr.’s notebooks at auction for $902,500.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New-York Historical Society, already the repository of several sets of original notes by delegates to the Constitutional Convention of 1787, received yet another last week: the notebooks of John Lansing Jr. of New York, who recorded many of the convention’s debates. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Historical Society Snags a Founding Father’s Notebooks', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hertog, Roger', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Library of Congress', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New-York Historical Society', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T17:10:41+0000', 'document_