ype_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ba443d2-3a40-54fc-9316-b9ed264413a9', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ba443d2-3a40-54fc-9316-b9ed264413a9'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama nominated Gen. Martin E. Dempsey to be the next chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, rounding out his realignment of the top Pentagon and intelligence posts.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/reaction-to-dempsey-appointment-as-joint-chiefs-chairman/', 'snippet': 'President Obama nominated Gen. Martin E. Dempsey to be the next chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, rounding out his realignment of the top Pentagon and intelligence posts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our colleagues Helene Cooper and Thom Shanker report on President Obama’s nomination of Gen. Martin E. Dempsey, the Army chief, to succeed Adm. Mike Mullen as chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff. In a brief Rose Garden announcement in the midst of Memorial Day observances, Mr. Obama highlighted the dual roles that General Dempsey, who, as chairman, will be the country’s highest-ranking military officer, must fill as the United States brings its troops home from Iraq, begins withdrawing from Afghanistan and takes part in the NATO operation in Libya. General Dempsey, the president said, understands that “military gains and political progress must go hand in hand.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaction to Dempsey Appointment', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cartwright, James E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dempsey, Martin E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joint Chiefs of Staff', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T17:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By At War', 'person': [{'firstname': 'At', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'War', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c94c8243-b201-5dff-92bb-abedcde75323', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c94c8243-b201-5dff-92bb-abedcde75323'}, {'abstract': 'A new campaign to market “106 eclectic festivals” features mash-ups of real animals, including a “foxutan” and a “deerus.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1.html', 'snippet': 'A new campaign to market “106 eclectic festivals” features mash-ups of real animals, including a “foxutan” and a “deerus.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a scene in the musical “Bells Are Ringing” in which a character gets so excited about meeting a “fabulous creature” that he joyously breaks into song. Imagine what he would have done if he had come across a new campaign with a menagerie of marvelous beasts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1-span/31adnewsletter1-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1-span/31adnewsletter1-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1-span/31adnesletter1-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1-span/31adnewsletter1-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/05/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1-span/31adnewsletter1-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strange Creatures Promote Montreal’s Festivals', 'kicker': 'Campaign Spotlight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strange Creatures Promote Montreal&#8217;s Festivals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montreal (Quebec)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Relations and Publicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T18:10:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdce5375-5a2e-5e5c-b404-10beedc8bd5d', 'word_count': 1565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdce5375-5a2e-5e5c-b404-10beedc8bd5d'}, {'abstract': 'Can Palestinians go their own way?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/peace-without-partners/', 'snippet': 'Can Palestinians go their own way?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems like months ago, rather than a week, that President Obama tried to move the Mideast ball down the field a bit, and Israel’s prime minister, Benjamin Netanyahu, came to Washington. The applause in Congress for Netanyahu’s speech last Tuesday — the adjective universally used was “rapturous” — found an odd counterpoint in the negative reaction among most commentators. Fareed Zakaria simply ruled Netanyahu out of history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peace Without Partners', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-05-31T18:18:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Malcomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malcomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9bd3787-dbb6-5b00-96f5-acb1a4a2aacb', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9bd3787-dbb6-5b00-96f5-acb1a4a2aacb'}, {'abstract': 'France’s Gael Monfils could not fulfill his country’s dreams of reaching the semifinals, falling to Roger Federer in straight sets.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/monfilss-magic-evaporates-as-federer-advances/', 'snippet': 'France’s Gael Monfils could not fulfill his country’s dreams of reaching the semifinals, falling to Roger Federer in straight sets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — France’s newest favorite tennis son, Gael Monfils, broke Roger Federer’s serve early in the first set, took 2-1 lead in their quarterfinal match on Tuesday at the French Open and made it look like it wasn’t much of an obstacle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monfils’s Magic Ev